window.ML4T={"generated": "2026-10-01", "counts": {"questions": 518, "ML": 308, "QF": 210, "withdrawn": 9, "pdfArtifacts": 5}, "heuristics": {"absolute_false_pct": 74, "negation_inacc_pct": 69, "accurate_base_pct": 58, "positional": {"A": 75, "B": 63, "C": 47, "D": 53, "E": 50}, "inverted_total": 95}, "domains": [{"part": "ML", "num": "01", "name": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "cheatsheet": "- **Supervised vs. unsupervised** is defined by the *task and the presence of paired target values (labels/Y)*, NOT by the input features, the output format, or how results are later interpreted.\n  - **Supervised**: each training example is paired with a known target (numeric return, class label, approval flag). Learns a mapping X → Y.\n  - **Unsupervised**: no targets supplied; the model discovers structure (clusters/regimes) from features (X) alone.\n  - A common **pipeline pattern**: unsupervised clustering produces \"signatures\"/regimes that are then fed as features into a downstream supervised model. Attaching a return/score to a discovered cluster afterward makes *that later step* supervised; it does not make the clustering supervised.\n- **Regression vs. classification** is defined by the *nature of the target*:\n  - **Regression** → quantitative/continuous target (price, YTM, basis-point spread, probability 0.0–1.0). Estimates E(Y | X). Evaluated with distance-based metrics: MSE, MAE.\n  - **Classification** → qualitative/discrete categories (up/down, approve/deny, multiclass). Estimates P(Y = j | X). Evaluated with error rate/accuracy (and cost-sensitive variants).\n  - **Same underlying phenomenon can be framed either way**; thresholding a continuous prediction into a category is a *later decision step*, not a change in the learned task. Binning to categories **discards magnitude/variance information**.\n- **Training vs. inference** (\"fit-once, apply-many\"):\n  - **Training** = fitting/estimating parameters from historical labeled data (often expensive, offline, periodic).\n  - **Inference** = applying the fitted model to new inputs (often the latency-critical, online step; exposed to live market risk).\n  - Training cost ≠ inference cost — no 1:1 relationship. A model can train for hours and infer in microseconds.\n  - **Eager (parametric) learners** (linear, trees) build a compact representation → cheap inference independent of N training rows (cost ~ number of features / tree depth). **Lazy learners** (KNN) do ~no training work but pay at inference, scaling with training-set size N; must retain the data in memory.\n- **Hypothesis space & model families**: choosing a model class constrains which functions the learner can represent. More expressive families capture more patterns but risk overfitting; interpretability/governance can justify a more constrained family. Different families (linear, tree, instance-based) search *different* hypothesis spaces and generally do NOT converge to the same predictor.\n- **Generalization vs. memorization / bias-variance**:\n  - Goal is performance on *unseen* data, not reproduction of the training set. Near-zero training error + high test error = overfitting/memorization = **high variance**.\n  - Underfitting = **high bias** (too simple to capture the true relationship). Adding data of the same type doesn't fix high bias — need more capacity/better features.\n  - Expected test error = **bias² + variance + irreducible error (ε)**. ε (unmeasured factors, inherent randomness) cannot be reduced by any model and sets the accuracy floor; the U-shaped test-error-vs-complexity curve persists above that floor.\n  - Generalization is defined for a **stationary** distribution; a regime shift (change in the data-generating process) can break even a well-generalizing model.\n  - **Regularization** trades training fit for a simpler, more stable hypothesis to favor generalization.\n- **What makes a \"good\" model** is contextual — judged against the decision it supports, not a single metric:\n  - Balances **accuracy, robustness (performance under distribution shift), and stability (consistent outputs/behavior under small perturbations)**.\n  - Averages can hide tail behavior; accuracy is a \"vanity metric\" on imbalanced data (e.g., rare crashes). Net objective (turnover, transaction costs, latency, tail-risk) often matters more than raw predictive score.", "traps": "- **Inverted-logic questions** (11 of 38 here) flip the meaning: the Answer Key notes \"**True = incorrect; False = accurate**.\" So \"False\" means the statement is *correct*. Always read the Answer Key polarity line first. Inverted here: G1Q2, G2Q2, G2Q4, G2Q5, G2Q6, G2Q7, G3Q1, G3Q4, G4Q2, G5Q2, G6Q3.\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices**: even in *normal-polarity* questions, several choices start \"It is incorrect to conclude that [an accurate statement].\" The Answer Key marks these **False** (\"the embedded claim is accurate, so saying it is incorrect is false\"). Parse the inner claim, judge its truth, then negate. Appears in G3Q5A, G3Q6A, G4Q5A, G4Q6A, G5Q4A, G5Q5A, G5Q6A, G6Q2B, G6Q4A/B, G6Q5A, G6Q6A.\n- **\"Presence of labels defines supervised, not the output or features.\"** Same feature rows can serve supervised or unsupervised tasks; identical inputs do NOT force the same paradigm. A buy/sell *action* can come from a rule without any labels.\n- **Post-hoc cluster naming ≠ supervision.** Naming clusters \"bull/bear/sideways\" or scoring them by average forward return *after* the fact does not provide labels *during* clustering — the clustering stays unsupervised. (But if you label *before* training so the model learns from labels, it becomes supervised.)\n- **Numeric output ≠ regression by itself.** A class *probability* is still a classification output; a probability/rate target (0–1) is still regression. It's the target's nature that decides.\n- **Thresholding/binning loses information** about within-category magnitude — and classification metrics (error rate) treat all misclassifications as equal regardless of distance from the boundary, unlike MSE.\n- **Training speed says nothing about inference speed** (and vice versa). \"Zero training time\" (KNN) can mean *slow, N-dependent* inference. Aggregate/total compute can hide per-prediction latency misses.\n- **In-sample fit does not prove generalization.** More runs on the development sample, or matching more historical detail, is not fresh evidence; an untouched, representative (ideally later/regime-matched) evaluation period is. More data helps *variance/overfitting*, not *bias*.", "references": "1. **ISLR — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning* (James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani):** §2.1 (supervised vs. unsupervised, regression vs. classification, inference vs. prediction), §2.2 (assessing model accuracy, bias-variance tradeoff, irreducible error, U-shaped test error), §2.1.3 (flexibility–interpretability), ch.4 (classification, Bayes classifier, P(Y|X)), §2.2.3 (KNN), ch.8 (trees), ch.12 (unsupervised learning), ch.5 (cross-validation).\n2. **ESL — *The Elements of Statistical Learning* (Hastie, Tibshirani, Friedman):** ch.7 (model assessment & selection, bias-variance decomposition, generalization gap), §7.9 (VC dimension).\n3. **scikit-learn User Guide:** \"Choosing the right estimator\" (map task→paradigm), \"Clustering,\" \"Nearest Neighbors\" (query cost), \"Metrics and scoring,\" \"Probability calibration\" (Brier score), \"Computational performance\" (train vs. predict latency).\n4. **Vapnik — Statistical Learning Theory:** empirical vs. structural risk minimization, capacity/VC dimension, regularization — for the generalization/memorization questions.\n5. **Grinold & Kahn — *Active Portfolio Management*:** connecting predictive signal to net-of-cost portfolio outcomes (turnover, transaction costs, implementation shortfall) for the \"good model\" questions.", "bookNotes": [{"book": "DeepLearning", "title": "Ch. 1 — The Deep Learning Revolution (Overview)", "file": "references/DeepLearning/Ch01_Overview.md", "md": "# Ch. 1 — The Deep Learning Revolution (Overview)\n\n**Source:** Bishop & Bishop, *Deep Learning: Foundations and Concepts* (Springer, 2024).\n**Book pages 1–21** (PDF pages 21–41). Section 1.1 pp. 2–5; §1.2 Tutorial Example pp. 6–15; §1.3 Brief History pp. 16–21.\n\n> **→ ML4T Exam-1 relevance: ML Domain 1 (Learning Paradigms), ML Domain 9 (Generalization / Overfitting), plus the core supervised-vs-unsupervised framing tested throughout.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. What deep learning is (§1.1)\n- **Deep learning** = machine learning based on multilayer **neural networks** trained on large data sets; a single general framework replaces many hand-crafted, application-specific algorithms.\n- **Learning / training** = the process of setting a model's adjustable **parameters (weights)** from data. Modern nets can have millions to ~1 trillion (10^12) parameters.\n- Application examples span medical diagnosis, protein structure (AlphaFold), image synthesis, and large language models (LLMs) — all variants of the *same* framework.\n\n### Learning paradigms (from the four examples) — **exam-critical**\n- **Supervised learning:** each training example has a known target/label.\n  - **Classification:** output assigned to a discrete set of classes (e.g., benign vs. malignant).\n  - **Regression:** output is one or more *continuous* variables (e.g., predict chemical yield from temp/pressure).\n- **Unsupervised learning:** data is *unlabelled* (e.g., generating new synthetic face images).\n- **Generative AI / autoregressive models:** LLMs take a word sequence and predict the next word; the augmented sequence is fed back to generate more (used for text generation).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The polynomial curve-fitting tutorial (§1.2) — the backbone example\n\nSetup: input `x`, target `t`; training set of N observations; goal is **generalization** = accurate prediction on previously unseen inputs. Data = underlying regularity (here sin(2πx)) + random (Gaussian) noise.\n\n**Linear model (polynomial):**\n$$y(x,\\mathbf{w}) = w_0 + w_1 x + \\dots + w_M x^M = \\sum_{j=0}^{M} w_j x^j \\tag{1.1}$$\n- Nonlinear in `x` but **linear in the coefficients w** → called a **linear model**.\n\n**Sum-of-squares error function:**\n$$E(\\mathbf{w}) = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\{y(x_n,\\mathbf{w}) - t_n\\}^2 \\tag{1.2}$$\n- Non-negative; zero iff the curve passes through every point. Quadratic in w → **unique closed-form minimum** w*.\n\n**Root-mean-square (RMS) error** (lets you compare across data-set sizes, same units as t):\n$$E_{RMS} = \\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{N}\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\{y(x_n,\\mathbf{w}^\\*) - t_n\\}^2} \\tag{1.3}$$\n\n### Model complexity & overfitting — **exam-critical**\n- Order **M is a hyperparameter** controlling model complexity.\n- Low M (0,1): underfits (too inflexible). M=3: good fit. **M=9: fits training data exactly (E=0) but oscillates wildly → over-fitting**, large test error.\n- Overfitting symptom: coefficient magnitudes grow very large as M increases (finely tuned to noise).\n- **More data reduces overfitting:** a given complex model over-fits less as N grows. Classical heuristic: N ≥ 5–10× the number of parameters (though deep learning routinely violates this).\n\n### Regularization (§1.2.5) — **exam-critical**\nAdd a penalty on coefficient size to the error:\n$$\\tilde{E}(\\mathbf{w}) = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\{y(x_n,\\mathbf{w}) - t_n\\}^2 + \\tfrac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\mathbf{w}\\|^2 \\tag{1.4}$$\nwhere $\\|\\mathbf{w}\\|^2 = w_0^2 + \\dots + w_M^2$; λ controls regularization strength.\n- Known as **shrinkage** (statistics) or **weight decay** (neural nets).\n- λ controls *effective* model complexity: too small → overfit; too large → underfit. (w0 often excluded from the penalty.)\n\n### Model selection (§1.2.6) — **exam-critical**\n- Hyperparameters (λ, M) **cannot** be chosen by minimizing training error (would drive λ→0, M large → overfit).\n- Partition data: **training set** (fit w) + **validation / hold-out / development set** (choose model) + optionally a **test set** for final unbiased evaluation.\n- **S-fold cross-validation:** split into S groups; train on S−1, validate on the held-out group; rotate; average scores. Uses (S−1)/S of data for training. **S = N → leave-one-out.**\n- Drawback: cost multiplies by S; combinatorial explosion with many hyperparameters. Modern large models rely more on heuristics/experience than exhaustive search.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Brief history of ML / neural networks (§1.3)\n\n**Single artificial neuron:**\n$$a = \\sum_{i=1}^{M} w_i x_i \\tag{1.5}, \\qquad y = f(a) \\tag{1.6}$$\n- `a` = pre-activation; `f(·)` = activation function; `y` = activation; `w_i` = weights (synapse strengths). Polynomial (1.1) is a special case (f = identity, inputs = powers of x).\n\n**Three phases:**\n1. **Single-layer networks / perceptron** (Rosenblatt): step activation (fires if weighted input > threshold). Perceptron algorithm provably converges if data is linearly separable. Minsky & Papert (1969) proved single-layer limitations (and wrongly conjectured they'd extend to multilayer) → \"AI winter.\" A modern net is still sometimes called a **multilayer perceptron (MLP)**.\n2. **Backpropagation era (mid-1980s):** replace step function with **differentiable** activations + differentiable error functions → gradients computable. **Hidden units** = middle-layer nodes (values not in training data). **Feed-forward networks**; trained by **error backpropagation** (Rumelhart, Hinton, Williams 1986) + **stochastic gradient descent**. Recognized central role of probability/statistics and **inductive bias / prior knowledge**. Limitation: only last ~2 layers learned useful values; heavy reliance on hand-crafted **feature extraction**.\n3. **Deep networks (2010s–now):** many-layer nets trainable effectively → **deep learning** (LeCun, Bengio, Hinton 2015). Driven by scale (parameters up to ~10^12), massive data, and **GPUs** (parallelism). Compute to train SOTA models: doubling ~2 yrs pre-2012 (Moore's law), then ~3.4 months after 2012.\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam anchors\n- Supervised = labelled; classification (discrete) vs. regression (continuous). Unsupervised = unlabelled.\n- Generalization is the goal; overfitting = fitting noise; controlled by complexity, data size, and regularization.\n- Validation set / cross-validation choose hyperparameters; test set gives final estimate.\n- Regularization = weight decay = shrinkage; λ penalizes large weights.\n"}, {"book": "DeepLearning", "title": "Ch. 2.1 — The Rules of Probability", "file": "references/DeepLearning/Ch02-1_Probability.md", "md": "# Ch. 2.1 — The Rules of Probability\n\n**Source:** Bishop & Bishop, *Deep Learning: Foundations and Concepts* (Springer, 2024).\n**Book pages 23–31** (PDF pages 43–51). Ch. 2 intro pp. 23–24; §2.1 The Rules of Probability pp. 25–31.\n\n> **→ ML4T Exam-1 relevance: ML Domain 1/2 (probabilistic foundations), Bayes' theorem, conditional probability, and the base-rate reasoning behind classifier evaluation.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Two kinds of uncertainty (Ch. 2 intro)\n- **Epistemic (systematic) uncertainty:** from having only a *finite* data set. Reduced by observing more data.\n- **Aleatoric (intrinsic / stochastic) uncertainty = noise:** persists even with infinite data; arises from observing only partial information. Reduced by gathering *different kinds* of data (e.g., biopsy vs. image alone).\n- Both handled by **probability theory**, governed by two rules: the **sum rule** and the **product rule**.\n\n## 2. Two views of probability\n- **Frequentist:** probability = frequency of a repeatable event in the limit of infinite trials (e.g., a bent coin landing concave-up 60% of the time).\n- **Bayesian:** probability = quantification of uncertainty; more general — includes frequentist as a special case (e.g., which unseen side is \"heads\" is unknown, not repeatable).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Sum and product rules (§2.1.2) — **exam-critical**\n\nFor random variables X (values x_i) and Y (values y_j), from counting cells n_ij out of N trials:\n- **Joint probability:** $p(X=x_i, Y=y_j) = n_{ij}/N$\n- **Marginal probability:** $p(X=x_i) = c_i/N$; probabilities sum to 1.\n- **Conditional probability:** $p(Y=y_j \\mid X=x_i) = n_{ij}/c_i$\n\n**Compact form of the two fundamental rules:**\n$$\\textbf{Sum rule:}\\quad p(X) = \\sum_{Y} p(X, Y) \\tag{2.8}$$\n$$\\textbf{Product rule:}\\quad p(X, Y) = p(Y \\mid X)\\, p(X) \\tag{2.9}$$\n- **Marginalization** = summing out the other variable(s) to get p(X).\n\n## 4. Bayes' theorem (§2.1.3) — **exam-critical**\nFrom the product rule + symmetry p(X,Y) = p(Y,X):\n$$p(Y \\mid X) = \\frac{p(X \\mid Y)\\, p(Y)}{p(X)} \\tag{2.10}, \\qquad p(X) = \\sum_Y p(X \\mid Y)\\, p(Y) \\tag{2.11}$$\n- The denominator p(X) is the **normalization constant** ensuring the posterior sums to 1.\n- Relates a conditional to its \"reversed\" conditional.\n\n## 5. Prior vs. posterior (§2.1.5) — **exam-critical**\n- **Prior probability p(C):** belief *before* observing evidence.\n- **Posterior probability p(C | T):** belief *after* observing evidence T, computed via Bayes' theorem.\n\n## 6. Independence (§2.1.6)\n- X and Y independent ⟺ joint factorizes: $p(X, Y) = p(X)\\,p(Y)$, equivalently $p(Y \\mid X) = p(Y)$.\n\n---\n\n## Worked example — medical screening (base rates) — **exam-critical**\nGiven: prevalence p(C=1)=0.01; test sensitivity p(T=1|C=1)=0.90; false-positive p(T=1|C=0)=0.03.\n- **Overall positive rate (sum + product rules):**\n  $p(T{=}1) = 0.03 \\times 0.99 + 0.90 \\times 0.01 = 0.0387$ (~4%).\n- **Posterior via Bayes:**\n  $p(C{=}1 \\mid T{=}1) = \\dfrac{0.90 \\times 0.01}{0.0387} = \\dfrac{90}{387} \\approx 0.23$ (23%).\n- **Lesson:** even an \"accurate\" test yields only 23% posterior because the **prior (base rate) is low** — strong evidence must be combined with the prior. This is the base-rate fallacy that underlies precision/recall intuition.\n"}, {"book": "Handbook_AI_BigData", "title": "Ch. 1 — On Machine Learning Applications in Investments", "file": "references/Handbook_AI_BigData/Ch01_ML_Applications_in_Investments.md", "md": "# Ch. 1 — On Machine Learning Applications in Investments\n\n**Source:** Chen, M. (Robeco) & Zhou, W. — *Handbook of AI and Big Data Applications in Investments*, CFA Institute Research Foundation (2023).\n**Book pages:** 2–19 (PDF pages 23–40).\n**ML4T exam relevance:** ML Domain 1 (supervised learning, regression/classification, ensembles, decision trees, overfitting), ML Domain 2 (evaluation, bias/variance, data splitting), QF Domain 9–10 (return prediction, Sharpe ratio, factor models, backtesting). Faithful to source; nothing invented.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why apply ML to investing (motivations)\n\n- **Primary attraction:** promise of higher **risk-adjusted return**. ML is built for **prediction on high-dimensional data with no functional-form specification**, so it should be good at predicting future equity returns.\n- Practitioners have used ML to predict: **cross-sectional equity returns**, **corporate earnings** (more accurate than traditional models), and **crash / severe price-drop probability** (superior to linear models using leverage ratio or distance-to-default).\n- **Key driver of outperformance vs. linear models:** ML does **not prespecify the functional relationship** between predictors (financial-statement metrics, past returns) and target (return, earnings). It can capture **interaction effects** and **nonlinear effects**.\n  - **Interaction effect:** output is not a linear combination of inputs because one input's effect depends on another's value (e.g., reported earnings matter only when an accounting \"red flag\" = 0; if red flag = 1, price drops 50% regardless of earnings).\n  - **Nonlinear effect:** e.g., relationship between a firm's CDS spread and its equity return (equity as embedded call option on firm assets). Neural networks introduce nonlinearity via **activation functions** (without them a deep net collapses to a linear model).\n  - Empirically (Gu, Kelly, Xiu 2020): **interaction effects drive most of the outperformance**; the jury is still out on nonlinear-only contribution (a generalized linear model with splines but no interactions barely beat purely linear).\n- **\"Data mining\" / \"knowledge discovery\":** ML can find relationships not prespecified by researchers. Attractive with the growing \"data deluge,\" but must guard against **spurious, nonreplicable correlations**.\n\n## 2. Why finance is HARD for ML (unique challenges)\n\n- **Low signal-to-noise ratio:** any single metric weakly determines a security's performance; returns compress company-specific, macro, behavioral, and positioning drivers into one dimension (up/down). Contrast with high-signal domains (e.g., video recommendations).\n- **Small data vs. big data:** financial data is small (US = ~100 yrs, at most ~1,200 monthly points per security) vs. billions/trillions in other domains. Small data limits true cross-validation, out-of-sample testing, hyperparameter tuning, robustness checks. **A simpler model on more data often beats a sophisticated model on little data.** (Alternative data is more abundant but overall finance data is still small.)\n- **Nonstationarity / adaptive markets / irrationality:** markets are **man-made and adaptive** — they \"learn\" as participants learn, and published research (e.g., Fama–French 1993) changes behavior (McLean & Pontiff 2016). The **data-generating process changes over time**; humans act irrationally (greed/fear). Contrast static domains (e.g., protein folding).\n\n## 3. Common pitfalls and how to avoid them (exam-critical)\n\n- **Overfitting:** biggest concern given short history + high degrees of freedom in ML. Symptom = great backtest, poor out-of-sample.\n  - Mitigations (cross-domain): **cross-validation, feature selection/removal, regularization, early stopping, ensembling, holdout data.**\n  - Finance advantage: **human intuition + economic domain knowledge** (\"smell tests\"). Use **SHAP values** (Shapley additive explanation — average marginal contribution of a feature) to check whether learned relationships make economic sense.\n  - **Feature engineering:** curate inputs using domain knowledge up front (e.g., profit margin, debt coverage are sensible crash predictors; CEO's last-name initial is not).\n- **Replicability:** even harder for ML than traditional stats — more tunable variables, and open-source algorithms vary by version/random seed/implementation. Mitigate with **robust data + code infrastructure**: version control, fixed parameters (algorithm version, universe, hyperparameters, feature sets), and documentation of all iterations including failures (\"research graveyards\").\n- **Lookahead bias / data leakage:**\n  - **Lookahead bias:** using data before it was actually available (use **point-in-time** data — e.g., quarterly results available 40 days after quarter-end must not be used at quarter-end).\n  - **Leakage in data:** information that belongs in one split (train/validation/test) leaks into another → overly optimistic results. Mitigate with a **gap period** (months–quarters) between train/validation/test splits.\n  - **Leakage in features:** an informative feature that would be unavailable at production time (e.g., undisclosed executive health issue).\n- **Implementation gap:** backtest trades that are impossible/infeasible live — e.g., alpha coming from small/micro-cap stocks (too costly, insufficient shares), or shorting stocks that can't be borrowed / cost too much. ML worsens this via **high turnover**.\n- **Explainability / performance attribution:** models are complex \"black boxes\" (though inspectable). Asset owners demand explanations. Technique = **partial dependence response**: fix a feature at value x, average predictions, sweep x over its range → decompose output into **linear, interaction, nonlinear** components; then form portfolios from each to attribute returns.\n\n## 4. Sample applications (examples + results)\n\n- **Predicting cross-sectional stock returns** (relative, not absolute; stock selection, not timing/allocation — an easier problem with more data).\n  - Setup: (1) investment universe, (2) ML algorithms (boosted trees/random forests, neural nets, ensembles), (3) feature set (value price-ratios, quality/profitability, momentum/past returns). Limiting the feature set = imposing domain-knowledge structure to fight small data.\n  - **Five consistent results:**\n    1. ML significantly and economically outperforms linear approaches. Gu et al. (2020): 4-layer NN long–short decile spread → out-of-sample **Sharpe 1.35 (value-weighted), 2.45 (equal-weighted)** vs. OLS **0.61 / 0.83**.\n    2. Outperformance comes from **higher-order effects (nonlinearity + interactions)**; interactions matter more than nonlinearity.\n    3. The **most important features are consistent across ML algorithms**: short-term reversal, stock & sector momentum, return volatility, firm size.\n    4. **Simple ML models beat complex ones** (less overfitting on small data).\n    5. **More data → better ML performance** (\"unreasonable effectiveness of data\").\n- **Predicting stock crashes:** crash = large idiosyncratic price drop vs. peers. Algorithms: logistic regression, random forest, gradient-boosted tree, ensemble. Features: distance-to-default, volatility, market beta + fundamentals. Used in **conservative/low-vol equity** (\"win by not losing\"). ML beats traditional in developed and emerging markets; returns come from stock selection, not sector allocation.\n- **Predicting fundamentals (earnings):** easier + more stable target than returns. Cao & You (2021), US ex-financials/utilities, 1975–2019, 28 line items + first differences (56 features). Linear ML beats traditional (~6%); nonlinear ML beats linear ML (~1–3%); **ensembles beat individuals** (\"ensembling is the closest thing to a free lunch, like diversification\").\n- **NLP in multiple languages:** embedding-based NLP to decode Chinese A-share retail-investor slang; word embeddings preserve vector arithmetic (King − Man + Woman ≈ Queen). Shows ML helps non-numerical tasks too.\n\n## 5. Conclusion takeaways\n\n- ML beats traditional methods **if applied to the right problem**; source of edge = nonlinear + interaction effects; **ensembling** adds value.\n- Finance is distinctive: less data, nonstationary, irrational participants — so know the technique's limits.\n- Other ML uses: sentiment analysis, foreign-language understanding, data cleaning, fraud detection, credit scoring, trading optimization.\n"}, {"book": "Handbook_AI_BigData", "title": "Ch. 2 — Alternative Data and AI in Investment Research", "file": "references/Handbook_AI_BigData/Ch02_Alternative_Data_and_AI.md", "md": "# Ch. 2 — Alternative Data and AI in Investment Research\n\n**Source:** Tierens, I. & Duggan, D. (Goldman Sachs Global Investment Research) — *Handbook of AI and Big Data Applications in Investments*, CFA Institute Research Foundation (2023).\n**Book pages:** 20–26 (PDF pages 41–47).\n**ML4T exam relevance:** ML Domain 1–2 (data quality, features, bias, sampling/selection bias, false positives/negatives, NLP/sentiment), QF Domain 9–10 (alpha, alternative data, backtesting caveats, systematic vs. fundamental strategies, descriptive vs. prescriptive analytics). Faithful to source; nothing invented.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Definitions and framing\n\n- **AI (broad):** using computers to mimic human problem solving.\n- **Alternative data (broad):** any data in a **nonstructured format**. The \"alternative\" label may be a **misnomer** — often it just delivers legacy information more scalably/timelier in digital form.\n- **Core principle:** alternative data and AI (incl. ML and NLP) are **tools in the research toolkit, not ends in themselves.** They **do not define the investment thesis — they help prove or disprove it.** Value only when aligned with a particular strategy.\n- Impact is **evolutionary, not revolutionary**; lines between \"traditional\" and \"alternative\" data (and what counts as \"AI\") are blurring.\n\n## 2. Where AI/alt-data is additive: systematic vs. fundamental strategies\n\n- **Systematic (breadth > depth):** need datasets/techniques applicable across **many securities**. Favor text-extracted factors (NLP repeatable across filings/news), econometric conversion of single alphas into multifactor alphas. Horizon matters — intraday news sentiment helps HFT but adds little for a weeks/months horizon.\n- **Fundamental (depth > breadth):** analyst goes **deep**, prizes **precision**; **niche datasets** complete the \"mosaic\" (e.g., social-media consumer sentiment, brick-and-mortar foot traffic, consumer search trends). Non-obvious wins: app downloads in medical devices, localized market-share for managed care, quarry production.\n\n## 3. Data sourcing in a world of oversupply\n\n- Overabundance of providers; **low barriers to entry.** With alt-data, **due-diligence burden shifts from producer to consumer** (parallel to news fact-checking).\n- Costs (onboarding, ingestion, testing, licensing) can outweigh benefits → GIR's acquisition is **demand-driven** (\"pull,\" working backward from a use case), not supply-pushed.\n- New data need not be **orthogonal** — value can come from delivering legacy data more scalably/timelier.\n- GIR keeps analysts (subject-matter experts) as **co-owners** of identifying data rather than a separate \"data scout\"; a central **Data Strategy team** helps with analysis (GS Data Works).\n\n### Three alternative-data categories (Exhibit 1)\n1. **Geospatial** — mobile foot traffic, satellite imagery, demographics, store/EV-charger locations.\n2. **Digital/online** — app downloads, online point-of-sale, website visits, product counts/pricing, search trends.\n3. **Text (NLP-addressable)** — earnings-call transcripts, filings, tweets, blogs (e.g., NLP-driven quarterly S&P 500 \"Beige Book\").\n- Categories **overlap**; combining dimensions builds a fuller mosaic (e.g., pricing power from product codes/prices + local market share + earnings-call commentary).\n\n## 4. The most time-consuming part: data \"grunt work\" (curation)\n\n- **Do not expect AI insight without understanding the data.** Data scientists succeed only by knowing data nuances, strengths, weaknesses.\n- **Algorithmic bias** stems from **biased training data**; investment apps aren't immune. Subject-matter experts (even without data-science skills) add real value here.\n- Run a **small test case** before sourcing; data can **evolve/degrade** over time (inputs removed/added/edited); derived data needs algorithm transparency.\n- **Data-quality questions by type:**\n  - **Large/complex data:** how comprehensive? **Missing values** bias averages/sums (e.g., web-scraped pricing). Understand any embedded analysis layer (e.g., auto-fill of missing data). Does the data allow the breakouts your thesis needs (e.g., style-specific SKU counts)?\n  - **Small data:** may rely on **sampling** → **undersampling / selection bias.** Examples: opt-in point-of-sale panels skew demographics beyond reweighting; satellite parking counts miss covered parking; mobile foot-traffic imprecise in malls/multitenant buildings. **Construction process determines advantages and limitations.**\n  - **NLP/text:** input details matter — paywalled content invisible to aggregators; source-specific biases (Instagram vs. Twitter vs. Reddit); guard against **false positives / false negatives** in sentiment (e.g., distinguish a bad product from out-of-stock frustration).\n- **Bottom line:** curation determines success/failure; the AI effort fails (or produces wrong outputs) if data gets less attention than the analysis. GIR keeps curation in-house (with SMEs + data scientists).\n\n## 5. The analysis spectrum\n\n- Also a **\"pull\" approach:** work backward from the use case; escalate to sophisticated methods only when simpler ones fail (outcomes swing on small assumption changes, outliers dominate).\n- **Weigh explainability + effort against AI's promise.** Prefer simplicity when it works — e.g., simply **counting mentions of \"inflation\"** in earnings calls was a robust proxy for a ~200-term screen. When complex, data scientists must supply enough **intuition** for analysts' comfort.\n- **Limits:** financial dynamics are **unstable/adaptive**; limited in-sample/out-of-sample data caps how far techniques can be pushed. **Breakpoints** (e.g., COVID-19) break statistical relationships (GIR \"nowcasting\" indicators had to be re-adapted; extreme outliers broke them) — **humans are critical** for curating data and continuously assessing algorithm results (\"man + machine\").\n- GIR's NLP focus = **surfacing content scalably** more than interpreting meaning/sentiment (accuracy-critical NLP flagged too many false positives/negatives).\n\n## 6. Measuring success — use a WIDE lens (three questions)\n\n1. **Does it create alpha?** Unlikely from a single dataset/technique. For **systematic** strategies you can **backtest with vs. without** the AI component to measure marginal alpha (usual backtesting caveats apply).\n2. **Does it produce unique insights more timely/precise?** For **fundamental** strategies direct alpha is hard to attribute; quantify improvement on specific dimensions. Most GIR AI/alt-data work is **descriptive** (feeds the thesis) rather than **prescriptive** (directly recommends a trade).\n3. **Does it create scalability** that saves analyst time or extends a hypothesis across more securities / more often? (e.g., NLP across an entire coverage universe + peers + multiple years.)\n- ROI hurdles for AI often unfairly stricter than for traditional data — questioning them is **healthy** and levels the playing field.\n\n## 7. Getting started — \"a marathon, not a sprint\"\n\n- Hire 1–2 hands-on data scientists genuinely interested in investment problems; pair with 1–2 analysts with a durable use case; start with **simple, non-complicated datasets.**\n- **Senior sponsorship** matters (cover to experiment + accountability). Involve **engineering, legal, compliance from Day 1** (storage, firewalls, terms/licensing). Consider **build vs. buy** as use cases mature.\n- **Centralized vs. embedded:** GIR chose **centralized** (extension of research teams globally/across asset classes) to connect dots across use cases.\n- Goal: **minimize algorithmic and data biases** via integrated, iterative, collaborative \"man + machine\" — until \"big\" and \"alternative\" labels may no longer be relevant.\n"}, {"book": "ISLP", "title": "ISLP Ch. 1 — Introduction (An Overview of Statistical Learning)", "file": "references/ISLP/Ch01_Introduction.md", "md": "# ISLP Ch. 1 — Introduction (An Overview of Statistical Learning)\n\n*Source: ISLP (James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani, Taylor), PDF pages 12–25 (book pp. 1–14).*\n\n> ML4T exam relevance: framing/vocabulary that underpins every domain — supervised vs.\n> unsupervised, regression vs. classification, and notation. → ML Domain 1 (ML fundamentals).\n\n## What statistical learning is\n- **Statistical learning** = a vast toolset for *understanding data*. Two broad families:\n  - **Supervised learning**: build a model to predict/estimate an **output** from one or more\n    **inputs**. (Business, medicine, astrophysics, public policy, finance.)\n  - **Unsupervised learning**: inputs but **no supervising output**; still learn structure/\n    relationships (e.g., grouping similar observations).\n\n## Three motivating datasets (map to problem types)\n- **Wage data** — predict a continuous/quantitative output (wage) from age, education, year →\n  a **regression** problem. Relationship of wage to age is **non-linear** (rises then falls ~age 60).\n- **Smarket (S&P 500) data** — predict a categorical output (market **Up** vs **Down**) from prior\n  days' % changes → a **classification** problem. Successive daily returns show little\n  association (efficient-market intuition); QDA reached ~60% directional accuracy on 2005.\n- **NCI60 gene expression data** — inputs only, no output → a **clustering / unsupervised**\n  problem; PCA reduces 6,830 measurements per cell line to 2 dimensions (Z1, Z2) for visualization.\n\n## Key distinctions to memorize\n- **Regression vs. classification**: quantitative/continuous output → regression; qualitative/\n  categorical output → classification.\n- **Supervised vs. unsupervised**: presence vs. absence of a response variable Y.\n\n## Brief history (context)\n- Least squares / **linear regression** — early 19th century (astronomy).\n- **Linear discriminant analysis** (1936); **logistic regression** (1940s); **generalized linear\n  models** (early 1970s).\n- Non-linear methods became practical in the 1980s: **classification & regression trees**,\n  **generalized additive models**, **neural networks** (1980s), **support vector machines** (1990s).\n\n## Notation (used throughout the book)\n- **n** = number of observations (data points). **p** = number of variables/predictors.\n  (Wage: n = 3,000, p = 11.)\n- **x_ij** = value of the *j*-th variable for the *i*-th observation; **X** is the n×p matrix.\n- **x_i** = row vector (length p) of one observation; **x_j** (bold) = column vector (length n)\n  of one variable. Vectors default to columns; `T` = transpose.\n- **y_i** = *i*-th response; observed data = {(x_1,y_1), …, (x_n,y_n)}.\n- Convention: vectors of length n in **lowercase bold**; matrices in **bold capitals**; random\n  variables in **capital normal font**; scalars in lowercase normal.\n\n## Book organization (what lives where — useful for exam topic mapping)\n- Ch 2: terminology + concepts, KNN classifier. Ch 3: linear regression. Ch 4: classification\n  (logistic regression, LDA). Ch 5: cross-validation & bootstrap. Ch 6: linear model selection/\n  regularization (ridge, lasso, PCR). Ch 7: non-linear (splines, GAMs). Ch 8: tree-based\n  (bagging, boosting, random forests). Ch 9: SVMs. Ch 10: deep learning. Ch 11: survival.\n  Ch 12: unsupervised (PCA, K-means, hierarchical). Ch 13: multiple testing.\n"}, {"book": "ProbabilisticML", "title": "Ch. 1 — Introduction (Murphy, *Probabilistic Machine Learning: An Introduction*, Book 1)", "file": "references/ProbabilisticML/Ch01_Introduction.md", "md": "# Ch. 1 — Introduction (Murphy, *Probabilistic Machine Learning: An Introduction*, Book 1)\n\n**Source:** Kevin P. Murphy, *Probabilistic Machine Learning: An Introduction* (MIT Press). Online version, April 18, 2025.\n**Chapter 1 span:** book pp. 1–29 = **PDF pp. 31–59** (Ch. 2 begins PDF p. 62).\n**Week-1 recommended reading for GT ML4T Exam 1.**\n\n> Study notes distilled faithfully from the text. Section numbers (e.g., §1.2.3) are the book's own.\n\n---\n\n## 1.1 What is machine learning? (PDF p. 31)\n\n- **Mitchell's definition:** \"A computer program learns from experience *E* w.r.t. tasks *T* and performance measure *P*, if its performance at *T*, measured by *P*, improves with *E*.\" The three levers of any ML setup are **T** (task), **P** (performance measure), **E** (training signal/experience).\n- This book takes a **probabilistic perspective**: treat all unknown quantities (predictions, model parameters) as **random variables** endowed with **probability distributions**.\n- Two reasons for the probabilistic approach: (1) it is the **optimal approach to decision-making under uncertainty**; (2) it is the shared language of science/engineering, giving a unifying framework.\n\n---\n\n## 1.2 Supervised learning (PDF p. 31)\n\n**Goal:** learn a mapping *f* from inputs **x ∈ X** to outputs **y ∈ Y**.\n\nKey terminology (memorize — exam loves synonyms):\n- **Inputs x** = features = covariates = predictors. Often a fixed-dim vector, X = R^D (D = number of features / dimensionality). In statsmodels: *exogenous* variables.\n- **Output y** = label = target = response. In statsmodels: *endogenous* variable.\n- **Experience E** = training set **D = {(xₙ, yₙ)}**, n = 1..N. **N = sample size.**\n- **Design matrix:** N × D matrix; each **row = an example**, each **column = a feature**.\n  - Tall & skinny: N ≫ D. Short & fat: D ≫ N (e.g., genomics). \"Big data\" = large N; \"wide data\" = large D.\n- **Tabular data** vs. variable-size inputs (sequences, graphs) → converted to fixed-size vectors via **featurization**.\n\n### 1.2.1 Classification (PDF p. 32)\n- Output space = **C unordered, mutually exclusive labels (classes)**, Y = {1,…,C}. Also called **pattern recognition**.\n- **Binary classification** = 2 classes (y ∈ {0,1} or {−1,+1}).\n- **Exploratory data analysis (EDA):** inspect data first (patterns, label noise, outliers). Tools: **pair plot** (scatter of feature pairs + marginal on diagonal), color-coded by class; for high-D data, do **dimensionality reduction** first.\n- **Decision rule / classifier** partitions input space via a **decision boundary**; nested rules form a **decision tree** inducing a **decision surface**. Tree stores, per node, feature index + threshold; all these are **parameters θ**.\n\n**Empirical Risk Minimization (ERM) (PDF p. 36):**\n- **Misclassification rate** on training set: L(θ) = (1/N) Σ I(yₙ ≠ f(xₙ;θ)), where **I** = indicator function (1 if true, else 0).\n- **Loss function ℓ(y, ŷ)** generalizes this; errors can be asymmetric (costlier one way — poisonous-flower loss matrix example).\n- **Empirical risk** = average loss on training set: L(θ) = (1/N) Σ ℓ(yₙ, f(xₙ;θ)).\n- **Zero-one loss** ℓ₀₁ = I(y ≠ ŷ) → makes empirical risk equal the misclassification rate.\n- **Model fitting / training** = **ERM**: θ̂ = argmin_θ L(θ). **But the true goal is to minimize expected loss on FUTURE data (generalize), not just training loss** (see §1.2.3).\n\n**Uncertainty (PDF p. 37):**\n- **Epistemic (model) uncertainty:** lack of knowledge of the mapping — *reducible* with more data.\n- **Aleatoric (data) uncertainty:** intrinsic/irreducible stochasticity.\n- Model the conditional distribution p(y=c|x;θ) = f_c(x;θ), with 0 ≤ f_c ≤ 1 and Σ f_c = 1.\n- **Softmax function** converts unnormalized **logits** a = f(x;θ) into a valid probability distribution: softmax(a)_c = e^{a_c} / Σ e^{a_c'}.\n\n**Logistic regression & MLE (PDF p. 38):**\n- Affine model f(x;θ) = b + wᵀx → passed through softmax = **logistic regression**.\n- Terminology: **w** = weights (stats: regression coefficients β); **b** = bias (stats: intercept). Bias here ≠ statistical bias.\n- Absorbing bias into weights turns an **affine** function into a **linear** one: f(x;w) = wᵀx.\n- **Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE):** use **negative log-likelihood (NLL)** as loss: ℓ = −log p(y|f(x;θ)); θ̂_mle = argmin NLL(θ). A good model assigns high probability to the true output.\n\n### 1.2.2 Regression (PDF p. 38)\n- Predict a **real-valued** output y ∈ R (vs. discrete class).\n- **Quadratic / ℓ₂ loss:** ℓ₂(y,ŷ) = (y − ŷ)². Penalizes large **residuals** (y − ŷ). Empirical risk = **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**.\n- Model output uncertainty with a **Gaussian/normal**: p(y|x;θ) = N(y | f(x;θ), σ²). With fixed σ², **NLL ∝ MSE** → MLE = minimizing squared error.\n- **Linear regression:** f(x;θ) = b + wx (w = slope, b = offset/intercept); **least squares** = argmin MSE. **Multiple linear regression** = many input features.\n- **Polynomial regression:** f(x;w) = wᵀφ(x) with φ(x) = [1, x, x²,…,x^D] — an example of **feature preprocessing / feature engineering**. Still **linear in the parameters w** (nonlinear in x) → MSE has a unique global optimum.\n- **Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) (PDF p. 42):** learn the feature extractor φ(x;V) automatically; stack of L nested functions f(x;θ) = f_L(f_{L−1}(…f₁(x)…)). Variants: **CNNs** (images), **RNNs** (sequences).\n\n### 1.2.3 Overfitting and generalization (PDF p. 42) ★ high-yield\n- A flexible model can drive **training loss to 0** by memorizing → **overfitting** (fits training data but too complex).\n- **Population risk** L(θ; p*) = expected loss under the true data distribution p*(x,y) — what we actually care about.\n- **Generalization gap** = L(θ; p*) − L(θ; D_train). Large gap (low train risk, high population risk) ⇒ overfitting.\n- p* is unknown → split data into **training set** and **test set**; approximate population risk with **test risk** L(θ; D_test).\n- **U-shaped test-error curve** vs. model complexity: too simple = **underfitting** (left); too complex = **overfitting** (right); middle = \"just right.\" Training error monotonically decreases with complexity.\n- **Model selection:** never pick by training loss (always chooses the most complex / most **degrees of freedom**). Use **three sets: training (fit), validation (model selection), test (estimate future/population risk only — never used for fitting or selection).**\n\n### 1.2.4 No Free Lunch (NFL) theorem (PDF p. 43)\n- \"All models are wrong, but some are useful.\" **No single model is best for all problems.**\n- Reason: a set of assumptions = **inductive bias** that helps in one domain may hurt in another.\n- Choose models via **domain knowledge** and/or trial-and-error (cross-validation, Bayesian methods). Keep many models/techniques in your toolbox.\n\n---\n\n## 1.3 Unsupervised learning (PDF p. 44)\n\n- Only inputs **D = {xₙ}**, no outputs. Goal: \"make sense of\" data, not learn a mapping.\n- Probabilistic view: fit an **unconditional model p(x)** (vs. supervised **conditional model p(y|x)**).\n- Advantages: no need for expensive labels; avoids arbitrary category definitions; forces the model to \"explain\" high-dimensional inputs → richer world models. (Supervised learning is \"glorified curve fitting.\")\n\n- **§1.3.1 Clustering:** partition inputs into regions of similar points. **No single correct number of clusters** — trade off model complexity vs. fit.\n- **§1.3.2 Latent factors of variation:** assume high-D xₙ generated from low-D **latent factors zₙ ∈ R^K**. Linear version = **Factor Analysis (FA)**; special case (Σ = σ²I) = **(probabilistic) PCA**. Nonlinear extension via DNN → **variational autoencoder (VAE)**.\n- **§1.3.3 Self-supervised learning:** create **proxy supervised tasks from unlabeled data** (e.g., predict masked words, colorize grayscale). Learns useful features for downstream supervised tasks.\n- **§1.3.4 Evaluating unsupervised learning:** hard — no ground truth. Approaches: (1) **density estimation** — measure NLL/probability assigned to unseen test data (good model isn't \"surprised\"); relates to **data compression**; (2) use learned representation as features for a downstream task → improves **sample efficiency**; (3) **gain understanding / interpretability** (\"carve nature at its joints\").\n\n---\n\n## 1.4 Reinforcement learning (RL) (PDF p. 47)\n\n- An **agent** learns to **interact with an environment** via a **policy a = π(x)** mapping states → actions.\n- Difference from supervised learning: agent is **not told the correct action**; it only gets an occasional **reward** (or punishment) signal. \"Learning with a critic\" (occasional thumbs up/down) vs. \"learning with a teacher.\"\n- Hard because reward is sparse/delayed → **credit-assignment problem** (which action earned the reward?).\n- LeCun's cake analogy: unsupervised = sponge (bulk), supervised = icing, RL = cherry.\n\n---\n\n## 1.5 Data (PDF p. 49)\n\n- Data nature/quality is as vital as the model.\n- **Image datasets:** MNIST (60k train / 10k test, 28×28 grayscale digits — the \"drosophila of ML\"), EMNIST, Fashion-MNIST, CIFAR-10/100, **ImageNet** (~14M images; ILSVRC benchmark; CNNs surpassed a human in 2015).\n- **Text / NLP datasets & tasks:** text classification / **sentiment analysis** (IMDB reviews), **machine translation** (WMT), **seq2seq** (summarization, question answering — SQuAD), **language modeling** (unconditional generative model of text p(x₁…x_T) — a form of unsupervised learning).\n\n### 1.5.3 Preprocessing discrete input data (PDF p. 53)\n- **One-hot encoding (dummy encoding):** categorical variable with K values → indicator vector, e.g., red = [1,0,0].\n- **Feature crosses:** linear model on one-hot features captures only **main effects**, not **interaction effects**. Explicit feature crosses (composite features) capture interactions → data becomes **wide format**.\n\n### 1.5.4 Preprocessing text data (PDF p. 53)\n- Challenges: variable length, high-cardinality categorical words, **out-of-vocabulary (OOV)** words.\n- **Bag of words (BoW):** ignore word order; map words → tokens; represent doc as count vector (**vector space model**). Preprocessing: drop punctuation, lowercase, **stop word removal**, **word stemming**.\n- **TF-IDF:** down-weight globally frequent words. IDF_i = log(N / (1+DF_i)); TFIDF_ij = log(TF_ij + 1) × IDF_i.\n- **Word embeddings:** map sparse one-hot vectors to dense low-D vectors so semantically similar words are close (fixes that one-hot/count vectors put \"man\" no closer to \"woman\" than to \"banana\"). Often use a **pre-trained embedding** → model stays linear in W.\n- **OOV / novel words:** replace with **UNK**, or use **subword units / wordpieces** via **byte-pair encoding (BPE)**.\n\n### 1.5.5 Handling missing data (PDF p. 56)\n- Missingness matrix M (M_nd = 1 if feature missing).\n- **MCAR** (missing completely at random): missingness independent of all features. **MAR** (missing at random): depends only on *observed* features. **NMAR** (not missing at random): depends on the hidden values themselves — must model the missingness mechanism.\n- Common fixes: **mean value imputation**, or fit a generative model to fill in missing values. (This book assumes MAR.)\n\n---\n\n## 1.6 Discussion (PDF p. 57)\n\n- **ML vs. related fields:** predictive analytics (business-focused supervised learning), data mining (structured/commercial DBs), data science (ML + stats + integration/visualization), **statistics** (very close), **AI** (ML now used so an AI system acquires its own knowledge; \"ML\" and \"AI\" sometimes used interchangeably).\n- **Structure of the book:** probability theory is the unifying lens. Part I = foundations; Parts II–IV = supervised; Part V = unsupervised. RL is covered in the sequel [Mur23].\n- **Caveats:** loss functions are hard to specify → **reward hacking**, part of the broader **alignment problem** (AI ethics/safety). **Inverse RL (IRL)** infers reward from human behavior. **AGI** (autonomous decisions) vs. **IA / augmented intelligence** (human in the loop).\n\n---\n\n## Key formulas cheat-sheet\n| Concept | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Empirical risk | L(θ) = (1/N) Σ ℓ(yₙ, f(xₙ;θ)) |\n| Misclassification rate | zero-one loss ℓ₀₁ = I(y ≠ ŷ) |\n| Softmax | softmax(a)_c = e^{a_c} / Σ_{c'} e^{a_{c'}} |\n| Regression (ℓ₂) loss / MSE | (y − ŷ)² ; MSE = (1/N) Σ (yₙ − f(xₙ;θ))² |\n| NLL (fitting probabilistic models) | −(1/N) Σ log p(yₙ | f(xₙ;θ)); MLE = argmin NLL |\n| Generalization gap | L(θ; p*) − L(θ; D_train) |\n\n---\n\n## ML4T Exam 1 relevance\n- **→ ML Domain 1 (Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing) [PRIMARY]:** supervised vs. unsupervised vs. reinforcement learning distinctions; classification vs. regression; the T/P/E framing; conditional p(y|x) vs. unconditional p(x) models; loss functions, empirical risk minimization, and MLE as the fitting objective. This is the core week-1 vocabulary the exam tests.\n- **→ ML Domain 8/9 (Model Evaluation, Overfitting & Generalization):** overfitting vs. underfitting, U-shaped test-error curve, generalization gap, train/validation/test split (validation = model selection; test = future-performance estimate only), degrees of freedom, No Free Lunch / inductive bias, and (for ML4T) why a model that memorizes in-sample data fails out-of-sample — directly analogous to backtest overfitting on historical price data.\n- **Supporting:** feature engineering/preprocessing (polynomial features, one-hot, feature crosses), linear vs. polynomial regression and least squares (ties to the course's regression-learner and decision-tree material), and epistemic vs. aleatoric uncertainty.\n"}]}, {"part": "ML", "num": "02", "name": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "cheatsheet": "- **Missing data mechanisms** decide what is safe to do:\n  - **MCAR (Missing Completely at Random)** — missingness is unrelated to any value (e.g., a random API/feed glitch). Dropping MCAR rows shrinks the sample but does **not** systematically bias it.\n  - **MAR (Missing at Random)** — missingness depends on *observed* variables, not the missing value itself.\n  - **MNAR (Missing Not at Random)** — missingness depends on the *unobserved value itself* (e.g., a firm stops filing *because* it defaulted; volume missing *because* liquidity evaporated). Any imputation that ignores the cause injects systematic bias. MNAR missingness is often an informative **feature** (a proxy for stress/distress/untradeability).\n- **Imputation is an estimate, not a measurement.** Treating an imputed value as an observed fact discards uncertainty and distorts relationships.\n  - **Mean imputation** shrinks apparent variance, blunts signal, and can hide the \"signal of failure/zero-liquidity.\"\n  - **Median imputation** is more robust to extreme observed values than the mean, but repeatedly inserting one central value still creates an artificial spike in the distribution.\n  - **Zero/neutral imputation** for sentiment forces a \"neutral\" assumption that dampens real trending signal.\n  - **Forward-fill** carries the last observation forward — reasonable for short gaps, but long fills make **stale prices look current** and conflate distinct causes (feed outage vs. no-trade).\n  - Best practice: **fit imputation parameters on training data only**, then apply to test; record missingness reason/duration as metadata; use different strategies for different *types* of missingness in the same column.\n- **Noise vs. outliers:** extremeness alone does **not** prove an observation is erroneous. Decide by *context and data-quality evidence* (source records, corporate actions, decimal-placement checks) and by the **model's intended use**, distinguishing a **data-integrity error** (delete/correct) from a **valid extreme / \"hard truth\"** (keep or manage robustly).\n  - **OLS / L2 (MSE) loss is the most outlier-sensitive**: squaring residuals gives extreme points disproportionate leverage over the fitted parameters. A single large-sigma outlier under MSE can dominate many small errors.\n  - **Robust methods** (Huber loss, MAE, ε-insensitive SVR) cap the influence of large residuals (linear, not squared, penalty beyond a threshold).\n  - **Trees** are relatively robust to an outlier's *magnitude*: once a value is past a split threshold, its exact size doesn't move the split; larger min-leaf size further prevents isolating single points. Trees are not fully immune — outliers can still affect split selection and leaf values.\n  - **High-capacity models (deep NNs)** are *more* prone to fitting/memorizing outliers, not immune; **Batch Norm does not neutralize outliers** (an extreme value skews the batch mean/variance).\n  - **Winsorizing/trimming** genuine tail events understates tail severity and can create a form of survivorship bias for risk models.\n- **Sampling bias & representativeness:** a sample must be a \"miniature version\" of the deployment population. Sample **size/volume/duration does NOT fix systematic bias**, and adding **more features does not fix a biased set of rows**.\n  - **Survivorship bias** — conditioning on entities that survived to the end (current index constituents; firms still listed today) omits failures, inflating apparent performance and hiding the negative/failure class.\n  - **Selection bias** — inclusion criteria correlated with the target (dividend payers, published trials → **publication bias**) skew feature/outcome distributions.\n  - **Point-in-time** membership/data better reconstructs what was investable/known at each past date. Evaluation data should resemble the intended deployment population/regime; a clean split only tests transfer *within the sampled source population*.\n- **Temporal structure & ordering:** financial rows are time-ordered and serially dependent (not exchangeable), so treat time as first-class.\n  - **Random shuffling** before splitting places later regimes in training and earlier ones in test, ignores information-arrival direction, and destroys autocorrelation signals (momentum, moving averages). Use **chronological / walk-forward** splits (train on earlier, test on later).\n  - **Look-ahead / forward-looking bias:** never let information unavailable at the decision time enter training — e.g., normalizing on the *full* dataset (global μ/σ), aligning macro data (BLS unemployment) to its *period* rather than its *release date*, or including a label whose forward window hasn't matured. Distinguish **event date** from **availability/publication date**; index by point-in-time.\n  - Walk-forward preserves the arrow of time, exposes regime drift, and simulates live deployment; expanding-window folds legitimately grow the training history (folds need not be equal size). Preserving order can lower but *make more meaningful* the score.\n- **Train/test separation discipline:** the test set must stay genuinely unseen. Avoiding test *labels* is **not** sufficient — **any data-dependent choice fit on test features leaks**:\n  - Preprocessing (scaling, median/KNN imputation), **feature selection (RFE)**, and hyperparameter/model selection are all part of the model-development pipeline and must be fit **on training data only** (or dynamically within each walk-forward fold).\n  - Combined-data standardization, global-median imputation, and KNN imputation over the full matrix (a **transductive/manifold leak** — a past row borrows a future neighbor) all contaminate the evaluation even without touching labels.\n  - Retraining the winning config on the training data does **not** erase test-based selection; upstream vendor feature lists built on full history carry the same leak (provenance matters).\n- **Data snooping & reuse:** repeatedly evaluating on the same held-out period and reacting to results makes that period part of development.\n  - **Multiple comparisons:** screening many candidates and reporting the max (best Sharpe of 5,000 indicators) favors positive noise; in-sample ranking is not independent confirmation. The *breadth of the search* determines how surprising the winner is.\n  - Leakage can occur through **human iteration and organization-wide feedback** (shared holdout scores) without ever copying labels into features. Access-controlling raw rows does not preserve independence.\n  - Mitigations: a genuinely untouched final set, limited evaluation frequency, and experiment tracking (which improves transparency but cannot restore spent independence).", "traps": "- **Inverted-logic questions (12 of 33):** the Answer Key reads \"**True = incorrect; False = accurate**,\" so a \"True\" verdict means the statement is *wrong* and \"False\" means it is *correct*. Read the polarity line before every table. Inverted here: **G1Q2, G1Q5, G1Q6, G2Q2, G3Q2, G3Q4, G3Q5, G4Q1, G4Q4, G4Q6, G5Q3, G6Q1.**\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices — \"The following claim is false: <accurate statement>\":** the inner claim is true, so the whole statement is false/inaccurate. In inverted questions that maps to a **True (incorrect)** verdict; in normal questions to a **False** verdict. Appears in **G1Q2A** (inverted → True), **G3Q2A** (inverted → True), **G4Q2A** (normal → False), **G6Q3A** (normal → False).\n- **\"It is incorrect to conclude that <false statement>\":** in **G4Q4A** (inverted) the embedded claim (global μ/σ gives a sound \"stable reference\") is itself inaccurate, so rejecting it is correct → the statement is **accurate → False**. Parse the inner claim, judge it, then apply the double negative and the polarity.\n- **Size/volume never cures bias.** Many choices bait you with \"the dataset is large / spans a decade / two exchanges / thousands of responses, therefore representative.\" A large but systematically filtered sample is still biased (G3Q1C, G3Q2, G3Q4E, G3Q5A/E, G3Q6C). Likewise **adding features cannot fix biased rows** (G3Q5E).\n- **\"No test labels used\" ≠ clean test.** Leakage hides in preprocessing, imputation, feature selection, and human/organizational iteration (G2, G5, G6 families). Fitting *any* parameter on data that includes the held-out portion contaminates the estimate.\n- **Extreme ≠ erroneous, and cleaning that lowers average error can worsen the decision.** Validity needs context, not a distance-from-mean threshold (G2Q1B/E, G2Q3E, G2Q4E). OLS/MSE is the *most* outlier-sensitive learner (recurring distractor claims it \"de-weights\" outliers — false).\n- **Calendar alignment vs. availability.** Aligning macro/label data to the period it *covers* rather than its *release* date is look-ahead bias; shuffling does not fix a causality violation, it just spreads the corrupted signal (G4Q6E, G4Q3, G6-style reasoning).", "references": "1. **de Prado — *Advances in Financial Machine Learning*:** the core reference for this domain — labeling and point-in-time data, look-ahead/forward-looking bias, purged & embargoed / walk-forward cross-validation, backtest overfitting, and fat-tail/outlier treatment in finance.\n2. **ISLR — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning* (James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani):** ch.3 (OLS, leverage/outliers, high-influence points), ch.5 (resampling, validation vs. test set), ch.6 (feature/subset selection), ch.8–9 (trees, SVM/SVR robustness).\n3. **ESL — *The Elements of Statistical Learning* (Hastie, Tibshirani, Friedman):** ch.7, esp. §7.10.2 \"The Wrong and Right Way to Do Cross-Validation\" (feature-selection and preprocessing leakage) and multiple-comparison bias.\n4. **Little & Rubin — *Statistical Analysis with Missing Data*:** MCAR/MAR/MNAR taxonomy, bias from complete-case deletion, and principled imputation/uncertainty.\n5. **scikit-learn User Guide:** \"Common pitfalls and recommended practices — Data leakage,\" `Pipeline`, `StandardScaler`, `SimpleImputer`/`KNNImputer`/`MissingIndicator`, `TimeSeriesSplit`, and cross-validation — fit all preprocessing on training data only.\n6. **Bailey, Borwein, López de Prado & Zhu / Dwork et al.:** \"The Deflated Sharpe Ratio\" and \"The reusable holdout\" — multiple-testing, selection under many comparisons, and adaptive (institutional) overfitting of held-out data.", "bookNotes": [{"book": "DeepLearning", "title": "Ch. 2.2 — Probability Densities", "file": "references/DeepLearning/Ch02-2_Probability_Densities.md", "md": "# Ch. 2.2 — Probability Densities\n\n**Source:** Bishop & Bishop, *Deep Learning: Foundations and Concepts* (Springer, 2024).\n**Book pages 32–35** (PDF pages 52–55). §2.2 Probability Densities; §2.2.1 Example distributions; §2.2.2 Expectations and covariances. (§2.3 Gaussian begins p. 36 — out of scope.)\n\n> **→ ML4T Exam-1 relevance: ML Domain 2 (continuous distributions), expected value / variance / covariance — the statistical machinery behind returns, risk, and portfolio math.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Probability density (§2.2) — **exam-critical**\n- For a **continuous** variable the probability of any exact value is effectively zero, so we use a **probability density** p(x): probability of x in (x, x+δx) ≈ p(x)δx as δx→0.\n- Probability over an interval:\n$$p(x \\in (a,b)) = \\int_a^b p(x)\\, dx \\tag{2.23}$$\n- **Validity conditions:**\n$$p(x) \\ge 0 \\tag{2.24}, \\qquad \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} p(x)\\, dx = 1 \\tag{2.25}$$\n- **Cumulative distribution function (CDF):** $P(z) = \\int_{-\\infty}^{z} p(x)\\, dx$ (2.26), with $P'(x) = p(x)$.\n- **Multivariate:** joint density p(x) over vector x with p(x) ≥ 0 and ∫p(x)dx = 1 over all space.\n\n**Sum/product rules and Bayes carry over to densities** (integrals replace sums):\n$$\\text{sum: } p(x) = \\int p(x,y)\\,dy \\tag{2.29}, \\quad \\text{product: } p(x,y) = p(y \\mid x)\\,p(x) \\tag{2.30}$$\n$$\\text{Bayes: } p(y \\mid x) = \\frac{p(x \\mid y)\\,p(y)}{p(x)} \\tag{2.31}, \\quad p(x) = \\int p(x \\mid y)\\,p(y)\\,dy \\tag{2.32}$$\n\n## 2. Example distributions (§2.2.1)\n- A constant density can't be normalized over the real line → **improper distribution**.\n- **Uniform** over (c,d): $p(x) = 1/(d-c)$ for x∈(c,d) (2.33).\n- **Exponential:** $p(x \\mid \\lambda) = \\lambda e^{-\\lambda x}, \\; x>0$ (2.34).\n- **Laplace** (exponential with movable peak μ): $p(x \\mid \\mu,\\gamma) = \\tfrac{1}{2\\gamma}\\exp(-|x-\\mu|/\\gamma)$ (2.35).\n- **Dirac delta** $\\delta(x-\\mu)$: zero everywhere except x=μ, integrates to 1 — an infinitely narrow, tall unit-area spike (2.36).\n- **Empirical distribution:** sum of deltas on the data points, $p(x \\mid \\mathcal{D}) = \\tfrac{1}{N}\\sum_{n=1}^N \\delta(x - x_n)$ (2.37).\n\n## 3. Expectations and covariances (§2.2.2) — **exam-critical**\n- **Expectation** (weighted average of f(x)):\n$$\\text{discrete: } \\mathbb{E}[f] = \\sum_x p(x) f(x) \\tag{2.38}, \\qquad \\text{continuous: } \\mathbb{E}[f] = \\int p(x) f(x)\\, dx \\tag{2.39}$$\n- **Monte Carlo / sample estimate** (exact as N→∞):\n$$\\mathbb{E}[f] \\approx \\tfrac{1}{N}\\sum_{n=1}^{N} f(x_n) \\tag{2.40}$$\n- **Conditional expectation:** $\\mathbb{E}_x[f \\mid y] = \\sum_x p(x \\mid y) f(x)$ (2.42), integral form (2.43); it is a function of y. Subscript $\\mathbb{E}_x$ marks the averaged variable.\n- **Variance** (spread around the mean):\n$$\\text{var}[f] = \\mathbb{E}[(f(x) - \\mathbb{E}[f(x)])^2] = \\mathbb{E}[f(x)^2] - \\mathbb{E}[f(x)]^2 \\tag{2.44, 2.45}$$\n$$\\text{var}[x] = \\mathbb{E}[x^2] - \\mathbb{E}[x]^2 \\tag{2.46}$$\n- **Covariance** (how two variables vary together):\n$$\\text{cov}[x,y] = \\mathbb{E}_{x,y}[\\{x - \\mathbb{E}[x]\\}\\{y - \\mathbb{E}[y]\\}] = \\mathbb{E}_{x,y}[xy] - \\mathbb{E}[x]\\mathbb{E}[y] \\tag{2.47}$$\n- **Independence ⟹ covariance = 0.** For vectors, covariance is a matrix (2.48); $\\text{cov}[x] \\equiv \\text{cov}[x,x]$.\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam anchors\n- Density: p(x) ≥ 0, integrates to 1; probabilities come from integrating over an interval; CDF is the integral, its derivative is the density.\n- E[·] = probability-weighted average; sample mean approximates it (basis of Monte Carlo).\n- var[x] = E[x²] − E[x]²; cov measures co-movement; independent ⇒ cov = 0 (directly relevant to portfolio variance and diversification).\n"}, {"book": "Handbook_AI_BigData", "title": "Ch. 1 — On Machine Learning Applications in Investments", "file": "references/Handbook_AI_BigData/Ch01_ML_Applications_in_Investments.md", "md": "# Ch. 1 — On Machine Learning Applications in Investments\n\n**Source:** Chen, M. (Robeco) & Zhou, W. — *Handbook of AI and Big Data Applications in Investments*, CFA Institute Research Foundation (2023).\n**Book pages:** 2–19 (PDF pages 23–40).\n**ML4T exam relevance:** ML Domain 1 (supervised learning, regression/classification, ensembles, decision trees, overfitting), ML Domain 2 (evaluation, bias/variance, data splitting), QF Domain 9–10 (return prediction, Sharpe ratio, factor models, backtesting). Faithful to source; nothing invented.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why apply ML to investing (motivations)\n\n- **Primary attraction:** promise of higher **risk-adjusted return**. ML is built for **prediction on high-dimensional data with no functional-form specification**, so it should be good at predicting future equity returns.\n- Practitioners have used ML to predict: **cross-sectional equity returns**, **corporate earnings** (more accurate than traditional models), and **crash / severe price-drop probability** (superior to linear models using leverage ratio or distance-to-default).\n- **Key driver of outperformance vs. linear models:** ML does **not prespecify the functional relationship** between predictors (financial-statement metrics, past returns) and target (return, earnings). It can capture **interaction effects** and **nonlinear effects**.\n  - **Interaction effect:** output is not a linear combination of inputs because one input's effect depends on another's value (e.g., reported earnings matter only when an accounting \"red flag\" = 0; if red flag = 1, price drops 50% regardless of earnings).\n  - **Nonlinear effect:** e.g., relationship between a firm's CDS spread and its equity return (equity as embedded call option on firm assets). Neural networks introduce nonlinearity via **activation functions** (without them a deep net collapses to a linear model).\n  - Empirically (Gu, Kelly, Xiu 2020): **interaction effects drive most of the outperformance**; the jury is still out on nonlinear-only contribution (a generalized linear model with splines but no interactions barely beat purely linear).\n- **\"Data mining\" / \"knowledge discovery\":** ML can find relationships not prespecified by researchers. Attractive with the growing \"data deluge,\" but must guard against **spurious, nonreplicable correlations**.\n\n## 2. Why finance is HARD for ML (unique challenges)\n\n- **Low signal-to-noise ratio:** any single metric weakly determines a security's performance; returns compress company-specific, macro, behavioral, and positioning drivers into one dimension (up/down). Contrast with high-signal domains (e.g., video recommendations).\n- **Small data vs. big data:** financial data is small (US = ~100 yrs, at most ~1,200 monthly points per security) vs. billions/trillions in other domains. Small data limits true cross-validation, out-of-sample testing, hyperparameter tuning, robustness checks. **A simpler model on more data often beats a sophisticated model on little data.** (Alternative data is more abundant but overall finance data is still small.)\n- **Nonstationarity / adaptive markets / irrationality:** markets are **man-made and adaptive** — they \"learn\" as participants learn, and published research (e.g., Fama–French 1993) changes behavior (McLean & Pontiff 2016). The **data-generating process changes over time**; humans act irrationally (greed/fear). Contrast static domains (e.g., protein folding).\n\n## 3. Common pitfalls and how to avoid them (exam-critical)\n\n- **Overfitting:** biggest concern given short history + high degrees of freedom in ML. Symptom = great backtest, poor out-of-sample.\n  - Mitigations (cross-domain): **cross-validation, feature selection/removal, regularization, early stopping, ensembling, holdout data.**\n  - Finance advantage: **human intuition + economic domain knowledge** (\"smell tests\"). Use **SHAP values** (Shapley additive explanation — average marginal contribution of a feature) to check whether learned relationships make economic sense.\n  - **Feature engineering:** curate inputs using domain knowledge up front (e.g., profit margin, debt coverage are sensible crash predictors; CEO's last-name initial is not).\n- **Replicability:** even harder for ML than traditional stats — more tunable variables, and open-source algorithms vary by version/random seed/implementation. Mitigate with **robust data + code infrastructure**: version control, fixed parameters (algorithm version, universe, hyperparameters, feature sets), and documentation of all iterations including failures (\"research graveyards\").\n- **Lookahead bias / data leakage:**\n  - **Lookahead bias:** using data before it was actually available (use **point-in-time** data — e.g., quarterly results available 40 days after quarter-end must not be used at quarter-end).\n  - **Leakage in data:** information that belongs in one split (train/validation/test) leaks into another → overly optimistic results. Mitigate with a **gap period** (months–quarters) between train/validation/test splits.\n  - **Leakage in features:** an informative feature that would be unavailable at production time (e.g., undisclosed executive health issue).\n- **Implementation gap:** backtest trades that are impossible/infeasible live — e.g., alpha coming from small/micro-cap stocks (too costly, insufficient shares), or shorting stocks that can't be borrowed / cost too much. ML worsens this via **high turnover**.\n- **Explainability / performance attribution:** models are complex \"black boxes\" (though inspectable). Asset owners demand explanations. Technique = **partial dependence response**: fix a feature at value x, average predictions, sweep x over its range → decompose output into **linear, interaction, nonlinear** components; then form portfolios from each to attribute returns.\n\n## 4. Sample applications (examples + results)\n\n- **Predicting cross-sectional stock returns** (relative, not absolute; stock selection, not timing/allocation — an easier problem with more data).\n  - Setup: (1) investment universe, (2) ML algorithms (boosted trees/random forests, neural nets, ensembles), (3) feature set (value price-ratios, quality/profitability, momentum/past returns). Limiting the feature set = imposing domain-knowledge structure to fight small data.\n  - **Five consistent results:**\n    1. ML significantly and economically outperforms linear approaches. Gu et al. (2020): 4-layer NN long–short decile spread → out-of-sample **Sharpe 1.35 (value-weighted), 2.45 (equal-weighted)** vs. OLS **0.61 / 0.83**.\n    2. Outperformance comes from **higher-order effects (nonlinearity + interactions)**; interactions matter more than nonlinearity.\n    3. The **most important features are consistent across ML algorithms**: short-term reversal, stock & sector momentum, return volatility, firm size.\n    4. **Simple ML models beat complex ones** (less overfitting on small data).\n    5. **More data → better ML performance** (\"unreasonable effectiveness of data\").\n- **Predicting stock crashes:** crash = large idiosyncratic price drop vs. peers. Algorithms: logistic regression, random forest, gradient-boosted tree, ensemble. Features: distance-to-default, volatility, market beta + fundamentals. Used in **conservative/low-vol equity** (\"win by not losing\"). ML beats traditional in developed and emerging markets; returns come from stock selection, not sector allocation.\n- **Predicting fundamentals (earnings):** easier + more stable target than returns. Cao & You (2021), US ex-financials/utilities, 1975–2019, 28 line items + first differences (56 features). Linear ML beats traditional (~6%); nonlinear ML beats linear ML (~1–3%); **ensembles beat individuals** (\"ensembling is the closest thing to a free lunch, like diversification\").\n- **NLP in multiple languages:** embedding-based NLP to decode Chinese A-share retail-investor slang; word embeddings preserve vector arithmetic (King − Man + Woman ≈ Queen). Shows ML helps non-numerical tasks too.\n\n## 5. Conclusion takeaways\n\n- ML beats traditional methods **if applied to the right problem**; source of edge = nonlinear + interaction effects; **ensembling** adds value.\n- Finance is distinctive: less data, nonstationary, irrational participants — so know the technique's limits.\n- Other ML uses: sentiment analysis, foreign-language understanding, data cleaning, fraud detection, credit scoring, trading optimization.\n"}]}, {"part": "ML", "num": "03", "name": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "cheatsheet": "- **Feature construction from raw inputs**: derived features (ratios, growth rates, margins) can expose relationships the raw level does not. A *growth* term encodes a temporal change; a *ratio/margin* (profit ÷ revenue) is scale-adjusted and comparable across firms. But a transformation only helps if it adds representable structure — restating existing information (a pure re-expression) can be redundant and add no new signal.\n- **Feature interactions**: a purely additive linear model (β₁x₁ + β₂x₂) cannot represent a *conditional/multiplicative* effect (e.g., \"high volume matters differently in rising vs. falling markets\"). An explicit interaction term (sign × volume) encodes that joint dependence. Adding flexibility can help, do nothing, or overfit — it never *guarantees* better out-of-sample performance.\n- **Nonlinear transforms for linear learners**: polynomial/other basis expansions let a linear combination express curved relationships. More terms = more capacity to fit noise, especially with limited data. Judge usefulness on **held-out** data, never training error alone.\n- **Scaling & normalization** — *learner-dependent*:\n  - **Distance/gradient-based learners (KNN, neural nets, SVM)** are scale-sensitive: an unscaled large-magnitude feature dominates Euclidean distance; unscaled inputs cause exploding/saturating gradients. Standardize (z-score) so features contribute on comparable scales. Scaling changes *numerical influence*, not information content, and does not equalize predictive value.\n  - **Tree-based learners (CART, Random Forest, GBDT)** split on order/thresholds → invariant to any monotonic (incl. positive-linear, z-score, quantile) per-feature transform. Removing an unneeded scaling step simplifies the pipeline without changing tree predictions. Trees are still vulnerable to noise, leakage, and irrelevant inputs.\n  - **Fit the scaler on training data only**, then apply to held-out/live data (a causal, backward-looking window). Refitting on the full/test window leaks future distribution info even though no *target* is touched.\n- **Non-stationarity traps in scaling** (finance-specific):\n  - **Rolling z-score in a volatility spike**: the denominator (rolling σ) balloons, *deflating* the z-score and masking a genuine tail event (Domain 3 G2Q4).\n  - **Standardizing an exponential/trending series**: training mean/σ become unrepresentative; production values drift out-of-distribution (G2Q5).\n  - **Cross-sectional quantile transform**: preserves rank but caps at [0,1]; a market-wide crash where *all* values spike leaves percentiles unchanged → an \"information blackout\" that strips the macro signal (G2Q6).\n- **Stationarity tests are limited**: the **ADF test** only rejects a unit root (stochastic trend in the *mean*); a series can pass ADF yet still suffer structural breaks or variance drift. Rolling OLS betas are unstable under regime shifts (non-stationarity); remedies are exponentially-weighted regression or state-space/Kalman filters (G1Q4–Q6).\n- **Irrelevant & redundant features**: random/irrelevant columns give a flexible learner more chances to fit spurious patterns → higher selection/evaluation variance; training correlation can be accidental. Highly **collinear** features carry overlapping info: in **OLS** they destabilize coefficient estimates (near-singular X'X, inflated standard errors); in **tree ensembles** they *dilute* feature importance (credit is split across duplicates) but do not break the splitting math. Redundancy is task-dependent — a correlated feature can still add conditional information. Remedies: judge on validated (out-of-sample) performance, weigh cost/latency/reliability, or project to orthogonal components via **PCA**.\n- **Feature selection intuition**: reducing the feature space lowers estimation variance and spurious-pattern opportunities — especially valuable when *features ≫ observations*. The tradeoff: pruning can discard weak-but-real signal (omitted-variable bias / underfitting) and typically *raises* in-sample error while lowering out-of-sample variance (bias–variance). Lowest training error ≠ best subset. **PCA** is *unsupervised* (maximizes X-variance, ignores y) so it may drop a low-variance but highly predictive direction, and its components lose interpretability. **Ridge (L2)** shrinks all coefficients toward (never exactly) zero, keeping every feature; **Lasso (L1)/Best-Subset** produce sparsity and win when the true model is sparse. Wrapper searches (SFS/Best-Subset) that optimize a backtest metric **overfit the selection step itself** (selection bias) even if the final model is small.\n- **Target leakage**: a feature that encodes the outcome (post-resolution status codes, \"days since eventual last transaction,\" post-alert review codes) yields unrealistic accuracy that vanishes in deployment. Leakage is about *timing/lineage*, not the column name — renaming does nothing. Leakage also travels through **preprocessing and feature-selection decisions** (fitting scalers, imputers, or target-guided selectors on pooled train+test data) and through **cross-sectional pooling** (computing a sector total/mean or cross-sectional σ over the full universe before splitting folds). Fix: reconstruct every feature point-in-time; fit all learned steps (scaling, imputation, selection) strictly within each training fold.\n- **Look-ahead bias in time-series**: using information not yet available at the decision timestamp. A **centered moving average / z-score** (uses t−k…t+k) peeks forward — replace with a **trailing** window (…t−1, t). A **chronological split does NOT fix a feature whose own construction looks forward**. Backfilling revised/restated fundamentals into earlier dates, joining by fiscal year without publication lags, using today's *close* to trade today's *open*, or using an auction print to participate in that same auction are all look-ahead. Future returns are valid as **labels** (once realized) but never as contemporaneous features. Use a **point-in-time (PIT)** database that stores each value's publication/effective date.\n- **\"Too good to be true\" features**: an abrupt, implausible jump in accuracy (53% → 99%), an opaque vendor feature, or a suspiciously high-importance field with unclear lineage/timing warrants a leakage/lineage audit. Correlation ≠ causation (e.g., ticker alphabetical order \"predicts\" returns); confidentiality, feature importance, and historical presence do not establish point-in-time availability. Diagnostics: verify feature timestamp precedes the decision, ablate/lag the feature, and replicate across untouched periods/universes.", "traps": "- **Inverted-logic questions (12 of 39):** the Answer Key reads \"**True = incorrect; False = accurate**,\" so a **True** verdict flags the statement as *wrong* and **False** confirms it is *correct*. Read the polarity line before every question. Inverted here: **G1Q2, G1Q4, G2Q3, G2Q6, G3Q3, G3Q5, G3Q6, G4Q2, G5Q2, G5Q6, G6Q1, G7Q2.**\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices:** a choice states \"**The following claim is false: [X]**\" or \"**It is incorrect to conclude that [X]**.\" Parse inner claim X, judge *its* truth, then negate. When X is accurate, asserting it is false/incorrect is itself wrong. In **normal** polarity these resolve to verdict **False** (G5Q3A, G5Q4A, G6Q2A, G6Q3A) or **True** when X is inaccurate (G2Q5A). In **inverted** polarity they flip again: G2Q3A → True (incorrect), G3Q3A → True, G4Q2A → True, G5Q6A → False (accurate), G6Q1... (G6Q1 has plain choices). Always separate the *polarity* flip from the *embedded-negation* flip.\n- **Scaling is learner-dependent — never \"mandatory for every algorithm.\"** Trees are invariant to monotonic per-feature transforms; distance/gradient learners are not. Watch for choices claiming scaling is universally required (false) or that trees are \"immune to all data problems\" (false — noise/leakage/irrelevance still bite).\n- **\"Chronological split prevents all temporal leakage\" is false.** A forward-looking feature (centered MA, t+k windows, execution using same-bar auction price) leaks regardless of how the train/test boundary is drawn.\n- **Leakage hides in preprocessing/selection, not just raw columns.** Fitting a scaler, imputer, target-correlation ranker, Lasso selector, or cross-sectional statistic on pooled data (or the full universe before splitting) contaminates the evaluation even when no future-dated column enters the final model. Excluding the target column is *not* sufficient.\n- **PCA and rank-based transforms are monotonic/rank-preserving — they do NOT alter within-window rank order.** Choices claiming z-score or quantile mapping \"changes the rank-order of the data points\" are wrong; the real failures are out-of-distribution drift, denominator inflation, or information blackout.\n- **A ratio scaled by a firm's *own contemporaneous, point-in-time* figure is clean** (G5Q6C); a ratio scaled by a *cross-sectional universe aggregate computed before the split* leaks (G5Q6A).", "references": "1. **ISLR — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning* (James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani):** §2.2.2 (bias-variance, overfitting), §3.3 (interactions §3.3.2, collinearity/VIF §3.3.3), §6.1 (subset selection & why fewer features help), §6.2 (ridge & lasso — shrinkage vs. sparsity), §6.3 / §12.2 (PCA / principal-components regression), §7.1 (polynomial/basis expansion), ch.5 (cross-validation and selection-inside-CV).\n2. **ESL — *The Elements of Statistical Learning* (Hastie, Tibshirani, Friedman):** §3.4 (ridge/lasso), ch.7 (model assessment, generalization), ch.14/15 (PCA, random forests & feature importance).\n3. **scikit-learn User Guide:** \"Common pitfalls and recommended practices — Data leakage\" (fit preprocessing/selection on train only; use `Pipeline`), \"Preprocessing data\" (StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler, QuantileTransformer — scale-sensitivity and monotonicity), \"Nearest Neighbors\" (distance dependence), \"Decision Trees / Random Forest\" (scale-invariance), \"Permutation importance with multicollinear features,\" \"Cross-validation\" / \"Nested cross-validation,\" \"Feature selection\" (`SelectFromModel`, SFS).\n4. **Marcos López de Prado — *Advances in Financial Machine Learning*:** point-in-time data, look-ahead bias, causal feature construction, labeling (triple-barrier), stationarity & fractional differentiation, backtest overfitting / deflated Sharpe.\n5. **Hamilton — *Time Series Analysis*:** unit-root / Augmented Dickey-Fuller testing scope and limits, structural breaks, non-stationarity, state-space/Kalman filtering.\n6. **Kaufman, Rosset, Perlich — \"Leakage in Data Mining: Formulation, Detection, and Avoidance\":** taxonomy of target leakage and detection via timing/lineage and ablation.", "bookNotes": [{"book": "ISLP", "title": "ISLP Section 3.5 — Comparison of Linear Regression with K-Nearest Neighbors", "file": "references/ISLP/Ch03-5_LinReg_vs_KNN.md", "md": "# ISLP Section 3.5 — Comparison of Linear Regression with K-Nearest Neighbors\n\n*Source: ISLP, PDF pages 120–124 (book pp. 111–115).*\n\n> ML4T exam relevance: parametric vs non-parametric trade-off, KNN regression, curse of\n> dimensionality — recurring exam themes. → ML Domain 4 (Linear Models) and ML Domain 3\n> (Instance-based / KNN), with bias-variance framing (Domain 8).\n\n## Parametric vs. non-parametric\n- **Linear regression = parametric**: assumes a functional form for f(X) (linear). Advantages:\n  easy to fit (few coefficients), interpretable coefficients, significance tests/p-values available.\n  Disadvantage: strong form assumption — if the true f is far from linear, prediction is poor.\n- **KNN regression = non-parametric**: no assumed form for f(X); more flexible.\n\n## KNN regression\n- For a value K and a prediction point x_0: find the K training points **N_0** closest to x_0, then\n  predict the **average** of their responses:\n  `f̂(x_0) = (1/K) Σ_{x_i ∈ N_0} y_i`.\n- Fit is a **step function**. K=1 interpolates training data exactly (rough steps); larger K averages\n  more points → smoother, less variable fit.\n- **K controls the bias-variance trade-off**: small K = flexible, **low bias / high variance**\n  (a region's prediction rests on one point); large K = smooth, **high bias / low variance** (averaging\n  can mask real structure). Choose optimal K via test-error estimation (cross-validation, Ch. 5).\n\n## When does each win?\n- **Linear regression wins when the true f is (close to) linear** — KNN pays a variance cost with no\n  offsetting bias reduction, so it is slightly inferior (esp. small K).\n- **KNN wins when f is substantially non-linear** — with p=1 it can massively beat linear regression;\n  as non-linearity grows, KNN test MSE barely changes while linear regression's test MSE rises sharply.\n\n## The catch: dimensionality and the curse of dimensionality\n- The KNN advantage in non-linear settings **collapses as p grows**. Example: strongly non-linear in\n  one variable plus added noise predictors — KNN beats linear regression at p=1,2; mixed at p=3;\n  linear regression superior at p ≥ 4. Adding dimensions barely hurt linear regression but caused a\n  >10× increase in KNN's MSE.\n- **Curse of dimensionality**: in high dimensions the K \"nearest\" neighbors can be very far from x_0\n  (spreading n observations across many dimensions ≈ a reduction in effective sample size), so KNN\n  predicts poorly.\n- **Rule of thumb**: parametric methods tend to beat non-parametric ones when there are **few\n  observations per predictor**.\n\n## Interpretability\n- Even when KNN's test MSE is slightly lower, linear regression may be preferred for\n  **interpretability** — a few coefficients, with p-values, vs. an opaque local-averaging model.\n"}]}, {"part": "ML", "num": "04", "name": "Linear Models & Regression", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "cheatsheet": "- **Form of a linear model**: prediction = intercept + a weighted (additive) sum of feature values, `ŷ = β₀ + Σ βⱼXⱼ`. The model is defined as **linear in its coefficients (parameters)**, NOT necessarily linear in the raw features — this is the single most-tested idea in the domain.\n- **Coefficient meaning & directionality**:\n  - A coefficient βⱼ is the modeled change in the prediction per one-unit change in Xⱼ, **holding the other included features fixed** (a *conditional/partial/marginal* association, not a total or unconditional effect).\n  - The **sign** gives the direction of the modeled association; a positive/negative sign does **not** establish causality.\n  - **Magnitude is NOT importance** unless features are on a common scale. Raw coefficient size depends on the feature's units/scale; comparing coefficients across differing units (percentage points vs. basis points) or across models with different specifications is misleading.\n  - **Standardizing** features (mean 0, variance 1) makes absolute coefficient magnitudes comparable as \"effect per one-standard-deviation change.\"\n  - **Rescaling** a feature forces its coefficient to rescale by the reciprocal factor; consistent rescaling of feature *and* coefficient leaves predictions unchanged (e.g., pp→bps: coefficient becomes 1/100).\n- **Intercept** = the prediction when all features equal zero (for standardized features, when all features are at their sample means). It is a fitted value, not a guarantee about any future realized outcome; may lack practical meaning.\n- **OLS assumptions / estimation**:\n  - OLS does **not** require features to be independent/uncorrelated. A **unique** OLS solution fails only under **exact linear dependence** (perfect multicollinearity / rank-deficient design matrix).\n  - **Multicollinearity** (high but imperfect correlation) inflates variance/standard errors of individual coefficients → estimates unstable, can swing or flip sign across samples — yet R², F-statistic, and *combined predictions* can stay stable. It does NOT bias coefficients and is distinct from serial correlation (which concerns residuals over time).\n  - **Zero conditional mean** `E[ε|X] = 0` is what buys unbiasedness; **omitted-variable bias** arises when an omitted variable is correlated with an included predictor (the coefficient then absorbs confounded effects).\n  - **Gauss–Markov / BLUE** requires linearity, exogeneity, homoscedasticity, no perfect collinearity — **normality of errors is NOT required** for BLUE (only for exact finite-sample t/F inference).\n- **Capacity & polynomial expansion**:\n  - Adding polynomial (X², X³…) or interaction (X₁X₂) terms increases capacity/flexibility to represent curvature and interactions while the model **stays linear in coefficients**.\n  - A squared term lets the slope vary with the feature (a turning point); an interaction lets one feature's marginal effect depend on another (∂ŷ/∂X₂ = β₂ + β₃X₁).\n  - **Bias–variance tradeoff**: higher degree lowers bias but raises variance; matching capacity to the true functional form (e.g., quadratic for a parabola) minimizes both. Degree 15 on noisy data overfits (wild oscillation), low OOS performance. Polynomial term count **grows fast** with the raw feature count. Raw powers of one feature are often **highly correlated** → multicollinearity; centering / orthogonal polynomials help.\n- **Residuals & fit**: residual `eᵢ = yᵢ − ŷᵢ` (observed − predicted); positive residual ⇒ model underpredicted. With an intercept, OLS residuals **sum to zero** (mean ≈ 0) by construction — so a near-zero mean residual proves nothing about correct specification. **Systematic patterns** (curvature vs. fitted values ⇒ nonlinearity/omitted structure; fan shape ⇒ heteroscedasticity; sign runs over time ⇒ serial correlation; heavy tails on Q-Q ⇒ non-normal errors). **MSE** (squared) penalizes large errors far more than **MAE** (absolute), so a single outlier can dominate MSE and the two metrics can rank models differently. **R² = 0** means \"explains none beyond the mean,\" NOT \"all residuals zero\" (that is R² = 1).\n- **When linear models fail**: cannot represent U-shaped/non-monotone relationships with a single raw term, disconnected/clustered decision regions, or interaction effects without engineered features. Logistic regression on raw features gives a **linear decision boundary**; adding interactions/polynomials/splines can curve it. **More data does not fix high bias** from an inappropriate model class.\n- **Regularization intuition**: add a coefficient penalty to the RSS objective to discourage large weights → accept higher training error for lower variance / better generalization (bias–variance tradeoff). **Ridge (L₂)** shrinks toward zero, keeps all features, shares weight among correlated ones; **Lasso (L₁)** can set coefficients exactly to zero (feature selection / sparsity) but its selection among correlated features is unstable. Larger λ ⇒ stronger penalty, less flexibility, more bias (too large ⇒ underfitting); choose λ by **cross-validation / validation set**, never by training error (which favors no penalty). A zero/near-zero coefficient does **not** prove no population relationship, and shrinkage does **not** confer causal meaning.", "traps": "- **Inverted-logic questions (8 of 36):** the Answer Key reads \"**True = incorrect; False = accurate**,\" so a **True** verdict means the statement is *wrong* and **False** means the statement is *correct*. Read the polarity line first. Inverted here: **G1Q3, G1Q4, G2Q2, G3Q3, G4Q1, G5Q1, G5Q2, G6Q1.**\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices** — the choice literally starts *\"The following claim is false: [an accurate statement].\"* The embedded statement is true, so asserting it is false is itself incorrect. In **normal** polarity these are keyed **False** (G5Q3A, G6Q2A, G6Q3A). In **inverted** polarity these are keyed **True (incorrect)** (G1Q3A, G3Q3A, G4Q1A). Parse the inner claim, judge its truth, then negate — and re-negate again for inverted polarity.\n- **Magnitude ≠ importance; \"always\" is a red flag.** Choices claiming a bigger coefficient is \"always more important,\" a term \"always\" reduces error/improves OOS, or added flexibility \"guarantees\" zero error are keyed false (or \"incorrect\" when inverted). Comparability requires standardized/same-scale features.\n- **Multicollinearity myths.** It does NOT (a) bias coefficients, (b) violate a core OLS independence assumption, (c) force R²/F toward zero, (d) create serial correlation, or (e) get eliminated by standardizing or by adding an interaction term. It only inflates coefficient variance; a unique solution fails only under *exact* linear dependence.\n- **\"Still a linear model.\"** Adding X², X₁X₂, or other transforms keeps the model linear in coefficients — choices claiming it \"is no longer a linear model\" or \"changes the OLS objective / creates local optima / non-convexity\" are false. OLS RSS is convex.\n- **In-sample fit ≠ truth.** Low training error, near-zero mean residual, or perfect training fit never proves correct specification or good generalization — always evaluate out-of-sample (time-aware for overlapping/serial data).", "references": "1. **ISLR — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning* (James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani):** ch.3 (Linear Regression) — §3.1 simple regression, intercept, residual sum-to-zero; §3.2 multiple regression and coefficient interpretation; §3.3.2 extensions (polynomial regression, interaction terms, removing the additive assumption); §3.3.3 potential problems (non-linearity, correlation of errors, non-constant variance, outliers, collinearity/VIF). ch.4 (§4.3, logistic regression and its linear decision boundary). ch.6 (§6.2, Ridge and Lasso shrinkage). ch.2 (§2.2, bias-variance tradeoff, MSE). ch.5 (cross-validation for tuning).\n2. **ESL — *The Elements of Statistical Learning* (Hastie, Tibshirani, Friedman):** ch.3 (linear methods, subset selection, ridge/lasso, Gauss-Markov / BLUE), ch.5 (basis expansions and orthogonal polynomials), ch.7 (model assessment, bias-variance).\n3. **scikit-learn User Guide — `linear_model`:** `LinearRegression` (OLS), `Ridge` (L₂), `Lasso` (L₁), `ElasticNet`; `preprocessing.StandardScaler` and `PolynomialFeatures` (standardization and polynomial expansion); \"Metrics and scoring\" (MSE vs. MAE, regression metrics).\n4. **OLS assumptions & concepts:** linearity, zero conditional mean E[ε\\|X]=0 (unbiasedness), homoscedasticity, no perfect multicollinearity, independence of errors; Gauss-Markov theorem / BLUE (normality not required); omitted-variable bias; multicollinearity vs. serial correlation; Q-Q plots and residual diagnostics.\n5. **Regularization & robustness:** Ridge/Lasso bias-variance mechanics, sparsity and selection instability under correlated features; robust regression (Huber loss) and Student-t error models for heavy-tailed residuals.", "bookNotes": [{"book": "ISLP", "title": "ISLP Section 2.2 — Assessing Model Accuracy", "file": "references/ISLP/Ch02-2_Assessing_Model_Accuracy.md", "md": "# ISLP Section 2.2 — Assessing Model Accuracy\n\n*Source: ISLP, PDF pages 38–49 (book pp. 28–39). Covers 2.2.1 Measuring Quality of Fit,\n2.2.2 Bias-Variance Trade-Off, 2.2.3 The Classification Setting.*\n\n> ML4T exam relevance: core of model evaluation — MSE, train vs. test error, overfitting,\n> bias-variance decomposition, Bayes error, KNN flexibility. → ML Domain 8 (Model Evaluation) and\n> the bias-variance framing behind every learner in ML Domain 4–7.\n\n## 2.2.1 Measuring the Quality of Fit (regression)\n- **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**: `MSE = (1/n) Σ_i ( y_i − f̂(x_i) )²`  (Eq. 2.5).\n  Small when predictions are close to truth.\n- **Training MSE** uses the data the model was fit on. We *do not* care about it per se — we care\n  about **test MSE** = `Ave( y_0 − f̂(x_0) )²` on previously unseen (x_0, y_0) (Eq. 2.6).\n  - Example intuition: predicting *tomorrow's* stock price, not last week's; predicting diabetes\n    for *future* patients, not the training patients.\n- **Key pitfall**: minimizing training MSE does **not** guarantee low test MSE. Many methods fit\n  coefficients specifically to minimize training MSE, driving it small while test MSE stays large.\n- **Flexibility vs. error** (fundamental pattern, holds for any dataset/method):\n  - Training MSE **decreases monotonically** as flexibility (degrees of freedom) increases.\n  - Test MSE is **U-shaped**: falls, then rises.\n- **Overfitting**: small training MSE but large test MSE — the method chases random patterns in\n  training data that don't exist in test data. Defined specifically as: a *less flexible* model\n  would have given a smaller test MSE. (Training MSE is almost always < test MSE regardless.)\n- **Degrees of freedom** = a summary of a curve's flexibility (linear regression = 2 df, most\n  restrictive). **Cross-validation** (Ch. 5) estimates test MSE using only training data.\n\n## 2.2.2 The Bias-Variance Trade-Off\n- The U-shape in test MSE comes from decomposing expected test MSE at a point x_0 into **three\n  non-negative** parts (Eq. 2.7):\n  `E( y_0 − f̂(x_0) )² = Var(f̂(x_0)) + [Bias(f̂(x_0))]² + Var(ε)`\n  = **Variance + squared Bias + irreducible error**.\n- Expected test MSE can **never** be below `Var(ε)` (the irreducible error).\n- **Variance**: how much f̂ would change with a different training set. More flexible → higher\n  variance (a wiggly curve shifts a lot if one point moves).\n- **Bias**: error from approximating a complex reality with a simpler model. More flexible → lower\n  bias. (Linear regression is badly biased when true f is non-linear.)\n- **General rule**: as flexibility ↑, variance ↑ and bias ↓. Early on bias falls faster than\n  variance rises (test MSE ↓); later variance dominates (test MSE ↑).\n- **The trade-off**: good test performance needs *both* low variance and low squared bias. Easy to\n  get one at the cost of the other (interpolating curve = zero bias/high variance; horizontal line\n  = low variance/high bias). Finding both-low is the challenge — a recurring theme of the book.\n\n## 2.2.3 The Classification Setting\n- **Training error rate**: `(1/n) Σ_i I(y_i ≠ ŷ_i)` (Eq. 2.8) — fraction misclassified;\n  `I(·)` is an indicator (1 if wrong, 0 if right).\n- **Test error rate**: `Ave( I(y_0 ≠ ŷ_0) )` (Eq. 2.9). A good classifier minimizes this.\n- **Bayes classifier**: assign x_0 to the **most likely class**, i.e. the class j maximizing the\n  conditional probability `Pr(Y = j | X = x_0)` (Eq. 2.10). Two-class: predict class 1 if\n  `Pr(Y=1|X=x_0) > 0.5`.\n  - **Bayes decision boundary**: the set of points where the probability is exactly 50%.\n  - **Bayes error rate** (lowest possible test error): `1 − E( max_j Pr(Y=j|X) )` (Eq. 2.11);\n    at a point it is `1 − max_j Pr(Y=j|X=x_0)`. Analogous to irreducible error. > 0 when classes\n    overlap (example dataset: ≈ 0.13). The Bayes classifier is an **unattainable gold standard**\n    (needs the true conditional distribution, which is unknown for real data).\n- **K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) classifier**: estimates `Pr(Y=j|X=x_0) = (1/K) Σ_{i∈N_0} I(y_i=j)`\n  over the K training points N_0 nearest to x_0 (Eq. 2.12); predicts the majority class among them.\n  - Can get surprisingly close to the Bayes boundary (e.g., K=10 test error 0.1363 vs. Bayes 0.1304).\n  - **K controls flexibility**: small K (K=1) = very flexible, low bias / high variance, jagged\n    boundary, training error = 0 but high test error. Large K (K=100) = inflexible, high bias /\n    low variance, near-linear boundary. **1/K** is the flexibility axis.\n  - Same U-shaped test-error pattern as regression; no strong link between training and test error.\n\n## One-line takeaways\n- Choose the model that minimizes **test** error, not training error.\n- The **right level of flexibility** is critical and dataset-dependent; the bias-variance trade-off\n  produces the U-shaped test-error curve. Cross-validation (Ch. 5) is how you find it in practice.\n"}, {"book": "ISLP", "title": "ISLP Sections 3.1–3.3 — Linear Regression", "file": "references/ISLP/Ch03-1to3_Linear_Regression.md", "md": "# ISLP Sections 3.1–3.3 — Linear Regression\n\n*Source: ISLP, PDF pages 80–119 (book pp. 71–110). 3.1 Simple LR, 3.2 Multiple LR,\n3.3 Other Considerations.*\n\n> ML4T exam relevance: the canonical parametric supervised learner — coefficients, fit metrics,\n> inference, and the assumption failures that break it. → ML Domain 4 (Linear Models) and\n> ML Domain 8 (Model Evaluation: R², RSE, F-test).\n\n## 3.1 Simple Linear Regression  (one predictor)\n- **Model**: `Y ≈ β0 + β1 X`. β0 = intercept (E[Y] at X=0), β1 = slope (avg. change in Y per\n  1-unit rise in X). Prediction: `ŷ = β̂0 + β̂1 x`.\n- **Residual**: `e_i = y_i − ŷ_i`. **RSS** = Σ e_i² (Eq. 3.3).\n- **Least squares estimates** (minimize RSS, Eq. 3.4):\n  `β̂1 = Σ(x_i−x̄)(y_i−ȳ) / Σ(x_i−x̄)²`,  `β̂0 = ȳ − β̂1 x̄`.\n\n### 3.1.2 Accuracy of the coefficient estimates\n- **Population regression line** = true best linear approx (unobserved); **least squares line** =\n  our estimate from one sample. Different samples → different lines, but least squares estimates\n  are **unbiased** (average over many samples = truth).\n- **Standard errors** (Eq. 3.8): `SE(β̂1)² = σ²/Σ(x_i−x̄)²`,\n  `SE(β̂0)² = σ²[1/n + x̄²/Σ(x_i−x̄)²]`, where σ² = Var(ε). SE(β̂1) is *smaller* when x's are more\n  spread out (more leverage to estimate slope). Assumes errors uncorrelated with common variance.\n- **Residual standard error** estimates σ: `RSE = √(RSS/(n−2))`.\n- **95% confidence interval** ≈ `β̂1 ± 2·SE(β̂1)` (Eq. 3.9).\n- **Hypothesis test**: H0: β1 = 0 (no relationship) vs Ha: β1 ≠ 0. **t-statistic**\n  `t = β̂1 / SE(β̂1)` (Eq. 3.14); # of SDs β̂1 is from 0. Under H0, t ~ t-dist with n−2 df.\n  **p-value** = prob. of seeing |t| this large by chance; small p → reject H0 (typical cutoffs 5%/1%).\n\n### 3.1.3 Accuracy of the model (goodness of fit)\n- **RSE** = `√(RSS/(n−2))` (Eq. 3.15) — absolute lack-of-fit, in units of Y (avg deviation from\n  the regression line).\n- **R² statistic** (Eq. 3.17): `R² = (TSS − RSS)/TSS = 1 − RSS/TSS`, where **TSS = Σ(y_i−ȳ)²**.\n  Proportion of variance in Y explained by X; always in [0,1], scale-free. In *simple* regression,\n  **R² = r²** (squared correlation `Cor(X,Y)`, Eq. 3.18). What counts as \"good\" R² is domain-dependent.\n\n## 3.2 Multiple Linear Regression  (p predictors)\n- **Model** (Eq. 3.19): `Y = β0 + β1X1 + β2X2 + … + βpXp + ε`. Interpret βj = avg effect on Y of a\n  1-unit rise in Xj **holding all other predictors fixed**. Fit by least squares (Eq. 3.22),\n  minimizing RSS (matrix algebra; software).\n- **Simple ≠ multiple coefficients**: correlated predictors change the story. Advertising example:\n  `newspaper` is significant *alone* but ~0 with TV+radio in the model — it was a **surrogate** for\n  radio (radio & newspaper correlated 0.35). (\"Shark attacks vs. ice cream\" — confounder = temperature.)\n\n### 3.2.2 Four key questions\n1. **Is at least one predictor useful?** Test H0: β1=…=βp=0 with the **F-statistic**\n   `F = [(TSS−RSS)/p] / [RSS/(n−p−1)]` (Eq. 3.23). F ≈ 1 under H0; F ≫ 1 → reject.\n   - F adjusts for p; individual t-p-values do **not** — with large p, ~5% of predictors look\n     significant by chance, so rely on F for the global test. (For a subset of q coefficients use\n     Eq. 3.24; each individual t-test equals the F-test dropping that one variable, i.e. its\n     partial effect.) If **p > n**, least squares can't be fit — see Ch. 6 (forward selection).\n2. **Which predictors matter? (variable selection)** 2^p possible subsets → infeasible for large p.\n   Classical automated searches: **forward selection** (start null, add best-RSS variable each step;\n   greedy, always usable), **backward selection** (start full, drop largest p-value; needs p<n),\n   **mixed selection** (add forward, drop if p-value rises). Model-quality criteria: Mallow's Cp,\n   AIC, BIC, adjusted R² (Ch. 6).\n3. **Model fit**: RSE and R² (here **R² = Cor(Y,Ŷ)²**). **R² always rises when adding any\n   predictor** (RSS falls on training data) → don't use raw R² to compare models of different size;\n   a tiny bump (e.g., adding newspaper) signals the variable is useless and may overfit.\n   `RSE = √(RSS/(n−p−1))` (Eq. 3.25) — can *increase* with more predictors if RSS barely drops.\n4. **Predictions** carry 3 uncertainties: (a) coefficient estimation error → **reducible**,\n   (b) **model bias** (linearity is an approximation), (c) irreducible error ε.\n   - **Confidence interval**: uncertainty about the *average* response f(X).\n   - **Prediction interval**: uncertainty about an *individual* Y; **always wider** (adds\n     irreducible error ε).\n\n## 3.3 Other Considerations\n\n### 3.3.1 Qualitative predictors\n- Two levels → one **dummy/indicator variable** (0/1; a.k.a. one-hot encoding). Coding is arbitrary\n  and doesn't change predictions, only coefficient interpretation.\n- >2 levels → **(#levels − 1) dummies**; the omitted level is the **baseline**. Test overall effect\n  with an F-test on the dummies (coding-independent).\n\n### 3.3.2 Extensions (relaxing key assumptions)\n- Standard model assumes **additive** and **linear** relationships.\n- **Interaction terms** (remove additivity): add `β3·X1·X2` (Eq. 3.31). Then effect of X1 depends on\n  X2. Advertising TV×radio interaction: R² 96.8% vs 89.7% → strong synergy. **Hierarchical\n  principle**: if you keep an interaction, keep its main effects even if their p-values are large.\n- **Polynomial regression** (remove linearity): include `X, X², …` as predictors (Eq. 3.36); still a\n  *linear model* in the coefficients. Higher degree → risk of over-wiggly fit.\n\n### 3.3.3 Potential problems (six)\n1. **Non-linearity** — detect via **residual plots** (residuals vs fitted); a pattern (U-shape) =\n   non-linear. Fix: transform predictors (log X, √X, X²).\n2. **Correlated error terms** — common in **time series** (adjacent errors track). Understated SEs →\n   too-narrow CIs, too-small p-values, false confidence.\n3. **Non-constant error variance (heteroscedasticity)** — funnel shape in residual plot. Fix:\n   transform Y (log Y, √Y) or use **weighted least squares** (weights ∝ 1/variance).\n4. **Outliers** — unusual **y** given x. Use **studentized residuals**; |value| > 3 flags an outlier.\n   Inflates RSE and lowers R² even if the line barely moves.\n5. **High-leverage points** — unusual **x**. Have large impact on the fit. **Leverage statistic**\n   h_i (Eq. 3.37), between 1/n and 1, average = (p+1)/n; a value ≫ (p+1)/n is high leverage. Worst\n   case = high leverage + outlier.\n6. **Collinearity** — two+ predictors closely related. Inflates SE(β̂j), shrinks t-statistics,\n   reduces power → may fail to reject H0. Detect: correlation matrix, or **VIF** (variance inflation\n   factor) = `1/(1 − R²_{Xj|X−j})`; VIF ≥ 5–10 is problematic (min = 1). Fix: drop one collinear\n   variable, or combine them into one.\n\n## 3.4 Marketing plan (worked answers, Advertising data)\nRelationship exists (F-test p≈0); ~90% variance explained (R²=0.897, RSE=1.69 ≈12% error); only\nTV & radio matter (newspaper CI includes 0, no collinearity — VIFs ≈1); use prediction interval for\nan individual, CI for the average; residual plot reveals non-linearity; TV×radio interaction shows\nsynergy.\n"}, {"book": "ISLP", "title": "ISLP Section 3.5 — Comparison of Linear Regression with K-Nearest Neighbors", "file": "references/ISLP/Ch03-5_LinReg_vs_KNN.md", "md": "# ISLP Section 3.5 — Comparison of Linear Regression with K-Nearest Neighbors\n\n*Source: ISLP, PDF pages 120–124 (book pp. 111–115).*\n\n> ML4T exam relevance: parametric vs non-parametric trade-off, KNN regression, curse of\n> dimensionality — recurring exam themes. → ML Domain 4 (Linear Models) and ML Domain 3\n> (Instance-based / KNN), with bias-variance framing (Domain 8).\n\n## Parametric vs. non-parametric\n- **Linear regression = parametric**: assumes a functional form for f(X) (linear). Advantages:\n  easy to fit (few coefficients), interpretable coefficients, significance tests/p-values available.\n  Disadvantage: strong form assumption — if the true f is far from linear, prediction is poor.\n- **KNN regression = non-parametric**: no assumed form for f(X); more flexible.\n\n## KNN regression\n- For a value K and a prediction point x_0: find the K training points **N_0** closest to x_0, then\n  predict the **average** of their responses:\n  `f̂(x_0) = (1/K) Σ_{x_i ∈ N_0} y_i`.\n- Fit is a **step function**. K=1 interpolates training data exactly (rough steps); larger K averages\n  more points → smoother, less variable fit.\n- **K controls the bias-variance trade-off**: small K = flexible, **low bias / high variance**\n  (a region's prediction rests on one point); large K = smooth, **high bias / low variance** (averaging\n  can mask real structure). Choose optimal K via test-error estimation (cross-validation, Ch. 5).\n\n## When does each win?\n- **Linear regression wins when the true f is (close to) linear** — KNN pays a variance cost with no\n  offsetting bias reduction, so it is slightly inferior (esp. small K).\n- **KNN wins when f is substantially non-linear** — with p=1 it can massively beat linear regression;\n  as non-linearity grows, KNN test MSE barely changes while linear regression's test MSE rises sharply.\n\n## The catch: dimensionality and the curse of dimensionality\n- The KNN advantage in non-linear settings **collapses as p grows**. Example: strongly non-linear in\n  one variable plus added noise predictors — KNN beats linear regression at p=1,2; mixed at p=3;\n  linear regression superior at p ≥ 4. Adding dimensions barely hurt linear regression but caused a\n  >10× increase in KNN's MSE.\n- **Curse of dimensionality**: in high dimensions the K \"nearest\" neighbors can be very far from x_0\n  (spreading n observations across many dimensions ≈ a reduction in effective sample size), so KNN\n  predicts poorly.\n- **Rule of thumb**: parametric methods tend to beat non-parametric ones when there are **few\n  observations per predictor**.\n\n## Interpretability\n- Even when KNN's test MSE is slightly lower, linear regression may be preferred for\n  **interpretability** — a few coefficients, with p-values, vs. an opaque local-averaging model.\n"}]}, {"part": "ML", "num": "05", "name": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "cheatsheet": "- **KNN core idea:** predict a query from the *k* stored training observations closest to it under a chosen distance metric. Classification = majority (or weighted) vote of neighbor labels; regression = (weighted) average of neighbor targets. It is **non-parametric** — no global equation is fit; predictions are local.\n- **Distance & similarity (G1):**\n  - Ordinary **Euclidean distance** `d(x,y)=√Σ(xᵢ−yᵢ)²` uses raw numerical differences and does **not** auto-correct for units. A feature with a much larger numerical range **dominates** the distance and drives neighbor selection.\n  - **Manhattan** distance `Σ|xᵢ−yᵢ|` sums absolute differences (no squaring) but still uses raw magnitudes → switching metric does **not** fix scale imbalance.\n  - **Feature scaling** (z-score `(x−μ)/σ`, min-max, rank/percentile) makes contributions comparable. Fit scaler on **training data only** (μ, σ, min/max, ranks) → apply to val/test/future to avoid **leakage**.\n  - Different metrics can rank the *same* candidates differently; the metric is a **hyperparameter** chosen with train/validation, never the test set. Scaling improves comparability but does **not** make distinct metrics equivalent, nor make an irrelevant/identifier feature meaningful.\n  - **Cosine similarity** is invariant to scaling the *whole vector* by a positive scalar, but **not** to rescaling individual features.\n- **Choice of k / bias–variance (G2):**\n  - **Small k (k=1):** low bias, **high variance**; jagged boundary / Voronoi cells; ~0 training error; overfits noise.\n  - **Large k:** high bias, low variance; smoother boundary; can **oversmooth** real local structure (underfit). At **k=N** every query returns the global mean (regression) / majority class (classification) — feature-independent, max bias.\n  - Capacity heuristic: **DoF ≈ N/k** (inverse to k). Training error alone never selects k; use **validation / cross-validation**. Test error is typically **U-shaped** in k.\n  - Even k is **valid** for regression (averaging targets, no tie-breaking vote needed).\n- **Noise & local structure (G3):** a single mislabeled/erroneous neighbor can dominate at k=1; larger k dilutes it (one of k votes) but not unconditionally (numerous/clustered/boundary outliers). Outlier influence is **strictly local** (unlike a high-leverage point in OLS). Neighbor selection uses **feature distance, not target extremeness**. Data cleaning helps a lazy learner, but not all outliers are errors (real crashes/shocks carry signal).\n- **Curse of dimensionality (G4):** adding irrelevant dimensions injects noise into distances; data becomes **sparse** relative to volume (to cover fraction q in p dims, side length r=q^(1/p)→1); **distance concentration** shrinks (Dmax−Dmin)/Dmin→0 so \"near\" vs \"far\" loses contrast. Scaling does **not** cure this; feature selection / dimensionality reduction (PCA) can — but PCA fit on the test period still **leaks**, and reduction is not guaranteed to help.\n- **Computational tradeoffs (G5):** KNN is a **lazy learner** — training ≈ O(1) (just store data); brute-force inference ≈ **O(N·p)** per query and memory ≈ **O(N·p)** (retains all instances). k-d / ball trees give ~O(log N) only in **low** dimensions; they degrade to O(N) once p ≳ 20–30. Eager models (linear/logistic) pay upfront but infer in ~O(p). k = neighbors used, **not** the count stored.\n- **Trading suitability (G6):** wrong for ultra-low-latency/HFT (per-query search blows the microsecond budget); **ANN** methods (HNSW, LSH) trade recall for speed to make it viable. Fine for low-frequency/offline tasks (daily TAA, regime detection, illiquid pricing) and offers **interpretability** (\"today resembles these historical dates\"). Appending data is cheap but does **not** solve concept drift — stale windows must be pruned. Large neighbor distance is itself a **signal** the query is unlike history (supports abstention).", "traps": "- **Inverted-logic questions (8 of 36):** the Answer Key reads \"**True = incorrect; False = accurate**,\" so a \"True\" verdict means the statement is *wrong* and \"False\" means it is *correct*. Read the polarity line first. Inverted here: **G1Q2, G1Q3, G1Q4, G2Q1, G2Q2, G4Q6, G5Q1, G5Q5.**\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices:** a choice wraps an inner claim in \"The following claim is false: …\" or \"It is incorrect to conclude that …\". Parse the inner claim, judge *its* truth, then negate. In **normal**-polarity questions the key marks an accurate embedded claim **False** (G2Q3A, G5Q6A) and an inaccurate one **True** (G6Q4A). An embedded claim also appears inside the **inverted** question **G1Q2A** (\"The following claim is false: …\"), where the negation compounds with the inverted polarity — resolve the inner claim first, then apply \"True = incorrect.\"\n- **Scale ≠ meaning ≠ relevance:** standardizing puts features on comparable scales but does **not** make an arbitrary identifier meaningful, guarantee equal predictive value, or cure the curse of dimensionality. Frequent distractor: \"scaling proves every feature now contributes meaningfully\" → false.\n- **Changing the metric is not a scaling fix:** Manhattan (or any raw-magnitude metric) still lets a billions-vs-units feature dominate. Distractor that \"Manhattan treats all features equally regardless of units\" is false.\n- **k=N ≠ zero variance / \"single best neighbor is most stable\":** k=N gives max bias but still sample-dependent (not literally zero variance); k=1 *maximizes* variance (a common reversed distractor claims k=1 minimizes it).\n- **Lazy ≠ fast in production:** \"no training\" does not imply cheap inference; trees do **not** rescue high-dimensional search; storing raw data is **more** memory than a parameter vector. Appending data does not defeat concept drift.", "references": "1. **ISLR — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning* (James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani):** §2.2.3 (K-Nearest Neighbors — classifier & regression, k and the bias-variance tradeoff, decision-boundary flexibility, KNN in high dimensions), §2.2 (assessing model accuracy, U-shaped test error), §5 (cross-validation for selecting k), ch.12 (unsupervised preprocessing/PCA).\n2. **ESL — *The Elements of Statistical Learning* (Hastie, Tibshirani, Friedman):** ch.13 (prototype methods & nearest-neighbor classifiers, edited/condensed NN, robustness), §2.5 (local methods in high dimensions, curse of dimensionality).\n3. **scikit-learn User Guide — \"Nearest Neighbors\":** distance metrics (Minkowski p=1/2, Euclidean/Manhattan), `weights='uniform'` vs `'distance'`, KDTree/BallTree vs brute force and algorithmic complexity (train O(1), query cost, high-dimension degradation); \"Preprocessing data\" (StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler, QuantileTransformer); \"Common pitfalls / data leakage\" (fit scalers/PCA on training only).\n4. **Concept references (no page numbers):** choice of k / bias–variance tradeoff and DoF≈N/k capacity heuristic; feature scaling for distance; curse of dimensionality — sparsity (r=q^{1/p}) and distance concentration ((Dmax−Dmin)/Dmin→0; Beyer et al., \"When Is 'Nearest Neighbor' Meaningful?\"); lazy vs. eager learning and train-vs-inference cost.\n5. **Approximate nearest neighbor for low-latency retrieval:** HNSW (Malkov & Yashunin) and Locality-Sensitive Hashing — trading recall for sub-millisecond search when exact KNN is infeasible.\n6. **\"Deep Learning: Foundations and Concepts\" (Bishop & Bishop), ch.1:** data preprocessing / feature scaling context cited in the source answer key for G1Q4.", "bookNotes": [{"book": "ISLP", "title": "ISLP Section 8.1 — The Basics of Decision Trees", "file": "references/ISLP/Ch08-1_Decision_Trees.md", "md": "# ISLP Section 8.1 — The Basics of Decision Trees\n\n*Source: ISLP, PDF pages 340–349 (book pp. 333–342). 8.1.1 Regression Trees, 8.1.2 Classification\nTrees, 8.1.3 Trees vs Linear Models, 8.1.4 Advantages/Disadvantages.*\n\n> ML4T exam relevance: CART fundamentals — splitting criteria, pruning, and the high-variance\n> weakness that motivates ensembles. → ML Domain 5 (Decision Trees / CART).\n\n## Terminology\n- **Terminal nodes / leaves**: the final regions R_1,…,R_J. **Internal nodes**: split points.\n  **Branches**: segments connecting nodes. Trees are drawn upside down (leaves at bottom).\n\n## 8.1.1 Regression Trees\n- **Two-step build**:\n  1. Partition predictor space into J distinct, non-overlapping regions R_1,…,R_J (boxes).\n  2. Predict the **mean** of the training responses in the region an observation falls into.\n- **Goal**: choose boxes minimizing `RSS = Σ_j Σ_{i∈R_j} (y_i − ŷ_{R_j})²` (Eq. 8.1), where ŷ_{R_j}\n  is the region mean. Considering all partitions is infeasible.\n- **Recursive binary splitting** (top-down, greedy): at each step pick predictor X_j and cutpoint s\n  splitting into `{X | X_j < s}` and `{X | X_j ≥ s}` giving the **greatest RSS reduction** (Eq. 8.2–8.3).\n  - *Top-down*: starts with all data in one region. *Greedy*: best split at the current step only,\n    no look-ahead. Continue until a stopping rule (e.g., no node > 5 observations).\n\n### Tree Pruning\n- A large tree overfits (low bias, high variance, poor test performance). Stopping early on an RSS\n  threshold is too short-sighted (a weak split may enable a great later split).\n- **Better**: grow a large tree T_0, then **prune** back to a subtree.\n- **Cost complexity pruning** (a.k.a. weakest-link pruning): index subtrees by tuning parameter\n  **α ≥ 0**, minimizing `Σ_{m=1}^{|T|} Σ_{i:x_i∈R_m} (y_i − ŷ_{R_m})² + α|T|` (Eq. 8.4).\n  - |T| = number of terminal nodes. α trades complexity vs training fit. α=0 → full tree T_0; larger\n    α → smaller subtree (penalty per leaf). Analogous to the **lasso** penalty.\n  - Branches prune in a nested, predictable way as α increases. **Select α via cross-validation**\n    (Algorithm 8.1: grow → prune to subtree sequence → K-fold CV to pick α → refit on full data).\n\n## 8.1.2 Classification Trees\n- Predicts the **most commonly occurring class** in a region (also report class proportions).\n- RSS can't be used for splits. Split-quality measures on p̂_mk (proportion of class k in region m):\n  - **Classification error rate**: `E = 1 − max_k(p̂_mk)` (Eq. 8.5). Not sensitive enough for growing.\n  - **Gini index**: `G = Σ_k p̂_mk(1 − p̂_mk)` (Eq. 8.6) — total variance across classes; small when a\n    node is **pure** (p̂'s near 0 or 1). A **node-purity** measure.\n  - **Entropy**: `D = − Σ_k p̂_mk log p̂_mk` (Eq. 8.7) — also small for pure nodes; numerically similar\n    to Gini.\n  - **Growing**: use Gini or entropy (more purity-sensitive). **Pruning**: any of the three, but\n    classification error rate preferred if final prediction accuracy is the goal.\n- Handles **qualitative predictors** natively (split assigns some categories to each branch).\n- Splits can yield two leaves with the **same predicted class** — still worthwhile because they\n  **increase node purity** (improves Gini/entropy even if classification error is unchanged),\n  raising prediction confidence.\n\n## 8.1.3 Trees vs. Linear Models\n- Linear model: `f(X) = β0 + Σ_j X_j β_j` (Eq. 8.8). Tree: `f(X) = Σ_m c_m · 1(X ∈ R_m)` (Eq. 8.9).\n- **Which is better depends on the true f**: linear structure → linear regression wins; highly\n  non-linear/complex → trees win. Choose by estimating test error (CV / validation set). Trees may\n  also be preferred for interpretability/visualization.\n\n## 8.1.4 Advantages & Disadvantages\n- **Pros**: very easy to explain (easier than linear regression); mirror human decision-making;\n  graphical, interpretable by non-experts (if small); handle qualitative predictors with **no dummy\n  variables**.\n- **Cons**: generally **lower predictive accuracy** than other methods; **non-robust** — a small\n  data change can drastically change the tree (**high variance**).\n- **Fix**: aggregate many trees via **bagging, random forests, boosting** (Section 8.2) → large\n  accuracy gains.\n"}]}, {"part": "ML", "num": "06", "name": "Decision Trees", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "cheatsheet": "- **Prediction flow (G1):** A single decision tree routes each observation down **one** root-to-leaf path determined by the split rules; the prediction comes from **that one leaf only**, never by averaging/combining all leaves (that is ensembles). Nodes off the observation's path are not evaluated for that observation. A **leaf is terminal** (a prediction), not another split; an **internal node** applies a feature-based rule.\n  - **Regression leaf** → prediction = **mean training response** in that leaf. **Classification leaf** → majority class / class-probabilities. A leaf may hold one *or many* training observations.\n  - A root-to-leaf path = a **conjunction of if-then conditions** → the source of tree interpretability.\n- **Splitting criteria (G2, G6):** CART is **greedy, top-down, recursive binary splitting** — at each node it does a *local exhaustive search* over every feature and threshold and picks the split with the **largest immediate impurity/error reduction**. Greedy = no lookahead; **locally optimal ≠ globally optimal**. Finding the truly optimal tree is NP-complete.\n  - **Classification impurity:** Gini `G(p)=2p(1−p)` and cross-entropy `H(p)=−p ln p−(1−p)ln(1−p)`. Both = **0 at pure nodes** (p=0 or 1), **maximum at p=0.5**. Classification error `E=1−max_k p̂` is **less sensitive** to class-proportion changes → used for pruning, less for growing.\n  - Weighted child impurity **can never exceed** parent impurity (concavity) → impurity reduction ≥ 0 (may equal 0).\n- **Complexity & overfitting (G3):** Depth / number of leaves = model capacity. Unrestricted growth → leaves down to 1 observation → **0 training error but high variance / poor generalization** (memorizes noise, especially in low signal-to-noise financial data). Shallow/over-constrained → **underfitting (high bias)**. Bias–variance is the governing tradeoff.\n- **Hyperparameters as controls (G4):** `max_depth` (caps root-to-leaf path length, uniform), `min_samples_leaf` (hard lower bound on leaf size → variance reduction), `min_samples_split` (min samples to *attempt* a split — does **not** guarantee leaf size), `max_leaf_nodes` (best-first / impurity-priority growth → asymmetric tree). These are **structural pre-pruning constraints**, distinct from L1/L2 penalties (they restrict allowed structures but keep the same impurity criterion). Cost-complexity pruning (α) is the penalized-objective analog.\n- **Stability & sensitivity (G5):** Individual trees are **high-variance / unstable** — small data changes (add/remove a few rows, a near-tie between candidate splits) can flip an early split and cascade through all downstream branches. Same hyperparameters do **not** guarantee same tree across different samples. Motivates **bagging / random forests** (average many trees to lower variance). Equal aggregate accuracy does not imply stable per-case predictions.\n- **Strengths (G6):** Handle **nonlinearity & interactions** via hierarchical conditional splits (no manual polynomial/spline/interaction terms); **no feature scaling needed** — splits depend on *ordering*, so any **strictly monotonic transform** (log, z-score, min-max) preserves the candidate partitions and the fitted tree (unlike distance-based KNN/SVM); **interpretable** if-then paths; implicit feature selection; relatively robust to feature-space outliers.\n- **Weaknesses / failure modes (G7):** **Axis-aligned splits only** → a diagonal/oblique boundary needs a many-split \"staircase\" approximation; **cannot extrapolate** beyond training-response range (leaf outputs are bounded constants); **piecewise-constant** → abrupt jumps at thresholds; **greedy** can miss good multi-level structure if the first split's immediate gain is weak; **high variance / instability**; **overfitting** without constraints. A learned threshold is predictive, **not causal or permanent** (regime shift breaks it).", "traps": "- **Inverted-logic questions (12 of 42):** the Answer Key flips scoring — \"**True = incorrect; False = accurate**.\" So a verdict of **False actually means the statement is correct**. Read the polarity line first. Inverted here: **G1Q1, G2Q5, G2Q6, G3Q3, G4Q1, G4Q2, G4Q4, G4Q6, G6Q2, G6Q4, G7Q3, G7Q5.** (Verdicts below are shown as `True (incorrect)` / `False (accurate)` for these.)\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices:** a choice asserts that an accurate statement is *false/incorrect*, so the whole choice is false.\n  - **G1Q1 A** (\"The following claim is false: the observation first follows momentum>2%…\") — inner claim is accurate, so the choice is incorrect → **True (incorrect)** under inverted scoring.\n  - **G4Q3 A** (\"The following claim is false: max depth and min leaf size both influence complexity\") — inner claim accurate → whole choice false → **False** (normal scoring).\n  - **G7Q4 A** (\"It is incorrect to conclude that trees struggle with smooth/diagonal boundaries…\") — inner conclusion is accurate, so calling it incorrect is **False** (normal scoring).\n  - In inverted questions **G4Q4E, G4Q6A, G6Q2, G6Q4** the key literally writes \"The statement is accurate → marked False\" — read the polarity, not the word.\n- **\"Single tree averages all paths / combines all leaves\"** is always wrong for one tree (that describes ensembles). One observation → one path → one leaf. (G1Q1C, G1Q4A.)\n- **Scaling/standardization is NOT required for trees** — repeated distractor. Monotonic transforms only change reported threshold numbers, not the partitions/structure. Claiming scaling is *mandatory* is false; claiming a monotonic transform *stabilizes* the tree is also false (G5Q5B, G5Q6B). Trees are the contrast to distance-based KNN/SVM.\n- **\"Greedy = globally optimal / looks ahead\"** is always wrong (G2Q3B, G2Q6B, G4Q6-related, G6Q5C, G6Q6E, G7Q5C). Greedy = best local split now, no lookahead.\n- **`min_samples_split` ≠ guaranteed leaf size:** a node of 10 (or 100) can split into children of 1 and 9 (99), so only `min_samples_leaf` bounds leaf size (G4Q4C, G4Q6C).\n- **Answer-key mismatch — G5Q5:** the key's letters are **shuffled relative to the choice text**. Verdicts below are mapped by **content** (the faithful reading) and flagged with a Note.", "references": "1. **ISLR — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning* (James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani), ch.8 (Tree-Based Methods):** §8.1 (regression & classification trees, recursive binary splitting, Gini/entropy/classification-error impurity, tree pruning / cost-complexity α, interpretability, axis-aligned splits and their limits); §8.2 (bagging, random forests — the variance-reduction motivation for tree instability).\n2. **ESL — *The Elements of Statistical Learning* (Hastie, Tibshirani, Friedman):** §9.2 (CART — greedy recursive splitting, impurity measures, cost-complexity pruning); ch.7 (bias–variance, model assessment); §8.7 & ch.15 (bagging, random forests).\n3. **scikit-learn User Guide — \"Decision Trees\":** mathematical formulation of splitting, `DecisionTreeClassifier`/`DecisionTreeRegressor`, hyperparameters (`max_depth`, `min_samples_split`, `min_samples_leaf`, `max_leaf_nodes`), advantages/disadvantages (no scaling needed, overfitting, instability), and \"Understanding the decision tree structure.\"\n4. **J. R. Quinlan — ID3/C4.5 (induction of decision trees):** information-gain / entropy-based splitting and greedy top-down tree induction; historical complement to CART's Gini/variance-reduction criteria.\n5. **ML4T course material (Georgia Tech):** random-tree construction and the \"correlation-of-feature\" split-selection detail (choosing a feature by correlation with the target and taking the mean of two random A-cut instances as the split value); axis-aligned partitioning and leaf-mean prediction.\n6. **Breiman, Friedman, Olshen & Stone — *Classification and Regression Trees* (CART, 1984):** the foundational reference for greedy binary splitting, impurity reduction, surrogate splits for missing values, and cost-complexity pruning.", "bookNotes": [{"book": "ISLP", "title": "ISLP Section 8.2 — Bagging, Random Forests, Boosting (and BART)", "file": "references/ISLP/Ch08-2_Bagging_RF_Boosting.md", "md": "# ISLP Section 8.2 — Bagging, Random Forests, Boosting (and BART)\n\n*Source: ISLP, PDF pages 350–361 (book pp. 343–354). 8.2.1 Bagging, 8.2.2 Random Forests,\n8.2.3 Boosting, 8.2.4 BART, 8.2.5 Summary.*\n\n> ML4T exam relevance: tree ensembles — the variance-reduction vs sequential-bias-reduction\n> distinction, key hyperparameters, and OOB error. → ML Domain 6 (Ensemble Learners: bagging,\n> random forests, boosting).\n\n## Ensemble idea\n- **Ensemble method**: combine many simple **weak learners** (here, trees) into one strong model.\n\n## 8.2.1 Bagging (Bootstrap AGGregatING)\n- **Motivation**: single trees have **high variance** (a different data split gives a very different\n  tree). Averaging n independent observations with variance σ² gives variance σ²/n → **averaging\n  reduces variance**.\n- **Procedure**: draw B **bootstrap** samples from the training data, fit a tree to each, average:\n  `f̂_bag(x) = (1/B) Σ_b f̂*^b(x)`. Trees are grown **deep and unpruned** (high variance, low bias);\n  averaging kills the variance.\n- **Classification**: **majority vote** across the B trees.\n- **B is not critical**: large B does **not** cause overfitting; pick B large enough for error to\n  settle (e.g., B=100).\n- **Out-of-Bag (OOB) error**: each bootstrap tree uses ~2/3 of observations; predict each obs. using\n  only the ~B/3 trees where it was OOB (average / majority-vote), then compute OOB MSE / error. A\n  valid test-error estimate — with large B ≈ leave-one-out CV — and cheap vs CV on big data.\n- **Variable importance**: bagging loses the single-tree interpretability. Summarize importance by\n  total **RSS decrease** (regression) or **Gini index decrease** (classification) from splits on\n  each predictor, averaged over B trees. Large decrease = important predictor.\n\n## 8.2.2 Random Forests\n- Like bagging (bootstrap + deep trees), **but at each split only a random subset of m predictors**\n  is considered as split candidates (fresh sample each split). Typically **m ≈ √p**.\n- **Why**: with one strong predictor, all bagged trees split on it first → trees **highly\n  correlated** → averaging correlated quantities barely reduces variance. Restricting to m predictors\n  **decorrelates** the trees (on avg (p−m)/p of splits ignore the strong predictor), reducing\n  variance more.\n- **m = p is just bagging.** Small m helps most when predictors are **many and correlated**.\n- Like bagging, **does not overfit** as B increases.\n\n## 8.2.3 Boosting\n- Trees grown **sequentially**, each using information from prior trees. **No bootstrap** — each tree\n  fit on a modified version of the data. Idea: **learn slowly**, fitting each new tree to the\n  **residuals** of the current model, then add a shrunken version in.\n- **Algorithm 8.2 (regression)**: init f̂(x)=0, r_i=y_i; for b=1..B: fit tree f̂^b with d splits to\n  (X, r); update `f̂(x) ← f̂(x) + λ f̂^b(x)`; update `r_i ← r_i − λ f̂^b(x_i)`. Output\n  `f̂(x) = Σ_b λ f̂^b(x)`.\n- **Three tuning parameters**:\n  1. **B (number of trees)** — boosting **CAN overfit** if B too large (slowly); choose B via CV.\n     (Contrast: bagging/RF don't overfit with large B.)\n  2. **λ (shrinkage)** — small positive learning rate, typically 0.01 or 0.001. Smaller λ needs\n     larger B.\n  3. **d (splits per tree = interaction depth)** — controls complexity. **d=1 = stump** → an additive\n     model (each term one variable). d = max interaction order.\n- Because each tree accounts for prior trees, **small trees (even stumps) often suffice** and aid\n  interpretability. In the gene-expression example, depth-1 stumps beat depth-2 and both beat a\n  random forest.\n\n## 8.2.4 Bayesian Additive Regression Trees (BART)  — brief\n- Hybrid of bagging/RF (each tree built with randomness) and boosting (each tree captures signal not\n  yet fit). Uses K trees over B iterations; **novelty**: each new tree is a random **perturbation**\n  of the previous iteration's tree (change structure by adding/pruning branches, or change\n  terminal-node predictions), favoring perturbations that improve fit to the **partial residual**\n  (Algorithm 8.3).\n- Perturbing (vs fitting fresh) plus small trees **guards against overfitting** — training and test\n  error stay close (unlike boosting, which overfits as iterations grow). Uses a **burn-in** period L\n  (discard early models); final prediction = average over post-burn-in iterations.\n- Can be viewed as a **Bayesian / MCMC** method (each perturbation ≈ draw from a posterior). Typical\n  settings K=200, B=1000, L=100; strong **out-of-box** performance with minimal tuning.\n\n## 8.2.5 Summary of tree ensembles (memorize the contrast)\n- **Bagging**: trees grown **independently** on bootstrap samples → similar trees; can get stuck in\n  local optima.\n- **Random forests**: independent bootstrap trees **+ random feature subset per split** →\n  decorrelated trees, better model-space exploration.\n- **Boosting**: **original data only**, trees grown **successively** on leftover signal, shrunken;\n  slow learning.\n- **BART**: original data only, trees grown successively but **perturbed** to avoid local minima /\n  explore more.\n- Trees make good weak learners: flexible, handle **mixed** (qualitative + quantitative) predictors.\n"}]}, {"part": "ML", "num": "07", "name": "Ensemble Methods", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "cheatsheet": "- **Why ensembles work**: combining multiple models by aggregation (averaging for regression, voting for classification) can produce more accurate *and* more stable predictions than any single model — but only under conditions, never as a guarantee.\n  - **Error cancellation requires imperfectly correlated errors.** If members make *different* mistakes, errors partly cancel in the average. Perfectly correlated (identical) members give no diversity benefit.\n  - **No observation-by-observation guarantee.** An ensemble improves *expected* performance; it is not guaranteed to beat its best member (or every member) on any given future point or regime.\n  - **Shared systematic bias survives aggregation.** If every learner has the same directional error, averaging preserves it. \"Wisdom of the crowd\" still requires out-of-sample testing.\n  - Combining weak/unstable learners can beat repeatedly picking one learner; the **aggregation rule matters** (mean, median, weighted vote all differ).\n- **Bagging (bootstrap aggregating)** — G2:\n  - A **bootstrap sample** draws N rows *with replacement* from the original N observations → some rows repeat, some are absent. Duplicated rows keep their original features and target (never relabeled/permuted).\n  - On average a bootstrap sample contains ~**63%** unique rows; the ~**37% \"out-of-bag\" (OOB)** rows give a free, built-in validation estimate (evaluate each row only on trees that never saw it).\n  - Bagging **reduces variance** of high-variance, low-bias learners → use **deep, unconstrained trees**. Averaging *shallow* high-bias trees reduces variance a little but leaves the systematic error intact (does not reduce bias).\n  - Bootstrap resampling promotes variation but does **not** guarantee independent errors — shared data/features keep trees correlated (a dominant feature becomes the root split in nearly every tree), which limits variance reduction.\n- **Random forests** — G3:\n  - Add **feature randomness**: at each split only a random subset of candidate features is considered. This **decorrelates** trees beyond what bootstrap sampling alone achieves.\n  - Trees do not use disjoint feature sets; subsets are re-drawn per split and features recur across trees. Predictions are still **aggregated** (averaging/voting).\n  - **Tradeoff (candidates per split, \"mtry\")**: fewer candidates → weaker individual trees but more diversity; using *all* features → collapses back to ordinary bagged trees. Fewer candidates does **not** guarantee lower test error — tune and validate.\n- **Diversity among learners** — G4:\n  - What matters is **behavioral diversity** (errors on different observations), not model count, file count, or different algorithm *names*. A linear model + KNN + tree can still share blind spots (e.g., same crisis dates) and produce correlated errors.\n  - Diversity helps **only if** the diverse learners retain predictive skill; injecting random/uninformative predictions degrades the aggregate.\n  - Standalone quality ≠ ensemble contribution — a lower-Sharpe but complementary model can add more incremental value than a top model highly correlated with existing members. Assess with **out-of-sample residual/prediction correlation**, not in-sample fit alone.\n- **Boosting intuition** — G5 (conceptual only):\n  - **Sequential**: each new (typically shallow) learner is trained to address the errors the current ensemble has not captured (residuals) or reweights hard/misclassified examples. Final prediction is an **additive, weighted** combination scaled by a **learning rate (shrinkage)**.\n  - Differs from bagging: bagged learners are trained **independently** (parallelizable); boosting stages **depend on** prior stages (sequential, not parallel).\n  - Boosting can **reduce bias** by adding corrections that expand effective capacity; weak learners combine into an expressive nonlinear model. But a mislabeled/noisy example can look persistently \"hard\" and attract harmful excess focus.\n- **Bias vs. variance in ensembles** — G6:\n  - Bagging unstable deep trees → **variance reduction** (stability across retraining samples). Boosting shallow underfit learners → **bias reduction**.\n  - Reduced variance does **not** imply unbiased: stability (consistency across samples) and accuracy (closeness to truth) are distinct properties. Averaging identical predictions changes nothing; averaging cannot create signal absent from every member.\n- **Failure modes** — G7:\n  - **Correlated base learners**: near-identical trees fail together; large member count ≠ diversification; a regime shift can expose a weakness shared by all.\n  - **Leakage-driven gains**: look-ahead / point-in-time leakage inflates validation; averaging many leaked models cannot repair invalid temporal availability and instead creates *false confidence* through correlated optimism. Reconstruct features as-of each prediction timestamp.\n  - **Overfitting in boosting**: training error → 0 while validation peaks early then deteriorates (memorization of noise). Use time-aware validation / early stopping / learning-rate / depth controls; zero training error ≠ smallest future loss.\n  - Averaging systematically low estimates stays systematically low; use **regime/segment stress testing** to reveal concentrated failures average metrics hide.", "traps": "- **Inverted-logic questions (8 of 24):** the Answer Key reads \"**True = incorrect; False = accurate**,\" so a verdict of **False means the statement is correct**. Read the polarity line first. Inverted here: **G1Q2, G1Q6, G2Q2, G3Q2, G4Q2, G5Q2, G6Q1, G7Q2.**\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices** that begin **\"The following claim is false: …\"**: judge the inner claim's truth, then negate (\"says X is false\"), then apply the question's polarity. Appears in **G2Q2A, G3Q2A, G3Q3A, G7Q3A**. Example: G3Q3A embeds an *accurate* claim, so \"the following claim is false\" is itself inaccurate → verdict **False** (normal polarity). G2Q2A/G3Q2A embed accurate claims inside *inverted* questions, so the choice is incorrect → verdict **True (incorrect)**.\n- **\"Averaging fixes everything\" fallacy:** aggregation cannot remove shared systematic bias, cannot repair look-ahead leakage, and cannot recover signal absent from every member. Recurs in G1Q1C, G4Q2C, G6Q1C, G7Q1B, G7Q3C.\n- **Bag deep, not shallow:** bagging wants high-variance/low-bias base learners (deep trees). \"Bag shallow max_depth=1–2 trees\" is a distractor (G1Q6D) — that leaves high bias untouched.\n- **OOB is ~37%, not one-third precisely, and it validates without a holdout** (G1Q6C) — the \"one-third\" phrasing is treated as incorrect wording in the inverted question.\n- **Member count ≠ diversity ≠ independence:** more trees never guarantees zero variance (G2Q2D), never guarantees an unbiased estimate (G7Q3C), and identical/duplicate models add no information (G4Q1B, G1Q5C).", "references": "1. **ISLR — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning* (James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani):** ch.8 (§8.2 tree-based ensembles: §8.2.1 bagging & out-of-bag error, §8.2.2 random forests / feature subsampling, §8.2.3 boosting & shrinkage), §2.2 (bias-variance tradeoff), ch.5 (§5.2 the bootstrap).\n2. **ESL — *The Elements of Statistical Learning* (Hastie, Tibshirani, Friedman):** ch.10 (boosting, AdaBoost, gradient boosting, regularization/shrinkage), §15 (random forests; variance of an average of correlated variables, OOB), ch.16 (ensemble learning, diversity), ch.7 (model assessment, bias-variance).\n3. **scikit-learn User Guide — \"Ensemble methods\":** Bagging, Random Forests / Extra-Trees (`max_features`), Voting Classifier/Regressor, Gradient Boosting / HistGradientBoosting (learning rate, early stopping), plus \"Imbalanced learning\" (stratified sampling) and OOB scoring.\n4. **Breiman:** \"Bagging Predictors\" (1996) and \"Random Forests\" (2001) — original bootstrap-aggregation and feature-randomness results; bias-variance framing of variance reduction.\n5. **López de Prado — *Advances in Financial Machine Learning*:** data leakage / point-in-time features, purged & embargoed cross-validation, and regime-aware backtest evaluation — for the failure-mode questions (leakage, overfitting, correlated-learner regime failure).\n6. **Concept references:** bagging vs. boosting (parallel/variance vs. sequential/bias), bootstrap sampling (~63% unique / ~37% OOB), error decorrelation and the correlation limit on ensemble variance reduction, and diversity-with-skill as prerequisites for aggregation gains.", "bookNotes": []}, {"part": "ML", "num": "08", "name": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "cheatsheet": "- **Three-way data split (train / validation / test)** — each split has a distinct, non-interchangeable role:\n  - **Training set**: estimate model parameters / learned structure from observed examples (fit).\n  - **Validation set**: choose hyperparameters and select among candidate models *before* the final test. It provides feedback for development decisions without touching the final evaluation.\n  - **Test set**: a *single, final* out-of-sample check. It is most informative only when earlier decisions have **not** been adapted to its results.\n  - After selection, refitting the chosen configuration on **train + validation** combined is legitimate **as long as the test set stays untouched**.\n  - **Why validation exists separately from test**: trying many candidates produces **selection optimism** — the best-of-many validation score is optimistically low. The test set stays clean precisely so it is not contaminated by that selection pressure.\n- **Test-set reuse / leakage** is the cardinal sin:\n  - Repeatedly inspecting the test score and revising features/settings turns the test set into part of the *development* process — even if test **rows are never passed to the fit method**. Human decisions based on test feedback are indirect adaptation.\n  - The \"best observed\" result after many attempts exploits chance variation and is **optimistic**, not unbiased.\n  - **Scarce data does not make reuse harmless** — small datasets make estimates *less* stable, not contamination-free.\n  - Test **labels** are needed to *score* (compute error) but must stay out of *fitting and tuning*.\n- **Error metrics — MAE, MSE, RMSE** (regression):\n  - **MAE** (mean absolute error): linear penalty on |error|; treats all errors proportionally; robust to outliers. Same units as target.\n  - **MSE** (mean squared error): squares errors → **disproportionate weight to large errors/outliers**; squaring also prevents +/− errors from canceling; units are **squared** (e.g., dollars²).\n  - **RMSE** = √MSE: restores the **target's original units** (easier to compare to the target scale than MSE), while keeping MSE's emphasis on large errors. RMSE is **more** sensitive to outliers than MAE, not less.\n  - **RMSE ≥ MAE always** for the same set of errors (equality only when all absolute errors are equal). A wider RMSE–MAE gap indicates **greater dispersion/variation in error sizes**.\n- **Baselines & benchmarking**:\n  - A metric value **in isolation is meaningless** — it needs the target scale plus a relevant comparator.\n  - **Regression baseline**: predict the **training-set mean** (a naive constant) — the reference for squared-error evaluation. If the model's test RMSE is worse than this baseline, it added no value under that metric.\n  - **Classification baseline**: predict the **majority class**. With 60% positive days, an always-up rule scores 60% accuracy; a 58% model is **below** that baseline.\n  - **Trading baselines**: buy-and-hold, and a cash / no-trade benchmark, evaluated over the **same period**. Baselines are deliberately **simple** — they need not be more complex than the candidate.\n  - Never compute a baseline (or scaling stats) from the **test targets** — that leaks the evaluation set.\n- **Fair model comparison**:\n  - Score all models on the **same held-out observations** (same period, same rows). Different test periods confound model quality with market-regime difficulty; a common metric does **not** fix unequal test conditions.\n  - Use the **same metric and same units** across models; comparing a displayed MAE against a displayed RMSE (or % vs. decimal returns) mixes definitions and scales.\n  - **Avoid leakage in preprocessing**: estimate scaling stats (mean/SD) from **training data only**, then apply unchanged to validation/test. Computing them on the full dataset leaks. Leakage is a flaw even for learners insensitive to scaling (e.g., trees), and KNN is especially scale-sensitive because it relies on distances.\n- **Metric selection & business alignment**:\n  - Low prediction RMSE does **not** guarantee profit — execution costs (spreads, commissions, market impact), decision rules, turnover, and risk sit between forecast and P&L. A statistical improvement that never changes a trade has ~no economic effect.\n  - **Do not discard statistical metrics** entirely for trading — they remain diagnostically useful, just not sufficient.\n  - **Symmetric metrics** (MAE/MSE/RMSE) ignore error **direction** — they don't distinguish a harmful underestimate from an equal overestimate. Tail-risk / asymmetric-cost settings may need **asymmetric or tail-focused** measures in addition.\n  - **Directional accuracy** weights every day equally regardless of move size — a model right more often can still be less profitable than one that captures the few large moves (magnitude, position sizing, costs decide P&L).\n  - An economically aligned metric still requires **disciplined out-of-sample evaluation**.\n- **Variability & stability of results**:\n  - Wide spread of scores across reasonable splits/seeds means performance is **sensitive to the evaluation period / randomness** (often market-regime differences). Report **central tendency AND dispersion**, not the single best run.\n  - Variation can arise from sampling and regime shifts **without any implementation defect**.\n  - Two models with equal **mean** error can differ greatly in **stability**; when large swings are costly, the more consistent model may be preferable. Window-level inspection reveals regime-specific failure that an average hides.\n  - **Record random seeds** (they affect resampling / model construction) for reproducibility. But seed-stability ≠ robustness to future **regime change**.", "traps": "- **Inverted-logic questions** (7 of 18 here) flip the meaning: the Answer Key states \"**True = incorrect; False = accurate**.\" So marking a statement **False means it is actually correct/accurate**, and **True means it is a flawed/incorrect claim**. Always read the polarity line first. Inverted here: **G1Q3, G2Q2, G3Q3, G4Q2, G5Q1, G5Q3, G6Q1.** In this file each inverted verdict is written as `False (accurate)` or `True (incorrect)` to keep the plain-English meaning visible.\n- **No double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices in Domain 8.** Unlike Domain 1, none of these choices are phrased \"It is incorrect to conclude that [accurate statement].\" Watch for the pattern anyway — if a choice buries an accurate claim inside a negation, parse the inner claim, judge it, then negate. It does not appear here, so the only polarity twist is the inverted-question header.\n- **The \"harmless because small data\" trap** (G1Q3-D): scarce data does NOT excuse test reuse — it makes contamination *worse*. Marked as a flawed (incorrect) claim.\n- **The \"RMSE < MAE always\" trap** (G2Q2-D): reversed inequality. RMSE is always **≥** MAE for the same errors — flagged incorrect.\n- **The \"more accurate / more complex must be better\" trap** (G3Q2-E, G3Q3-B, G5Q3-B): higher accuracy frequency, higher raw return, or greater model sophistication does **not** by itself establish superiority once baselines, costs, risk, and magnitude enter. These are the classic \"sounds obviously true\" distractors that are actually false/flawed.\n- **The \"leakage is OK for trees\" trap** (G4Q2-C): a learner's insensitivity to a leak does not make the leak methodologically acceptable — flagged incorrect.\n- **The \"aligned metric removes need for out-of-sample testing\" trap** (G5Q3-E) and **\"variation proves the code is buggy\" trap** (G6Q1-D): both are flagged incorrect — a good metric still needs OOS validation, and split-to-split variance can be pure sampling/regime effect.", "references": "1. **ISLR — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning* (James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani):** §2.2.1 (measuring quality of fit, MSE, training vs. test error, out-of-sample generalization); §5.1–5.2 (the validation-set approach, cross-validation, variance of the estimate, temporal/leakage considerations); ch.4 (classification, accuracy, confusion matrices); §8.2 (bagging / variance reduction).\n2. **ESL — *The Elements of Statistical Learning* (Hastie, Tibshirani, Friedman):** ch.7 (model assessment and selection, optimism of the training error, cross-validation, the \"one-standard-error\" rule for choosing simpler models).\n3. **scikit-learn User Guide:** \"Metrics and scoring\" (MAE, MSE, RMSE units and outlier sensitivity, precision/recall/F1, ROC-AUC); \"Cross-validation: evaluating estimator performance\" (shared folds, mean ± std reporting); \"Common pitfalls and recommended practices: data leakage\" (fit preprocessing on the training fold only); \"Dummy estimators\" (mean / majority-class baselines).\n4. **Concept references (metric definitions):** RMSE ≥ MAE relationship for a fixed error set; symmetric vs. asymmetric loss and direction-insensitivity of MAE/MSE/RMSE; quantile (pinball) loss and expected shortfall for tail-cost objectives; Brier score for probabilistic outputs.\n5. **Grinold & Kahn — *Active Portfolio Management*:** connecting predictive signal to net-of-cost outcomes (turnover, transaction costs, risk, benchmark-relative evaluation) for the metric-selection / business-alignment questions.", "bookNotes": [{"book": "ISLP", "title": "ISLP Section 2.2 — Assessing Model Accuracy", "file": "references/ISLP/Ch02-2_Assessing_Model_Accuracy.md", "md": "# ISLP Section 2.2 — Assessing Model Accuracy\n\n*Source: ISLP, PDF pages 38–49 (book pp. 28–39). Covers 2.2.1 Measuring Quality of Fit,\n2.2.2 Bias-Variance Trade-Off, 2.2.3 The Classification Setting.*\n\n> ML4T exam relevance: core of model evaluation — MSE, train vs. test error, overfitting,\n> bias-variance decomposition, Bayes error, KNN flexibility. → ML Domain 8 (Model Evaluation) and\n> the bias-variance framing behind every learner in ML Domain 4–7.\n\n## 2.2.1 Measuring the Quality of Fit (regression)\n- **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**: `MSE = (1/n) Σ_i ( y_i − f̂(x_i) )²`  (Eq. 2.5).\n  Small when predictions are close to truth.\n- **Training MSE** uses the data the model was fit on. We *do not* care about it per se — we care\n  about **test MSE** = `Ave( y_0 − f̂(x_0) )²` on previously unseen (x_0, y_0) (Eq. 2.6).\n  - Example intuition: predicting *tomorrow's* stock price, not last week's; predicting diabetes\n    for *future* patients, not the training patients.\n- **Key pitfall**: minimizing training MSE does **not** guarantee low test MSE. Many methods fit\n  coefficients specifically to minimize training MSE, driving it small while test MSE stays large.\n- **Flexibility vs. error** (fundamental pattern, holds for any dataset/method):\n  - Training MSE **decreases monotonically** as flexibility (degrees of freedom) increases.\n  - Test MSE is **U-shaped**: falls, then rises.\n- **Overfitting**: small training MSE but large test MSE — the method chases random patterns in\n  training data that don't exist in test data. Defined specifically as: a *less flexible* model\n  would have given a smaller test MSE. (Training MSE is almost always < test MSE regardless.)\n- **Degrees of freedom** = a summary of a curve's flexibility (linear regression = 2 df, most\n  restrictive). **Cross-validation** (Ch. 5) estimates test MSE using only training data.\n\n## 2.2.2 The Bias-Variance Trade-Off\n- The U-shape in test MSE comes from decomposing expected test MSE at a point x_0 into **three\n  non-negative** parts (Eq. 2.7):\n  `E( y_0 − f̂(x_0) )² = Var(f̂(x_0)) + [Bias(f̂(x_0))]² + Var(ε)`\n  = **Variance + squared Bias + irreducible error**.\n- Expected test MSE can **never** be below `Var(ε)` (the irreducible error).\n- **Variance**: how much f̂ would change with a different training set. More flexible → higher\n  variance (a wiggly curve shifts a lot if one point moves).\n- **Bias**: error from approximating a complex reality with a simpler model. More flexible → lower\n  bias. (Linear regression is badly biased when true f is non-linear.)\n- **General rule**: as flexibility ↑, variance ↑ and bias ↓. Early on bias falls faster than\n  variance rises (test MSE ↓); later variance dominates (test MSE ↑).\n- **The trade-off**: good test performance needs *both* low variance and low squared bias. Easy to\n  get one at the cost of the other (interpolating curve = zero bias/high variance; horizontal line\n  = low variance/high bias). Finding both-low is the challenge — a recurring theme of the book.\n\n## 2.2.3 The Classification Setting\n- **Training error rate**: `(1/n) Σ_i I(y_i ≠ ŷ_i)` (Eq. 2.8) — fraction misclassified;\n  `I(·)` is an indicator (1 if wrong, 0 if right).\n- **Test error rate**: `Ave( I(y_0 ≠ ŷ_0) )` (Eq. 2.9). A good classifier minimizes this.\n- **Bayes classifier**: assign x_0 to the **most likely class**, i.e. the class j maximizing the\n  conditional probability `Pr(Y = j | X = x_0)` (Eq. 2.10). Two-class: predict class 1 if\n  `Pr(Y=1|X=x_0) > 0.5`.\n  - **Bayes decision boundary**: the set of points where the probability is exactly 50%.\n  - **Bayes error rate** (lowest possible test error): `1 − E( max_j Pr(Y=j|X) )` (Eq. 2.11);\n    at a point it is `1 − max_j Pr(Y=j|X=x_0)`. Analogous to irreducible error. > 0 when classes\n    overlap (example dataset: ≈ 0.13). The Bayes classifier is an **unattainable gold standard**\n    (needs the true conditional distribution, which is unknown for real data).\n- **K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) classifier**: estimates `Pr(Y=j|X=x_0) = (1/K) Σ_{i∈N_0} I(y_i=j)`\n  over the K training points N_0 nearest to x_0 (Eq. 2.12); predicts the majority class among them.\n  - Can get surprisingly close to the Bayes boundary (e.g., K=10 test error 0.1363 vs. Bayes 0.1304).\n  - **K controls flexibility**: small K (K=1) = very flexible, low bias / high variance, jagged\n    boundary, training error = 0 but high test error. Large K (K=100) = inflexible, high bias /\n    low variance, near-linear boundary. **1/K** is the flexibility axis.\n  - Same U-shaped test-error pattern as regression; no strong link between training and test error.\n\n## One-line takeaways\n- Choose the model that minimizes **test** error, not training error.\n- The **right level of flexibility** is critical and dataset-dependent; the bias-variance trade-off\n  produces the U-shaped test-error curve. Cross-validation (Ch. 5) is how you find it in practice.\n"}, {"book": "ISLP", "title": "ISLP Sections 3.1–3.3 — Linear Regression", "file": "references/ISLP/Ch03-1to3_Linear_Regression.md", "md": "# ISLP Sections 3.1–3.3 — Linear Regression\n\n*Source: ISLP, PDF pages 80–119 (book pp. 71–110). 3.1 Simple LR, 3.2 Multiple LR,\n3.3 Other Considerations.*\n\n> ML4T exam relevance: the canonical parametric supervised learner — coefficients, fit metrics,\n> inference, and the assumption failures that break it. → ML Domain 4 (Linear Models) and\n> ML Domain 8 (Model Evaluation: R², RSE, F-test).\n\n## 3.1 Simple Linear Regression  (one predictor)\n- **Model**: `Y ≈ β0 + β1 X`. β0 = intercept (E[Y] at X=0), β1 = slope (avg. change in Y per\n  1-unit rise in X). Prediction: `ŷ = β̂0 + β̂1 x`.\n- **Residual**: `e_i = y_i − ŷ_i`. **RSS** = Σ e_i² (Eq. 3.3).\n- **Least squares estimates** (minimize RSS, Eq. 3.4):\n  `β̂1 = Σ(x_i−x̄)(y_i−ȳ) / Σ(x_i−x̄)²`,  `β̂0 = ȳ − β̂1 x̄`.\n\n### 3.1.2 Accuracy of the coefficient estimates\n- **Population regression line** = true best linear approx (unobserved); **least squares line** =\n  our estimate from one sample. Different samples → different lines, but least squares estimates\n  are **unbiased** (average over many samples = truth).\n- **Standard errors** (Eq. 3.8): `SE(β̂1)² = σ²/Σ(x_i−x̄)²`,\n  `SE(β̂0)² = σ²[1/n + x̄²/Σ(x_i−x̄)²]`, where σ² = Var(ε). SE(β̂1) is *smaller* when x's are more\n  spread out (more leverage to estimate slope). Assumes errors uncorrelated with common variance.\n- **Residual standard error** estimates σ: `RSE = √(RSS/(n−2))`.\n- **95% confidence interval** ≈ `β̂1 ± 2·SE(β̂1)` (Eq. 3.9).\n- **Hypothesis test**: H0: β1 = 0 (no relationship) vs Ha: β1 ≠ 0. **t-statistic**\n  `t = β̂1 / SE(β̂1)` (Eq. 3.14); # of SDs β̂1 is from 0. Under H0, t ~ t-dist with n−2 df.\n  **p-value** = prob. of seeing |t| this large by chance; small p → reject H0 (typical cutoffs 5%/1%).\n\n### 3.1.3 Accuracy of the model (goodness of fit)\n- **RSE** = `√(RSS/(n−2))` (Eq. 3.15) — absolute lack-of-fit, in units of Y (avg deviation from\n  the regression line).\n- **R² statistic** (Eq. 3.17): `R² = (TSS − RSS)/TSS = 1 − RSS/TSS`, where **TSS = Σ(y_i−ȳ)²**.\n  Proportion of variance in Y explained by X; always in [0,1], scale-free. In *simple* regression,\n  **R² = r²** (squared correlation `Cor(X,Y)`, Eq. 3.18). What counts as \"good\" R² is domain-dependent.\n\n## 3.2 Multiple Linear Regression  (p predictors)\n- **Model** (Eq. 3.19): `Y = β0 + β1X1 + β2X2 + … + βpXp + ε`. Interpret βj = avg effect on Y of a\n  1-unit rise in Xj **holding all other predictors fixed**. Fit by least squares (Eq. 3.22),\n  minimizing RSS (matrix algebra; software).\n- **Simple ≠ multiple coefficients**: correlated predictors change the story. Advertising example:\n  `newspaper` is significant *alone* but ~0 with TV+radio in the model — it was a **surrogate** for\n  radio (radio & newspaper correlated 0.35). (\"Shark attacks vs. ice cream\" — confounder = temperature.)\n\n### 3.2.2 Four key questions\n1. **Is at least one predictor useful?** Test H0: β1=…=βp=0 with the **F-statistic**\n   `F = [(TSS−RSS)/p] / [RSS/(n−p−1)]` (Eq. 3.23). F ≈ 1 under H0; F ≫ 1 → reject.\n   - F adjusts for p; individual t-p-values do **not** — with large p, ~5% of predictors look\n     significant by chance, so rely on F for the global test. (For a subset of q coefficients use\n     Eq. 3.24; each individual t-test equals the F-test dropping that one variable, i.e. its\n     partial effect.) If **p > n**, least squares can't be fit — see Ch. 6 (forward selection).\n2. **Which predictors matter? (variable selection)** 2^p possible subsets → infeasible for large p.\n   Classical automated searches: **forward selection** (start null, add best-RSS variable each step;\n   greedy, always usable), **backward selection** (start full, drop largest p-value; needs p<n),\n   **mixed selection** (add forward, drop if p-value rises). Model-quality criteria: Mallow's Cp,\n   AIC, BIC, adjusted R² (Ch. 6).\n3. **Model fit**: RSE and R² (here **R² = Cor(Y,Ŷ)²**). **R² always rises when adding any\n   predictor** (RSS falls on training data) → don't use raw R² to compare models of different size;\n   a tiny bump (e.g., adding newspaper) signals the variable is useless and may overfit.\n   `RSE = √(RSS/(n−p−1))` (Eq. 3.25) — can *increase* with more predictors if RSS barely drops.\n4. **Predictions** carry 3 uncertainties: (a) coefficient estimation error → **reducible**,\n   (b) **model bias** (linearity is an approximation), (c) irreducible error ε.\n   - **Confidence interval**: uncertainty about the *average* response f(X).\n   - **Prediction interval**: uncertainty about an *individual* Y; **always wider** (adds\n     irreducible error ε).\n\n## 3.3 Other Considerations\n\n### 3.3.1 Qualitative predictors\n- Two levels → one **dummy/indicator variable** (0/1; a.k.a. one-hot encoding). Coding is arbitrary\n  and doesn't change predictions, only coefficient interpretation.\n- >2 levels → **(#levels − 1) dummies**; the omitted level is the **baseline**. Test overall effect\n  with an F-test on the dummies (coding-independent).\n\n### 3.3.2 Extensions (relaxing key assumptions)\n- Standard model assumes **additive** and **linear** relationships.\n- **Interaction terms** (remove additivity): add `β3·X1·X2` (Eq. 3.31). Then effect of X1 depends on\n  X2. Advertising TV×radio interaction: R² 96.8% vs 89.7% → strong synergy. **Hierarchical\n  principle**: if you keep an interaction, keep its main effects even if their p-values are large.\n- **Polynomial regression** (remove linearity): include `X, X², …` as predictors (Eq. 3.36); still a\n  *linear model* in the coefficients. Higher degree → risk of over-wiggly fit.\n\n### 3.3.3 Potential problems (six)\n1. **Non-linearity** — detect via **residual plots** (residuals vs fitted); a pattern (U-shape) =\n   non-linear. Fix: transform predictors (log X, √X, X²).\n2. **Correlated error terms** — common in **time series** (adjacent errors track). Understated SEs →\n   too-narrow CIs, too-small p-values, false confidence.\n3. **Non-constant error variance (heteroscedasticity)** — funnel shape in residual plot. Fix:\n   transform Y (log Y, √Y) or use **weighted least squares** (weights ∝ 1/variance).\n4. **Outliers** — unusual **y** given x. Use **studentized residuals**; |value| > 3 flags an outlier.\n   Inflates RSE and lowers R² even if the line barely moves.\n5. **High-leverage points** — unusual **x**. Have large impact on the fit. **Leverage statistic**\n   h_i (Eq. 3.37), between 1/n and 1, average = (p+1)/n; a value ≫ (p+1)/n is high leverage. Worst\n   case = high leverage + outlier.\n6. **Collinearity** — two+ predictors closely related. Inflates SE(β̂j), shrinks t-statistics,\n   reduces power → may fail to reject H0. Detect: correlation matrix, or **VIF** (variance inflation\n   factor) = `1/(1 − R²_{Xj|X−j})`; VIF ≥ 5–10 is problematic (min = 1). Fix: drop one collinear\n   variable, or combine them into one.\n\n## 3.4 Marketing plan (worked answers, Advertising data)\nRelationship exists (F-test p≈0); ~90% variance explained (R²=0.897, RSE=1.69 ≈12% error); only\nTV & radio matter (newspaper CI includes 0, no collinearity — VIFs ≈1); use prediction interval for\nan individual, CI for the average; residual plot reveals non-linearity; TV×radio interaction shows\nsynergy.\n"}, {"book": "ProbabilisticML", "title": "Ch. 1 — Introduction (Murphy, *Probabilistic Machine Learning: An Introduction*, Book 1)", "file": "references/ProbabilisticML/Ch01_Introduction.md", "md": "# Ch. 1 — Introduction (Murphy, *Probabilistic Machine Learning: An Introduction*, Book 1)\n\n**Source:** Kevin P. Murphy, *Probabilistic Machine Learning: An Introduction* (MIT Press). Online version, April 18, 2025.\n**Chapter 1 span:** book pp. 1–29 = **PDF pp. 31–59** (Ch. 2 begins PDF p. 62).\n**Week-1 recommended reading for GT ML4T Exam 1.**\n\n> Study notes distilled faithfully from the text. Section numbers (e.g., §1.2.3) are the book's own.\n\n---\n\n## 1.1 What is machine learning? (PDF p. 31)\n\n- **Mitchell's definition:** \"A computer program learns from experience *E* w.r.t. tasks *T* and performance measure *P*, if its performance at *T*, measured by *P*, improves with *E*.\" The three levers of any ML setup are **T** (task), **P** (performance measure), **E** (training signal/experience).\n- This book takes a **probabilistic perspective**: treat all unknown quantities (predictions, model parameters) as **random variables** endowed with **probability distributions**.\n- Two reasons for the probabilistic approach: (1) it is the **optimal approach to decision-making under uncertainty**; (2) it is the shared language of science/engineering, giving a unifying framework.\n\n---\n\n## 1.2 Supervised learning (PDF p. 31)\n\n**Goal:** learn a mapping *f* from inputs **x ∈ X** to outputs **y ∈ Y**.\n\nKey terminology (memorize — exam loves synonyms):\n- **Inputs x** = features = covariates = predictors. Often a fixed-dim vector, X = R^D (D = number of features / dimensionality). In statsmodels: *exogenous* variables.\n- **Output y** = label = target = response. In statsmodels: *endogenous* variable.\n- **Experience E** = training set **D = {(xₙ, yₙ)}**, n = 1..N. **N = sample size.**\n- **Design matrix:** N × D matrix; each **row = an example**, each **column = a feature**.\n  - Tall & skinny: N ≫ D. Short & fat: D ≫ N (e.g., genomics). \"Big data\" = large N; \"wide data\" = large D.\n- **Tabular data** vs. variable-size inputs (sequences, graphs) → converted to fixed-size vectors via **featurization**.\n\n### 1.2.1 Classification (PDF p. 32)\n- Output space = **C unordered, mutually exclusive labels (classes)**, Y = {1,…,C}. Also called **pattern recognition**.\n- **Binary classification** = 2 classes (y ∈ {0,1} or {−1,+1}).\n- **Exploratory data analysis (EDA):** inspect data first (patterns, label noise, outliers). Tools: **pair plot** (scatter of feature pairs + marginal on diagonal), color-coded by class; for high-D data, do **dimensionality reduction** first.\n- **Decision rule / classifier** partitions input space via a **decision boundary**; nested rules form a **decision tree** inducing a **decision surface**. Tree stores, per node, feature index + threshold; all these are **parameters θ**.\n\n**Empirical Risk Minimization (ERM) (PDF p. 36):**\n- **Misclassification rate** on training set: L(θ) = (1/N) Σ I(yₙ ≠ f(xₙ;θ)), where **I** = indicator function (1 if true, else 0).\n- **Loss function ℓ(y, ŷ)** generalizes this; errors can be asymmetric (costlier one way — poisonous-flower loss matrix example).\n- **Empirical risk** = average loss on training set: L(θ) = (1/N) Σ ℓ(yₙ, f(xₙ;θ)).\n- **Zero-one loss** ℓ₀₁ = I(y ≠ ŷ) → makes empirical risk equal the misclassification rate.\n- **Model fitting / training** = **ERM**: θ̂ = argmin_θ L(θ). **But the true goal is to minimize expected loss on FUTURE data (generalize), not just training loss** (see §1.2.3).\n\n**Uncertainty (PDF p. 37):**\n- **Epistemic (model) uncertainty:** lack of knowledge of the mapping — *reducible* with more data.\n- **Aleatoric (data) uncertainty:** intrinsic/irreducible stochasticity.\n- Model the conditional distribution p(y=c|x;θ) = f_c(x;θ), with 0 ≤ f_c ≤ 1 and Σ f_c = 1.\n- **Softmax function** converts unnormalized **logits** a = f(x;θ) into a valid probability distribution: softmax(a)_c = e^{a_c} / Σ e^{a_c'}.\n\n**Logistic regression & MLE (PDF p. 38):**\n- Affine model f(x;θ) = b + wᵀx → passed through softmax = **logistic regression**.\n- Terminology: **w** = weights (stats: regression coefficients β); **b** = bias (stats: intercept). Bias here ≠ statistical bias.\n- Absorbing bias into weights turns an **affine** function into a **linear** one: f(x;w) = wᵀx.\n- **Maximum Likelihood Estimation (MLE):** use **negative log-likelihood (NLL)** as loss: ℓ = −log p(y|f(x;θ)); θ̂_mle = argmin NLL(θ). A good model assigns high probability to the true output.\n\n### 1.2.2 Regression (PDF p. 38)\n- Predict a **real-valued** output y ∈ R (vs. discrete class).\n- **Quadratic / ℓ₂ loss:** ℓ₂(y,ŷ) = (y − ŷ)². Penalizes large **residuals** (y − ŷ). Empirical risk = **Mean Squared Error (MSE)**.\n- Model output uncertainty with a **Gaussian/normal**: p(y|x;θ) = N(y | f(x;θ), σ²). With fixed σ², **NLL ∝ MSE** → MLE = minimizing squared error.\n- **Linear regression:** f(x;θ) = b + wx (w = slope, b = offset/intercept); **least squares** = argmin MSE. **Multiple linear regression** = many input features.\n- **Polynomial regression:** f(x;w) = wᵀφ(x) with φ(x) = [1, x, x²,…,x^D] — an example of **feature preprocessing / feature engineering**. Still **linear in the parameters w** (nonlinear in x) → MSE has a unique global optimum.\n- **Deep Neural Networks (DNNs) (PDF p. 42):** learn the feature extractor φ(x;V) automatically; stack of L nested functions f(x;θ) = f_L(f_{L−1}(…f₁(x)…)). Variants: **CNNs** (images), **RNNs** (sequences).\n\n### 1.2.3 Overfitting and generalization (PDF p. 42) ★ high-yield\n- A flexible model can drive **training loss to 0** by memorizing → **overfitting** (fits training data but too complex).\n- **Population risk** L(θ; p*) = expected loss under the true data distribution p*(x,y) — what we actually care about.\n- **Generalization gap** = L(θ; p*) − L(θ; D_train). Large gap (low train risk, high population risk) ⇒ overfitting.\n- p* is unknown → split data into **training set** and **test set**; approximate population risk with **test risk** L(θ; D_test).\n- **U-shaped test-error curve** vs. model complexity: too simple = **underfitting** (left); too complex = **overfitting** (right); middle = \"just right.\" Training error monotonically decreases with complexity.\n- **Model selection:** never pick by training loss (always chooses the most complex / most **degrees of freedom**). Use **three sets: training (fit), validation (model selection), test (estimate future/population risk only — never used for fitting or selection).**\n\n### 1.2.4 No Free Lunch (NFL) theorem (PDF p. 43)\n- \"All models are wrong, but some are useful.\" **No single model is best for all problems.**\n- Reason: a set of assumptions = **inductive bias** that helps in one domain may hurt in another.\n- Choose models via **domain knowledge** and/or trial-and-error (cross-validation, Bayesian methods). Keep many models/techniques in your toolbox.\n\n---\n\n## 1.3 Unsupervised learning (PDF p. 44)\n\n- Only inputs **D = {xₙ}**, no outputs. Goal: \"make sense of\" data, not learn a mapping.\n- Probabilistic view: fit an **unconditional model p(x)** (vs. supervised **conditional model p(y|x)**).\n- Advantages: no need for expensive labels; avoids arbitrary category definitions; forces the model to \"explain\" high-dimensional inputs → richer world models. (Supervised learning is \"glorified curve fitting.\")\n\n- **§1.3.1 Clustering:** partition inputs into regions of similar points. **No single correct number of clusters** — trade off model complexity vs. fit.\n- **§1.3.2 Latent factors of variation:** assume high-D xₙ generated from low-D **latent factors zₙ ∈ R^K**. Linear version = **Factor Analysis (FA)**; special case (Σ = σ²I) = **(probabilistic) PCA**. Nonlinear extension via DNN → **variational autoencoder (VAE)**.\n- **§1.3.3 Self-supervised learning:** create **proxy supervised tasks from unlabeled data** (e.g., predict masked words, colorize grayscale). Learns useful features for downstream supervised tasks.\n- **§1.3.4 Evaluating unsupervised learning:** hard — no ground truth. Approaches: (1) **density estimation** — measure NLL/probability assigned to unseen test data (good model isn't \"surprised\"); relates to **data compression**; (2) use learned representation as features for a downstream task → improves **sample efficiency**; (3) **gain understanding / interpretability** (\"carve nature at its joints\").\n\n---\n\n## 1.4 Reinforcement learning (RL) (PDF p. 47)\n\n- An **agent** learns to **interact with an environment** via a **policy a = π(x)** mapping states → actions.\n- Difference from supervised learning: agent is **not told the correct action**; it only gets an occasional **reward** (or punishment) signal. \"Learning with a critic\" (occasional thumbs up/down) vs. \"learning with a teacher.\"\n- Hard because reward is sparse/delayed → **credit-assignment problem** (which action earned the reward?).\n- LeCun's cake analogy: unsupervised = sponge (bulk), supervised = icing, RL = cherry.\n\n---\n\n## 1.5 Data (PDF p. 49)\n\n- Data nature/quality is as vital as the model.\n- **Image datasets:** MNIST (60k train / 10k test, 28×28 grayscale digits — the \"drosophila of ML\"), EMNIST, Fashion-MNIST, CIFAR-10/100, **ImageNet** (~14M images; ILSVRC benchmark; CNNs surpassed a human in 2015).\n- **Text / NLP datasets & tasks:** text classification / **sentiment analysis** (IMDB reviews), **machine translation** (WMT), **seq2seq** (summarization, question answering — SQuAD), **language modeling** (unconditional generative model of text p(x₁…x_T) — a form of unsupervised learning).\n\n### 1.5.3 Preprocessing discrete input data (PDF p. 53)\n- **One-hot encoding (dummy encoding):** categorical variable with K values → indicator vector, e.g., red = [1,0,0].\n- **Feature crosses:** linear model on one-hot features captures only **main effects**, not **interaction effects**. Explicit feature crosses (composite features) capture interactions → data becomes **wide format**.\n\n### 1.5.4 Preprocessing text data (PDF p. 53)\n- Challenges: variable length, high-cardinality categorical words, **out-of-vocabulary (OOV)** words.\n- **Bag of words (BoW):** ignore word order; map words → tokens; represent doc as count vector (**vector space model**). Preprocessing: drop punctuation, lowercase, **stop word removal**, **word stemming**.\n- **TF-IDF:** down-weight globally frequent words. IDF_i = log(N / (1+DF_i)); TFIDF_ij = log(TF_ij + 1) × IDF_i.\n- **Word embeddings:** map sparse one-hot vectors to dense low-D vectors so semantically similar words are close (fixes that one-hot/count vectors put \"man\" no closer to \"woman\" than to \"banana\"). Often use a **pre-trained embedding** → model stays linear in W.\n- **OOV / novel words:** replace with **UNK**, or use **subword units / wordpieces** via **byte-pair encoding (BPE)**.\n\n### 1.5.5 Handling missing data (PDF p. 56)\n- Missingness matrix M (M_nd = 1 if feature missing).\n- **MCAR** (missing completely at random): missingness independent of all features. **MAR** (missing at random): depends only on *observed* features. **NMAR** (not missing at random): depends on the hidden values themselves — must model the missingness mechanism.\n- Common fixes: **mean value imputation**, or fit a generative model to fill in missing values. (This book assumes MAR.)\n\n---\n\n## 1.6 Discussion (PDF p. 57)\n\n- **ML vs. related fields:** predictive analytics (business-focused supervised learning), data mining (structured/commercial DBs), data science (ML + stats + integration/visualization), **statistics** (very close), **AI** (ML now used so an AI system acquires its own knowledge; \"ML\" and \"AI\" sometimes used interchangeably).\n- **Structure of the book:** probability theory is the unifying lens. Part I = foundations; Parts II–IV = supervised; Part V = unsupervised. RL is covered in the sequel [Mur23].\n- **Caveats:** loss functions are hard to specify → **reward hacking**, part of the broader **alignment problem** (AI ethics/safety). **Inverse RL (IRL)** infers reward from human behavior. **AGI** (autonomous decisions) vs. **IA / augmented intelligence** (human in the loop).\n\n---\n\n## Key formulas cheat-sheet\n| Concept | Formula |\n|---|---|\n| Empirical risk | L(θ) = (1/N) Σ ℓ(yₙ, f(xₙ;θ)) |\n| Misclassification rate | zero-one loss ℓ₀₁ = I(y ≠ ŷ) |\n| Softmax | softmax(a)_c = e^{a_c} / Σ_{c'} e^{a_{c'}} |\n| Regression (ℓ₂) loss / MSE | (y − ŷ)² ; MSE = (1/N) Σ (yₙ − f(xₙ;θ))² |\n| NLL (fitting probabilistic models) | −(1/N) Σ log p(yₙ | f(xₙ;θ)); MLE = argmin NLL |\n| Generalization gap | L(θ; p*) − L(θ; D_train) |\n\n---\n\n## ML4T Exam 1 relevance\n- **→ ML Domain 1 (Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing) [PRIMARY]:** supervised vs. unsupervised vs. reinforcement learning distinctions; classification vs. regression; the T/P/E framing; conditional p(y|x) vs. unconditional p(x) models; loss functions, empirical risk minimization, and MLE as the fitting objective. This is the core week-1 vocabulary the exam tests.\n- **→ ML Domain 8/9 (Model Evaluation, Overfitting & Generalization):** overfitting vs. underfitting, U-shaped test-error curve, generalization gap, train/validation/test split (validation = model selection; test = future-performance estimate only), degrees of freedom, No Free Lunch / inductive bias, and (for ML4T) why a model that memorizes in-sample data fails out-of-sample — directly analogous to backtest overfitting on historical price data.\n- **Supporting:** feature engineering/preprocessing (polynomial features, one-hot, feature crosses), linear vs. polynomial regression and least squares (ties to the course's regression-learner and decision-tree material), and epistemic vs. aleatoric uncertainty.\n"}]}, {"part": "ML", "num": "09", "name": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "cheatsheet": "- **Overfitting vs. underfitting** is read off the *pair* of training and out-of-sample errors, never one alone:\n  - **Overfitting**: near-zero (very low) training error but much worse test/validation error → the model memorized sample-specific noise. Symptom of **high variance**. Fixes: reduce capacity, add representative data, regularize, early-stop.\n  - **Underfitting**: high error on *both* training and test, roughly similar → the model is too simple to capture the true structure. Symptom of **high bias**. Fixes: add capacity / richer features; more data does NOT help.\n  - A **small train–test gap alone does not prove generalization** — two similarly *poor* scores mean the model is consistently weak, not useful. You need low *and* similar errors.\n- **Bias–variance tradeoff** (expected out-of-sample error = bias² + variance + irreducible error):\n  - Increasing model complexity/capacity typically **lowers bias but raises variance**; it does *not* reduce both at once. The best model minimizes *total* expected out-of-sample error, not either term in isolation.\n  - Validation error is **U-shaped** in complexity: falls then rises. The depth/complexity with lowest *validation* error is generally a better choice than the one with lowest *training* error.\n  - **High variance ≠ high training error** — a high-variance model often fits each training set extremely well (low training error) yet swings wildly across samples.\n  - **Low variance ≠ accurate** — a stable model (e.g., a straight line on a curved relationship) can be consistently and systematically wrong (high bias).\n- **Model capacity vs. data size**:\n  - More *representative* data reduces variance/overfitting by making recurring patterns easier to distinguish from noise — but it **cannot cure bias**: it never expands the functions a fixed hypothesis class can express (a linear model stays linear no matter how many rows).\n  - **Duplicated rows add no independent information**; only genuinely new observations help.\n  - More data does **not guarantee** an arbitrarily complex model won't overfit (capacity, noise, leakage, bad sampling still bite), and quantity **cannot compensate** for data drawn from the wrong distribution/regime (representativeness ≥ raw count).\n- **Cross-validation & generalization estimation**:\n  - k-fold: each fold is held out for evaluation exactly once; a *separate* model is fit per round (the held-out fold is excluded from that round's training). Averaging fold scores gives a less split-specific estimate than one arbitrary holdout.\n  - **Selection bias / optimism**: picking the best of many tuned alternatives makes the winning CV score optimistic. A separate **untouched test set** is needed for an honest final estimate — don't reuse the same evidence for selection and final assessment.\n  - **Leakage discipline**: any preprocessing that *learns from data* (normalization, scaling) must be fit **within each training fold**, never once on the full dataset — global fitting leaks held-out info.\n  - **Time order matters**: for time series, random folds can train on the future and test on the past (unrealistic). Use forward/rolling (time-aware) splits. More folds do NOT repair a wrong splitting design; CV is not \"invalid for all time series,\" just needs adaptation.\n- **Detecting overfitting in practice**:\n  - Large train–test gap; **instability** (splits/structure/returns change dramatically across resamples); **\"too good to be true\"** results.\n  - Implausibly strong scores → check for **leakage** (e.g., a feature built from the *next day's* close) and evaluation errors before believing a \"market inefficiency.\"\n  - **Selection/search overfitting**: trying hundreds of configs on one validation period will surface a chance winner; its edge collapses out of sample. Record the full search and re-evaluate the pick on fresh data.\n  - Reporting only the best of several runs hides variance and creates false optimism.\n- **Controlling model complexity** (hyperparameters as capacity knobs):\n  - Trees: **lower max depth** and **larger min leaf size** reduce effective complexity. **KNN**: larger *k* smooths / lowers capacity (more bias, less variance); *k*=1 is highest capacity (low bias, high variance, ~0 training error).\n  - **Regularization**: penalizing large coefficients constrains (does not free) the fit; moderate strength trades a little bias for lower variance and better generalization; too strong → underfitting. A positive penalty alone does not \"eliminate\" the need for validation.\n  - **Early stopping**: halt near best validation iteration — a form of complexity control. Stop too early → underfit. Lowest *training* error does not imply best out-of-sample model.\n  - Complexity knobs should be tuned on **validation** evidence, keeping the final **test set independent** of tuning. No setting is guaranteed to help on every dataset.", "traps": "- **Inverted-logic questions** (6 of 18 here): the Answer Key reads \"**True = incorrect; False = accurate**,\" so a **False** verdict means the statement is *actually correct*, and **True** means it is *flawed*. Read the polarity line first every time. Inverted here: **G2Q1, G2Q2, G3Q2, G4Q2, G5Q1, G6Q1.**\n- **Double-negative / trap \"must / always / necessarily / guarantees\" choices**: absolute claims are the planted wrong answers. Watch \"must reduce both bias and variance\" (G2Q1D), \"necessarily correct the problem\" (G1Q2C), \"always identifies the best model\" (G1Q3D), \"guarantees … cannot overfit\" (G3Q1D), \"every model class can represent every target\" (G3Q2D), \"guarantees … all future periods\" (G4Q2D), \"always increases capacity\" (G2Q3E), \"automatically correct any violation\" (G4Q3E), \"guaranteed to improve … every dataset\" (G6Q1D), \"eliminates the need for validation\" (G6Q2E), \"must produce the best out-of-sample model\" (G6Q3D).\n- **Small train–test gap ≠ good model.** Two similarly poor scores (underfitting) show consistency, not predictive value (G1Q2E, G1Q3, G6). Judge on *level* and *gap* together.\n- **\"Too good to be true\" = suspect leakage first.** A next-day-close feature or full-dataset normalization inflates scores; a high score does not prove a durable edge (G5Q1, G5Q3). In inverted G5Q1, choice D (\"high score proves durable inefficiency\") is the one flawed claim marked **True**.\n- **More data fixes variance, not bias.** Adding rows (even a million) to a linear model on a curved target does not remove approximation error (G3Q2); duplicated rows add nothing (G3Q1C); wrong-regime data doesn't generalize (G3Q3).\n- **Instability is a variance symptom.** Splits/returns swinging across resamples = high variance/overfitting even with ~0 training error; never report only the best run (G5Q2).", "references": "1. **ISLR — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning* (James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani):** §2.2 (assessing model accuracy, bias-variance tradeoff, irreducible error, U-shaped test-error curve), §2.2.3 (KNN flexibility vs. k), ch.5 (cross-validation, the validation set and k-fold CV), §6.2 (shrinkage / regularization: ridge and lasso), ch.8 (trees, depth and pruning as complexity controls).\n2. **ESL — *The Elements of Statistical Learning* (Hastie, Tibshirani, Friedman):** ch.7 (model assessment & selection, bias-variance decomposition, generalization gap, optimism of the training error rate), ch.3 (ridge regression), ch.8 (bagging / tree instability under resampling).\n3. **scikit-learn User Guide:** \"Underfitting vs. overfitting\" example, \"Validation curves\" and \"Learning curves,\" \"Cross-validation\" (k-fold, TimeSeriesSplit), and \"Common pitfalls and recommended practices: data leakage\" (preprocessing fit inside CV folds).\n4. **Concept references:** bias-variance decomposition (expected error = bias² + variance + irreducible error); regularization / shrinkage and early stopping as capacity control; learning curves (data size vs. error); generalization gap.\n5. **Non-stationarity / backtest overfitting:** regime shift and covariate/distribution shift; look-ahead and target leakage; multiple-comparisons / data-snooping bias in strategy search (e.g., deflated Sharpe ratio) for the detection-in-practice questions.", "bookNotes": [{"book": "DeepLearning", "title": "Ch. 1 — The Deep Learning Revolution (Overview)", "file": "references/DeepLearning/Ch01_Overview.md", "md": "# Ch. 1 — The Deep Learning Revolution (Overview)\n\n**Source:** Bishop & Bishop, *Deep Learning: Foundations and Concepts* (Springer, 2024).\n**Book pages 1–21** (PDF pages 21–41). Section 1.1 pp. 2–5; §1.2 Tutorial Example pp. 6–15; §1.3 Brief History pp. 16–21.\n\n> **→ ML4T Exam-1 relevance: ML Domain 1 (Learning Paradigms), ML Domain 9 (Generalization / Overfitting), plus the core supervised-vs-unsupervised framing tested throughout.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. What deep learning is (§1.1)\n- **Deep learning** = machine learning based on multilayer **neural networks** trained on large data sets; a single general framework replaces many hand-crafted, application-specific algorithms.\n- **Learning / training** = the process of setting a model's adjustable **parameters (weights)** from data. Modern nets can have millions to ~1 trillion (10^12) parameters.\n- Application examples span medical diagnosis, protein structure (AlphaFold), image synthesis, and large language models (LLMs) — all variants of the *same* framework.\n\n### Learning paradigms (from the four examples) — **exam-critical**\n- **Supervised learning:** each training example has a known target/label.\n  - **Classification:** output assigned to a discrete set of classes (e.g., benign vs. malignant).\n  - **Regression:** output is one or more *continuous* variables (e.g., predict chemical yield from temp/pressure).\n- **Unsupervised learning:** data is *unlabelled* (e.g., generating new synthetic face images).\n- **Generative AI / autoregressive models:** LLMs take a word sequence and predict the next word; the augmented sequence is fed back to generate more (used for text generation).\n\n---\n\n## 2. The polynomial curve-fitting tutorial (§1.2) — the backbone example\n\nSetup: input `x`, target `t`; training set of N observations; goal is **generalization** = accurate prediction on previously unseen inputs. Data = underlying regularity (here sin(2πx)) + random (Gaussian) noise.\n\n**Linear model (polynomial):**\n$$y(x,\\mathbf{w}) = w_0 + w_1 x + \\dots + w_M x^M = \\sum_{j=0}^{M} w_j x^j \\tag{1.1}$$\n- Nonlinear in `x` but **linear in the coefficients w** → called a **linear model**.\n\n**Sum-of-squares error function:**\n$$E(\\mathbf{w}) = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\{y(x_n,\\mathbf{w}) - t_n\\}^2 \\tag{1.2}$$\n- Non-negative; zero iff the curve passes through every point. Quadratic in w → **unique closed-form minimum** w*.\n\n**Root-mean-square (RMS) error** (lets you compare across data-set sizes, same units as t):\n$$E_{RMS} = \\sqrt{\\tfrac{1}{N}\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\{y(x_n,\\mathbf{w}^\\*) - t_n\\}^2} \\tag{1.3}$$\n\n### Model complexity & overfitting — **exam-critical**\n- Order **M is a hyperparameter** controlling model complexity.\n- Low M (0,1): underfits (too inflexible). M=3: good fit. **M=9: fits training data exactly (E=0) but oscillates wildly → over-fitting**, large test error.\n- Overfitting symptom: coefficient magnitudes grow very large as M increases (finely tuned to noise).\n- **More data reduces overfitting:** a given complex model over-fits less as N grows. Classical heuristic: N ≥ 5–10× the number of parameters (though deep learning routinely violates this).\n\n### Regularization (§1.2.5) — **exam-critical**\nAdd a penalty on coefficient size to the error:\n$$\\tilde{E}(\\mathbf{w}) = \\tfrac{1}{2}\\sum_{n=1}^{N}\\{y(x_n,\\mathbf{w}) - t_n\\}^2 + \\tfrac{\\lambda}{2}\\|\\mathbf{w}\\|^2 \\tag{1.4}$$\nwhere $\\|\\mathbf{w}\\|^2 = w_0^2 + \\dots + w_M^2$; λ controls regularization strength.\n- Known as **shrinkage** (statistics) or **weight decay** (neural nets).\n- λ controls *effective* model complexity: too small → overfit; too large → underfit. (w0 often excluded from the penalty.)\n\n### Model selection (§1.2.6) — **exam-critical**\n- Hyperparameters (λ, M) **cannot** be chosen by minimizing training error (would drive λ→0, M large → overfit).\n- Partition data: **training set** (fit w) + **validation / hold-out / development set** (choose model) + optionally a **test set** for final unbiased evaluation.\n- **S-fold cross-validation:** split into S groups; train on S−1, validate on the held-out group; rotate; average scores. Uses (S−1)/S of data for training. **S = N → leave-one-out.**\n- Drawback: cost multiplies by S; combinatorial explosion with many hyperparameters. Modern large models rely more on heuristics/experience than exhaustive search.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Brief history of ML / neural networks (§1.3)\n\n**Single artificial neuron:**\n$$a = \\sum_{i=1}^{M} w_i x_i \\tag{1.5}, \\qquad y = f(a) \\tag{1.6}$$\n- `a` = pre-activation; `f(·)` = activation function; `y` = activation; `w_i` = weights (synapse strengths). Polynomial (1.1) is a special case (f = identity, inputs = powers of x).\n\n**Three phases:**\n1. **Single-layer networks / perceptron** (Rosenblatt): step activation (fires if weighted input > threshold). Perceptron algorithm provably converges if data is linearly separable. Minsky & Papert (1969) proved single-layer limitations (and wrongly conjectured they'd extend to multilayer) → \"AI winter.\" A modern net is still sometimes called a **multilayer perceptron (MLP)**.\n2. **Backpropagation era (mid-1980s):** replace step function with **differentiable** activations + differentiable error functions → gradients computable. **Hidden units** = middle-layer nodes (values not in training data). **Feed-forward networks**; trained by **error backpropagation** (Rumelhart, Hinton, Williams 1986) + **stochastic gradient descent**. Recognized central role of probability/statistics and **inductive bias / prior knowledge**. Limitation: only last ~2 layers learned useful values; heavy reliance on hand-crafted **feature extraction**.\n3. **Deep networks (2010s–now):** many-layer nets trainable effectively → **deep learning** (LeCun, Bengio, Hinton 2015). Driven by scale (parameters up to ~10^12), massive data, and **GPUs** (parallelism). Compute to train SOTA models: doubling ~2 yrs pre-2012 (Moore's law), then ~3.4 months after 2012.\n\n---\n\n## Quick exam anchors\n- Supervised = labelled; classification (discrete) vs. regression (continuous). Unsupervised = unlabelled.\n- Generalization is the goal; overfitting = fitting noise; controlled by complexity, data size, and regularization.\n- Validation set / cross-validation choose hyperparameters; test set gives final estimate.\n- Regularization = weight decay = shrinkage; λ penalizes large weights.\n"}]}, {"part": "ML", "num": "10", "name": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "cheatsheet": "- **No free lunch.** No single learner is universally best; the \"right\" model is contextual — chosen against the *problem's* structure, data, and the decision it supports, not from theoretical flexibility alone. Compare candidates on representative (ideally time-aware, out-of-sample) validation, not on which family \"can represent more functions.\"\n- **Match model to problem structure:**\n  - Approximately **linear / proportional** relationships → a linear model is a strong, retrainable baseline (fast, transparent, low variance).\n  - **Threshold / interaction** effects → decision trees encode conditional splits without hand-specifying each interaction; linear models can still reach some nonlinearity via feature engineering (transforms, interaction terms).\n  - Flexibility ≠ accuracy: a more expressive learner can fit **noise** and generalize *worse*, especially with limited/noisy samples where a simpler, lower-variance model often wins.\n- **Accuracy vs. interpretability tradeoff.** A small accuracy gain from a black-box model may not justify the loss of explainability when governance, risk, or compliance requires justifying decisions (e.g., adverse-action explanations). Interpretability can be a *binding constraint* even when performance differs — and being \"interpretable\" does **not** certify inputs are leakage-free or the design is sound. Document the accepted tradeoff to make it reviewable. Interpretability and accuracy are **not** always perfectly opposed — a model can improve one without harming the other.\n- **Training time vs. inference time — evaluate each against the workflow where its cost occurs.** They are decoupled: a model can train slowly and infer in microseconds (or the reverse). **Eager/parametric** learners (linear, trees) pay to fit once, then predict cheaply from a compact representation. **Lazy** learners (**KNN**) do almost no fitting (just store data) but push heavy computation to inference — neighbor search cost grows with the stored set N and consumes memory. A 40-min overnight train is fine in a 4-hr window; millisecond inference for a weekly model is irrelevant slack.\n- **Sensitivity to noise / data quality.** Least-squares regression is highly sensitive to extreme target values (residuals are **squared**). KNN is sensitive to **feature scale** (large-magnitude features dominate distance) — fix with scaling; larger *k* damps isolated noisy neighbors but adds bias. Deep single trees are unstable — a few corrected rows can restructure early splits. Fix root-cause data defects at the source; **bagging** reduces *variance* but does **not** immunize against *systematic* errors/corruption shared across all learners.\n- **Data size vs. model complexity.** More candidate features enlarge the hypothesis space and *increase* the data required; limited observations relative to flexibility → overfitting (perfect training fit is weak evidence of generalization). Restrict depth / min-leaf-size / feature count to cut effective capacity. More data helps distinguish signal from noise but does **not** eliminate the need to control complexity — and raw row count can **overstate** usable information if much of it comes from obsolete regimes (distribution shift). Effective sample *relevance* matters, not just count.\n- **Real-time constraints.** A model that violates the latency budget is unusable regardless of offline accuracy — a timely, slightly-less-accurate model can produce better realized outcomes. Measure **end-to-end latency under peak load** (feature computation + system overhead + tail/high-percentile latency, not just one isolated prediction or the average). Latency requirements are strategy-specific: market-making (microseconds) ≠ daily rebalance (minutes) — \"must be HFT-suitable\" is not a universal requirement.\n- **Robustness & stability in production.** Strong *average* backtest performance can hide failure in economically important regimes (e.g., high volatility) — evaluate segmented by regime. Historical success does not guarantee future stability (regimes shift). Prefer models whose outputs are stable under small perturbations and across time splits (bagging lowers prediction variance); a simpler, dependable fallback can be valuable even if not top performer in calm conditions. Stability analysis must consider both predictions *and* downstream decisions (turnover, exposures). Retraining more often does **not** necessarily increase stability — it can amplify sensitivity to short-term sample changes.\n- **Comparative reasoning (linear / KNN / tree):** linear = fast, transparent, stable, but underfits without feature engineering; tree = captures thresholds and is inspectable (subject to depth/pruning), but deep trees are unstable; KNN = accurate offline but slow/memory-heavy at inference with the full dataset and provides no compact global equation. Practical selection *manages* these tradeoffs (feature engineering, depth limits/bagging, reference-set reduction) rather than declaring one algorithm inherently superior.", "traps": "- **Inverted-logic questions (11 of 24): mark True if the statement is *incorrect*, False if *accurate*.** The Answer Key line reads \"True = incorrect; False = accurate.\" Read that polarity line FIRST. Inverted here: **G1Q2, G1Q3, G2Q2, G3Q2, G4Q3, G5Q1, G5Q2, G6Q1, G7Q1, G8Q1, G8Q2.** On these, a \"False\" verdict means the choice is a *correct* statement, and \"True\" flags the *flawed/wrong* claim you were asked to find.\n- **The trap-within-a-trap.** On inverted questions the plausible-sounding, textbook-correct statements resolve to **False**, and the one overreaching/absolute claim (e.g., \"guarantees,\" \"always,\" \"need not understand,\" \"proves\") is the **True** answer. Do not let \"this statement is true in real life\" push you to mark True — that is exactly backwards under inverted polarity.\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices:** none appear in Domain 10 — no choice uses the \"It is incorrect to conclude that [accurate statement]\" construction. (Watch for it in other domains, where the Answer Key marks such choices False because the inner claim is accurate.) The nearest analog here is the inverted-polarity resolution above.\n- **Absolute-language distractors are the reliable \"flawed\" pick.** Words like *always, guarantees, automatically, must, eliminates, immune, universal, need not, dominates, irrelevant* signal the overreaching claim — the intended wrong statement in nearly every question (normal or inverted).\n- **Flexibility ≠ better generalization; more data / more features ≠ automatically better.** Recurs across G1, G3, G5. More flexibility or more inputs can raise variance and data requirements, not lower error.\n- **\"Interpretable / good validation score / bagging / infrequent retraining\" do NOT certify what they seem to.** Transparency doesn't validate data or rule out leakage (G2Q1E); a good aggregate score doesn't certify record-level integrity (G4Q1E); bagging doesn't fix systematic corruption (G4Q3C); rare retraining doesn't make inference cost irrelevant (G3Q3D). These \"guarantee\" claims are the planted errors.", "references": "1. **ISLR — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning* (James, Witten, Hastie, Tibshirani):** §2.1.3 (flexibility–interpretability tradeoff), §2.2 (assessing accuracy, bias–variance, overfitting, non-stationarity), §2.2.3 (KNN and inference cost), ch.3 (linear regression, outliers/leverage, collinearity), ch.5 (cross-validation/resampling), ch.6 (subset selection, regularization), ch.8 (trees, pruning, bagging, random forests).\n2. **ESL — *The Elements of Statistical Learning* (Hastie, Tibshirani, Friedman):** ch.7 (model assessment & selection), ch.8/ch.15 (bagging, ensembles, variance reduction), ch.10 (robust loss functions).\n3. **scikit-learn User Guide:** \"Choosing the right estimator\" (task→model), \"Nearest Neighbors\" (query complexity/memory), \"Preprocessing\" (feature scaling for distance-based models), \"Computational performance\" (train vs. inference latency, throughput), \"Model interpretation.\"\n4. **Concept — No-Free-Lunch theorem & Occam's razor:** no learner is best across all problems; prefer the simplest model adequate to the data and constraints.\n5. **Concept — Deployment constraints (latency, throughput, memory, tail latency) and model risk management:** realized vs. offline performance, regime-segmented evaluation, stability/robustness monitoring, graceful-degradation fallbacks.\n6. **Grinold & Kahn — *Active Portfolio Management*:** linking predictive signal to net-of-cost portfolio outcomes (turnover, exposure stability, transaction costs) for the production-suitability questions.", "bookNotes": []}, {"part": "QF", "num": "01", "name": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "cheatsheet": "- **One-period (holding-period) return:** `r = (P_end − P_begin + income) / P_begin`. The percentage depends on the *starting price*, not the raw dollar change — an $8 move on $80 (10%) is worse than a $4 move on $20 (20%).\n- **Linking periods multiplicatively:** cumulative return over consecutive periods is `(1+r₁)(1+r₂)…(1+rₙ) − 1`, **not** the sum of the returns. Summing (`r₁+r₂`) is only a first-order approximation and ignores compounding.\n- **Each period's return applies to the value entering that period.** A −6% second-quarter return applies to the *post-first-quarter* balance ($10.8M), not the original ($10M).\n- **CAGR (compound annual growth rate):** `CAGR = (V_end / V_begin)^(1/n) − 1` — the single constant annual rate that connects the endpoints. Example: $100M → $121M over 2 yrs ⇒ `(121/100)^(1/2) − 1 = 10%`. Dividing total return by n (21%/2 = 10.5%) is **wrong** — it ignores compounding.\n- **Compounding growth factor:** `$1 → (1+r)^n`. `(1.07)^30 ≈ 7.61`. Small annual rate gaps compound into large terminal-wealth gaps; you cannot evaluate a 0.25pp gap by multiplying 0.25% × years.\n- **Rule of 72:** doubling time ≈ `72 / (annual % return)`. 72/6 ≈ 12 yrs (exact `ln2/ln1.06 ≈ 11.9`); 72/4 ≈ 18 yrs. It is an approximation, not a guarantee.\n- **Arithmetic mean** `(Σrᵢ)/n` = best estimate of a *single* future period's return. **Geometric mean** `(Π(1+rᵢ))^(1/n) − 1` = the realized constant compound rate over multiple periods (describes terminal wealth).\n- **Volatility drag / Jensen gap:** with any return dispersion, geometric mean ≤ arithmetic mean; they are equal only when every period's return is identical. +30%/−20% ⇒ arithmetic 5% but geometric `√1.04 − 1 ≈ 1.98%`.\n- **Annualization units differ:** *returns* compound → `(1+r_period)^(periods/yr) − 1`; *volatility* scales with the **square root of time** → `σ_period × √(periods/yr)` (under i.i.d. assumption). E.g. 1% monthly → `(1.01)^12 − 1 ≈ 12.68%`; 3% monthly σ → `3%×√12 ≈ 10.39%`. Trading-day count (252) drives both figures.\n- **Portfolio return = weighted sum of asset returns:** `r_p = Σ wᵢ rᵢ`. An asset's *return* (10%) differs from its *contribution* (weight × return = 6pp at 60% weight). Long weights >0, short weights <0; a negative weight × negative return = positive contribution.\n- **Leverage:** invested weight >100% funded by borrowing magnifies both gains and losses; net financing cost (`−borrow% × borrow rate`) is subtracted. 120% ETF at 10% less 20% borrowed at 1% ⇒ `12% − 0.2% = 11.8%`.\n- **Gross vs net vs benchmark-relative:** net = gross − fees − costs. Excess (relative) return = fund return − benchmark return. A fund can beat its benchmark on a relative basis while posting a negative absolute return (−6% vs −10% ⇒ +4pp). Hedge-fund fees layer: management fee on capital, then incentive fee on *post-management-fee* profit.\n- **Path dependence / sequence-of-returns:** without cash flows, order doesn't matter (multiplication commutes). *With* withdrawals or contributions, order matters — same returns produce different terminal wealth. Equal ± percentage moves don't offset: +50% then −50% ⇒ $100→$150→$75.\n- **Recovery asymmetry:** a loss of x% needs a gain larger than x% to recover, because the gain applies to a smaller base. −30% (100→70) needs `100/70 − 1 ≈ 42.86%`, not 30%; a 30% gain only reaches $91M (still 9% below peak).", "traps": "- **Inverted-logic questions in this slice: NONE.** All 21 questions (G1Q1–G7Q3) use a plain `Answer Key:` — True = accurate statement, False = inaccurate. Do not assume inversion just because rationales say \"reverses the correct conclusion\" (that phrase describes the *choice's* embedded double negative, not the question's polarity).\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices — the real trap here.** Many choices are phrased as a negation, e.g. \"Its CAGR is **not** 10% per year\" or \"The two-year cumulative return is **not** 4%.\" When the underlying fact IS true (CAGR *is* 10%), the negated statement is **False**. Read the whole sentence: verdict tracks the *negated* statement, and the rationale \"reverses the correct conclusion\" is your signal the writer buried a truth inside a \"not.\"\n- **\"A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that…\" choices.** These flip twice. If the underlying claim is correct, then \"would be wrong to accept it\" is itself **False** (rationale: \"The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted\"). Examples: G1Q3-A, G3Q3-A/B, G4Q2-E, G7Q3-C.\n- **Summing instead of compounding.** Adding periodic returns (8% + −6% = 2%; +50% + −50% = 0%) is only an approximation and understates the compounding effect; the exact link is multiplicative. Classic distractor in G1Q2-C, G3Q1, G7Q1.\n- **Arithmetic-for-geometric substitution.** Reporting the arithmetic mean as \"the return you actually earned\" overstates realized compound growth whenever returns vary (volatility drag). G3Q1-E, G3Q3-D.\n- **Volatility annualized like a return.** Choices that scale σ by the period count (×12 or ×252) instead of by √(periods) are False — σ scales with √time. G4Q1-D, G4Q2-D.\n- **Leverage \"nets out.\"** Claims that a 100% *net* exposure means leverage is irrelevant ignore *gross* exposure and financing; both the >100% position and the borrow cost matter. G5Q3-D, G5Q2-D.", "references": "- **CFA Program Curriculum** — Quantitative Methods: Time Value of Money, Rates of Return, and Statistical Measures of Asset Returns (arithmetic vs geometric mean, holding-period and money-/time-weighted returns).\n- **Bodie, Kane & Marcus, *Investments*** — chapters on rates of return, risk and return over multiple periods, portfolio return aggregation, leverage, and hedge-fund fee structures.\n- **Investopedia** — \"Compound Annual Growth Rate (CAGR),\" \"Geometric Mean,\" \"Arithmetic vs Geometric Mean,\" \"Rule of 72,\" \"Volatility Drag,\" \"Annualized Volatility,\" \"Sequence Risk,\" \"Drawdown.\"\n- **Investopedia** — \"Holding Period Return,\" \"Excess/Net Return,\" \"Effective Annual Rate,\" and \"Weighted Average Return\" for periodic-return and portfolio-aggregation mechanics.", "bookNotes": []}, {"part": "QF", "num": "02", "name": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "cheatsheet": "- **Volatility / variance / standard deviation** measure *dispersion of returns around the mean*, not a fixed loss.\n  - **Variance** = average of squared deviations from the mean (units = squared returns). **Standard deviation (σ)** = √variance, back in the same units as returns.\n  - Squaring gives **outliers disproportionate weight** — one extreme observation can dominate measured dispersion.\n  - **Equal average returns do NOT imply equal risk**: a constant-return series has zero variance; an alternating series with the same mean is more dispersed.\n  - A \"24% volatility\" is a spread estimate, *not* a guaranteed loss of 24% or a frequency of loss. Low volatility does **not** eliminate tail risk, estimation error, or the possibility of loss.\n  - Every included observation affects **both** the mean and the dispersion — you cannot drop a point's effect from one and not the other.\n- **Downside risk & drawdown** are *path-dependent* — they depend on the order of returns, unlike σ.\n  - **Drawdown** = decline from a prior *peak (high-water mark)*, measured `(trough − peak)/peak`. **Maximum drawdown (MDD)** = largest such peak-to-subsequent-trough drop over the path.\n  - Drawdown is measured from the running peak, **not** from inception or to the ending value — a fund up on inception can still have had a deep drawdown.\n  - **Recovery asymmetry**: a loss of `L` needs a gain of `1/(1−L) − 1` to recover. −25% needs +33.3%; a fall from 125→90 needs +38.9% to regain 125.\n  - σ and MDD are **not** the same feature: a strategy can show low volatility for years and then suffer a severe drawdown. Deep interim losses matter because withdrawals, governance/liquidity limits, or behavior can force loss realization even if the strategy later recovers.\n- **Sharpe ratio** = (return − risk-free rate) / total volatility = **excess return per unit of total risk**.\n  - The **risk-free rate must be subtracted** first (that's what makes it *excess* return). Higher Sharpe = more reward per unit of σ.\n  - Raw return alone is insufficient — a higher-return fund can have a *lower* Sharpe if its volatility is disproportionately larger.\n  - **Annualization**: under IID daily returns, Sharpe scales by **√T** (e.g., ×√252), NOT ×252. Numerator and denominator must share the same time scale.\n  - The √time rule **breaks** under autocorrelation or time-varying volatility; Sharpe also under-describes **non-normal** (skewed, fat-tailed) distributions and identical Sharpes can hide very different loss/drawdown profiles.\n- **Sortino ratio** = (return − minimum acceptable return, \"MAR\") / **downside deviation** (dispersion of returns *below* the target only).\n  - Sharpe penalizes upside and downside volatility equally; **Sortino penalizes only shortfalls**, so it aligns with a client who fears losses but welcomes upside surprises.\n  - Large *positive* deviations do **not** raise downside deviation. Similar total σ can mask very different downside behavior → Sortino can rank funds differently than Sharpe.\n  - The **chosen target/MAR is not optional**: it defines the numerator (excess over target) and which outcomes count as shortfalls; changing it can flip the comparison.\n  - With **few/no observed downside months** the denominator is near-zero → the ratio is unstable or undefined; a benign short sample makes tail-selling strategies look artificially safe.\n- **Information ratio (IR)** = **active return** / **tracking error** = benchmark-relative reward per unit of active risk.\n  - **Active return** = portfolio return − benchmark return. **Tracking error** = volatility (std dev) of the benchmark-relative (active) returns, usually annualized.\n  - IR measures skill of *active management* vs. a benchmark; Sharpe measures total risk-adjusted return vs. the *risk-free* rate. Different reference returns, different risk denominators.\n  - A larger raw excess return is **not** better if it used proportionally more tracking error (e.g., 2%/2.5% = 0.80 beats 4%/8% = 0.50).\n  - A portfolio can have a modest Sharpe yet a strong IR (tracks a risky benchmark closely and adds consistent value) — the two properties are distinct.\n- **Risk-return tradeoffs are investor-specific.** Higher expected return normally comes with higher risk; the \"right\" choice depends on horizon, liquidity needs, drawdown tolerance, governance, and willingness/capacity to bear loss. There is **no universal ranking** — a single summary statistic cannot capture all constraints.\n- **Limitations of risk metrics** (why raw metrics mislead):\n  - σ treats upside and downside symmetrically → two funds with equal σ can have opposite skew/tail behavior; a downside measure distinguishes them.\n  - Metrics are **sample-period sensitive**: choosing a calm window (excluding a crisis) lowers apparent risk and flatters performance. Recency alone doesn't make a window valid.\n  - **Non-normal / fat-tailed** returns make extremes more likely than a normal model implies; historical estimates are unstable and can be **gamed** by selective windows or methodology.\n  - **Backtest overfitting**: extensive model/hyperparameter selection inflates the chosen backtest; low backtested MDD is an observed statistic, not a guaranteed future bound. Out-of-sample, sensitivity, and stress testing probe robustness a headline metric conceals.", "traps": "- **No inverted-logic questions in this domain.** All 21 items (QF-D2G1Q1 … QF-D2G7Q3) use a plain `Answer Key:` (normal polarity: True = accurate, False = inaccurate). There is *no* \"True = incorrect; False = accurate\" block anywhere in QF_D02, so do not invert — but stay alert, since sibling domains do embed the trap.\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices** — the phrasing *\"A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that [an accurate statement]\"*. The embedded claim is true, so being \"wrong to accept\" it is **False**. Parse the inner claim, judge it, then negate. Appears in **G2Q3C** and **G6Q1D** (both marked **False**, \"the underlying claim is correct and should be accepted\").\n- **\"Reverses the correct conclusion\" negation choices** — statements written in the negative that flip a *true* fact into a false claim (e.g., \"is not a 28% drawdown,\" \"the annualized Sharpe is not ~0.79,\" \"tracking error is not 6%,\" \"Manager B's IR is not 0.50,\" \"Sharpe is not ~0.53\"). These read plausibly but the Key marks them **False**. Compute the number, then check whether the \"not\" makes the sentence lie. Seen in G2Q1A/B, G3Q2B, G4Q1A, G5Q1B, G5Q2B, G6Q2A.\n- **Equal means / equal ending wealth / equal σ ≠ equal risk.** Recurring bait: identical averages (G1Q1C), identical terminal wealth (G2Q3A), or identical σ (G7Q1B) are offered as proof of identical risk. Always **False** — dispersion, path, skew, and tails can differ.\n- **Volatility ≠ a guaranteed loss.** \"24% volatility means it loses 24% one year in two\" (G1Q2C) and \"lower volatility guarantees no large loss\" (G1Q2D) are both **False** — σ is a dispersion estimate, not a loss magnitude or frequency.\n- **Annualization scale trap.** Sharpe scales by **√T**, not T (G3Q2C False, G3Q2B/E False). Numerator and denominator must be on the same time base.\n- **A high Sharpe/Sortino does not prove tail safety** (G3Q3A, G4Q3A False) — especially for short samples of tail-selling (\"disaster insurance\") strategies where the denominator is tiny and the loss simply hasn't shown up yet.", "references": "1. **Grinold & Kahn — *Active Portfolio Management*:** definition and use of the Information Ratio (active return / tracking error), active risk, and the distinction between active management skill and total risk-adjusted return — for the G5 (information ratio) questions.\n2. **CFA Institute curriculum — Quantitative Methods & Portfolio Management:** measures of dispersion (variance, standard deviation, downside deviation), skewness/kurtosis and non-normality, risk-adjusted performance (Sharpe, Sortino, Information Ratio), and investor objectives/constraints (IPS, risk tolerance vs. ability) — foundational across all seven topic groups.\n3. **Investopedia — Sharpe Ratio:** definition, excess-return-over-risk-free construction, √time annualization, and limitations under non-normal returns — G3 and G6.\n4. **Investopedia — Sortino Ratio & Downside Deviation:** downside-only risk measurement, minimum acceptable return (MAR), and instability with near-zero downside samples — G4.\n5. **Investopedia — Standard Deviation / Volatility / Variance:** dispersion measurement, symmetric treatment of upside and downside, and sensitivity to outliers — G1 and G7.\n6. **Investopedia — Maximum Drawdown / Drawdown:** peak-to-trough (high-water-mark) loss, path dependence, recovery asymmetry, and drawdown vs. volatility — G2 and G6. (Also \"Backtesting,\" \"Fat Tail,\" \"Value at Risk\" for the metric-limitation items in G7.)", "bookNotes": []}, {"part": "QF", "num": "03", "name": "Diversification & Correlation", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "cheatsheet": "- **Correlation (ρ)** measures the *strength and direction of linear co-movement*, standardized to **[-1, +1]**. It is unitless. It is a *tendency*, not a period-by-period rule: ρ = -0.60 means the assets have tended to move oppositely, NOT that one gains every time the other loses, and NOT that \"60% of losses were offset.\"\n  - **Correlation ≠ causation** — a statistical association alone never establishes that one series drives the other.\n  - Correlation is an **estimate** from a finite sample; it varies with the data window and market regime (estimation uncertainty).\n  - Near-zero linear ρ means little *linear* association, but does **not** prove independence — nonlinear dependence can remain.\n  - Strong positive ρ (e.g., 0.70) still allows individual periods where the two returns have opposite signs.\n- **Covariance vs. correlation**: `Cov(A,B) = ρ · σ_A · σ_B`, and equivalently `ρ = Cov(A,B) / (σ_A · σ_B)`. Covariance carries the product of the return units; correlation is the standardized (unitless) version.\n  - Doubling *both* volatilities (ρ fixed) multiplies covariance by **4** (2×2), not 2 — covariance scales with the *product* of the standard deviations.\n  - Individual variances are always **≥ 0**; a *negative* covariance describes *joint* movement, not a negative stand-alone variance.\n- **Two-asset portfolio variance**: `σ_p² = w_A²σ_A² + w_B²σ_B² + 2·w_A·w_B·Cov(A,B)`. Portfolio volatility = `√σ_p²` — NOT the weighted average of the component variances/volatilities. Omitting the cross-covariance term is wrong; it is exactly where diversification lives.\n  - With positive weights, a **larger covariance raises** portfolio variance; a **lower/negative covariance lowers** it. This is why two identically-volatile assets can form portfolios with very different risk.\n  - **Expected return** of the portfolio is just the weighted average of expected returns — it is unaffected by correlation. So risk can fall via imperfect co-movement *without* a proportional cut in expected return.\n- **Specific (idiosyncratic) vs. systematic risk**:\n  - **Specific/idiosyncratic**: tied to one issuer/event (a regulatory ruling, an accounting scandal). **Diversifiable** — spreading across many unrelated names dilutes it.\n  - **Systematic/market-wide**: recessions, rate shocks, economy-wide deleveraging. Affects most securities and **cannot be diversified away** within that asset class, no matter how many names you hold.\n  - Diversification reduces specific risk; it never immunizes against common market declines.\n- **Diversification benefits**: combining **imperfectly correlated** (ρ < 1) assets lowers combined risk. Negative correlation acts as a **hedge**; with ρ = -1 and correct weights, paired variability can theoretically be eliminated. A hedge/diversifier need NOT have the highest stand-alone return — value comes from covariance reduction, and giving up some upside in booms is the price of protection.\n- **Concentration & position sizing**: a large single weight amplifies both issuer-specific event risk and *estimation error* in the thesis (a 35% weight × 50% loss = 17.5% portfolio hit). Equal *single-name* weights do NOT remove **sector/factor** concentration (e.g., 8 of 10 names being regional banks). Inverse-volatility sizing that ignores correlations can hand a large weight to an asset that is heavily correlated with the rest — risk contribution depends on weight AND covariance, not stand-alone vol alone. Use multi-dimensional limits (single-name, sector, factor, scenario).\n- **Diminishing marginal diversification**: the first few independent additions remove the most idiosyncratic risk; later additions help less as the diversifiable component shrinks toward a **floor set by systematic risk**. Adding a **redundant** holding (high ρ, same signals) — regardless of a different manager name — provides little/no benefit and can even raise risk. Marginal benefit must be judged *relative to the existing portfolio*, not in isolation; a higher-vol but genuinely different return driver can beat a low-vol look-alike.\n- **Correlation instability / crisis behavior**: correlations are **regime-dependent** and tend to **rise toward 1 during stress** (common deleveraging, funding/liquidity shocks synchronize returns). Calm-period estimates understate crisis co-movement, so full-sample averages can hide tail dependence exactly when protection is needed. **Leverage** magnifies the damage when realized correlations exceed the forecast. Mitigate with stress/scenario analysis, shrinkage, and exposure limits — instability is a reason to *manage* estimates, not to abandon diversification.", "traps": "- **NO inverted-logic questions in this slice.** Every question uses a standard \"Answer Key:\" (True = accurate). There is no \"True = incorrect; False = accurate\" header anywhere in QF-D3. Do NOT assume polarity flips just because many choices sound negative — read each verdict straight.\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices are the real trap.** Many choices assert the *negation* of a true fact, so the correct verdict is **False**. The Answer Key flags these with the phrase **\"The statement reverses the correct conclusion.\"** Parse the inner claim, judge its truth, then apply the negation. These occur at: **G1Q1-E, G2Q1-A, G2Q2-D, G2Q2-E, G3Q1-A, G3Q1-B, G3Q3-B, G3Q3-D, G4Q1-C, G4Q2-E, G5Q1-D, G5Q2-C, G5Q3-C, G5Q3-E, G6Q2-D, G7Q1-A, G7Q1-D.**\n- **\"Cannot / could not / not\" framing.** Choices like \"diversification cannot reduce…\", \"correlation cannot rise…\", \"a limit cannot constrain…\" negate a true statement → **False**. When a choice claims something is *impossible* about a real diversification/correlation effect, suspect it is False.\n- **Correlation ≠ causation, offset %, or a period-by-period guarantee.** ρ = -0.60 does not mean 60% of losses are offset (G1Q1-B, False) and does not prove causation (G1Q1-D, False). Strong ρ still permits opposite-sign periods (G1Q2-D, False).\n- **Stand-alone stats never settle portfolio risk.** \"Unchanged individual volatilities ⇒ unchanged portfolio risk\" (G1Q3-D) and \"equal expected returns ⇒ covariance is irrelevant\" (G2Q3-E) are both **False** — covariance is the missing term.\n- **Counting names ≠ diversification.** Equal weights, dollar neutrality, or a large position count do not eliminate shared-driver/systematic risk (G3Q3-C, G5Q2-C, G6Q2-E). A different manager label does not make a return stream independent (G6Q2-C).", "references": "1. **Markowitz portfolio theory** (Markowitz, 1952, \"Portfolio Selection\"): mean-variance framework; portfolio variance `σ_p² = ΣΣ w_i w_j Cov(i,j)`; diversification benefit from imperfect (ρ < 1) and negative correlation; the ρ = -1 risk-elimination case; sensitivity of optimized weights to covariance inputs.\n2. **Bodie, Kane & Marcus — *Investments*:** two-asset and multi-asset portfolio math; diversification vs. portfolio size and the systematic-risk floor; firm-specific (diversifiable) vs. market (systematic) risk; the perfect-hedge case.\n3. **CFA Institute curriculum — Portfolio Management & Risk:** correlation as an estimated input, estimation uncertainty; factor/shared-driver and sector concentration beyond single-name limits; position sizing and risk controls; stress testing and tail/regime risk; estimation risk in portfolio construction.\n4. **Investopedia:** \"Covariance vs. Correlation,\" \"Correlation Coefficient,\" \"Systematic vs. Unsystematic (Idiosyncratic) Risk,\" \"Diversification\" (and its limits), \"Diworsification,\" \"Correlation Breakdown.\"\n5. **Ledoit & Wolf — covariance-matrix shrinkage:** improving robustness of estimated covariance/correlation inputs for portfolio optimization (relevant to G7Q3).", "bookNotes": []}, {"part": "QF", "num": "04", "name": "Portfolio Optimization", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "cheatsheet": "- **Portfolio weights & budget constraint.** Weights `w_i` are the fraction of capital in each asset. **Fully invested** ⇒ `Σ w_i = 1` (100%). Meeting the sum-of-weights (budget) constraint alone does NOT rule out negative or >100% weights — you need *separate* sign/bound constraints for that.\n  - **Long-only**: every `w_i ≥ 0` (no shorts). A negative weight is a **short position**; a weight > 1 is a **levered long**.\n  - **Net exposure** = `Σ w_i` (signed). **Gross exposure** = `Σ |w_i|`. Net can be 100% while gross far exceeds 100% (e.g., 120/30/−50 → net 100%, gross 200%) — offsetting longs/shorts create **leverage** even at 100% net.\n  - **Position bounds** (min/max per asset, e.g. 5%–35%) shrink the feasible set and can change the optimum *even when expected returns and the covariance matrix are unchanged*. Five 5% minimums pre-commit 25% of capital; three 35% maxes already sum to 105% (infeasible under a 100% budget).\n- **Minimum-variance & the Global Minimum-Variance (GMV) portfolio.**\n  - Portfolio variance: `σ_p² = Σ_i Σ_j w_i w_j σ_ij` = `wᵀΣw`. For two assets: `σ_p² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂·ρ₁₂σ₁σ₂`. Covariance `σ_ij = ρ_ij σ_i σ_j`.\n  - **GMV** = feasible portfolio with the *lowest* variance overall — a risk objective, not a return objective. It generally is NOT the single lowest-volatility asset: **diversification** (imperfect/negative correlation) can push a combination below the volatility of any component alone.\n  - Identifying GMV requires the **covariance matrix**, not merely a ranking of stand-alone volatilities. Moving off GMV trades higher risk for higher expected return.\n- **Maximum-Sharpe & the tangency portfolio.**\n  - **Sharpe ratio** = `(E[R_p] − R_f) / σ_p` = excess return per unit of volatility. Maximizing Sharpe considers *both* excess return and risk — the highest-return portfolio is NOT automatically max-Sharpe.\n  - The **tangency portfolio** is the risky-asset portfolio with the maximum Sharpe ratio; graphically the **Capital Allocation Line (CAL)** drawn from `R_f` is tangent to the efficient frontier there (steepest slope = highest Sharpe). With lending/borrowing at `R_f`, investors hold a mix of the tangency portfolio + risk-free asset — the mix (not a different risky portfolio) sets their risk level.\n  - Changing `R_f` changes the excess-return numerator by the same amount for all portfolios but divides by *different* `σ`, so the Sharpe *ranking* can flip when candidates have different volatilities. Raising `R_f` (risky returns fixed) *lowers* every excess return. Tangency ≠ GMV in general.\n- **Efficient frontier.** Set of portfolios offering the **minimum risk for each target return** (equivalently max return per risk). A portfolio is **dominated** if another offers ≥ return at ≤ risk (with one strict). The frontier is the *upper* branch above the GMV point; anything below the GMV return is dominated by GMV. Interior/dominated portfolios are **inefficient**. An **efficient** portfolio cannot raise return without raising risk, nor cut risk without cutting return.\n- **Estimation error / error-maximization.** MV optimization is highly sensitive to its inputs, *especially expected returns*. Small changes in expected-return estimates can produce large swings in weights (\"**error maximization**\" — the optimizer over-weights assets with over-estimated returns). Extreme/concentrated allocations often reflect **noise, not genuine edge**. Similar covariance matrices do NOT guarantee similar solutions if expected-return vectors differ. Numerical precision ≠ input accuracy. **Garbage in, garbage out**: exact math can't rescue bad inputs. Remedies: **shrinkage** (e.g., Ledoit-Wolf), longer/alternative samples, stress scenarios, position bounds, regularization.\n- **Constraints & practical design.** Long-only, leverage/gross-exposure caps, sector/asset-class limits, and **turnover** limits change the feasible set. A constraint is **binding** when the optimum sits on its boundary. Constraints can only *reduce* the in-sample (modeled) objective value (the unconstrained feasible set contains all constrained portfolios) — but they improve real-world robustness, implementability, and control of concentrated estimation error. **One-way turnover** here = `½ · Σ|Δw_i|`; a binding turnover cap spreads movement toward the target across several rebalances.\n- **In-sample vs. out-of-sample.** Using the same data to choose weights *and* to report performance is **in-sample** and flatters the strategy by fitting noise that won't repeat. Real **out-of-sample (OOS)** tests apply fixed weights to later, previously unused data. **Ex post** (\"after the fact\") optimal weights use information not available at decision time → **look-ahead bias**; correct math doesn't cure improper timing. Large weight swings across adjacent estimation windows signal instability; the *ex post* optimal portfolio need not stay optimal next period.", "traps": "- **No inverted-logic questions in this slice.** All 21 items use **Normal polarity** (`Answer Key:` with plain True/False; \"True\" = accurate). There is no \"True = incorrect; False = accurate\" block here — but confirm the polarity line on every question anyway, since sibling domains do invert.\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices are the primary trap.** Many *false* choices are phrased as a negation of a true fact, and the key stamps them **False** with \"*reverses the correct conclusion.*\" Parse the inner fact, judge its truth, then apply the negation. Offenders: **G1Q2D** (\"gross exposure is *not* 200%\" — it is), **G1Q3A** (\"would *not* violate\" — it does), **G2Q2C** (\"variance is *not* 0.0100\" — it is), **G2Q3C** (\"removing a constraint *cannot* enlarge the feasible set\" — it can), **G3Q1B** (\"Sharpe is *not* 0.50\" — it is), **G3Q2D** (\"*cannot* combine tangency with risk-free\" — you can), **G3Q3A** (\"Sharpe is *not* 0.80\" — it is), **G4Q1A/B** (\"*not* dominated\" — both are dominated), **G4Q3B/C**, **G5Q1D**, **G5Q2D**, **G5Q3E**, **G6Q1A/C**, **G6Q2B**, **G6Q3D/E**, **G7Q1A**, **G7Q2C**, **G7Q3B**.\n- **Worst offender — G7Q2A: a stacked double negative.** \"Ex post weights use information that *was not unavailable* at the start of that year.\" \"not unavailable\" = \"available\" → the statement claims the info was available; the key marks it **False** because full-year returns were in fact *not* knowable at year start. Read these letter-by-letter.\n- **Net vs. gross exposure.** A portfolio summing to 100% net (satisfying a budget/net-exposure rule) can still be heavily levered — gross = Σ of absolute weights. \"Weights sum to 100% ⇒ no leverage\" is always wrong when shorts exist.\n- **Objective confusion.** GMV minimizes *variance* (not return); max-Sharpe maximizes *excess-return-per-risk* (not raw return); efficient ≠ \"numerically in the middle.\" Watch choices that swap one objective's property onto another.\n- **Constraints can't raise the modeled optimum.** Any choice claiming a constraint *must improve* the modeled Sharpe/objective is false — restricting the feasible set can only hold or lower the in-sample optimum; its payoff is robustness/implementability, not a higher backtest number.", "references": "1. **Markowitz, H. (1952), \"Portfolio Selection,\" *Journal of Finance*; and *Portfolio Selection: Efficient Diversification of Investments* (1959):** mean-variance optimization, portfolio variance `wᵀΣw`, the efficient frontier, GMV portfolio, and diversification via covariance.\n2. **Grinold, R. & Kahn, R., *Active Portfolio Management*:** practical constraints (long-only, leverage/gross-exposure caps, sector limits, turnover), implementation shortfall/transaction costs, and the gap between mathematically optimal and implementable portfolios.\n3. **CFA Institute curriculum, \"Portfolio Risk and Return\" (Levels I–II):** Sharpe ratio, Capital Allocation Line, capital market line, tangency portfolio, two-fund separation, and inputs to optimization.\n4. **Investopedia:** \"Efficient Frontier,\" \"Tangency Portfolio,\" \"Capital Allocation Line,\" \"Minimum Variance Portfolio,\" \"Sharpe Ratio,\" \"Gross/Net Exposure,\" \"Portfolio Constraints,\" \"Portfolio Turnover,\" \"Estimation Error,\" \"Look-Ahead Bias,\" \"Backtesting.\"\n5. **Michaud, R. (1989), \"The Markowitz Optimization Enigma: Is 'Optimized' Optimal?\" *Financial Analysts Journal*; Ledoit & Wolf (2003/2004) covariance shrinkage:** estimation error, error maximization, and robustness remedies (shrinkage, resampling, constraints).", "bookNotes": []}, {"part": "QF", "num": "05", "name": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "cheatsheet": "- **Best bid / best ask**: the highest price a buyer will pay (bid) and the lowest a seller will accept (ask). A market **buy** lifts the **ask**; a market **sell** hits the **bid**.\n- **Quoted spread** = ask − bid (in dollars). **Midpoint** = (bid + ask) / 2. The spread is a cost paid for *immediacy* — crossing it is the price of demanding an instant fill.\n- **Relative (percentage) spread** = dollar spread / midpoint. A larger *dollar* spread does not by itself mean less liquid; compare relative spread, depth, volume, resilience, and trade size.\n- **Market order** = certainty of *execution*, uncertain *price* (can walk the book). **Limit order** = control of *price*, uncertain *execution* (fill risk; a resting limit may never trade even if the market touches its price, due to queue priority and available size).\n- **Buy limit** sets the *maximum* acceptable price; **sell limit** sets the *minimum*. A buy limit priced at/above the ask becomes marketable; below the ask, it rests as bid-side liquidity.\n- **Order book / market depth**: price levels and the quantity resting at each. Best price ≠ total available liquidity. A large order \"walks the book,\" consuming successively worse levels. Identical spreads can hide very different depth. Displayed depth is not guaranteed — it can be canceled or hidden.\n- **Order imbalance**: asymmetry in displayed depth. Can carry *probabilistic* short-horizon information, but never guarantees direction and can be spoofed or evaporate.\n- **Slippage** = realized execution price − quoted/decision price. **Market impact** = the order's own effect on price. Impact splits into **temporary** (transient pressure that reverts) and **permanent** (lasting information effect). Isolating the two needs a benchmark methodology and more data than a quote.\n- **Liquidity risk** scales with order size *relative to available liquidity/volume*, not dollars alone. A profitable paper strategy can be infeasible at scale (capacity constraint).\n- **Short selling**: borrow shares → **sell-to-open**; later **buy-to-close** and return them. Profit when price *falls*. Costs: borrow fee + owed dividends to the lender. **Loss is theoretically unlimited** (price has no upper bound); gain is capped (price floor = 0). **Short squeeze / recall risk** can force covering against the trader's view; collateral does not remove economic/operational risk.\n- **Implementation shortfall** = decision-price benchmark vs. actual outcome, including price move (delay + execution) and commissions. Decision value = shares × decision price. Later P&L does not erase implementation cost.\n- **Transaction costs** = commissions + spread + market impact + delay cost. Gross return overstates realized (net) performance. High turnover amplifies per-trade cost sensitivity; zero commission still leaves spread and impact.\n- **Execution strategy**: split large orders to lower instantaneous impact at the cost of *timing risk* (adverse moves, signal decay) and possible *information leakage* from predictable patterns. Optimal schedule balances expected alpha decay against transaction costs; no algorithm eliminates impact, timing, and non-completion risk simultaneously.", "traps": "- **No fully inverted-polarity questions in this slice.** None carry the \"Answer Key (True = incorrect; False = accurate)\" header, so every question below is scored with **Normal** polarity (True verdict = statement accurate). Still read each Answer Key line literally.\n- **Embedded-claim / double-negative choices** — the biggest trap here. Phrases like *\"A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that X\"* and *\"It would be wrong to reject X\"* flip the truth value: if X is actually correct, the choice is **False** (\"the underlying claim is correct and should be accepted\"). Affected: **[QF-D5G1Q3] A**, **[QF-D5G3Q1] C**, **[QF-D5G7Q3] C and E**.\n- **Negation traps (\"... is not ...\")** — a choice that asserts a value is *not* the correct number is **False** when that number is in fact correct. Affected: **[QF-D5G1Q1] B**, **[QF-D5G1Q2] D**, **[QF-D5G3Q1] D**, **[QF-D5G5Q2] C**, **[QF-D5G6Q1] A**, **[QF-D5G6Q2] B**, **[QF-D5G6Q3] D**. The Answer Key tags these \"The statement reverses the correct conclusion.\"\n- **Round-trip / direction sign errors**: buying at the ask and selling at the bid *loses* the spread, not gains it ([QF-D5G1Q1] D). For a *seller*, a lower execution price is *worse* ([QF-D5G6Q3] E); a buyer prefers lower ([QF-D5G4Q1] C).\n- **\"Guarantee\" and \"eliminate\" absolutes** are almost always False: limit orders don't guarantee fills ([QF-D5G2Q2] C), market orders don't eliminate price risk ([QF-D5G2Q3] E), splitting doesn't guarantee lower cost ([QF-D5G7Q1] D), displayed depth isn't guaranteed to persist ([QF-D5G3Q2] E), collateral doesn't eliminate short risk ([QF-D5G5Q3] E).\n- **\"Proves\" overreach**: an observed price level or unchanged midpoint never *proves* unchanged liquidity or an exact permanent-value change ([QF-D5G1Q3] D, [QF-D5G4Q3] C).", "references": "- Larry Harris, *Trading and Exchanges: Market Microstructure for Practitioners* — bid/ask quotes, limit order book, depth, market impact (temporary vs. permanent), execution algorithms, and information leakage.\n- CFA Program curriculum — Trading, Execution, and Costs: order types, implementation shortfall (decision-price benchmark and cost decomposition), transaction-cost analysis.\n- Investopedia — *Bid-Ask Spread* (cost of immediacy, relative spread).\n- Investopedia — *Market Order vs. Limit Order* (execution vs. price certainty, fill risk).\n- Investopedia — *Slippage* and *Market Impact* / *Order Book & Market Depth* (walking the book, liquidity).\n- Investopedia — *Short Selling* (sell-to-open/buy-to-close, borrow fees, dividend obligation, unlimited loss, short squeeze/recall risk).", "bookNotes": []}, {"part": "QF", "num": "06", "name": "Valuation & Mispricing", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "cheatsheet": "- **Market price vs. intrinsic value**: market price is an *observed* transaction/quotation value; intrinsic value is an *estimate* derived from a model and its inputs (expected cash flows, growth, discount rate). The two need not agree, and a gap between them is the *basis* for a trade — not proof of one.\n  - Reasonable analysts can defensibly disagree on intrinsic value (different growth forecasts, risk assessments, discount rates, methods). The same market price can look overvalued under one model and undervalued under another.\n  - A price move (e.g., on a merger rumor) can reflect the market's **probability-weighted** assessment of multiple outcomes, not a single certain value. Expected value depends on both scenario payoffs *and* their probabilities.\n  - Market price does **not** uniquely reveal investors' intrinsic-value estimates or scenario probabilities.\n- **Book value / asset-based valuation**: shareholder equity = assets − liabilities; book value per share = equity ÷ shares; price-to-book (P/B) = price ÷ book value per share.\n  - Market value can *rationally* exceed book value because of earning power, growth, or unrecorded intangibles (human capital, internally developed brands/client networks). A high P/B is **not** conclusive evidence of overvaluation.\n  - Book value is an accounting measure of *recorded* assets less liabilities — not the present value of future profits. P/B comparisons are most informative among firms with similar business models and accounting practices.\n  - In **distress/liquidation**, reported book value can *overstate* recovery: assets may realize far below carrying amounts, liquidation creates costs, and creditors are senior — common equity is a residual and may recover nothing even when book equity is positive.\n- **Time value of money (TVM)**: a dollar today is worth more than a dollar later. PV = FV / (1+r)^n; FV = PV × (1+r)^n. The discount rate reflects opportunity cost and the risk of the cash flow.\n  - A larger nominal amount later is **not** automatically preferable — compare on a PV basis at the appropriate rate. Equal nominal payments at different times have *unequal* PV whenever r ≠ 0; only at r = 0 are they equal.\n  - **Compounding** earns returns on prior returns, so growth exceeds simple interest. Longer-dated cash flows are more sensitive to the discount rate; a higher r lowers PV.\n- **DCF & dividend valuation**: value = PV of future cash flows. Gordon constant-growth DDM: V = D₁ / (r − g), valid only when g < r for a finite positive value.\n  - Higher r → lower value; higher g → higher value (narrows the denominator). As g → r the denominator shrinks and valuation becomes explosive/unstable and hypersensitive.\n  - **Terminal value** often dominates a DCF's present value, so long-run assumptions deserve careful sensitivity analysis. A precise-looking output is still only as good as its uncertain inputs.\n- **Growth-based valuation**: compound projections build growth on prior-year growth, so multi-year cumulative growth ≠ (annual rate × years). Value is highly sensitive to small changes in the growth assumption (nonlinear). A biased starting cash-flow scales the entire projected path.\n  - \"Growth\" vs. \"value\" are *descriptive* labels, not performance guarantees. A high multiple doesn't preclude undervaluation; a low multiple can reflect genuine impairment (a value trap). Long-run growth should be economically sustainable, not extrapolated from a single strong year.\n- **Margin of safety & scenario analysis**: use bear/base/bull cases and probability-weighting to build a valuation *range* and avoid false precision. A margin of safety (buy sufficiently below conservative estimate) cushions estimation error but **cannot** guarantee against loss. Scenarios illuminate model risk but cannot eliminate it.\n- **Long/short from mispricing**: go long undervalued, short overvalued. But correct valuation ≠ successful trade — timing/catalyst risk, financing/borrow costs, risk limits, and adverse price paths can force liquidation before convergence. A short has *theoretically unlimited* loss (price can rise without bound); position sizing must reflect downside scenarios, not just central-case upside.", "traps": "- **No inverted-logic questions in this domain.** All 21 items (G1Q1–G7Q3) use plain \"Answer Key:\" → **Normal polarity (True = accurate).** There is no \"(True = incorrect; False = accurate)\" header anywhere in QF-D06, so do not invert. (Flag retained because inverted items *do* appear in sibling domains — always re-check the polarity line per question.)\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices** — phrased \"*A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that* [an accurate statement].\" The embedded claim is true and *should* be accepted, so the choice is **False**. Parse the inner claim, confirm it's accurate, then the \"would be wrong to accept\" wrapper makes the verdict False. Appears in: **G3Q2E, G4Q1C, G6Q1C, G6Q3E, G7Q2B, G7Q2C.**\n- **Negation / \"reversal\" choices** — phrased \"is **not** $X\" or \"would **not**…\" where the arithmetic actually *is* X. The Answer Key marks these **False** with \"The statement reverses the correct conclusion.\" Do the math, then negate the negation. Appears in: G2Q1A, G2Q1B, G2Q2B, G2Q3A, G2Q3C, G3Q1D, G3Q2D, G4Q3A, G4Q3B, G5Q1E, G5Q2D, G6Q1A, G6Q3D, G7Q1A.\n- **\"Guarantee/proof\" absolutes are almost always False.** A price-value gap does not *guarantee* a profit (G1Q1D); market price does not *prove* estimates wrong (G1Q2E) or reveal the \"true\" value (G1Q3E); trading above book does not *prove* overvaluation (G2Q1D); a high P/B is not *conclusive* mispricing (G2Q2C); probability-weighted value does not *guarantee* a positive return (G6Q1D); a margin of safety cannot *guarantee* against loss; a correct thesis is not *sufficient* for a winning trade (G7Q3D).\n- **Gordon-model denominator sensitivity**: watch g approaching r — value explodes and becomes *more* (not less) sensitive to error (G5Q2E). Requires g < r for a finite positive value (G4Q2D).\n- **Simple vs. compound growth**: cumulative compound growth (e.g., 1.08³ ≈ 25.97%) is greater than rate × years (24%) (G5Q1C); annual compounding beats simple interest (G3Q3C).\n- **Short-selling payoff mechanics**: gross gain/loss = sell price − cover price; max loss is unbounded (not capped at the initial share price) (G7Q2D). Exiting early (forced cover) forfeits any later favorable move (G7Q3B).", "references": "1. **Damodaran — *Investment Valuation* / *The Dark Side of Valuation*:** intrinsic value vs. price, DCF and terminal-value mechanics, relative valuation/multiples, and the role of uncertainty and assumptions in valuation.\n2. **CFA Program Curriculum — Equity Valuation & Quantitative Methods:** discounted cash flow (FCFF/FCFE), dividend discount models (constant-growth/Gordon), time value of money, scenario/sensitivity analysis, and asset-based/liquidation valuation.\n3. **Investopedia:** \"Intrinsic Value,\" \"Discounted Cash Flow,\" \"Dividend Discount Model,\" \"Gordon Growth Model,\" \"Book Value Per Share,\" \"Price-to-Book (P/B) Ratio,\" \"Time Value of Money,\" \"Present/Future Value,\" \"Compound Interest,\" \"Terminal Value,\" \"Growth/Value Stock,\" \"Margin of Safety,\" \"Short Selling.\"\n4. **Benjamin Graham — *The Intelligent Investor*:** the margin-of-safety principle and buying below conservative intrinsic-value estimates.\n5. **General finance principles:** mean reversion / convergence of price to fair value, expected-value (probability-weighted) valuation, and the distinction between a correct valuation thesis and a successful trade (timing, catalyst, and financing risk).", "bookNotes": []}, {"part": "QF", "num": "07", "name": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "cheatsheet": "- **Market model / return decomposition.** A single-factor model writes an asset's return as\n  `r = α + β·r_market + ε`:\n  - **Market-related (systematic) component** = `β·r_market` — the part explained by the modeled benchmark exposure.\n  - **Alpha (α, intercept)** = predicted return when the market return *and* residual are both zero; the benchmark-unexplained expected return.\n  - **Residual (ε, idiosyncratic/asset-specific) component** = `actual − predicted` = the part left unexplained after applying the fitted model. A negative residual can coexist with a positive total return (and vice versa) when the other components dominate.\n  - Total = intercept + market-related + residual. These simply **sum**.\n- **Beta = slope = sensitivity.** `β = Cov(asset, market) / Var(market) = ρ · (σ_asset / σ_market)`.\n  - β > 1 → amplifies market moves; β < 1 → dampens; β = 1 → moves with market; β < 0 → moves inverse (negative covariance). Zero covariance → **β = 0** (zero numerator).\n  - Beta is an **estimate** of an *average* linear relationship — realized returns still contain residual variation, so beta never \"guarantees\" an exact move on any given day.\n  - Beta measures **exposure, not skill**; a high beta amplifies bull-market returns without producing alpha.\n  - Beta is empirical: it can change with return horizon, sample period, frequency, and market regime.\n- **Alpha and active performance.** Realized alpha (Jensen's alpha) = `actual return − CAPM-implied return`, where CAPM-implied = `r_f + β·(r_market − r_f)`.\n  - Raw return vs. market is **not** risk-adjusted: a high-beta fund \"should\" beat the market in an up year purely from exposure. Alpha isolates skill from that exposure.\n  - One positive observation ≠ persistent skill (luck, residual risk, estimation error). And a positive *point estimate* is not automatically statistically significant — check estimate/standard-error ratio.\n  - **Statistical vs. economic significance are distinct.** An effect can be statistically detectable yet vanish after costs (net alpha = gross alpha − implementation cost), or be economically large yet imprecisely estimated.\n- **Correlation vs. beta.** Correlation is *standardized directional co-movement* (unitless, in [−1, 1]); beta scales correlation by the *volatility ratio* `σ_asset/σ_market`. They are **not** interchangeable.\n  - High correlation can pair with a **low** beta (asset less volatile than the market).\n  - High total volatility can pair with a **low** beta (low correlation → most of the volatility is idiosyncratic, unrelated to the market).\n- **Portfolio beta = weighted average of component betas** (weights relative to equity). Long positions add, shorts subtract, cash contributes zero.\n  - A positive-weighted average lies within the range of the component betas.\n  - **Beta-weighted (dollar) exposure** = `Σ (position$ × β)`; portfolio beta relative to equity = net beta-dollars / equity.\n  - **Leverage** shows up in **gross** exposure (sum of absolute risky weights), not in signed weights — signed weights can sum to 100% while gross exposure exceeds it.\n- **Dollar neutrality ≠ beta (market) neutrality.**\n  - **Dollar neutral** = equal long and short *dollar* amounts. **Beta neutral** = beta-weighted exposures cancel.\n  - Equal dollars do **not** imply equal market risk when the two legs have different betas; to neutralize beta you generally need **unequal** dollar amounts (short more of a lower-beta leg, less of a higher-beta leg). Hedge ratio = `β_long/β_hedge`.\n- **Hedging and residual risk.** A market/index hedge is designed to remove **systematic** return while **retaining** asset-specific (alpha) return before costs.\n  - It does **not** eliminate all loss: idiosyncratic / company-specific news, basis risk, liquidity, and model risk remain.\n  - Hedges are **imperfect**: beta estimation error and time-varying beta leave unintended exposure; pair trades carry relative-value / relationship-breakdown risk; and financing, trading costs, and imperfect index matching create return drag.", "traps": "- **No inverted-logic questions in this domain.** Every question here is **Normal polarity (True = accurate)**. There is no \"True = incorrect; False = accurate\" block in QF Domain 7 — but always re-read each Answer Key line, because the trap below mimics inversion at the *choice* level.\n- **Negation-framed \"…is not X\" choices (the dominant trap).** Many choices assert a *correct* number/relationship in negated form (\"The market excess return is **not** 8%\", \"Portfolio beta is **not** 1.04\"). Because the true value *is* that number, the negated claim is **False**, and the Answer Key repeatedly says *\"The statement reverses the correct conclusion.\"* Compute the quantity first, then check whether the \"not\" makes the sentence lie. Negation-framed False choices: **G1Q1 A, G1Q1 B, G1Q3 B, G2Q3 C, G3Q1 A, G3Q3 B, G3Q3 C, G4Q3 A, G5Q1 C, G5Q1 D, G5Q2 D, G5Q3 C, G5Q3 D, G6Q2 C, G6Q3 A, G7Q2 B, G7Q3 A, G7Q3 B.** (When the \"not\" statement instead negates a *false* claim, it flips True — e.g., G7Q1 A-style; read carefully.)\n- **\"Guarantees / must / exactly / every\" absolutes are almost always False.** Beta, alpha, correlation, and hedge ratios are *estimates of averages*. Choices claiming a beta \"guarantees\" an exact move (G2Q1 C), a negative beta \"guarantees\" a positive return in a decline (G2Q2 C), an in-sample intercept guarantees future alpha (G1Q2 C), one year proves persistent skill (G3Q1 E), or a hedge keeps realized beta \"exactly zero in every future period\" (G7Q3 D) are wrong.\n- **Exposure ≠ skill; raw outperformance ≠ alpha.** A high-beta fund beating the market (G3Q3 D, G3Q3 C) or high raw returns in a bull market (G3Q3 E) reflect systematic exposure, not manager alpha. Comparing fund return to market return without adjusting for beta (G3Q1 D) does **not** risk-adjust.\n- **Dollar neutral is not market neutral.** Watch every choice that calls a dollar-balanced book \"market neutral\" (G6Q1) — beta-weighted exposures must cancel, which usually requires unequal dollars (G6Q2, G7Q2). Conversely a beta-neutral book is typically **not** dollar neutral (G6Q2 D).\n- **Hedges retain idiosyncratic risk and alpha.** Choices saying a hedge \"eliminates all possible losses\" (G7Q1 B) or \"cannot retain\" a positive asset-specific return (G7Q1 D) invert the purpose of a market hedge: it removes systematic risk and *keeps* residual/alpha, but leaves company-specific, basis, model, and cost risk.", "references": "1. **Sharpe — Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM):** the Security Market Line, `E(r) = r_f + β·(r_market − r_f)`, and `β = Cov(i,m)/Var(m)` — foundation for expected return, alpha as deviation from the SML, and beta as systematic-risk sensitivity.\n2. **Bodie, Kane & Marcus — *Investments*:** the index/market model and Security Characteristic Line, return decomposition into systematic + firm-specific components, portfolio beta, and hedging market risk with index instruments (beta hedge ratio, basis risk).\n3. **Grinold & Kahn — *Active Portfolio Management*:** residual (idiosyncratic) return and risk, alpha vs. beta, the information ratio / t-statistic of alpha, and beta-neutral / dollar-neutral portfolio construction and exposures.\n4. **CFA Institute curriculum (Portfolio Management / Quantitative Methods):** CAPM and the SML, correlation vs. beta, statistical vs. economic significance and hypothesis testing of alpha, and performance evaluation.\n5. **Investopedia:** \"Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)\" and \"Security Market Line,\" \"Beta\" / \"Negative Beta,\" \"Jensen's Measure (Alpha),\" \"Market-Neutral Strategy,\" \"Pairs Trade,\" \"Beta Hedging,\" and \"Basis Risk.\"", "bookNotes": []}, {"part": "QF", "num": "08", "name": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "cheatsheet": "- **Efficient Market Hypothesis (EMH) — three nested forms** defined by *which information set* is already impounded in price:\n  - **Weak form**: prices reflect all *past price and volume* data. Implication: purely technical/past-return rules cannot earn *consistent, exploitable* abnormal returns. Motivated by the **random-walk** intuition (price changes are unpredictable from history).\n  - **Semi-strong form**: prices reflect all *publicly available* information (financial statements, announcements, filings). Implication: **fundamental analysis on already-public data** should not consistently earn abnormal returns; public news is incorporated *rapidly*.\n  - **Strong form**: prices reflect *all* information, **public and private/insider**. Implication: even insiders cannot systematically earn abnormal returns. Rarely holds in practice; insider-trading regulation restricts behavior but does **not** prove the strong form.\n  - Each higher form **contains** the lower one (strong ⊃ semi-strong ⊃ weak).\n- **What efficiency does NOT claim**: it does not require prices to be *perfectly correct* in an absolute sense, nor instantaneous perfection with no price discovery, nor that prices \"can never rise two days in a row.\" Runs of gains/losses, brief price-discovery windows, and partial reversals are all compatible with efficiency.\n- **Testing an inefficiency = \"does a reliable, tradeable edge survive beyond chance and costs?\"** A profitable *in-sample* backtest can arise from **data snooping / overfitting**; a gross edge that is negative after **transaction costs** is not economically exploitable. Predictability (academically interesting) ≠ profitability (net-of-cost tradeability).\n- **Arbitrage & the law of one price (LOOP)**: identical/equivalent state-contingent cash flows must command the *same* price in a frictionless market. A price gap between equivalents is a *theoretical* arbitrage — buy cheap, sell dear, pocket the difference; profit comes from the **contemporaneous discrepancy**, not a directional forecast. Simultaneous execution of both legs limits execution price risk. Repeated arbitrage pushes prices to **convergence**.\n  - **Riskless vs. risky arbitrage**: truly identical payoffs ⇒ riskless; only *highly correlated* (not identical) payoffs ⇒ residual (basis) risk remains.\n  - **ETF mechanism**: authorized creation/redemption keeps market price near NAV/basket value; but timing, execution, financing, and tracking risks can erase a small apparent spread.\n- **Statistical arbitrage & mean reversion**: exploit *temporary* deviations from a historical relationship (pair trading). **z-score = (spread − mean) / std**; a large |z| flags historical unusualness, **not** guaranteed convergence. Short the relatively expensive leg, buy the cheap leg; the spread can narrow with either/both legs moving. Key risk: the **historical relationship breaks** (structural change). Not riskless — carries model, convergence, market, and execution risk.\n- **Dollar-neutral ≠ risk-free**: equal long/short notionals limit *net directional dollar* exposure but do **not** eliminate *relative-price* risk; the short can rise more than the long and lose money even when both rise.\n- **Event-driven & relative-value strategies**:\n  - **Merger arbitrage**: post-announcement target trades below the offer (a *deal spread*) reflecting deal-failure/timing risk — the spread is **not** a guaranteed profit. Annualized return depends on time-to-close.\n  - **Relative-value / pairs**: long one asset, short a related one — expresses a *relative* (not absolute/directional) view; equal notionals do **not** equalize differing sensitivities (betas, hedges, leverage); company-specific news and borrow costs still matter.\n  - **Earnings events**: returns depend on the surprise *relative to what price already implied*, which can differ from published analyst consensus — beating consensus can still trigger a decline.\n- **Limits to arbitrage (why mispricing persists despite informed investors)** — Shleifer/Vishny:\n  - **Timing/horizon uncertainty**: a correct long-run valuation can still lose if the position can't be held to convergence.\n  - **Funding/margin constraints & leverage**: adverse interim moves trigger margin calls and **forced liquidation** (\"the market can stay irrational longer than you can stay solvent\").\n  - **Noise-trader risk**: other traders push price *further* from value before it corrects.\n  - **Short-sale constraints**: high borrow fees, limited availability, and **recall** risk cap how fully negative views get expressed and let overpricing persist.\n  - **Crowded trades**: common positioning creates endogenous liquidity/feedback loops — one fund's redemption-driven unwind moves prices against others and cascades.", "traps": "- **No inverted-logic questions in this domain.** *Every* [QF-D8...] item uses a plain \"Answer Key:\" (True = accurate, False = inaccurate). There is no \"True = incorrect; False = accurate\" header here — do not invent one. (Flagged explicitly because sibling domains *do* contain inverted items; this one does not.)\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" / \"reverses the correct conclusion\" choices are the primary trap.** Many choices are phrased in the negative (\"The net profit is *not* $0.30\", \"the two positions should *not* have the same price\", \"leverage *cannot* convert…\") and are marked **False** because the embedded factual claim is true. Rule: strip the negation, judge the inner statement, then flip. These appear in **G2Q3E, G3Q2B, G4Q1B, G4Q1D, G4Q2A, G4Q3A, G4Q3B, G5Q2E, G6Q1A, G6Q1B, G6Q3C, G6Q3E, G7Q1E, G7Q2A, G7Q2C, G7Q3C.**\n- **\"Guaranteed / cannot lose / riskless\" absolutes are almost always False** in stat-arb, merger-arb, and dollar-neutral contexts (G5Q1D, G5Q2D/E, G6Q1C, G4Q3C). A large z-score, an announced deal, or balanced notionals never *guarantee* convergence or eliminate risk.\n- **In-sample profit ≠ inefficiency; net-of-cost loss ≠ exploitable edge.** Data snooping (G1Q1C, G5Q3A/B) can manufacture a winning historical rule; a 6% gross edge behind 7% costs is −1% net and not exploitable (G1Q3B/C).\n- **Efficiency ≠ perfection.** Choices demanding instantaneous, single-transaction final prices, multi-day mandated drift, or \"every investor must read/own the data\" are False (G2Q1B, G2Q2B, G2Q3B, G3Q3B). Price discovery, disagreement, and informed-subset trading are all compatible with efficiency.\n- **Form confusion**: semi-strong concerns *public* info only and does **not** require *private* (undisclosed) results to be in the price; strong form does (G3Q1C, G3Q2B, G3Q3E). Regulation existing ≠ strong form holding (G3Q1E).", "references": "1. **Fama, E. (1970) \"Efficient Capital Markets: A Review of Theory and Empirical Work\"** — the canonical weak / semi-strong / strong-form taxonomy and the information-set definitions; also Fama (1965) on the random-walk model of prices.\n2. **Shleifer, A. — *Inefficient Markets: An Introduction to Behavioral Finance*** and **Shleifer & Vishny (1997) \"The Limits of Arbitrage\"** — noise-trader risk, funding/horizon constraints, short-sale constraints, and why mispricing persists despite informed investors.\n3. **CFA Institute curriculum** — Market Efficiency (forms of EMH, implications for technical/fundamental analysis), no-arbitrage pricing and the law of one price, and event-driven / relative-value (merger arbitrage, pairs) strategies.\n4. **Investopedia** — \"Efficient Market Hypothesis,\" \"Arbitrage,\" \"Statistical Arbitrage,\" \"Merger Arbitrage,\" \"ETF Arbitrage,\" and \"No Free Lunch\" — accessible definitions matching the question phrasing.\n5. **Grossman & Stiglitz (1980) \"On the Impossibility of Informationally Efficient Markets\"** — costly information acquisition as a structural limit to full incorporation (alternative-data / acquisition-cost questions).\n6. **James, Witten, Hastie & Tibshirani — *An Introduction to Statistical Learning*, ch.5 (cross-validation / out-of-sample testing)** — data-snooping, selection bias, and validation for the backtest-overfitting questions.", "bookNotes": []}, {"part": "QF", "num": "09", "name": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "cheatsheet": "- **Time-series ordering / look-ahead bias**: a backtest may only use information that was *actually available* at the simulated decision time. Any feature, label, or preprocessing step that pulls in future data leaks look-ahead bias and inflates results.\n  - **Random train/test splits break chronology** — they let 2025 rows sit in \"training\" while 2012 rows sit in \"testing,\" which does NOT simulate deploying in 2012. Autocorrelation between nearby observations also makes randomly split samples unusually similar → over-optimistic scores.\n  - **Chronological (train-early / test-late) splits** preserve the direction of time and better represent live deployment.\n  - **Centered windows leak the future** (a centered 21-day moving average uses 10 future closes); a **trailing window** uses only past data. Even a trailing feature must be **shifted/lagged** if the current bar's close was not yet known at the decision moment. Feature timing must match the exact time the simulated order could have been placed.\n  - **Overlapping labels near a train/test boundary leak** — a 5-day-forward label dated June 30 contains July returns. **Purging** (dropping boundary observations whose label windows cross into the test period) reduces this; longer horizons need *wider* separation. Shuffling rows does not fix overlap (shared future information remains).\n  - Using only lagged features does NOT by itself guarantee a leak-free design — leakage can enter via splitting, preprocessing, labels, or universe construction.\n- **Survivorship bias**: testing only securities/funds that survived to today (current index members, live hedge funds) systematically omits failures, **overstating returns and understating losses/volatility**. Closure/delisting is correlated with poor performance, so the missingness is *systematic*, not random. Fix with **point-in-time constituent** and **dead-fund** databases; adding more history for the same survivors does NOT restore the missing names.\n- **Selection bias**: choosing the market/period/subsample *after* observing results (reporting the 3 best of 20 countries, best decade) overstates how a pre-specified rule would perform. A strong selected subsample does not prove universality. Disclosing the full search and testing on **untouched** data/markets provides genuine validation.\n- **Data snooping / multiple testing**: testing many strategies and keeping the \"significant\" ones manufactures false discoveries. At a 5% threshold, ~50 of 1,000 truly-null signals look significant *by chance*. Selecting the best of many trials is NOT the same as testing one pre-specified strategy. **Multiple-testing corrections** account for the number/structure of hypotheses; a p<0.05 on a mined signal does not prove durable economic value. Retained signals must be validated on data that did not participate in their selection.\n- **Reusing the holdout (\"p-hacking\")**: repeatedly tweaking parameters against the same \"test\" set until Sharpe looks good makes the holdout part of model development — it is no longer independent. Calling data a \"test set\" doesn't preserve independence; *functional use* does. Keep a log of all variants; final evaluation must be on a truly untouched dataset. **Winner's curse**: the max of many noisy estimates overstates true performance; freezing the specification before further testing prevents new tuning on validation data. Replication across later periods/markets is stronger evidence than the in-sample result; broad failure suggests the effect did not generalize.\n- **Point-in-time (PIT) data and revisions**: financial statements, EPS, and macro series (GDP) get **restated/revised** years later. Using the latest-vintage series as if it were known on the original date embeds hindsight. **Economic reference period ≠ public availability date**: a fiscal year ending Dec 31 with a March 15 release cannot be traded on Jan 2. A PIT / real-time vintage database preserves original releases, revisions, and availability dates. \"Cleanest current series\" ≠ most realistic historical simulation. Add a conservative processing/execution delay after release.\n- **Corporate actions and price adjustments**:\n  - **Splits**: a 2-for-1 split halves per-share price and doubles share count → position value ~unchanged; unadjusted prices record a spurious −50% \"return.\" Split-adjusted series place pre/post prices on a comparable basis; total market cap is ~unchanged (not doubled).\n  - **Dividends**: a $2 dividend with a $2 ex-date price drop is ~0% total return, but a price-only series shows −4%. Dividend-adjusted **total-return** series capture distributions. Dividends (cash distribution) and splits (unit change) are *different* actions.\n  - **Delistings**: ending a series at the last exchange quote ($4) when the investor recovered only $0.50 omits a real −87.5% loss → survivorship-style upward bias. Investigate delisting codes and terminal recovery values rather than treating the last quote as full value.\n- **Missing, stale, and irregular data**:\n  - **Forward-filling** a non-trading price repeats the last value → artificial zero returns, then a jump when a real trade prints; this understates measured volatility/autocorrelation and biases correlations. A stale quote ≠ an executable price (liquidity, spreads, depth matter).\n  - **Non-synchronous calendars**: filling a U.S. holiday with the prior close and aligning it to a foreign market that traded distorts the \"same-day\" relationship (returns cover different information intervals). Align common trading intervals or model lead-lag.\n  - **Informative missingness**: if firms omit a disclosure when results are poor, missingness carries information. **Mean imputation** attenuates the true signal and falsely assumes missing-at-random; imputed values are modeling choices, not observations. Add a **missingness indicator** and run sensitivity analyses.\n- **Nonstationarity and regime change**: return distributions and relationships shift over time (structural breaks, strategy decay). A historical beta need not hold after a firm doubles leverage / changes industry; more data from the *old* regime yields a precise-but-obsolete estimate. Rolling estimates, stability/break tests, and per-regime (stress) reporting reveal instability. A positive full-sample average can mask severe regime-specific tail losses (e.g., a short-vol strategy's 2020 loss); dropping an adverse-but-relevant regime is itself selection bias. Widespread adoption can shrink/accelerate an edge below net-profitability after costs; retraining does not *guarantee* useful structure exists in the new regime.", "traps": "- **No inverted-logic questions in this domain.** Every question in this slice uses the standard Answer Key form (\"A. True – …\" / \"B. False – …\", True = accurate). There is no \"True = incorrect; False = accurate\" polarity flip here — but always re-read each Answer Key line before trusting that.\n- **Negation-flip distractors are the dominant trap.** Most *False* choices are phrased as \"cannot,\" \"does not,\" \"guarantees,\" \"necessarily,\" \"is irrelevant,\" or \"reverses the correct conclusion.\" The Answer Key repeatedly tags these False with the note \"The statement reverses the correct conclusion.\" Read each choice for a hidden negation before answering. Examples: G1Q2C, G1Q3D, G2Q1E, G2Q2B/E, G3Q1C, G4Q3B, G5Q1C/D, G6Q1C(no)/G6Q3C, G7Q1D/E, G7Q3A.\n- **Embedded double-negative \"claim\" choice — [QF-D9G4Q2] C.** \"A reviewer would be *wrong to accept the claim that* delaying first use until public availability would improve temporal validity.\" The inner claim is *accurate*, so the choice (which calls it wrong) is **False**. Parse the inner claim, judge its truth, then negate. Same pattern in **[QF-D9G5Q3] E** (\"Researchers should *not* investigate delisting codes … rather than automatically treating the last quote as full value\" → False, because they *should* investigate).\n- **\"It became historical / it's public, therefore it was known\" fallacy.** Later availability (a restatement, a delayed 10-K, revised GDP, public data) does NOT make information available at the historical decision time. See G1Q2D, G4Q1C/E, G4Q2D, G4Q3D.\n- **Significance / in-sample strength ≠ durable value.** A single low p-value, a max Sharpe among many trials, or a favorable selected subsample does not prove persistence, universality, or net-of-cost profitability (G2Q3C, G3Q1E, G3Q3A/C).\n- **\"More data\" red herrings.** Adding years for the same survivors doesn't remove survivorship bias (G2Q2D); more observations from an obsolete regime just make a stale estimate precise (G7Q1C).", "references": "1. **Lopez de Prado — *Advances in Financial Machine Learning*:** ch. 7 (purged/embargoed cross-validation, why random splits leak in time series), ch. 3 (labeling, meta-labeling), ch. 11–12 (backtesting pitfalls, backtest overfitting, strategy decay) — core for look-ahead bias, overlapping labels, and multiple-testing on backtests.\n2. **Bailey & Lopez de Prado — backtest-overfitting work** (\"The Probability of Backtest Overfitting\"; deflated Sharpe ratio; \"Pseudo-Mathematics and Financial Charlatanism\"): multiple testing, selection bias, winner's curse, and why the max Sharpe of many trials overstates true performance.\n3. **CFA Institute curriculum (Quantitative Methods / Backtesting & Simulation, Big Data / Data Science):** survivorship and look-ahead bias, point-in-time data, data snooping, out-of-sample and stress testing, nonstationarity and structural breaks.\n4. **Investopedia — bias & data reference articles:** \"Survivorship Bias,\" \"Look-Ahead Bias,\" \"Data Snooping,\" \"Point-in-Time Data,\" \"Stale Price,\" \"Adjusted Closing Price\"/stock splits, and \"Total Return\" — plain-language definitions for each trap in this domain.", "bookNotes": [{"book": "Handbook_AI_BigData", "title": "Ch. 1 — On Machine Learning Applications in Investments", "file": "references/Handbook_AI_BigData/Ch01_ML_Applications_in_Investments.md", "md": "# Ch. 1 — On Machine Learning Applications in Investments\n\n**Source:** Chen, M. (Robeco) & Zhou, W. — *Handbook of AI and Big Data Applications in Investments*, CFA Institute Research Foundation (2023).\n**Book pages:** 2–19 (PDF pages 23–40).\n**ML4T exam relevance:** ML Domain 1 (supervised learning, regression/classification, ensembles, decision trees, overfitting), ML Domain 2 (evaluation, bias/variance, data splitting), QF Domain 9–10 (return prediction, Sharpe ratio, factor models, backtesting). Faithful to source; nothing invented.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why apply ML to investing (motivations)\n\n- **Primary attraction:** promise of higher **risk-adjusted return**. ML is built for **prediction on high-dimensional data with no functional-form specification**, so it should be good at predicting future equity returns.\n- Practitioners have used ML to predict: **cross-sectional equity returns**, **corporate earnings** (more accurate than traditional models), and **crash / severe price-drop probability** (superior to linear models using leverage ratio or distance-to-default).\n- **Key driver of outperformance vs. linear models:** ML does **not prespecify the functional relationship** between predictors (financial-statement metrics, past returns) and target (return, earnings). It can capture **interaction effects** and **nonlinear effects**.\n  - **Interaction effect:** output is not a linear combination of inputs because one input's effect depends on another's value (e.g., reported earnings matter only when an accounting \"red flag\" = 0; if red flag = 1, price drops 50% regardless of earnings).\n  - **Nonlinear effect:** e.g., relationship between a firm's CDS spread and its equity return (equity as embedded call option on firm assets). Neural networks introduce nonlinearity via **activation functions** (without them a deep net collapses to a linear model).\n  - Empirically (Gu, Kelly, Xiu 2020): **interaction effects drive most of the outperformance**; the jury is still out on nonlinear-only contribution (a generalized linear model with splines but no interactions barely beat purely linear).\n- **\"Data mining\" / \"knowledge discovery\":** ML can find relationships not prespecified by researchers. Attractive with the growing \"data deluge,\" but must guard against **spurious, nonreplicable correlations**.\n\n## 2. Why finance is HARD for ML (unique challenges)\n\n- **Low signal-to-noise ratio:** any single metric weakly determines a security's performance; returns compress company-specific, macro, behavioral, and positioning drivers into one dimension (up/down). Contrast with high-signal domains (e.g., video recommendations).\n- **Small data vs. big data:** financial data is small (US = ~100 yrs, at most ~1,200 monthly points per security) vs. billions/trillions in other domains. Small data limits true cross-validation, out-of-sample testing, hyperparameter tuning, robustness checks. **A simpler model on more data often beats a sophisticated model on little data.** (Alternative data is more abundant but overall finance data is still small.)\n- **Nonstationarity / adaptive markets / irrationality:** markets are **man-made and adaptive** — they \"learn\" as participants learn, and published research (e.g., Fama–French 1993) changes behavior (McLean & Pontiff 2016). The **data-generating process changes over time**; humans act irrationally (greed/fear). Contrast static domains (e.g., protein folding).\n\n## 3. Common pitfalls and how to avoid them (exam-critical)\n\n- **Overfitting:** biggest concern given short history + high degrees of freedom in ML. Symptom = great backtest, poor out-of-sample.\n  - Mitigations (cross-domain): **cross-validation, feature selection/removal, regularization, early stopping, ensembling, holdout data.**\n  - Finance advantage: **human intuition + economic domain knowledge** (\"smell tests\"). Use **SHAP values** (Shapley additive explanation — average marginal contribution of a feature) to check whether learned relationships make economic sense.\n  - **Feature engineering:** curate inputs using domain knowledge up front (e.g., profit margin, debt coverage are sensible crash predictors; CEO's last-name initial is not).\n- **Replicability:** even harder for ML than traditional stats — more tunable variables, and open-source algorithms vary by version/random seed/implementation. Mitigate with **robust data + code infrastructure**: version control, fixed parameters (algorithm version, universe, hyperparameters, feature sets), and documentation of all iterations including failures (\"research graveyards\").\n- **Lookahead bias / data leakage:**\n  - **Lookahead bias:** using data before it was actually available (use **point-in-time** data — e.g., quarterly results available 40 days after quarter-end must not be used at quarter-end).\n  - **Leakage in data:** information that belongs in one split (train/validation/test) leaks into another → overly optimistic results. Mitigate with a **gap period** (months–quarters) between train/validation/test splits.\n  - **Leakage in features:** an informative feature that would be unavailable at production time (e.g., undisclosed executive health issue).\n- **Implementation gap:** backtest trades that are impossible/infeasible live — e.g., alpha coming from small/micro-cap stocks (too costly, insufficient shares), or shorting stocks that can't be borrowed / cost too much. ML worsens this via **high turnover**.\n- **Explainability / performance attribution:** models are complex \"black boxes\" (though inspectable). Asset owners demand explanations. Technique = **partial dependence response**: fix a feature at value x, average predictions, sweep x over its range → decompose output into **linear, interaction, nonlinear** components; then form portfolios from each to attribute returns.\n\n## 4. Sample applications (examples + results)\n\n- **Predicting cross-sectional stock returns** (relative, not absolute; stock selection, not timing/allocation — an easier problem with more data).\n  - Setup: (1) investment universe, (2) ML algorithms (boosted trees/random forests, neural nets, ensembles), (3) feature set (value price-ratios, quality/profitability, momentum/past returns). Limiting the feature set = imposing domain-knowledge structure to fight small data.\n  - **Five consistent results:**\n    1. ML significantly and economically outperforms linear approaches. Gu et al. (2020): 4-layer NN long–short decile spread → out-of-sample **Sharpe 1.35 (value-weighted), 2.45 (equal-weighted)** vs. OLS **0.61 / 0.83**.\n    2. Outperformance comes from **higher-order effects (nonlinearity + interactions)**; interactions matter more than nonlinearity.\n    3. The **most important features are consistent across ML algorithms**: short-term reversal, stock & sector momentum, return volatility, firm size.\n    4. **Simple ML models beat complex ones** (less overfitting on small data).\n    5. **More data → better ML performance** (\"unreasonable effectiveness of data\").\n- **Predicting stock crashes:** crash = large idiosyncratic price drop vs. peers. Algorithms: logistic regression, random forest, gradient-boosted tree, ensemble. Features: distance-to-default, volatility, market beta + fundamentals. Used in **conservative/low-vol equity** (\"win by not losing\"). ML beats traditional in developed and emerging markets; returns come from stock selection, not sector allocation.\n- **Predicting fundamentals (earnings):** easier + more stable target than returns. Cao & You (2021), US ex-financials/utilities, 1975–2019, 28 line items + first differences (56 features). Linear ML beats traditional (~6%); nonlinear ML beats linear ML (~1–3%); **ensembles beat individuals** (\"ensembling is the closest thing to a free lunch, like diversification\").\n- **NLP in multiple languages:** embedding-based NLP to decode Chinese A-share retail-investor slang; word embeddings preserve vector arithmetic (King − Man + Woman ≈ Queen). Shows ML helps non-numerical tasks too.\n\n## 5. Conclusion takeaways\n\n- ML beats traditional methods **if applied to the right problem**; source of edge = nonlinear + interaction effects; **ensembling** adds value.\n- Finance is distinctive: less data, nonstationary, irrational participants — so know the technique's limits.\n- Other ML uses: sentiment analysis, foreign-language understanding, data cleaning, fraud detection, credit scoring, trading optimization.\n"}, {"book": "Handbook_AI_BigData", "title": "Ch. 2 — Alternative Data and AI in Investment Research", "file": "references/Handbook_AI_BigData/Ch02_Alternative_Data_and_AI.md", "md": "# Ch. 2 — Alternative Data and AI in Investment Research\n\n**Source:** Tierens, I. & Duggan, D. (Goldman Sachs Global Investment Research) — *Handbook of AI and Big Data Applications in Investments*, CFA Institute Research Foundation (2023).\n**Book pages:** 20–26 (PDF pages 41–47).\n**ML4T exam relevance:** ML Domain 1–2 (data quality, features, bias, sampling/selection bias, false positives/negatives, NLP/sentiment), QF Domain 9–10 (alpha, alternative data, backtesting caveats, systematic vs. fundamental strategies, descriptive vs. prescriptive analytics). Faithful to source; nothing invented.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Definitions and framing\n\n- **AI (broad):** using computers to mimic human problem solving.\n- **Alternative data (broad):** any data in a **nonstructured format**. The \"alternative\" label may be a **misnomer** — often it just delivers legacy information more scalably/timelier in digital form.\n- **Core principle:** alternative data and AI (incl. ML and NLP) are **tools in the research toolkit, not ends in themselves.** They **do not define the investment thesis — they help prove or disprove it.** Value only when aligned with a particular strategy.\n- Impact is **evolutionary, not revolutionary**; lines between \"traditional\" and \"alternative\" data (and what counts as \"AI\") are blurring.\n\n## 2. Where AI/alt-data is additive: systematic vs. fundamental strategies\n\n- **Systematic (breadth > depth):** need datasets/techniques applicable across **many securities**. Favor text-extracted factors (NLP repeatable across filings/news), econometric conversion of single alphas into multifactor alphas. Horizon matters — intraday news sentiment helps HFT but adds little for a weeks/months horizon.\n- **Fundamental (depth > breadth):** analyst goes **deep**, prizes **precision**; **niche datasets** complete the \"mosaic\" (e.g., social-media consumer sentiment, brick-and-mortar foot traffic, consumer search trends). Non-obvious wins: app downloads in medical devices, localized market-share for managed care, quarry production.\n\n## 3. Data sourcing in a world of oversupply\n\n- Overabundance of providers; **low barriers to entry.** With alt-data, **due-diligence burden shifts from producer to consumer** (parallel to news fact-checking).\n- Costs (onboarding, ingestion, testing, licensing) can outweigh benefits → GIR's acquisition is **demand-driven** (\"pull,\" working backward from a use case), not supply-pushed.\n- New data need not be **orthogonal** — value can come from delivering legacy data more scalably/timelier.\n- GIR keeps analysts (subject-matter experts) as **co-owners** of identifying data rather than a separate \"data scout\"; a central **Data Strategy team** helps with analysis (GS Data Works).\n\n### Three alternative-data categories (Exhibit 1)\n1. **Geospatial** — mobile foot traffic, satellite imagery, demographics, store/EV-charger locations.\n2. **Digital/online** — app downloads, online point-of-sale, website visits, product counts/pricing, search trends.\n3. **Text (NLP-addressable)** — earnings-call transcripts, filings, tweets, blogs (e.g., NLP-driven quarterly S&P 500 \"Beige Book\").\n- Categories **overlap**; combining dimensions builds a fuller mosaic (e.g., pricing power from product codes/prices + local market share + earnings-call commentary).\n\n## 4. The most time-consuming part: data \"grunt work\" (curation)\n\n- **Do not expect AI insight without understanding the data.** Data scientists succeed only by knowing data nuances, strengths, weaknesses.\n- **Algorithmic bias** stems from **biased training data**; investment apps aren't immune. Subject-matter experts (even without data-science skills) add real value here.\n- Run a **small test case** before sourcing; data can **evolve/degrade** over time (inputs removed/added/edited); derived data needs algorithm transparency.\n- **Data-quality questions by type:**\n  - **Large/complex data:** how comprehensive? **Missing values** bias averages/sums (e.g., web-scraped pricing). Understand any embedded analysis layer (e.g., auto-fill of missing data). Does the data allow the breakouts your thesis needs (e.g., style-specific SKU counts)?\n  - **Small data:** may rely on **sampling** → **undersampling / selection bias.** Examples: opt-in point-of-sale panels skew demographics beyond reweighting; satellite parking counts miss covered parking; mobile foot-traffic imprecise in malls/multitenant buildings. **Construction process determines advantages and limitations.**\n  - **NLP/text:** input details matter — paywalled content invisible to aggregators; source-specific biases (Instagram vs. Twitter vs. Reddit); guard against **false positives / false negatives** in sentiment (e.g., distinguish a bad product from out-of-stock frustration).\n- **Bottom line:** curation determines success/failure; the AI effort fails (or produces wrong outputs) if data gets less attention than the analysis. GIR keeps curation in-house (with SMEs + data scientists).\n\n## 5. The analysis spectrum\n\n- Also a **\"pull\" approach:** work backward from the use case; escalate to sophisticated methods only when simpler ones fail (outcomes swing on small assumption changes, outliers dominate).\n- **Weigh explainability + effort against AI's promise.** Prefer simplicity when it works — e.g., simply **counting mentions of \"inflation\"** in earnings calls was a robust proxy for a ~200-term screen. When complex, data scientists must supply enough **intuition** for analysts' comfort.\n- **Limits:** financial dynamics are **unstable/adaptive**; limited in-sample/out-of-sample data caps how far techniques can be pushed. **Breakpoints** (e.g., COVID-19) break statistical relationships (GIR \"nowcasting\" indicators had to be re-adapted; extreme outliers broke them) — **humans are critical** for curating data and continuously assessing algorithm results (\"man + machine\").\n- GIR's NLP focus = **surfacing content scalably** more than interpreting meaning/sentiment (accuracy-critical NLP flagged too many false positives/negatives).\n\n## 6. Measuring success — use a WIDE lens (three questions)\n\n1. **Does it create alpha?** Unlikely from a single dataset/technique. For **systematic** strategies you can **backtest with vs. without** the AI component to measure marginal alpha (usual backtesting caveats apply).\n2. **Does it produce unique insights more timely/precise?** For **fundamental** strategies direct alpha is hard to attribute; quantify improvement on specific dimensions. Most GIR AI/alt-data work is **descriptive** (feeds the thesis) rather than **prescriptive** (directly recommends a trade).\n3. **Does it create scalability** that saves analyst time or extends a hypothesis across more securities / more often? (e.g., NLP across an entire coverage universe + peers + multiple years.)\n- ROI hurdles for AI often unfairly stricter than for traditional data — questioning them is **healthy** and levels the playing field.\n\n## 7. Getting started — \"a marathon, not a sprint\"\n\n- Hire 1–2 hands-on data scientists genuinely interested in investment problems; pair with 1–2 analysts with a durable use case; start with **simple, non-complicated datasets.**\n- **Senior sponsorship** matters (cover to experiment + accountability). Involve **engineering, legal, compliance from Day 1** (storage, firewalls, terms/licensing). Consider **build vs. buy** as use cases mature.\n- **Centralized vs. embedded:** GIR chose **centralized** (extension of research teams globally/across asset classes) to connect dots across use cases.\n- Goal: **minimize algorithmic and data biases** via integrated, iterative, collaborative \"man + machine\" — until \"big\" and \"alternative\" labels may no longer be relevant.\n"}]}, {"part": "QF", "num": "10", "name": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "cheatsheet": "- **Investment hypothesis, not just correlation.** A defensible hypothesis names a *plausible economic mechanism* (why the signal predicts returns) and the *conditions* under which it should hold, weaken, or reverse. Correlation alone can come from confounding, reverse causality, data leakage, or chance — and searching many arbitrary partitions (ticker-letter groups, etc.) guarantees some spurious \"winner\" by multiple testing.\n- **Signal design & falsifiability.** Translate an idea into a measurable feature (e.g., a past-return lookback for continuation) without picking the lookback that maximized full-sample performance (overfitting). Corroborating variables (volume, fund flows) and pre-specified conditional predictions (liquid vs. illiquid behavior) make the hypothesis *more* falsifiable, not less.\n- **Forecast ≠ trade.** The *sign* of a forecast sets direction, but whether to trade depends on **magnitude vs. cost, confidence/uncertainty, risk, and portfolio constraints**. A no-trade threshold reserves capital for signals whose expected *net* edge clears costs. An accurate forecast can still lose money; a positive forecast never *guarantees* profit.\n- **Directional accuracy is not P&L.** A 60% hit rate can coexist with a *negative* average return if the wrong calls land on the big moves. Return-weighted objectives capture economic magnitude that classification accuracy discards.\n- **Position construction is a separate design problem.** Raw forecast-proportional sizing concentrates risk in high-magnitude / volatile / illiquid names; **volatility scaling** equalizes risk per position, and liquidity/position limits can override an unconstrained signal. **Dollar neutrality ≠ risk neutrality** — equal long/short dollars can still carry beta, sector, factor, and concentration exposures.\n- **Backtesting & benchmarks.** Pick a *relevant opportunity-cost* benchmark (the investable index for a long-only equity mandate), not just cash. In-sample = any data used to specify or tune the strategy; a credible **out-of-sample** test freezes the design and runs it on untouched *later* data. **Walk-forward** repeatedly trains on the past and evaluates on subsequent windows while preserving time order. Repeatedly retuning after seeing \"holdout\" results silently pulls that data back into development.\n- **Performance attribution.** Beating the market can come from a static tilt (e.g., persistent tech overweight in a tech rally), not skillful timing. Exposure-matched benchmarks, average-weight counterfactuals, and factor regressions separate genuine timing/selection alpha from compensation for systematic exposures.\n- **Turnover, costs, net alpha.** Deploy on **net**, not gross, performance. Small statistically-significant edges can be entirely consumed by spreads, commissions, and market impact at high turnover. Costs scale with dollars traded (turnover × AUM × cost rate) and are *not* constant — they vary with order size, liquidity, volatility, and execution conditions. Reducing turnover (no-trade bands) can improve net returns even if gross accuracy dips slightly.\n- **Capacity, liquidity, crowding.** Percentage returns don't survive scaling: larger orders walk the book and add market impact, so a profitable small strategy may not scale. Net edge falls as impact rises with size (0.40% → 0.20% → −0.20% in the worked example). Participation-rate/ADV limits, spreading trades (impact vs. delay/price risk), and **crowding** (correlated positions across independent funds, worst exit liquidity precisely when everyone unwinds) all cap capacity. Maximizing AUM ≠ maximizing net performance.\n- **Leverage & exposure.** Leverage magnifies **both** gains and losses (3:1 → a 2% portfolio move is ~6% of equity either way). Financing is neither free nor guaranteed; adverse moves trigger margin calls / forced deleveraging even if the thesis is right. Gross exposure = long + short (600% of equity in the worked case); net-dollar-neutral portfolios can still take big losses when longs fall and shorts rise. Net dollars miss sector/beta/factor/liquidity/concentration risk.\n- **Risk controls.** A fixed % stop-loss does not guarantee the exit price (gaps/illiquidity), hits volatile assets differently, and can whipsaw (crystallize a loss right before recovery). Controls tuned to normal conditions may misbehave in gaps/illiquidity/correlated stress. Position sizing and portfolio-level limits *complement* (not replace) stops.\n- **Robustness, degradation, live deployment.** Regime shifts change both feature distributions and the feature→return relationship; retraining on recent data is *not* a guaranteed fix and may be the wrong response if the economic mechanism is gone (consider recalibration, redesign, or retirement). Backtest success ≠ production success — implementation assumptions (close-price fills), data quality, broker/order-pipeline failures, and timing separate live from theoretical results. Monitor distributions/residuals, IC, turnover, slippage; use paper trading / staged rollout; compare fills to prices *actually attainable after the decision time*. Retirement decisions should weigh statistical uncertainty, implementation quality, current conditions, and whether the thesis still holds — no single metric or one unlucky week decides it.", "traps": "- **No inverted-logic questions in this domain.** Every question here uses a standard per-choice Answer Key (\"A. False – …\", \"B. True – …\"), i.e., **Normal polarity throughout (True = accurate).** There is no \"Answer Key (True = incorrect; False = accurate)\" header on any D10 item. Do not invent inversion — grade each statement on its literal truth.\n- **\"The statement reverses the correct conclusion\" negation traps (pervasive).** Many *False* choices are the correct idea stated backwards, so they read plausibly. Examples flagged in the key: G1Q2A/E, G2Q1B/C, G2Q2D, G2Q3B/E, G3Q1D, G3Q2A/D, G4Q1A/C, G5Q1B, G5Q2A, G5Q3C, G6Q1E, G6Q2D, G6Q3C/E, G7Q1A, G7Q2A/D. Always read the sentence's literal claim, decide the true statement, then check whether the choice matches or *flips* it.\n- **Double-negative \"embedded claim\" choices (\"A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that …\").** The inner claim is *accurate*, so \"would be wrong to accept it\" is **False**. Appears at **G4Q3E** (impact + spreads should be included when material — accept it) and **G6Q1B** (a 2% loss at 3:1 is ~6% of equity — accept it). Parse the inner claim, judge its truth, then negate.\n- **\"Necessarily / always / guarantees / cannot\" absolutes are almost always False.** Statistical significance ⇏ net profitability (G1Q1E); a positive forecast ⇏ guaranteed profit (G2Q1E); accuracy ⇏ profitability (G2Q2A); dollar neutrality ⇏ neutral to all factors (G2Q3D); strong in-sample ⇏ future performance (G3Q2C); faster reaction ⇏ superior (G4Q3C); more AUM ⇏ more profit (G5Q2E); zero net dollars ⇏ no large loss (G6Q2C); a stop ⇏ exact fill (G6Q3A); retraining ⇏ restored performance (G7Q1B).\n- **Arithmetic checks matter.** Several choices hinge on a computation: net edge = gross − cost (G4Q2, G4Q3, G5Q2), turnover × AUM × rate = dollar cost (G4Q2), leverage math (G6Q1A, G6Q2B), 12% − 14% = −2 pp underperformance (G3Q1C). Do the math; a plausible-sounding sign or figure is often the wrong one.\n- **Gross vs. net, correlation vs. mechanism, accuracy vs. P&L, dollar-neutral vs. risk-neutral** are the four recurring conceptual confusions — most wrong choices collapse one of these distinctions.", "references": "1. **Lopez de Prado — *Advances in Financial Machine Learning*:** backtest overfitting and selection bias under multiple testing, the **deflated Sharpe ratio**, purged/embargoed cross-validation and walk-forward, transaction-cost/market-impact realism, strategy capacity, structural breaks/drift, and false discoveries in strategy research (D10 G1, G3, G4, G5, G7).\n2. **Grinold & Kahn — *Active Portfolio Management*:** the **fundamental law of active management** (information coefficient × breadth), signal-to-position construction and risk models, benchmarks/residual return, attribution, net-of-cost alpha, and long/short exposure (D10 G2, G3, G4, G6, G7).\n3. **CFA Program Curriculum:** performance evaluation and attribution, benchmark quality, leverage and financing/margin risk, liquidity and market-structure risk, and risk management/stress testing (D10 G3, G5, G6).\n4. **Investopedia:** \"Backtesting,\" \"Data mining bias,\" \"Overfitting,\" \"In-sample vs. out-of-sample,\" \"Walk-forward optimization,\" \"Transaction costs,\" \"Market impact,\" \"Turnover ratio,\" \"Leverage,\" \"Margin call,\" \"Gross/Net exposure,\" \"Stop-loss order,\" \"Slippage,\" \"Implementation shortfall,\" \"Crowded trade\" (definitional anchors across all groups).\n5. **Concept references (durable):** turnover-to-cost arithmetic (turnover × AUM × cost rate), gross-vs-net alpha, dollar-neutral vs. risk-neutral exposure, and the accuracy-vs-P&L distinction — standard quantitative-strategy evaluation concepts underlying D10 G2, G4, G5, G6.", "bookNotes": []}], "questions": [{"id": "ML-D1G1Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You have just joined a multi-strategy hedge fund. The Portfolio Manager (PM) asks you to “build something that finds patterns in the market and tells us when to buy or sell, ” while a client suggests clustering regimes without using returns. You are asked to clarify how these approaches differ.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Predicting next-day returns using labeled historical data is a supervised learning problem.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Predicting next-day returns from labeled history is supervised: the realized next-day return is a known target (Y) paired with each feature row, so the model learns a mapping X → Y.", "whyPool": "The historical next-day returns serve as labeled targets."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Clustering days into regimes without labels is supervised learning.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Clustering days into regimes *without* labels is unsupervised — there is no target to predict; the model only groups by feature similarity.", "whyPool": "Clustering observations without labels is unsupervised learning."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Discovering structure without labels is an unsupervised learning task.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Discovering structure with no supplied targets is the definition of unsupervised learning (e.g., clustering, dimensionality reduction).", "whyPool": "Unsupervised learning seeks structure when target labels are not supplied."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A buy/sell output always implies supervised learning.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A buy/sell output does not imply supervised learning — a hand-coded rule or an unsupervised signal can also emit actions. The *action format* doesn't reveal how (or whether) learning used labels.", "whyPool": "A rule can produce buy/sell actions without having learned from labeled examples."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The presence of labels determines whether a problem is supervised.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "It is exactly the availability of example-target pairs that makes a problem supervised; without paired targets you cannot do supervised learning.", "whyPool": "Supervised learning requires examples paired with target values or classes."}], "reference": "Supervised vs. unsupervised learning — ISLR ch.2 (§2.1); scikit-learn \"Choosing the right estimator\" flowchart.", "n": 1}, {"id": "ML-D1G1Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "An asset manager wants to study unusual trading behavior. The compliance officer asks for a system that groups accounts by trading patterns, but no account has been labeled “normal” or “suspicious”; a junior analyst nevertheless 14 Rev:081020261230 proposes training a suspicious-account classifier. The team must decide what can reasonably be learned from the available data. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Grouping accounts using only similarities in their observed behavior is an unsupervised learning task.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Grouping accounts by observed-behavior similarity with no labels is genuinely unsupervised.", "whyPool": "The model is discovering structure without labeled targets."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The absence of suspicious-account labels prevents the team from discovering any structure in the data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: lacking suspicious labels does NOT prevent finding structure — unsupervised methods still surface groups, similarities, and outliers/anomalies.", "whyPool": "Unsupervised methods can identify similarities, groups, or unusual observations without labels."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If investigators later label accounts as suspicious or not suspicious, those labels could support a supervised classification task.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: once investigators label accounts, those discrete labels can train a supervised classifier.", "whyPool": "A discrete labeled outcome can be used to train a supervised classifier."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A cluster produced by the unsupervised model is automatically a verified compliance category.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a cluster reflects patterns in the chosen features, not a verified legal/compliance category — it carries no ground-truth \"suspicious\" meaning by itself.", "whyPool": "A cluster reflects patterns in the selected data and features, not a verified legal or compliance conclusion."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Domain experts would still need to interpret whether a discovered cluster represents suspicious behavior.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: humans must interpret whether statistical structure maps to real-world (suspicious) meaning.", "whyPool": "Human interpretation is needed to connect statistical structure to real-world meaning."}], "reference": "Unsupervised anomaly/cluster discovery — ISLR ch.12 (unsupervised learning); scikit-learn \"Clustering\" and \"Novelty and outlier detection.\"", "n": 2}, {"id": "ML-D1G1Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative research team has historical market features and realized one-week returns. The Head of Research wants both a return forecast and a map of recurring market regimes, but requires the team to explain which outputs depend on labeled outcomes. The same historical rows may be used in two different analyses.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Training a model to predict realized one-week return uses realized return as a supervised target.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Predicting realized one-week return uses that return as a supervised numeric target (each example paired with a known outcome → regression).", "whyPool": "Each training example is paired with a known numerical outcome."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Removing the return column and grouping weeks by feature similarity changes the task to an unsupervised one.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Drop the return column and group weeks by feature similarity → no target supplied → the task becomes unsupervised structure discovery.", "whyPool": "Without supplied targets, the model is being asked to discover structure."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because both analyses use the same feature rows, they must belong to the same learning paradigm.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Using the same feature rows does not force one paradigm; the paradigm is set by the task/target, not by which rows are used.", "whyPool": "The learning paradigm depends on the task and targets, not merely on the feature rows used."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Naming clusters “bull, ” “bear, ” and “sideways” after inspecting them does not retroactively make the clustering procedure supervised.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Naming clusters \"bull/bear/sideways\" after inspecting them is post-hoc interpretation — it supplies no labels *during* learning, so the clustering stays unsupervised.", "whyPool": "Post hoc interpretation of clusters does not provide labels during learning."}, {"L": "E", "text": "An unsupervised regime analysis is guaranteed to produce groups that are useful for predicting future returns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Unsupervised structure is not guaranteed useful for prediction — discovered groups may be unrelated to the trading objective.", "whyPool": "Discovered structure may be unrelated to the trading objective."}], "reference": "Task-defines-paradigm distinction — ISLR §2.1; concept \"clustering is not classification.\"", "n": 3}, {"id": "ML-D1G1Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a senior quant developer at a high-frequency trading firm. The Head of Strategy presents a plan for a \"Market State Classifier. \" The plan requires an algorithm to take 50 historical order-book metrics and group them into four distinct \"Market Regimes\" without using any historical price direction or pre-existing labels. After these regimes are identified, a second system is tasked with taking these regime IDs and predicting the probability of a liquidity spike in the next 100 milliseconds. The Chief Risk Officer asks you to identify the paradigms that underpin this strategy.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The initial step of grouping order-book metrics into four \"Regimes\" without using target outcomes is an Unsupervised Learning task.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Grouping order-book metrics into regimes without target outcomes is unsupervised (clustering).", "whyPool": "Identifying clusters or regimes without target labels is the defining characteristic of unsupervised learning."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Training the second system to predict the probability of a \"Liquidity Spike\" (a binary event) is an example of Supervised Classification.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Predicting spike vs. no-spike (a discrete binary event) is supervised classification.", "whyPool": "Predicting the probability of a discrete category (spike vs. no spike) is a supervised classification task."}, {"L": "C", "text": "In the first step, the \"four distinct regimes\" are discovered by the algorithm based on patterns in the features (X), rather than being mapped to a known \"ground truth. \"", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Regimes are discovered from patterns in the features X, not mapped to a known ground truth — the hallmark of clustering.", "whyPool": "Unsupervised clustering seeks to find inherent structure within the inputs alone."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Supervised Learning is generally preferred over Unsupervised Learning when the objective is to explore the natural structure of a new, unlabeled financial dataset.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "For exploring natural structure in a *new, unlabeled* dataset, unsupervised is the appropriate choice; supervised learning cannot run without labels.", "whyPool": "Unsupervised learning is specifically designed for structure discovery in unlabeled data; Supervised learning requires labels to function."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the Head of Strategy decides to manually label the regimes as \"High Volatility\" or \"Low Volatility\" before the model runs, the task remains Unsupervised because the inputs are still the same.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Manually labeling regimes (High/Low Volatility) *before* the model learns turns the task into supervised classification — same inputs, but now learning from labels.", "whyPool": "Providing labels (High vs. Low Volatility) for the model to learn transforms the problem into a Supervised Classification task. 16 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Two-stage unsupervised→supervised pipelines — ISLR ch.12 + ch.4 (classification); scikit-learn clustering as feature engineering.", "n": 4}, {"id": "ML-D1G1Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You have been hired by a FinTech startup to audit their \"Black Box\" market analysis tool. The lead developer claims the tool is \"highly adaptive\" because it takes 200 technical indicators and, every hour, automatically groups the thousands of stocks in the Russell 3000 into 12 \"neighborhoods\" of similar price-action behavior. The tool then assigns each neighborhood a sentiment score based on the average 1-hour forward return of the stocks within that group. The CEO is confused by the marketing material, which calls this \"Hybrid Learning, \" and asks you to clarify the underlying paradigms.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The process of grouping thousands of stocks into 12 \"neighborhoods\" without using sector labels or target categories is a Supervised Learning task.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Grouping without sector labels/target categories is unsupervised (clustering), not supervised.", "whyPool": "Grouping data points based on similarity without pre-existing labels is Unsupervised Learning (specifically clustering)."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Identifying clusters of stocks that behave similarly in high-dimensional feature space is a core application of Unsupervised Learning.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Finding clusters of similar behavior in high-dimensional feature space is a core unsupervised application.", "whyPool": "Unsupervised learning is designed to find latent structure or groupings in data when no response variable is provided."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Once the tool calculates the average 1-hour forward return for a group to assign a sentiment score, it is using a \"label\" (return) that allows for a Supervised evaluation of that group's performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Using average forward return as a score attaches a target (Y) to groups → a supervised evaluation of the clusters' predictive utility.", "whyPool": "The forward return acts as a supervised target (Y) that allows the system to measure the predictive utility of the discovered clusters."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The tool's ability to group stocks is fundamentally dependent on having a \"Ground Truth\" label for every stock that tells the model which neighborhood it belongs to before the grouping begins.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Clustering does NOT need a pre-existing ground-truth neighborhood label per stock; it derives groupings from features X alone.", "whyPool": "Unsupervised learning does not require pre-existing labels; it discovers the groupings mathematically based on the features (X) alone."}, {"L": "E", "text": "In this scenario, Unsupervised Learning is used for structure discovery (the neighborhoods), while the returns provide a quantitative basis for a Supervised prediction task.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "This is the standard pipeline: unsupervised for structure discovery (neighborhoods) + supervised prediction using the returns.", "whyPool": "This represents a common pipeline where unsupervised methods find patterns that are subsequently used or labeled for supervised tasks."}], "reference": "Unsupervised feature construction feeding supervised targets — ISLR ch.12; \"clustering + downstream prediction\" concept.", "n": 5}, {"id": "ML-D1G1Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a senior quant at a prop trading desk. The Head of Research is proposing a \"Market Signature\" project. The goal is to take 200 different technical indicators for 1,000 stocks and ask a model to discover \"natural\" clusters of stocks that behave similarly during periods of high volatility, without using any sector labels or prior groupings. Once these clusters are identified, the team plans to use them as a foundation for a separate model that predicts the next-day volatility for each cluster. You are asked to present the technical requirements for this project to the investment committee. 17 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The process of discovering \"natural\" clusters based on price-action behavior without using pre-defined labels is an unsupervised learning task.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Discovering natural clusters by feature similarity without predefined labels is unsupervised learning.", "whyPool": "Clustering data points based on inherent similarity in feature space without target labels is the definition of unsupervised learning."}, {"L": "B", "text": "In the first phase of the project, the model requires \"Target Labels\" to help it understand which clusters are most profitable for the desk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Phase-1 clustering is driven by relationships among features X, not by external target labels indicating \"profitable\" clusters.", "whyPool": "Unsupervised learning discovery is driven by the relationship between the features (X), not by external target labels."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The second phase, which involves predicting a specific numerical value (next-day volatility), is a supervised regression task.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Predicting a continuous value (next-day volatility) is supervised regression.", "whyPool": "Predicting a quantitative/continuous response variable like volatility is a supervised regression problem."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Unsupervised learning is fundamentally incapable of being used as a preprocessing step for a supervised learning pipeline.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Unsupervised clustering is commonly used as a preprocessing/feature-engineering step for supervised pipelines — not \"incapable.\"", "whyPool": "It is very common to use unsupervised clustering to create features or \"signatures\" that are then fed into supervised models."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The presence of a \"supervisor\" in this scenario refers to the numerical volatility value used to train the second model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "In supervised learning the \"supervisor\" is the ground-truth target (Y) — here the numerical volatility that trains the second model.", "whyPool": "In supervised learning, the \"supervisor\" is the ground truth label (Y) that guides the algorithm's parameter adjustments. G2: Problem Type – Regression vs. Classification"}], "reference": "\"Supervisor = Y\" terminology and unsupervised-as-preprocessing — ISLR §2.1, ch.12.", "n": 6}, {"id": "ML-D1G2Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio manager wants a model for tomorrow’s S&P 500 movement. One team proposes forecasting the exact percentage return, while another proposes predicting whether the return will exceed 0.50%; the risk team wants to understand why the two teams may require different evaluation methods. Both teams use the same market features.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Forecasting the exact percentage return is naturally framed as regression.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Exact percentage return is a continuous numeric target → regression.", "whyPool": "The target is a continuous numerical value."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Predicting whether the return exceeds 0.50% is naturally framed as binary classification.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "\"Exceeds 0.50%?\" has two discrete classes defined by a threshold → binary classification.", "whyPool": "The target has two discrete classes defined by the threshold."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Using identical input features forces both teams to use the same problem type.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Identical features do not force the same problem type — the *target definition* does.", "whyPool": "The target definition, not the feature set alone, determines the framing."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Converting the continuous return into an above-threshold label discards some information about return magnitude.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Converting a continuous return to an above/below label discards magnitude: very different returns can share the same class.", "whyPool": "Returns with different magnitudes can receive the same class label."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A model with low regression error must also classify the above-threshold outcome perfectly.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Low regression error does not guarantee perfect classification — a tiny numeric error near the threshold flips the class.", "whyPool": "Even small numerical errors can place observations on the wrong side of a threshold."}], "reference": "Regression vs. classification framing & thresholding — ISLR §2.1.5, ch.4.", "n": 7}, {"id": "ML-D1G2Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A brokerage is building a client-risk alert. The product owner initially requests a probability that each account will receive a margin call within five days, then asks whether the system could instead estimate the dollar size of the expected shortfall. Operations can investigate only the highest-priority cases each morning. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Predicting whether a margin call occurs is a classification problem.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: margin call vs. none is a two-class discrete outcome → classification.", "whyPool": "The realized outcome belongs to one of two discrete classes."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Predicting the dollar size of a shortfall is a regression problem.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: dollar shortfall is a continuous target → regression.", "whyPool": "Dollar shortfall is a continuous target."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Producing a probability for the margin-call class changes the underlying task into regression solely because the output is numeric.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: producing a class *probability* does not turn classification into regression — a probability is a standard classification output.", "whyPool": "A class probability is still an output of a classification task."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The choice between the two framings should reflect how operations will use the result.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the framing should follow how operations will actually use the result.", "whyPool": "The useful target depends on the decision the model must support."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Classification and regression targets can always be evaluated with the same metric without changing its interpretation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: classification and regression targets require *different* metrics; the same metric can't be applied without changing its meaning.", "whyPool": "Metrics must be appropriate for the target and decision objective."}], "reference": "Class probabilities vs. regression outputs, metric selection — ISLR ch.4; scikit-learn \"Metrics and scoring.\"", "n": 8}, {"id": "ML-D1G2Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A trading desk wants to predict whether each stock will underperform, track, or outperform its sector next month. A researcher suggests predicting continuous excess return first and then assigning the three categories using thresholds, while the desk head proposes learning the categories directly. The desk cares most about avoiding large long positions in eventual underperformers. 19 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Directly predicting the three named outcomes is a multiclass classification task.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Assigning one of three named outcomes is multiclass classification.", "whyPool": "Each stock is assigned to one of three discrete classes."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Predicting excess return first is a regression task, even if the forecast is later converted into a category.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Predicting excess return is regression even if later binned — the learned target stays continuous; thresholding is a separate decision step.", "whyPool": "The learned target remains continuous; thresholding is a later decision step."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The direct classifier and the thresholded regression model are guaranteed to make identical decisions if trained on the same observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A direct classifier and a thresholded regressor optimize different objectives and can learn different decision boundaries — not guaranteed identical.", "whyPool": "The models optimize different targets and can learn different boundaries."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The desk’s asymmetric concern about underperformers should influence evaluation of the resulting decisions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The desk's asymmetric cost (underperformers hurt more) should shape how decisions are evaluated (cost-sensitive / asymmetric loss).", "whyPool": "Errors involving underperformers carry different practical costs for this desk."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Defining narrower category thresholds preserves all information contained in the continuous return target.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Any conversion to categories loses within-category magnitude information, regardless of how narrow the thresholds are.", "whyPool": "Any conversion to categories loses within-category magnitude information."}], "reference": "Multiclass classification & cost-sensitive evaluation — ISLR ch.4; scikit-learn \"Multiclass and multioutput,\" confusion-matrix costs.", "n": 9}, {"id": "ML-D1G2Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Research Lead at a mid-sized asset management firm. Your Head of Trading wants to build a system that monitors the \"Big 7\" tech stocks. She asks for two specific outputs: first, a model that predicts the exact closing price of NVIDIA (NVDA) for tomorrow's session to help with entry points; and second, a model that simply flags whether a stock will be \"Bullish\" (Up) or \"Bearish\" (Down) to trigger automated hedging. You must explain to the junior developers how these two tasks differ in their mathematical framing. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The task of predicting the exact closing price of NVDA is a regression problem because the output variable is continuous.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: exact price is a continuous response → regression.", "whyPool": "Regression involves predicting a quantitative/continuous response."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Flagging a stock as \"Bullish\" or \"Bearish\" is a classification problem because the output falls into discrete categories.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: bullish/bearish is a discrete category → classification.", "whyPool": "Classification involves predicting a qualitative/categorical response."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A regression model can be converted into a classification model by applying a threshold to its continuous output (e.g., if predicted return > 0, then \"Bullish\").", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a regression output can be thresholded/binned into a categorical decision (e.g., return > 0 ⇒ Bullish).", "whyPool": "We often use regression to predict a value and then \"bin\" that value to make a categorical decision."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Mean Squared Error (MSE) is an appropriate evaluation metric for the \"Bullish/Bearish\" flagging model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: MSE is a regression metric; a bullish/bearish classifier is judged by error rate/accuracy, not MSE.", "whyPool": "MSE is used for regression; classification typically uses metrics like Error Rate or Accuracy."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Statistical learning for classification aims to estimate the probability that an observation belongs to a particular category.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: classifiers typically work by estimating P(class | X).", "whyPool": "Most classifiers work by first estimating the probability of class membership."}], "reference": "Regression vs. classification metrics, probabilistic classifiers — ISLR §2.2, ch.4.", "n": 10}, {"id": "ML-D1G2Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Risk Analyst at a large investment bank. Your team is developing a new suite of models to analyze corporate bonds. Your Director wants one model to estimate the exact yield-to-maturity (YTM) of a bond based on its credit spread and duration. At the same time, the compliance department requires a second model that simply flags whether a bond should be \"Approved\" or \"Denied\" for the firm's buy-list based on its risk profile. You are tasked with explaining the fundamental differences in how these models are evaluated. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The task of estimating the exact yield-to-maturity (YTM) of a bond is a regression problem because the output is a continuous numerical value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: exact YTM is continuous → regression.", "whyPool": "Regression is used when the target variable is quantitative and continuous."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The \"Approved/Denied\" model is a classification task because it predicts a discrete, qualitative category.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: approve/denied is a discrete qualitative category → classification.", "whyPool": "Classification aims to assign observations to distinct, non-numerical categories."}, {"L": "C", "text": "For the YTM model, a primary goal is to minimize a loss function like Mean Squared Error (MSE), which measures the distance between the predicted and actual yields.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: minimizing MSE (distance between predicted and actual yields) is the standard regression goal.", "whyPool": "MSE is the standard metric for assessing the accuracy of a regression model."}, {"L": "D", "text": "In the \"Approved/Denied\" model, the output would typically be a continuous range of values from negative infinity to positive infinity, where the magnitude of the number indicates the bond's price.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a classifier outputs class labels or class probabilities, not an unbounded (−∞, +∞) range whose magnitude encodes price.", "whyPool": "Classification models typically output class labels or the probability of belonging to a specific class, not an unbounded continuous range."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Both tasks fall under the supervised learning paradigm because they require historical data where the \"ground truth\" (actual YTM or actual Approval status) is known.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: both are supervised — each needs labeled history (actual YTM / actual approval).", "whyPool": "Both regression and classification are supervised paradigms that rely on labeled training data. 21 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Supervised regression vs. classification & loss functions — ISLR §2.1, ch.4; MSE definition.", "n": 11}, {"id": "ML-D1G2Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are the Lead Architect for a \"Macro-Regime\" trading desk. The desk wants to predict the Consumer Price Index (CPI) to determine if the economy is entering a \"Hyper-inflationary\" or \"Stable\" state. A junior quantitative researcher argues that because the ultimate goal is to trade a binary outcome (Long vs. Flat), the model should be trained as a Classifier from the start. You must evaluate the implications of this modeling choice versus a standard Regression approach. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "If the model is trained to output the exact CPI value (e.g., 2.1%), it is a regression problem; if it is trained to output a \"Regime ID\" (e.g., 1 for Hyper, 0 for Stable), it is a classification problem.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: outputting exact CPI = regression; outputting a regime ID (1/0) = classification. The output's nature defines the problem type.", "whyPool": "The nature of the output (continuous vs. discrete) defines the problem type."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Transforming a continuous CPI target into binary categories (\"Hyper\" vs. \"Stable\") prior to training inherently discards information about the magnitude and variance of the underlying data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: binarizing continuous CPI before training discards magnitude and variance information.", "whyPool": "Categorization \"collapses\" the data, losing the specific distance between points."}, {"L": "C", "text": "In a classification framing of this problem, the model's loss function would typically penalize a \"Stable\" prediction for a 2.1% CPI the same way it would penalize a \"Stable\" prediction for a 10.5% CPI, assuming both are above the \"Hyper\" threshold.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a classification loss (error rate) penalizes a wrong \"Stable\" call for 2.1% CPI the same as for 10.5% CPI if both exceed the threshold — distance from boundary is ignored.", "whyPool": "Classification metrics (like Error Rate) generally treat all misclassifications as equal, regardless of the distance from the decision boundary."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Accuracy is the most robust metric for evaluating a regression model's ability to capture the sensitivity of CPI to interest rate changes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: accuracy is a *classification* metric; a regression model's sensitivity is assessed with distance metrics (MSE/MAE), not accuracy.", "whyPool": "Accuracy is a classification metric; regression requires distance-based metrics like MSE or MAE."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Choosing a classification framing over regression for a continuous underlying phenomenon is often a \"Model Selection\" tradeoff that prioritizes decision-making simplicity over predictive granularity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: classifying a continuous phenomenon is a model-selection tradeoff — decision simplicity vs. predictive granularity.", "whyPool": "This represents a tradeoff between the interpretability of a binary signal and the statistical richness of a numerical forecast."}], "reference": "Information loss from binning, metric mismatch — ISLR §2.1.5, §2.2.3.", "n": 12}, {"id": "ML-D1G2Q7", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are a Senior Quantitative Strategist for a fixed-income desk. Your Portfolio Manager is exploring a \"Yield Curve Regime\" strategy. She believes that when the spread between the 10-year and 2-year Treasury yields is negative (inverted), the model should switch to a defensive posture. A data scientist suggests two ways to model this: 1. Model A: Predict the numerical basis point spread (e.g., -12 bps) and then check if it is below zero. 2. Model B: Train the model directly to output a Binary Indicator (1 for Inverted, 0 for Normal) based on macroeconomic features. You are asked to evaluate the mathematical implications of these choices during the quarterly risk review. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate. 22 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Model A represents a Regression framing, while Model B represents a Classification framing of the same underlying economic phenomenon.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: continuous spread target (A) = regression; binary indicator target (B) = classification.", "whyPool": "The choice between a continuous numerical target and a discrete binary target defines the task type."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If the 10-year/2-year spread is historically -10 bps, and Model A predicts -40 bps, the Mean Squared Error (MSE) will penalize this error more heavily than if the model had predicted -15 bps.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: MSE grows with the squared deviation, so predicting −40 (true −10) is penalized more than −15.", "whyPool": "MSE is a distance-based metric where the magnitude of the deviation from the true value directly increases the loss."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the true state is \"Inverted\" (-10 bps) and Model B predicts \"Inverted\" (1), the classification error rate will remain zero regardless of whether the actual spread was -5 bps or -500 bps.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: if Model B predicts \"Inverted\" correctly, error rate is 0 whether the true spread was −5 or −500 bps — magnitude is ignored.", "whyPool": "Classification metrics are typically \"all-or-nothing\" regarding the label; they do not account for the \"distance\" from the decision boundary in the same way regression metrics do."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Model B is inherently more \"informative\" than Model A because it directly identifies the regime that the Portfolio Manager cares about for decision-making.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: Model A is *more* informative — it gives the sign (regime) plus the magnitude/severity of inversion; B discards magnitude.", "whyPool": "Model A is technically more informative because it provides the regime (via the sign) plus the magnitude of the inversion, which might indicate the severity of the signal."}, {"L": "E", "text": "In a regression task (Model A), the model attempts to estimate the conditional mean of the spread, whereas in a classification task (Model B), the model estimates the probability of class membership.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: regression estimates E(Y \\| X); classification estimates P(Y = j \\| X).", "whyPool": "These are the fundamental mathematical objectives: regression estimates E( Y ∣ X ), while classification estimates P( Y=j ∣ X)."}], "reference": "Conditional mean vs. class probability objectives — ISLR §2.1.1, §2.2.3, ch.4 (Bayes classifier).", "n": 13}, {"id": "ML-D1G2Q8", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Researcher at a global macro fund. The Chief Investment Officer (CIO) wants to develop an automated risk-on/risk-off system. The system must evaluate the current yield spread between high-yield corporate bonds and 10-year Treasuries to determine the Probability of a Recession within the next 12 months (expressed as a decimal from 0.0 to 1.0). A junior developer argues that since the goal is to make a binary trade decision (Risk-On or Risk- Off), the model should be evaluated using an Accuracy metric.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The task of predicting a Probability of Recession (e.g., 0.65) based on yield spreads is a regression problem because the response variable is quantitative and continuous.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Predicting a value on a continuous scale (a probability 0–1) is a regression task, since the response is quantitative/continuous.", "whyPool": "Predicting a value on a continuous scale (like a probability from 0 to 1) is a regression task."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Accuracy is an appropriate evaluation metric for this model because probabilities are fundamentally used to make categorical decisions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Accuracy is a classification metric; a continuous probability output needs distance-based metrics (MSE). Note: the pool treats this as pure regression; in practice probabilistic classifiers are also scored with log-loss/AUC — see reference.", "whyPool": "Accuracy is a classification metric; continuous outputs require distance-based metrics like MSE."}, {"L": "C", "text": "In this regression context, the Mean Squared Error (MSE) would measure the average squared difference between the predicted probability and the actual historical occurrence (1 for recession, 0 for no recession). 23 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "MSE here = mean squared difference between predicted probability and the 0/1 outcome — this is the Brier score.", "whyPool": "In probability estimation, MSE (often called a Brier score in this context) evaluates how close the predicted probability was to the actual outcome."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the model were modified to only output \"Recession\" or \"No Recession, \" the mathematical task would shift from regression to classification.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Restricting output to \"Recession\"/\"No Recession\" (a discrete category) shifts the task from regression to classification.", "whyPool": "Predicting a discrete category (qualitative response) is the definition of classification. ("}, {"L": "E", "text": "Regression models in finance are strictly limited to predicting price and cannot be used to estimate probabilities or rates.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Regression handles any quantitative response — interest rates, probabilities, economic indicators — not just price.", "whyPool": "Regression is used for any quantitative response, including interest rates, probabilities, and economic indicators. G3: Training vs. Inference"}], "reference": "Brier score / probability calibration — Brier (1950); scikit-learn \"Probability calibration\"; ISLR ch.4 for the regression-of-probabilities view.", "withdrawn": true, "n": 14}, {"id": "ML-D1G3Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A proprietary trading firm retrains a signal model overnight and applies it to thousands of securities during the trading day. The Head of Trading accepts a lengthy nightly computation but requires each live prediction within 10 milliseconds. A junior developer argues that the model’s total daily compute time is the only performance measure that matters. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Estimating the model’s parameters from historical examples occurs during training.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: estimating parameters from historical examples is training.", "whyPool": "Training is the process of fitting the model from data."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Applying the fitted model to a new feature vector occurs during inference.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: applying the fitted model to a new feature vector is inference.", "whyPool": "Inference uses the fitted model to generate a prediction for new input."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Slow overnight training necessarily violates the 10-millisecond live prediction constraint.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: the 10 ms limit constrains *inference*, not overnight fitting; slow training doesn't violate it.", "whyPool": "The stated latency constraint applies to live inference, not overnight fitting. 24 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "A model may be practical here if training is expensive but inference satisfies the live latency limit.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Wrong statement to flag as incorrect — actually a model IS practical if training is expensive but inference meets latency; different stages tolerate different costs. (Key marks it True = \"incorrect\" because the choice is phrased in the negative frame of \"flawed claims\"; per the rationale, different stages can tolerate different costs.)", "whyPool": "Different deployment stages can tolerate different computational costs."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Measuring only total daily compute time can conceal whether individual live predictions miss their deadlines.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Wrong: an aggregate daily-compute number can hide whether individual predictions blow the per-prediction deadline.", "whyPool": "An aggregate measure does not establish per-prediction latency."}], "reference": "Training vs. inference cost, latency SLAs — ISLR §2.1 (fit vs. predict); scikit-learn \"Computational performance\" / prediction latency.", "n": 15}, {"id": "ML-D1G3Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A wealth manager retrains a risk model once per quarter but scores every client portfolio each evening. The CTO wants to move the scoring service to a smaller server and asks whether the team can judge feasibility using the quarterly training benchmark alone. The service must finish all client scores before overnight reports are generated.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The frequency of scoring makes inference cost operationally important even though training is infrequent.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Frequent scoring makes inference cost operationally important even though training is rare — repeated application can dominate total cost.", "whyPool": "Repeated application can dominate operational cost."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A quarterly training benchmark is sufficient to determine whether nightly scoring will finish on time.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A training benchmark does not establish scoring throughput/latency — they are different operations.", "whyPool": "Training performance does not establish scoring throughput or latency."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Memory needed to store and apply the fitted model can matter during inference.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Inference-time memory (to store and apply the fitted model) is a real deployment constraint.", "whyPool": "Deployment feasibility includes inference-time memory and computation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Retraining the model is required before every client can receive a prediction.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "One fitted model is normally applied to many observations; you don't retrain per prediction.", "whyPool": "One fitted model can normally be applied to many new observations."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The team should benchmark the fitted model on a realistic nightly scoring workload.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The right test is a representative nightly-scoring (inference) benchmark against the reporting deadline.", "whyPool": "A representative inference benchmark directly tests the reporting constraint."}], "reference": "Inference-time resource planning — scikit-learn \"Computational performance\"; fit-once/apply-many concept.", "n": 16}, {"id": "ML-D1G3Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A market-making desk observes that its model’s behavior has degraded as market conditions changed. The risk officer asks for daily retraining, while engineering notes that fitting takes six hours and the resulting model can score orders almost instantly. The desk must distinguish model updating from live model use when assessing the proposal.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Generating a quote decision with the currently fitted model is inference.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Generating a quote with the currently fitted model is inference (applying the model).", "whyPool": "The fitted model is being applied to current inputs."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Incorporating the latest labeled observations by refitting the model is training.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Refitting on the latest labeled observations is training.", "whyPool": "Refitting from updated examples is a training operation."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Fast inference proves that daily retraining can be completed within the available maintenance window.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Fast inference says nothing about the 6-hour fit fitting within the maintenance window.", "whyPool": "Inference speed provides no guarantee about the six-hour fitting process."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A retraining schedule introduces an operational tradeoff even when live inference is fast.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A retraining schedule is an operational tradeoff (model freshness vs. fit time/logistics) even when inference is fast.", "whyPool": "Model freshness must be weighed against fitting time and deployment logistics."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Once a model has been trained, changing market conditions cannot affect the quality of its future predictions. 25 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Changing market conditions (distribution shift) can degrade a trained model's future predictions.", "whyPool": "A changed data-generating environment can reduce generalization quality."}], "reference": "Model staleness / retraining cadence & distribution shift — ISLR §2.2 (irreducible error, changing f); concept \"concept drift.\"", "n": 17}, {"id": "ML-D1G3Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Software Engineer at a low-latency proprietary trading firm. Your team is developing a \"Market Microstructure Momentum\" strategy. The Senior Trader notes that while it takes several hours to optimize the model parameters using a massive historical database of limit order books, the actual execution of the model to produce a trade signal must occur in under 500 microseconds once a new data packet arrives. You are tasked with explaining the resource allocation for these two distinct phases. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The \"Training\" phase is the fit-once (or periodic) process where the model learns the relationship between order book features and future price movement.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: training is the fit-once/periodic phase that learns the feature→price-move relationship.", "whyPool": "Training is the resource-intensive process of determining model weights from historical labels."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The \"Inference\" phase is the apply-many process where the pre-trained model is used to make real-time predictions on live data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: inference is the apply-many phase using the pre-trained model on live data.", "whyPool": "Inference refers to using the model in production to generate outputs from new inputs."}, {"L": "C", "text": "In this high-frequency setting, the computational cost (latency) of the Inference phase is generally more critical to the strategy's success than the duration of the Training phase.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: in HFT, tick-to-trade inference latency is more critical to success than training duration (training can be offline).", "whyPool": "In trading, especially HFT, the \"tick-to-trade\" latency occurs during inference; training can happen offline."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because the model takes hours to train, the firm must also expect the Inference phase for a single trade to take several hours to complete.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: no 1:1 link between training and inference time — models can train for hours yet infer in microseconds.", "whyPool": "There is no direct 1:1 correlation between training time and inference time; many models take weeks to train but microseconds to apply."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A model that is computationally expensive during training (e.g., complex ensemble methods) can still be viable for low- latency trading if its inference step is mathematically simple (e.g., basic arithmetic).", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: fit-once/apply-many means heavy training can coexist with cheap inference (simple arithmetic) → viable for low latency.", "whyPool": "This is the \"Fit-once, Apply-many\" paradigm; the heavy lifting is done upfront so the live execution is lightweight. 26 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Fit-once/apply-many, offline training — ISLR §2.1; HFT tick-to-trade latency concept.", "n": 18}, {"id": "ML-D1G3Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quant Architect at a global multi-strategy fund. Your desk is evaluating a \"Global Macro Sweep\" model that utilizes thousands of macroeconomic indicators to predict monthly bond yields. A junior researcher suggests a Lazy Learning approach (like K Nearest Neighbors) because it requires \"zero time for training. \" However, the Lead Risk Officer points out that the fund needs to generate predictions for 50,000 different global instruments simultaneously every morning within a 10-minute window. You must evaluate the scalability of this suggestion.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that in the \"Lazy Learning\" paradigm, the computational burden is shifted from the training phase to the inference phase.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (\"lazy learning shifts burden from training to inference\") is accurate, so calling it \"incorrect\" is false. KNN does ~no training work and computes distances at inference.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. Lazy learners (like KNN) do virtually no work during training and perform all calculations (like distance matching) at the moment of inference."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Because a \"Lazy Learner\" does not build an explicit model during training, its inference speed is independent of the number of historical observations in the training set.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "KNN inference speed is NOT independent of N — it must search all stored observations to find neighbors, so it scales with training-set size.", "whyPool": "In lazy learning, inference speed is directly tied to the size of the training data (N) because the model must search through all stored observations to find neighbors."}, {"L": "C", "text": "For a \"Parametric\" model (like Linear Regression), the inference cost is generally determined by the number of features, not the number of training observations used to find the coefficients.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "For a parametric model (linear regression), inference cost depends on the number of features (a dot product), not on how many rows fit the coefficients.", "whyPool": "Once coefficients are learned, a linear model's inference is a simple dot product regardless of whether it was trained on 1,000 or 1,000,000 rows."}, {"L": "D", "text": "In a high-throughput environment requiring 50,000 simultaneous predictions, an \"Eager Learner\" is typically more efficient at inference than a \"Lazy Learner. \"", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Eager learners build compact representations (equation/tree) that evaluate faster in batch than a search-based lazy learner → better for 50k simultaneous predictions.", "whyPool": "Eager learners build a compact mathematical representation (like a tree or an equation) that is significantly faster to evaluate in batch than a search-based lazy learner. ("}, {"L": "E", "text": "The \"Fit-once, Apply-many\" paradigm is highly effective for trading systems because it allows for a computationally expensive training process to occur offline without impacting live execution latency.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Fit-once/apply-many lets expensive training run offline without hurting live execution latency — ideal for trading.", "whyPool": "This is the standard operational model for quant desks: heavy lifting happens on historical data so the \"Live\" signal generation is instantaneous."}], "reference": "Lazy vs. eager, KNN cost O(N) — ISLR §2.2.3 (KNN), §3.5; scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" (query complexity, KD/Ball trees).", "n": 19}, {"id": "ML-D1G3Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Consultant hired to audit the infrastructure of a \"Statistical Arbitrage\" fund. The fund is migrating its flagship model from a local server to a cloud-based \"Prediction-as-a-Service\" architecture. The Chief Technology Officer (CTO) is concerned about the variable costs associated with cloud computing. She notes that the model is retrained weekly on five years of tick data, but it is queried for inference millions of times per day as it scans for micro- arbitrage opportunities across global exchanges. 27 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that the cost of \"Training\" in this scenario is a recurring fixed cost incurred once per week, whereas the cost of \"Inference\" scales linearly with the number of market opportunities identified.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (training = periodic fixed weekly cost; inference cost scales with opportunities) is accurate, so calling it \"incorrect\" is false.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. Training happens on a schedule (periodic), while inference cost is driven by the frequency of live data processing and decision-making."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If the fund switches to an algorithm with a higher \"Hypothesis Space\" complexity, both the training time and the inference time will always increase by the same percentage.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Higher hypothesis-space complexity does not scale training and inference by the same percentage — e.g., a deep tree trains slowly but infers fast vs. KNN.", "whyPool": "There is no universal 1:1 scaling; for example, a Deep Decision Tree takes longer to train but can still be extremely fast at inference compared to a simple KNN model."}, {"L": "C", "text": "In a \"Production\" environment, the memory footprint required to store a trained Parametric model (e.g., a set of weights) is typically much smaller than the memory footprint required to store the data for a Lazy Learner (e.g., the entire training database).", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A parametric model stores only weights (small footprint); a lazy learner must retain the whole training set for similarity search (large footprint).", "whyPool": "Parametric models discard the training data once weights are learned, whereas lazy learners must keep the data accessible to perform similarity searches during inference."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Training latency is a \"Forward-Looking\" constraint that impacts how quickly a model can adapt to new market regimes after they occur.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Long training latency is forward-looking: the model can't absorb \"today's\" regime shift until the training cycle completes.", "whyPool": "If training takes days to complete, the model cannot incorporate \"today's\" market shift into its logic until the training cycle finishes."}, {"L": "E", "text": "For a trading firm, \"Training\" is an offline activity used to minimize empirical risk, while \"Inference\" is an online activity where the model is exposed to actual market risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Training is offline empirical-risk minimization on the past; inference is the online moment parameters face live market risk.", "whyPool": "Training optimizes the model's parameters based on the past; inference is the moment those parameters are tested against live, unseen market conditions. G4: Hypothesis Space & Model Families"}], "reference": "Parametric vs. non-parametric memory footprint; empirical risk minimization — ISLR §2.1, §2.2.3; Vapnik, empirical vs. structural risk.", "n": 20}, {"id": "ML-D1G4Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quant team must model a relationship between valuation indicators and future returns. The research director prefers a linear model for transparency, but analysts suspect that different indicator thresholds and interactions matter. The team is deciding on a model family before fitting any candidates.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Choosing a linear model restricts the forms of relationships the learning process can represent.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Choosing linear restricts the representable functions to linear combinations — the family constrains the candidate functions.", "whyPool": "The selected family constrains the candidate functions available to the learner."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A decision tree and a linear model search exactly the same hypothesis space because both use the same training data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees and linear models search *different* hypothesis spaces (different functional structure); sharing training data doesn't equate them.", "whyPool": "The two families represent predictions through different functional structures."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A more expressive model family may represent relationships that a constrained linear family cannot.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A more expressive family can represent relationships (nonlinearities, interactions) a linear family cannot.", "whyPool": "Expressive families can capture a broader range of patterns."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Greater expressiveness guarantees better performance on unseen market data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "More expressiveness does NOT guarantee better out-of-sample performance — extra flexibility can fit noise (overfit).", "whyPool": "Added flexibility can fit noise and does not guarantee generalization."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Interpretability requirements can be a legitimate reason to prefer a more constrained hypothesis space. 28 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Interpretability/governance is a legitimate reason to prefer a more constrained family even at some accuracy cost.", "whyPool": "Model selection may reflect governance and communication needs as well as predictive fit."}], "reference": "Hypothesis space, expressiveness vs. interpretability — ISLR §2.1.3 (flexibility-interpretability tradeoff), Fig. 2.7.", "n": 21}, {"id": "ML-D1G4Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A risk committee is comparing a linear regression model, a decision tree, and KNN for estimating portfolio stress losses. An analyst claims that model-family choice is merely a software preference because sufficiently careful training will make all three produce the same function. The committee needs a conceptual response before approving experimentation. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Each model family imposes different assumptions or structure on possible predictions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: each family imposes different assumptions/structure on possible predictions (its inductive bias).", "whyPool": "Model families differ in the candidate mappings they can express and favor."}, {"L": "B", "text": "KNN can base a prediction on nearby stored observations rather than fitting one global linear relationship.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: KNN is instance-based — it predicts from nearby stored points via local similarity, not one global line.", "whyPool": "KNN is instance-based and uses local similarity at inference."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A decision tree can represent threshold-based behavior without requiring the final prediction function to be globally linear.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a tree represents threshold/piecewise behavior without a globally linear function.", "whyPool": "Trees partition the feature space through successive splits."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Careful training guarantees that all three families converge to the same fitted predictor.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: careful training does NOT force all three to the same predictor — distinct hypothesis spaces and fitting procedures yield different functions.", "whyPool": "Distinct hypothesis spaces and fitting procedures can yield different predictors."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Comparing model families can reveal whether the problem benefits from a different form of inductive constraint.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: comparing families reveals which structural/inductive constraint best suits the task.", "whyPool": "Model-family comparison tests which structural assumptions suit the task. 29 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Model families & inductive bias — ISLR §2.1.3, ch.3 (linear), ch.8 (trees), §2.2.3 (KNN).", "n": 22}, {"id": "ML-D1G4Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A small hedge fund has only two years of weekly observations but hundreds of candidate features. The PM proposes using the most flexible available model because it can reproduce nearly any pattern in the sample; the risk manager favors stronger constraints until more evidence is available. The team cares about reliable forecasts, not merely historical fit.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A highly expressive hypothesis space can include models that fit accidental patterns in a small dataset.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A highly expressive space can fit accidental/spurious patterns in a small sample (overfitting).", "whyPool": "Flexibility allows both genuine and spurious sample patterns to be represented."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Constraining the model family can introduce bias while reducing the opportunity to fit noise.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Constraining the family adds bias but reduces the chance of fitting noise — a bias-variance tradeoff favoring stability.", "whyPool": "Constraints trade some representational freedom for potentially better stability."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The model with the broadest hypothesis space must have the lowest error on future observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The broadest hypothesis space does not guarantee the lowest future error; it can increase overfitting.", "whyPool": "A broader space can increase overfitting and does not guarantee lower future error."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Excellent fit within the available sample is sufficient evidence that the chosen hypothesis will generalize.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Excellent in-sample fit is not evidence of generalization — it doesn't test unseen data.", "whyPool": "In-sample fit alone does not test performance on unseen observations."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Dataset size and the strength of the available signal should influence how much flexibility the team accepts.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Appropriate capacity depends on dataset size and signal strength (little informative data ⇒ favor constraints).", "whyPool": "Appropriate capacity depends partly on how much informative data supports estimation."}], "reference": "Bias-variance tradeoff, capacity vs. sample size — ISLR §2.2.2; curse of dimensionality (§6, KNN discussion).", "n": 23}, {"id": "ML-D1G4Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Senior Model Validator at a quantitative hedge fund. You are reviewing a new \"Earnings Surprise\" model that uses a high-degree polynomial function to fit 10 years of historical stock data. The researcher proudly shows that the model has a Mean Squared Error (MSE) of near zero on the training data. However, when you run the model on last quarter's \"out-of-sample\" data, the error is significantly higher than a simple linear baseline.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The high-degree polynomial model is likely suffering from High Variance, meaning it has overfit the noise in the training set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Near-zero training error + high test error indicates high variance — it overfit noise and fails to generalize.", "whyPool": "Models that fit the training data too closely often fail to generalize to new data because they capture random fluctuations (noise)."}, {"L": "B", "text": "In the Bias-Variance tradeoff, \"Bias\" refers to the error introduced by approximating a complex real-life problem with a much simpler model (like using a straight line to fit a curve).", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Bias is the error from approximating a complex reality with a too-simple model (e.g., a line for a curve); high bias → underfitting.", "whyPool": "Bias is the \"simplification error\"; high bias usually leads to underfitting."}, {"L": "C", "text": "As you increase the complexity of a model, the Training MSE typically decreases, but the Test MSE eventually starts to increase in a \"U-shape\" curve.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "As complexity rises, training MSE falls monotonically but test MSE follows a U-shape (falls, then rises).", "whyPool": "This is the fundamental visualization of the tradeoff: training error always goes down with complexity, but test error bottoms out and then rises."}, {"L": "D", "text": "To reduce the variance of this polynomial model and improve its generalization, the researcher should increase the flexibility of the model even further.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "To reduce variance you *decrease* flexibility (fewer parameters/simpler form), not increase it.", "whyPool": "To reduce variance, one must typically decrease model flexibility (e.g., using fewer parameters or a simpler model form)."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A \"perfect\" model in statistical learning is one that achieves zero variance and zero bias simultaneously, regardless of the noise level (ε) in the data. 30 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Even with zero bias and variance, irreducible error ε remains (unmeasured factors), so \"zero-zero regardless of noise\" is impossible.", "whyPool": "Even with perfect bias and variance, the Irreducible Error (ε) remains because the response variable is always a function of unmeasured factors."}], "reference": "Bias-variance decomposition & U-shaped test error — ISLR §2.2.2 (Eqs. 2.7, 2.9), Figs. 2.9–2.12.", "n": 24}, {"id": "ML-D1G4Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Risk Manager at a hedge fund reviewing a portfolio of 100 different trading models. You observe that a specific group of models, based on high-order decision trees, consistently has a Mean Squared Error (MSE) of 0.02 on historical data but spikes to 0.85 MSE on live data. Conversely, a group of simple linear models has a consistent MSE of 0.40 on both historical and live data. You are asked to characterize the error profiles of these two groups.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that the high-order decision trees exhibit high variance because their predictions change significantly when the training data is slightly altered.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (trees have high variance because predictions swing with slightly altered training data) is accurate, so calling it \"incorrect\" is false.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. High variance refers to the high sensitivity of a model to the specificities of the training dataset."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The linear models exhibit high bias because their simplistic functional form cannot capture the underlying non-linear complexities of the market.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The linear models show high bias — a too-simple form can't capture nonlinear market structure (consistent, moderate error).", "whyPool": "Bias is the error that results from approximating a complex real-world relationship with a much simpler mathematical model."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The high test error of the decision trees is a classic symptom of overfitting, where the model has \"memorized\" the noise in the training set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A large train-vs-test gap (0.02 → 0.85) is the classic overfitting/memorized-noise symptom.", "whyPool": "A large gap between training and test error is the defining characteristic of overfitting. 31 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "To improve the performance of the linear models, you should primarily focus on collecting more historical data of the same type rather than adding more complex features.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "For a high-bias (underfitting) model, more data of the same type won't help — you need more capacity or better features.", "whyPool": "If a model has high bias (underfitting), adding more data of the same type usually doesn't help; you need to increase the model's complexity or provide better features."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The \"Total Error\" of any of these models can be decomposed into the sum of its squared bias, its variance, and the variance of the irreducible error term.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Total expected error = bias2 + variance + variance of the irreducible error term (bias-variance decomposition).", "whyPool": "Under the bias–variance decomposition, a model’s expected prediction error equals its squared bias plus its variance plus the variance of the irreducible error."}], "reference": "Bias-variance decomposition, overfitting gap — ISLR §2.2.2; ESL §7.3.", "n": 25}, {"id": "ML-D1G4Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Research Lead evaluating a \"Cross-Sectional Momentum\" strategy. The model uses a highly flexible non-parametric method to estimate the relationship between 200 different firm-specific factors and next-month returns. During the backtest, you notice that while the Training MSE is extremely low, the Test MSE is significantly higher. A junior analyst proposes that the \"Irreducible Error\" (ε) must be extremely large in financial markets, and therefore the fund should accept the high Test MSE as an unavoidable market reality.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that the \"Irreducible Error\" (ε) is a constant representing the variance of the noise in the true relationship Y=f(X)+ε, and its value is independent of the model choice or the training set size.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (ε is the noise variance in Y = f(X)+ε, independent of model choice/sample size) is accurate, so calling it \"incorrect\" is false.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. ε represents unmeasured variables and inherent randomness; it cannot be reduced by statistical learning."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The gap between the low Training MSE and the high Test MSE is a direct measure of the model's Bias, indicating that the model is too simple to capture the market's true signal.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A large train-vs-test gap is the hallmark of variance (overfitting), not bias — it does not mean the model is too simple.", "whyPool": "A large gap between training and test error is the hallmark of Variance (overfitting), not Bias (underfitting)."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the analyst is correct that ε is large, the \"Test MSE\" curve will have a higher minimum \"floor, \" but the U-shape of the curve relative to model flexibility will still exist.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If ε is truly large, the test-MSE curve has a higher floor, but the bias-variance U-shape vs. flexibility still holds.", "whyPool": "Irreducible error sets the theoretical limit for accuracy, but the tradeoff between bias and variance still dictates the shape of the error curve."}, {"L": "D", "text": "To mathematically determine if the model is overfitting, one must evaluate if the increase in Variance caused by the model's flexibility exceeds the reduction in Squared Bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Overfitting occurs when added flexibility's variance increase outweighs the squared-bias reduction, raising expected test error.", "whyPool": "As model flexibility increases, overfitting occurs when the increase in variance outweighs the reduction in squared bias, causing the expected test error to rise."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Adding more irrelevant features to the model would likely decrease the Training MSE further while simultaneously increasing the Test MSE due to added variance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Adding irrelevant features lets the model fit more training noise (lower train MSE) while raising test MSE via added variance.", "whyPool": "More features give the model more \"freedom\" to fit noise in the training set (lower training MSE), but this rarely generalizes to new data (higher test MSE). 32 Rev:081020261230 G5: Generalization vs. Memorization"}], "reference": "Irreducible error floor, variance from irrelevant features — ISLR §2.2.2, §6.1 (feature selection); ESL §7.3.", "n": 26}, {"id": "ML-D1G5Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A research model reproduces every return in its training history almost exactly but performs poorly when evaluated on the following year. The PM says the perfect historical fit proves that the model learned the market, while the research lead suspects memorization. Capital will not be allocated unless the team can explain the difference.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Near-perfect training performance combined with poor performance on later data is evidence against reliable generalization.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Near-perfect training + poor later performance is evidence *against* reliable generalization.", "whyPool": "The observed gap indicates that historical fit did not transfer to new data."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Memorizing idiosyncrasies in the training set can reduce performance on unseen observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Memorizing training idiosyncrasies (noise) hurts unseen performance because that noise won't recur.", "whyPool": "Sample-specific noise is unlikely to repeat consistently."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Perfect training fit guarantees that the underlying market relationship has been identified.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Perfect training fit does not prove the true relationship was found — the model may have captured noise/coincidences.", "whyPool": "The model may have captured noise or historical coincidences."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Evaluation on unseen, temporally later data is relevant to the desk’s intended use.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Evaluating on unseen, temporally later data matches the desk's intended use (predicting after the training period).", "whyPool": "The intended task is prediction after the training period."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A model that generalizes well must reproduce every training observation exactly.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A well-generalizing model need not reproduce every training point exactly; some training error can accompany better recovery of persistent patterns.", "whyPool": "Some training error can accompany better recovery of persistent patterns."}], "reference": "Overfitting vs. generalization, out-of-time validation — ISLR §2.2.2; concept \"walk-forward / out-of-sample testing.\"", "n": 27}, {"id": "ML-D1G5Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A vendor demonstrates a trading model using the same five-year dataset on which it was developed and tuned. The investment committee is impressed by the backtest, but the CIO asks whether the model has shown that it can handle observations it did not encounter during development. The vendor argues that more runs on the same sample will answer that question. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Repeatedly reporting performance on the development sample does not establish performance on genuinely unseen data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: repeatedly reporting on the development sample does not establish performance on genuinely unseen data.", "whyPool": "Reusing the same evidence cannot provide a fresh test of transfer."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Generalization concerns whether learned relationships remain useful beyond the observations used to fit the model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: generalization is about learned relationships holding beyond the fitting examples.", "whyPool": "Generalization is performance beyond the fitting examples."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A complex model’s ability to match more details in the five-year history is necessarily evidence of better generalization. 33 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: matching more detail in the 5-year history can be fitting noise, not better generalization.", "whyPool": "Matching additional historical detail may amount to fitting noise."}, {"L": "D", "text": "An untouched evaluation period would provide more relevant evidence than another report on the development sample.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: an untouched evaluation period gives more relevant evidence than re-reporting on the dev sample.", "whyPool": "An untouched period better approximates the intended use on new data."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because financial markets change over time, strong historical generalization evidence guarantees identical live performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: markets change, so strong historical generalization does NOT guarantee identical live performance.", "whyPool": "Distribution change and new events can still alter live results."}], "reference": "Data leakage / train-test hygiene, non-stationarity — ISLR §5.1 (validation); \"look-ahead bias\" concept.", "n": 28}, {"id": "ML-D1G5Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A credit-focused fund trains a model using bond observations from a calm market period and then plans to deploy it during a liquidity crisis. The PM assumes that withholding a random subset of the calm-period bonds is enough to prove the model will work in the crisis. The risk officer questions whether the evaluation represents the planned environment.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Good performance on unseen calm-period observations is evidence of some generalization within that environment.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Good performance on held-out calm-period bonds is evidence of *some* generalization within that environment.", "whyPool": "The model has transferred to held-out examples from a similar setting."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Such performance alone proves that the model will generalize to materially different crisis conditions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "It does not prove generalization to materially different crisis conditions — the distribution differs.", "whyPool": "Crisis conditions may differ substantially from the training distribution."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Generalization is easier to assess when evaluation data reflect the conditions in which predictions will be used.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Generalization is easier to assess when evaluation data reflect the deployment conditions.", "whyPool": "Representative evaluation better supports claims about intended deployment."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Memorizing unique identifiers for bonds could appear useful in the training sample without learning transferable risk patterns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Memorizing bond identifiers could look useful in-sample without learning transferable risk patterns.", "whyPool": "Identifier-based matching can capture sample membership rather than durable relationships."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the test observations were not used in fitting, their economic context is irrelevant to interpreting the result.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A clean train/test split doesn't remove concern about mismatch between evaluation and deployment (economic context still matters).", "whyPool": "A clean split does not remove concerns about mismatch between evaluation and deployment conditions. 34 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Distribution shift / covariate shift, representative validation — ISLR §2.2; concept \"regime-aware / stress backtesting.\"", "n": 29}, {"id": "ML-D1G5Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Senior Model Validator at a quantitative fund auditing a \"Statistical Arbitrage\" system. The strategy uses a non- parametric learner to forecast mean-reversion in high-frequency pairs. During your audit, you observe that the model’s Empirical Risk (loss on the training set) is near zero, while its Structural Risk (expected loss on new data) is significantly higher. The lead researcher argues that because the model was trained on millions of ticks, any pattern it found must be a \"General\" market truth rather than \"Memorized\" noise.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that the researcher’s argument is flawed because \"Memorization\" can occur even with large datasets if the model's capacity (VC-dimension) is sufficiently high to accommodate the noise.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (memorization can occur even on big data if capacity/VC-dimension is high enough to fit noise) is accurate, so calling it \"incorrect\" is false.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. Model capacity relative to data volume determines the risk of memorization; massive data does not provide immunity if the model is too complex."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Generalization gap is defined as the difference between the training error and the test error; a narrowing gap as N→∞ is a necessary condition for a model to be considered \"Generalizing. \"", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Generalization gap = training vs. test error; as N→∞ a well-specified model's train and test performance should converge (a necessary condition).", "whyPool": "As the number of training observations increases, a well-specified model's training performance and test performance should converge."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A model that has effectively \"Memorized\" the training set has reached a state where it can no longer distinguish between the underlying signal f(X) and the stochastic realization of the error term ε.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A model that memorized can no longer separate signal f(X) from the stochastic realization of ε.", "whyPool": "Memorization is the failure to separate the structural relationship from the transient noise of a specific sample."}, {"L": "D", "text": "To mathematically enforce generalization over memorization, the team could introduce a regularization term that penalizes the complexity of the hypothesis space, even if it increases the training error.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Adding a complexity-penalizing regularization term enforces generalization over memorization, even at higher training error.", "whyPool": "This is the core of \"Regularization\": trading off fit (Empirical Risk) for a more restricted, stable model (Generalization)."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the market undergoes a \"Regime Shift\" (a change in the underlying data-generating process), a model that previously exhibited high generalization will inherently maintain its predictive accuracy.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Generalization is defined for a stationary distribution; a regime shift can break even a perfectly generalizing model.", "whyPool": "Generalization is defined within the context of a stationary distribution; if the underlying distribution changes (Regime Shift), even a perfectly generalizing model may fail."}], "reference": "VC dimension, empirical vs. structural risk minimization, regularization — Vapnik (SLT); ISLR §6.2 (shrinkage); ESL §7.9.", "n": 30}, {"id": "ML-D1G5Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Researcher at a crypto-asset fund. You have developed a \"Volatility-Clustering\" model that utilizes a high-capacity non-parametric learner. The model achieves near-perfect accuracy on a specific 12-month training period of Bitcoin price action. However, when the model is applied to the subsequent quarter, it fails to capture even basic trend reversals that a simple moving average identifies correctly. You are investigating whether the model has successfully extracted the signal or simply performed an \"identity mapping\" of the training data. 35 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that the model’s failure to perform on the subsequent quarter despite near-perfect training accuracy indicates that it has memorized the stochastic noise of the training sample rather than generalizing the underlying volatility dynamics.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (near-perfect train + failure on the next quarter = memorizing noise, not generalizing) is accurate, so calling it \"incorrect\" is false.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. Near-perfect training fit followed by a complete failure on new data is the hallmark of memorization over generalization."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Generalization is achieved when a model’s complexity is perfectly matched to the entropy of the signal, allowing it to ignore the irreducible error (ε) present in the training set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A generalizing model captures structural f(X) while staying invariant to the specific ε realization in the training sample.", "whyPool": "A generalizing model captures the structural f(X) while remaining invariant to the specific realization of ε in the training sample."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the training dataset size were significantly increased, the model’s propensity for memorization would likely increase because it would have more noise to fit.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Increasing training-data volume typically forces focus on persistent signal and makes memorizing unique noise harder, not easier.", "whyPool": "Increasing the training data volume typically forces a model to focus on persistent signals and makes it harder to \"memorize\" unique noise patterns."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Memorization in high-capacity models is often a result of the model’s hypothesis space being too large relative to the amount of training data, allowing it to \"draw a line\" through every individual data point.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Memorization in high-capacity models comes from too-large a hypothesis space relative to data, letting it fit every point (identity map).", "whyPool": "Excessive flexibility allows the model to accommodate every training observation, effectively storing them as an \"identity map\" rather than a rule."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A model that generalizes well will exhibit consistent performance across different \"out-of-sample\" periods, provided the underlying data-generating process remains stationary.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A generalizing model performs consistently across out-of-sample periods *provided the data-generating process is stationary*.", "whyPool": "Generalization implies the model has captured the \"laws\" of the data which should hold true for any sample from that same distribution."}], "reference": "Capacity vs. data volume, stationarity — ISLR §2.2.2; ESL §7 (model assessment).", "n": 31}, {"id": "ML-D1G5Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Consultant performing a \"post-mortem\" on a failed trading strategy. The model, a complex Kernel- based learner, was designed to exploit high-frequency arbitrage opportunities. In a simulated backtest using 5 years of historical data, the model achieved a near-perfect equity curve. However, when deployed in a live environment, the model's performance was no better than a random coin flip. You discover that the model had effectively created a high- dimensional \"lookup table\" of the specific historical price paths.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that the model’s failure in production is a consequence of \"Memorization, \" where the model has encoded specific historical noise as deterministic signals.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (the live failure is memorization — encoding historical noise as deterministic signal) is accurate, so calling it \"incorrect\" is false.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. Memorization is the process of storing specific training instances rather than learning a predictive rule."}, {"L": "B", "text": "To transition from memorization to \"Generalization, \" the model must be constrained so that it only captures patterns that are invariant across different historical samples.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "To move from memorization to generalization, constrain the model to capture only patterns invariant across historical samples.", "whyPool": "Generalization requires the model to ignore the \"accidents\" of the training set in favor of universal features."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the historical data contained a high degree of \"Irreducible Error\" (ε), the model's high-capacity architecture likely treated this noise as an \"Identity Mapping\" to be replicated. 36 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With high irreducible error ε, a high-capacity model may map noise as an identity mapping to be replicated → perfect train fit, no live edge.", "whyPool": "In a high-capacity model, noise is often erroneously mapped as a signal, leading to a perfect training fit that vanishes in live trading."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Increasing the number of training iterations (epochs) would have likely forced the model to eventually stop memorizing and start generalizing.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "More training iterations/epochs on the same data typically *reinforce* memorization and worsen generalization, not fix it.", "whyPool": "Overtraining on the same dataset typically reinforces memorization and leads to worse generalization. ("}, {"L": "E", "text": "A model that generalizes effectively will exhibit a stable \"Generalization Gap, \" where the difference between training and test error remains small even as data volume increases.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A stable, small generalization gap across data volumes indicates the model learned the underlying data-generating process.", "whyPool": "Stability across datasets is the primary indicator that a model has learned the underlying data-generating process. G6: What Defines a “Good” Model"}], "reference": "Overfitting/memorization, early stopping, generalization gap — ESL §7; ISLR §2.2.2; regularization concept.", "n": 32}, {"id": "ML-D1G6Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two models forecast daily portfolio returns. Model A has slightly lower average prediction error but occasionally produces extreme mistakes during volatile markets; Model B has slightly higher average error but more stable behavior. The Chief Risk Officer prioritizes limiting severe surprises, while the research team initially ranks models using average error alone.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Model A’s lower average error is sufficient to declare it better for every stakeholder objective.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Lower average error is not sufficient for every objective — a single average can omit the tail outcomes the CRO values.", "whyPool": "A single average metric may omit the outcomes the risk officer values most."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The cost of extreme errors is relevant when choosing between the models.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The cost of extreme errors is relevant; tail mistakes can have disproportionate financial impact.", "whyPool": "Tail mistakes can have disproportionate financial consequences."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Model B may be preferable if its stability better aligns with the risk objective.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Model B may be preferable if its stability better aligns with the risk objective (a modest accuracy sacrifice can be worth it).", "whyPool": "A modest accuracy tradeoff may be justified by improved stability under the stated objective."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A good model should be evaluated against the decision it is intended to support.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A good model is judged against the decision it supports — quality is contextual.", "whyPool": "Model quality is contextual and should reflect intended use. 37 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Considering robustness means that predictive accuracy no longer matters at all.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Considering robustness is an *additional* criterion; it does not make accuracy irrelevant.", "whyPool": "Robustness is an additional criterion, not a reason to ignore accuracy."}], "reference": "Loss-function/metric choice, tail risk — ISLR §2.2.1; concept \"expected shortfall / cost-sensitive evaluation.\"", "n": 33}, {"id": "ML-D1G6Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A pension fund compares a transparent model with a marginally more accurate but highly unstable alternative. The investment committee requires explanations for major allocation changes and wants consistent recommendations when inputs change only slightly. A data scientist argues that the model with the best single test score is automatically the correct choice.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The explanation requirement can legitimately affect which model is considered good.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The explanation requirement legitimately affects which model counts as \"good\" (interpretability is a stated objective).", "whyPool": "Interpretability is part of the stated operational and governance objective."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Sensitivity to small, economically insignificant input changes is relevant to model stability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Sensitivity to small, economically insignificant input changes is a valid measure of (in)stability.", "whyPool": "Large output changes from tiny perturbations indicate instability."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A marginally higher score on one test sample guarantees superior future decisions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A marginally higher score on one test sample doesn't guarantee superior future decisions — a single estimate is uncertain.", "whyPool": "A single estimate is uncertain and does not guarantee future superiority."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The committee may rationally trade a small amount of measured accuracy for interpretability and stability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The committee may rationally trade a little measured accuracy for interpretability and stability.", "whyPool": "The preferred tradeoff depends on the fund’s requirements."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Model quality can be determined without considering how predictions enter the allocation process.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Model quality can't be determined without considering how predictions enter the allocation process (use context matters).", "whyPool": "The consequences and use of predictions help define relevant performance."}], "reference": "Interpretability & stability as objectives — ISLR §2.1.3; concept \"model risk / governance.\"", "n": 34}, {"id": "ML-D1G6Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A long-only fund develops a model that predicts next-day returns accurately, but its small forecast changes cause frequent portfolio turnover and transaction costs that erase the apparent gain. The PM asks the team to decide whether the model is successful, while the modeling lead points only to its prediction score. The fund’s objective is improved net portfolio performance subject to a turnover limit. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Predictive accuracy alone may be insufficient to judge the model’s value to this strategy.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: predictive accuracy alone may be insufficient — the model is part of a decision system judged on net outcomes/constraints.", "whyPool": "The model is part of a decision system evaluated on net outcomes and constraints."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Transaction costs and turnover are relevant because they connect model outputs to the fund’s actual objective.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: turnover and transaction costs connect model outputs to the fund's true objective.", "whyPool": "These implementation effects can eliminate nominal predictive gains."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The model must be considered good because its forecast accuracy is high.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: high forecast accuracy does not establish alignment with the portfolio objective (net-of-costs performance).", "whyPool": "High forecast accuracy does not establish alignment with the stated portfolio objective."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A less accurate but more stable model could produce better net results after trading costs. 38 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a less accurate but more stable model can yield better net results after costs.", "whyPool": "Reduced turnover can outweigh a modest loss in predictive accuracy."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Changing the decision rule that converts forecasts into trades could affect practical performance without retraining the predictive model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: changing the forecast→trade decision rule can alter trades/costs without retraining the predictor.", "whyPool": "Deployment logic can change trades and costs while leaving the fitted predictor unchanged."}], "reference": "Net-of-cost / implementation shortfall, decision rules — Grinold & Kahn \"Active Portfolio Management\"; concept \"signal vs. execution.\"", "n": 35}, {"id": "ML-D1G6Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Risk Management Consultant reviewing a \"Tail-Risk Protection\" strategy for a pension fund. The strategy uses a complex model that achieved 99% accuracy in backtests by correctly predicting \"Normal\" market days. However, it failed to predict the only two \"Flash Crashes\" that occurred in the 10-year period, resulting in a 30% drawdown. The pension fund's board is debating whether this is a \"good\" model based on its high accuracy.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Accuracy is a sufficient metric for defining a \"good\" model in this scenario because the model correctly identified the vast majority of market sessions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Accuracy is NOT sufficient here — on imbalanced data it's a \"vanity metric\" that ignores utility in the rare, high-impact cases.", "whyPool": "In imbalanced financial datasets, accuracy is often a \"vanity metric\" that fails to capture the model's utility in high-impact scenarios."}, {"L": "B", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that A model is considered \"Robust\" if its performance remains effective and reliable even when subjected to market conditions or data inputs that differ from its training environment.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (a model is robust if it stays reliable under inputs differing from training) is accurate, so calling it \"incorrect\" is false; robustness = handling distributional shift/noise.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. Robustness is a measure of how the model handles \"distributional shift\" or noisy, unexpected data."}, {"L": "C", "text": "In this context, \"Alignment with problem objectives\" suggests that the model is poor because the cost of missing a rare \"Tail Event\" (False Negative) far outweighs the benefit of correctly identifying \"Normal\" days.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Alignment with objectives makes it a poor model — the cost of missing a rare tail event (false negative) far outweighs correctly calling normal days.", "whyPool": "A \"good\" model must be fit-for-purpose; if the objective is tail-risk protection, a model that misses the tail has failed its primary objective. 39 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "A \"Stable\" model is one that produces consistent outputs and maintains its predictive power over time, rather than exhibiting high sensitivity to small changes in input features.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A stable model gives consistent outputs and holds predictive power over time rather than reacting sharply to small input changes.", "whyPool": "Stability ensures the model doesn't require constant re-tuning or produce erratic signals due to market \"jitter. \""}, {"L": "E", "text": "The tradeoff between \"Accuracy\" and \"Robustness\" often means that a \"Good\" model might accept a lower overall accuracy on common data points to ensure better performance on critical, rare events.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The accuracy-vs-robustness tradeoff means a good model may accept lower overall accuracy to perform better on critical rare events.", "whyPool": "Model selection involves balancing competing criteria; sometimes \"the best\" model is the most reliable one, not the one with the lowest training error."}], "reference": "Class imbalance & metric choice (precision/recall, cost of false negatives), robustness — scikit-learn \"Metrics\" (imbalanced); ISLR §4 (confusion matrices).", "n": 36}, {"id": "ML-D1G6Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Senior Quantitative Analyst designing a high-frequency market-making algorithm. Your model needs to balance Latency (the speed of execution), Predictive Accuracy (how often the price move is correct), and Computational Stability (the consistency of performance during high-volatility bursts). Your CTO suggests that a more complex deep learning model could improve accuracy by 2%, but it would double the execution latency and increase the variance of the model's response time during market stress.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that in this scenario, \"alignment with problem objectives\" dictates that the 2% gain in accuracy is only valuable if it doesn't push the execution latency beyond the threshold where the arbitrage opportunity disappears.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (the 2% accuracy gain is only valuable if latency stays below the arbitrage-vanishing threshold) is accurate, so calling it \"incorrect\" is false.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. A model's utility is relative to its environment; in HFT, a \"slow but accurate\" model may be objectively \"bad\" if it misses the trade entirely."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Choosing a simpler, faster model over the 2% more accurate one is an example of prioritizing Stability and Robustness over pure Accuracy.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Choosing the simpler, faster model prioritizes stability/robustness over pure accuracy.", "whyPool": "Reliability and speed are often more critical than marginal accuracy gains in production-level financial engineering."}, {"L": "C", "text": "\"Tradeoffs between competing criteria\" implies that a \"Good\" model in high-frequency trading is defined primarily by its training MSE, regardless of its operational constraints.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A good HFT model must balance multiple criteria (efficiency, business constraints); training MSE alone doesn't define it.", "whyPool": "A \"good\" model must balance multiple criteria, including computational efficiency and business constraints; MSE is only one piece of the puzzle."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A model is considered \"Stable\" in this context if its latency remains predictable and within a tight range even when the volume of incoming market data spikes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stability here means predictable latency in a tight range even when incoming data volume spikes.", "whyPool": "Stability refers to consistent behavior (both in predictions and operational performance) across varying conditions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The decision to reject the more accurate model because it is \"brittle\" during high-volatility events is an evaluation of the model's Robustness.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Rejecting the more accurate but brittle model is an evaluation of robustness (integrity under stressful/outlying data).", "whyPool": "Robustness measures how well the model maintains its integrity and performance when faced with stressful or outlying data environments. 40 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Multi-criteria model selection, latency/robustness — concept \"production ML tradeoffs\"; ISLR §2.1.3 (flexibility-interpretability).", "n": 37}, {"id": "ML-D1G6Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Learning Paradigms & Problem Framing", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Research Lead overseeing the deployment of an automated market-making strategy. The model, which predicts short-term order flow imbalances, was trained on data from a period of high market volume. During a subsequent \"low-liquidity\" holiday session, the model's error rate spiked, and its internal risk parameters began to fluctuate wildly in response to minor price jitters. You are conducting a post-mortem to determine why the model failed to meet the criteria of a high-quality production system.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that the model's erratic behavior during the holiday session, despite its high accuracy during the training period, is a primary failure of robustness.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (erratic holiday behavior despite high training accuracy is a robustness failure) is accurate, so calling it \"incorrect\" is false; robustness = staying effective when the input distribution shifts (high→low liquidity).", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. Robustness is the measure of a model's ability to remain effective when the input data distribution shifts (e.g., from high to low liquidity)."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If the model’s predictions remained consistent and its internal parameters did not swing wildly despite the change in market volume, the model would be characterized as having high stability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If predictions/parameters stayed consistent despite the volume change, the model would be characterized as having high stability.", "whyPool": "Stability refers to the consistency of the model’s outputs and internal state when faced with perturbations or different data samples."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A model is considered a \"good\" model solely if it minimizes the Mean Squared Error (MSE) during the backtest, as operational behavior during live trading is an engineering concern rather than a modeling one.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A good model is not defined *solely* by backtest MSE — operational behavior in live trading is part of model quality, not just an engineering afterthought.", "whyPool": "In a professional context, a model's \"goodness\" is defined by a combination of statistical performance, operational reliability, and its fit for the specific environment."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The high sensitivity of the model's risk parameters to minor price jitters suggests that the model's hypothesis space was likely too flexible, leading to a lack of stability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "High parameter sensitivity to minor jitter suggests an overly flexible hypothesis space → instability (over-reaction to small variations).", "whyPool": "Excessive flexibility often causes models to \"over-react\" to small variations in data, which is a hallmark of an unstable model."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Choosing a model that maintains consistent execution times and risk thresholds over one that offers 1% higher accuracy but unpredictable performance is an example of prioritizing reliability in model selection.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Preferring consistent execution/risk thresholds over 1% higher but unpredictable accuracy is prioritizing reliability in model selection.", "whyPool": "Model selection involves weighing different performance dimensions to ensure the system meets the actual requirements of the trading desk. 41 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Robustness (distribution shift) vs. stability (perturbation sensitivity); flexibility-driven instability — ISLR §2.2.2; concept \"production model reliability.\"", "n": 38}, {"id": "ML-D2G1Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative equity team receives a fundamental dataset in which young firms often lack long operating histories and some distressed firms stop reporting selected fields. The PM proposes deleting every row containing a missing value because “the remaining data will be clean. ” The research lead asks how that choice could affect a model intended to score the full investable universe.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Deleting all incomplete rows can bias the sample if missingness is related to firm characteristics.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If missingness correlates with firm characteristics (age, distress), deleting incomplete rows changes the population the sample represents — i.e., it becomes MNAR-driven selection bias, not a random thinning.", "whyPool": "Systematic missingness can cause deletion to change the population represented by the sample."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Complete-case deletion is harmless whenever the resulting dataset remains large.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A large *retained* sample can still be systematically unrepresentative: dropping all young/distressed firms removes a whole segment regardless of how many rows remain.", "whyPool": "A large retained sample can still be systematically unrepresentative."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A flag indicating that a value was missing may contain predictive information.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The fact that a value is missing can itself be predictive — a missingness flag can encode age/distress/reporting-failure signal that a plain deletion throws away.", "whyPool": "The reporting failure itself may be associated with age, distress, or another relevant condition."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Any imputed number should be treated as though it were an observed measurement with no uncertainty.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Any imputed number is an estimate carrying uncertainty; treating it as an observed measurement understates variance and can distort feature–target relationships.", "whyPool": "Imputation is an estimate and can alter uncertainty and relationships in the data."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The team should compare the characteristics of retained and removed firms before adopting the deletion rule.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing retained vs. removed firms is exactly the diagnostic that reveals whether the deletion rule disproportionately excludes certain firm types before you adopt it.", "whyPool": "This comparison can reveal whether the rule disproportionately excludes certain types of firms. 43 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Missing-data mechanisms (MCAR/MAR/MNAR) and complete-case bias — Little & Rubin, *Statistical Analysis with Missing Data*; scikit-learn \"Imputation of missing values.\"", "n": 39}, {"id": "ML-D2G1Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A risk team is modeling bond spreads using several financial ratios. Missing leverage values are replaced with the median calculated from the entire ten-year dataset before the observations are divided into training and test periods. An analyst argues that median imputation is always safe because it does not use the target variable. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Median imputation can be more resistant to extreme observed values than mean imputation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Note the embedded double-negative: the statement asserts \"*The following claim is false: median imputation can be more resistant to extreme values than mean imputation.*\" The inner claim is actually true (the median *is* more robust to extremes), so calling it false is itself incorrect.", "whyPool": "The median is less affected by extreme values than the mean."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Computing the median from the entire dataset allows information from the test period to influence preprocessing.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: computing the median over the *entire* dataset lets test-period observations shape the preprocessing parameter, leaking held-out information into training.", "whyPool": "The preprocessing parameter reflects observations that are supposed to remain held out."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The absence of direct target values in the calculation guarantees that the evaluation remains uncontaminated.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: not using the target does not guarantee an uncontaminated evaluation — test-*feature* information (via the global median) still influences development, so the test is no longer independent.", "whyPool": "Test-feature information can still influence model development and make the evaluation less independent."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The imputation value should normally be estimated using the training data and then applied to the test data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: fitting the imputation value on training data and applying it to test preserves the intended separation and mimics live deployment.", "whyPool": "Fitting preprocessing only on training data preserves the intended separation."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Replacing all missing values with one median can reduce the apparent variability of that feature.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: replacing many missing values with one median creates an artificial concentration (spike) that reduces the feature's apparent variability.", "whyPool": "Repeated use of a central value creates an artificial concentration in the feature distribution."}], "reference": "Preprocessing leakage and fit-on-train discipline — scikit-learn \"Common pitfalls: data leakage\" and `Pipeline`/`SimpleImputer`; median vs. mean robustness (basic robust statistics).", "n": 40}, {"id": "ML-D2G1Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A trading desk combines prices from multiple vendors. A missing quote can mean either a temporary feed outage or that an illiquid security did not trade, and the head trader wants every gap forward-filled so the model always receives a complete matrix. The team must decide whether one rule is appropriate for both causes.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Forward-filling assumes that the last observed value is a reasonable substitute until a new observation arrives.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Forward-filling substitutes the last observed value until a new observation arrives — that is precisely the assumption it encodes.", "whyPool": "Forward-filling explicitly carries the previous observation into later missing positions."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A feed outage and a no-trade interval necessarily carry the same economic information.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A feed outage (a data-system failure) and a no-trade interval (a liquidity condition) carry different economic meaning; treating them identically conflates them.", "whyPool": "One is a data-system failure, while the other may reflect market liquidity."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Long forward-filled stretches can make stale prices appear current.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Long forward-filled stretches make a stale price look current, masking that no fresh price was actually observed.", "whyPool": "Repeated old values can conceal the fact that no current price was observed."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Recording the duration or reason for missingness may help distinguish stale data from genuine observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Recording the duration/reason of missingness preserves information a single filled value would erase, letting the model distinguish stale from genuine data.", "whyPool": "Missingness metadata can preserve information that a filled value alone would discard."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Once missing values are filled, the team no longer needs to examine how the missingness arose.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The missingness mechanism governs whether an imputation rule is defensible and how to interpret results, so it cannot be ignored once gaps are filled.", "whyPool": "The missingness mechanism affects whether an imputation rule is defensible and how the result should be interpreted."}], "reference": "Time-series imputation pitfalls (stale-quote/forward-fill) and missingness metadata — de Prado, *Advances in Financial Machine Learning* (data structures); general data-cleaning practice.", "n": 41}, {"id": "ML-D2G1Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Researcher analyzing a dataset of historical corporate bond prices to build a predictive model for credit spread movements. You discover that for several small-cap issuers, the \"Daily Trading Volume\" field is frequently missing. Upon further investigation, you realize these gaps occur primarily during periods of extreme market volatility when liquidity for these specific bonds evaporates entirely.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "In this context, the missing data is \"Missing at Random\" (MAR), as the missingness is related to the observed volatility in the broader market rather than the unobserved trading behavior of the small-cap bonds themselves.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "It is **not** MAR: the volume is missing *because* the (unobserved) volume was effectively zero/untradeable — the missingness depends on the value itself, which is MNAR.", "whyPool": "If the data is missing because the volume was too low to record or the market didn't trade (which is the variable itself), it is Missing Not at Random (MNAR)."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Replacing the missing volume data with the mean volume of the entire dataset (mean imputation) would likely lead to an underestimation of the model's risk, as it ignores the informative nature of the missingness.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Mean imputation of volume artificially reduces variance and erases the \"zero-liquidity\" signal, so the model underestimates risk.", "whyPool": "Mean imputation artificially reduces variance and fails to capture the \"signal\" that the missing data represents a state of zero liquidity."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Dropping all rows with missing volume data (complete case analysis) is the most robust strategy here, as it ensures the model is trained only on high-quality, verified data points without introducing imputation noise.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Complete-case deletion drops exactly the volatile stress periods, creating survivorship-style bias — the model never learns crisis behavior it most needs.", "whyPool": "Dropping rows (Listwise deletion) during volatile periods would lead to \"survivorship bias\" in the training set, as the model would never learn how the market behaves during the most critical stress events. (Ref: Handbook of AI Ch. 2.4)"}, {"L": "D", "text": "The fact that data is missing specifically during liquidity crunches suggests that the \"missingness\" itself is a highly informative feature that could serve as a proxy for market stress.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Missingness that coincides with liquidity crunches is itself an informative feature: a null in a liquidity field flags untradeability, valuable to a risk model.", "whyPool": "In trading, a \"null\" result in a liquidity field is often a signal that the asset is untradeable, which is vital information for a risk model."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the missingness is \"Missing Not at Random\" (MNAR), any imputation strategy that does not explicitly model the cause of the missing data will likely introduce significant systematic bias into the strategy’s backtest.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "When data is MNAR, any imputation that does not model the *cause* of missingness introduces systematic bias into the backtest.", "whyPool": "When the reason for missingness is tied to the values themselves, standard statistical fixes often distort the true relationship between features and targets. 45 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "MNAR / informative missingness and missing-indicator features — Little & Rubin; scikit-learn `MissingIndicator`.", "n": 42}, {"id": "ML-D2G1Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Strategist developing a model to trade \"Distressed Debt. \" Your primary feature is the \"Interest Coverage Ratio\" (Earnings / Interest Expense). In your dataset of 5,000 firms, you find that 15% of the values are missing. Further analysis reveals that 10% of these missing values belong to firms that have recently defaulted and ceased filing financial statements, while the other 5% are missing due to a technical error in the data provider's API. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The 5% of data missing due to the API error is \"Missing Completely at Random\" (MCAR), and dropping these specific rows would likely not introduce systematic bias into the model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: an API glitch is unrelated to firm performance, so those 5% are MCAR; dropping them only reduces sample size without skewing results.", "whyPool": "Since an API glitch is unrelated to the firms' actual performance, it is MCAR; deleting these rows reduces sample size but doesn't skew the results."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The 10% of data missing from defaulted firms is \"Missing Not at Random\" (MNAR), as the reason for the missingness is directly related to the poor financial health of the companies.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the 10% from defaulted firms is MNAR — missingness is directly tied to the firms' poor financial health (a selection effect of bankruptcy).", "whyPool": "The missingness is a \"Selection Effect\" caused by the bankruptcy process itself."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Dropping all missing values (Listwise Deletion) is the safest approach because it avoids the \"hallucination\" of financial ratios for companies that no longer exist.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: deleting the defaulted firms creates survivorship bias, so the model learns only \"successful distressed debt\" and massively overestimates expected returns.", "whyPool": "Deleting the defaulted firms creates \"Survivorship Bias. \" The model would only learn what successful distressed debt looks like, leading to a massive overestimation of the strategy's expected returns."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If you choose to impute the missing ratios for the defaulted firms using the \"Sector Average, \" the model will likely develop an optimistic bias, as it replaces \"failing\" metrics with \"average\" ones.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: imputing a sector average for a firm with zero earnings and high debt hides the failure signal, blinding the model to the very risk it should manage.", "whyPool": "Imputing the average for a company that has zero earnings and high debt hides the \"signal\" of failure, making the model blind to the primary risk it's supposed to manage."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A more rigorous \"Data Discipline\" approach would be to impute the API-error gaps while treating the defaulted-firm gaps as a distinct category or assigned \"floor\" value to reflect their distressed state.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: rigorous practice uses different strategies per missingness type — impute the API gaps, but treat defaulted-firm gaps as a distinct category or distressed \"floor\" value.", "whyPool": "Professional quant workflows often use different strategies for different \"types\" of missingness within the same column."}], "reference": "Distinguishing MCAR/MNAR within one column; survivorship bias — Little & Rubin; de Prado (backtest pitfalls). Note: source labels B's mechanism a \"Selection Effect\" rather than naming MNAR, but the accurate/inaccurate verdict is preserved.", "n": 43}, {"id": "ML-D2G1Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Researcher backtesting a strategy that utilizes \"Intraday Sentiment Scores\" derived from news headlines. You notice that the sentiment data is missing for several hours each day during the Asian trading session. You are considering two different strategies to handle this missingness: 1. Zero Imputation: Filling the gaps with a score of 0 (neutral). 2. Mean Imputation: Filling the gaps with the average sentiment score of the entire 5-year training dataset. 46 Rev:081020261230 For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Zero imputation in this context is likely to introduce a \"conservative bias, \" as it forces the model to assume a neutral market state during periods where sentiment might actually be trending.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: forcing a neutral 0 dampens the signal and makes the model less reactive to genuine overnight sentiment shifts — a conservative bias.", "whyPool": "Arbitrarily setting values to zero (neutral) dampens the signal and makes the model less reactive to potential overnight shifts. ("}, {"L": "B", "text": "Mean imputation is a superior strategy because it ensures the model remains \"centered\" on the long-term average sentiment, preventing erratic trades during the Asian session.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: mean imputation is *not* superior — it blunts the signal and ignores the temporal context, typically hurting live performance.", "whyPool": "Mean imputation blunts the signal and ignores the specific temporal context of the data, often leading to poor live performance."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Mean imputation introduces \"Look-Ahead Bias\" because the global average is calculated using data from the future relative to the missing observations in the early years of the backtest.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a mean computed from the full dataset uses information from the future relative to early-year gaps, i.e., look-ahead bias.", "whyPool": "Using a \"global mean\" from a full dataset is a classic data discipline violation, as that mean incorporates information that was not yet known."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the news provider only fails to report sentiment during periods of extreme market crashes (MNAR), both imputation strategies will systematically understate the strategy's historical risk (Drawdown).", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: filling crash-period gaps with neutral/average scores means the model never learns the association between missing data and extreme losses, understating drawdown risk.", "whyPool": "By filling \"crash\" periods with neutral or average scores, the model never \"learns\" the association between missing data and extreme losses."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Dropping the hours with missing data (Listwise Deletion) would be the most biased approach, as it would likely remove the very periods of \"overnight\" price discovery that are critical for morning gap strategies.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: deleting the missing hours destroys time-series continuity, producing a fragmented, unrealistic backtest.", "whyPool": "Deleting temporal gaps in financial time-series destroys the continuity of price action, leading to a fragmented and unrealistic backtest. G2: Noise and Outliers"}], "reference": "Look-ahead bias from global statistics; imputation and signal attenuation — de Prado (backtest overfitting); scikit-learn data-leakage guidance.", "n": 44}, {"id": "ML-D2G2Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A market-data pipeline records a stock price of $9,800 between observations near $98. The operations manager wants the row removed immediately, while the PM notes that genuine price jumps can also appear extreme. The research team must investigate before changing the training set.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Comparing the observation with source records and corporate-action data can help determine whether it is an error.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Cross-checking source records and corporate-action data (splits/dividends) is how you separate a bad record from a legitimate event.", "whyPool": "External context can distinguish a bad record from a legitimate event."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Every statistically extreme observation should be deleted before model training.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Not every statistically extreme point is an error; deleting all of them blindly discards valid rare events.", "whyPool": "Extremeness does not by itself imply that an observation is erroneous."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the value is a decimal-placement error, retaining it can distort learned relationships.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If it is a decimal-placement error, keeping it can distort learned relationships and statistics (especially under squared-error fitting).", "whyPool": "A large erroneous value can exert disproportionate influence on some learners and statistics."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the value reflects a genuine market event, removing it may eliminate behavior the live model must handle. 47 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If it reflects a real market event, removing it strips out behavior the deployed model must handle.", "whyPool": "Rare but valid events can be important to the deployment environment."}, {"L": "E", "text": "An observation’s distance from the sample mean alone establishes whether it is invalid.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Distance from the mean alone does not establish invalidity — validity needs contextual and data-quality evidence.", "whyPool": "Validity requires contextual and data-quality evidence, not only a statistical threshold."}], "reference": "Outlier validation vs. blind removal; error vs. valid extreme — ISLR §3.3.3 (leverage/outliers); data-cleaning best practice.", "n": 45}, {"id": "ML-D2G2Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A portfolio analytics group compares a linear regression model with a tree-based model for estimating transaction costs. A few trades have exceptionally large costs, and a junior analyst claims those observations must affect both learners in exactly the same way because both receive the same rows. The group wants to understand model sensitivity before deciding how to treat the trades. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Large residuals can have strong influence on a least-squares regression fit because their errors are squared.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: least-squares squares the residuals, so large residuals exert disproportionate influence on the fit.", "whyPool": "Squared-error fitting gives large residuals disproportionate weight."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A tree is completely immune to noisy or extreme observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a tree is *not* immune to outliers — they can affect split selection, leaf predictions, and stability.", "whyPool": "Outliers can affect split selection, leaf predictions, and tree stability."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Different model families can react differently to the same outliers.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: different model families have different structures/fitting criteria and therefore different outlier sensitivities.", "whyPool": "Their structures and fitting criteria produce different sensitivities."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Increasing a tree’s minimum leaf size may reduce its ability to isolate a single unusual observation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: increasing minimum leaf size prevents very small groups (single unusual points) from being modeled on their own, reducing isolation of an outlier.", "whyPool": "Larger leaves prevent very small groups from being modeled independently."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Removing the high-cost trades is automatically correct if doing so improves training error.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: lower *training* error from removing high-cost trades is not automatic justification — you must first determine if they are errors or valid, and evaluate out of sample.", "whyPool": "The team must determine whether the trades are errors or valid cases and evaluate performance beyond the training sample. 48 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Squared-error sensitivity vs. tree robustness; min-leaf regularization — ISLR §3 (OLS) and ch.8 (trees); ESL ch.9.", "n": 46}, {"id": "ML-D2G2Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A volatility model performs well on ordinary days but badly during a handful of market shocks. The PM calls those shock days “noise” and proposes winsorizing every extreme return, while the risk officer says those are precisely the days that matter most. The intended use is portfolio risk control during both normal and stressed markets.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Whether the shock observations should be retained depends partly on the model’s intended use.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Whether to retain shock observations depends partly on intended use — a risk-control model must handle exactly those conditions.", "whyPool": "Data treatment should reflect the conditions the model is expected to handle."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Labeling rare observations as noise is justified solely because they occur infrequently.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Rarity alone does not make an observation noise; rare events can be valid and economically important.", "whyPool": "Rare observations may be valid and economically important."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Winsorization changes extreme values and can understate the severity of genuine tail events.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Winsorization caps extreme values, so it can understate the true severity of genuine tail events.", "whyPool": "Capping replaces observed tail magnitudes with less extreme values."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The team can evaluate performance separately in ordinary and stressed regimes to expose the tradeoff.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Evaluating separately in ordinary vs. stressed regimes exposes the tradeoff that a single average metric would hide.", "whyPool": "Segment-level evaluation shows whether average performance conceals stress failures."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A cleaning procedure that improves average error necessarily improves the model’s usefulness for risk control.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Lower *average* error does not necessarily mean more usefulness for risk control — it may come at the cost of worse behavior in the most consequential periods.", "whyPool": "Average improvement may come at the cost of worse behavior during the most consequential periods."}], "reference": "Winsorization/tail treatment and segment-wise evaluation — de Prado (fat tails, backtest evaluation); robust-statistics tradeoffs.", "n": 47}, {"id": "ML-D2G2Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are training a linear regression model to predict the \"Price-to-Earnings (P/E) Ratio\" of technology stocks. Your dataset contains a few \"Penny Stocks\" where the earnings are nearly zero, resulting in extreme P/E ratios (e.g., over 5,000), while the sector average is 30. You observe that the resulting regression line is pulled sharply toward these extreme values, significantly increasing the error for the rest of the stocks in your universe.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The extreme P/E ratios are examples of \"Outliers\" that have high leverage, meaning they disproportionately influence the slope of the linear regression line.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Extreme P/E points far from the predictor mean are high-leverage outliers that disproportionately influence the OLS slope.", "whyPool": "In OLS regression, points far from the mean of the predictor variables exert high leverage and can dominate the parameter estimation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "In this case, the best strategy is to keep the outliers and use an L2 loss function (Mean Squared Error), as this will force the model to prioritize fitting these high-impact observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Keeping the outliers *and* using L2 (MSE) loss is the wrong fix — squaring errors increases the penalty for outliers, making the model even more sensitive to them.", "whyPool": "L2 loss squares the errors, actually increasing the penalty for outliers and making the model even more sensitive to them."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A more \"Robust\" learner, such as a Support Vector Regressor or a model using Huber Loss, would be less sensitive to these extreme P/E ratios than a standard Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Robust learners (Huber loss, SVR) are designed to limit outlier influence on the final parameters, so they are less sensitive than plain OLS.", "whyPool": "Robust statistical methods are specifically designed to limit the influence of outliers on the final model parameters."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the extreme P/E ratios are the result of accurate financial reporting (not data errors), they must be kept in the dataset to ensure the model captures the full reality of the technology sector.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Even accurate outliers that don't represent the broader population can harm generalization (high variance); \"accurate\" does not mandate keeping them.", "whyPool": "Even if data is \"accurate, \" keeping outliers that don't represent the broader population can lead to poor generalization and high variance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The decision to remove these outliers should be based on whether they represent the \"True Process\" you are trying to model or if they are \"Noise\" that obscures the generalizable relationship for typical stocks. 49 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The decision hinges on whether the outlier represents the \"true process\" you want to model or \"noise\" that obscures the typical relationship — a use-dependent judgment.", "whyPool": "Data discipline requires a subjective judgment on whether an outlier is a \"valid extreme\" or an \"irrelevant anomaly\" for the specific trading objective."}], "reference": "Leverage/influence in OLS, robust regression (Huber, SVR) — ISLR §3.3.3; ESL §6.9 / robust loss functions.", "n": 48}, {"id": "ML-D2G2Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Research Lead reviewing a proposed data-cleaning pipeline for a high-frequency trend-following strategy. The analyst suggests that because extreme price spikes in liquid markets like the S&P 500 E-mini futures are rare and often represent \"untradeable\" liquidity gaps, they should be treated as systematic errors and handled aggressively to keep the model's coefficients stable.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "In a linear model using Ordinary Least Squares (OLS), the squaring of residuals is an effective mechanism for naturally \"de-weighting\" outliers, ensuring they have less influence on the final parameters than the majority of the data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "OLS does not \"de-weight\" outliers by squaring — squaring *amplifies* them, making OLS the most outlier-sensitive of these choices.", "whyPool": "OLS is actually the most sensitive to outliers; squaring the residuals gives extreme values disproportionate leverage over the model. 50 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "If a price spike is a valid market event (e.g., a reaction to a surprise interest rate hike), removing it via Trimming is considered best practice because it helps the model generalize to the \"typical\" market regimes it will face 99% of the time.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trimming a *valid* market event creates survivorship bias and a model unprepared for real risk — effectively snooping for a cleaner history.", "whyPool": "Removing real market events via trimming creates \"Survivorship Bias\" and leads to a model that is unprepared for real-world risk, effectively \"snooping\" for a cleaner history."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A Support Vector Regressor (SVR) using an ε-insensitive loss function is highly sensitive to outliers because it attempts to fit a hyperplane that encompasses every single data point, regardless of its distance from the mean.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "SVR with ε-insensitive loss is actually *robust*: it ignores errors below ε and penalizes larger ones linearly (like Huber/MAE), not by squaring.", "whyPool": "SVR with ε-insensitive loss is actually robust; it ignores errors smaller than ε and uses a linear (not squared) penalty for larger errors, similar to a Huber loss or MAE."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A Decision Tree learner is highly sensitive to the exact magnitude of outliers because it uses the variance of the entire feature column to determine the optimal location for every split.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees are relatively robust to magnitude — once a value is past a split threshold, whether it is 100 or 1,000,000 doesn't change the tree structure; trees do not use the whole column's variance to place every split.", "whyPool": "Trees are robust to outliers; once a value is beyond a split threshold, its specific magnitude (whether it's 100 or 1,000,000) does not change the tree's structure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Outliers that represent genuine but extreme market realizations should generally be retained or managed through robust loss functions (like Huber Loss) rather than being deleted, as they contain critical information about the \"Fat Tails\" of the risk distribution.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Genuine extreme realizations should generally be retained or managed with robust loss (Huber), not deleted, because they carry critical fat-tail risk information.", "whyPool": "Data discipline in finance requires distinguishing between \"Bad Noise\" (errors) and \"Valid Extremes. \" Valid extremes should be capped or modeled robustly, not erased."}], "reference": "OLS vs. robust losses; ε-insensitive SVR; tree robustness to magnitude — ISLR §3, ch.8/9; ESL §12 (SVM/SVR).", "n": 49}, {"id": "ML-D2G2Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Consultant auditing a \"Risk Parity\" fund that uses a high-capacity Neural Network to estimate covariance matrices. The fund’s primary researcher argues that because the model is trained on decades of data, there is no need to manually address outliers or \"flash crashes, \" as the model will eventually learn to treat them as statistical anomalies.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "High-capacity learners like Deep Neural Networks are naturally immune to outliers because the large volume of training data provides enough \"normal\" observations to cancel out the effect of extreme noise.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "High-capacity models are *more* prone to fitting outliers — enough flexibility to memorize an anomaly as a complex rule rather than ignore it.", "whyPool": "High-capacity models are actually more prone to fitting outliers; they have the flexibility to \"memorize\" the anomaly as a complex rule rather than ignoring it."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If the dataset contains a \"Broken Ticker\" (e.g., a stock that erroneously reflects a price of $0.01 for one day), the neural network may attempt to \"explain\" this outlier by adjusting its weights, leading to a distorted feature space for all other stocks.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A broken ticker ($0.01) creates a leverage effect: the network distorts its internal representation to minimize the huge loss from the error, degrading the feature space for all stocks.", "whyPool": "This is the \"leverage\" effect; the model distorts its internal representation to minimize the massive loss associated with a clear data error."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Using Batch Normalization within the neural network architecture is a guaranteed way to eliminate the influence of outliers, as it scales all inputs to a mean of zero and unit variance regardless of their initial magnitude.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Batch Normalization does not eliminate outlier influence — a single extreme value skews the batch mean/variance, polluting normalization for the whole batch.", "whyPool": "Batch Normalization is sensitive to outliers; a single extreme value in a batch can skew the mean and variance for the entire batch, potentially \"polluting\" the training of normal samples."}, {"L": "D", "text": "In a professional trading environment, an outlier that is proven to be a \"Data Integrity Error\" should be removed or corrected, whereas an \"Extreme Market Realization\" should be managed using robust optimization or clipping.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct data discipline: remove/correct proven data-integrity errors, but manage genuine extreme realizations via robust optimization or clipping.", "whyPool": "This is the core of data discipline: distinguish between \"Bad Data\" (delete) and \"Hard Truths\" (keep/manage). 51 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "If you use a Mean Squared Error (MSE) loss to train the network, a single 20-sigma outlier will have less impact on the final model than 1,000 small, 0.1-sigma noisy observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Because MSE squares errors, one 20-sigma outlier contributes ~400 while 1,000 separate 0.1-sigma errors contribute ~10 combined — the single extreme has far greater impact.", "whyPool": "Because MSE squares each error, a single 20-sigma outlier contributes a loss of 400, while 1,000 separate 0.1- sigma errors contribute a combined loss of only 10. Therefore, the single extreme outlier has far greater impact on the model. G3: Sampling Bias and Representativeness"}], "reference": "Outlier fragility of high-capacity models; BatchNorm sensitivity; MSE quadratic weighting — ESL ch.11; Ioffe & Szegedy (Batch Normalization); loss-function robustness concept.", "n": 50}, {"id": "ML-D2G3Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher builds a stock-selection model using only companies that remain in the S&P 500 at the end of the study period. The PM wants to use the backtest as evidence that the strategy would have worked throughout the prior decade. A risk analyst questions what happened to firms that left the index.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Restricting the history to current constituents can create survivorship bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Restricting to firms that survived in the index to the end conditions the sample on survival — the textbook definition of survivorship bias.", "whyPool": "The sample is conditioned on surviving in the index until the end of the study."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Excluding firms that failed, merged, or were removed may make historical performance look better than it was.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Excluding firms that failed/merged/were removed drops poor outcomes, making historical performance look better than it truly was.", "whyPool": "Removed firms can include poor outcomes that a survivor-only sample omits."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A large number of current constituents eliminates the selection problem.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A large count of *current* constituents does not correct a systematic inclusion rule — size cannot fix a biased selection.", "whyPool": "Sample size does not correct a systematic inclusion rule."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A point-in-time constituent history would better represent the securities available at each past date.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A point-in-time constituent history reconstructs the securities actually investable at each past date, avoiding the bias.", "whyPool": "It reconstructs the investable membership known at each time."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Survivorship bias affects only descriptive statistics and cannot affect a trained model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Survivorship bias is not limited to descriptive statistics — biased training examples change learned relationships and evaluated performance.", "whyPool": "Biased training examples can change learned relationships and evaluated performance."}], "reference": "Survivorship bias and point-in-time data — de Prado (backtesting); Grinold & Kahn (data quality); standard quant-finance practice.", "n": 51}, {"id": "ML-D2G3Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A brokerage trains a client-churn model using responses from a voluntary satisfaction survey. Highly engaged clients and very dissatisfied clients respond most often, while the model will be applied to every client. The product director assumes that thousands of responses make the sample representative. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Voluntary response patterns can create selection bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double-negative: the statement says \"*The following claim is false: voluntary response patterns can create selection bias.*\" The inner claim is true (they *can* create bias), so denying it is incorrect.", "whyPool": "Participation is related to client characteristics rather than being random."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A large sample can still misrepresent the full client population. 52 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: more observations reduce random uncertainty but do not remove systematic sampling bias.", "whyPool": "More observations reduce random uncertainty but do not remove systematic sampling bias."}, {"L": "C", "text": "High predictive accuracy within the respondent sample proves equivalent accuracy for nonrespondents.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: high accuracy within respondents does not prove equal accuracy for nonrespondents — performance can change in underrepresented groups.", "whyPool": "Performance may change in groups underrepresented or absent from development data."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing respondent and nonrespondent characteristics can help diagnose representativeness.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: comparing respondent vs. nonrespondent characteristics can reveal systematic coverage differences.", "whyPool": "Such comparisons can reveal systematic differences in coverage."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Reweighting may help only if the team has useful information about how the sampled and target populations differ.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: reweighting only helps if you have variables that describe the relevant selection differences between sampled and target populations.", "whyPool": "Effective weighting requires variables that describe relevant selection differences."}], "reference": "Selection/self-selection (voluntary response) bias; reweighting/covariate shift — survey-sampling theory; scikit-learn \"sample weighting\" / dataset-shift concepts.", "n": 52}, {"id": "ML-D2G3Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A credit fund trains a default model entirely on large U.S. public companies from a period of low interest rates, then plans to score small private companies during a tightening cycle. The investment committee asks whether a random held-out subset with strong results is enough to approve deployment. The modeling team agrees that the split was technically clean.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A clean random split can estimate performance on observations drawn from a similar source population.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A clean random split estimates performance on rows drawn from the *same source population* — valid within that sampled setting.", "whyPool": "Holding out source-population rows tests transfer within that sampled setting."}, {"L": "B", "text": "It guarantees representative evaluation for small private companies in a different rate environment.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "It does not guarantee representative evaluation for small private firms in a different rate regime — the deployment population and environment differ.", "whyPool": "The deployment population and environment may differ materially."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Differences in company size, disclosure, and macroeconomic conditions can limit generalization.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Differences in size, disclosure, and macro conditions can shift both feature distributions and outcome relationships, limiting generalization.", "whyPool": "These differences can change both feature distributions and outcome relationships."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Evaluation data resembling the intended deployment population would provide more relevant evidence.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Evaluation data resembling the intended deployment population provides more relevant, decision-supporting evidence.", "whyPool": "Representative evaluation better supports the proposed real-world use."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Sampling bias is impossible if no test rows were used during training.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A clean train/test split and population representativeness are distinct concerns; no test-row use during training does not rule out sampling bias.", "whyPool": "Train/test separation and population representativeness are distinct concerns. 53 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Dataset shift / covariate shift vs. train-test hygiene — Quiñonero-Candela et al., *Dataset Shift in Machine Learning*; ISLR ch.5 (resampling limits).", "n": 53}, {"id": "ML-D2G3Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Strategist backtesting a \"High-Yield Dividend\" strategy. Your dataset is comprised of all US-listed stocks that paid a dividend in the 2010–2020 period. You discover that many of the highest-performing stocks in your training set were small-cap companies that were eventually acquired by larger firms at a significant premium. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Training only on companies that paid a dividend ensures the sample is representative of the broader market, as dividend policy is a reliable proxy for overall corporate health and stability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: filtering to dividend payers excludes growth and non-payers, so the sample is *not* representative of the total equity market.", "whyPool": "Selecting for dividends is a specific filter that excludes growth stocks and non-dividend payers, making the sample unrepresentative of the total equity market."}, {"L": "B", "text": "This approach suffers from Selection Bias because the sample is restricted by a characteristic (paying a dividend) that is likely correlated with the factors you are trying to predict, such as low volatility or value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: this is selection bias — the inclusion criterion (paying a dividend) skews the distribution of features and outcomes toward value/low-volatility.", "whyPool": "Selection bias occurs when the criteria for inclusion in the study (dividend payment) skews the distribution of the features and outcomes."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The inclusion of stocks that were eventually acquired introduces a form of Survivorship Bias if your database only includes the price history of companies that are still trading under their own ticker today.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: if acquired winners are dropped because they no longer trade under their ticker, the backtest ignores a primary source of the strategy's historical return (survivorship bias).", "whyPool": "If the \"acquired\" winners are missing from the data because they no longer have an active ticker, the backtest ignores a primary source of the strategy's potential historical return."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because the 2010–2020 period was characterized by declining interest rates, the model will likely generalize to a high- rate environment because dividend-paying stocks are inherently resistant to changes in the discount rate.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a model trained during falling rates will likely *fail* when rates rise — dividend \"bond proxies\" underperform, so it does not automatically generalize.", "whyPool": "Dividend stocks often act as \"bond proxies\"; a model trained during falling rates will likely fail to understand the risk of these stocks underperforming when rates rise. ("}, {"L": "E", "text": "A dataset consisting of 10 years of every dividend-paying stock in the US is sufficiently large to guarantee that any findings regarding \"alpha\" are a result of true market signals rather than sampling artifacts.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: volume and duration do not eliminate systematic bias; a sample restricted to one market slice/regime stays a prisoner of that slice.", "whyPool": "Volume and duration do not eliminate systematic bias. If the sample is restricted to a specific \"slice\" of the market, the model remains a prisoner of that slice's unique regime."}], "reference": "Selection vs. survivorship bias; regime dependence of dividend \"bond proxies\" — de Prado; Grinold & Kahn; standard factor-investing critique.", "n": 54}, {"id": "ML-D2G3Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are developing a machine learning model to predict the probability of corporate bankruptcy. Your training set is constructed by gathering the financial statements of 10,000 firms listed on the NYSE and NASDAQ as of December 2025, and you label them based on whether they filed for Chapter 11 at any point between 2015 and 2025. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate. 54 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This sampling method is an effective way to capture a representative history of the market because it includes a decade of data across two of the world's largest stock exchanges.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a snapshot of currently existing firms is a \"winner's history,\" not a representative decade — it omits the failures needed for a balanced classification task.", "whyPool": "A sample of \"currently existing\" firms is not a representative history; it is a \"winner's history\" that omits the failures required for a balanced classification task."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The dataset is susceptible to Survivorship Bias because the selection criteria (being listed as of 2025) naturally excludes the very firms that the model is trying to identify—those that went bankrupt and were delisted prior to 2025.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: this is classic survivorship bias — looking backward from the present, you only see firms that survived the interval.", "whyPool": "This is a classic example of survivorship bias. By looking backward from the present, you only see those that survived the interval, skewing the model’s understanding of risk."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because the firms in the sample are all from the NYSE and NASDAQ, the model's findings can be generalized to characterize the bankruptcy risks of small, private startups without further adjustments.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: NYSE/NASDAQ firms have fundamentally different risk profiles and data-generating processes than small private startups; results don't generalize without adjustment.", "whyPool": "This is a failure of representativeness. Publicly traded \"Blue Chip\" or mid-cap firms have fundamentally different risk profiles and data-generating processes than private startups."}, {"L": "D", "text": "This is a form of Selection Bias where the sampling mechanism is correlated with the target variable, resulting in a dataset that is systematically missing the most critical \"negative\" outcomes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: it is selection bias where the survival filter is correlated with the target, so the training set is systematically missing the critical \"bankrupt\" outcomes.", "whyPool": "The selection filter (survival to 2025) is directly tied to the outcome (not being bankrupt), ensuring the training set lacks the necessary diversity of the target class."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Increasing the number of features in this specific dataset (e.g., adding more technical indicators) will fix the underlying sampling error and allow the model to generalize accurately to the total population of firms.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: adding more features cannot fix a biased row set — if failed firms are absent, extra columns only help the model overfit the survivor bias.", "whyPool": "You cannot fix a biased sample by adding more features. If the \"failed\" firms are missing from the rows, adding more columns only helps the model overfit the \"survivor\" bias."}], "reference": "Survivorship + selection bias; \"features can't fix biased rows\" — de Prado; general sampling-bias theory.", "n": 55}, {"id": "ML-D2G3Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are building a model to predict the probability of success for clinical trials in the pharmaceutical industry. Your training dataset consists of every trial that was successfully completed and published in a major medical journal between 2010 and 2025. You plan to use this model to help a venture capital firm decide which early-stage drug candidates to fund.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The dataset is likely a representative sample of all clinical trials because major medical journals are the primary repository for high-quality scientific evidence and trial outcomes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Published journals are a heavy filter; the universe of *all* trials is far larger and more diverse than published successes, so the sample is not representative.", "whyPool": "Relying on published journals introduces a heavy filter; the \"universe\" of all trials is much larger and more diverse than the \"universe\" of published successes."}, {"L": "B", "text": "This approach suffers from Selection Bias (specifically \"Publication Bias\") because trials that failed to meet their primary endpoints or showed negative results are significantly less likely to be published, creating an overly optimistic training set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "This is selection bias — specifically publication bias: failed/negative trials are less likely to be published, producing an overly optimistic training set.", "whyPool": "Selection bias occurs when the data you can see is systematically different from the data you cannot see due to a filtering mechanism (like editorial approval)."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Using a sample of 15 years of published trials provides a sufficient volume of data to ensure that the model can generalize to the total population of all drug development efforts. 55 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Fifteen years of *published* trials cannot fix a sample skewed toward one outcome type; volume does not cure the bias.", "whyPool": "Volume and time duration cannot fix a sample that is fundamentally skewed toward one type of outcome. ("}, {"L": "D", "text": "The model will likely underestimate the \"True Failure Rate\" of drug development because the \"negative\" outcomes— the thousands of trials that were discontinued or never published—are entirely missing from the training distribution.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The model will underestimate the true failure rate because discontinued/unpublished negative outcomes are entirely absent from the training distribution.", "whyPool": "By training on a \"sanitized\" version of history, the model loses the ability to calculate the base rate of failure for the asset class."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A model trained on this biased sample will likely provide the venture capital firm with a false sense of security, as it has never learned to identify the subtle signals that precede a trial's failure or abandonment.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Trained on a sanitized history, the model gives false confidence — it never learned the signals preceding failure/abandonment, so it fails in production.", "whyPool": "Generalization requires the training data to be a \"miniature version\" of the real world; if the training data is 90% success but the real world is 90% failure, the model will fail in production. G4: Temporal Structure and Ordering"}], "reference": "Publication/selection bias and base-rate distortion — Ioannidis, \"Why Most Published Research Findings Are False\"; general survivorship/selection-bias concept.", "n": 56}, {"id": "ML-D2G4Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A team predicts next-day returns using ten years of daily observations. To obtain similar class proportions in both subsets, an analyst randomly shuffles all days before splitting them into training and test data. The PM asks whether the resulting score reflects how the model would actually have been developed and deployed through time. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A chronological split better preserves the sequence in which past data become available for future prediction.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a chronological split preserves the past→future sequence — it trains on earlier data and evaluates on later data, matching deployment.", "whyPool": "It trains on earlier information and evaluates on later observations. 56 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Random shuffling can place later market regimes in training while earlier observations appear in the test set.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: random shuffling can place later regimes in training and earlier ones in test, ignoring the direction in which information actually arrives.", "whyPool": "Shuffling ignores the direction in which information arrives."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Random shuffling is always valid whenever individual rows have distinct dates.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: distinct dates per row do not make shuffling valid — dated rows still have temporal dependence and a real forecasting order.", "whyPool": "Dated rows can still have temporal dependence and a forecasting deployment order."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Financial observations near one another in time may be dependent rather than exchangeable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: nearby-in-time financial observations can be serially dependent (overlapping signals, persistent conditions), so they are not exchangeable.", "whyPool": "Market conditions and overlapping signals can create serial dependence."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Preserving order can make evaluation harder while still making it more realistic.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: preserving order can make evaluation harder yet more realistic — a genuine future period may differ from the training period and yield a lower but more meaningful estimate.", "whyPool": "A realistic future period may differ from the training period and produce a lower but more meaningful estimate."}], "reference": "Time-series cross-validation and non-exchangeability — scikit-learn `TimeSeriesSplit`; de Prado (walk-forward, purging/embargo).", "n": 57}, {"id": "ML-D2G4Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A monthly trading model is evaluated with cross-validation. An engineer proposes ordinary random folds, while the research director proposes a walk-forward design that repeatedly trains on earlier months and evaluates on later months. Several features and labels are based on overlapping return windows.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Walk-forward evaluation respects the intended past-to-future prediction direction.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The statement is \"*The following claim is false: walk-forward respects the past-to-future direction.*\" The inner claim is true (walk-forward predicts each period from only preceding periods), so denying it is false.", "whyPool": "Each evaluation period is predicted using only preceding training periods."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Random folds can mix observations from both sides of a test month into its training set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Random folds can put observations from both sides of a test month into its training set, breaking chronological availability.", "whyPool": "Ordinary random assignment does not preserve chronological availability."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Overlapping windows can make nearby observations share information and appear more independent than they are.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Overlapping windows share underlying days, so nominally separate examples share information and appear more independent than they are (motivating purging/embargo).", "whyPool": "Shared underlying days can increase dependence between nominally separate examples."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Every walk-forward fold must use the same number of training observations to be valid.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Walk-forward folds need not be equal size — expanding-window designs legitimately use a growing training history.", "whyPool": "Expanding-window designs legitimately use a growing training history."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Temporal validation design should reflect when inputs and labels would actually have been available.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Temporal validation should mirror when inputs and labels would actually have been available in practice.", "whyPool": "Availability timing determines whether the simulated prediction could have been made in practice."}], "reference": "Walk-forward/purged CV, overlapping-label leakage — de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (ch. on cross-validation); scikit-learn `TimeSeriesSplit`.", "n": 58}, {"id": "ML-D2G4Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund trains a model each Friday to trade the following week. Its dataset assigns each Friday observation a label equal to the return through the next Friday, but the training set includes the most recent Friday even though that label would not yet be known at the retraining time. The backtest developer says the row is historical now, so its use is acceptable.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The relevant question is whether the label was available at the simulated Friday decision time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The right question is whether the label was available at the simulated Friday decision time — a valid backtest reproduces historical information availability.", "whyPool": "A valid backtest must reproduce historical information availability."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Including the unresolved forward return gives the simulated model information it could not have had then.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Including the unresolved forward return feeds the simulated model information it could not have had then (the return is known only later).", "whyPool": "The future return becomes known only after the simulated training event."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A value’s presence in today’s completed database proves it was available at every earlier model-training date.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A value's presence in today's completed database does not prove it existed at every earlier training date — databases often contain later-known values.", "whyPool": "Modern databases often contain values that were unknown at earlier points in time."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The training cutoff may need to exclude observations whose label windows have not finished. 57 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The training cutoff may need to exclude observations whose label windows haven't finished; only realized labels can support that run.", "whyPool": "Only labels realized by the cutoff can legitimately support that training run."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Respecting row dates alone is sufficient even when labels mature after those dates.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Respecting row *dates* alone is insufficient when labels mature *after* those dates — label availability can lag the timestamp and must also be checked.", "whyPool": "Label availability can lag the observation timestamp and must also be considered."}], "reference": "Look-ahead bias from unmatured labels; point-in-time labeling — de Prado (labeling, backtest hygiene); \"event date vs. availability date\" concept.", "n": 59}, {"id": "ML-D2G4Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A researcher is developing a \"Mean Reversion\" strategy. To improve the signal-to-noise ratio, they decide to normalize their features (like RSI and Bollinger Bands) by subtracting the mean and dividing by the standard deviation calculated over the entire 20-year historical dataset before splitting the data into training and testing sets. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that this preprocessing step is mathematically sound because using the global mean and standard deviation provides the model with a \"Stable Reference Point\" that remains consistent across all market regimes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "\"*It is incorrect to conclude that this preprocessing is sound because the global μ/σ give a stable reference across all regimes.*\" The embedded claim (it's sound / a legitimate stable point) is itself inaccurate — a 2010 trader cannot know 2024's mean — so rejecting it is correct → the statement is accurate.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is inaccurate, so rejecting it is correct. While it provides a \"stable\" point, it is an illegal point. A trader in 2010 cannot know the mean volatility of 2024 to normalize their data."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Normalizing on the full dataset is an effective way to prevent Temporal Autocorrelation, as it ensures that each daily observation is scaled relative to the long-term equilibrium of the market.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: scaling does not address temporal autocorrelation — it only changes units while leaking future information into the past.", "whyPool": "Scaling does not address autocorrelation; it merely changes the units of the features while simultaneously leaking future information into the past."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because the normalization parameters (μ,σ) are based on the entire population, this technique is considered the most robust way to ensure that the model generalizes to \"unseen\" future data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: global-parameter normalization *ruins* generalization — the model expects a global scale it can never compute live, where the future mean is unknown.", "whyPool": "It actually ruins generalization; the model learns to expect a specific \"global\" scale that it will never be able to calculate in live trading, where the \"future\" mean is unknown. 58 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "This process does not introduce Look-Ahead Bias because the normalization only affects the \"Scale\" of the features and does not reveal the actual \"Direction\" of future price movements to the model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: this *is* look-ahead bias — the global mean/variance encode future distribution info even if specific future dates weren't \"seen.\"", "whyPool": "This is a classic form of look-ahead bias; if the global mean is high, the model \"knows\" that future prices/volatility will be high, even if it hasn't \"seen\" those dates yet."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The model’s high performance in the backtest is likely an artifact of Data Leakage, as the training samples now contain information about the mean and variance of the \"future\" test set that would not have been available to a trader in real- time.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: to avoid leakage, normalization parameters must be fit only on the training set (or a rolling window) and then applied to test.", "whyPool": "To avoid this, normalization parameters must be calculated only on the training set (or via a rolling window) and then applied to the test set."}], "reference": "Data leakage from full-sample scaling; fit-transform on train only — scikit-learn \"Common pitfalls: data leakage,\" `StandardScaler` in a `Pipeline`; de Prado (look-ahead bias).", "n": 60}, {"id": "ML-D2G4Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are implementing a \"Walk-Forward\" validation scheme for a trend-following algorithm. In this setup, you use a sliding window: you train on the first 3 years of data, test on the 4th year, then shift the entire window forward by 1 year and repeat.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This \"Walk-Forward\" methodology is the most robust way to evaluate a trading strategy because it preserves the Temporal Order of the data, ensuring the model is never tested on information that occurred chronologically before the training set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Walk-forward preserves temporal order, ensuring the model is never tested on data chronologically preceding its training set — the finance \"gold standard.\"", "whyPool": "Walk-forward testing is the \"Gold Standard\" in finance because it maintains the arrow of time and simulates how a model would actually be deployed in a live environment."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Shuffling the individual days within each 3-year training window is a recommended step to ensure the model focuses on broad patterns rather than specific dates, and this does not introduce leakage since the test year remains untouched.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Shuffling days *within* the training window is harmful: features like momentum/moving averages depend on sequence, and shuffling destroys the autocorrelation the model should capture.", "whyPool": "Even within the training set, shuffling is harmful. Many financial features (like Momentum or Moving Averages) rely on the sequence of data; shuffling destroys the autocorrelation signals the model is meant to capture."}, {"L": "C", "text": "This approach helps the researcher detect Regime Drift, as the model’s performance is evaluated across different chronological \"slices\" of the market (e.g., a low-volatility period followed by a high-volatility period).", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Because the model is retrained and retested moving through time, walk-forward reveals regime drift — when/why a strategy starts failing as conditions change.", "whyPool": "Because the model is retrained and retested as it moves through time, you can see exactly when and why a strategy begins to fail as market conditions evolve."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The \"Walk-Forward\" method is essentially a form of Data Leakage because the model \"sees\" the same historical patterns multiple times as the training window slides forward through the 20-year dataset.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Re-seeing historical patterns as the window slides is *not* leakage: as long as each fold's test set follows its training set chronologically, the future stays unknown at each step.", "whyPool": "This is not leakage. As long as the test set for any given fold always follows the training set chronologically, the \"future\" remains unknown to the model at each step."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A potential drawback of this method is that it requires significantly more data than a standard split, as the early folds have very small training sets compared to the later folds in an \"Expanding Window\" variation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A practical drawback: early folds train on smaller samples (especially in expanding-window variants), so initial results are less reliable and it is data-hungry.", "whyPool": "This is a practical constraint; your initial \"Walk-Forward\" results may be less reliable because the model was trained on a much smaller sample than the final results. 59 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Walk-forward validation and within-window shuffling harm — de Prado (backtesting through time); scikit-learn `TimeSeriesSplit`.", "withdrawn": true, "n": 61}, {"id": "ML-D2G4Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are building a regime-switching model to pivot between an S&P 500 equity strategy and a cash position. One of your primary features is the \"Macroeconomic Unemployment Rate. \" Your backtesting script pulls the official Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) monthly unemployment figures, aligning the value for a given month (e.g., January's 4.0% unemployment) directly with the market price action on the final trading day of that same month (January 31). For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This temporal alignment is standard practice because matching the macroeconomic state of a specific calendar month with that month's closing stock prices ensures the model learns the exact contemporaneous relationship between the economy and the market.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: aligning by calendar month rather than release date lets the model trade on information invisible to participants at that time.", "whyPool": "Aligning data based on the calendar month rather than the public release date allows the model to trade on information that was invisible to market participants at that time."}, {"L": "B", "text": "This pipeline introduces a severe Forward-Looking Bias because the unemployment rate for a given month is typically not finalized, compiled, and publicly released by the government until the first or second Friday of the following month.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: this is fatal look-ahead bias — January's figure isn't known until the early-February BLS release, so using it on Jan 31 is cheating.", "whyPool": "This is a fatal look-ahead bias. If your model \"knows\" January's unemployment figures on January 31, it is cheating; those numbers are not known until the BLS release in early February."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because macroeconomic data is highly persistent and slow-moving, a 1- or 2-week reporting lag is statistically irrelevant and will not alter the model's predictive accuracy or signal generation in a live environment.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: even a short reporting lag is catastrophic in systematic trading — unlagged data lets the backtest \"avoid\" downturns perfectly, then fail live.", "whyPool": "In systematic trading, even a short informational lag is catastrophic. A model trained on unlagged data will exploit the \"future\" knowledge to avoid market downturns perfectly in the backtest, then fail completely in production."}, {"L": "D", "text": "This is a classic example of a Timestamp Mismatch error, where the \"event date\" (when the economic activity occurred) is mistakenly treated as the \"availability date\" (when a trader could actually execute an order based on that data).", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: valid time-series validation needs point-in-time data indexed by publication timestamp, not the period covered — this is a timestamp-mismatch (event vs. availability date) error.", "whyPool": "To ensure valid time-series validation, financial models must utilize \"Point-in-Time\" data matrices that index observations by the exact publication timestamp rather than the period covered."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Running a random shuffle on the final dataset will neutralize this reporting lag by distributing the 45-day informational advantage evenly across both the training and validation sets.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: shuffling does not fix a causality violation — it just spreads the corrupted look-ahead signal across the dataset, guaranteeing an overfit model.", "whyPool": "Shuffling does not fix a fundamental violation of causality; it simply spreads the corrupted, look-ahead signal across the entire dataset, guaranteeing an overfit model. 60 Rev:081020261230 G5: Train/Test Separation Discipline"}], "reference": "Point-in-time data, publication-lag / look-ahead bias — de Prado (backtest hygiene); \"event date vs. availability date\"; economic-data vintages (e.g., ALFRED/real-time datasets) concept.", "n": 62}, {"id": "ML-D2G5Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A research team splits observations into training and test sets, but then standardizes every feature using the mean and standard deviation calculated from the combined data. The analyst notes that the test labels were never used and claims the test score is therefore fully independent. The PM asks whether the preprocessing design matters.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Combined-data standardization lets the test-feature distribution influence the training pipeline.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Combined-data standardization lets the test-feature distribution influence the training pipeline — the fitted mean/std summarize held-out observations.", "whyPool": "The fitted means and standard deviations summarize held-out observations."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Avoiding test labels is the only requirement for an uncontaminated test set.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Avoiding test *labels* is not the only requirement; test *features* can also affect learned preprocessing and development choices.", "whyPool": "Test features can also affect learned preprocessing and development choices."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Scaling parameters should normally be estimated from training data and then applied unchanged to test data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Scaling parameters should be estimated from training data and applied unchanged to test, simulating how new post-deployment observations would be transformed.", "whyPool": "This simulates how new observations would be transformed after deployment."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Preprocessing is part of the model-development pipeline for purposes of train/test separation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Preprocessing is part of the model-development pipeline for train/test separation — data-dependent choices must be learned without the final test set.", "whyPool": "Data-dependent preprocessing choices must be learned without the final test set."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The contamination becomes acceptable if the same formula is applied to every row.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Applying the same formula to every row does not undo the fact that test information was used to *estimate* the parameters.", "whyPool": "Uniform application does not undo the use of test information when estimating the parameters."}], "reference": "Preprocessing leakage; scale on train only — scikit-learn \"Common pitfalls: data leakage,\" `Pipeline`; ISLR ch.5.", "n": 63}, {"id": "ML-D2G5Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A desk compares 40 model and hyperparameter combinations directly on its designated test set, selects the highest- scoring one, and reports that same score as the final performance estimate. The head of research calls the process efficient because each candidate was trained without the test rows. The validation set was not used.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Selecting among models based on test performance makes the test set part of model development.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Selecting among models by test performance makes the test set part of model development — its results drove the choice.", "whyPool": "Test results influenced which configuration was chosen."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The reported maximum score can be optimistic because it includes chance variation across many comparisons.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The reported maximum is optimistically biased: with many comparisons, the winner partly reflects chance variation specific to that test sample.", "whyPool": "The winner may partly reflect noise specific to that test sample."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A validation set could be used for model selection while reserving the test set for final evaluation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A validation set could handle model selection while the test set is reserved for a single final generalization estimate.", "whyPool": "This separates selection from the final generalization estimate."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The test set remains fully unseen merely because its rows were not used to fit candidate parameters.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The test set is not \"unseen\" just because its rows didn't fit candidate parameters — viewing and acting on test scores is itself exposure.", "whyPool": "Viewing and acting on test scores is a form of exposure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Retraining the chosen configuration on the training data erases the information gained from test-based selection.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Retraining the chosen config on training data does not erase test-based selection; the choice still encodes what was learned from the test set.", "whyPool": "The configuration choice still encodes what the team learned from the test set."}], "reference": "Validation vs. test set separation; multiple-comparison optimism — ISLR ch.5–6; scikit-learn \"Cross-validation\" / nested CV.", "n": 64}, {"id": "ML-D2G5Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A vendor supplies a preselected feature list after evaluating hundreds of candidates on the full historical dataset. Your team then makes a fresh train/test split and fits only the supplied features, claiming the test is clean because your own code never inspected those rows before splitting. The investment committee wants to know whether the upstream selection matters. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Feature selection performed on the full dataset can transmit information from future test rows into the pipeline.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: feature selection on the full dataset transmits information from future test rows into the pipeline — the list reflects relationships seen across the entire history.", "whyPool": "The selected list reflects relationships observed in the entire history."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A later split automatically removes contamination introduced by earlier data-dependent decisions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a later split does *not* remove contamination from earlier data-dependent decisions; the prior selection remains informed by the future test observations.", "whyPool": "The prior selection decision remains informed by the future test observations."}, {"L": "C", "text": "To preserve separation, feature selection should be repeated using only the training portion.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: to preserve separation, feature selection (a data-dependent step) must be redone using only the training portion.", "whyPool": "Data-dependent selection belongs inside the training process."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Documentation of how the vendor created the feature list is relevant to evaluating the claimed independence.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: documentation of how the vendor built the list (provenance) is exactly what determines whether held-out information shaped the features.", "whyPool": "Provenance determines whether held-out information shaped the features."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Contamination can occur through development choices even when test rows are never passed directly to the final fitting function.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: contamination can occur through development choices even when test rows never reach the final fitting function — indirect influence still compromises evaluation.", "whyPool": "Indirect influence through preprocessing or selection can compromise an evaluation."}], "reference": "Feature-selection leakage / provenance — ISLR §6 and the classic \"selecting features on all data\" error (ESL §7.10.2, \"the wrong way to do CV\"); scikit-learn data-leakage guidance.", "n": 65}, {"id": "ML-D2G5Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An analyst is building a cross-sectional equity model to predict the next-day returns of 500 tech stocks. Before splitting the 10-year dataset into a 70% training set and a 30% testing set, they handle missing values in their accounting features (such as debt-to-equity ratios) by applying a global median imputation across the entire dataset to maximize sample integrity. 62 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This preprocessing pipeline is structurally sound because using a global median preserves the overarching cross- sectional distribution of the sector, ensuring that both the train and test splits have identical feature spaces.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Preserving the global distribution by leaking test data into training destroys out-of-sample validity — \"structurally sound\" is wrong.", "whyPool": "Preserving the global distribution at the cost of leaking test-set data into the training phase destroys the validity of the out-of-sample evaluation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "This approach introduces Data Contamination (Leakage) because the median values used to fill gaps in the training set are partially calculated using information from the future test set, violating the rule of absolute separation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "This is a classic preprocessing leak: the median filling training gaps is partly computed from future test values, so the test is no longer unseen.", "whyPool": "This is a classic preprocessing leak. If test-set values influence the training set's features (even through a summary statistic like a median), the test data is no longer truly \"unseen. \""}, {"L": "C", "text": "Since median imputation only replaces missing values and does not alter the existing, non-missing data points, it cannot leak directional price signals or cause an artificial inflation of backtest performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Even a non-directional summary statistic leaks variance/scale information about the future space, letting the model adapt to the test distribution prematurely.", "whyPool": "Even non-directional summary statistics leak information about the variance and scale of the future data space, which allows the model to adapt to the test distribution prematurely."}, {"L": "D", "text": "This is a common violation of proper splitting where information from the out-of-sample period is inadvertently mixed into the in-sample training phase, leading to overly optimistic generalization metrics.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "It is a common splitting violation — out-of-sample information is mixed into the in-sample phase, inflating generalization metrics.", "whyPool": "This contamination masks how the model would actually handle missing data in real-time streaming environments where future medians are mathematically unavailable."}, {"L": "E", "text": "To maintain strict train/test discipline, the analyst must compute the median parameters using only the training data, and then apply those pre-calculated metrics to impute any missing values found in the test set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct discipline: compute the median on training data only, then apply it to impute test-set gaps.", "whyPool": "All feature engineering, scaling, and imputation parameters must be fit strictly on the training partition to ensure zero downstream contamination."}], "reference": "Imputation leakage; fit imputer on train — scikit-learn `SimpleImputer` in a `Pipeline`, \"data leakage\" pitfalls.", "n": 66}, {"id": "ML-D2G5Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher is building a model to predict the credit default risk of small businesses using a 10-year dataset. To address a high rate of missing values in the \"Annual Revenue\" column, they decide to use K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) Imputation. They pass the entire feature matrix (the full 10 years of data) into the imputer to ensure the \"neighbors\" found for each missing value are as accurate as possible, then they perform a chronological 80/20 train-test split.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This methodology is an advanced form of data cleaning that improves model robustness by ensuring the training set's feature distributions are as complete and accurate as possible before the split.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The improved accuracy is \"illegal\" — the training phase is contaminated with data that in reality would sit in the unknown future.", "whyPool": "While accuracy is improved, it is \"illegal\" accuracy. You have contaminated the training phase with data points that, in a real-world scenario, would be in the \"unknown future. \""}, {"L": "B", "text": "This approach violates Train/Test Separation Discipline because the imputed values in the training set are calculated using the feature values of \"neighbors\" that reside in the future test set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "This violates separation: a training row's imputed value can be derived from a nearest neighbor located in the future test set (a transductive leak).", "whyPool": "This is a classic \"Transductive Leak. \" If a row from 2018 is missing data and its nearest neighbor is found in 2024, the 2018 row now contains information derived from the future."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Since KNN imputation only uses feature values (X) to fill gaps and does not look at the target labels (y), this process is considered \"leakage-free\" and safe for financial time-series applications. 63 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Leakage is not limited to labels — if training features are influenced by test features, the model gains a hidden familiarity with the test distribution.", "whyPool": "Leakage is not limited to labels. If the features in your training set are influenced by the features in your test set, the model will develop a \"hidden familiarity\" with the test distribution."}, {"L": "D", "text": "This is an example of Manifold Contamination, where the underlying structure of the test set is \"leaked\" into the training set through the proximity calculations of the imputation algorithm.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Using the whole dataset to define neighborhoods lets the geometry of the future warp the representation of the past — a manifold/structure leak.", "whyPool": "By using the entire dataset to define the \"neighborhoods, \" the researcher has allowed the geometry of the future to warp the representation of the past."}, {"L": "E", "text": "To maintain proper discipline, the researcher must fit the KNN imputer only on the training data, and then use that fitted model to impute missing values in both the training and test sets.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct discipline: fit the KNN imputer on training data only, then use that fitted imputer to fill gaps in both train and test.", "whyPool": "Strict discipline requires that the \"rules\" for imputation are learned solely from the past. If the training set cannot stand alone to solve its own missing data, the split is compromised."}], "reference": "Transductive/neighbor-based imputation leakage — scikit-learn `KNNImputer` fit-on-train guidance; ESL §7.10.2 (leakage through preprocessing).", "n": 67}, {"id": "ML-D2G5Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative developer is building a high-frequency market-making model. To optimize the model's performance, they perform Feature Selection (e.g., Recursive Feature Elimination) using the entire available 5-year dataset. After identifying the top 10 most predictive features, they then apply a Walk-Forward Validation scheme to train and test the model using only those 10 features.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This workflow is a statistically sound way to reduce the \"Curse of Dimensionality, \" ensuring the model only focuses on the most robust signals before entering the validation phase.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reducing dimensionality by peeking at the test set yields a \"survivor\" feature set tailored to a future it shouldn't know — not statistically sound.", "whyPool": "Reducing dimensionality is good, but doing it by peeking at the test set creates a \"survivor\" set of features that are tailored to a future they shouldn't know yet."}, {"L": "B", "text": "This approach constitutes a Global Information Leak, as the decision of which features to include was informed by looking at the price action and volatility of the entire out-of-sample period.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "This is a global information leak: the choice of which features to include was informed by the entire out-of-sample period.", "whyPool": "This is a common and subtle violation. If you use the test set to decide how to build your model (including which variables to use), the test set is no longer an independent measure of performance."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because the actual \"Training\" of the model weights happens within a walk-forward split, the initial feature selection process is considered an \"Independent Preprocessing Step\" and does not contaminate the test results.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Feature selection is part of model-building; if the selection \"sees\" the future, the subsequent walk-forward training is already biased toward future-relevant features.", "whyPool": "Feature selection is part of the model-building process. If the selection \"sees\" the future, the resulting \"independent\" training is already biased toward those future-relevant features."}, {"L": "D", "text": "This is an example of Selection Bias (also known as \"Data Snooping\"), where the features chosen are likely those that happened to perform well over the specific 5-year window by chance, leading to a massive overestimation of live performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "It is selection bias / data snooping — the chosen features may have performed well over that specific window by chance, overstating live performance.", "whyPool": "This \"snooping\" results in features that look like \"Universal Truths\" in the backtest but are actually just artifacts of that specific historical period."}, {"L": "E", "text": "To maintain proper train/test separation, feature selection must be performed dynamically within each training fold of the walk-forward split, using only the data available up to that point in time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct discipline: perform feature selection *inside* each training fold of the walk-forward, using only data available up to that point.", "whyPool": "In a real-world scenario, a trader must decide which indicators to use without knowing which ones will be most profitable next year. Your validation must mimic this ignorance. G6: Data Snooping and Reuse"}], "reference": "Feature-selection-inside-CV rule; snooping via selection — ESL §7.10.2; de Prado (backtest overfitting); scikit-learn `RFE` within a CV pipeline.", "n": 68}, {"id": "ML-D2G6Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A quant team evaluates a strategy on the same 2018–2025 test period every week. After each disappointing result, it changes features, holding periods, or learners until the backtest looks strong. The team never explicitly copies test labels into the training matrix, so the PM believes the final test result remains unbiased. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Repeatedly modifying the strategy in response to the same test results makes that period part of development.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: repeatedly modifying the strategy in response to the same test results makes that period part of development (feedback guides the design).", "whyPool": "Feedback from the test period guides the evolving design."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The final performance estimate may capture chance patterns peculiar to the reused period.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: enough iterations adapt the strategy to sample-specific noise, so the final estimate can capture chance patterns peculiar to the reused period.", "whyPool": "Enough iterations can adapt the strategy to sample-specific noise."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Leakage is impossible unless test labels appear as training features.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: leakage is *not* impossible without label-copying — information leaks through human selection and iteration.", "whyPool": "Information can leak through human selection and iteration without direct feature copying."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A genuinely untouched evaluation period would provide stronger evidence about the final design.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a genuinely untouched evaluation period tests the selected design on observations that did not shape it, giving stronger evidence.", "whyPool": "It tests the selected design on observations that did not shape it."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Keeping a written record of experiments can reveal the extent of repeated search, although it does not by itself restore test independence.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: experiment tracking improves transparency but cannot make previously used data unseen again.", "whyPool": "Experiment tracking improves transparency but cannot make previously used data unseen again. 65 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Backtest overfitting from repeated reuse; human-in-the-loop leakage — de Prado & Bailey, \"The Deflated Sharpe Ratio\" / \"Pseudo-mathematics and financial charlatanism\"; multiple-testing concept.", "n": 69}, {"id": "ML-D2G6Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund screens 5,000 technical indicators on one historical period and publishes the ten with the best Sharpe ratios. It then presents those same ten in-sample results as evidence that the signals are economically meaningful. The CIO asks whether multiple comparisons change how the winners should be interpreted.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Searching many weak or useless indicators can still produce apparently strong winners by chance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Searching many weak/useless indicators can still produce apparently strong winners by pure chance.", "whyPool": "Extreme results can arise from random variation when enough candidates are tried."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Selecting the maximum observed Sharpe ratio tends to favor positive estimation noise.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Selecting the maximum observed Sharpe favors estimates that benefited from unusually favorable sample noise (selection/winner's-curse effect).", "whyPool": "Selection favors estimates that benefited from unusually favorable sample noise."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The ten winners are validated because they outperformed the other 4,990 indicators on the search data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Outperforming the other 4,990 on the *search* data is not independent confirmation — in-sample ranking proves nothing about persistence.", "whyPool": "Ranking within the search sample is not independent confirmation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Testing the chosen indicators on untouched data can help distinguish persistent signal from selection luck.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Testing the chosen indicators on untouched data helps separate persistent signal from selection luck.", "whyPool": "Fresh evidence avoids reusing the sample that produced the winners."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The number of alternatives examined is irrelevant once only the winners are reported.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The number of alternatives examined is highly relevant — the breadth of the search determines how surprising the winning values really are.", "whyPool": "The breadth of the search affects how surprising the winning values really are."}], "reference": "Multiple-testing / data snooping and the Sharpe-ratio winner's curse — Bailey & de Prado, \"Deflated Sharpe Ratio\"; White's \"Reality Check\"; ESL §7 (multiple comparisons).", "n": 70}, {"id": "ML-D2G6Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Data & Sampling Discipline", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Three research teams independently use the same corporate “golden” holdout period for final evaluation. They share their scores at monthly meetings, abandon weak ideas, and develop replacements based on what succeeded for the other teams. Governance records still label the period “untouched” because no one retrains directly on its rows.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Organization-wide feedback can cause the holdout to influence later model designs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The statement is \"*The following claim is false: org-wide feedback can cause the holdout to influence later designs.*\" The inner claim is true (shared results shape which approaches the org pursues), so denying it is false.", "whyPool": "Shared results shape which approaches the organization pursues."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A holdout remains independent indefinitely as long as its raw rows are access-controlled.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A holdout does not stay independent indefinitely just because raw rows are access-controlled — repeated outcome feedback compromises independence without exposing rows.", "whyPool": "Repeated outcome feedback can compromise independence without exposing raw rows. 66 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Aggregate scores can reveal enough information to guide adaptation to the holdout.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Aggregate scores can reveal enough information to guide adaptation: teams learn which choices worked on that specific period and respond accordingly.", "whyPool": "Teams learn which choices worked on that specific period and respond accordingly."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Limiting evaluation frequency and reserving a newer final set can reduce the risk of institutional overfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Limiting evaluation frequency and reserving a newer final set reduces institutional overfitting and preserves a more credible evaluation boundary.", "whyPool": "Reduced exposure and fresh final data preserve a more credible evaluation boundary."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Models selected through this process may have performance estimates that are too optimistic for genuinely new data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Models selected through this process can have over-optimistic estimates because the selection favors approaches tailored to the repeatedly observed holdout.", "whyPool": "The selection process can favor approaches tailored to the repeatedly observed holdout. 67 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Institutional/adaptive overfitting of a shared holdout — Dwork et al., \"The reusable holdout\" (adaptive data analysis); de Prado (backtest overfitting).", "n": 71}, {"id": "ML-D3G1Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An equity team predicts next-month returns from company financial statements. The PM proposes using raw revenue alone, while an analyst argues that revenue growth and profit margin may better compare firms of different sizes. The team must improve the signal without incorporating information unavailable on the prediction date.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Revenue growth can capture a change over time that a single revenue level does not express directly.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Revenue *growth* encodes a change over time; a single revenue *level* cannot express that temporal relationship directly.", "whyPool": "Growth represents a temporal relationship rather than only the current level."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Dividing profit by revenue may create a scale-adjusted feature that is more comparable across firms.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Profit ÷ revenue is a margin — a scale-adjusted feature that normalizes for firm size and supports cross-firm comparison.", "whyPool": "A margin normalizes profit by firm activity and can support cross-firm comparisons."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Every derived feature necessarily adds new information that was absent from the raw inputs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A transformation does *not* necessarily add information; it may merely restate existing inputs and be redundant.", "whyPool": "A transformation may merely restate existing information and can be redundant."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The dates on which financial statements became publicly available matter when constructing these features.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Publication dates matter: using figures before they became public would make the historical feature unavailable in practice (look-ahead bias).", "whyPool": "Using figures before their publication would make the historical feature unavailable in practice. 69 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Feature engineering is useful only for linear models and cannot help distance-based learners.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Appropriate transformations also improve distance measures and neighborhood structure — not only linear models benefit.", "whyPool": "Appropriate transformations can also improve distance measures and neighborhood structure."}], "reference": "Feature construction & scale-adjusted ratios — ISLR ch.3 (linear model features); point-in-time availability / look-ahead bias concept.", "n": 72}, {"id": "ML-D3G1Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A futures desk models daily returns using price and volume data. The head trader believes the effect of unusual volume may depend on whether price is rising or falling, but the current linear model includes only the two raw variables separately. The research team considers adding an interaction between return sign and relative volume. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "An interaction feature can represent a relationship that depends on the joint values of two inputs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: an interaction feature represents a relationship depending on the joint values of two inputs — effects beyond additive contributions.", "whyPool": "Interactions encode joint effects beyond independent additive contributions."}, {"L": "B", "text": "In a purely additive linear model, separate price and volume terms automatically represent every possible interaction between them.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: separate additive linear terms do NOT automatically represent a multiplicative/conditional interaction between the variables.", "whyPool": "Separate linear terms do not generally express a multiplicative or conditional effect."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A price-direction-by-volume feature could allow high volume to have different implications in rising and falling markets.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a price-direction × volume feature lets high volume mean different things in rising vs. falling markets.", "whyPool": "The engineered feature can distinguish the two market contexts."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Adding the interaction guarantees better out-of-sample performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: adding the interaction does not *guarantee* better out-of-sample performance — the added flexibility may help, do nothing, or overfit.", "whyPool": "The added flexibility may help, do nothing, or overfit."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The interaction should be computed using only information known when the trade decision is made.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a deployable interaction must be computed only from information known at the trade-decision time.", "whyPool": "A deployable feature must respect the decision-time information set."}], "reference": "Interaction terms & additive-model limits — ISLR §3.3.2 (interaction terms, hierarchy); bias-variance of added flexibility §2.2.2.", "n": 73}, {"id": "ML-D3G1Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A credit fund uses days-to-maturity, coupon, and yield to predict bond price changes. A junior researcher wants to add dozens of polynomial terms because “more transformations let the model discover everything, ” while the risk officer is concerned about instability and limited historical defaults. The team must balance representation against generalization.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A nonlinear transformation can make some curved relationships easier for a linear learner to represent.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A nonlinear (e.g., polynomial) transform lets a linear learner express curved relationships via a linear combination of basis terms.", "whyPool": "Transformed inputs can let a linear combination express nonlinear patterns."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Adding many polynomial features can increase model complexity and overfitting risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Many polynomial features raise model complexity and overfitting risk — more terms = more chances to fit noise, especially with little data.", "whyPool": "More terms provide more opportunities to fit noise, especially with limited data."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If a transformation is calculated only from existing features, it cannot affect a model’s predictions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A transform computed only from existing features *can* change predictions — re-expressing inputs alters what the learner can fit.", "whyPool": "Reexpressing inputs can materially change what a learner can fit."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The usefulness of engineered terms should be assessed on data not used to fit or select them. 70 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Usefulness must be assessed on data not used to fit or select the terms — held-out evaluation estimates generalization.", "whyPool": "Held-out evaluation is needed to estimate whether the terms generalize."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A complicated feature is preferable to a simple feature whenever it lowers training error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Lower training error alone does not establish out-of-sample value; a complicated feature is not preferable just because it cuts training loss.", "whyPool": "Lower training error alone does not establish out-of-sample value."}], "reference": "Polynomial/basis expansion & overfitting — ISLR §7.1 (polynomial regression), §2.2.2 (bias-variance); cross-validation ch.5.", "n": 74}, {"id": "ML-D3G1Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A multi-factor equity model utilizes a rolling 12-month Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) Linear Regression to estimate the beta (β) of individual technology stocks relative to the Nasdaq 100 index. The model uses these rolling beta estimates as a primary feature to predict cross-sectional next-week returns. During a sudden, high-volatility market pivot, the model's predictive performance degrades rapidly, and an audit reveals that the estimated coefficients were highly unstable. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The instability of the beta coefficients is a classic symptom of Non-Stationarity in financial time series, meaning the underlying statistical relationship between the individual stocks and the index is changing over time.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: beta instability is a classic symptom of non-stationarity — the stock-index relationship changes over regimes; a bull-market beta misrepresents risk in a shock.", "whyPool": "Financial regimes are dynamic. A beta calculated during a low-volatility bull market will completely misrepresent an asset's systemic risk when a market shock occurs."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Using a fixed, rolling 12-month window completely eliminates the risk of non-stationarity because it forces the OLS regression to constantly discard obsolete historical data, ensuring a stable parameter space.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a fixed rolling window *mitigates* but does not *eliminate* non-stationarity — a structural break inside the window leaves OLS parameters as a polluted average of two regimes.", "whyPool": "Rolling windows mitigate, but do not eliminate, non-stationarity. If a structural break occurs within the current window, the OLS parameters will be a polluted average of two completely different regimes. 71 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "This pipeline suffers from a structural flaw because standard OLS regression assumes that the underlying data- generating process is Stationary (constant mean and variance), an assumption that routinely breaks down during market regime shifts.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: standard OLS assumes an invariant data-generating process (its residual/independence assumptions break when parameters shift at regime changes).", "whyPool": "OLS regression assumes that the residuals are homoscedastic and independent. When regimes shift, the parameters shift, violating the core assumption that the underlying distribution is invariant."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The rapid degradation in model performance is due to a Structural Break, where the historical covariance matrix calculated over the stable 12-month period fails to capture the sudden, step-function change in market dynamics.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: rapid degradation reflects a structural break — the historical 12-month window acts as an anchor that fails to capture the sudden step-change until old data rolls off.", "whyPool": "A structural break fundamentally changes the parameters of the system. The historical 12-month data acts as an anchor, preventing the model from recognizing the new structural reality until the old data drops out of the window."}, {"L": "E", "text": "To adapt this linear framework to a non-stationary environment without changing the feature definitions, the weights of the regression should be modified using an exponential decay factor that prioritizes recent observations over distant ones.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: without changing feature definitions, exponentially weighting recent observations (EWMA regression / a Kalman-filter state-space model) lets parameters adapt to drift.", "whyPool": "Switching to an exponentially weighted moving regression or a state-space model (like a Kalman Filter) allows the parameter estimation to adapt dynamically to drifting distributions."}], "reference": "Non-stationarity, structural breaks, adaptive estimation — Hamilton *Time Series Analysis* (structural change, state-space/Kalman); ESL §7 (model assessment under drift).", "withdrawn": true, "n": 75}, {"id": "ML-D3G1Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An algorithmic momentum portfolio uses a machine learning classifier to predict whether a stock index will experience a positive or negative return over the next 20 trading days. The raw feature input is the 50-day rolling variance of daily returns. The system evaluates this feature using an Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) Test, which rejects the null hypothesis of a unit root (p<0.01). Based on this result, the pipeline passes the raw rolling variance series directly into a deep neural network without further transformation. During a prolonged market regime shift, the model's accuracy collapses, and an internal audit reveals that the rolling variance feature exhibited severe structural drift. Which of the following statements correctly diagnoses why the ADF test failed to prevent this distribution shift, and identifies the correct mathematical remedy?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The ADF test is structurally incapable of detecting non-stationarity driven by stochastic trends, meaning it can only validate stationarity for series with fixed, deterministic linear trends.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: ADF is *designed* to detect stochastic trends (unit roots) and handles deterministic trends via a time-trend (βt) term — it is not limited to fixed linear trends.", "whyPool": "The ADF test is specifically designed to detect stochastic trends (unit roots). It handles deterministic trends by including a time-trend component (βt) in its test regression."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The rolling variance series failed to maintain stationarity because the ADF test requires a minimum window length of 252 trading days to properly evaluate the presence of a unit root in second-moment statistical features.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: there is no 252-day minimum; ADF validity depends on sample size, lag structure, and the DGP, not a calendar constraint.", "whyPool": "There is no arbitrary 252-day structural minimum required by the ADF test framework; its validity depends on sample size, lag structure, and the underlying data-generating process, not a specific calendar constraint."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The rejection of the null hypothesis by the ADF test mathematically guarantees that all statistical moments—including the mean, variance, and unconditional autocovariance—are perfectly invariant over the entire backtest horizon.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: rejecting the null does not guarantee all moments are invariant — it only rules out a random walk; breaks/heteroscedasticity/seasonality can remain.", "whyPool": "A rejected ADF test is not a blanket guarantee of absolute stationarity. It simply means the series does not exhibit a random walk; it can still suffer from structural breaks, heteroscedasticity, or seasonal non-stationarity. 72 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "The collapse occurred because the ADF test assumes the underlying data follows a Gaussian distribution; when applied to bounded, non-negative features like rolling variance, the test produces a high rate of false negatives.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: ADF does not mechanically invert its error rates just because a feature is bounded/non-negative; the DF distribution assumes i.i.d. errors, not Gaussian data.", "whyPool": "While the standard Dickey-Fuller distribution assumes independent and identically distributed errors, the test does not mechanically fail or invert its error rates solely because a feature is bounded or non-negative."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The ADF test only checks for a specific type of non-stationarity (a unit root/stochastic trend in the mean); it can easily register a series as \"stationary\" even if the feature suffers from structural breaks or structural drift in its variance over time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: ADF checks only a unit root / stochastic trend in the *mean*; a mean-reverting series can still have its variance/distribution warp under regime shifts — the test does not account for that.", "whyPool": "This highlights a critical limitation of the test in feature engineering. A series can be mean-reverting (no unit root) but still have its underlying distribution warp due to regime shifts or variance changes, which the ADF test does not account for."}], "reference": "Augmented Dickey-Fuller test scope & limits — Hamilton *Time Series Analysis* (unit-root testing); stationarity vs. structural-break distinction.", "n": 76}, {"id": "ML-D3G1Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An algorithmic momentum portfolio uses a machine learning classifier to predict whether a stock index will experience a positive or negative return over the next 20 trading days. The raw feature input is the 50-day rolling variance of daily returns. The system evaluates this feature using an Augmented Dickey-Fuller (ADF) Test, which rejects the null hypothesis of a unit root (p<0.01). Based on this result, the pipeline passes the raw rolling variance series directly into a deep neural network without further transformation. During a prolonged market regime shift, the model's accuracy collapses, and an internal audit reveals that the rolling variance feature exhibited severe structural drift. Which of the following statements correctly diagnoses why the ADF test failed to prevent this distribution shift, and identifies the correct mathematical remedy?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The ADF test is structurally incapable of detecting non-stationarity driven by stochastic trends, meaning it can only validate stationarity for series with fixed, deterministic linear trends.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: ADF specifically targets stochastic trends and models deterministic trends with a βt term.", "whyPool": "The ADF test is specifically designed to detect stochastic trends (unit roots). It handles deterministic trends by including a time-trend component (βt) in its test regression."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The rolling variance series failed to maintain stationarity because the ADF test requires a minimum window length of 252 trading days to properly evaluate the presence of a unit root in second-moment statistical features.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: no arbitrary 252-day structural minimum exists for ADF validity.", "whyPool": "There is no arbitrary 252-day structural minimum required by the ADF test framework; its validity depends on sample size, lag structure, and the underlying data-generating process, not a specific calendar constraint."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The rejection of the null hypothesis by the ADF test mathematically guarantees that all statistical moments—including the mean, variance, and unconditional autocovariance—are perfectly invariant over the entire backtest horizon.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: an ADF rejection is not a blanket guarantee of stationarity; breaks/heteroscedasticity/seasonality persist.", "whyPool": "A rejected ADF test is not a blanket guarantee of absolute stationarity. It simply means the series does not exhibit a random walk; it can still suffer from structural breaks, heteroscedasticity, or seasonal non-stationarity."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The collapse occurred because the ADF test assumes the underlying data follows a Gaussian distribution; when applied to bounded, non-negative features like rolling variance, the test produces a high rate of false negatives.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: boundedness/non-negativity does not mechanically flip the test's error rates.", "whyPool": "While the standard Dickey-Fuller distribution assumes independent and identically distributed errors, the test does not mechanically fail or invert its error rates solely because a feature is bounded or non-negative. 73 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "The ADF test only checks for a specific type of non-stationarity (a unit root/stochastic trend in the mean); it can easily register a series as \"stationary\" even if the feature suffers from structural breaks or structural drift in its variance over time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: ADF only checks a mean unit root; variance/distribution drift under regime shift escapes it — a key feature-engineering limitation.", "whyPool": "This highlights a critical limitation of the test in feature engineering. A series can be mean-reverting (no unit root) but still have its underlying distribution warp due to regime shifts or variance changes, which the ADF test does not account for. G2: Scaling and Normalization Effects"}], "reference": "Same as G1Q5 — ADF scope/limits (Hamilton); stationarity vs. structural break.", "duplicateOf": "ML-D3G1Q5", "n": 77}, {"id": "ML-D3G2Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A wealth manager uses KNN to identify historical days similar to the current market. One input is daily return measured near 0.01, while another is trading volume measured in millions of shares. The PM asks why the neighbors appear to be determined almost entirely by volume.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Without scaling, a large-magnitude feature can dominate a Euclidean distance calculation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Without scaling, a large-magnitude feature dominates a Euclidean distance calculation.", "whyPool": "Numerical magnitude directly affects distance unless the inputs are adjusted."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Standardizing the features can cause both return and volume to contribute on more comparable numerical scales.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Standardizing puts return and volume on comparable numerical scales so both contribute to distance.", "whyPool": "Standardization reduces dominance caused solely by differing units or ranges."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Feature scaling guarantees that both features contain equal predictive information.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Scaling changes numerical influence, not underlying information content — it does not equalize predictive value.", "whyPool": "Scaling changes numerical influence, not the underlying information content."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The scaling parameters should be estimated from the training data rather than the held-out test data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Scaling parameters should be estimated from training data, not the held-out test data (else train/test separation weakens).", "whyPool": "Fitting preprocessing on test data weakens train/test separation."}, {"L": "E", "text": "KNN predictions are always unchanged by rescaling one input feature.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Rescaling can change which observations are nearest — KNN predictions are *not* always unchanged by rescaling.", "whyPool": "Rescaling can change which observations are nearest."}], "reference": "Feature scaling for distance-based learners — scikit-learn \"Preprocessing data\" (StandardScaler), \"Nearest Neighbors\"; ISLR §2.2.3.", "n": 78}, {"id": "ML-D3G2Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A research group compares a decision tree with KNN using the same set of standardized financial ratios. The research director asks whether scaling should improve both learners in the same way. The group is also trying to keep the production pipeline as simple as possible.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A conventional decision tree chooses threshold splits based on ordering, so positive linear rescaling usually leaves its partition structure unchanged.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A conventional tree splits on value ordering, so a positive linear (monotonic) rescale leaves its partition structure unchanged.", "whyPool": "A monotonic linear rescaling preserves value order and corresponding split partitions."}, {"L": "B", "text": "KNN can be sensitive to scaling because its neighborhoods depend on distances.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "KNN neighborhoods depend on distances, so it is sensitive to changing relative feature scales.", "whyPool": "Changing relative feature scales can change neighbor selection."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Standardization is mandatory for every machine-learning algorithm. 74 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Standardization is not mandatory for every algorithm — the need depends on the learner and representation.", "whyPool": "The need for scaling depends on the learner and representation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Removing an unnecessary scaling step may simplify the tree’s production pipeline without materially changing its predictions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Removing an unnecessary scaling step can simplify the tree's pipeline without materially changing predictions.", "whyPool": "Unneeded preprocessing adds operational complexity without a corresponding modeling benefit."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because trees usually do not require scaling, they are immune to all feature-quality problems.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees not needing scaling does not make them immune to noise, leakage, irrelevant inputs, or other data problems.", "whyPool": "Trees remain vulnerable to noise, leakage, irrelevant inputs, and other data problems."}], "reference": "Scale-invariance of trees vs. distance-based models — scikit-learn \"Preprocessing\" & \"Decision Trees\"; pipeline hygiene (`sklearn.pipeline`).", "n": 79}, {"id": "ML-D3G2Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A global macro desk trains a distance-based model across rolling market windows. Volatility rises sharply in the test period, and an analyst proposes normalizing every observation using the mean and standard deviation of its entire window, including later test dates. The PM wants current observations to be comparable without introducing hindsight. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Using later test observations to determine earlier scaling values introduces future distribution information into preprocessing.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded: \"The following claim is *false*: using later test obs to set earlier scaling introduces future info.\" The inner claim is accurate, so declaring it false is itself incorrect — full-window scaling *does* leak future distribution info.", "whyPool": "Earlier transformed values would depend on data not then available."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Applying training-period scaling parameters to later observations preserves the original train/test boundary.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: applying *training-period* scaling parameters to later observations preserves the train/test boundary (the rule was learned without seeing them).", "whyPool": "The test data are transformed using a preprocessing rule learned without seeing them."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A large standardized value in the test period can legitimately indicate that the market moved beyond the training distribution.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a large standardized test value can legitimately signal a genuine distribution shift beyond the training range — not a preprocessing error.", "whyPool": "The extreme value can reflect a genuine distribution shift rather than a preprocessing error."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Recomputing a scaler using the full test period is safe because scaling does not use target returns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: recomputing a scaler over the full test period is *not* safe — leakage occurs through future feature statistics even without using the target.", "whyPool": "Leakage can occur through future feature statistics even without direct use of the target."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A causal rolling scaler may use observations available before the decision time, provided its window is defined consistently.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a causal rolling scaler using only pre-decision observations (a consistently defined backward window) is reproducible at deployment.", "whyPool": "A backward-looking window can be reproduced at deployment time. 75 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Leakage via preprocessing statistics — scikit-learn \"Common pitfalls: data leakage\" (fit scaler on train only, `Pipeline`); look-ahead bias in time-series.", "n": 80}, {"id": "ML-D3G2Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A multi-strategy hedge fund utilizes a machine learning model to predict weekly changes in corporate credit spreads. The input features include various macroeconomic indices and fundamental corporate ratios. Before feeding these features into a gradient-boosted decision tree (GBDT) classifier, a data engineering pipeline automatically standardizes the feature matrix using a standard Z-score transformation based on a rolling 36-month window. During a sudden market-wide liquidity freeze, the model unexpectedly experiences a massive drop in precision. An investigation shows that while the absolute values of the macroeconomic indicators spiked to unprecedented levels, their transformed Z-score feature values remained capped within historically normal boundaries. Which of the following statements correctly diagnoses the statistical flaw in this standardization pipeline and identifies the appropriate structural remedy?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The Z-score standardization process mathematically failed because a rolling 36-month window is fundamentally too short for the Central Limit Theorem to hold, which prevents the feature distributions from properly converging to a normal Gaussian bell curve.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: the CLT concerns the distribution of sample *means*, not standardizing individual points; ~156 weekly obs is ample for parameter estimation.", "whyPool": "The Central Limit Theorem applies to the distribution of sample means, not the standardization of individual feature data points. Furthermore, 36 months of weekly data (~156 observations) is statistically sufficient for standard parameter estimation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The standardization pipeline suffered from Outlier Truncation Bias, which is an inherent limitation of linear transformations where any feature value that falls more than three standard deviations away from the rolling mean is automatically replaced with a maximum value of +3.0.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: standard z-score does not automatically truncate/cap at ±3.0 — that would require an added clipping/winsorization step.", "whyPool": "Standard Z-score transformation does not automatically truncate or cap extreme values at +3.0 or −3.0. Such limits would occur only if the pipeline included an additional clipping or winsorization step."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The pipeline failed because standardizing time-series features using a rolling mean and standard deviation alters the rank-order of the observations within each window, which destroys the GBDT classifier's ability to calculate optimal split thresholds.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: z-scoring is monotonic and preserves within-window rank order; tree models are invariant to monotonic per-feature transforms anyway.", "whyPool": "Linear transformations like Z-scoring are monotonic, meaning they preserve the exact rank-order of the data points within that window. Tree-based models are invariant to monotonic transformations of individual features."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The transformation artificially suppressed the extreme market shock because calculating a rolling Z-score under conditions of rapidly expanding volatility causes the denominator (the rolling standard deviation) to balloon, which systematically compresses the magnitude of the anomalous feature spikes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: in a volatility spike the rolling σ (denominator) balloons, deflating the z-score and masking the anomaly's true severity — a classic non-stationary-standardization pitfall.", "whyPool": "This is a classic pitfall when standardizing non-stationary financial data. When an extreme regime shift occurs, the rolling standard deviation (σ) increases rapidly. This expanding denominator deflates the resulting Z-score, masking the true severity of the anomaly and preventing the model from recognizing that the market has entered an unprecedented tail-risk state. 76 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Replacing the rolling standard deviation with a scale estimate fitted only on calm historical data would guarantee reliable anomaly detection in every future market regime.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a fixed calm-period scaler avoids denominator expansion but no scaling choice can *guarantee* reliable behavior under every future distribution shift.", "whyPool": "A fixed historical scaler avoids contemporaneous denominator expansion, but no scaling choice can guarantee reliable behavior under every future distribution shift."}], "reference": "Standardization under non-stationarity / denominator inflation — scikit-learn \"Preprocessing\" (StandardScaler caveats); Marcos López de Prado *Advances in Financial ML* (stationarity/fractional differencing).", "n": 81}, {"id": "ML-D3G2Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A systematic macro fund runs a multi-asset allocation model that processes global macroeconomic indices. The data engineering team notes that many of these indices (such as Industrial Production or Retail Sales) exhibit exponential structural growth trends over long horizons. To prepare these features for a deep learning model, the team implements a preprocessing step that centers and scales each feature vector by subtracting its historical mean and dividing by its standard deviation, computed over the entire training set. Which of the following statements correctly diagnoses how this scaling method fails during production, and contrasts it with how the unscaled data would behave?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that the standardization fails because subtracting the mean from an exponentially growing series introduces a look-ahead bias, as the historical mean is structurally higher than any data point observed in the first half of the dataset.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Embedded: \"It is incorrect to conclude that standardization fails *because of look-ahead bias*.\" The inner leakage claim is inaccurate — the failure is a distribution-shift issue, not an accidental data leak — so rejecting it is correct.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is inaccurate, so rejecting it is correct. While computing a global mean across the entire dataset during backtesting can cause look-ahead bias if leaked into the training slice, the fundamental statistical failure of standardization on an untransformed trend is a distribution shift issue, not an accidental data leak. (Ref: Handbook of AI Ch. 5.1)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "The standardization fails because an exponential trend means the mean and standard deviation are constantly drifting upward; as time moves forward into production, the standardized features will continuously grow larger and drift into regions the model never saw during training.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: with an exponential trend the mean/σ drift upward, so standardized production values keep growing into regions never seen in training (distribution shift).", "whyPool": "Because the underlying series is non-stationary and grows exponentially, the mean and standard deviation calculated from the training set become increasingly unrepresentative of future observations. As the series continues growing in production, its standardized values move progressively beyond the range encountered during training, creating an increasingly severe distribution shift."}, {"L": "C", "text": "On unscaled data, a deep learning model would automatically ignore the exponential trend because neural networks are naturally invariant to monotonic shifts in feature levels over time.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: deep nets are *not* invariant to unscaled trending inputs — on raw exponential data gradients explode/saturate, destabilizing training from the start.", "whyPool": "Deep learning models (unlike tree-based models) are highly sensitive to raw, unscaled inputs with large variations in scale. On unscaled data with an exponential trend, the gradients would explode or saturate early in training, rendering the network completely unstable from day one. 77 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "The unscaled data would remain stable during a regime shift because raw macroeconomic indices are bounded by global economic capacity, preventing them from drifting into uncalibrated territories.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: raw macro indices are not mean-reverting/bounded — they grow with inflation and expansion, which is exactly why engineers try to scale them.", "whyPool": "Raw macroeconomic indicators are not mean-reverting; they grow with inflation and economic expansion. They do not remain stable, which is precisely why engineers attempt to scale them in the first place."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The scaled pipeline breaks down because standardizing a non-stationary series changes the relative ranking of the historical data points, a distortion that would not occur on the unscaled data and which paralyzes the model's pattern recognition.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: z-scoring is linear and preserves rank order; the breakdown is out-of-distribution values in production, not a distortion of past rankings.", "whyPool": "Standard Z-score standardization is a linear transformation. It shifts and scales the data but preserves the exact rank-order of the points. The breakdown is caused by out-of-distribution values in production, not a distortion of past relative rankings."}], "reference": "Distribution shift from non-stationary features; gradient stability of NN inputs — scikit-learn \"Preprocessing\"; López de Prado (stationarity), ISLR §2.2.", "n": 82}, {"id": "ML-D3G2Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A cross-sectional alpha model uses the Book-to-Market (B/M) Ratio as a key value feature to rank a universe of 500 equities. The raw B/M values are heavily right-skewed because a small number of deeply distressed companies exhibit exceptionally high ratios. To prevent these extreme values from dominating the model's loss function, the production pipeline passes the raw cross-sectional ratios through a Quantile Transformation (mapping the empirical distribution to a uniform distribution between 0.0 and 1.0) before feeding them into a neural network. Which of the following statements correctly diagnoses the mathematical side-effects of this transformation during a structural market regime shift, and contrasts it with how the unscaled data would behave? For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The transformation fails because mapping data to a uniform quantile distribution changes the fundamental rank-order of the equities within that specific cross-section, a distortion that would not occur on the raw, unscaled B/M features.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: quantile mapping is monotonic (percentile-based) and *preserves* the cross-sectional rank order — it does not change it.", "whyPool": "Quantile mapping is a monotonic transformation based on percentiles. It preserves the exact relative rank-order of the assets within the cross-section."}, {"L": "B", "text": "During a market-wide systemic crash where all asset prices plummet and raw B/M ratios double across the board, the quantile-transformed features will still output a fixed range from 0.0 to 1.0, completely masking the absolute macro destruction from the neural network.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: in a market-wide crash absolute values spike but relative percentiles stay identical, so the [0,1] output masks the macro destruction — a real blind spot of rank-based normalization.", "whyPool": "This is a major structural blind spot of purely cross-sectional, rank-based normalization. If the entire market enters a severe tail-risk state, the absolute values spike, but the relative percentiles remain identical. The scaled pipeline strips out the macro signal, whereas unscaled data would immediately show the network that the entire distribution has shifted. 78 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "On unscaled data, the neural network would remain completely unaffected by the absolute magnitude of the right- skewed tail because backpropagation automatically cancels out the variance of extreme outliers during gradient descent updates.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: on unscaled right-skewed data, extreme outliers dominate MSE and make gradients explode — backprop does not \"cancel out\" their variance.", "whyPool": "On unscaled, heavily right-skewed data, extreme outliers generate massive errors that dominate the mean squared error (MSE) loss function. This causes the gradients to explode during backpropagation, destabilizing the network's weights."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The quantile pipeline breaks down during a crisis because passing raw values that exceed the training set's historical maximum into an empirical cumulative distribution function (ECDF) causes the transformation equation to yield complex numbers.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: an ECDF maps real inputs to [0,1] and cannot produce complex/imaginary numbers, however extreme the outlier.", "whyPool": "An empirical cumulative distribution function maps real inputs to a real probability scale [0,1]. It cannot mathematically produce imaginary or complex numbers, regardless of how extreme the input outlier is."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Unlike Min-Max scaling, which would output values far greater than 1.0 when processing unprecedented raw spikes during a crash, the Quantile Transformation compresses all extreme tail-risk anomalies strictly into the 1.0 boundary bucket, making a severe outlier look identical to a mild one.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: unlike Min-Max (which exceeds 1.0 on new maxima), the quantile transform caps extremes at the 1.0 bucket — removing the out-of-bounds issue but creating an information blackout that makes a severe outlier look like a mild one.", "whyPool": "This highlights the core contrast. While Min-Max scaling extends past 1.0 into uncalibrated territory when encountering new maximums, a Quantile Transformation caps the value at the 1.0 ceiling (the 100th percentile). It removes the mathematical out-of-bounds issue but replaces it with an information blackout, blinding the model to the true severity of the extreme tail event. G3: Feature Relevance and Redundancy"}], "reference": "QuantileTransformer vs. MinMaxScaler behavior on tails — scikit-learn \"Preprocessing\" (non-linear/quantile transforms); rank-based normalization limitations.", "n": 83}, {"id": "ML-D3G3Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A hedge fund adds hundreds of randomly generated columns to a modest dataset because the PM believes a capable learner will simply ignore anything useless. The validation results vary widely across splits, and the risk team questions whether the expanded feature set is helping. The model must remain stable on unseen markets.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Irrelevant features can create opportunities for a flexible learner to fit accidental patterns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Irrelevant features give a flexible learner more chances to fit accidental (spurious) patterns.", "whyPool": "Flexible models may mistake random sample associations for useful signal."}, {"L": "B", "text": "With finite data, adding random inputs can increase variance in model selection and evaluation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With finite data, adding random inputs increases variance in model selection and evaluation.", "whyPool": "More candidate relationships can make fitted models less stable."}, {"L": "C", "text": "An irrelevant feature is guaranteed to receive zero influence from every learning algorithm.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "An irrelevant feature is *not* guaranteed zero influence — many algorithms assign it weight due to sampling variation.", "whyPool": "Many algorithms can assign influence to noise because of sampling variation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing held-out performance with and without the random features can help assess their effect.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing held-out performance with vs. without the random features tests their effect on generalization.", "whyPool": "Out-of-sample comparison tests whether the additions improve generalization."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A feature’s presence is justified whenever it is correlated with the target in the training sample.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Training-sample correlation with the target may be accidental, unstable, or leakage-driven — not sufficient justification.", "whyPool": "Training correlation may be accidental, unstable, or caused by leakage."}], "reference": "Curse of dimensionality / spurious features — ISLR §6.1 (why fewer features help), §2.2.2; ESL §7.", "n": 84}, {"id": "ML-D3G3Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A bank predicts default risk using both debt-to-equity and a second field that is an almost exact duplicate supplied by another vendor. A compliance stakeholder wants every available variable retained, while the modeling lead worries that redundant inputs complicate interpretation. The team is comparing linear and tree-based learners. 79 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Highly correlated features may contain overlapping rather than independent information.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Highly correlated features carry overlapping rather than independent information.", "whyPool": "Similar variables may largely repeat the same underlying signal."}, {"L": "B", "text": "In a linear model, strong collinearity can make individual coefficient estimates unstable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "In a linear model, strong collinearity makes individual coefficient estimates unstable.", "whyPool": "Small data changes can shift attribution among correlated predictors."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Two redundant features necessarily double the model’s predictive information.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Two redundant features do not double predictive information — duplication adds no independent signal.", "whyPool": "Duplication does not create an independent signal."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A tree may choose one of several similar features, causing feature-importance assignments to vary across samples.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A tree may pick one of several similar features, so feature-importance attributions vary across samples.", "whyPool": "Similar split candidates can substitute for one another and destabilize attribution."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Removing one redundant variable must always reduce out-of-sample accuracy.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Removing a redundant variable does not *always* reduce out-of-sample accuracy — it may leave it unchanged or improve stability.", "whyPool": "Removal may leave performance unchanged or even improve stability."}], "reference": "Multicollinearity effects & tree importance splitting — ISLR §3.3.3 (collinearity, VIF); scikit-learn \"Permutation importance with multicollinear features.\"", "n": 85}, {"id": "ML-D3G3Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A commodity fund finds that warehouse inventory and weeks-of-supply are strongly correlated, but the latter also incorporates expected demand. The portfolio manager proposes dropping one automatically based on the correlation coefficient. The analyst argues that redundancy should be judged in the context of the prediction task. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Strong pairwise correlation does not prove that two features are interchangeable for prediction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded: \"The following claim is *false*: strong correlation does not prove interchangeability.\" The inner claim is accurate (correlated features can differ in nonlinear/conditional behavior), so calling it false is incorrect.", "whyPool": "The variables may differ in nonlinear behavior, noise, or conditional relationships."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A feature can add conditional information even when it is strongly correlated with another feature.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a feature can add conditional information even when strongly correlated with another — residual differences may still predict the target.", "whyPool": "The residual differences may still help predict the target."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Correlation between predictors establishes that one of them is causally irrelevant.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: predictor correlation alone does not establish that one feature is causally irrelevant.", "whyPool": "Predictor correlation alone does not establish causality or irrelevance. 80 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing validated models can help determine whether removing one feature sacrifices useful information.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: comparing validated models reveals whether removing one feature sacrifices useful (generalizable) information.", "whyPool": "Out-of-sample evidence can reveal whether the feature adds generalizable value."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Operational cost and data reliability can be considered when choosing between largely redundant features.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: with similar predictive value, operational cost, latency, and data reliability can decide between redundant features.", "whyPool": "Similar predictive value can make cost, latency, and reliability decisive."}], "reference": "Correlation vs. causation / conditional information — ISLR §3.3.3; feature-selection tradeoffs (cost/reliability) — production ML practice.", "n": 86}, {"id": "ML-D3G3Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative portfolio team adds 50 new technical indicators—mostly subtle variations of rolling momentum and moving average crossovers—to an existing ensemble model trading liquid futures. While the training and backtest performance metrics improve marginally, the model's live execution profile displays a significant drop in risk-adjusted returns and an inflation of transaction costs due to high portfolio turnover. An audit reveals that many of these new inputs are highly collinear. Which of the following statements correctly diagnoses the mathematical and operational impact of introducing these redundant features into the model?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Tree-based models like Random Forests fail completely when presented with collinear features because multicollinearity forces the mathematical inversion of the feature covariance matrix to fail, preventing the calculation of optimal split thresholds.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: trees do not invert a covariance matrix to split — they evaluate features sequentially; collinearity degrades interpretability but does not make them \"fail completely.\"", "whyPool": "Tree-based models do not invert a covariance matrix to calculate splits; they evaluate individual features sequentially. They do not \"fail completely\" mathematically, though their interpretability degrades."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The inclusion of highly correlated momentum indicators dilutes the feature importance metrics (such as Mean Decrease Impurity), splitting the credit across redundant inputs and obscuring the truly dominant structural signals from the risk-management team.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: correlated indicators dilute Mean-Decrease-Impurity importance — the ensemble randomly uses one or another across nodes, splitting the credit and hiding dominant signals.", "whyPool": "When two features carry identical information, a tree ensemble will randomly use one or the other across different nodes. This splits their apparent importance, making two critical, highly correlated features look like two mediocre ones."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Collinear features artificially narrow the variance of the model's predictions during market shocks, causing the algorithm to underestimate portfolio risk and over-allocate capital to highly correlated asset groups.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: collinearity typically *increases* out-of-sample prediction variance via unstable estimation, not artificially narrows prediction variance.", "whyPool": "Collinearity typically increases prediction variance out-of-sample due to unstable parameter estimation, rather than artificially narrowing the variance of the predictions themselves. 81 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "In a linear regression framework, highly correlated features can make the individual coefficient estimates unstable and sensitive to small changes in the training data, even when the model’s overall predictions remain relatively stable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: in linear regression, high collinearity makes individual coefficients unstable and data-sensitive even when overall predictions stay relatively stable.", "whyPool": "Multicollinearity is a severe violation in linear frameworks. It destabilizes the ordinary least squares (OLS) math because the columns of the feature matrix are nearly linearly dependent, leading to massive swings in beta weights given tiny changes in the data."}, {"L": "E", "text": "To systematically eliminate these redundant features without discarding the underlying directional signal, the team should pass the collinear matrix through a Principal Component Analysis (PCA) pipeline and retain only the top orthogonal components.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: PCA projects correlated features into orthogonal components, isolating core variance and stripping collinear redundancy.", "whyPool": "PCA projects the correlated features into a new coordinate system where the resulting components are orthogonal (uncorrelated) by construction, isolating the core variance while stripping out collinear redundancy."}], "reference": "MDI dilution under correlation; OLS instability; PCA decorrelation — scikit-learn \"Permutation importance with multicollinear features,\" \"PCA\"; ISLR §3.3.3, §6.3 (PCA/PCR).", "n": 87}, {"id": "ML-D3G3Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A multi-strategy quant desk is optimizing an asset-allocation model that uses a collection of 15 macroeconomic sentiment indices. A statistical analysis reveals that 4 of these indices share a correlation coefficient of r>0.92 with each other. Rather than dropping any variables, the team decides to train two different model structures to compare how they handle this redundancy: a Random Forest regressor and an unregularized OLS Linear Regression. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates how these two models behave when processing this redundant feature set? For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The unregularized OLS model will remain stable because highly correlated features do not increase the variance or instability of the individual coefficient estimates.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: sample size does not cure multicollinearity — OLS estimates stay extremely sensitive to minor data variation regardless of N.", "whyPool": "Sample size does not cure multicollinearity. When features are highly correlated, the OLS parameter estimates become extremely sensitive to minor variations in the data, leading to high variance regardless of sample size."}, {"L": "B", "text": "In the Random Forest model, the highly correlated features will dilute each other's calculated feature importance metrics (e.g., Mean Decrease Impurity), as the tree ensemble randomly substitutes one redundant feature for another across different split nodes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the RF splits node selections across the ~identical indices, lowering each one's importance and making a predictive group look trivial on an importance plot.", "whyPool": "Because the 4 sentiment indices carry nearly identical structural information, a tree ensemble will split the node selections among them somewhat evenly. This artificially lowers the individual importance score of each index, making a highly predictive group of variables look trivial on an importance plot."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The OLS model's out-of-sample predictions will become highly unstable because severe multicollinearity causes the feature covariance matrix to approach singularity, dramatically inflating the standard errors of the regression coefficients.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: severe multicollinearity inflates coefficient variance/standard errors, which can make OLS out-of-sample predictions unstable — especially if correlations shift between train and test.", "whyPool": "Severe multicollinearity inflates the variance and standard errors of the individual coefficient estimates, which can make the OLS model’s out-of-sample predictions unstable—particularly when the relationships among the correlated features change between the training and test data. 82 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "The Random Forest model's out-of-sample predictive accuracy will collapse to zero because tree-based architectures cannot mathematically calculate a split threshold when two feature vectors are nearly collinear.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: RFs split sequentially/greedily and do not invert a covariance matrix; collinearity degrades importance interpretability but does not break the splitting math or collapse accuracy to zero.", "whyPool": "Random Forests split on features sequentially and greedily; they do not invert a covariance matrix. Collinearity degrades the interpretability of the tree's feature importances, but it does not cause the splitting mathematics to fail or break down."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Unlike the OLS model, which suffers from variance inflation when redundant features are present, the Random Forest model naturally handles the redundancy by automatically isolating and ignoring all but one of the highly correlated features during the bootstrap sampling phase.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: RFs do not auto-isolate one of a correlated set — bootstrap samples *rows*, not features; a random feature subset is considered per split, so redundant features are used throughout and dilute importances.", "whyPool": "Random Forests do not automatically isolate or ignore all but one of a set of correlated features. Bootstrap sampling selects observations, not features, while a random subset of features is considered at each split. Consequently, different redundant features may be used throughout the ensemble, diluting their individual importance scores."}], "reference": "Bootstrap samples rows not features; multicollinearity persists with large N — scikit-learn \"Random Forest,\" \"Permutation importance with multicollinear features\"; ISLR §3.3.3, ch.8.", "n": 88}, {"id": "ML-D3G3Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A data scientist is constructing a machine learning pipeline to trade a cross-section of global equities. The initial feature pool contains 150 technical and fundamental factors. To optimize the feature space, the pipeline employs a Sequential Forward Selection (SFS) wrapper algorithm to iteratively add features that maximize the model's out-of-sample Sharpe ratio. The final selected subset contains 12 features and achieves an exceptional Sharpe ratio in the historical backtest, but when deployed in live trading, the strategy experiences severe drawdown and underperforms the baseline model that used all 150 features. Which of the following statements correctly diagnoses the statistical flaw in this feature selection process? For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "SFS is a deterministic greedy algorithm that evaluates features sequentially, meaning it is fundamentally incapable of capturing interaction effects where two features are only predictive when selected together.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: SFS adds features one at a time and can miss interaction effects where two features help only together; a feature rejected early is not reconsidered with later additions.", "whyPool": "Because SFS evaluates and adds features one at a time, it may miss interaction effects when two features are useful together but neither provides sufficient predictive value individually. Once a feature is rejected at an early stage, standard SFS does not reconsider it in combination with features added later."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The collapse in performance is a result of Selection Bias (Overfitting the Backtest), where the iterative wrapper search treats the backtest performance as an optimization objective, essentially \"mining\" the historical noise for a subset that fits that specific period perfectly.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the collapse is selection bias / backtest overfitting — the wrapper treats backtest performance as the objective and \"mines\" historical noise for a lucky subset.", "whyPool": "This is a massive trap in quantitative finance. By running a wrapper algorithm over thousands of feature combinations against a fixed backtest, you are performing a combinatorial search. The algorithm will easily find a subset that fits the historical data beautifully by pure chance, leading to severe out-of-sample disappointment. 83 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "SFS artificially alters the joint probability distribution of the feature matrix, violating the stationarity assumptions required by downstream machine learning architectures.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: selecting a column subset does not change the underlying joint distribution of those variables — it just discards others; the failure is over-optimizing on noise, not a distributional alteration.", "whyPool": "Selecting a subset of columns from a matrix does not change the underlying joint probability distribution of those specific variables; it merely discards the other columns. The failure is due to over-optimizing on noise, not a structural alteration of the data distribution."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Wrapper methods like SFS are structurally flawed because they optimize features independently of the downstream model's internal hyperparameter settings, creating an uncalibrated feature-model mismatch.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: wrapper methods evaluate subsets using the *exact* downstream model (they are directly tuned to it) — the opposite of \"optimizing independently of hyperparameters.\"", "whyPool": "Wrapper methods actually do the opposite: they evaluate feature subsets using the exact downstream model being trained. They are directly tuned to the model, though they remain prone to overfitting the validation data."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The failure occurred because a greedy wrapper algorithm will always over-allocate weight to low-variance, irrelevant features, completely missing the high-variance regimes that dominate live market returns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: greedy SFS does not favor low-variance features by default — it picks whatever maximizes the objective (Sharpe); failure is noise capitalization, not a low-variance bias.", "whyPool": "SFS does not favor low-variance features by default; it greedily selects whatever feature maximizes the objective function (in this case, the Sharpe ratio). The failure is driven by the capitalization on historical noise, not an inherent bias toward low variance. G4: Feature Selection Intuition"}], "reference": "Wrapper-method selection bias / multiple-testing on backtests — ISLR §6.1 (subset selection, validation), ch.5; López de Prado (backtest overfitting, deflated Sharpe).", "withdrawn": true, "n": 89}, {"id": "ML-D3G4Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A small-cap strategy has only three years of weekly observations but several hundred candidate accounting and sentiment features. The PM wants to keep all variables to avoid missing a signal, while the research lead fears that the model will search through too many accidental relationships. The final model must perform reliably outside the development period.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Reducing the feature space can lower the number of spurious patterns available to the learner.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Reducing the feature space lowers the number of spurious patterns available to the learner.", "whyPool": "Fewer inputs can constrain the learner’s opportunity to fit noise."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Feature selection can be especially valuable when the number of candidate features is large relative to the observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Feature selection is especially valuable when candidate features are large relative to observations.", "whyPool": "Limited observations make reliable estimation across many predictors difficult."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Selecting features based on the full dataset, including the test period, preserves an unbiased final test.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Selecting features using the full dataset (incl. the test period) contaminates the final test — it does not preserve an unbiased evaluation.", "whyPool": "Test-period information would influence model development."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Removing features can reduce overfitting but may also discard genuine signal.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Removing features reduces overfitting but may also discard genuine signal (a tradeoff).", "whyPool": "Selection trades reduced complexity against possible information loss."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The feature subset with the lowest training error is necessarily the best choice.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The lowest-training-error subset is not necessarily best — training fit does not measure generalization and favors complexity.", "whyPool": "Training fit does not measure generalization and often favors greater complexity. 84 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Feature selection when p≫n; validation hygiene — ISLR §6.1, ch.5 (cross-validation); curse of dimensionality.", "n": 90}, {"id": "ML-D3G4Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A trading firm ranks 1,000 features by their correlation with next-day returns, retains the top 20, and reports performance on the same historical observations used for ranking. The head of research says the final learner used only 20 inputs, so overfitting is no longer a concern. The validation officer challenges the process. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Searching across 1,000 candidates can overfit the feature-selection step even if the final model uses only 20.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded: \"The following claim is *false*: searching 1,000 candidates can overfit the selection step even if the final model uses 20.\" The inner claim is accurate, so declaring it false is incorrect — the search itself can pick chance-correlated predictors.", "whyPool": "The search can select predictors that correlated with the target by chance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Evaluating the selected subset on the same observations used to rank features can produce optimistic results.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: evaluating the selected subset on the same observations used to rank features produces optimistic results.", "whyPool": "The evaluation rewards the same sample-specific relationships used for selection."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Feature selection is outside the modeling process and therefore does not need validation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: feature selection is a learned preprocessing decision and *must* be validated (contained within the CV loop), not treated as outside the model.", "whyPool": "Selection is a learned preprocessing decision and must be contained within validation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Selection should be repeated within each training fold when cross-validation is used to estimate performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: selection should be repeated within each training fold so each validation fold stays uninvolved in choosing its features.", "whyPool": "Each validation fold must remain uninvolved in choosing its model’s features."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A final untouched test period can provide a more independent assessment after the selection procedure is fixed.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a final untouched test period gives a more independent assessment once the selection procedure is frozen.", "whyPool": "An untouched test period evaluates the complete frozen development process."}], "reference": "Selection-inside-CV / leakage from feature ranking — scikit-learn \"Common pitfalls: data leakage,\" \"Nested cross-validation\"; ISLR ch.5, §6.1.", "n": 91}, {"id": "ML-D3G4Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A pension manager wants a transparent risk model that can be maintained by a small team. A 12-feature model performs nearly as well as a 150-feature model in rolling validation, but several of the 150 inputs require expensive alternative-data feeds. The team must choose between a small accuracy difference and operational simplicity.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Similar validated performance can favor the smaller model when cost and maintainability matter.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Similar validated performance can favor the smaller model when cost and maintainability matter.", "whyPool": "Model choice can reflect operational objectives as well as predictive performance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Fewer features can reduce exposure to feed failures and future changes in data availability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Fewer features reduce exposure to feed failures and future changes in data availability.", "whyPool": "Each external input introduces another dependency and possible failure mode."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The larger feature set is automatically superior because it contains the smaller set.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The larger set is not automatically superior for containing the smaller one — extra inputs add noise, instability, cost, and overfitting risk.", "whyPool": "More inputs can add noise, instability, cost, and overfitting risk."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Removing features may improve interpretability by reducing the number of inputs stakeholders must understand.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Fewer inputs improve interpretability — fewer variables for stakeholders to understand.", "whyPool": "A compact representation is generally easier to inspect and explain."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Feature selection decisions should ignore deployment constraints if predictive accuracy differs at all.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A small accuracy difference should not override deployment constraints; the gain may not justify cost/fragility.", "whyPool": "A small performance gain may not justify significant cost or fragility."}], "reference": "Parsimony / operational-cost tradeoffs — ISLR §6.1 (model interpretability); production ML practice (data-dependency risk).", "n": 92}, {"id": "ML-D3G4Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A data scientist is managing an alpha-generation pipeline that contains 80 technical, fundamental, and alternative data features. The downstream model is a regularized linear framework optimized to predict next-week asset returns. To combat out-of-sample decay, the scientist decides to restrict the model's feature space. They implement a strict feature- control protocol that uses a tree-based feature-importance threshold to prune the feature matrix down to the top 15 variables before fitting the linear framework. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the statistical benefits and tradeoffs of reducing the feature space in this manner?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Reducing the feature space from 80 to 15 variables decreases the model's estimation variance because estimating fewer parameters prevents the optimization algorithm from fitting the idiosyncratic noise present in the training data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: fewer parameters reduce estimation variance — the linear model focuses on robust relationships rather than high-dimensional noise.", "whyPool": "Limiting the number of inputs is a core mechanism for controlling overfitting. With fewer parameters to estimate, the linear framework focuses on dominant, statistically robust relationships rather than chasing high-dimensional noise."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Shrinking the feature space improves the model's out-of-sample performance across all possible market regimes because removing variables mathematically guarantees a reduction in the model's structural bias.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: shrinking the feature space does not reduce bias — constraining inputs typically *increases* bias (less flexibility).", "whyPool": "Reducing the feature space does not reduce bias; it typically increases it. By restricting the model's inputs, you constrain its flexibility, meaning it is less capable of modeling complex real-world trends perfectly."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The primary risk of this pruning strategy is the potential introduction of omitted variable bias, where dropping 65 features might inadvertently discard subtle, weak-signal variables that are vital for capturing multi-factor market dynamics.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the classic risk is omitted-variable bias — dropping 65 features may discard weak-but-collectively-useful signals, causing underfit.", "whyPool": "This is the classic tradeoff of feature selection. While pruning clears out noise, it risks discarding \"weak predictors\" that may collectively contain meaningful predictive power when combined, leading to an underfit model. 86 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Using a non-linear tree-based model to select features for a downstream linear framework is structurally flawed because tree-based feature selection permanently alters the rank-order of the remaining features, invalidating OLS parameter estimation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: tree-based selection does not alter the rank-order/values of the data and does not invalidate downstream OLS estimation.", "whyPool": "Tree-based feature selection does not alter the rank-order or values of the underlying data points. While mixing model architectures requires care regarding feature interactions, it does not mathematically invalidate the downstream parameter estimation."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Restricting the feature space limits the model's capacity to overfit, but it introduces a strict tradeoff where the training error (in-sample loss) will typically increase as the model's flexibility is constrained.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: restricting capacity limits overfitting but usually raises in-sample (training) error — the bias-variance tradeoff in action.", "whyPool": "This is the fundamental bias-variance tradeoff in action. Restricting the feature space reduces model capacity, which almost always results in higher in-sample error in exchange for more stable, lower-variance out-of-sample predictions."}], "reference": "Bias-variance of feature reduction; omitted-variable bias — ISLR §6.1, §2.2.2; feature selection via tree importance (scikit-learn `SelectFromModel`).", "n": 93}, {"id": "ML-D3G4Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A multi-asset hedge fund deploys a high-dimensional neural network to predict next-day currency movements using a raw pool of 300 macroeconomic, sentiment, and order-flow features. To control overfitting and enhance the model's signal-to-noise ratio, a quantitative analyst proposes a pre-processing step: compress the 300 features into 10 orthogonal factors using Principal Component Analysis (PCA) and train the neural network exclusively on these 10 components. Which of the following statements correctly diagnoses the mathematical tradeoffs and structural risks of this feature- reduction protocol?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Compressing the feature space via PCA eliminates the risk of out-of-sample performance decay because projecting data onto orthogonal axes mathematically prevents the neural network from memorizing noise.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: PCA controls collinearity/dimensionality but does not eliminate overfitting — a deep net can still overfit 10 components if its capacity is unconstrained.", "whyPool": "While PCA controls collinearity and reduces dimensionality, it does not \"eliminate\" the risk of overfitting. A deep neural network can still easily overfit the 10 remaining principal components if its capacity (layers/nodes) is unconstrained relative to the sample size."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The primary tradeoff of this approach is that PCA is an unsupervised technique; the 10 components are selected because they maximize variance within the feature space, meaning PCA might completely discard low-variance features that are highly predictive of the target currency movements.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: PCA is unsupervised (maximizes X-variance, ignores y), so it may discard a low-variance direction that is highly predictive of the target.", "whyPool": "This is the classic pitfall of unsupervised dimensionality reduction in a supervised learning pipeline. PCA looks only at the features (X) and knows nothing about the target (y). A low-variance regime or subtle alternative data signal might be discarded as \"noise\" by PCA, even though it holds the highest predictive power for currency movements."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Using PCA components as inputs causes the neural network's gradient descent updates to fail because orthogonal inputs eliminate the cross-covariance required by backpropagation to calculate weight adjustments.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: orthogonal inputs *help* NN training — decorrelation reduces ill-conditioning of the loss surface and aids gradient-descent convergence.", "whyPool": "Orthogonal features actually optimize the training dynamics of neural networks. Eliminating multicollinearity in the input space reduces the ill-conditioning of the loss surface, helping gradient descent converge more efficiently. 87 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "By reducing the input dimensions from 300 to 10, the analyst lowers the model's estimation variance, creating a more stable, parsimonious model that is less likely to overfit the historical data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: reducing 300→10 dimensions lowers estimation variance, yielding a more parsimonious model less prone to overfit.", "whyPool": "Compressing the feature space severely restricts the flexibility of the input layer. By forcing the network to look at a compact 10-dimensional representation instead of a sprawling 300-dimensional space, the analyst limits the model's ability to hunt for spurious, high-dimensional correlations in the training data."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A major operational tradeoff of this transformation is the loss of interpretability, as each of the 10 input factors is now a linear combination of all 300 raw features, making it difficult to attribute portfolio risk to specific real-world economic drivers.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: interpretability is lost — each component is a linear combination of all 300 raw features, so risk cannot be attributed to specific economic drivers.", "whyPool": "This is a critical risk-management tradeoff. If the model suddenly shifts its allocations, the investment committee cannot easily determine why because the features are abstract mathematical projections (e.g., \"Component 3 spiked\") rather than distinct, identifiable metrics like \"Inflation increased. \""}], "reference": "PCA as unsupervised dimensionality reduction (variance ≠ predictiveness); conditioning benefits — ISLR §6.3 (PCR), §12.2; scikit-learn \"PCA,\" \"Decomposition.\"", "n": 94}, {"id": "ML-D3G4Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative research team is debating how to manage a high-dimensional feature dataset consisting of 250 micro- structural order flow variables. The objective is to build an intraday equity model. One faction wants to use a subset selection wrapper (such as Best Subset Selection) to find a sparse, 20-feature model. The other faction advocates for keeping all 250 features but applying heavy Ridge Regression (L2 Regularization) to penalize the coefficients. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the tradeoffs, benefits, and structural differences between these two feature-control methodologies?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Best Subset Selection reduces the feature space to a sparse matrix, which minimizes estimation variance, but it introduces a major risk of selection variance because the combinatorial search can overfit the training data by selecting features that fit the noise by pure chance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: Best-Subset reduces estimation variance but adds *selection variance* — the combinatorial search can overfit by picking noise-fitting features by chance.", "whyPool": "This is a critical hazard of subset selection methods. When you search across a massive combination of features to maximize a performance metric (like R-squared or Sharpe ratio), you turn the feature selection process itself into an optimization problem, which heavily overfits the historical data."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Ridge Regression manages overfitting by shrinking coefficients toward zero, which reduces model flexibility and estimation variance without discarding any variables from the feature space.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: Ridge shrinks all coefficients toward zero, cutting flexibility and estimation variance while retaining every variable.", "whyPool": "Ridge Regression controls overfitting by shrinking all coefficient estimates toward zero. This reduces model flexibility and estimation variance while retaining all 250 features in the model."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Selecting a sparse 20-feature model via Best Subset Selection mathematically guarantees a lower training error (in- sample loss) than a Ridge Regression model that utilizes all 250 features.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a 250-feature Ridge model (larger functional space) will almost always achieve lower/equal *training* error than a 20-feature subset — sparsity does not guarantee lower in-sample loss.", "whyPool": "Best Subset Selection constrains the model to just 20 parameters, limiting its flexibility. A model with 250 features—even under Ridge regularization—will almost always achieve a lower or equal training error because it retains a vastly larger and more flexible functional space to fit the training data. 88 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "A major tradeoff of keeping all 250 features under Ridge Regression is computational drag during live production execution, as the model must continuously ingest, process, and calculate transformations for 230 more variables than the sparse model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: keeping 250 features under Ridge is an operational drag — production must ingest/transform 230 more variables than the sparse model.", "whyPool": "This is an operational tradeoff. While regularized dense models can be highly predictive, they require maintaining a heavy data engineering pipeline in production to feed all 250 features in real-time, whereas a sparse model drastically simplifies the production data footprint."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the underlying asset returns are truly driven by a small handful of dominant structural variables while the remaining 230 variables are pure noise, Ridge Regression will perform better out-of-sample than Best Subset Selection because L2 regularization will force the noise coefficients to exactly zero.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: if the true model is sparse, Lasso/Best-Subset generally beat Ridge — Ridge shrinks but never sets coefficients exactly to zero, so it cannot drop the noise variables.", "whyPool": "Ridge regression shrinks coefficients close to zero, but mathematically it never sets them to exactly zero. If the true data-generating process is sparse (dominated by only a few signals), Lasso (L1) or Best Subset Selection will generally outperform Ridge because Ridge cannot completely drop the noise variables. G5: Target Leakage"}], "reference": "Ridge vs. Lasso/Best-Subset (shrinkage vs. sparsity, selection variance) — ISLR §6.1–§6.2 (subset selection, ridge, lasso); ESL §3.4.", "n": 95}, {"id": "ML-D3G5Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An asset manager predicts whether a borrower will default within 90 days. One feature is the account status code recorded after the collections department closes the case, and the model achieves nearly perfect accuracy. The credit officer wants to deploy immediately, but the model-risk team questions when that code becomes available.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A post-resolution status code may encode the target outcome and create target leakage.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A post-resolution status code may encode the outcome itself → target leakage.", "whyPool": "The field may reveal the outcome rather than predict it prospectively."}, {"L": "B", "text": "High accuracy proves that the feature will be available when a new prediction is required.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: high accuracy says nothing about whether the feature is available at prediction time.", "whyPool": "Accuracy says nothing about decision-time availability."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The team should reconstruct each feature using only information available at the prediction timestamp.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Each feature should be reconstructed using only information available at the prediction timestamp (point-in-time).", "whyPool": "Point-in-time reconstruction tests the model under deployable information constraints."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Removing the leaked field will likely reduce measured performance but make the evaluation more realistic.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Removing the leaked field likely lowers measured performance but yields a more realistic, credible estimate.", "whyPool": "Unrealistic predictive power disappears, yielding a more credible estimate."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Target leakage is harmless if the leaked feature is strongly correlated with default.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: strong association *caused by outcome information* is precisely why leakage is misleading, not harmless.", "whyPool": "Strong association caused by outcome information is precisely why leakage is misleading."}], "reference": "Target leakage / point-in-time reconstruction — scikit-learn \"Common pitfalls: data leakage\"; Kaufman et al. \"Leakage in Data Mining.\"", "n": 96}, {"id": "ML-D3G5Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A brokerage predicts customer account closures using a monthly table. The table includes “days since final transaction, ” calculated after the month ends using the customer’s eventual last transaction date. A product manager argues that the feature is not the closure label itself and therefore cannot be leakage. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate. 89 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A feature can leak target information without being an exact copy of the target label.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a feature can leak target information without being an exact copy of the label.", "whyPool": "Derived variables can indirectly encode the outcome."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Using an eventual last-transaction date can reveal future customer behavior unavailable at the monthly prediction date.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: using an *eventual* last-transaction date reveals future behavior unavailable at the monthly prediction date.", "whyPool": "The calculation depends on knowledge acquired after the decision time."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Renaming the field would eliminate the leakage.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: renaming the field does not eliminate leakage — leakage depends on the underlying data and timing, not the column name.", "whyPool": "Leakage depends on the underlying data and timing, not the column name."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Auditing feature lineage and timestamps can expose how the field was calculated.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: auditing feature lineage and timestamps exposes how the field was calculated and whether it postdates the decision.", "whyPool": "Lineage reveals source events and whether they postdate the prediction."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A valid replacement could calculate days since the most recent transaction known as of each prediction date.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a valid replacement computes days since the most recent transaction *known as of* each prediction date (reproducible live).", "whyPool": "This backward-looking version can be reproduced for a live customer."}], "reference": "Derived-feature leakage & lineage auditing — scikit-learn \"data leakage\"; point-in-time feature engineering.", "n": 97}, {"id": "ML-D3G5Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quant team standardizes every feature, selects predictors, tunes a model, and then reports results. Although the raw inputs are available before each trade, the feature selector used correlations with target returns from both the training and test periods. The PM insists there is no leakage because no future-return column enters the final model.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Target information used to choose features can leak into the model-development process.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded: \"The following claim is *false*: target info used to choose features can leak into development.\" The inner claim is accurate (target-guided selection is part of fitting the pipeline), so calling it false is incorrect.", "whyPool": "Target-guided selection is part of fitting the model pipeline."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Excluding the target column from the final input matrix is sufficient to guarantee a clean evaluation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: excluding the target column is *not* sufficient — the target can influence development indirectly via selection.", "whyPool": "The target can influence development indirectly without becoming an input column."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The test-period targets influenced which predictors survived, so the reported test result is no longer independent.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: test-period targets shaped which predictors survived, so the reported test result is no longer independent.", "whyPool": "The test outcomes helped define the evaluated model."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Feature selection should be fitted on training data and then applied unchanged to the corresponding held-out data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: feature selection should be fitted on training data and applied unchanged to held-out data.", "whyPool": "This preserves the held-out role of the evaluation data."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Leakage can occur through preprocessing and selection decisions as well as through raw feature columns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: leakage can travel through preprocessing and selection decisions, not just raw feature columns.", "whyPool": "Any learned step can transmit forbidden information into the result. 90 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Leakage through selection/preprocessing; selection-inside-CV — scikit-learn \"Common pitfalls,\" \"Nested CV\"; ISLR ch.5.", "n": 98}, {"id": "ML-D3G5Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A cross-sectional alpha model uses corporate fundamental data to predict whether a company's stock will outperform its industry peers over the next quarter. One of the features engineered by the team is Relative Profitability, which scales a company's net income against the total net income of all companies within its specific GICS industry sector. The training dataset includes 800 firms. Due to an architectural oversight in the feature engineering script, the sector-wide total net income used as the denominator for each firm is calculated globally across the entire cross-section before splitting the data into cross-validation folds. Which of the following statements correctly diagnoses why this configuration suffers from Target Leakage, and contrasts it with how a properly isolated pipeline would behave?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that target leakage occurs because calculating the sector-wide denominator across the entire cross-section before splitting leaks information about the target labels of the validation firms into the feature values of the training firms.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded: \"It is incorrect to conclude that target leakage occurs because the global denominator leaks validation info into training.\" The inner claim is accurate (this *is* cross-sectional leakage via pooling), so declaring it incorrect is false.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. This is an example of cross-sectional target leakage (often called data leakage via pooling). By calculating the sector denominator globally, the training data absorbs information about the scale and performance of the validation data points, bridging the firewall between folds."}, {"L": "B", "text": "This setup creates an artificial performance boost because the global denominator contains structural information about the scale, health, and distribution of the entire peer universe, a data point that will be uncalibrated and missing if a new firm enters the production environment alone.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the global denominator embeds the scale/health/distribution of the whole peer universe — metadata that is uncalibrated/missing when a new firm is scored alone in production.", "whyPool": "The model overfits to the precise cross-sectional distribution of the historical slice. In live production, if you score a single company or a modified universe, the global denominator changes or is unavailable, causing the feature scaling to break down because the model relies on the leaked universe metadata to make its predictions."}, {"L": "C", "text": "This pipeline does not suffer from target leakage because the data leak is strictly cross-sectional rather than temporal, meaning no future-dated price information was used to construct the feature matrix.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: leakage is not exclusively temporal — absorbing info from validation/test partitions across peers is also leakage.", "whyPool": "Target leakage is not exclusively temporal. Any time a feature matrix absorbs information from the validation or testing partitions—whether across time or across peers—it constitutes leakage and compromises the validity of the backtest."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A reliable way to detect this specific leak in practice is to compare the model's performance on a completely held-out, structurally isolated out-of-universe test set against its cross-validation scores; a sharp drop in out-of-universe accuracy exposes the leak.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: comparing CV scores against a structurally isolated out-of-universe test set exposes the leak (a sharp drop reveals it).", "whyPool": "Because the leak occurs within the cross-validation loops via the shared global denominator, a completely independent out-of-universe dataset (e.g., a separate geographical market or a completely quarantined cohort) will lack this shared information, causing the model's inflated performance to immediately collapse and revealing the flaw. 91 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "To properly remediate this leak, the data engineering pipeline must be refactored so that any cross-sectional summary statistics (such as sector means or totals) are computed strictly and exclusively within the boundaries of each individual training fold.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: remediation requires computing cross-sectional summary statistics strictly within each training fold, then applying that static mapping to validation.", "whyPool": "Strict isolation requires that the parameters for any transformation must be learned solely from the training fold. The sector totals must be computed only using the training firms, and that static mapping is then applied to the validation fold to ensure zero cross-contamination."}], "reference": "Cross-sectional / pooling leakage; fit-transform within folds — scikit-learn \"Common pitfalls: data leakage,\" `Pipeline`+`cross_val_score`.", "n": 99}, {"id": "ML-D3G5Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A data scientist is building a predictive maintenance model for a high-frequency market-making architecture. The goal is to predict whether the trading system's network latency will spike above a critical threshold (y=1) during the next 5-minute interval. The data pipeline extracts a large set of system metrics. To maximize predictive accuracy, the scientist uses an embedded Lasso (L1-regularized) Regression model as a feature selection script. The script is fit on the entire historical dataset to determine which features have non-zero coefficients. These selected features are then extracted, and a deep neural network is trained on an 80/20 train/test split of the data. The neural network achieves an exceptional out-of- sample precision score. Which of the following statements correctly diagnoses why this pipeline exhibits Target Leakage, and describes its statistical consequences?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Target leakage occurs because Lasso is a linear framework; running it on non-linear network latency data causes the selected feature coefficients to absorb future residual variance from the test partition.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: Lasso's linearity does not make it \"absorb future residual variance\" — the leak is structural (pooling before the split), not a functional-form effect.", "whyPool": "Lasso's linearity does not cause it to absorb future residual variance. The leak is structural (pooling data before the split) rather than a consequence of the algorithm's functional form."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The pipeline is flawed because performing feature selection on the entire dataset before splitting allows information about the target labels and joint distributions in the test set to dictate which features are passed to the neural network.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: selecting features on the full dataset lets test-set target/joint-distribution info dictate which features reach the neural net.", "whyPool": "This is an example of target leakage through feature selection pooling. By using the entire dataset (including what will become the test set) to decide which features \"matter, \" the model selection process leaks the target-signal relationships of the test set into the training environment."}, {"L": "C", "text": "This setup creates an optimistic bias in the test metrics because the neural network is evaluated on a test set whose specific characteristics have already been used to prune and shape the feature space during the selection phase.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the test metrics are an illusion — the 20% test set helped select the features, so the net sees a space pre-optimized to fit those exact records.", "whyPool": "The resulting test metrics are a statistical illusion. Because the 20% test partition helped select the features, the neural network encounters a feature space that has been pre-optimized to fit those exact test records, inflating out- of-sample performance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The pipeline does not suffer from target leakage because Lasso is an embedded regularization technique that penalizes coefficients dynamically, which mathematically prevents information from crossing the train/test barrier.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: regularization does not prevent leakage when run on pooled data — Lasso uses whole-dataset info to shrink coefficients, bridging the split.", "whyPool": "Regularization does not prevent leakage if the algorithm is executed on the pooled data. Lasso will happily use information from the entire dataset to shrink coefficients, bridging the firewall between splits. 92 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "To properly remediate this leak, the feature selection process must be completely isolated: the Lasso script must be fit exclusively on the training partition to identify the active features, and the test partition must remain completely unseen until the final neural network is evaluated.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: remediation fits the Lasso selector on the training partition only; the test partition stays blind until final evaluation.", "whyPool": "Strict isolation requires that all model choices—including feature engineering and feature selection—must be derived solely from the training data. The test partition must act as a completely independent, blind validation check."}], "reference": "Feature-selection pooling leakage — scikit-learn \"Common pitfalls: data leakage,\" `SelectFromModel` inside a `Pipeline`; ISLR ch.5, §6.2.", "n": 100}, {"id": "ML-D3G5Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A data scientist at a systematic multi-strategy fund is auditing a machine learning pipeline designed to trade cross- sectional equity momentum. The model predicts whether a stock's 5-day forward return will rank in the top quintile of the universe (y=1) or not (y=0). The pipeline processes a universe of 600 stocks and uses a K-Fold cross-validation strategy. The auditor reviews the data engineering scripts for five different alpha signals developed for this pipeline. Four of these signals contain classic, structural implementations of target leakage, while only one is mathematically and structurally clean. Which of the following signal implementations is free of target leakage? For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that Signal Alpha: A technical momentum indicator that standardizes a stock's raw price momentum by dividing it by the cross-sectional standard deviation of momentum calculated across all 600 stocks daily, performed before rows are split into cross-validation folds.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Embedded: \"It is incorrect to conclude that Signal Alpha [cross-sectional σ over all 600 stocks before splitting] leaks.\" The inner claim is inaccurate — it *is* cross-sectional leakage — so rejecting it is correct.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is inaccurate, so rejecting it is correct. This represents cross-sectional target leakage. Calculating the cross-sectional standard deviation across the entire universe before splitting the data allows information about the feature distributions of the validation stocks to leak into the training partition."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Signal Beta: A liquidity feature that replaces missing daily trading volume values (NaNs) with the historical median volume of that specific stock, calculated using the entire 10-year dataset slice before assigning rows to training and validation sets.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: Signal Beta imputes NaNs with a median over the entire 10-year slice before splitting → temporal/partition leakage via preprocessing.", "whyPool": "This represents temporal and partition leakage via preprocessing. Calculating a global median across the entire 10-year horizon incorporates data from the validation folds into the imputation parameter, creating an optimistic bias in backtest metrics."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Signal Gamma: A fundamental value factor that deflates a company's raw research and development (R&D) expenditure by dividing it by the firm's total quarterly revenue, where both metrics are sourced strictly from Point-in-Time financial statements using the definitive publication date.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: Signal Gamma deflates R&D by the firm's own point-in-time quarterly revenue (definitive publication date) → no future info, no cross-partition contamination → clean.", "whyPool": "This implementation is structurally clean. Using Point-in-Time data ensures that the fundamental metrics are lagged to their actual public release dates, and scaling a firm's features by its own contemporaneous revenue introduces no future information or cross-partition contamination. 93 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Signal Delta: A macroeconomic sentiment feature that calculates a 10-day rolling average of an investor confidence index, where the rolling window for day t is indexed using an engine that aggregates data from day t−2 through day t+7.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: Signal Delta's rolling window for day t spans t−2...t+7 → classic look-ahead, absorbing the future the model is forecasting.", "whyPool": "This represents classic look-ahead target leakage. By allowing the rolling window to peek forward to day t+7, the feature matrix absorbs the exact future data that the model is attempting to forecast within its 5-day execution window."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Signal Epsilon: An alternative text-mining factor that selects predictive sentiment keywords from earnings call transcripts by running a chi-square relevance test against the target variable (y) across the entire historical database prior to modeling.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: Signal Epsilon selects keywords via a chi-square test against y across the whole database → feature-selection pooling leakage.", "whyPool": "This represents leakage via feature selection pooling. Using the target variable (y) across the entire database to filter and select predictive keywords pre-optimizes the feature space to fit the validation set, creating a statistical illusion of high out-of-sample accuracy. G6: Look-Ahead Bias in Time-Series"}], "reference": "Point-in-time cleanliness vs. pooling/look-ahead leakage across preprocessing, imputation, selection — scikit-learn \"Common pitfalls\"; López de Prado (PIT, CV in finance).", "n": 101}, {"id": "ML-D3G6Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A strategy trades at each day’s market open using an indicator calculated from that day’s official closing price. The backtest shows a high Sharpe ratio, and the PM wants to allocate capital next week. The execution team points out that the closing price does not exist when the opening trade is placed. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The indicator contains future information relative to the trade decision.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the indicator contains future information relative to the trade decision (the close occurs after the open).", "whyPool": "The day’s close occurs after the opening decision."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Shifting the closing-price feature so that an opening trade uses the previous day’s close can restore causal timing.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: shifting the feature so an opening trade uses the *previous* day's close restores causal timing (prior close is known before the next open).", "whyPool": "The prior close is known before the next market open."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The bias disappears if the same formula is applied consistently throughout the backtest.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: applying the biased formula consistently does not remove the bias — consistent use of unavailable info remains biased.", "whyPool": "Consistent use of unavailable information remains biased."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A strong backtest can result from information that would be impossible to obtain live.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a strong backtest can arise from information impossible to obtain live (hindsight/artificial predictive power).", "whyPool": "Hindsight can create artificial predictive power."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Feature timestamps should be aligned with the actual decision and execution timestamps.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: feature timestamps must be aligned with actual decision/execution times — calendar labels alone don't prove availability.", "whyPool": "Calendar labels alone do not establish that a feature was available in time."}], "reference": "Look-ahead bias, causal feature timing — López de Prado *Advances in Financial ML* (labeling/timing); backtest realism principles.", "n": 102}, {"id": "ML-D3G6Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A global equity model uses annual fundamentals joined to daily prices by fiscal year. Revised financial statements are copied backward to every date in that fiscal year, and the research director says this is acceptable because all values refer to the same company-year. The risk analyst asks whether the historical dataset represents what traders actually knew. 94 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Backfilling a later filing or revision into earlier dates can create look-ahead bias.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded: \"The following claim is *false*: backfilling a later filing/revision into earlier dates can create look-ahead bias.\" The inner claim is accurate, so declaring it false is incorrect — earlier simulated decisions would receive later-published info.", "whyPool": "Earlier simulated decisions receive information published only later."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A point-in-time database should retain the publication or effective date of each reported value.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a point-in-time database must retain each value's publication/effective date to reconstruct the live information set.", "whyPool": "Availability dates are needed to reconstruct the live information set."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because the statements describe past operations, their values are available to traders throughout the fiscal year.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: describing past operations does not mean the values were *available* throughout the year — economic coverage ≠ public-availability date.", "whyPool": "Economic coverage and public availability are different dates."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Restatements should not silently replace the originally available figures in a historical simulation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: restatements should not silently replace originally available figures in a historical simulation.", "whyPool": "Otherwise the simulation grants past traders knowledge of later corrections."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Joining by fiscal year alone may ignore reporting delays between the measured period and public release.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: joining by fiscal year alone ignores reporting delays between the measured period and public release.", "whyPool": "A statement typically becomes usable only after it is released."}], "reference": "Point-in-time databases, restatement/reporting-lag bias — López de Prado (PIT data); survivorship/look-ahead bias literature.", "n": 103}, {"id": "ML-D3G6Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A machine-learning pipeline labels each day with the return over the next five trading days and constructs a centered ten- day moving average using five past and five future observations. The team uses a chronological train/test split and believes this alone prevents all temporal leakage. The head trader asks for a deployment-faithful review.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: A chronological split does not prevent leakage inside a feature computed with future observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded: \"The following claim is *false*: a chronological split does not prevent leakage inside a feature computed with future obs.\" The inner claim is accurate, so calling it false is incorrect — splitting cannot repair a forward-looking feature.", "whyPool": "Correct splitting cannot repair a feature whose own construction looks forward."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The centered moving average uses prices unavailable at the day being scored.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the centered MA uses prices unavailable at the day being scored (half its window is in the future).", "whyPool": "Half of its window occurs after the feature timestamp."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Replacing it with a trailing moving average can make the feature causal.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: replacing it with a *trailing* MA (current + prior obs only) makes the feature causal.", "whyPool": "A trailing window uses only current and prior observations."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Future returns may be used as training labels even though they cannot be used as contemporaneous prediction features.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: future returns may serve as training *labels* (once realized) even though they cannot be contemporaneous prediction features.", "whyPool": "Future outcomes define what historical examples teach the model to predict. 95 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Any use of later dates anywhere in supervised learning is necessarily invalid.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: later dates *can* validly serve as labels once realized — the restriction concerns availability at prediction time and evaluation separation, not any use of later dates.", "whyPool": "Later outcomes can validly serve as labels once realized; the restriction concerns what is available at prediction time and how evaluation is separated."}], "reference": "Look-ahead in feature construction vs. valid future labels — López de Prado (labeling, triple-barrier); trailing vs. centered windows.", "n": 104}, {"id": "ML-D3G6Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher is developing a \"Mean Reversion\" strategy. To identify overextended price moves, they calculate a Z-Score for the current price relative to a \"Centered Moving Average. \" The feature for day t is calculated as the price at t minus the average price from days t−5 to t+5, divided by the standard deviation of that same 11-day window.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This feature is highly effective for mean reversion because it identifies how far the current price has deviated from the local \"true mean\" of the surrounding two-week period.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a centered mean is not a tradeable \"true mean\" — it relies on the future to define the present.", "whyPool": "While mathematically a \"mean, \" a centered mean is not a \"trading mean\" because it relies on the future to define the present."}, {"L": "B", "text": "This methodology introduces a fatal Look-Ahead Bias because the feature for day t incorporates the actual closing prices of the next 5 days (t+1 to t+5), which are unknown at the time the trade must be placed.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the feature for day t incorporates the actual closes of t+1...t+5, unknown when the trade must be placed → look-ahead bias.", "whyPool": "This is the most literal form of look-ahead bias. If your indicator knows where the price will be in 5 days, it isn't \"predicting\" the reversion; it is simply reporting it after the fact."}, {"L": "C", "text": "This is an example of Smoothing Contamination, where the use of future data points creates a \"perfect\" technical indicator that will appear to have 90%+ predictive accuracy in a backtest but is impossible to execute live.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: this smoothing contamination yields a \"perfect\" indicator that looks 90%+ accurate in backtest but is impossible to execute live.", "whyPool": "In a backtest, this indicator will look like a \"Holy Grail\" because it will always signal a \"sell\" exactly at the peak, since the peak is defined by the prices that follow it."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Using a \"Centered\" window is a standard signal processing technique to remove phase lag, and as long as the model is only tested on historical data, the temporal violation does not affect the validity of the Sharpe Ratio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: using future data to remove phase lag renders the backtest fiction — a backtest must be a sequence of decisions made in \"blind\" time.", "whyPool": "Phase lag is a real problem, but \"solving\" it by using future data renders the entire backtest a work of fiction. A backtest must represent a sequence of decisions made in \"blind\" time."}, {"L": "E", "text": "To correct this bias while maintaining the intent of the feature, the researcher must switch to a Trailing Moving Average, using only data from t−10 to t to calculate the mean and standard deviation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: switching to a trailing window (t−10...t) for the mean and σ enforces the causal constraint.", "whyPool": "The \"Causal\" constraint of finance requires that all inputs at time t are derived strictly from the set {...,t−2,t−1,t}."}], "reference": "Causal (trailing) vs. centered indicators, look-ahead bias — signal-processing phase lag vs. tradeability; López de Prado (causal features).", "n": 105}, {"id": "ML-D3G6Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher is developing a \"Gap-Trading\" strategy that buys stocks that open significantly lower than their previous day's close. In the backtest, the model calculates the \"Gap Percentage\" using the Official Opening Price from the exchange's 9:30 AM auction. The backtest then assumes the trade is executed at that same opening price to capture the immediate reversal. 96 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This is a highly accurate simulation because the opening auction price is a single, executable print that represents the first available liquidity of the day.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: the price is real but the sequence is not — you cannot use an auction's *result* to participate in that same auction.", "whyPool": "While the price is \"real, \" the sequence is not. You cannot use the result of an auction to participate in that same auction."}, {"L": "B", "text": "This setup contains an Execution Look-Ahead Bias because the \"Gap Percentage\" cannot be mathematically confirmed until the opening price is published, at which point the 9:30 AM auction has already concluded.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: execution look-ahead — the Gap % cannot be confirmed until the open is published, by which time the auction has already concluded.", "whyPool": "This is a subtle but common temporal error. By the time you know the \"Gap\" is large enough to trade, the price at which you calculated that gap is no longer available for new orders."}, {"L": "C", "text": "This is an example of Simultaneity Bias, where the information required to trigger the signal (the Open) is the same information used for the execution price, making the trade impossible to catch in a live environment.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: using the same information for both the signal and the execution price is simultaneity bias — impossible in a live latency environment.", "whyPool": "This creates a \"Perfect Execution\" scenario in the backtest that ignores the reality of message latency and order processing time."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Using the opening price for both the signal and the entry is a valid \"Zero-Slippage\" assumption that is common in academic research to establish a baseline for strategy alpha.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a \"zero-slippage\" baseline that ignores temporal sequence is a baseline for a crystal ball, not a trading bot.", "whyPool": "In ML4T, we must distinguish between \"Academic Baselines\" and \"Causal Reality. \" A baseline that ignores temporal sequence is a baseline for a crystal ball, not a trading bot."}, {"L": "E", "text": "To correct this bias, the researcher should use the Opening Price to calculate the gap but execute the entry at the Volume Weighted Average Price (VWAP) of the first five minutes of trading (9:30 AM – 9:35 AM).", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: compute the gap from the open but execute at, e.g., the 9:30–9:35 VWAP so the signal is fully formed and knowable before capital is committed.", "whyPool": "Delaying the execution to the post-open market ensures that the signal was fully formed and \"knowable\" before the model committed capital."}], "reference": "Execution/simultaneity look-ahead bias — realistic fill assumptions; López de Prado (execution realism).", "n": 106}, {"id": "ML-D3G6Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An algorithmic execution engine is being calibrated to optimize a \"VWAP Participation Strategy. \" The objective function optimizes an execution schedule across the trading day to minimize market impact. The model utilizes a volume- prediction feature calculated as a \"Normalized 30-Minute Intraday Volatility Factor. \" For any given 30-minute bar (e.g., 10:00 AM to 10:30 AM), the script calculates the variance of 1-minute mid-prices spanning from 9:45 AM to 10:15 AM to determine the participation rate for that entire 10:00–10:30 AM block.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This feature design is temporally sound because the 30-minute calculation window starts and ends exactly 15 minutes before the conclusion of the execution block, providing a fresh leading indicator.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a feature is not a \"leading indicator\" if its calculation window overlaps the period during which the model is actively executing.", "whyPool": "A feature cannot be considered a \"leading indicator\" if its calculation window overlaps with the time period during which the model is actively executing trades."}, {"L": "B", "text": "This setup introduces a structural Look-Ahead Bias because the participation rate determined for the first half of the block (10:00 AM to 10:15 AM) relies on 1-minute mid-prices that have not yet occurred in the market.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: structural look-ahead — the rate for 10:00–10:15 relies on 1-minute mid-prices that have not yet occurred.", "whyPool": "This is an execution-level look-ahead bias. If the algorithm modifies its behavior at 10:01 AM based on an indicator that requires the 10:15 AM price print, it is cheating by observing its own future environment."}, {"L": "C", "text": "This is an example of an Overlapping Horizon Infraction, where the feature's calculation window crosses into the execution engine's active decision window, rendering the backtested performance physically unachievable. 97 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: this overlapping-horizon infraction masks real high-frequency feature latency, creating an unearned variance reduction in the backtest.", "whyPool": "This specific overlap masks the real-world latency of high-frequency feature engineering, creating an unearned variance reduction in the backtested execution profile."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because mid-prices are non-directional and do not contain explicit order flow information, using future mid-prices inside the current execution window does not grant the strategy a directional advantage.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: intraday variance directly drives optimal execution sizing — knowing a future volatility spike lets the backtest artificially avoid/exploit impact, inflating execution alpha.", "whyPool": "Intraday variance shifts directly dictate optimal execution sizing. Knowing that a volatility spike occurs between 10:00 AM and 10:15 AM allows the backtest to artificially avoid or exploit impact, inflating the execution alpha."}, {"L": "E", "text": "To correct this temporal violation, the feature's calculation window must be strictly truncated or shifted to end at or before t=10:00 AM (e.g., using the interval from 9:30 AM to 10:00 AM) to preserve a causal pipeline.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: truncate/shift the window to end at or before t=10:00 AM (e.g., 9:30–10:00) so the boundary information is finalized before execution logic begins.", "whyPool": "Strict temporal separation requires that the information state at the boundary line (t) is completely finalized before any execution logic for the interval (t→t+n) is initiated. G7: “Too Good to Be True” Features"}], "reference": "Execution-level look-ahead / overlapping windows — causal feature boundaries; market-microstructure backtest realism.", "n": 107}, {"id": "ML-D3G7Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G7", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A new alternative-data field raises a stock-direction model’s test accuracy from 53% to 99%. The vendor calls the field a proprietary “sentiment score” and will not explain its construction, while the PM wants to trade immediately. The model- risk officer requests evidence that the score is genuinely available before each prediction.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "An abrupt, implausibly large performance increase is a reason to investigate leakage and data lineage.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "An abrupt, implausibly large accuracy jump is a reason to investigate leakage and data lineage.", "whyPool": "Exceptional results warrant verification before they are trusted."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A proprietary feature should be presumed valid because its calculation is confidential.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: confidentiality does not establish causal availability or clean construction — a proprietary feature is not presumed valid.", "whyPool": "Confidentiality does not establish causal availability or clean construction. 98 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Testing whether the feature timestamp precedes the trading decision can help assess deployability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Testing whether the feature timestamp precedes the trading decision helps assess deployability.", "whyPool": "A feature must exist before the action it is meant to inform."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing performance after lagging or removing the feature can reveal whether its predictive power depends on timing.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing performance after lagging or removing the feature reveals whether its power depends on timing (contemporaneous/future info).", "whyPool": "Sensitivity to timing changes can expose contemporaneous or future information."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Near-perfect historical accuracy guarantees near-perfect live accuracy.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: near-perfect historical accuracy does not guarantee live accuracy — leakage, overfitting, and regime change can destroy it.", "whyPool": "Leakage, overfitting, and regime change can destroy live performance."}], "reference": "\"Too good to be true\" diagnostics; leakage/lineage audit — Kaufman et al. \"Leakage in Data Mining\"; ablation/lag testing.", "n": 108}, {"id": "ML-D3G7Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G7", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A researcher finds that the alphabetical position of a stock ticker strongly predicts returns during one backtest. The strategy sponsor argues that correlation is sufficient because trading models need not explain causes, while the research lead suspects a dataset artifact. The team has several independent periods and universes available for testing. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A strong historical correlation does not by itself show that the feature has a stable economic relationship with returns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: strong historical correlation alone does not show a stable economic relationship with returns.", "whyPool": "Correlation can be unstable or induced by the research process."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The ticker result may arise from selection effects, data ordering, or chance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the ticker result may arise from selection effects, data ordering, or pure chance.", "whyPool": "Non-economic artifacts can create apparently predictive associations."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Replication across untouched periods and alternative universes can test the relationship’s stability.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: replication across untouched periods and alternative universes tests the relationship's stability.", "whyPool": "Independent replication reduces reliance on a single accidental sample."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Lack of an obvious causal mechanism proves with certainty that the feature is useless.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: lack of an obvious causal mechanism does NOT *prove with certainty* the feature is useless — absence of a mechanism is suggestive, not definitive.", "whyPool": "A mechanism is helpful but its absence alone is not definitive proof."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the correlation disappears outside the discovery sample, the original result is weak evidence for deployment.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: if the correlation disappears outside the discovery sample, the original result is weak evidence for deployment.", "whyPool": "Failure to generalize suggests the discovery captured sample-specific noise."}], "reference": "Correlation vs. causation, replication/robustness testing — spurious-correlation literature; out-of-sample replication practice.", "n": 109}, {"id": "ML-D3G7Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Feature Engineering & Leakage", "group": "G7", "weeks": [1, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fraud-detection model for a trading platform relies heavily on a database field called review_outcome_code. Documentation is incomplete, but the field has unusually high importance and is populated for nearly every historical fraud case. Operations says the field may be written during a manual investigation that occurs after the alert would need to fire.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The combination of high importance, unclear lineage, and suspicious timing justifies a focused audit.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "High importance + unclear lineage + suspicious timing together justify a focused audit.", "whyPool": "These warning signs make the feature a plausible source of unrealistic performance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Feature importance establishes that the field causes fraud.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: feature importance measures predictive use within the model, not causality — it does not establish the field causes fraud.", "whyPool": "Importance measures predictive use within the model, not causality."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Examining when the field is created relative to the prediction timestamp can reveal outcome-derived leakage. 99 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Examining when the field is created relative to the prediction timestamp reveals outcome-derived leakage.", "whyPool": "Post-alert creation would make the field unavailable for prospective detection."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Running the model without the field can help quantify how dependent reported performance is on it.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Running the model without the field quantifies how dependent the reported performance is on it (ablation).", "whyPool": "Ablation exposes whether the headline result collapses without the suspicious input."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because the field exists in the historical database, it must have been available to the live model before each fraud event.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: historical presence in the database does not establish point-in-time availability before each fraud event.", "whyPool": "Historical presence does not establish point-in-time availability. 100 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Target leakage from post-outcome fields; importance ≠ causality; ablation — Kaufman et al. \"Leakage in Data Mining\"; scikit-learn \"Permutation importance.\"", "n": 110}, {"id": "ML-D4G1Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An equity analyst fits a linear model to predict next-month return from earnings growth and valuation. The PM wants to turn the fitted equation directly into an economic story, while the analyst cautions that the inputs are correlated and the model is predictive rather than causal. The team needs an interpretation appropriate for an investment memo.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A positive earnings-growth coefficient means the model predicts a higher return as earnings growth rises, holding the other included feature fixed.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A linear coefficient is the modeled change in prediction as earnings growth rises with the other included feature held fixed — the standard conditional interpretation.", "whyPool": "A linear coefficient describes the modeled change with the other included inputs held constant."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The intercept is the model's prediction when all included feature values equal zero.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The intercept is algebraically the prediction when all features equal zero, even if that point is not economically meaningful.", "whyPool": "That is the algebraic role of the intercept, although the point may lack practical meaning."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A larger numerical coefficient always proves that its feature is more important, even when the features use different units.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Coefficient magnitude depends on the feature's units/scale, so a larger number does not prove greater importance when features differ in units.", "whyPool": "Coefficient magnitude depends on the feature's units and scale."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The sign of a fitted coefficient by itself establishes that changing the feature will cause the predicted change in return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A predictive (associational) sign does not by itself establish that changing the feature *causes* the predicted change.", "whyPool": "A predictive association does not by itself establish causality."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Correlation among the inputs can make individual coefficient interpretations less stable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correlated inputs allow several nearly equivalent fits that allocate weight differently, making individual coefficients less stable.", "whyPool": "Correlated inputs can permit several similar fits with different coefficient allocations. 102 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "OLS coefficient interpretation & correlation of predictors — ISLR ch.3 (§3.1–3.2, multiple regression, interpreting coefficients); concept \"association vs. causation.\"", "n": 111}, {"id": "ML-D4G1Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A futures desk models daily contract returns using changes in interest rates measured in percentage points. A developer changes the input to basis points but retrains nothing and assumes the old coefficient can still be reported unchanged. The risk officer asks what must change for the same economic relationship to be represented.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Changing a feature's units can change the numerical value required for its coefficient.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A coefficient expresses change in prediction per unit of its feature, so changing units changes the required numerical coefficient.", "whyPool": "A coefficient expresses change in prediction per unit of its feature."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If one percentage point equals 100 basis points, the coefficient per basis point should be one hundredth of the coefficient per percentage point for equivalent predictions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "One pp = 100 bps, so the per-basis-point coefficient must be 1/100 of the per-percentage-point coefficient for equivalent predictions.", "whyPool": "Each basis-point unit is smaller, so the per-unit effect scales down accordingly."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The sign of the coefficient must reverse when the feature is converted from percentage points to basis points.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Multiplying by a positive conversion factor preserves direction; the sign does not reverse.", "whyPool": "Multiplication by a positive conversion factor preserves direction."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Consistent rescaling of the feature and coefficient can leave the model's predictions unchanged.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Consistent rescaling of feature and coefficient leaves the product (and thus predictions) identical.", "whyPool": "The product of feature value and coefficient can remain identical."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Coefficients can be compared meaningfully across models without checking feature definitions or units.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Comparing raw coefficients across models without checking units/definitions can be misleading.", "whyPool": "Different units or definitions can make raw coefficient comparisons misleading."}], "reference": "Units, scaling, and coefficient equivalence — ISLR ch.3 (§3.1, interpreting coefficients); scikit-learn `preprocessing`/`StandardScaler` docs (feature scaling).", "n": 112}, {"id": "ML-D4G1Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A portfolio risk team predicts a fund's daily return from market and sector returns using a linear model with an intercept. The CIO sees a positive market coefficient and a small positive intercept and asks whether those quantities guarantee future profits. The team must separate the model's structure from claims the historical fit cannot support. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: The market coefficient represents the model's estimated sensitivity to market return, conditional on the sector input.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (\"the market coefficient is the modeled sensitivity conditional on the sector input\") is accurate, so labeling it \"false\" is itself incorrect.", "whyPool": "It is the modeled marginal association with the market while the sector feature is held fixed."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A positive intercept guarantees a positive realized return whenever the market return is zero.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a positive intercept is a fitted prediction, not a guarantee of a positive realized return when market return is zero.", "whyPool": "The intercept is a fitted prediction, not a guarantee about an individual future outcome."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The model prediction is formed by adding the intercept to the weighted feature values.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: adding the intercept to the weighted feature values is exactly the additive form of an ordinary linear model.", "whyPool": "That additive weighted form defines an ordinary linear model."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If both feature values are zero, the fitted prediction equals the intercept.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: when all features are zero, every feature contribution vanishes and the prediction equals the intercept.", "whyPool": "All feature contributions vanish at zero, leaving the intercept."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A coefficient estimated from historical data may change when the market regime changes. 103 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: an estimated relationship need not remain stable under a distribution or regime shift.", "whyPool": "Estimated relationships need not remain stable under distribution or regime shifts."}], "reference": "Linear model structure, intercept meaning, non-stationarity — ISLR ch.3 (§3.1); concept \"regime shift / parameter instability.\"", "n": 113}, {"id": "ML-D4G1Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A quantitative equity hedge fund is building a linear regression model to predict next-month stock returns using valuation, momentum, and quality factors. During a research review, the Portfolio Manager asks whether the signs and magnitudes of the model coefficients can be used to explain the model's investment thesis to clients. Meanwhile, the risk team warns that misinterpreting coefficients could lead to unintended factor exposures and portfolio tilts. You are asked to evaluate the following statements before the model is deployed. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "In a linear regression model, a positive coefficient on a momentum factor implies that higher momentum is associated with a higher predicted return, holding the other factors constant.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a positive momentum coefficient means higher momentum is associated with higher predicted return, holding other factors fixed.", "whyPool": "The sign of a coefficient indicates the direction of the relationship between the predictor and the model's prediction, conditional on the other variables in the regression."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If the coefficient on a valuation factor is (-0.8), increasing the valuation factor by one unit decreases the predicted return by 0.8 units, assuming all other predictors remain unchanged.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a −0.8 valuation coefficient means a one-unit increase lowers the prediction by 0.8, other predictors held constant.", "whyPool": "In a linear regression model, a one-unit increase in a predictor changes the predicted response by the value of its coefficient while holding the other predictors constant."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The intercept of a linear regression model represents the model's predicted return when all predictor values are equal to zero.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the intercept is the predicted return when all predictors equal zero; economic meaning depends on scaling/context.", "whyPool": "The intercept is the predicted value of the response when all predictors are equal to zero. Whether this value is economically meaningful depends on the context and feature scaling."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A factor with a coefficient of 2.0 is always more economically important than a factor with a coefficient of 0.5.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: magnitudes cannot generally be compared directly (different units/scales), so a coefficient of 2.0 is not \"always\" more economically important than 0.5.", "whyPool": "Coefficient magnitudes cannot generally be compared directly because predictors may be measured in different units or scales. A larger coefficient does not automatically imply greater economic importance. 104 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Two factors can have coefficients with opposite signs, indicating that one contributes positively and the other negatively to the model's prediction when the remaining predictors are held fixed.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: opposite signs indicate opposite directional associations after accounting for the other variables.", "whyPool": "Opposite coefficient signs indicate opposite directional associations with the predicted outcome after accounting for the other variables in the model."}], "reference": "Multiple-regression coefficient interpretation & scale — ISLR ch.3 (§3.1–3.2); concept \"standardization for comparability.\"", "n": 114}, {"id": "ML-D4G1Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A systematic equity hedge fund is developing a cross-sectional linear regression model to rank stocks based on expected next-month returns. The model includes standardized momentum, value, and earnings-revision factors. During a model review, the Portfolio Manager argues that the factor with the largest coefficient must be the most important investment signal, while the Head of Research cautions that coefficient interpretation is more nuanced. Before presenting the model to investors as an interpretable alternative to more complex machine learning methods, you are asked to evaluate the following statements.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "If all predictors have been standardized to have mean 0 and variance 1, a coefficient with a larger absolute value generally indicates a larger change in the model prediction for a one-standard-deviation change in that predictor.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With standardized predictors, a larger absolute coefficient corresponds to a larger prediction change per one-standard-deviation move, so magnitudes become comparable.", "whyPool": "When predictors are standardized, coefficient magnitudes become more directly comparable because each coefficient corresponds to the effect of a one-standard-deviation change in its predictor on the model prediction."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If the coefficient on momentum changes substantially after a value factor is added to the regression, this necessarily implies that the original momentum coefficient was estimated incorrectly.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Coefficients commonly shift when a correlated predictor is added; that does not mean the original estimate was \"incorrect\" for its specification.", "whyPool": "Coefficients often change when new predictors are added, especially when predictors are correlated. The original estimate may have been valid for the original specification even though the new model produces a different estimate."}, {"L": "C", "text": "In a multiple linear regression model, a positive coefficient on earnings revisions indicates that higher earnings revisions are associated with higher predicted returns after accounting for the other factors in the model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "In multiple regression each coefficient measures the association with the response holding the other predictors fixed.", "whyPool": "In multiple regression, each coefficient measures the association between a predictor and the response while holding the other predictors fixed."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the coefficient on value is close to zero, this proves that value has no relationship with future stock returns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A near-zero coefficient does not prove no relationship — the effect may be nonlinear, captured by correlated predictors, or masked by noise.", "whyPool": "A near-zero coefficient in a particular linear model does not prove the absence of a relationship. The effect may be nonlinear, captured by correlated predictors, or obscured by noise."}, {"L": "E", "text": "An interpretable coefficient does not necessarily imply that the estimated relationship reflects a causal economic mechanism.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "An interpretable coefficient describes a statistical association, not necessarily a causal economic mechanism.", "whyPool": "Regression coefficients describe statistical associations learned from data. Even when easily interpretable, they do not by themselves establish causality. 105 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Standardized coefficients, omitted/added-variable effects — ISLR ch.3 (§3.2–3.3); ESL ch.3 (subset/coefficient behavior).", "n": 115}, {"id": "ML-D4G1Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative research team constructs a multivariable linear regression model to predict next-month equity returns for a universe of large-cap stocks. The model uses three fundamental features: •  X₁: Earnings Yield •  X₂: Debt-to-Equity Ratio •  X₃: Three-Month Historical Volatility After standardizing each feature to have a mean of zero and a standard deviation of one, the team fits the model using Ordinary Least Squares (OLS). The estimated regression equation is: ŷ = 0.02 + 0.045X₁− 0.012X₂− 0.038X₃ Which of the following statements correctly interpret the structure, coefficients, or algebraic properties of this linear regression model?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The intercept of 0.02 indicates that when a stock’s earnings yield, debt-to-equity ratio, and historical volatility are all equal to their respective sample means, the model predicts a next-month return of 2%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Standardized features have mean zero, so at the sample means X1=X2=X3=0 and the prediction equals the intercept, 0.02 (2%).", "whyPool": "Standardized features have a mean of zero. When all three features equal their respective sample means, X₁ = X₂ =X₃ = 0, so the model’s prediction equals the intercept: 0.02, or 2%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The coefficient of −0.012 for X₂implies that a high debt-to-equity ratio unconditionally causes a decrease in next- month stock returns, independent of the other features included in the model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The −0.012 coefficient is a conditional association holding other features fixed, not an unconditional or causal effect of debt-to-equity.", "whyPool": "The coefficient represents the estimated association between debt-to-equity and next-month returns while holding the other included features constant. It does not establish an unconditional relationship or a causal effect."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because the features were standardized before fitting, the absolute coefficient magnitudes indicate that X₁has the largest estimated effect on the model’s prediction per one-standard-deviation increase, followed by X₃and then X₂, holding the other features constant.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Standardization puts features on a common scale, so \\|0.045\\| > \\|0.038\\| > \\|0.012\\| ranks estimated per-SD effects: X1 then X3 then X2.", "whyPool": "Standardization places the features on a common scale. Therefore, their absolute coefficient magnitudes can be compared as the changes in the model’s prediction associated with one-standard-deviation increases in the respective features, holding the other features constant. Here, X₁has the largest estimated effect, followed by X₃and then X₂."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because the model is additive and contains no interaction terms, increasing X₁by one standard deviation changes the predicted next-month return by a fixed amount of +0.045, regardless of the value of X₃.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With no interaction terms the marginal effect of X1 is constant, so a one-SD increase changes the prediction by +0.045 at every value of X3.", "whyPool": "Without an interaction term between earnings yield and historical volatility, the model assumes that the marginal effect of X₁is constant. A one-standard-deviation increase in X₁therefore changes the prediction by +0.045 at every value of X₃. 106 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "If X₂and X₃are highly correlated, the OLS optimization routine will necessarily fail to find a unique mathematical solution for the intercept and coefficient estimates.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "High correlation inflates coefficient variance but does not necessarily break uniqueness; only *exact* linear dependence (perfect multicollinearity) prevents a unique OLS solution.", "whyPool": "High correlation among features can inflate the variance of the coefficient estimates and make them unstable, but it does not necessarily prevent a unique OLS solution. A unique solution fails only when there is exact linear dependence among the model’s columns, resulting in perfect multicollinearity. G2: Assumptions of Linear Models"}], "reference": "Standardized OLS interpretation, additive marginal effects, multicollinearity vs. rank deficiency — ISLR ch.3 (§3.2, §3.3.3); concept \"perfect multicollinearity.\"", "n": 116}, {"id": "ML-D4G2Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A commodities fund uses temperature to predict natural-gas demand. Residual plots suggest demand changes little on mild days but rises rapidly during extreme cold, while the PM argues that a straight line should be retained because it is easy to explain. The research lead must assess the consequence of that simplification.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A single linear temperature term assumes a constant marginal change in predicted demand for each one-unit temperature change.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "An untransformed single-feature linear model has a constant slope — a fixed marginal change per unit of temperature.", "whyPool": "The slope of an untransformed one-feature linear model is constant."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Systematic curvature in the residuals is evidence that the linear form may be misspecified.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Systematic curvature in residuals is evidence the linear form is misspecified (missing part of the relationship).", "whyPool": "Curved residual structure indicates that predictions systematically miss part of the relationship."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A high in-sample correlation guarantees that a linear specification is appropriate at all temperatures.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "High in-sample correlation can coexist with important unmodeled nonlinear structure, so it does not guarantee a linear form is appropriate everywhere.", "whyPool": "Correlation can be high even when important nonlinear structure remains."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Adding a suitable nonlinear transformation could preserve a linear-in-coefficients model while representing curvature.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A nonlinear transformation (e.g., a polynomial term) can represent curvature while keeping the model linear in its coefficients.", "whyPool": "Polynomial or other transformed features can add curvature while coefficients remain linear."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Interpretability prevents a linear model from underfitting.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Interpretability aids explanation but does not ensure adequate capacity; a simple model can still underfit.", "whyPool": "Simplicity may aid explanation but does not ensure adequate capacity."}], "reference": "Linearity assumption, residual diagnostics, transformations — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.3, non-linearity of the response-predictor relationships).", "n": 117}, {"id": "ML-D4G2Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A credit team predicts bond spreads using leverage and debt-to-equity ratio. Because the two features are closely related, two retraining runs produce similar predictions but noticeably different individual coefficients. The portfolio manager wants to remove whichever feature has the smaller coefficient and calls it unimportant. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Strongly related inputs can make individual coefficient estimates unstable. 107 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: strongly related inputs make it hard to uniquely assign effects, so individual estimates can be unstable.", "whyPool": "The model can have difficulty uniquely assigning effects among overlapping inputs."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Similar predictions can arise from different allocations of weight across correlated features.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: compensating weight shifts across correlated features can leave the combined contribution (and predictions) similar.", "whyPool": "Compensating coefficient changes may leave their combined contribution similar."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A small coefficient necessarily means the feature contains no predictive information.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a small coefficient does not mean no predictive information — scale, redundancy, and other variables all affect coefficient size.", "whyPool": "Scale, redundancy, and the presence of other variables all affect coefficient size."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing out-of-sample performance can help evaluate whether removing one of the redundant features is acceptable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: out-of-sample comparison tests whether dropping a redundant feature preserves generalization.", "whyPool": "Held-out performance tests whether the simpler representation preserves generalization."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Linear regression mathematically requires every pair of input features to have exactly zero correlation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: linear regression does not require every feature pair to have exactly zero correlation; correlation is allowed.", "whyPool": "Correlation is allowed, though severe redundancy can complicate estimation and interpretation."}], "reference": "Multicollinearity, coefficient instability, feature selection by validation — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.3, collinearity); ch.6 (subset selection).", "n": 118}, {"id": "ML-D4G2Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A statistical-arbitrage team fits a return model to overlapping five-day observations. A junior analyst treats every row as an independent piece of evidence and reports extremely precise coefficients. The risk manager notes that adjacent targets share several trading days and questions the confidence implied by the analysis.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Overlapping return windows can create dependence between neighboring observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Overlapping windows reuse some of the same underlying returns, creating dependence between neighboring observations.", "whyPool": "Adjacent targets reuse some of the same underlying returns."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Treating dependent observations as fully independent can overstate the effective amount of information in the sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Treating dependent rows as independent overstates the effective (independent) information in the sample, inflating apparent precision.", "whyPool": "More rows do not imply proportionally more independent evidence."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Dependence between observations automatically makes every fitted prediction numerically wrong.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Dependence affects inference and evaluation but does not make every fitted prediction numerically wrong.", "whyPool": "Dependence affects inference and evaluation but does not prove every prediction is wrong."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A time-aware validation design is more appropriate than assuming randomly shuffled rows are exchangeable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A time-aware validation design respects how the data arise better than assuming randomly shuffled, exchangeable rows.", "whyPool": "Temporal evaluation better respects how the data arise and how the model will be used."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Increasing the number of overlapping rows necessarily creates the same informational benefit as collecting equally many independent periods.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Redundant overlapping rows provide less new information than an equal number of independent periods.", "whyPool": "Redundant overlapping observations provide less new information than independent periods. 108 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Independence assumption, autocorrelation, time-aware validation — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.3, correlation of error terms); ch.5 (validation); concept \"overlapping-sample bias.\"", "n": 119}, {"id": "ML-D4G2Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative analyst is building an Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) linear regression model to predict the next-day return of an equity index using two macroeconomic features: •  X₁: Change in the 10-year Treasury yield •  X₂: Change in the 2-year Treasury yield Because both features reflect movements along the same yield curve, they move closely together and have a correlation coefficient of r = 0.92. Which of the following statements correctly evaluate how this high correlation affects the assumptions, stability, and predictive behavior of the linear regression model?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The high correlation between X₁and X₂directly violates a core OLS assumption that the features must be statistically independent, making the coefficient estimates biased and mathematically invalid.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "OLS does not require independent/uncorrelated features; high-but-imperfect correlation does not bias estimates or invalidate OLS — only exact linear dependence breaks uniqueness.", "whyPool": "OLS does not require the explanatory features to be statistically independent or uncorrelated. High but imperfect correlation does not inherently bias the coefficient estimates or make OLS mathematically invalid. A unique OLS solution fails only when there is exact linear dependence among the model’s columns."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The severe multicollinearity inflates the variance and standard errors of the individual coefficient estimates. Consequently, small changes in the training sample can cause the estimated coefficients to change substantially or even reverse signs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Multicollinearity inflates the variance/standard errors of individual coefficients, so small sample changes can move them substantially or flip signs.", "whyPool": "Multicollinearity makes it difficult to isolate the independent contribution of each feature. This inflates the variance and standard errors of the coefficient estimates, making them sensitive to relatively small changes in the training data. Individual coefficients may change substantially or even reverse signs across samples."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because the features are highly correlated, the model’s R₂and overall F-statistic must approach zero, even if the two features are collectively useful for predicting index returns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "R2 and the F-statistic can stay high when correlated features are collectively predictive, even though individual coefficients are imprecise.", "whyPool": "Multicollinearity does not necessarily reduce the model’s overall explanatory power. The R₂and overall F- statistic may remain high when the correlated features are collectively associated with the target, even though their individual coefficients cannot be estimated precisely."}, {"L": "D", "text": "High correlation between the features automatically causes the model’s residuals to exhibit serial correlation, thereby violating the assumption that errors are independent across time.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Multicollinearity concerns relationships among features; serial correlation concerns residuals over time — the former does not automatically create the latter.", "whyPool": "Multicollinearity concerns relationships among the explanatory features, whereas serial correlation concerns relationships among residuals across time. High correlation between X₁and X₂does not automatically create serially correlated errors."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Although the individual coefficient estimates may be unstable, the model’s combined predictions can remain relatively stable when new observations follow the same joint distribution and correlation structure as the training data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Individual coefficients can be unstable while combined predictions stay reliable for new data drawn from the same joint distribution/correlation structure.", "whyPool": "Multicollinearity can make the individual coefficients unstable without necessarily making the model’s combined predictions unstable. Predictions may remain relatively reliable for new observations drawn from a joint distribution similar to the training data. However, prediction performance may deteriorate if the relationship or correlation structure between the features changes in production. 109 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Multicollinearity effects, variance inflation, serial correlation distinction — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.3, collinearity and VIF); concept \"prediction vs. inference under collinearity.\"", "n": 120}, {"id": "ML-D4G2Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative equity hedge fund is stress-testing a linear regression model used to predict next-month stock returns using a mix of valuation ratios, momentum signals, and liquidity measures. The Head of Research is concerned that the model may behave unpredictably during regime shifts, while a junior analyst argues that “as long as the residuals look small, the model assumptions are fine. ” Meanwhile, the risk team notes that several predictors are highly correlated and that some features may be influenced by omitted macroeconomic variables. You are asked to evaluate the validity of several statements about linear regression assumptions and implications in this setting.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A key condition for unbiased OLS coefficient estimates is that the error term has a conditional mean of zero for every combination of predictor values: E[ε ∣ X] = 0.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The zero-conditional-mean condition E[ε\\|X] = 0 is required for unbiased OLS estimates; violation (e.g., correlation with an omitted variable) biases coefficients.", "whyPool": "The zero conditional mean assumption requires that the predictors contain no systematic information about the error term. If this condition is violated—for example, because a predictor is correlated with an omitted variable contained in the error term—the OLS coefficient estimates may be biased."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If the residuals of a fitted linear regression model have mean approximately zero, this is sufficient evidence that the model is correctly specified.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A near-zero residual mean is a built-in property of OLS with an intercept and does not imply correct functional form or absence of misspecification.", "whyPool": "A near-zero mean of residuals is a built-in property of ordinary least squares with an intercept and does not imply correct functional form or absence of misspecification."}, {"L": "C", "text": "High multicollinearity among predictors can increase the variance of coefficient estimates, making them more sensitive to small changes in the data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Multicollinearity inflates coefficient variance, making estimates unstable and sensitive to small data changes even if prediction is unaffected.", "whyPool": "Multicollinearity inflates the variance of coefficient estimates, making them unstable and sensitive to small perturbations in the data, even if predictive performance is not heavily affected."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Standardizing predictors eliminates multicollinearity because all features are placed on the same numerical scale.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Standardization rescales variables but leaves correlations unchanged, so it does not address multicollinearity.", "whyPool": "Standardization changes the scale of variables but does not alter correlations among predictors, so it does not address multicollinearity."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Adding nonlinear transformations or interaction terms to a linear regression model always improves out-of-sample predictive performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Nonlinear/interaction terms can raise variance and overfitting risk, so OOS performance depends on the bias-variance tradeoff, not a guaranteed improvement.", "whyPool": "Adding nonlinear terms or interactions can improve flexibility, but it may also increase variance and overfitting risk, so out-of-sample performance depends on the bias-variance tradeoff rather than monotonic improvement."}], "reference": "Zero conditional mean, omitted-variable bias, multicollinearity, standardization — ISLR ch.3 (§3.2–3.3); ESL ch.3 (regularization vs. scaling).", "n": 121}, {"id": "ML-D4G2Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A systematic equity fund is deploying a linear regression model to predict cross-sectional stock returns using momentum, value, and volatility factors. The Head of Quant Research is concerned that the model behaves inconsistently during different volatility regimes, while the Portfolio Manager insists that “as long as the training error is low, the model must be capturing the true structure. ” A senior risk analyst also points out that several predictors are 110 Rev:081020261230 derived from overlapping financial statements and may embed hidden dependencies. You are asked to evaluate the following statements about assumptions, diagnostics, and interpretation in linear regression.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "If a linear regression model is correctly specified, residuals should exhibit no systematic patterns when plotted against fitted values.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Under correct specification residuals should look like noise with no systematic pattern against fitted values; visible patterns suggest misspecification.", "whyPool": "Under correct specification, residuals should behave like noise with no systematic structure; visible patterns often indicate misspecification such as nonlinearity or omitted variables."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A low training error in a linear regression model is sufficient evidence that the model has captured the true underlying relationship between predictors and returns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Low training error can reflect overfitting/in-sample fit and does not imply the true data-generating process was captured.", "whyPool": "Low training error may reflect overfitting or in-sample fit and does not imply that the model has captured the true data-generating process."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If two predictors are highly correlated, their individual coefficient estimates may vary substantially across different samples even if the overall predictive performance remains stable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With correlated predictors, individual coefficients can vary substantially across samples even when overall predictive performance is stable.", "whyPool": "Multicollinearity can lead to instability in coefficient estimates, causing them to vary across samples even when predictive performance is relatively unchanged."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Violations of linearity assumptions primarily affect coefficient interpretability but do not meaningfully impact predictive performance in most cases.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Linearity violations can degrade both interpretability and predictive performance, especially when the true relationship is strongly nonlinear.", "whyPool": "Violations of linearity can degrade both interpretability and predictive performance, particularly when the true relationship is strongly nonlinear."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If omitted variables are correlated with included predictors, the estimated coefficients may reflect both the direct effect of the included variables and the influence of the omitted variables.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Omitted variables correlated with included predictors cause coefficients to reflect confounded (direct + omitted) effects.", "whyPool": "When relevant variables are omitted and correlated with included predictors, coefficients may capture confounded effects rather than pure partial relationships. G3: Model Capacity and Polynomial Expansion"}], "reference": "Residual diagnostics, overfitting, omitted-variable confounding — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.3); ch.2 (assessing model accuracy).", "n": 122}, {"id": "ML-D4G3Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A market-impact model uses order size to predict execution cost. A straight line misses the increasingly steep cost of large orders, so the trading desk considers adding squared order size. The head trader wants better fit but does not want an unnecessarily fragile production model.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Adding a squared feature allows the predicted slope to vary with order size.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A squared term makes the prediction quadratic, so its derivative (slope) varies with order size.", "whyPool": "The derivative of a quadratic prediction changes as order size changes."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The expanded model can still be linear in its fitted coefficients.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The expanded model is still linear in its coefficients — they enter as an additive weighted combination of features.", "whyPool": "The coefficients still enter as an additive weighted combination of features."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Adding the squared term guarantees lower error on every future sample. 111 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Extra capacity can fit training noise and does not guarantee lower error on every future sample.", "whyPool": "Extra capacity can fit training noise and may not improve future performance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing time-ordered validation results can help determine whether the extra capacity generalizes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Time-ordered held-out testing measures whether the added term generalizes under realistic deployment.", "whyPool": "Held-out temporal testing measures the value of the additional term under realistic deployment."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A polynomial model of degree two can represent every possible relationship between order size and cost.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A degree-2 polynomial is flexible but still a restricted functional form; it cannot represent every possible relationship.", "whyPool": "A quadratic is flexible but remains a restricted functional form."}], "reference": "Polynomial regression, linear-in-coefficients, generalization testing — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.2, polynomial regression); ch.7 (§7.1).", "n": 123}, {"id": "ML-D4G3Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quant researcher fits polynomial regressions of degrees 1 through 15 to a small history of volatility and next-day returns. Training error falls at every degree, but validation error improves only through degree 3 and then rises sharply. The PM asks which pattern should drive model selection.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Falling training error as degree increases is consistent with an expanding hypothesis space.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A higher-degree polynomial nests the lower-degree ones and can fit extra variation, so training error falls as degree rises.", "whyPool": "A higher-degree polynomial can generally reproduce the lower-degree alternatives and fit additional variation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Rising validation error after degree 3 is evidence that higher-degree models may be overfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Better training fit paired with worse validation error after degree 3 is a classic overfitting signal.", "whyPool": "Better training fit paired with worse validation performance is a classic generalization warning."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Degree 15 should be selected because it has the lowest training error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Lowest training error rewards capacity even when it fits noise, so degree 15 should not be selected on that basis.", "whyPool": "Training error alone rewards capacity even when it fits noise."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Degree 1 may underfit if both training and validation errors are materially worse than those of modest-degree models.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If both training and validation errors are materially worse at degree 1, the straight line is underfitting (lacking useful flexibility).", "whyPool": "Improvement on both datasets suggests the straight line lacked useful flexibility."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The degree should be chosen using the final untouched test set repeatedly until its error is minimized.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Repeatedly tuning on the final test set contaminates it and no longer gives an unbiased performance estimate.", "whyPool": "Repeated test-set selection contaminates the final performance estimate. 112 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Model selection, validation curves, test-set hygiene — ISLR ch.5 (cross-validation); ch.2 (bias-variance / U-shaped test error).", "n": 124}, {"id": "ML-D4G3Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A global equity model contains 40 raw features. A researcher proposes adding every pairwise product and several powers of each feature, arguing that polynomial expansion is inexpensive compared with collecting more data. The model- governance team is concerned about a limited training history and unstable behavior. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Polynomial expansion can increase the number of candidate terms rapidly as the original feature count grows.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (\"polynomial expansion can increase candidate terms rapidly as feature count grows\") is accurate, so labeling it \"false\" is itself incorrect.", "whyPool": "Powers and cross-products can cause dimensionality to grow quickly."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The expanded representation can capture some interactions that an additive model on the raw features misses.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: product terms let one feature's modeled effect depend on another, capturing interactions an additive model misses.", "whyPool": "A product term allows one feature's modeled effect to depend on another."}, {"L": "C", "text": "More polynomial terms always reduce both bias and variance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: more terms do not always reduce both bias and variance — greater capacity typically lowers bias but raises variance.", "whyPool": "Greater capacity may reduce bias while increasing variance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Limited data relative to the expanded feature space can increase overfitting risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: many adjustable terms relative to limited data increase overfitting risk.", "whyPool": "Many adjustable terms provide opportunities to fit sample-specific noise."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Regularization or selective feature construction may help control the added capacity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: regularization or disciplined feature construction can control the added capacity.", "whyPool": "Constraints or disciplined selection can limit reliance on unsupported terms."}], "reference": "Curse of dimensionality, interactions, regularization — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.2, interactions), ch.6 (regularization); ESL ch.5 (basis expansions).", "n": 125}, {"id": "ML-D4G3Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative researcher is building a model to predict the implied volatility smile of index options using the option's moneyness (the asset price divided by the strike price) as the sole feature (X). The true underlying structural relationship is highly non-linear and shaped like a parabola. To capture this relationship within a linear regression framework, the researcher decides to increase the model's capacity by implementing a Polynomial Feature Expansion. They test three different configurations: Model A (1st-degree linear model), Model B (2nd-degree quadratic model), and Model C (15th- degree polynomial model). Each model is fit using standard Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) on a training sample of historical option prices. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the relationship between model capacity, polynomial degrees, and the bias-variance tradeoff in this configuration?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Model A suffers from high bias (underfitting) because its structural capacity is too restricted to capture the parabolic curvature of the true volatility smile, leading to poor performance on both the training and validation data. 113 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A straight line cannot fit a parabola — it forces a constant slope, producing systematic underfitting (high bias) on both training and validation data.", "whyPool": "A standard 1st-degree linear model lacks the structural flexibility to fit a curve. It assumes a constant rate of change, forcing a straight line through parabolic data, which results in systematic underfitting and high bias."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Increasing the polynomial expansion to a 15th degree (Model C) increases the model's flexibility, which mathematically guarantees a drop in the model's training error variance and a massive reduction in out-of-sample prediction error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Degree 15 drives training error down but inflates variance; OOS error rises as it overfits the sample's noise — no guaranteed reduction.", "whyPool": "While increasing the polynomial degree to 15 will reduce training error (often driving it close to zero), it dramatically inflates the model's variance. Out-of-sample prediction error will skyrocket because the model overfits the training sample's specific noise."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Model B achieves an optimal balance in the bias-variance tradeoff because its structural capacity matches the degree of the true data-generating process, resulting in low bias and low variance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Degree 2 matches the true quadratic data-generating process, capturing signal without absorbing noise → low bias and low variance.", "whyPool": "When the model's structural capacity aligns with the true functional form of the data-generating process, it successfully captures the signal without absorbing the noise, minimizing both systematic error (bias) and sensitivity to the training sample (variance)."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Model C exhibits high variance (overfitting) because its excessive capacity allows the OLS optimization routine to chase idiosyncratic noise and outliers in the training set, causing the regression curve to oscillate wildly between data points.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Degree 15's excess capacity lets unregularized OLS chase idiosyncratic noise, producing wild oscillation between points and poor generalization.", "whyPool": "High-degree polynomials have immense flexibility. In an OLS framework without regularization, the model will use this capacity to pass as close to every training point as possible, generating extreme, erratic oscillations that generalize poorly to unseen validation data."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Compared with Model A, Model C’s additional polynomial terms reduce the risk of multicollinearity because the predictors X,X₂, ... ,X₁₅represent distinct nonlinear transformations of X.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Raw powers of the same feature can be highly correlated (multicollinearity), especially uncentered; higher degree does not inherently reduce multicollinearity — centering/orthogonal polynomials do.", "whyPool": "Distinct polynomial terms are not necessarily weakly correlated. When raw powers of the same feature are used, they can be highly correlated, particularly when Xis not centered or spans a limited range. This multicollinearity can make the OLS coefficient estimates numerically unstable and sensitive to small changes in the training data. Centering or using orthogonal polynomial features can improve numerical stability, but increasing the polynomial degree does not inherently reduce multicollinearity."}], "reference": "Bias-variance tradeoff, polynomial capacity, multicollinearity of raw powers — ISLR ch.2 (§2.2), ch.3 (§3.3.2); ESL ch.5 (orthogonal polynomials).", "n": 126}, {"id": "ML-D4G3Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative analyst is modeling the transaction cost of executing large equity orders. The target variable, y, is slippage measured in basis points. The sole input feature, X, is order size expressed as a percentage of the stock’s Average Daily Volume (ADV). The analyst believes that slippage has an approximately quadratic relationship with order size. To model this nonlinear relationship using Ordinary Least Squares (OLS), the analyst applies a fourth-degree polynomial expansion, producing the following predictors: X, X₂, X₃, X₄ The resulting model is fit to a historical dataset containing 10,000 executed trades. 114 Rev:081020261230 Which of the following statements correctly evaluate the statistical consequences of this polynomial expansion?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Using raw polynomial terms can create substantial multicollinearity because X, X₂, X₃, and X₄may be highly correlated. This can inflate the variances and standard errors of the individual coefficient estimates, making them unstable and difficult to interpret.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Raw powers of one feature can be highly correlated (especially uncentered/limited range), inflating coefficient variances/standard errors and making estimates unstable and hard to interpret.", "whyPool": "Raw polynomial terms derived from the same feature can be highly correlated, particularly when the feature is not centered or spans a limited range. This multicollinearity can inflate the variances and standard errors of the individual OLS coefficient estimates, making the coefficients unstable and difficult to interpret. Centering Xor using orthogonal polynomial features can improve numerical stability."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Applying a fourth-degree polynomial expansion changes the OLS optimization objective from minimizing the sum of squared residuals to minimizing the absolute rank-order distance between predicted and observed values.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Polynomial expansion changes the feature matrix, not the objective — OLS still minimizes the sum of squared residuals.", "whyPool": "Polynomial expansion changes the feature matrix, not the OLS objective function. The model still minimizes the sum of squared residuals: ෍( ௡ ̂ୀ₁ ŷ −ŷ̂)₂"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because the third- and fourth-degree terms provide more flexibility than is required to represent the true quadratic relationship, they can reduce approximation bias but increase estimation variance, creating a greater risk of fitting noise and unusual observations in the training sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Cubic/quartic terms exceed the true quadratic form: they may slightly cut bias but raise estimation variance, increasing the risk of fitting noise/outliers.", "whyPool": "A quadratic model is sufficient to represent the assumed functional form. Adding unnecessary cubic and quartic terms increases flexibility and may slightly reduce approximation bias, but it also increases estimation variance. Those additional terms may fit sample-specific noise or unusual observations, increasing the risk of overfitting and poorer out- of-sample performance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Polynomial feature expansion is mathematically equivalent to transforming the input using its cumulative distribution function, thereby eliminating the model’s sensitivity to extreme observations and structural regime changes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Polynomial expansion creates powers of the feature, not a CDF transform; it neither removes sensitivity to extremes nor protects against regime change.", "whyPool": "Polynomial expansion creates powers of the original feature; it does not transform values according to a cumulative distribution function. It neither eliminates sensitivity to extreme observations nor protects the model from structural regime changes."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The added flexibility of a fourth-degree polynomial guarantees zero out-of-sample prediction error because a fourth- degree function can perfectly represent any continuous relationship in financial data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Added flexibility guarantees neither zero training error nor any particular OOS error; financial data are noisy and extra terms may fit non-generalizing idiosyncrasies.", "whyPool": "Greater flexibility does not guarantee zero training error and cannot guarantee any particular out-of-sample error. Financial data contain noise, and unnecessary polynomial terms may fit idiosyncrasies in the training sample that do not generalize to new trades."}], "reference": "Multicollinearity of polynomial terms, OLS objective, overfitting — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.2–3.3.3); scikit-learn `PolynomialFeatures` docs.", "n": 127}, {"id": "ML-D4G3Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative equity hedge fund is enhancing its cross-sectional return prediction model. The current system uses a standard linear regression on momentum, value, and quality factors. The Head of Research proposes expanding the feature space using polynomial transformations of momentum (e.g., (M^2, M^3)) to capture potential nonlinear effects observed during recent regime shifts. However, the Portfolio Manager is concerned about overfitting and model 115 Rev:081020261230 instability, especially given that the model is used for portfolio construction rather than purely prediction. The risk team emphasizes that any increase in model flexibility must be justified by out-of-sample performance improvements.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Adding polynomial features to a linear regression model increases the model’s capacity, allowing it to represent nonlinear relationships between predictors and returns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Polynomial expansion increases capacity, letting linear regression represent nonlinear relationships via the transformed input space.", "whyPool": "Polynomial feature expansion increases model flexibility by allowing linear regression to fit nonlinear relationships through transformed input space."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A higher-degree polynomial model will always outperform a lower-degree polynomial model on out-of-sample data if it achieves lower training error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Lower training error does not guarantee better OOS performance, especially for high-capacity models prone to overfitting.", "whyPool": "Lower training error does not necessarily translate to better out-of-sample performance due to overfitting, especially in high-capacity models."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Increasing polynomial degree reduces bias but may increase variance, reflecting a bias–variance tradeoff.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Increasing degree typically lowers bias but raises variance — the fundamental bias-variance tradeoff.", "whyPool": "Increasing model complexity typically reduces bias but increases variance, illustrating the fundamental bias– variance tradeoff in statistical learning."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A polynomial regression model is no longer a linear model because it violates the assumption of linearity in the predictors.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Polynomial regression is still linear *in the parameters*, so it remains a linear model in the statistical sense even though it is nonlinear in the raw features.", "whyPool": "Polynomial regression is still linear in the parameters, even though it is nonlinear in the original features, and thus remains a linear model in the statistical sense."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If a polynomial model fits training data extremely well, it indicates that the model has successfully captured the true underlying data-generating process.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Strong in-sample fit does not imply the true data-generating process was captured; it may reflect overfitting to noise.", "whyPool": "Strong in-sample fit does not imply that the model has learned the true data-generating process; it may instead reflect overfitting to noise. G4: Residuals and Model Fit"}], "reference": "Linear-in-parameters definition, capacity, bias-variance — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.2), ch.2 (§2.2); ESL ch.5.", "n": 128}, {"id": "ML-D4G4Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A currency desk defines each residual as actual return minus predicted return. During a review, the PM sees several large positive residuals around central-bank announcements and asks whether the sign and pattern matter. The team wants to distinguish ordinary misses from a systematic modeling gap. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: A positive residual means the realized return exceeded the model's prediction under the stated convention. 116 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (\"a positive residual means realized return exceeded the prediction\") is accurate under actual−predicted, so labeling it \"false\" is itself incorrect.", "whyPool": "Actual minus predicted is positive when the model underpredicts the outcome."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A residual of zero means the prediction matched that observation exactly.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a zero residual means the observed and predicted values matched exactly for that observation.", "whyPool": "The observed and predicted values are equal in that case."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Large residuals clustered around announcements may indicate omitted event-related structure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: large residuals clustered around announcements suggest omitted event-related structure (non-random errors).", "whyPool": "Context-specific clustering suggests the errors may not be random."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Residuals must all equal zero for a fitted model to be useful.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: useful models retain irreducible and estimation error; residuals need not all equal zero.", "whyPool": "Useful models normally retain irreducible and estimation error."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Averaging signed residuals alone can hide large positive and negative errors that cancel.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: averaging signed residuals can hide large positive and negative misses that cancel.", "whyPool": "Opposite signs can offset despite substantial prediction misses."}], "reference": "Residual definition and diagnostics — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.3); concept \"signed vs. absolute error, error cancellation.\"", "n": 129}, {"id": "ML-D4G4Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk model predicts daily portfolio losses. Its residual plot is narrow for small predicted losses but fans out as predicted loss grows. The chief risk officer worries that a single headline error number understates uncertainty precisely when exposure is greatest.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The fan shape suggests that residual variability changes with the prediction level.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A fan shape indicates residual variability changes with the prediction level — non-constant error variance (heteroscedasticity).", "whyPool": "Increasing spread indicates non-constant error variance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A near-zero mean residual would prove that the error variance is constant.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A near-zero mean residual is a different property from constant variance; it does not prove homoscedasticity.", "whyPool": "Mean error and variation in error are different properties."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The model may provide less reliable point predictions in the high-loss region.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Wider residuals in the high-loss region imply less reliable point predictions there.", "whyPool": "Wider residuals imply greater uncertainty around those predictions."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Inspecting residuals by prediction range can reveal behavior hidden by one aggregate metric.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Inspecting residuals by prediction range reveals behavior hidden by one aggregate error metric.", "whyPool": "Segmented diagnostics expose where errors concentrate."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The fan shape necessarily means that every coefficient has the wrong sign.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Changing error variance does not determine coefficient signs.", "whyPool": "Changing variance does not determine coefficient signs. 117 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Heteroscedasticity, residual-vs-fitted diagnostics — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.3, non-constant variance of error terms).", "n": 130}, {"id": "ML-D4G4Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two regression models forecast monthly fund flows. Model A has many small errors and one extremely large miss; Model B has somewhat larger typical errors but no catastrophic miss. The client is especially sensitive to large forecasting failures and asks the team to compare MAE and squared-error measures.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Squaring residuals gives unusually large errors greater influence than they receive under absolute error.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Squaring residuals gives unusually large errors more relative weight than absolute error does.", "whyPool": "Squaring increases the relative penalty as error magnitude grows."}, {"L": "B", "text": "MAE and mean squared error must always rank two models identically.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Because MSE and MAE weight large errors differently, they can rank the two models differently.", "whyPool": "Their different weighting of large errors can reverse model rankings."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Model A's single extreme miss could dominate its mean squared error.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Model A's single extreme miss contributes its squared magnitude and can dominate its MSE.", "whyPool": "An outlying residual contributes its squared magnitude."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The appropriate metric should reflect the client's relative cost of ordinary and extreme misses.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The chosen metric should reflect the client's relative cost of ordinary vs. extreme misses.", "whyPool": "Evaluation should align with the operational loss associated with mistakes."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A lower training error under either metric guarantees lower future error under that metric.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Lower training error under a metric does not guarantee lower future error under that metric (generalization is not assured).", "whyPool": "Training fit alone does not guarantee generalization."}], "reference": "MAE vs. MSE, outlier sensitivity, loss selection — ISLR ch.2 (§2.2, MSE); scikit-learn \"Metrics and scoring\" (regression metrics).", "n": 131}, {"id": "ML-D4G4Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A systematic equity hedge fund is evaluating a linear regression model used to predict next-month stock returns for portfolio construction. The Portfolio Manager is focused on how well the model explains return variation across stocks, while the Head of Quant is reviewing diagnostic plots to assess model adequacy. The risk team warns that recent regime changes may have altered relationships between factors and returns, potentially making historical fit statistics misleading. You are asked to evaluate several statements about residuals and model fit before the model is approved for production use.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Residuals represent the difference between observed returns and model-predicted returns for each observation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A residual is the observed return minus the model-predicted return for each observation (ei = yi − ŷi).", "whyPool": "For each observation, the residual is the observed return minus the return predicted by the fitted model: ê =ŷ −ŷ̂ A positive residual means the observed return was higher than predicted, while a negative residual means it was lower than predicted."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If a model has low training error, it implies that residuals are randomly distributed with no structure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Low training error does not guarantee structureless residuals; systematic patterns can persist under misspecification even with small in-sample error.", "whyPool": "Low training error does not guarantee that residuals are structureless; systematic patterns can still exist even when in-sample error is small, especially under misspecification. ("}, {"L": "C", "text": "Systematic patterns in residual plots (e.g., curvature when plotted against fitted values) can indicate model misspecification.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Systematic patterns (e.g., curvature vs. fitted values) can indicate misspecification such as nonlinearity or omitted variables.", "whyPool": "Structured patterns in residual plots often indicate violations of model assumptions such as nonlinearity or omitted variables."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The sum of residuals in an ordinary least squares regression with an intercept is exactly zero in-sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With an intercept, OLS residuals sum to exactly zero in-sample by the first-order optimality conditions.", "whyPool": "In ordinary least squares regression with an intercept, residuals sum to zero by construction due to the first- order optimality conditions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A model with smaller residuals always leads to better out-of-sample trading performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Smaller in-sample residuals may reflect overfitting and do not always translate to better OOS trading performance.", "whyPool": "Lower in-sample residuals may reflect overfitting and do not necessarily translate to improved out-of-sample performance in trading contexts."}], "reference": "Residual definition, residual sum-to-zero, diagnostics — ISLR ch.3 (§3.1, §3.3.3).", "n": 132}, {"id": "ML-D4G4Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A data scientist fits an Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) linear regression model to predict the annualized Sharpe ratio of a systematic trend-following portfolio using a set of market microstructure features. After fitting the model, the data scientist calculates the residual for each observation: ê =ŷ −ŷ̂ where ŷis the observed Sharpe ratio and ŷ̂is the Sharpe ratio predicted by the model. The residuals are then examined using diagnostic plots to evaluate the adequacy of the fitted model. Which of the following statements correctly interpret residuals or explain what particular residual patterns reveal about the model?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A residual is the signed vertical difference between an observed target value and its fitted value. It represents the portion of that individual observation’s outcome that the fitted model did not explain.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A residual is the signed vertical distance between the observed target and the fitted value — the part of that observation the model did not explain.", "whyPool": "A residual is defined as ê =ŷ −ŷ̂. It measures the signed vertical difference between an observation and the fitted regression model. A positive residual indicates that the observed value exceeded the prediction, while a negative residual indicates that the observed value was below the prediction. 119 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "A distinct U-shaped or parabolic pattern in a plot of residuals against an input feature confirms that the model has captured the relevant relationship and that no additional feature engineering is needed.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A U-shaped/parabolic residual pattern signals *unmodeled* nonlinear structure, not a well-captured relationship; it suggests adding a transformation.", "whyPool": "A systematic curved pattern indicates that the residuals still contain structure. A U-shaped or parabolic pattern commonly suggests that the model has failed to capture a nonlinear relationship. Adding an appropriate transformation, such as a quadratic term, may improve the model. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 3.3)"}, {"L": "C", "text": "OLS estimates the model coefficients by minimizing the sum of the absolute residuals, and this optimization objective guarantees that the residuals have a sample mean of zero.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "OLS minimizes the sum of *squared* residuals, not absolute residuals; the intercept's first-order condition is what forces the residual mean to zero.", "whyPool": "OLS minimizes the sum of squared residuals: ෍ê₂ ௡ ̂ୀ₁ It does not minimize the sum of absolute residuals. When an intercept is included, the OLS first-order condition for the intercept causes the in-sample residuals to sum to zero and therefore have a mean of zero. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 3.1)"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Long clusters of positive residuals followed by long clusters of negative residuals when plotted chronologically provide evidence of serial correlation in the model’s errors.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Long runs of same-sign residuals plotted chronologically indicate serial correlation (autocorrelation) in the errors.", "whyPool": "Persistent clusters of residuals with the same sign suggest that nearby errors are related rather than independent. In chronological data, this is evidence of serial correlation or autocorrelation. The pattern does not, by itself, identify a nonlinear interaction between input features. (Ref: Handbook of AI Ch. 5.1)"}, {"L": "E", "text": "For an OLS model with an intercept, an in-sample R₂of 0.00 means that every residual is zero and that the fitted regression passes through every observed data point.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "R2 = 0 means the model explains none of the variation beyond the sample mean, not that every residual is zero (that would be R2 = 1).", "whyPool": "For an OLS model with an intercept, R₂ = 0means that the fitted model explains none of the target variation beyond what is explained by predicting the sample mean. It does not mean that the residuals are zero. A perfect in-sample fit in which every residual equals zero corresponds to R₂ = 1, provided the target variable has nonzero variance. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 3.1)"}], "reference": "Residuals, OLS objective, serial correlation, R2 interpretation — ISLR ch.3 (§3.1, §3.3.3).", "n": 133}, {"id": "ML-D4G4Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative macro researcher is evaluating a multivariable linear regression model built to predict the forward one- month volatility of the Euro–U.S. Dollar (EUR/USD) exchange rate. The model uses four structural economic features. The researcher’s diagnostics indicate that the errors have a conditional mean of zero given the predictors, E[ε ∣ X] = 0, and exhibit constant variance. However, a histogram and Normal Quantile–Quantile (Q-Q) plot of the historical training residuals, ê =ŷ −ŷ̂, show that the residuals are symmetric and centered around zero but have an extremely heavy-tailed (leptokurtic) distribution. In the Q-Q plot, the observations deviate sharply from the 45-degree reference line at both extremes. Which of the following statements correctly diagnose the residual distribution and evaluate its implications for the linear regression framework?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The heavy-tailed distribution observed in the Q-Q plot indicates a violation of the classical assumption that the regression error terms are normally distributed.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Points curving away from the 45° reference line at both ends show the residuals have heavier tails than a normal distribution — a violation of the normality assumption.", "whyPool": "A normal Q-Q plot maps the quantiles of the sample residuals against the theoretical quantiles of a standard normal distribution. When the points curve away from the 45-degree reference line at both ends, it mathematically demonstrates that the residuals possess heavier tails (more extreme outliers) than a normal distribution allows. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 3.3)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Because the zero conditional mean condition is satisfied, the heavy-tailed error distribution does not, by itself, cause the OLS coefficient estimates to be biased.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Unbiasedness rests on E[ε\\|X] = 0, not normality, so heavy tails alone do not bias the coefficients (though they raise sensitivity to extremes).", "whyPool": "Unbiasedness of the OLS coefficient estimates depends on the zero conditional mean condition, E[ε ∣ X] = 0, rather than on normally distributed errors. Therefore, heavy tails alone do not bias the coefficient estimates. They can, however, make the estimates more sensitive to extreme observations and weaken the reliability of conventional finite- sample statistical inference."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A departure from normality in the error terms means that the OLS optimization objective breaks down, causing the model to lose its mathematical status as the Best Linear Unbiased Estimator (BLUE).", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Gauss-Markov requires linearity, exogeneity, homoscedasticity, and no perfect collinearity — not normality; OLS remains BLUE without normal errors (normality is only for exact finite-sample testing).", "whyPool": "The Gauss-Markov theorem states that OLS is the Best Linear Unbiased Estimator (BLUE) under the assumptions of linearity, strict exogeneity, homoscedasticity, and non-collinearity. Normality of the error terms is not a requirement for OLS to be BLUE; it is only required for exact finite-sample hypothesis testing."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The primary operational risk of heavy-tailed residuals is that standard statistical inference fails; the standard errors of the coefficients become unreliable, leading to invalid t-statistics and compromised confidence intervals in finite samples. 120 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Heavy-tailed errors invalidate the finite-sample formulas behind t-statistics and confidence intervals, so inference (significance) becomes unreliable.", "whyPool": "When residuals are heavily non-normal (leptokurtic), the standard formulas used to compute t-statistics and p- values lose their exact finite-sample validity. In the presence of fat tails, the model will frequently underestimate the probability of extreme errors, leading the researcher to overstate the statistical significance of predictive features."}, {"L": "E", "text": "To remediate this specific structural issue and force the error distribution to conform to classical normality assumptions, the researcher must implement an interaction term that multiplies the two most collinear features together.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Adding an interaction term changes functional form, not the noise distribution; fat tails call for robust regression (e.g., Huber) or a Student-t error model.", "whyPool": "Adding an interaction term changes the structural relationship between features, which can fix a misspecified functional form (like a U-shape in the residuals), but it does not alter the underlying statistical distribution of the target's stochastic noise. To address fat-tailed errors, the analyst should explore robust regression techniques (like Huber loss) or assume a Student's t-distribution for the errors. G5: When Linear Models Fail"}], "reference": "Error-distribution diagnostics, Gauss-Markov / BLUE, robust regression — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.3); ESL ch.3; concept \"Q-Q plot / leptokurtosis.\"", "n": 134}, {"id": "ML-D4G5Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A volatility-trading desk observes that both unusually low and unusually high market volatility can precede positive strategy returns, while moderate volatility does not. A model using only a single raw volatility term predicts a monotonic relationship. The PM asks why its decisions repeatedly miss both extremes. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A single raw feature in a linear model cannot directly represent a U-shaped relationship.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: one straight-line slope cannot turn direction across the range, so a single raw feature cannot represent a U-shape.", "whyPool": "One straight-line slope cannot turn direction across the feature range."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Adding a squared volatility term could represent this pattern.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a squared volatility term permits curvature and a turning point.", "whyPool": "A quadratic term permits curvature and a turning point."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Changing only the intercept can make one straight line curve upward at both extremes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: the intercept only shifts the line vertically; changing it cannot make a line curve upward at both extremes.", "whyPool": "The intercept shifts a line vertically but does not change its shape."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A model can have low overall error yet still fail systematically in economically important regions. 121 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a model can have low overall error yet fail systematically in economically important regions.", "whyPool": "Aggregate performance can conceal localized failure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The repeated pattern in residuals is a reason to reconsider the feature representation or model family.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a repeated structured residual pattern is a reason to reconsider feature representation or model family.", "whyPool": "Structured errors suggest missing capacity rather than purely random noise."}], "reference": "Non-monotone relationships, polynomial terms, localized failure — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.2–3.3.3); ch.7 (basis functions).", "n": 135}, {"id": "ML-D4G5Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A merger-arbitrage team believes deal size matters differently when regulatory risk is high. Its additive linear model includes deal size and regulatory risk separately but no interaction. The head of trading asks whether the current equation can express a size effect whose direction changes with regulatory risk. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "In the additive model, the coefficient on deal size gives the same marginal size effect at every regulatory-risk value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: without an interaction, the additive slope on deal size is constant at every regulatory-risk value.", "whyPool": "Without an interaction, the additive slope on size is constant."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A deal-size-by-regulatory-risk interaction can allow the modeled size effect to depend on regulatory risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a deal-size × regulatory-risk product lets the modeled size effect depend on regulatory risk.", "whyPool": "The product term changes the effective size slope as risk changes."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Including both raw variables separately is mathematically equivalent to including their product.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: including both raw variables separately is not equivalent to including their product; additive effects cannot reproduce a multiplicative term.", "whyPool": "Separate additive effects cannot generally reproduce a multiplicative term."}, {"L": "D", "text": "An interaction may improve representation but should still be evaluated out of sample.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: an added interaction may capture signal or noise, so generalization must be tested out-of-sample.", "whyPool": "Added capacity may capture signal or noise, so generalization must be tested."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If an interaction is useful, the underlying relationship is no longer representable by a model linear in coefficients.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: the product can be treated as another feature with its own coefficient, so the model stays linear in coefficients.", "whyPool": "The product can be treated as another feature with its own linear coefficient. 122 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Interaction terms, linear-in-coefficients, additivity assumption — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.2, removing the additive assumption).", "n": 136}, {"id": "ML-D4G5Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A lending desk reframes a return forecast as a decision to approve or reject a trade. Profitable observations form two separated clusters around an unprofitable center in feature space. A manager proposes using one linear scoring boundary because it is fast and transparent.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: A single linear boundary divides the feature space into two half-spaces.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (\"a single linear boundary divides the feature space into two half-spaces\") is accurate — a linear rule separates with a hyperplane — so calling it \"false\" is itself false.", "whyPool": "A linear decision rule separates observations with a hyperplane."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Two separated profitable regions may not be captured well by one linear boundary.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Two disconnected profitable regions can require a nonlinear representation that one linear boundary cannot capture.", "whyPool": "Disconnected regions can require nonlinear representation."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Collecting more data guarantees that a misspecified linear boundary will represent the clustered structure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "More data cannot remove a structural limitation of the chosen hypothesis space (a misspecified linear boundary).", "whyPool": "More data cannot remove a structural limitation in the chosen hypothesis space."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Engineered nonlinear features or a more flexible model family may address the geometry.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Engineered nonlinear features or a more flexible model family can produce a boundary better matched to the geometry.", "whyPool": "Either approach can create a boundary better matched to the pattern."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Operational simplicity can still justify a linear model if the performance tradeoff is understood and acceptable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Operational simplicity can still justify a linear model if the performance tradeoff (latency, stability, governance) is understood and acceptable.", "whyPool": "Model choice can legitimately balance predictive performance against latency, stability, and governance needs."}], "reference": "Linear separability, hyperplane boundaries, feature engineering — ISLR ch.4 (§4.3, logistic regression boundary), ch.9 (§9.1, hyperplanes).", "n": 137}, {"id": "ML-D4G5Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative research team is developing a machine learning model to predict whether a corporate bond will experience a rating downgrade. The binary target is coded as 1 for a downgrade and 0 for a stable or upgraded rating. The model uses two standardized financial features: •  X₁: Interest Coverage Ratio •  X₂: Leverage Ratio Historical evidence suggests that downgrade risk increases sharply when a firm simultaneously has very low interest coverage and very high leverage. Either condition alone is less concerning because management may still have sufficient financial flexibility. The team fits a standard logistic regression model using only the two original features: logቆ p(X) 1 −p(X)ቇ=β₀+β₁X₁+β₂X₂ Despite the clean, standardized data, the model performs poorly and fails to capture the elevated downgrade risk associated with the combination of extreme values. Which of the following statements correctly diagnose the model’s limitations or identify ways to address them? 123 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Although logistic regression applies a nonlinear sigmoid function to produce probabilities, a model containing only X₁and X₂produces a linear decision boundary in the original two-dimensional feature space.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The sigmoid makes probability nonlinear in the linear predictor, but at any fixed threshold the boundary β0+β1X1+β2X2 = const is a straight line in (X1,X2) — so raw-feature logistic regression cannot draw a curved boundary.", "whyPool": "The sigmoid function makes the predicted probability a nonlinear function of the linear predictor, but it does not make the standard classification boundary nonlinear in the original features. For any fixed probability threshold c, the boundary satisfies β₀+β₁X₁+β₂X₂ = logቀ c 1 −cቁ, which is a straight line in the (X₁,X₂)feature space. Therefore, logistic regression using only the two raw features cannot directly represent a curved decision boundary. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 4.3)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Without an interaction term, the model assumes that the effect of X₁on the log-odds of a downgrade does not depend on the value of X₂, preventing it from directly representing the hypothesized compounding effect.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Without an interaction the log-odds are additive: the effect of X1 is always β1 regardless of X2, so the model cannot express the compounding \"low coverage dangerous only when leverage is high\" effect.", "whyPool": "Without an interaction term, the model is additive in the log-odds. The change in log-odds associated with a one-unit increase in X₁is always β₁, regardless of the value of X₂. The model therefore cannot directly express a relationship in which low coverage becomes especially dangerous only when leverage is also high. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 3.3)"}, {"L": "C", "text": "The model’s poor fit indicates that maximum-likelihood estimation probably became trapped in a local optimum created by the nonlinear sigmoid function.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The logistic negative log-likelihood is convex, so there are no multiple local optima to trap optimization; the poor fit is inadequate feature specification, not a local-optimum problem.", "whyPool": "The negative log-likelihood objective for standard logistic regression is convex, so the sigmoid function does not create multiple local optima in which routine optimization becomes trapped. A unique finite solution is not guaranteed in every dataset—for example, exact feature dependence or complete class separation can create estimation problems—but the poor fit described here reflects an inadequate feature specification rather than a local-optimum problem."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The team can retain the logistic regression framework while adding an interaction feature, X₁X₂, allowing the effect of one financial ratio on the predicted log-odds to depend on the other ratio.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Adding X1X2 keeps the model linear in coefficients while letting each ratio's effect on log-odds depend on the other, producing a nonlinear boundary in the original space.", "whyPool": "Adding X₁X₂produces the model logቆ p(X) 1 −p(X)ቇ=β₀+β₁X₁+β₂X₂+β₃X₁X₂. The model remains linear in its coefficients, but the interaction term allows the effect of X₁on the log-odds to vary with X₂, and vice versa. This can represent the hypothesized joint distress effect and produce a nonlinear boundary in the original feature space. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 3.3)"}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the true classes follow a sufficiently complex nonlinear pattern, such as a checkerboard-style boundary, no transformation or expansion of the input features could ever allow a logistic regression model to classify the observations accurately.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Logistic regression is linear in the *expanded* features, so suitable polynomial/interaction/spline/RBF transforms can separate complex patterns — though richer expansions raise cost, variance, and overfitting risk.", "whyPool": "Logistic regression is linear in the expanded features supplied to it, not necessarily in the original inputs. Appropriate polynomial terms, interaction terms, splines, radial basis functions, or other nonlinear transformations can map a complex pattern into a feature space where a linear separator performs well. However, increasingly complex expansions can raise computational cost, estimation variance, and overfitting risk, so strong training accuracy is not guaranteed to generalize. 124 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Logistic regression boundary, interactions, convex likelihood, feature maps — ISLR ch.4 (§4.3), ch.3 (§3.3.2); ESL ch.4.", "pdfArtifacts": "ቆቇ", "n": 138}, {"id": "ML-D4G5Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative portfolio manager is developing a linear regression model to predict next-month cross-sectional stock returns. The model uses two standardized features: •  X₁: Log Market Capitalization •  X₂: Six-Month Momentum The manager hypothesizes that momentum operates differently across size regimes: stronger momentum predicts higher returns among smaller companies but lower returns among larger companies. The manager initially fits the following additive Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) model: ŷ =β₀+β₁X₁+β₂X₂ Out-of-sample testing suggests that the model does not capture the hypothesized size-dependent momentum effect. Which of the following statements correctly diagnose the model’s limitation or identify how the model could represent this relationship?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "In the additive model, a one-unit increase in momentum changes the predicted return by β₂, regardless of the company’s market capitalization. The model therefore cannot directly represent a momentum effect whose slope changes with company size.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "In the additive model ∂ŷ/∂X2 = β2 is constant across all X1, so it can estimate only one common momentum slope and cannot flip sign with company size.", "whyPool": "In the additive model, ŷ =β₀+β₁X₁+β₂X₂, the marginal effect of momentum is ∂ŷ ∂X₂ =β₂. This effect is constant at every value of X₁. Consequently, the model cannot directly represent a positive momentum slope for smaller companies and a negative slope for larger companies. It can estimate only one common momentum coefficient across the entire sample."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The model’s poor performance indicates that the OLS objective became non-convex and that the optimization procedure was trapped in a suboptimal local minimum.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "OLS minimizes a convex quadratic RSS, so a missing interaction produces a poorly specified model, not local minima; the solution is still the global minimum (unique if the design has full column rank).", "whyPool": "OLS minimizes the residual sum of squares, which is a convex quadratic function of the coefficients. Missing an important interaction may produce a poorly specified model, but it does not create local minima in the OLS objective. The fitted coefficients still provide a global minimum of the specified objective, although the solution may not be unique if the feature matrix lacks full column rank. 125 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Adding the interaction feature X₁X₂would allow the estimated effect of momentum on predicted returns to vary as a function of market capitalization.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Adding X1X2 gives ∂ŷ/∂X2 = β2 + β3X1, letting the momentum slope vary with size and cross zero within the observed range.", "whyPool": "Adding the interaction term produces ŷ =β₀+β₁X₁+β₂X₂+β₃X₁X₂. The marginal effect of momentum then becomes ∂ŷ ∂X₂ =β₂+β₃X₁. The momentum slope can therefore vary with company size. If the estimated coefficients cause β₂+β₃X₁to cross zero within the observed size range, the model can represent momentum predicting higher returns for smaller companies and lower returns for larger companies."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Once the interaction term X₁X₂is added, the model is no longer a linear regression model because its prediction is no longer an additive function of the original features.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The expanded model is still linear regression (linear in β0...β3); the interaction makes it nonadditive/nonlinear in the original space but OLS still fits a linear combination of feature columns.", "whyPool": "The expanded model remains a linear regression model because it is linear in the coefficients β₀,β₁,β₂,β₃. The interaction makes the relationship nonadditive—and potentially nonlinear when viewed in the original (X₁,X₂)feature space—but OLS still estimates a linear combination of the supplied feature columns."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Adding the interaction term will necessarily eliminate multicollinearity between market capitalization and momentum, producing more stable coefficient estimates.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Adding an interaction does not eliminate multicollinearity — the product can itself be correlated with its components; centering may reduce it but neither guarantees stable estimates.", "whyPool": "Adding an interaction term does not eliminate multicollinearity between the original predictors. The interaction feature may itself be correlated with one or both component features, depending on their distributions and whether they have been centered. Centering the predictors before constructing the interaction can sometimes reduce nonessential multicollinearity, but neither centering nor adding an interaction guarantees stable coefficient estimates."}], "reference": "Interaction terms, marginal effects, convex OLS objective, multicollinearity — ISLR ch.3 (§3.3.2–3.3.3).", "n": 139}, {"id": "ML-D4G5Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a quantitative researcher at a hedge fund evaluating whether a linear regression model should be used to predict next-day stock returns. After several months of testing, the Portfolio Manager notices that the model performs well during calm markets but consistently misses sharp reversals during volatile periods. She asks whether simply collecting more data will solve the problem or if the modeling approach itself is fundamentally limited.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "If the true relationship between the features and next-day returns is highly nonlinear, a simple linear regression model may exhibit systematic prediction errors even with abundant training data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If the true relationship is highly nonlinear, a linear model shows systematic (bias) errors regardless of dataset size.", "whyPool": "Linear regression assumes a linear relationship between predictors and the response. When the underlying relationship is nonlinear, the model exhibits systematic bias regardless of dataset size. Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 (Statistical Learning), Chapter 3.1–3.3 (Linear Regression)."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A linear regression model can naturally capture interactions between two features without explicitly including interaction terms.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Standard linear regression models only additive effects; interactions must be explicitly engineered (e.g., X1X2) before they can be learned.", "whyPool": "Standard linear regression only models additive effects. Feature interactions must be explicitly created (e.g., (X_1X_2)) before they can be learned. Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 3.1–3.3 (Multiple Linear Regression)."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A residual plot showing a clear curved pattern suggests that the linear model is failing to capture the underlying relationship.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A clearly curved residual pattern indicates unmodeled nonlinear structure.", "whyPool": "A non-random residual pattern, such as curvature, indicates that important nonlinear structure has not been captured by the model. Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 3.3 (Assessing Model Accuracy)."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Increasing the size of the training dataset always eliminates prediction errors caused by an overly simple linear model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "More data reduces estimation variance but cannot overcome high bias from an inappropriate model class — an underpowered linear model stays systematically biased.", "whyPool": "More data reduces estimation variance but cannot overcome high bias caused by an inappropriate model class. An underpowered linear model remains systematically biased. Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 (Bias–Variance Tradeoff); Chapter 3 (Linear Regression)."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Adding engineered nonlinear features (such as polynomial or interaction terms) can allow a linear regression model to fit relationships that would otherwise be impossible with only the original features.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Polynomial and interaction features increase expressive power while keeping the model linear in its coefficients.", "whyPool": "Polynomial features and interaction terms increase the expressive power of a linear model while keeping the model linear in its coefficients. Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 3.3 (Extensions of Linear Regression); Deep Learning: Foundations and Concepts, Chapter 1 (Model Capacity). G6: Regularization Intuition"}], "reference": "Bias from misspecification, additivity, capacity via feature engineering — ISLR ch.2 (§2.2, bias-variance), ch.3 (§3.3.2–3.3.3).", "n": 140}, {"id": "ML-D4G6Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A factor model has hundreds of correlated signals but only a few years of data. It achieves very low training error using large positive and negative coefficients, yet small changes in the sample produce very different positions. The risk committee asks whether regularization could make the strategy more stable. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Regularization can discourage the model from relying on extremely large coefficients.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a coefficient penalty makes extreme weights costlier, discouraging reliance on very large coefficients.", "whyPool": "A coefficient penalty makes extreme weights more costly to the fitting objective."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Constraining coefficients may increase training error while improving out-of-sample performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: constraining coefficients can raise training error while improving OOS performance (accept bias, cut variance).", "whyPool": "Accepting some bias can reduce variance and improve generalization."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Regularization guarantees that every retained coefficient has the correct causal interpretation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: shrinkage does not turn predictive associations into causal effects, so it guarantees no causal interpretation.", "whyPool": "Shrinkage does not transform predictive associations into causal effects."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Large offsetting coefficients on correlated features can be a sign of an unstable fit.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: large offsetting coefficients on correlated features are a sign of an unstable fit.", "whyPool": "Small data changes can shift weight dramatically between redundant signals. 127 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "The appropriate regularization strength should be selected using out-of-sample validation rather than training error alone.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: regularization strength should be chosen by out-of-sample validation, not training error (which favors weaker constraints).", "whyPool": "Training error generally favors weaker constraints, even when they overfit."}], "reference": "Regularization (Ridge/Lasso), bias-variance, validation for λ — ISLR ch.6 (§6.2, shrinkage methods).", "n": 141}, {"id": "ML-D4G6Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quant team compares an unregularized regression with increasingly constrained versions. As the penalty strengthens, the coefficients shrink and training error rises; validation error first falls and then rises. The PM concludes that the strongest possible penalty must be safest because it produces the smallest coefficients.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: The initial validation improvement is consistent with reducing overfitting.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (\"the initial validation improvement is consistent with reducing overfitting\") is accurate — moderate shrinkage cuts sensitivity to noise — so calling it \"false\" is itself false.", "whyPool": "Moderate shrinkage can reduce sensitivity to sample noise."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The later validation deterioration can indicate underfitting from excessive constraint.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The later validation deterioration can indicate underfitting from excessive shrinkage suppressing genuine structure.", "whyPool": "Excessive shrinkage can suppress genuine predictive structure."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The strongest penalty is always optimal whenever model stability matters.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The strongest penalty is not always optimal; an over-constrained model can be stable but uninformative.", "whyPool": "An overly constrained model may become stable but uninformative."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Regularization introduces a tradeoff between fitting the observed sample and limiting model complexity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Regularization trades data fit against coefficient size/complexity — that tradeoff is the whole point.", "whyPool": "The objective balances data fit against coefficient size or complexity."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The penalty with the lowest validation error is a more defensible choice than the penalty with the smallest training error.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The penalty with the lowest validation error is more defensible than the one with the smallest training error, which favors overfitting.", "whyPool": "Validation performance better estimates the constraint's generalization tradeoff."}], "reference": "Regularization tuning, validation curves, bias-variance — ISLR ch.6 (§6.2); ch.5 (cross-validation).", "n": 142}, {"id": "ML-D4G6Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A systematic fund adds many weak and redundant predictors to a return model. One proposal uses a penalty that smoothly shrinks coefficients; another favors a sparse solution by driving some coefficients to zero. The governance lead wants a smaller model but does not want to assume that any penalty automatically identifies the true economic signals.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Different regularization penalties can produce different coefficient and feature-retention behavior.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim (\"different penalties can produce different coefficient and feature-retention behavior\") is accurate — penalty shape governs how weight is distributed/removed — so calling it \"false\" is itself false.", "whyPool": "The shape of the penalty affects how the model distributes and removes weight."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A penalty capable of producing zero coefficients can support a sparser deployed model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A penalty that can produce exactly-zero coefficients (Lasso) supports a sparser deployed model.", "whyPool": "Zero coefficients allow those inputs to be omitted from the prediction equation."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A zero regularized coefficient proves that the corresponding feature has no relationship with returns in the population.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A zero regularized coefficient does not prove no population relationship — it depends on sample, penalty strength, scaling, and competing predictors.", "whyPool": "The result depends on the sample, penalty strength, scaling, and competing predictors."}, {"L": "D", "text": "With strongly correlated predictors, the particular feature retained can be sensitive to the data sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With strongly correlated predictors, which feature is retained can be sensitive to the sample (redundant variables substitute for one another).", "whyPool": "Redundant variables can substitute for one another under small sample changes."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Sparsity, predictive performance, and stability are distinct criteria that should be evaluated rather than treated as identical. 128 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Sparsity, predictive performance, and stability are distinct criteria to evaluate separately, not treat as identical.", "whyPool": "A sparse model is not automatically the most accurate or most stable one."}], "reference": "Lasso sparsity vs. Ridge shrinkage, selection instability — ISLR ch.6 (§6.2, the lasso, comparison with ridge).", "n": 143}, {"id": "ML-D4G6Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative research team is developing a linear regression model to predict next-week asset returns using 150 macroeconomic and technical indicators. Many of the indicators are highly correlated, and the number of features is large relative to the available training sample. When the team fits an unregularized Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) model, the estimated coefficients are large and unstable: relatively small changes in the training sample produce substantial changes in individual coefficient estimates. The team therefore considers Ridge and Lasso regression. All predictors are standardized before the models are fit so that the penalty is applied comparably across features. Which of the following statements correctly describe the mechanics and tradeoffs of regularization in this setting?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Ridge and Lasso modify the OLS objective by adding a penalty based on the coefficient magnitudes, accepting some deterioration in training fit in exchange for potentially lower estimation variance and better out-of-sample performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Ridge and Lasso add a coefficient penalty to the RSS, accepting worse training fit for lower estimation variance and potentially better OOS performance.", "whyPool": "Ridge and Lasso augment the residual sum of squares with a coefficient penalty. In general form, the optimization problem is 129 Rev:081020261230 ⎩⎪⎨ ⎪⎧ ̂ ቌŷ −β₀−෍x̂௝ ௣ ௝ୀ₁ β௝ቍ ₂௡ ̂ୀ₁ +λP(β) ⎭⎪⎬ ⎪⎫ . The penalty discourages large coefficient estimates. This introduces bias, but it can substantially reduce variance when the feature set is large or highly correlated. The resulting model may therefore generalize better even though its training error is usually higher than that of OLS. The intercept is ordinarily excluded from the penalty."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Ridge regression uses an L₁penalty, λ෍∣ ௣ ௝ୀ₁ β௝ ∣, which typically sets some coefficients exactly to zero and therefore performs automatic feature selection.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Ridge uses the L2 penalty (Σβj2) and shrinks toward zero without zeroing coefficients; it is Lasso's L1 penalty that performs feature selection.", "whyPool": "Ridge regression uses the L₂penalty, λ෍β௝₂ ௣ ௝ୀ₁ . It generally shrinks coefficients toward zero without setting them exactly to zero. Lasso uses the L₁penalty, λ෍∣ ௣ ௝ୀ₁ β௝ ∣, and can set coefficients exactly to zero, thereby performing feature selection. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 6.1)"}, {"L": "C", "text": "When several predictors contain similar information, Ridge regression often distributes the predictive contribution across the correlated predictors, whereas Lasso may retain some predictors and set others to zero.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With similar-information predictors, Ridge distributes weight across the correlated group while Lasso may keep some and zero others.", "whyPool": "Ridge commonly retains all predictors and shares coefficient weight among correlated features. Lasso can produce a sparse solution by setting some coefficients to zero. When several highly correlated variables contain similar predictive information, however, Lasso may select one and discard others without the selected variable necessarily being uniquely important."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Increasing λstrengthens the regularization penalty. This generally reduces model flexibility and estimation variance while increasing bias, so the value of λshould be selected using validation data or cross-validation rather than chosen solely to minimize training error.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Larger λ strengthens the penalty, reducing flexibility/variance and raising bias, so λ should be chosen by validation/cross-validation, not training error.", "whyPool": "At λ= 0, the regularized model reduces to OLS. As λincreases, coefficient estimates are constrained more strongly, generally decreasing flexibility and variance while increasing bias. An excessively large value can cause underfitting. Because training error alone favors little or no regularization, λis ordinarily chosen using cross-validation or a separate validation set."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the true return-generating process depends on only a small subset of the 150 indicators, Lasso may be useful for producing a sparse model; however, when predictors are highly correlated, the particular variables selected by Lasso can be unstable across training samples.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Lasso can produce a sparse model when the true signal is sparse, but its selected variables can be unstable across samples when predictors are highly correlated.", "whyPool": "Lasso is especially useful when the underlying signal is sparse because its L₁penalty can remove features by assigning them coefficients of exactly zero. However, feature selection is not necessarily stable when predictors are highly correlated: small changes in the sample may cause Lasso to select a different member of a correlated group. Consequently, sparsity does not guarantee that the selected variables have uniquely reliable interpretations."}], "reference": "Ridge (L2) vs. Lasso (L1), penalty form, λ selection — ISLR ch.6 (§6.2); scikit-learn `linear_model` (`Ridge`, `Lasso`) docs.", "pdfArtifacts": "ୀ௝௣෍", "n": 144}, {"id": "ML-D4G6Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are developing a predictive model for a quantitative equity fund using hundreds of financial indicators. During model validation, the Chief Risk Officer notices that the model fits the historical training data exceptionally well but performs inconsistently on unseen market data. She asks whether constraining the model could improve its reliability, even if it slightly reduces training accuracy.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Regularization reduces the tendency of a linear regression model to overfit by discouraging excessively large coefficient values. 130 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Regularization adds a penalty that discourages large coefficients, reducing complexity and helping prevent overfitting.", "whyPool": "Regularization adds a penalty to the optimization objective that discourages large coefficient values, reducing model complexity and helping prevent overfitting. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 6 – Linear Model Selection and Regularization.)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "A regularized regression model always achieves lower training error than an unregularized regression model trained on the same dataset.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Regularization typically *increases* training error relative to unregularized OLS (though it may lower test error).", "whyPool": "Because regularization constrains the model, it typically increases training error relative to an unregularized model, although it may reduce test error. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 6 – Linear Model Selection and Regularization.)"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Applying regularization can improve test performance even if it slightly increases the model's training error.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Accepting a small rise in training error can cut variance and improve OOS performance — the bias-variance tradeoff.", "whyPool": "Accepting a small increase in training error can reduce variance and improve performance on unseen data, illustrating the bias–variance tradeoff. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – Bias–Variance Tradeoff; Chapter 6 – Linear Model Selection and Regularization.)"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Regularization is primarily intended to increase the complexity and flexibility of a linear regression model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Regularization reduces effective complexity by constraining coefficients; it does not increase flexibility.", "whyPool": "The purpose of regularization is to reduce effective model complexity by constraining the coefficients, not to make the model more flexible. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 6 – Linear Model Selection and Regularization.)"}, {"L": "E", "text": "When many features are noisy or only weakly predictive, regularization can improve the model's ability to generalize to new data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With many noisy or weakly predictive features, regularization reduces their influence and improves generalization.", "whyPool": "Regularization reduces the influence of noisy or weak predictors, leading to models that are often more stable and generalize better to future observations. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 6 – Linear Model Selection and Regularization.)"}], "reference": "Regularization purpose, bias-variance, generalization — ISLR ch.6 (§6.2); ch.2 (§2.2).", "n": 145}, {"id": "ML-D4G6Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Linear Models & Regression", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are building a linear regression model to forecast weekly returns for a portfolio of growth stocks. After adding dozens of new macroeconomic and technical indicators, the model achieves nearly perfect performance on the training set, but its predictions become highly unstable on new market data. The Portfolio Manager asks whether intentionally limiting the model's flexibility could produce more reliable investment decisions.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Regularization introduces a penalty during model training that discourages overly complex models.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Regularization penalizes large coefficients during training, encouraging simpler models less prone to overfitting.", "whyPool": "Regularization penalizes large coefficient values during training, encouraging simpler models that are less likely to overfit the training data. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 6 – Linear Model Selection and Regularization.)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "If a model is already underfitting the data, increasing the strength of regularization will generally improve both training and test performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "If a model is already underfitting, stronger regularization increases bias and worsens both training and test performance.", "whyPool": "If a model is already underfitting, stronger regularization generally increases bias and worsens performance by making the model even less flexible. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – Bias–Variance Tradeoff; Chapter 6 – Linear Model Selection and Regularization.)"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Regularization can reduce the sensitivity of a model to small changes in the training data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Limiting coefficient magnitudes makes the model less sensitive to small training-data changes, improving stability/generalization.", "whyPool": "By limiting coefficient magnitudes, regularization produces models that are often more stable and less sensitive to variations in the training dataset, improving generalization. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 6 – Linear Model Selection and Regularization.)"}, {"L": "D", "text": "A model with the lowest possible training error is always the model with the best expected performance on unseen data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The lowest training error often signals overfitting; the goal is generalization, not perfect in-sample fit.", "whyPool": "Minimizing training error alone often leads to overfitting. The objective is to achieve strong generalization performance on unseen data, not perfect performance on the training set. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – Statistical Learning; Chapter 6 – Linear Model Selection and Regularization.)"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Selecting an appropriate amount of regularization involves balancing model bias and variance. 131 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Choosing the regularization amount balances bias and variance — too little overfits, too much underfits.", "whyPool": "The amount of regularization controls the tradeoff between bias and variance. Too little regularization can overfit, while too much can underfit the data. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – Bias–Variance Tradeoff; Chapter 6 – Linear Model Selection and Regularization.) 132 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Regularization strength, bias-variance tradeoff, generalization — ISLR ch.6 (§6.2); ch.2 (§2.2).", "n": 146}, {"id": "ML-D5G1Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G1", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher uses KNN to predict next-day return from two features: daily return expressed as a decimal, typically between -0.03 and 0.03, and trading volume measured in millions of shares, typically between 1 and 500. The features are used without scaling. The researcher notices that the selected neighbors have nearly identical volume but quite different recent returns.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Under Euclidean distance, the volume feature is likely to dominate because its numerical range is much larger.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Volume's numerical range dwarfs return's, so its squared differences dominate Euclidean distance and drive neighbor selection.", "whyPool": "A feature with a much larger numerical range can dominate Euclidean distance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Converting volume from shares to millions of shares can change which observations are nearest unless the features are scaled consistently.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Changing volume's units (shares → millions) rescales its contribution to distance and can reorder which observations are nearest unless features are scaled consistently.", "whyPool": "Changing one feature's units changes its contribution to distance and may change neighbor rankings."}, {"L": "C", "text": "KNN automatically compensates for different feature units when calculating ordinary Euclidean distance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Ordinary Euclidean distance uses the supplied numbers as-is; it performs no automatic unit correction across features.", "whyPool": "Ordinary Euclidean distance uses the supplied numerical values without automatic unit correction."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Standardizing both features using training-set statistics can make their contributions to distance more comparable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Z-score standardization from training statistics equalizes marginal scales so raw magnitude alone no longer decides similarity.", "whyPool": "Standardization can prevent raw scale alone from determining similarity."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Feature scaling must be learned from the full dataset, including the test period, to produce unbiased distances.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Scaling parameters (μ, σ) must come from training data only; including the test period leaks information and biases distances.", "whyPool": "Scaling parameters should be estimated from training data to avoid test-data leakage. 134 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Feature scaling for distance-based methods — ISLR ch.2 (KNN, §2.2.3); scikit-learn \"Preprocessing data\" (StandardScaler) + \"Nearest Neighbors.\"", "n": 147}, {"id": "ML-D5G1Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G1", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A trading team represents each market day using standardized momentum, volatility, and liquidity features. It considers both Euclidean and Manhattan distance for a KNN model. One candidate neighbor differs moderately from the query day on all three features, while another differs greatly on one feature but is close on the other two. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Different distance metrics can rank the same candidate neighbors differently.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded claim \"different metrics can rank the same neighbors differently\" is *true*; the choice asserts that claim is false, so the choice is incorrect. Metrics encode similarity differently and can reorder neighbors.", "whyPool": "Metrics encode similarity differently and can therefore produce different rankings."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Euclidean distance squares coordinate differences before aggregation, so a large difference on one feature can have substantial influence.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: Euclidean squares coordinate differences, giving a large gap on one feature disproportionate influence.", "whyPool": "Squared differences give relatively more weight to a large coordinate gap."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Manhattan distance is the sum of absolute coordinate differences.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: Manhattan distance is by definition the sum of absolute coordinate differences.", "whyPool": "That is the definition of Manhattan distance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Once features are standardized, every reasonable distance metric must select exactly the same neighbors.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: standardization improves comparability but does not force distinct metrics to select identical neighbors.", "whyPool": "Scaling improves comparability but does not make distinct metrics equivalent."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Choosing a distance metric is part of the model design and should be evaluated using training and validation data rather than the test set.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the metric is a hyperparameter, chosen with training/validation data, not final test performance.", "whyPool": "The metric is a hyperparameter choice that should be made without using final test performance."}], "reference": "Distance-metric choice as hyperparameter — ISLR §2.2.3; scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" (metric parameter, Minkowski p=1 vs 2).", "n": 148}, {"id": "ML-D5G1Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G1", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A KNN model uses standardized price momentum, bid-ask spread, and an integer security identifier. The validation results are poor even though each feature has been placed on a similar numerical scale. An analyst argues that scaling proves every input now contributes meaningful information to similarity. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Standardizing the security identifier does not make numerical closeness between identifiers economically meaningful.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: standardizing an arbitrary identifier does not make numerical closeness between IDs economically meaningful.", "whyPool": "Scaling cannot create a meaningful geometry for an arbitrary identifier."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Distance-based models require attention to feature meaning as well as feature scale.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: useful distances need feature *meaning*, not just comparable *scale*.", "whyPool": "Useful distances depend on whether numerical proximity represents relevant similarity."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Removing an irrelevant feature can change which training observations are selected as neighbors.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: every included coordinate contributes to computed distances, so removing an irrelevant one can change neighbors.", "whyPool": "Each included coordinate contributes to the computed distances and rankings."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If all features have variance one, each feature is guaranteed to be equally useful for prediction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: unit variance does not imply equal relevance or predictive value.", "whyPool": "Equal variance does not imply equal relevance or predictive value."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A feature that is unrelated to the target can still distort neighbor selection. 135 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: an irrelevant coordinate can push genuinely similar observations apart and distort neighbor selection.", "whyPool": "Irrelevant coordinates can make genuinely similar observations appear farther apart."}], "reference": "Relevance vs. scale in distance models; feature selection — ESL ch.13 (prototype/nearest-neighbor methods); scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" + feature selection guide.", "n": 149}, {"id": "ML-D5G1Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G1", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are a quantitative analyst building a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) model to identify stocks with similar trading behavior. Your dataset includes daily returns (typically between -5% and +5%), trading volume (millions of shares), and market capitalization (billions of dollars). A junior analyst argues that KNN will automatically determine which features are most important. The Head of Quant Research asks you to evaluate whether the current feature representation will produce meaningful similarity measurements. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "In KNN, two observations are considered similar if they are close according to the chosen distance metric.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: two observations are \"similar\" if close under the chosen distance metric — the definition of KNN similarity.", "whyPool": "KNN predicts outcomes by identifying observations that are closest according to a selected distance metric, such as Euclidean distance. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – K-Nearest Neighbors.)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "If market capitalization has values that are orders of magnitude larger than the other features, it can dominate the distance calculation unless the features are appropriately scaled.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a feature with orders-of-magnitude larger values (market cap) can dominate distance unless scaled.", "whyPool": "Features with much larger numerical ranges can dominate Euclidean distance, causing KNN to emphasize those features disproportionately unless scaling or normalization is applied. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – K-Nearest Neighbors; Deep Learning: Foundations and Concepts, Chapter 1 – Data Preprocessing.)"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Scaling features has little effect on Euclidean distance because KNN evaluates all features independently.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: Euclidean distance combines all features jointly, so scale differences *directly* change computed distances and neighbors.", "whyPool": "Euclidean distance combines differences across all features, so differences in feature scales directly influence the computed distances. Scaling can substantially change which observations are considered nearest neighbors. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – K-Nearest Neighbors.) 136 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Choosing an inappropriate distance metric can lead KNN to identify neighbors that are not truly similar for the prediction task.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: an inappropriate metric can yield neighbors that are numerically close but not meaningfully similar for the task.", "whyPool": "The distance metric determines what \"similarity\" means. An inappropriate metric may produce neighbors that are close numerically but not meaningfully similar for the underlying prediction problem. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – K-Nearest Neighbors.)"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Features measured on comparable scales generally allow each feature to contribute more fairly to the distance calculation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: comparable scales let multiple relevant features contribute fairly to distance.", "whyPool": "Scaling features to comparable ranges prevents a single feature from dominating the distance calculation, allowing multiple relevant features to contribute to similarity assessment. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – K-Nearest Neighbors; Deep Learning: Foundations and Concepts, Chapter 1 – Data Preprocessing.)"}], "reference": "KNN similarity & scaling — ISLR ch.2 (KNN); \"Deep Learning: Foundations and Concepts\" ch.1 (data preprocessing).", "n": 150}, {"id": "ML-D5G1Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G1", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative analyst is building a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) model to classify stocks as “High Risk” or “Low Risk. ” The model uses two raw financial features: •  X₁: Market Capitalization, measured in U.S. dollars and ranging from approximately $100 million to $1 trillion •  X₂: Debt-to-Equity Ratio, ranging from approximately 0.0 to 5.0 The analyst uses standard Euclidean distance to identify the nearest historical observations. During validation, changing a test stock’s Debt-to-Equity Ratio has almost no effect on which stocks are selected as its neighbors, while relatively small percentage changes in Market Capitalization substantially alter the selected neighborhood. Which of the following statements correctly explain this behavior or identify appropriate remedies?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Because Euclidean distance is calculated from absolute numerical differences, the much larger numerical scale of Market Capitalization can dominate the distance calculation and therefore determine most of the neighbor ordering.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Euclidean distance uses absolute numerical differences, so market cap's hundreds-of-billions range dominates and drives neighbor ordering.", "whyPool": "Euclidean distance between observations xand yis d(x,y) =ඥ(x₁−y₁)₂+(x₂−y₂)₂. The calculation uses differences in the original units of each feature. Because Market Capitalization varies by hundreds of billions of dollars while Debt-to-Equity varies by only a few units, the Market Capitalization term can overwhelmingly dominate the distance. Neighbor selection will then be driven primarily by differences in Market Capitalization. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 2.2)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "KNN is invariant to feature scaling because it depends only on the rank ordering of observations within each individual feature.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "KNN is *highly* sensitive to scaling — distances are computed jointly, so rescaling one feature changes neighbors and predictions (not rank-invariant per feature).", "whyPool": "KNN is highly sensitive to feature scaling because its predictions depend on distances calculated jointly across the features. Rescaling one feature can change the relative distances between observations, alter which observations are selected as neighbors, and ultimately change the predicted class. 137 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Without feature scaling, variation in the Debt-to-Equity Ratio may contribute so little to the total distance that the model behaves almost as though Market Capitalization were its only meaningful feature.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "D/E is not removed, but its numerical contribution becomes negligible; the 2-D neighborhood behaves almost 1-D (market cap only).", "whyPool": "The Debt-to-Equity Ratio is not literally removed from the distance calculation, but its numerical contribution can become negligible relative to that of Market Capitalization. In practical terms, the two-dimensional neighborhood may behave almost like a one-dimensional neighborhood determined by Market Capitalization."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Replacing Euclidean distance with Manhattan distance would, by itself, eliminate the scale imbalance because Manhattan distance treats all features equally regardless of their units.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Manhattan distance still uses raw magnitudes, so a billions-scale gap keeps dominating; changing the metric does not address incompatible scales.", "whyPool": "Manhattan distance is calculated as d(x,y) =∣x₁ −y₁ ∣ +∣x₂−y₂ ∣. Although Manhattan distance does not square the feature differences, it still uses their raw numerical magnitudes. A difference measured in billions of dollars can therefore continue to dominate a difference measured in single-digit ratio units. Changing the distance metric does not remove the need to address incompatible feature scales."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Standardizing each feature using its training-sample mean and standard deviation can place the features on comparable scales, although it does not guarantee that the two features will be equally predictive or economically important.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Z-score standardization (training μ, σ) makes contributions comparable, though it does not guarantee equal predictive/economic importance; fit on training data to avoid leakage.", "whyPool": "Z-score standardization transforms a feature according to z=x−μ σ . After standardization, each feature has a training-sample mean of zero and standard deviation of one. This places changes in Market Capitalization and Debt-to-Equity on comparable standardized scales, allowing both to influence the distance calculation. However, standardization does not guarantee equal predictive importance, equal economic relevance, or equal contributions to every pairwise distance. The mean and standard deviation used for validation or test observations must be estimated from the training data to avoid data leakage."}], "reference": "Scale dominance & standardization — ISLR §2.2 (KNN); scikit-learn StandardScaler + \"common pitfalls / data leakage.\"", "n": 151}, {"id": "ML-D5G1Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G1", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative portfolio manager is developing a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) model that identifies historical market regimes resembling the current trading environment. The model uses three features: •  X₁: Daily VIX level, typically ranging from 10 to 80 •  X₂: 10-Year Treasury yield, expressed as a decimal, such as 0.04for 4% •  X₃: Daily S&P 500 return, expressed as a decimal, such as −0.02for −2% Because KNN selects neighbors using geometric similarity, the manager considers Euclidean distance, cosine similarity, and several feature-scaling methods. Any scaling transformation is fitted using the training data and then applied unchanged to validation, test, and future observations. Which of the following statements correctly describe how distance metrics and feature scaling affect similarity calculations in this setting?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "If Euclidean distance is calculated using the raw features, numerical differences in the VIX will generally dominate differences in Treasury yields and daily returns, causing neighbor selection to depend primarily on the VIX.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "On raw features, VIX (tens of points) dwarfs decimal yield/return differences, so neighbor selection depends mainly on VIX.", "whyPool": "Euclidean distance between two observations aand bis d(a,b)=ට₅X₁,௔−X₁,௕₉₂+₅X₂,௔−X₂,௕₉₂+₅X₃,௔−X₃,௕₉₂. A VIX difference might be measured in whole numbers or tens of points, while yield and return differences are represented by small decimals. Consequently, the VIX term can dominate the distance calculation and determine the neighbor ordering. The other features are not literally removed, but their contributions may become negligible on the raw numerical scales. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 2.2)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Replacing Euclidean distance with cosine similarity eliminates the need to examine feature scales because cosine similarity is invariant to independently rescaling any individual feature.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Cosine similarity is invariant to scaling the *whole vector*, not to rescaling *individual* features; changing one feature's units changes vector orientation and cosine.", "whyPool": "Cosine similarity is cos(a,b) = aTb ∥a∥₂∥b∥₂ . It is invariant to multiplying an entire observation vector by a positive scalar, but it is not invariant to independently rescaling individual features. Changing the units or scale of one feature changes the orientation of the vectors and can therefore change their cosine similarity. Large feature baselines and a lack of appropriate centering can also make cosine comparisons difficult to interpret in this financial setting."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Z-score standardization expresses each feature in units of its training-sample standard deviation, so a one-standard- deviation difference in VIX and a one-standard-deviation difference in Treasury yields have the same squared contribution to Euclidean distance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "After z-scoring, a one-SD difference in any feature contributes equally (same squared term) to Euclidean distance — comparable, not equally predictive.", "whyPool": "Z-score standardization transforms a feature according to ẑ௝ =x̂௝−μ௝ σ௝ , where μ௝and σ௝are estimated from the training data. After standardization, a one-standard-deviation difference corresponds to a difference of one standardized unit for any feature. Under Euclidean distance, such differences therefore receive the same squared geometric contribution. This does not imply that the features are equally predictive, economically important, or statistically independent. It only places their differences on comparable standardized scales."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because min-max normalization places training observations within [0,1], it is necessarily superior to z-score standardization when the training data contain extreme crisis observations. 138 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Min-max is not \"necessarily superior\"; extreme crisis observations can compress normal data into a narrow range, and z-score is likewise outlier-sensitive — robust/rank methods may be better.", "whyPool": "Min-max normalization is typically defined as xnorm= x−x₅₁₆ x₅ୟ୶−x₅₁₆ . Training observations are mapped into [0,1], assuming a nonconstant feature, but extreme minimum or maximum observations can determine almost the entire range. Crisis observations may therefore compress ordinary observations into a narrow portion of the interval and reduce distance resolution among normal regimes. Min-max normalization is not universally superior to z-score standardization. Moreover, z-score standardization is also sensitive to outliers because extreme values affect both the mean and standard deviation. Robust scaling based on medians and interquartile ranges, clipping, or rank-based transformations may be worth considering when extreme observations are a major concern. Future observations can also fall outside [0,1]when the scaler is based only on the training range. 139 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Rank-based percentile scaling preserves the ordering of each feature while reducing the influence of the numerical magnitude of extreme observations. It is therefore invariant to strictly increasing transformations that preserve the observations’ ranks.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Rank/percentile scaling keeps ordering while curbing extreme magnitudes, so it is invariant to strictly increasing (rank-preserving) transforms, at the cost of discarding gap-size information.", "whyPool": "Rank-based percentile scaling replaces raw magnitudes with relative positions in the feature’s empirical distribution. A strictly increasing transformation preserves the ordering of the observations and therefore preserves their ranks, subject to the method used to handle ties. Because an extremely large value is represented primarily by its rank rather than by its raw distance from the rest of the data, its magnitude cannot stretch the scale and compress ordinary observations in the same way that it can under min- max normalization. However, this robustness comes at a cost: rank scaling discards information about the sizes of the original gaps between observations. G2: Choice of k and Bias–Variance Tradeoff"}], "reference": "Scaling methods & metric invariances — scikit-learn \"Preprocessing\" (StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler, QuantileTransformer/rank); cosine vs. Euclidean geometry.", "n": 152}, {"id": "ML-D5G2Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G2", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A researcher fits KNN regressors to predict five-day stock returns. With k = 1, training error is nearly zero but validation predictions change sharply when a few training observations are replaced. With k = 75, predictions are stable but miss recurring local patterns. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The k = 1 model is exhibiting behavior associated with high variance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: heavy dependence on individual observations = high variance for k=1.", "whyPool": "Heavy dependence on individual observations makes small-k predictions unstable."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Increasing k generally smooths the fitted prediction function.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: averaging over more neighbors smooths the fitted function.", "whyPool": "Averaging over more neighbors tends to reduce local fluctuations."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The k = 75 model may have more bias than the k = 1 model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: excessive averaging (k=75) can suppress real local structure → more bias than k=1.", "whyPool": "Excessive averaging can suppress real local structure and increase bias."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The value of k that minimizes training error is necessarily the best choice for unseen data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: the k minimizing *training* error (very small k) can generalize poorly; it is not necessarily best.", "whyPool": "Very small k can fit training data closely while generalizing poorly."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Validation performance can be used to select k before the final test evaluation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: k is a hyperparameter, selected on validation data before final test evaluation.", "whyPool": "Hyperparameters such as k should be selected with validation data rather than the test set."}], "reference": "Bias–variance tradeoff & k — ISLR §2.2 (KNN, bias-variance); §5 (cross-validation for hyperparameters).", "n": 153}, {"id": "ML-D5G2Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G2", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A classifier labels market regimes as bullish or bearish using KNN. At k = 3, the decision boundary has many small bends and isolated pockets. At k = 51, the boundary is much smoother and a small but genuine bearish region disappears. 140 Rev:081020261230 The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A small k can produce a complex decision boundary that follows local observations closely.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: small k gives a complex boundary that tracks local observations closely.", "whyPool": "Small neighborhoods allow labels to change rapidly across feature space."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A larger k can eliminate small regions because the vote includes more distant observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: larger k pulls in more distant votes and can erase small regions.", "whyPool": "Broader voting neighborhoods smooth small local structures."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Increasing k always improves classification accuracy on unseen data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: increasing k does not *always* improve unseen accuracy — it eventually adds excessive bias (underfit).", "whyPool": "Increasing k can reduce variance but eventually create excessive bias."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The disappearance of a real local regime is an example of possible underfitting.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: smoothing away a real local regime is exactly underfitting.", "whyPool": "Smoothing away useful structure is consistent with underfitting."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The best k depends on the data distribution and cannot be determined from k alone.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the best k depends on the data distribution and must be assessed empirically.", "whyPool": "The appropriate tradeoff must be assessed empirically for the problem."}], "reference": "k vs. boundary complexity — ISLR §2.2.3 (KNN classifier, Fig. of k effects); scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors Classification.\"", "n": 154}, {"id": "ML-D5G2Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G2", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A team applies KNN regression to 120 historical observations and considers k values of 1, 5, 25, and 120. The target is next-week portfolio return. The PM asks what happens at the largest value and whether an even k is inherently invalid for regression.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: With k = 120, every query prediction is the mean target of all training observations if neighbors are equally weighted.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The embedded claim (k=120 equal-weight → every prediction is the mean of all targets) is *accurate*, so asserting it is false is itself false; equal-weight regression averages all selected neighbors' targets.", "whyPool": "Equal-weight regression averages the targets of all selected neighbors."}, {"L": "B", "text": "At k = 120, the input features no longer affect the prediction when all training observations are always included.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With the identical full set for every query, feature values no longer differentiate predictions — all get the same equal-weight mean.", "whyPool": "When every query uses the identical full set, all receive the same equal-weight mean."}, {"L": "C", "text": "An even value of k is invalid for KNN regression because regression requires a majority vote.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Regression averages numerical targets; it needs no majority vote, so an even k is not invalid.", "whyPool": "Regression aggregates numerical targets and does not require a majority vote. 141 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Using all training observations is likely to have lower variance but higher bias than using k = 1.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Global averaging (k=N) is stable (low variance) but can miss substantial local structure (higher bias) vs. k=1.", "whyPool": "Global averaging is stable but can miss substantial local structure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Distance-weighted KNN can give closer neighbors more influence than farther neighbors.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Distance-weighted KNN gives closer neighbors more influence — a valid alternative to equal weighting.", "whyPool": "Weighting provides an alternative to treating every selected neighbor equally."}], "reference": "k=N limit & weighting schemes — ISLR §2.2.3; scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" (weights='uniform' vs 'distance').", "n": 155}, {"id": "ML-D5G2Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G2", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative research team is using a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) classifier to predict short-term equity direction (Up vs. Down) based on normalized order-flow imbalance indicators. To optimize model accuracy and prevent generalization errors, the team tests different values of K (ranging from K=1 to K=250) on a historical dataset containing 1,000 trading instances. The team analyzes how the choice of K alters the decision boundary in feature space and evaluates the model's structural bias-variance tradeoff. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the impact of K on decision boundary complexity, bias, variance, and model capacity?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Setting K=1 creates an extremely flexible, high-capacity model with a complex, highly jagged decision boundary, resulting in zero training error (low bias) but extreme vulnerability to overfitting (high variance).", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "k=1 gives each point its own Voronoi cell → 100% training accuracy (low bias) but a boundary that wraps every noise spike (high variance, overfit).", "whyPool": "At K=1, every training point creates its own Voronoi cell where it is its own nearest neighbor, guaranteeing 100% training accuracy (0% training error, low bias). However, because the decision boundary wraps tightly around every individual data point and noise spike, the model exhibits high variance and poor out-of-sample generalization."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Setting K=1 minimizes model variance because the model relies on the single most informative historical neighbor, making its out-of-sample predictions exceptionally stable across different training folds.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "k=1 *maximizes* variance, not minimizes it; a single neighbor makes predictions unstable to small training changes.", "whyPool": "K=1 maximizes model variance. Because the prediction depends entirely on a single neighbor, any small shift or random noise in the training set will drastically alter the localized decision boundary."}, {"L": "C", "text": "As K increases toward the total sample size (K→N), the decision boundary becomes smoother and more linear, increasing the model's bias while reducing its variance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "As k→N the boundary smooths and simplifies, lowering variance while raising bias as local dynamics are averaged away.", "whyPool": "Increasing K averages predictions over larger neighborhoods, which smooths out localized fluctuations and noise. This structural reduction in flexibility lowers model variance, but increases systematic bias as local feature dynamics are smoothed away."}, {"L": "D", "text": "At the extreme limit where K=N (e.g., K=1,000), the KNN classifier completely ignores local feature structure and simply predicts the majority class of the entire training set for every test instance, maximizing model bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At k=N every query evaluates all points → predicts the global majority class everywhere (flat boundary, max bias, zero flexibility).", "whyPool": "When K=N, every test instance evaluates all N training points. The neighborhood becomes the entire dataset, so the prediction defaults to the global baseline majority class across all inputs, producing a flat, zero-flexibility decision boundary (maximum bias, zero variance). 142 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Increasing Kincreases the model’s effective degrees of freedom because incorporating more neighbors allows the classifier to respond more precisely to local variation in the training data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Increasing k *reduces* effective DoF (heuristic DoF ≈ N/k) and flexibility; smaller k means higher capacity, not larger k.", "whyPool": "Increasing Kreduces model flexibility. Each prediction is based on a larger neighborhood, so local fluctuations and isolated observations have less influence on the classification boundary. As a useful heuristic, KNN model capacity is inversely related to K, sometimes expressed as DoF ≈N K. Thus, smaller values of Kproduce greater effective capacity, more complex decision boundaries, and higher variance; larger values produce lower capacity, smoother boundaries, and higher bias. The expression is a capacity heuristic rather than an exact degrees-of-freedom formula for every KNN classifier."}], "reference": "Model capacity & DoF≈N/k — ISLR §2.2 (KNN flexibility, effective degrees of freedom); ESL ch.13.", "n": 156}, {"id": "ML-D5G2Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G2", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are developing a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) model to classify whether stocks will outperform the market over the next month. During model validation, a junior quantitative analyst recommends setting k = 1 because it achieves the highest training accuracy. The Head of Research is concerned that the model may perform poorly in live trading and asks you to evaluate the tradeoffs associated with different values of k.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Choosing a very small value of k, such as k = 1, generally produces a model with low bias and high variance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Very small k (k=1) closely tracks training data → low bias, high variance, overfit risk.", "whyPool": "A very small value of k closely follows the training data, resulting in low bias but high variance and an increased risk of overfitting. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – K-Nearest Neighbors; Chapter 2 – Bias– Variance Tradeoff.)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Increasing the value of k generally produces smoother decision boundaries because predictions are averaged over more neighboring observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Larger k averages over more neighbors → smoother, less complex boundaries.", "whyPool": "As k increases, predictions are based on a larger neighborhood, producing smoother and less complex decision boundaries. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – K-Nearest Neighbors.)"}, {"L": "C", "text": "If k is increased to include nearly every training observation, the model becomes more sensitive to random noise in individual data points.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Increasing k makes predictions *less* sensitive to individual noisy points; extremely large k instead causes underfitting.", "whyPool": "Increasing k generally makes predictions less sensitive to individual noisy observations because each prediction averages information from many neighbors. Extremely large values of k can instead lead to underfitting. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – K-Nearest Neighbors.)"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Selecting the optimal value of k involves balancing the risks of overfitting and underfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Selecting k balances overfitting (small k) against underfitting (large k) — the bias-variance tradeoff.", "whyPool": "The choice of k directly controls the bias–variance tradeoff: small k tends to overfit, while large k tends to underfit. Selecting k requires balancing these competing effects. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – Bias–Variance Tradeoff; Chapter 2 – K-Nearest Neighbors.)"}, {"L": "E", "text": "The value of k has no effect on the complexity of the decision boundary learned by KNN.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "k strongly controls boundary complexity: small k → irregular, large k → smooth.", "whyPool": "The value of k strongly influences the complexity of the KNN decision boundary. Smaller values produce more irregular boundaries, while larger values produce smoother, simpler boundaries. (Reference: Introduction to Statistical Learning, Chapter 2 – K-Nearest Neighbors.) 143 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "k and the bias–variance tradeoff — ISLR §2.2 (KNN; bias-variance); scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors Classification.\"", "n": 157}, {"id": "ML-D5G2Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G2", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative macro strategist is using K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) regression to forecast next-week changes in the 10- Year Treasury yield. The model uses two normalized economic features: •  X₁: Inflation Momentum •  X₂: Central Bank Balance Sheet Growth The training sample contains N= 500historical weekly observations. During validation, the strategist evaluates values of Kranging from K= 1to K= 500, comparing training error, validation error, and the smoothness of the fitted regression surface. Which of the following statements correctly describe how Kaffects model flexibility, bias, variance, and out-of-sample performance?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "With K= 1, the prediction at each training observation generally equals that observation’s own response, producing a highly flexible fitted surface with very low training error and high sensitivity to noise in the training sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At k=1 a training point is its own neighbor → fitted value equals its response (≈0 training error), a highly flexible surface with high noise sensitivity (ties/duplicates/LOO can prevent exactly zero).", "whyPool": "In KNN regression, the prediction for an input x₀is fመ(x₀) =1 K ෍ ŷ ̂∈N಼(₅బ) . When K= 1, a training observation is ordinarily its own nearest neighbor, so its fitted value equals its observed response. This produces zero training error when feature vectors are distinct and the implementation includes the observation itself. However, the fitted surface is highly responsive to individual observations and noise, resulting in high variance. Duplicate feature vectors, ties, or leave-one-out prediction can prevent training error from being exactly zero."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Increasing Kmust decrease the training error because averaging more neighboring observations provides a more reliable estimate of the response.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Increasing k generally *raises* in-sample training error (broader averaging smooths away observation-specific fit); it does not have to decrease.", "whyPool": "Increasing Kgenerally increases—or at least does not improve—the ordinary in-sample training fit because each prediction averages responses from a broader neighborhood. At K= 1, the model can closely interpolate the training data. Larger neighborhoods smooth away observation-specific variation, typically increasing training error even though they may improve validation or test performance."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Increasing Kgenerally smooths the fitted regression surface and reduces sensitivity to individual observations, but it can introduce additional bias when the true relationship contains important local variation or sharp nonlinear changes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Larger k lowers variance but adds bias where the true relationship has sharp local variation across regimes.", "whyPool": "Larger values of Kaverage responses across wider regions of the feature space. This reduces the influence of individual observations and ordinarily lowers variance. The tradeoff is increased bias: if expected Treasury-yield changes vary sharply across nearby macroeconomic regimes, a large neighborhood may combine observations governed by meaningfully different local relationships. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 2.2) 144 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "When K=N, every test observation receives the same prediction—the mean response in the training sample—so the model ignores all feature-dependent variation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At k=N every prediction is the training mean ȳ — a constant, feature-independent, high-bias surface.", "whyPool": "When K=N, the neighborhood for every test observation contains the complete training sample. Consequently, fመ(x₀) =1 N෍ŷ ே ̂ୀ₁ =yത for every x₀. The fitted surface is therefore constant and does not use the feature values to distinguish among macroeconomic environments. This is an extreme high-bias, low-flexibility model. Its prediction remains sample- dependent, however, so its sampling variance should not be described as literally zero."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because increasing Kreduces variance, validation or test error must decline continuously as Kincreases, making K= Nthe optimal choice whenever the features are normalized.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Lower variance does not guarantee monotonically improving test error; bias rises with k, so test error is typically U-shaped — select k via validation/CV.", "whyPool": "Reducing variance does not guarantee continuously improving out-of-sample performance because bias generally increases as Kbecomes larger. Test error often follows a U-shaped pattern: small Kmay overfit, an intermediate Kmay provide the best bias-variance balance, and very large Kmay underfit. Feature normalization improves the meaning of the distance calculation, but it does not eliminate this tradeoff. The appropriate value of Kshould therefore be selected using validation data or cross-validation. G3: Sensitivity to Noise & Local Structure"}], "reference": "KNN regression f̂(x0)=(1/k)Σyi, U-shaped test error — ISLR §2.2.3; §5 (cross-validation).", "n": 158}, {"id": "ML-D5G3Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G3", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A KNN regressor estimates next-day volatility. One training record has a data-entry error that gives it an extreme target value, but its feature vector lies very close to a commonly observed market state. Predictions near that state become extreme when k = 1 and less extreme when k = 15.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "With k = 1, the erroneous record can determine the prediction for nearby queries by itself.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At k=1 the single closest observation supplies the entire prediction, so the erroneous record alone can determine it.", "whyPool": "The single closest observation supplies the entire k = 1 regression prediction."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Averaging across more neighbors can reduce the influence of one corrupted target.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Averaging over more neighbors makes the corrupted target just one component of the mean, reducing its influence.", "whyPool": "The erroneous value becomes only one component of a larger average."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Increasing k guarantees that noisy observations have no influence on any prediction.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Larger k does not *guarantee* zero influence — a noisy point can still be included and shift the aggregate.", "whyPool": "A noisy point may still be included and influence the aggregate."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Cleaning suspected data errors can improve the model without changing the KNN algorithm.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Cleaning suspected errors improves a lazy learner because its stored examples *are* the model — no algorithm change needed.", "whyPool": "Data quality directly affects an instance-based learner's stored examples."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A target outlier located far from a query in feature space must be selected as its nearest neighbor because its target is extreme.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Neighbor selection uses feature distance, not target extremeness; a far-away outlier is not selected just because its target is large.", "whyPool": "Neighbor selection uses feature distance, not the extremeness of the target. 145 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Noise sensitivity & data quality in instance-based learners — ISLR §2.2.3; ESL ch.13 (nearest-neighbor robustness).", "n": 159}, {"id": "ML-D5G3Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G3", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A global linear model and a KNN model are used to forecast returns. The data contain two clusters: high-volatility days follow one relationship, while low-volatility days follow another. KNN performs better when enough historical observations exist near each query.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "KNN can represent different local relationships without fitting one global equation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "KNN forms predictions from local neighborhoods, representing different local relationships without one global equation.", "whyPool": "Predictions are formed from local neighborhoods rather than a single global parametric form."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The quality of a KNN prediction depends on whether relevant nearby examples exist.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Prediction quality depends on whether relevant nearby examples exist for the query.", "whyPool": "KNN relies directly on the targets of similar stored cases."}, {"L": "C", "text": "KNN necessarily assumes that the same linear relationship holds throughout feature space.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "KNN is non-parametric and does not assume a single global linear relationship.", "whyPool": "KNN is nonparametric and need not impose a global linear relationship."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A sparse region can yield unreliable predictions because even its nearest observations may be far away.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "In sparse regions the nearest available cases can be poor analogues, yielding unreliable predictions.", "whyPool": "The nearest available cases may be poor analogues in a sparse region."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Local structure is useful only for classification and cannot benefit KNN regression.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Local averaging models nonlinear structure in regression too, not only classification.", "whyPool": "Local averaging can model nonlinear structure in regression as well."}], "reference": "Non-parametric local modeling — ISLR §2.2.3 (KNN vs. linear); ch.7 (local/flexible methods).", "n": 160}, {"id": "ML-D5G3Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G3", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A KNN classifier predicts whether a trade will be profitable. A mislabeled training observation sits inside a dense region whose other points are labeled profitable. The team compares k = 1 with k = 9 and also considers distance-weighted voting.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The mislabeled point can create a small incorrect prediction region when k = 1.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At k=1 queries nearest the mislabeled point inherit its wrong label, creating a small incorrect region.", "whyPool": "Queries closest to that observation inherit its label under 1-NN."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Majority voting with k = 9 may overcome the isolated bad label if nearby labels are mostly correct.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With k=9 the surrounding correct labels can outvote a single mislabeled case.", "whyPool": "The surrounding correct observations can outvote a single mislabeled case. 146 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Distance weighting always eliminates the effect of mislabeled observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Distance weighting does not *always* eliminate the effect — a close mislabeled neighbor can still get large weight.", "whyPool": "A mislabeled close neighbor can still receive substantial or dominant weight."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the mislabeled observation is extremely close to a query, distance weighting may increase its influence.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If the mislabeled point is extremely close, common weighting rules (larger weight for smaller distance) can *increase* its influence.", "whyPool": "Common weighting rules assign larger weights to smaller distances."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Examining label quality is unnecessary because KNN is inherently robust to all label noise.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "KNN is not inherently robust to all label noise; stored bad labels directly affect later predictions, so label quality matters.", "whyPool": "Stored noisy labels can directly affect later predictions."}], "reference": "Label noise & weighted voting — scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" (weights='distance'); ESL ch.13.", "n": 161}, {"id": "ML-D5G3Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G3", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative analyst is building a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) model to classify market regimes into \"High Volatility\" vs. \"Low Volatility\" based on macro features. The training dataset includes several historical \"black swan\" shocks—short- lived, anomalous market days where prices fluctuated wildly due to fat-tailed market noise rather than persistent structural shifts. The analyst evaluates how the presence of these noisy outlier observations impacts the KNN algorithm's local decision boundaries and prediction stability. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates KNN's sensitivity to noise, local structure, and outliers?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Setting K=1 makes the model exceptionally vulnerable to noisy outliers, as a single anomalous historical data point will create an isolated island (\"lake\") in the decision boundary and incorrectly misclassify surrounding normal test instances.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At k=1 a single anomalous point owns 100% of the vote, creating an isolated island (\"lake\") that misclassifies nearby test points.", "whyPool": "At K=1, the model assigns 100% predictive weight to the immediate nearest neighbor. If that neighbor is a noisy, non-representative outlier, it constructs an isolated pocket in the decision boundary that corrupts predictions for any test point falling near it."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Because KNN is a non-parametric model that relies on spatial proximity rather than global parameter estimation, an extreme outlier point exerts a global distorting effect across the entire feature space regardless of the choice of K.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Outlier influence in KNN is strictly *local* (bounded by the neighborhood), unlike a high-leverage point that pivots a global OLS line.", "whyPool": "Outliers in KNN have strictly local influence, bounded by the local spatial neighborhood. Unlike Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) or linear models—where a single high-leverage outlier can pivot the global regression line/hyperplane across all feature space—KNN's non-parametric nature restricts an outlier's impact solely to its immediate geometric vicinity."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Increasing Kcan make the classifier more robust to an isolated mislabeled or anomalous training observation because that observation contributes only one vote within a larger neighborhood. However, an excessively large Kmay blur genuine local regime structure and increase bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Larger k makes the anomaly one of k votes (more robust) but excessive k blurs genuine local regimes and adds bias — pick k via validation.", "whyPool": "KNN classification predicts using the class votes of the Knearest training observations. When K= 1, a single noisy observation can determine the prediction for nearby test points. With a larger K, that observation supplies only one of the Kvotes and will ordinarily be outvoted if the remaining neighbors reflect the underlying local class. 147 Rev:081020261230 This increased robustness is not unconditional. If outliers are numerous, clustered, or located near a genuine class boundary, increasing Kmay not eliminate their influence. Moreover, a very large Kcan combine observations from distinct market regimes, oversmooth meaningful local structure, and increase classification bias. Thus, Kshould be selected using validation data or cross-validation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If an outlier lies deep inside a dense cluster of opposite-class points, a large K will isolate that outlier and force the local decision boundary to warp around it, whereas a small K will ignore it.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reversed: a *small* k (k=1) is warped by an outlier deep in opposite-class points; a *large* k outvotes it.", "whyPool": "The opposite is true. A small K (especially K=1) easily falls prey to an isolated outlier surrounded by opposite- class points, forcing the decision boundary to create an artificial pocket around it. A large K easily outvotes the single outlier, smoothly overriding it."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Pre-filtering the dataset with an outlier removal algorithm (such as Isolation Forest or Distance-to-Median filtering) stabilizes KNN performance by preventing noisy, non-representative data points from polluting local neighborhoods.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Pre-filtering (Isolation Forest, distance-to-median) stabilizes KNN by keeping non-representative points out of stored neighborhoods.", "whyPool": "Instance-based learners like KNN are directly dependent on the quality of stored training instances (\"lazy learning\"). Cleaning the feature space by stripping out erroneous or non-repeatable regime outliers prevents noise from distorting local neighborhood calculations during inference."}], "reference": "Local outlier influence vs. OLS leverage; outlier filtering — ESL ch.13; scikit-learn \"Novelty and Outlier Detection.\"", "n": 162}, {"id": "ML-D5G3Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G3", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative portfolio manager is developing an instance-based regime-detection model using K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN). The model uses historical macro features to classify current market conditions into \"Expansion\" vs. \"Contraction. \" The historical training set contains several noisy, fat-tailed market anomalies—such as transient flash crashes and temporary liquidity freezes—that do not reflect persistent macroeconomic regimes. The manager evaluates how these noisy outlier points alter local decision boundaries, influence global predictions, and affect model stability across different choices of K. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the impact of noise, outliers, and local structure on the KNN algorithm?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Setting K=1 provides maximum protection against noisy, non-representative outliers because single-neighbor predictions isolate anomalous points and prevent their errors from spreading across the local feature space.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "k=1 gives *maximum* sensitivity to outliers (100% weight to one neighbor), creating an artificial \"island,\" not protection.", "whyPool": "Setting K=1 makes the model maximally sensitive to noisy outliers. Because the model assigns 100% predictive weight to the single nearest neighbor, an isolated noisy point creates an artificial \"island\" in the decision space that misclassifies any test point landing near it. 148 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Increasing K to a larger number (e.g., K=20) dilutes the impact of isolated, noisy observations by averaging over a broader spatial neighborhood, making the decision boundary far more robust to local data corruptions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "k=20 makes a lone noisy point one of 20 votes, smoothing the boundary — robust but not guaranteed if outliers are numerous/clustered/near a boundary; too-large k oversmooths (use validation).", "whyPool": "With K= 20, a single noisy observation contributes only one of the 20 votes used to classify a test point. If the other nearby observations represent the underlying local regime, they will ordinarily outvote the anomaly, producing a smoother and more stable decision boundary than K= 1. This robustness is not guaranteed. Increasing Kmay be insufficient when noisy observations are numerous, clustered, or concentrated near a genuine class boundary. A value of Kthat is too large can also combine observations from distinct market regimes, oversmooth meaningful local patterns, and increase classification bias. The appropriate value should therefore be selected using validation data or cross-validation."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because KNN relies on spatial distance calculations, a single extreme outlier located far away in feature space exerts a global distorting effect on the fitted model, shifting decision boundaries across the entire dataset in the same manner as a high-leverage point in OLS regression.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A distant outlier's influence is local (bounded by its neighborhood), unlike a high-leverage OLS point that shifts the global slope everywhere.", "whyPool": "Outliers in KNN have strictly local influence restricted to their immediate spatial neighborhood. Unlike Ordinary Least Squares (OLS)—where a single high-leverage point can alter the global slope of a fitted hyperplane across the entire feature space—KNN's non-parametric spatial lookup prevents distant outliers from affecting predictions in other regions of the feature space."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Unlike linear models, a K-Nearest Neighbors classifier becomes increasingly sensitive to individual noisy training points as K approaches N (the total training sample size).", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "As k→N, KNN becomes *less* sensitive to individual noise (predicts global majority), not more.", "whyPool": "As K→N, KNN becomes less sensitive to individual noisy points, not more. At K=N, the model simply predicts the global majority class across all instances, completely ignoring local noise (maximizing bias and reducing local variance to zero)."}, {"L": "E", "text": "An isolated outlier point surrounded entirely by data points of the opposite class will have zero impact on the local decision boundary when K=1, because the algorithm automatically filters out points that deviate from their immediate geometric surroundings.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "At k=1 KNN does no filtering; it blindly picks the closest point even if it is an isolated anomaly, warping the boundary around it.", "whyPool": "At K=1, KNN does not perform any spatial filtering or outlier detection. It blindly selects the single closest neighbor regardless of whether that neighbor is an isolated anomaly or part of a dense cluster, causing the local decision boundary to warp directly around the outlier."}], "reference": "k→N smoothing & local outlier influence — ISLR §2.2.3; ESL ch.13.", "n": 163}, {"id": "ML-D5G3Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G3", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Researcher at a hedge fund developing a short-term equity trading signal. A junior analyst proposes using KNN because “the model simply looks at similar historical market situations, ” but the Portfolio Manager is concerned that unusual market events and noisy observations could cause unstable trading decisions. The PM asks you to evaluate how KNN behaves when local patterns are distorted by noisy data and outliers before approving deployment.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A single noisy observation can have a large impact on KNN predictions because KNN relies on nearby examples rather than learning a global relationship.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "KNN depends directly on the closest examples, so one noisy/mislabeled nearby point can strongly sway a prediction.", "whyPool": "KNN predictions depend directly on the closest training examples. If a nearby observation is noisy or incorrectly labeled, it can strongly influence the prediction."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Increasing the value of (k) in KNN generally makes the model more sensitive to individual noisy observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Increasing k generally *reduces* sensitivity to individual noise (averaging), though excessively large k adds bias.", "whyPool": "Increasing (k) usually reduces sensitivity to individual noisy observations because predictions are averaged over more neighbors. However, excessively large (k) can make the model less responsive and introduce higher bias. 149 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "KNN can perform well when local similarity patterns exist in the data, even if the relationship between inputs and outputs is complex.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "As a flexible non-parametric method, KNN performs well when similar observations have similar outcomes, even with complex/nonlinear relationships.", "whyPool": "KNN is a flexible, non-parametric method that can capture complex relationships when similar observations have similar outcomes. It does not require the relationship to be linear."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Removing every outlier from a financial dataset before using KNN is always beneficial because all unusual market events represent errors.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Not all outliers are errors — crashes/shocks can be genuine signal; blind removal can discard important information.", "whyPool": "Some outliers represent genuine market events, such as crashes or volatility shocks, and removing them blindly can eliminate important information. Data cleaning decisions depend on whether observations are errors or meaningful events."}, {"L": "E", "text": "KNN is typically more affected by noisy labels and unusual observations than many global parametric models.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Because it relies on local neighborhoods, KNN is typically more affected by noisy labels/unusual points than global parametric models.", "whyPool": "Because KNN bases predictions on local neighborhoods, noisy observations and mislabeled examples can directly distort predictions. Models that estimate broader global relationships may be less locally sensitive. G4: High-Dimensional Effects (Curse of Dimensionality)"}], "reference": "Local sensitivity vs. global models; data-cleaning judgment — ISLR §2.2.3; ESL ch.13.", "n": 164}, {"id": "ML-D5G4Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G4", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A KNN model begins with five economically motivated features. A developer then adds 200 weak or irrelevant indicators, expecting that more information can only help. Validation performance falls, and the distance to the nearest observation becomes similar to the distance to many other observations.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Adding irrelevant dimensions can degrade the usefulness of a distance measure.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Irrelevant coordinates inject noise into the similarity calculation, degrading the distance measure.", "whyPool": "Irrelevant coordinates add noise to similarity calculations."}, {"L": "B", "text": "In high dimensions, distances can concentrate so that nearest and farther observations become less distinguishable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "In high dimensions distances concentrate, so nearest vs. farther observations become less distinguishable.", "whyPool": "Reduced contrast among distances is a common high-dimensional effect."}, {"L": "C", "text": "More features guarantee improved KNN predictions because the original features remain present.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "More features do not guarantee improvement — extra dimensions can distort rankings and increase sparsity.", "whyPool": "Extra dimensions can distort neighbor rankings and increase sparsity."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Feature selection or dimensionality reduction may improve neighborhood quality.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Feature selection / dimensionality reduction can restore more meaningful neighborhoods.", "whyPool": "Removing unhelpful dimensions can restore more meaningful distances."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Standardizing every feature completely eliminates the curse of dimensionality.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Scaling addresses feature *magnitude*, not high-dimensional sparsity or distance concentration.", "whyPool": "Scaling addresses feature magnitude, not high-dimensional sparsity or distance concentration."}], "reference": "Curse of dimensionality & distance concentration — ISLR §2.2.3 (KNN in high dimensions); ESL ch.2/13.", "n": 165}, {"id": "ML-D5G4Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G4", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher wants equally dense coverage after increasing a KNN feature space from two dimensions to ten. The available history remains fixed at 5,000 daily observations. The researcher assumes the same sample will provide comparably local neighbors. 150 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A fixed number of observations becomes sparser relative to the space as dimensionality increases.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A fixed sample grows sparser relative to the space as dimensionality (and volume) increases.", "whyPool": "The volume of the feature space grows rapidly with dimension."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Maintaining similar local coverage can require dramatically more data as dimensions are added.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Maintaining similar local coverage can require dramatically more data as dimensions are added.", "whyPool": "Sample requirements can grow sharply when comparable coverage is desired."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The nearest observation in a high-dimensional dataset is necessarily close in an absolute sense.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The nearest point can be far in absolute terms — it is \"nearest\" only because all points are far.", "whyPool": "A point can be nearest merely because all available observations are far away."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Sparse neighborhoods can force KNN to use observations that are not very similar to the query.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Sparse neighborhoods force KNN to use observations that are not truly similar to the query.", "whyPool": "Limited local coverage weakens the analogy on which KNN relies."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The term “curse of dimensionality” refers only to increased file-storage requirements.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The curse of dimensionality is statistical/geometric, not merely a storage-size problem.", "whyPool": "It includes statistical and geometric problems, not just storage cost."}], "reference": "Data sparsity in high dimensions (r=q^(1/p)) — ISLR §2.2.3; ESL §2.5.", "n": 166}, {"id": "ML-D5G4Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G4", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A model uses 80 standardized technical indicators, many of which are variations of the same signal. The team proposes principal component analysis (PCA) before KNN but plans to fit PCA once using all dates, including the test period.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Reducing redundant dimensions can make distance comparisons more informative.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Reducing redundant dimensions can make distance comparisons more informative.", "whyPool": "A compact representation can reduce redundant or noisy contributions to distance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "PCA can produce a lower-dimensional representation, although it does not use the prediction target in ordinary form.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Ordinary PCA builds components from input-feature variation and does not use the target.", "whyPool": "Ordinary PCA constructs components from variation in the input features."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Fitting PCA on all dates is acceptable because PCA never uses target labels.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Even without labels, fitting PCA on all dates lets test-period feature information shape the transform.", "whyPool": "Test-period feature information can still influence the learned transformation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Even unsupervised preprocessing can leak information when fitted using the test-period feature distribution.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Unsupervised preprocessing can still leak when fit on the test-period distribution — fit within training only.", "whyPool": "Preprocessing parameters should be learned within the training process."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Dimensionality reduction is guaranteed to improve out-of-sample accuracy for every KNN problem.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Dimensionality reduction is not guaranteed to help; useful information can be discarded, so validate empirically.", "whyPool": "Useful information may be discarded, so performance must be validated empirically. 151 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Leakage from preprocessing fit on test data; PCA + KNN — scikit-learn \"common pitfalls / data leakage,\" \"Decomposition (PCA)\"; ISLR ch.12.", "n": 167}, {"id": "ML-D5G4Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G4", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Analyst at a proprietary trading firm building a machine learning system to classify short-term market regimes. The research team has added hundreds of technical indicators, alternative data features, and macroeconomic variables, and the Head of Research is excited because the model performs well on the training dataset. However, the Risk Officer questions whether the KNN-based model can still identify meaningful “similar market days” when the feature space becomes extremely large.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "As the number of features increases, the distance between observations in KNN can become less informative because many points may appear similarly far apart.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "In high dimensions distances become similar across points, making truly close neighbors hard to distinguish.", "whyPool": "In high-dimensional spaces, distances between observations tend to become more similar, making it harder for KNN to distinguish truly close neighbors from unrelated points. This reduces the usefulness of nearest-neighbor comparisons."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Adding more features will always improve KNN performance because more information allows the model to find better neighbors.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Extra features can hurt — irrelevant/noisy variables distort distance-based similarity.", "whyPool": "Additional features can hurt KNN performance if they add irrelevant information or noise. Since KNN relies on distance calculations, unnecessary variables can distort similarity measurements."}, {"L": "C", "text": "High-dimensional datasets can require substantially more observations for KNN to identify reliable local patterns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Higher dimensionality spreads observations out, requiring more data to keep enough nearby examples.", "whyPool": "As dimensionality increases, observations become more spread out, requiring more data points to maintain enough nearby examples for reliable prediction."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The curse of dimensionality occurs because data becomes increasingly sparse as the number of dimensions grows.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The curse of dimensionality is exactly the difficulty of learning as dimensions grow and data becomes sparse.", "whyPool": "The curse of dimensionality refers to the difficulty of learning from data when the number of dimensions grows because the available observations become sparse throughout the feature space."}, {"L": "E", "text": "KNN is generally unaffected by irrelevant features because it automatically ignores variables that do not help prediction.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "KNN does not auto-ignore useless features; irrelevant/redundant ones change distances and worsen neighbor selection.", "whyPool": "KNN does not automatically determine which features are useful. Irrelevant or redundant features can change distance calculations and lead to poorer neighbor selection."}], "reference": "Curse of dimensionality — ISLR §2.2.3; ESL §2.5.", "n": 168}, {"id": "ML-D5G4Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G4", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative research team is expanding a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) model used to predict stock-return co- movements. The original model uses three standardized macroeconomic features. The expanded model uses 250 standardized inputs, including order-flow indicators, technical signals, and other market variables. Although every feature is standardized using statistics estimated from the training data, the expanded model performs substantially worse out of sample. The team also observes that the nearest and farthest training observations are becoming similar in distance from a typical test observation. 152 Rev:081020261230 Which of the following statements correctly describe the curse of dimensionality and its effects on this KNN model?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "With a fixed number of training observations, increasing the number of dimensions makes the observations increasingly sparse relative to the volume of the feature space, so identifying genuinely local neighbors becomes more difficult.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With fixed N, adding dimensions makes points sparse relative to volume — capturing fraction q needs side length r=q^(1/p)→1, so \"local\" neighbors span most of each range.", "whyPool": "Consider a bounded feature space such as a p-dimensional unit hypercube. A neighborhood whose side length is roccupies a fraction r௣ of the total volume. To capture a fixed fraction qof the observations, the required side length is r =q₁/௣. As pincreases, rapproaches 1. Thus, a neighborhood must span an increasingly large portion of each feature’s range to include the same fraction of a fixed training sample. The observations are not necessarily farther apart under every possible scaling convention, but they become sparse relative to the rapidly expanding space, undermining KNN’s assumption that nearby observations provide genuinely local information. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 2.2)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Under commonly studied high-dimensional data distributions, distances may concentrate so that the relative difference between the nearest and farthest observations becomes small, weakening the distinction between “near” and “far. ”", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Distance concentration: (Dmax−Dmin)/Dmin→0 (equivalently Dmin/Dmax→1) as p→∞, weakening the near/far contrast (not literally identical distances).", "whyPool": "Distance concentration describes the tendency, under commonly used distributional assumptions, for distances from a query point to occupy an increasingly narrow relative range as dimensionality grows. One expression of this effect is D₅ୟ୶−D₅₁₆ D₅₁₆ → 0 as p→ ∞, where D₅₁₆and D₅ୟ୶are the minimum and maximum distances from the query point among the available observations. Equivalently, when the quantities are well defined, D₅₁₆ D₅ୟ୶ → 1. This does not mean that all distances become literally identical or that neighbor selection becomes mathematically random. It means that the relative contrast supporting the designation “nearest” becomes weak, making neighborhoods less stable and potentially less informative. 153 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Standardizing every feature to zero mean and unit variance prevents the curse of dimensionality because each feature then contributes on the same numerical scale.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Standardization fixes units/marginal scales but not sparsity, concentration, irrelevant dimensions, or coverage — the model still lives in 250-D.", "whyPool": "Standardization addresses differences in feature units and marginal scales, but it does not solve the curse of dimensionality. After standardization, each feature may have mean zero and variance one in the training sample, yet the model still operates in a 250-dimensional space. Data sparsity, distance concentration, irrelevant dimensions, feature dependence, and limited sample coverage can all remain. Standardization is generally important for distance-based models, but it is not a substitute for choosing informative features or reducing dimensionality."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Adding standardized but irrelevant features can degrade neighbor selection because their accumulated differences may overwhelm the distance contribution of the smaller subset of genuinely informative features.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Each standardized irrelevant feature adds a nonzero term to squared distance; many such terms can swamp the few informative dimensions.", "whyPool": "For standardized features, squared Euclidean distance is d₂(x,z) =෍( ௣ ௝ୀ₁ x௝ −z௝)₂. An irrelevant feature can still contribute a nonzero term to this sum. When many irrelevant or weakly informative features are included, their combined contributions can dominate the relatively small number of dimensions containing useful predictive structure. Two observations that are close in the informative subspace may therefore appear far apart in the full feature space, while observations that are economically dissimilar may be selected as neighbors. The precise share of distance attributable to noise depends on the data distribution, correlations, and signal structure; it cannot be inferred solely from the number of features. Nevertheless, standardization does not prevent irrelevant dimensions from corrupting neighbor selection."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Replacing Euclidean distance with Manhattan distance or a fractional distance measure completely eliminates high- dimensional sparsity and guarantees that KNN will recover meaningful neighborhoods without feature selection or dimensionality reduction.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Manhattan or fractional distances change severity but still aggregate all 250 dims; none eliminates high-dimensional sparsity or guarantees good neighborhoods — feature selection/reduction may still be needed.", "whyPool": "Alternative distance measures can change the severity and practical behavior of distance concentration, but they do not eliminate the fundamental high-dimensional problem. Manhattan distance, d₁(x,z) =෍∣ ௣ ௝ୀ₁ x௝ −z௝ ∣, still aggregates differences across all 250 dimensions. A fractional dissimilarity measure may reduce the relative dominance of large coordinate differences in some datasets, but for an exponent below one it is not a true norm and does not satisfy all metric properties. No choice among these distance measures guarantees meaningful neighborhoods or flawless KNN performance. Feature selection, dimensionality reduction, metric learning, and validation of both Kand the distance measure may still be necessary."}], "reference": "Sparsity (r=q^{1/p}) & distance concentration — ISLR §2.2.3; ESL §2.5; Beyer et al. \"When is nearest neighbor meaningful?\"", "n": 169}, {"id": "ML-D5G4Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G4", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are a Machine Learning Engineer at a quantitative hedge fund developing a KNN model to identify historical market conditions that resemble the current environment. The Head of Trading notices that the model performs well with 5 carefully selected indicators but deteriorates after the team expands the feature set to include hundreds of signals. She asks whether adding more market information should always make the model smarter or whether the additional dimensions may create problems. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Adding many irrelevant features can reduce KNN performance because they can distort the distance calculation used to find neighbors. 154 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: irrelevant features change distances and reduce prediction quality.", "whyPool": "KNN depends on distance calculations, so irrelevant features can change which observations are considered neighbors and reduce prediction quality."}, {"L": "B", "text": "In a very high-dimensional feature space, two observations that appear close under a distance metric are not necessarily meaningfully similar.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: in high-D, \"close\" under a metric need not mean meaningfully similar (distances become indistinguishable).", "whyPool": "In high-dimensional spaces, distances between observations become less distinguishable because many observations may have similar distances from one another. This makes “nearest” neighbors less informative."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The curse of dimensionality primarily affects KNN because it relies on measuring similarity between observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: KNN is especially vulnerable to the curse of dimensionality because it depends on meaningful proximity.", "whyPool": "KNN is particularly vulnerable to the curse of dimensionality because its core assumption is that nearby observations have similar outcomes. When meaningful proximity becomes harder to define, performance declines."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Increasing the number of dimensions always improves the reliability of nearest-neighbor relationships because more characteristics are being considered.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: more dimensions do not reliably improve nearest-neighbor relationships — they add noise, sparsity, and less reliable similarity.", "whyPool": "More dimensions do not necessarily provide more useful information. Additional features can introduce noise, increase sparsity, and make similarity measurements less reliable."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Feature selection can improve KNN performance by removing variables that add noise without improving similarity measurements.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: feature selection can improve KNN by dropping variables that add noise without improving similarity.", "whyPool": "Removing irrelevant or redundant variables can improve KNN by allowing distance calculations to better reflect meaningful relationships between observations. G5: Computational Tradeoffs"}], "reference": "Curse of dimensionality & feature selection — ISLR §2.2.3; scikit-learn feature selection guide.", "n": 170}, {"id": "ML-D5G5Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G5", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A desk compares linear regression with a straightforward KNN regressor. The KNN implementation stores the historical training observations and does little computation until a prediction request arrives. The production team is concerned about response time as the dataset grows. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "KNN is commonly called a lazy learner because it defers much of its work until inference.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: KNN is a lazy learner — it defers most work to inference.", "whyPool": "KNN largely stores examples rather than estimating a compact predictive function during fitting."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A basic KNN implementation has relatively little model-fitting cost.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: basic KNN fitting mainly records data and config (little model-fitting cost).", "whyPool": "Basic fitting mainly records the data and associated configuration."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A basic KNN prediction may require computing distances to many or all stored training observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: brute-force prediction may compute distances to many/all stored observations.", "whyPool": "Brute-force neighbor search compares a query with the stored instances."}, {"L": "D", "text": "KNN always produces faster predictions than linear regression because it has no fitted coefficients.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: KNN is not always faster — a linear model evaluates a compact equation while KNN must search for neighbors.", "whyPool": "A linear model can evaluate a compact equation, while KNN must search for neighbors."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Increasing the stored dataset can increase inference time for a brute-force implementation. 155 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: more stored candidates generally increase inference time for brute-force search.", "whyPool": "More candidates generally mean more distance calculations."}], "reference": "Lazy vs. eager; O(N·p) inference — scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" (algorithm/complexity); ISLR §2.2.3.", "n": 171}, {"id": "ML-D5G5Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G5", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A KNN system retains ten million feature vectors and their targets. A manager says the trained model should occupy almost no memory because KNN estimates no coefficient vector. Engineers consider indexing and approximate-neighbor methods to improve deployment.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "KNN may require substantial memory because it retains training instances.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "KNN can need substantial memory because it retains the training instances as its representation.", "whyPool": "The examples themselves constitute a major part of the learned representation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The absence of a conventional coefficient-fitting step implies that dataset size has no deployment impact.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Dataset size affects memory, search time, and operational complexity even without coefficient fitting.", "whyPool": "Dataset size affects memory, search time, and operational complexity."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A search index can trade preprocessing and storage complexity for faster neighbor queries.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A search index does preprocessing/storage work in advance to speed up neighbor queries.", "whyPool": "Index construction performs work in advance to support faster retrieval."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Approximate nearest-neighbor search may reduce latency while occasionally returning neighbors that are not exact.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Approximate nearest-neighbor search cuts latency by occasionally returning non-exact neighbors.", "whyPool": "Approximation sacrifices exactness to improve computational performance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Deleting stored observations can never change KNN predictions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Deleting stored observations can change predictions — removed points may have been neighbors for some queries.", "whyPool": "Removed observations may have been selected as neighbors for some queries."}], "reference": "Memory O(N·p); ANN tradeoffs — scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" (KDTree/BallTree, approximate methods); HNSW/LSH literature.", "n": 172}, {"id": "ML-D5G5Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G5", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A nightly research pipeline adds new observations to a KNN model. The team appreciates that it can append records without solving a new optimization problem, but its batch forecast now takes hours. The lead proposes comparing algorithmic, data, and system-level remedies.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Appending a new labeled instance can be simpler than refitting many eager-learning models. 156 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Appending a labeled instance can be simpler than refitting many eager models.", "whyPool": "A basic KNN learner can incorporate a new case by storing it."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Cheap updates imply that KNN inference remains constant-time as the dataset grows.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Cheap updates do not make inference constant-time — brute-force search grows with the dataset.", "whyPool": "A brute-force search generally becomes more expensive with more stored instances."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Reducing the number of stored prototypes can lower search cost but may alter predictions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Reducing stored prototypes lowers search cost but can alter predictions by dropping useful local examples.", "whyPool": "Fewer candidates reduce work but may remove useful local examples."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Efficient data structures are equally effective in every dimension and for every distance metric.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Efficient data structures are not equally effective in every dimension/metric — they degrade in high-D and vary by metric.", "whyPool": "Index effectiveness can deteriorate in high dimensions and varies by metric and structure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Computational suitability depends on dataset size, dimensionality, latency needs, and implementation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Suitability depends jointly on dataset size, dimensionality, latency needs, and implementation.", "whyPool": "These factors jointly determine whether the approach is practical."}], "reference": "Prototype reduction; index degradation in high-D — ESL ch.13 (prototype methods); scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" complexity notes.", "n": 173}, {"id": "ML-D5G5Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G5", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative trading team is evaluating a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) model to generate real-time execution signals for a mid-frequency equities trading strategy. The team maintains a rolling historical database of N=100,000 market snapshots, each containing p=40 normalized microstructural features. The strategy receives a new market snapshot every 500 ms and must evaluate the K-nearest historical regimes (K=25) using Euclidean distance to output a signal before the next snapshot arrives. The research team conducts a computational analysis comparing the training, storage, and inference performance of KNN against eager learning models (e.g., Logistic Regression and Neural Networks). Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the computational tradeoffs, memory footprint, and time- complexity properties of this KNN setup?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "As a classic \"lazy learning\" algorithm, KNN has a training time complexity of O(1) (or O(0) if no indexing structures are built), because it defers all model fitting and pattern abstraction until inference time by simply storing the training dataset in memory.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Lazy learning: training just stores data, so construction cost is effectively O(1).", "whyPool": "KNN is the quintessential lazy learner. During the training phase, it performs no parameter estimation or model optimization; it simply stores the dataset in memory. Thus, nominal training construction cost is effectively O(1)."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The brute-force search time complexity to process a single incoming test snapshot during real-time inference is O(N⋅p), requiring the algorithm to calculate 100,000×40 feature differences for every single trade decision.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Brute-force inference computes distance to every stored instance across all features → O(N·p) per query.", "whyPool": "A brute-force nearest-neighbor lookup must compute the pairwise Euclidean distance between the new test point and every single stored instance across all feature dimensions. For N instances and p features, calculating the distances takes O(N⋅p) operations per inference query."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Building spatial indexing trees (such as k-d trees or Ball trees) reduces inference search complexity to O(logN) on average, guaranteeing ultra-fast real-time lookups even when the feature dimension is very large (p=40).", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "k-d/ball trees give ~O(log N) only in low dimensions; at p=40 the curse of dimensionality collapses them back toward O(N).", "whyPool": "While k-d trees reduce search time to O(logN) in low-dimensional spaces (p<10 or 20), their tree-partitioning efficiency degrades rapidly in high dimensions (p=40). Due to the curse of dimensionality, searching a k-d tree in high dimensions collapses back to an exhaustive O(N) brute-force search."}, {"L": "D", "text": "KNN is exceptionally memory-efficient compared to parametric models like Logistic Regression, because storing raw numerical data arrays requires far less memory space than storing learned parameter vectors and covariance matrices.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "KNN must retain the full data → O(N·p) memory, far more than logistic regression's O(p) coefficient vector.", "whyPool": "KNN must ordinarily retain the full training dataset, requiring O(Np)memory for Nobservations with pfeatures. By comparison, a fitted Logistic Regression model can typically discard the training observations and retain only its coefficient vector β∈ R௣ା₁, requiring O(p)model storage. KNN therefore generally has a substantially larger memory footprint than a comparable parametric model. The value of Kdetermines how many neighbors contribute to each prediction; it does not mean that only Ktraining observations need to be stored in advance. Data-reduction or prototype-selection methods can lower the storage requirement, but those are additional approximations rather than properties of standard KNN."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Unlike eager learning algorithms that condense the training data into a compact parameter vector, KNN is exceptionally memory-efficient because it stores only the Kobservations needed for each future prediction. 157 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Eager models pay upfront training for ~O(p) inference; KNN flips this — ~0 training but expensive, N-scaling inference and memory.", "whyPool": "Parametric models pay a heavy computational cost upfront during training, but offer near-instantaneous O(p) inference. KNN flips this trade-off: zero training cost, but an expensive inference phase where execution time and memory scale linearly with sample size N, making real-time deployment challenging for large N."}], "reference": "Time/space complexity, tree degradation — scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" (algorithm complexity); ESL ch.13.", "withdrawn": true, "n": 174}, {"id": "ML-D5G5Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G5", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Developer at a hedge fund designing a machine learning pipeline for intraday trading signals. The Head of Trading likes KNN because it can quickly adapt to changing market patterns, but the Technology Lead is concerned that the model may not scale as the firm stores millions of historical market observations. The team must decide whether KNN’s simplicity during model development outweighs its computational costs during live prediction. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "KNN is considered a lazy learning algorithm because most computation is delayed until a prediction request is made.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: KNN is lazy — it stores data and defers computation to prediction time.", "whyPool": "KNN is a lazy learner because it does not construct a generalized model during training. Instead, it stores the training data and performs computation when a prediction is requested."}, {"L": "B", "text": "KNN generally requires more memory than many eager learning algorithms because it stores training examples for future comparisons.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: retaining the training set makes KNN memory-heavy vs. parameter-summarizing models.", "whyPool": "Since KNN retains the training dataset and uses it during prediction, it can require significant memory compared with models that summarize data through learned parameters."}, {"L": "C", "text": "KNN usually has very fast inference because it does not build an explicit model during training.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: KNN inference is often *slow* (distances to many points), even though training is cheap.", "whyPool": "KNN typically has low training cost but potentially high inference cost because it must calculate distances to many observations when making predictions."}, {"L": "D", "text": "For a very large historical dataset, KNN prediction latency can increase because the algorithm must search through many stored observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: more stored observations increase distance calculations and inference latency at scale.", "whyPool": "As the number of stored observations grows, the number of distance calculations required during inference can increase, creating latency challenges in large-scale applications."}, {"L": "E", "text": "KNN may be practical for some trading applications when prediction speed is less critical and the dataset size is manageable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: KNN can be practical when latency is relaxed and dataset size is manageable — selection depends on constraints, not accuracy alone.", "whyPool": "KNN can still be useful when datasets are moderate in size, prediction latency requirements are relaxed, or the ability to use local patterns is valuable. Model selection depends on practical constraints, not only predictive ability."}], "reference": "Lazy learning inference cost — scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" (computational performance); ISLR §2.2.3.", "n": 175}, {"id": "ML-D5G5Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G5", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Engineer at a market-making firm evaluating whether KNN should be deployed for a new trading signal system. The Head of Trading wants a model that can identify similar historical price patterns, but the Infrastructure Manager warns that the system must process thousands of predictions per second with strict latency requirements. The team must assess whether KNN’s minimal training requirements actually translate into operational efficiency.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that A major advantage of KNN is that training is computationally inexpensive because it primarily stores the training data rather than estimating many model parameters.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The embedded claim (KNN training is cheap because it mostly stores data) is accurate, so calling it incorrect is false — KNN is lazy and does little at training time.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is accurate, so saying that it is incorrect is false. KNN is a lazy learning method that performs little computation during training. It mainly stores examples and delays computation until predictions are requested."}, {"L": "B", "text": "KNN can have high inference costs because each prediction may require computing distances to many stored training observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Inference can be costly: each prediction may compute distances to many stored observations.", "whyPool": "During prediction, KNN must compare a new observation against stored examples to identify the nearest neighbors. This can become expensive as the dataset grows."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Increasing the size of the training dataset can improve KNN’s ability to find similar examples while also potentially increasing prediction latency.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "More historical data improves neighbor availability but increases search/latency during inference.", "whyPool": "More historical examples may provide better opportunities to find relevant neighbors, but searching through a larger dataset increases the computational burden during inference. 159 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "KNN is always preferable to linear models in production systems because avoiding a training phase makes the overall system faster.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Avoiding a training phase does not make the whole system faster — KNN trades low training cost for high prediction cost, unlike fast-inference linear models.", "whyPool": "Avoiding training computation does not mean the overall system is faster. KNN often trades lower training cost for higher prediction cost, while models such as linear regression may have slightly more training effort but extremely fast inference."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The difference between training and inference costs is an important consideration when choosing KNN for a real-time trading application.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Deployment must weigh both training and inference costs; in latency-sensitive trading, prediction speed can dominate.", "whyPool": "Deployment decisions must consider both training and inference requirements. In latency-sensitive trading environments, prediction speed may matter more than training speed. G6: Practical Use in Trading Systems"}], "reference": "Train-vs-infer cost tradeoff — scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" (performance); ISLR §2.2.3.", "n": 176}, {"id": "ML-D5G6Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G6", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A high-frequency trading system must make a decision within microseconds after each quote update. A proposed KNN model would search several million stored market states using a brute-force implementation before returning every prediction.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The proposed search may violate the system's latency budget.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Millions of per-query distance calculations can exceed a microsecond deadline.", "whyPool": "Millions of per-query distance calculations can be too slow for a microsecond deadline."}, {"L": "B", "text": "KNN's low fitting cost guarantees that it is suitable for latency-critical inference.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Cheap fitting does not imply cheap prediction — inference is the constraint here.", "whyPool": "Cheap fitting does not imply cheap prediction."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Prediction-time cost matters more than offline training convenience for this deployment decision.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "For this deployment, prediction-time cost dominates over offline training convenience.", "whyPool": "The production requirement is dominated by the strict response-time constraint."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Reducing the reference set or using an optimized search method could lower latency, subject to validation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Reducing the reference set or using an optimized search can cut latency, subject to validation of predictive impact.", "whyPool": "Engineering alternatives can reduce cost but may change predictive behavior."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A model that is accurate offline is automatically deployable in a real-time trading system.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Offline accuracy alone is not enough — deployment also needs acceptable latency, reliability, and resource use.", "whyPool": "Deployment also requires acceptable latency, reliability, and resource use."}], "reference": "Latency constraints, ANN methods — scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\"; HNSW/LSH for low-latency retrieval.", "n": 177}, {"id": "ML-D5G6Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G6", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An asset manager uses KNN once each evening to estimate next-week returns for 500 securities. The dataset contains several years of daily observations, the features are carefully scaled, and the complete batch finishes in two minutes within a one-hour research window.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "KNN may be operationally reasonable because the latency requirement is modest.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The modest latency requirement makes KNN operationally reasonable here.", "whyPool": "The observed runtime fits comfortably inside the stated operational window."}, {"L": "B", "text": "KNN is inappropriate for every trading application merely because it is an instance-based method. 160 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Being instance-based does not make KNN inappropriate for *every* application — suitability depends on frequency/scale/latency.", "whyPool": "Suitability depends on the particular frequency, scale, and latency constraints."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The feature scaling remains important even when predictions are made only once per day.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Feature scaling still affects neighbor identity regardless of how often inference runs.", "whyPool": "Scaling affects neighbor identity regardless of how often inference occurs."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The team should still evaluate out-of-sample performance and stability rather than relying on acceptable runtime alone.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Acceptable runtime does not establish predictive validity — still evaluate out-of-sample performance and stability.", "whyPool": "Computational feasibility does not establish predictive validity."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A two-minute batch runtime proves that the model will remain feasible after arbitrary growth in data and query volume.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A two-minute batch today does not prove feasibility after arbitrary growth in data/query volume.", "whyPool": "Future growth can increase memory and search requirements."}], "reference": "Operational suitability & scaling — ISLR §2.2.3; scikit-learn preprocessing + performance notes.", "n": 178}, {"id": "ML-D5G6Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G6", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk team uses KNN to find historical days similar to the current market and estimate a distribution of possible portfolio losses. During an unprecedented market shock, the system still returns the mathematically nearest historical days, even though all are far from the current query.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "KNN always returns neighbors when enough stored observations exist, even if none is genuinely close.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A ranking always exists given enough stored data, even when the best matches are poor.", "whyPool": "A ranking exists even when the best available matches are poor."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A large distance to the nearest neighbors can warn that the query is unlike the historical reference set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A large nearest-neighbor distance warns the query is unlike the historical reference set.", "whyPool": "Absolute distance can provide information beyond the relative neighbor order."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The nearest-neighbor label guarantees that the selected days are economically valid analogues.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Mathematical proximity under chosen features does not guarantee the days are valid economic analogues.", "whyPool": "Mathematical proximity under chosen features does not guarantee economic comparability."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Monitoring neighbor distances can support an abstention or fallback policy for unfamiliar states.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Monitoring neighbor distances supports an abstention/fallback policy for unfamiliar states.", "whyPool": "A distance threshold can flag cases where local historical evidence is weak."}, {"L": "E", "text": "KNN inherently extrapolates a new regression trend far beyond the observed target patterns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Standard KNN combines stored neighbor targets; it does not fit an extrapolative trend beyond observed patterns.", "whyPool": "Standard KNN combines stored neighbor targets rather than fitting an extrapolative trend. 161 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Distance thresholds / abstention; extrapolation limits — ESL ch.13; concept \"out-of-distribution detection via neighbor distance.\"", "n": 179}, {"id": "ML-D5G6Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G6", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Analyst at a proprietary trading firm evaluating KNN for a daily stock-selection model. The Chief Investment Officer believes that because KNN is simple and requires little training, it will automatically be the fastest and easiest model to deploy at scale. The Engineering Lead asks you to review this assumption because the system must process a large universe of stocks using years of historical market data.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that KNN is always faster than linear models in production because it avoids the need to train a model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The embedded claim (KNN is always faster than linear models because it avoids training) is inaccurate, so rejecting it is correct — KNN's inference search can be expensive.", "whyPool": "The embedded claim is inaccurate, so rejecting it is correct. KNN often has low training cost, but this does not mean it is faster in production. Its inference phase can be expensive because it must search through stored observations."}, {"L": "B", "text": "KNN requires almost no memory because it only stores the final prediction rules instead of historical observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "KNN is memory-intensive: it stores training observations rather than compact prediction rules.", "whyPool": "KNN is a memory-intensive method because it typically stores the training examples and uses them during prediction rather than compressing the data into a small set of learned parameters."}, {"L": "C", "text": "KNN is an ideal choice for high-frequency trading because its prediction process does not require any distance calculations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "KNN predictions require distance calculations, which is why it struggles in latency-sensitive settings.", "whyPool": "KNN predictions require distance calculations to determine which observations are nearest. This computation is one reason KNN can be challenging for latency-sensitive applications."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Once KNN has been trained, its inference cost remains constant regardless of dataset size.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "KNN inference cost grows with the number of stored examples — it is not constant regardless of dataset size.", "whyPool": "KNN inference cost can grow with the number of stored training examples because additional observations must be considered during neighbor searches."}, {"L": "E", "text": "KNN may have low training cost but can have higher prediction cost because computation is performed when making predictions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "KNN has low training cost but potentially high prediction cost because computation shifts to inference (lazy learning).", "whyPool": "KNN is a lazy learning algorithm: it performs little work during training and shifts computational effort to prediction time. This tradeoff must be considered when selecting a model for deployment."}], "reference": "Lazy-learning deployment tradeoffs — scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" (performance); ISLR §2.2.3.", "n": 180}, {"id": "ML-D5G6Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G6", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative high-frequency trading (HFT) firm is designing an automated order-routing system. The system needs to classify short-term order-book regimes into \"Liquidity Abundant\" vs. \"Liquidity Deficient\" to adjust execution algorithms in real time. The execution engine receives sub-millisecond market feed updates and requires inference latency to remain strictly under 100 microseconds per update. A junior researcher proposes deploying a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) model trained on a rolling window of N=500,000 order-book snapshots, each consisting of p=50 microstructural features. The quantitative lead rejects using a pure KNN 162 Rev:081020261230 model for this sub-millisecond execution pipeline, but suggests alternative scenarios where instance-based learning concepts could still be viable. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the real-time latency constraints, system suitability, and practical trade-offs of deploying KNN in financial trading systems?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Exact brute-force KNN is fundamentally unsuitable for high-frequency sub-millisecond trading execution because its O(N⋅p) inference complexity forces the engine to evaluate tens of millions of float operations per feed update, far exceeding sub-millisecond latency budgets.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Brute-force O(N·p) ≈ 25M float ops per tick takes milliseconds — far beyond a 100-μs budget, so exact KNN is unsuitable for sub-ms HFT.", "whyPool": "In a sub-millisecond HFT environment, every microsecond matters. A brute-force KNN query across N=500,000 snapshots with p=50 features requires 25 million floating-point operations per incoming tick (O(N⋅p)). Computing this sequentially in software takes tens of milliseconds, completely violating the 100 microsecond latency limit."}, {"L": "B", "text": "In high-frequency, sub-millisecond trading pipelines, exact spatial indexing trees (like k-d trees or Ball trees) completely resolve the KNN latency bottleneck for p=50 features, reducing lookup times to sub-microsecond levels.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Exact spatial trees degrade to O(N) once p ≳ 20–30 (curse of dimensionality), so at p=50 they give almost no speedup.", "whyPool": "Due to the curse of dimensionality, tree-based indexing structures (k-d trees, Ball trees) degrade to O(N) brute- force search efficiency when the feature dimension p exceeds roughly 20–30. For p=50, spatial trees provide almost no speedup over a standard exhaustive scan."}, {"L": "C", "text": "To make instance-based neighbor search feasible for low-latency trading systems, practitioners can replace exact KNN with Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) algorithms (such as Hierarchical Navigable Small World (HNSW) graphs or Locality-Sensitive Hashing (LSH)), trading a marginal amount of search recall for orders-of-magnitude faster inference.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "ANN methods (HNSW, LSH) trade a little recall for orders-of-magnitude faster, sub-ms lookups on high-D data, making instance-based retrieval viable.", "whyPool": "Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) methods relax the requirement of finding the absolute true nearest point in exchange for logarithmic or constant-time lookups. Graph-based indices like HNSW can achieve sub-millisecond lookup speeds on high-dimensional datasets, making instance-based retrieval viable where exact search fails."}, {"L": "D", "text": "KNN is ideally suited for real-time trade execution because, unlike neural networks or decision trees, its zero-training- time nature (O(1)) allows it to update its parameters instantaneously as new order-book ticks arrive without triggering costly retraining loops.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "No formal retraining does not fix the inference bottleneck; eager models evaluate in ~O(p) (nanoseconds), so KNN is not \"ideally suited\" to real-time execution.", "whyPool": "While KNN has no formal model retraining phase (it simply appends new data points to RAM), its inference bottleneck makes it unfit for real-time execution. Eager learning models (like Logistic Regression or linear decision boundaries) require upfront training time, but evaluate in O(p) time during execution (simple matrix multiplication), executing in nanoseconds."}, {"L": "E", "text": "While unsuited for sub-millisecond trade execution, instance-based learning (KNN) can still be effective in offline, low- frequency financial tasks—such as daily macro regime classification, historical scenario stress-testing, or OTC illiquid bond pricing—where inference latency budgets are measured in seconds or minutes rather than microseconds.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "KNN fits offline/low-frequency tasks (daily macro regime classification, stress-testing, illiquid bond pricing) where latency budgets are seconds/minutes.", "whyPool": "KNN excels when the goal is to find \"analogous historical regimes\" in offline or daily macro analysis. When speed is not a hard constraint (e.g., daily asset allocation, finding comparable illiquid corporate bonds for pricing), KNN provides intuitive, non-parametric predictions based directly on past historical matches. 163 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "HFT latency, tree degradation, ANN — scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\"; HNSW (Malkov & Yashunin), LSH literature.", "n": 181}, {"id": "ML-D5G6Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Instance-Based Learning (KNN)", "group": "G6", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative trading team is evaluating machine learning architectures to power two distinct trading strategies: Strategy 1 (Ultra-Low Latency Market Making): Operates on tick-by-tick order-book updates, placing and canceling limit orders within microsecond execution windows (<500 μs). 2. Strategy 2 (End-of-Day Tactical Asset Allocation): Rebalances portfolio weights once per day based on historical macroeconomic indicators, market valuation ratios, and volatility indices. The lead researcher compares a K-Nearest Neighbors (KNN) approach against an eager learning Feedforward Neural Network (FNN) for both strategies. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the suitability, algorithmic characteristics, and practical deployment considerations of KNN vs. eager models across these two trading regimes?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Strategy 1 is poorly suited for exact KNN because inference latency scales directly with dataset size (O(N⋅p)), whereas an FNN—despite taking hours to train offline—evaluates incoming ticks via fixed matrix multiplications (O(p) per layer), executing in microsecond timeframes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "For Strategy 1, exact KNN inference scales O(N·p) with stored data, while an FNN (hours to train) evaluates fixed matrix multiplies in ~O(p) per layer — microsecond-feasible.", "whyPool": "In low-latency trading, inference speed is paramount. KNN requires evaluating distances against all stored instances (O(N⋅p)), which grows rapidly with historical memory size. An eager model like an FNN pays the computational cost upfront during training, leaving a lightweight forward pass during live inference that completes in constant, deterministic time relative to data size (N)."}, {"L": "B", "text": "In Strategy 1, KNN's memory footprint (O(N⋅p)) can be constrained without sacrificing historical search depth by streaming real-time order-book updates directly to disk storage rather than maintaining data structures in RAM.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Streaming the dataset to disk cannot meet microsecond limits: disk I/O is millisecond-scale, thousands of times slower than RAM.", "whyPool": "Disk read/write operations (I/O latency) operate on millisecond scales, which is thousands of times slower than RAM lookups (nanosecond scales). Offloading a low-latency model's historical dataset to disk during live inference would destroy any chance of meeting microsecond execution constraints."}, {"L": "C", "text": "In Strategy 2, KNN provides strong practical value because its non-parametric nature allows portfolio managers to easily trace predictions back to specific historical analog dates (e.g., \"1998 LTCM Crisis\" or \"2020 COVID Shock\"), enhancing model interpretability for risk committees.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "For Strategy 2, KNN's non-parametric nature is interpretable — predictions trace to specific historical analog dates (e.g., 1998 LTCM, 2020 COVID), aiding risk committees.", "whyPool": "In low-frequency asset allocation, model transparency is crucial for risk management. Unlike black-box neural networks, KNN is inherently explainable: a prediction can be presented as \"The current macro regime is 85% similar to these 5 specific historical dates, \" giving portfolio managers intuitive, actionable context."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because KNN continuously incorporates new data points simply by appending them to its stored memory array (O(1) dynamic updating), it completely eliminates the risks associated with concept drift in fast-changing financial markets.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Appending data (O(1)) does not defeat concept drift; stale non-stationary instances keep being selected unless windows are pruned/re-weighted.", "whyPool": "Appending new data points updating memory does not protect against concept drift (changes in the underlying data-generating process). If old, obsolete historical instances remain in the stored memory array alongside new market 164 Rev:081020261230 regime data, KNN will continue to select outdated, non-stationary neighbors, corrupting real-time predictions unless historical data windows are pruned or re-weighted."}, {"L": "E", "text": "When applying KNN to Strategy 2, if the historical dataset grows over a 30-year daily macro series (N≈7,500 observations, p=15 features), the inference calculation executes in milliseconds, making KNN's inference overhead entirely negligible for daily rebalancing.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "For daily TAA, N≈7,500 and p=15 (~112,500 ops) is trivial within a minutes/hours budget, so KNN's O(N·p) inference overhead is negligible.", "whyPool": "For daily TAA strategies, N=7,500 and p=15 represents a tiny computational workload (7,500×15=112,500 operations). Since a daily strategy's execution budget is measured in minutes or hours rather than microseconds, KNN's O(N⋅p) inference overhead is completely trivial in this regime. 165 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Lazy vs. eager inference; concept drift; interpretability — ISLR §2.2.3; scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\"; concept \"instance-based interpretability.\"", "n": 182}, {"id": "ML-D6G1Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A decision tree predicts whether the next trading day will have a positive return. Its root tests whether 20-day momentum is greater than 2%. If yes, the next node tests whether volatility is below 1.5%; the resulting leaf predicts “positive. ” A market day has 3% momentum and 1.2% volatility. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: The observation first follows the branch corresponding to momentum greater than 2%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double-negative: \"the claim is false\" that momentum>2% branch is taken — but 3% > 2% is satisfied, so the inner claim is accurate and calling it false is incorrect.", "whyPool": "The root condition is satisfied because 3% exceeds 2%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The observation then follows the branch corresponding to volatility below 1.5%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Volatility 1.2% < 1.5% satisfies the second test, so the observation does follow that branch.", "whyPool": "The observation's volatility of 1.2% satisfies the second condition. 167 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "The prediction is produced by combining the outputs of every leaf in the tree.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a single tree predicts from the **one** leaf the observation reaches, not by combining outputs of every leaf (that is ensemble behavior).", "whyPool": "A single tree normally predicts from the one leaf reached by the observation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "For this observation, a split in an unvisited branch cannot change its prediction.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: only tests along the observation's own root-to-leaf path affect its prediction; a split in an unvisited branch cannot change it.", "whyPool": "Only tests along the observation's root-to-leaf path affect its prediction."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The leaf reached by the observation predicts “positive. ”", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the scenario defines the reached leaf as carrying the \"positive\" prediction.", "whyPool": "The scenario specifies that the reached leaf carries the positive prediction."}], "reference": "Tree prediction flow / single-path routing — ISLR ch.8 (§8.1 tree basics); scikit-learn \"Decision Trees\" (prediction).", "n": 183}, {"id": "ML-D6G1Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A regression tree estimates next-week portfolio return. One terminal leaf contains training targets of 1%, 2%, -1%, and 2%. A new observation follows the tree's tests into that leaf, and the implementation uses the mean target within each leaf.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The prediction for the new observation is 1%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Mean = (1+2−1+2)/4 = 1%, the leaf's constant prediction.", "whyPool": "The mean is (1% + 2% - 1% + 2%) / 4 = 1%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Every observation reaching that leaf receives the same prediction under this implementation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Every observation reaching a leaf gets that leaf's single aggregate prediction.", "whyPool": "The leaf stores one aggregate prediction used for all observations reaching it."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The tree must calculate a distance from the new observation to every training observation at prediction time.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A tree follows split rules; it does not compute KNN-style distances to all training points at inference.", "whyPool": "Tree inference follows split rules and does not require a KNN-style exhaustive distance search."}, {"L": "D", "text": "An internal node applies a feature-based rule that determines which branch to follow.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Internal nodes route observations by their feature-based split conditions.", "whyPool": "Internal nodes route observations according to their split conditions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A terminal leaf must contain exactly one training observation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A leaf may contain one or many training observations, not exactly one.", "whyPool": "A leaf may contain one or many training observations."}], "reference": "Regression-tree leaf mean prediction — ISLR §8.1 (regression trees); scikit-learn `DecisionTreeRegressor`.", "n": 184}, {"id": "ML-D6G1Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk officer reviews a tree that flags trades for manual review. The path for one trade is: leverage > 2.0, liquidity score ≤ 40, and expected holding period > 10 days, leading to a “review” leaf. The tree contains many other nodes involving sector and market capitalization.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The three listed conditions describe the decision path for this trade.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Those three tests were encountered in sequence before the prediction — they describe this trade's path.", "whyPool": "Those tests were encountered sequentially before the prediction was made."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The sector and market-capitalization nodes must also have been evaluated because they appear somewhere in the tree.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Nodes off the selected path (sector, market cap) are not evaluated for this trade merely because they exist in the tree.", "whyPool": "Nodes outside the selected path are not evaluated for this trade."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The root-to-leaf path can be translated into a conjunction of conditions explaining this prediction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The root-to-leaf path translates into a conjunction (if-and-then) rule explaining the prediction.", "whyPool": "The path provides an if-and-then rule for the individual prediction."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Changing the trade's leverage so that it takes the opposite root branch may lead to a different leaf. 168 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Changing leverage so the opposite root branch is taken changes the subsequent path and possibly the leaf.", "whyPool": "Taking a different branch changes the subsequent path and potentially the output."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A leaf is another split that sends the trade to two child nodes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A leaf is terminal (gives a prediction); it is not another split sending the trade to children.", "whyPool": "A leaf is terminal and provides a prediction rather than another split."}], "reference": "Root-to-leaf paths as conjunctive rules / interpretability — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn \"Understanding the decision tree structure.\"", "n": 185}, {"id": "ML-D6G1Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Researcher at an asset management firm building an interpretable machine learning model to explain portfolio allocation decisions. The Chief Risk Officer prefers decision trees because the investment committee wants to understand the logic behind each trade recommendation. A junior analyst claims that decision trees are difficult to interpret because they behave like black-box models, and you are asked to evaluate whether that assumption is correct.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Decision trees generate predictions by averaging the outputs of all possible paths through the tree rather than following one specific path.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees do **not** average all possible paths; each observation follows one path to one leaf.", "whyPool": "A decision tree does not average across all possible paths. Each observation follows one specific path determined by the splitting rules until it reaches a leaf node where the prediction is made."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Decision trees require all numerical features to be standardized before creating splits.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "No standardization required — threshold splits on individual variables, not distance calculations.", "whyPool": "Decision trees do not require feature scaling because they make decisions using threshold-based splits on individual variables rather than distance calculations."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Decision trees cannot represent nonlinear relationships because each split only considers a simple threshold.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Individual splits are simple, but many combined splits capture nonlinearities and interactions.", "whyPool": "While each individual split is simple, a combination of many splits allows decision trees to capture nonlinear relationships and interactions among variables."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Decision trees always produce identical structures when trained on the same dataset, regardless of small changes in the data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees can be unstable: small data changes can alter selected splits → different structures.", "whyPool": "Decision trees can be unstable because small changes in training data may change which splits are selected, resulting in different tree structures. 169 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "A decision tree makes a prediction by following a sequence of feature-based splits until reaching a terminal leaf node.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct definition: prediction = routing through feature-based splits from root to a terminal leaf.", "whyPool": "A decision tree makes predictions by routing an observation through a sequence of feature-based decisions from the root node to a terminal leaf node containing the final prediction."}], "reference": "Tree interpretability, nonlinearity, instability — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn \"Decision Trees\" (advantages/disadvantages).", "n": 186}, {"id": "ML-D6G1Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative portfolio manager is developing a dynamic asset-allocation strategy for an equity portfolio. The strategy uses a Decision Tree Regressor to predict next-month excess returns from two technical indicators: •  X₁: 14-Day Relative Strength Index (RSI), bounded between 0 and 100 •  X₂: Simple Moving Average Crossover Ratio, a continuous decimal-valued feature The manager trains a standard CART regression tree using squared-error impurity. Before deployment, the risk team examines how the tree’s axis-aligned splits, greedy construction process, instability, and piecewise-constant predictions affect its use in a trading application. Which of the following statements correctly describe the architecture and behavior of a standard CART Decision Tree Regressor?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Feature scaling is required because CART compares the numerical magnitudes of different features when deciding which feature should be used at a node.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "CART evaluates one feature at a time; a split depends on which points fall each side of the threshold, not on relative feature scale → no standardization needed.", "whyPool": "CART evaluates a split using one feature at a time. For a candidate split X௝ ≤s, the algorithm depends on which observations fall on each side of the threshold—not on whether X௝is measured on a numerically larger or smaller scale than another feature. Consequently, standardization and min-max normalization are not ordinarily required for decision trees. A strictly monotonic transformation of a feature preserves its ordering and therefore preserves the candidate partitions available to the tree, apart from numerical precision, ties, implementation details, and corresponding changes in the reported threshold values. This differs from distance-based methods such as KNN, in which the numerical scales of multiple features jointly determine geometric distances."}, {"L": "B", "text": "CART partitions the feature space using axis-aligned splits of the form X௝ ≤s. These splits can represent rectangular, threshold-based regimes efficiently but may require many successive splits to approximate a diagonal relationship such as X₁+X₂ >C.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Splits X_j ≤ s are axis-aligned rectangles; a diagonal boundary X1+X2>C needs a many-split staircase approximation.", "whyPool": "A standard CART split has the form R₁(j,s) =൛x:x௝ ≤sൟ, R₂(j,s) =൛x:x௝ >sൟ. 170 Rev:081020261230 Each split is perpendicular to one feature axis and parallel to the remaining axes. Repeated splits therefore partition the feature space into axis-aligned rectangular regions. This structure is well suited to threshold effects, such as a return regime that changes when RSI exceeds a particular value. However, a diagonal boundary such as X₁+X₂ >C does not align with either feature axis. A standard tree must approximate it using a staircase of horizontal and vertical partitions, potentially requiring many splits. The precise number depends on the data distribution and the desired approximation accuracy."}, {"L": "C", "text": "At each internal node, CART greedily searches candidate features and split thresholds and selects the split that produces the largest reduction in the regression-tree objective, such as the weighted sum of squared errors within the resulting child nodes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Greedy top-down recursive binary splitting selects the (feature, threshold) minimizing within-node squared error / maximizing impurity reduction.", "whyPool": "CART uses greedy, top-down recursive binary splitting. For regression with squared-error loss, it searches candidate pairs (j,s)and selects the pair that minimizes the total within-node squared error: ෍ ₅ŷ −yത̂భ₉₂ ₅೔∈̂భ₅j,s₉ + ෍ ₅ŷ −yത̂మ₉₂ ₅೔∈̂మ₅j,s₉ . Equivalently, it chooses the split that produces the greatest reduction in squared-error impurity relative to the parent node. The search is greedy because CART selects the best split for the current node without jointly optimizing the complete future tree. Conceptually, it considers the available features and admissible thresholds, although implementations may use sorting and other computational optimizations rather than recomputing every split naively."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A deep, minimally constrained tree generally has low training bias but high variance because relatively small changes in the training sample can change an early split and consequently alter many downstream branches.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Deep, minimally constrained trees have low training bias but high variance: a changed early split propagates through downstream branches.", "whyPool": "A deep tree can adapt closely to the training observations, giving it low training bias and potentially very low training error. That flexibility also makes it unstable: a modest change in the sample can change which feature or threshold appears best at an early node. Because all later branches depend on earlier partitions, the resulting tree and its predictions can change substantially. This high-variance behavior is one reason trees are commonly pruned or constrained using parameters such as maximum depth, minimum leaf size, and minimum impurity reduction. Ensemble methods such as bagging and random forests also reduce variance by averaging predictions across multiple trees. The sensitivity is possible rather than inevitable: not every small data change will alter a root-level split, but individual deep trees are generally less stable than lower-variance models."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because a Decision Tree Regressor is non-parametric, it can naturally extrapolate beyond the range of response values observed in the training data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A mean-predicting regression tree cannot extrapolate; out-of-range inputs still land in an existing leaf and get its bounded mean, so ŷ ∈ [y_min, y_max] of training.", "whyPool": "A standard regression tree predicts a constant value within each terminal region. For a leaf R௠, the prediction is typically the mean training response among the observations assigned to that leaf: fመ(x) =yത̂೘ = 1 ∣ R௠∣ ෍ ŷ ̂:₅೔∈̂೘ . Even when a future observation has feature values far outside the training feature range, it is routed through the existing splits into one of the existing leaves. Its prediction remains that leaf’s training-sample mean. Therefore, for an ordinary mean-predicting regression tree, ŷnew∈ൣy₅₁₆,train,y₅ୟ୶,train₁. The model can extend an existing terminal prediction into a previously unobserved feature region, but it cannot extrapolate a continuing linear trend to produce response values beyond the observed training-response range. 171 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "CART regression: axis-aligned splits, greedy SSE, variance, no extrapolation — ISLR §8.1; ESL ch.9 (CART).", "pdfArtifacts": "௝", "n": 187}, {"id": "ML-D6G1Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative analyst is constructing a Decision Tree Classifier to classify short-term market environments as either “High Liquidity” (y= 1) or “Low Liquidity” (y= 0). The training dataset contains 1,000 historical intraday observations and three continuous market-microstructure features: •  X₁: Bid-Ask Spread •  X₂: Order Book Depth •  X₃: Trade Volume The analyst uses the standard CART framework and evaluates candidate splits using Gini impurity. The risk team also compares Gini impurity with cross-entropy and classification error and examines how transformations of the input features affect the resulting tree. Which of the following statements correctly describe node impurity, split selection, and the structural behavior of Decision Tree Classifiers?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Classification error, E௠= 1 − max௞ (p̂௠௞), is generally less sensitive than Gini impurity or cross-entropy to changes in the class proportions within a node, making it less suitable for growing a classification tree.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Classification error E=1−max_k p̂ is piecewise-linear and less sensitive to class-proportion changes → less effective for growing trees (used more for pruning).", "whyPool": "Classification error depends only on the probability of the majority class: E௠= 1 − max௞ (p̂௠௞). In binary classification, this becomes E(p) = min(p,1 −p). The metric does respond to some changes in class proportions, so it is not literally constant whenever the majority class remains unchanged. However, it is piecewise linear and less sensitive than Gini impurity or cross-entropy to changes in the complete class-probability distribution. This reduced sensitivity can make classification error less effective for distinguishing among candidate splits during tree construction. Gini impurity and cross-entropy are therefore commonly used to grow trees, while classification error may still be useful when evaluating or pruning them. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 8.1) 172 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "In a binary node with p̂௠₁=p, both Gini impurity and cross-entropy attain their maximum values at p= 0or p= 1, because completely pure nodes provide the strongest possible evidence for a split.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Gini and entropy are **0** at pure endpoints p=0/1 and **maximal at p=0.5** — they do not attain maxima at the pure endpoints.", "whyPool": "For a binary node, Gini impurity is G(p) =p(1 −p)+(1 −p)p= 2p(1 −p), and cross-entropy is H(p) = −pln(p)−(1 −p)ln(1 −p). Both measures equal zero at the pure-node endpoints p= 0and p= 1. They reach their maximum values at p= 0.5, where the two classes are equally represented and uncertainty is greatest. Thus, pure nodes minimize impurity; balanced, mixed-class nodes maximize it."}, {"L": "C", "text": "When a binary node is completely pure, both its Gini impurity and cross-entropy equal zero.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At a pure node both Gini (2·1·0) and entropy (−1·ln1−0·ln0, with 0ln0=0) equal 0.", "whyPool": "At a pure node, all observations belong to the same class. If p= 1, then G(1) = 2(1)(0) = 0, and H(1) = −1ln(1)− 0ln(0) = 0. The same result holds at p= 0. In the entropy expression, the conventional limiting definition 0ln(0) = 0 is used. Both metrics therefore assign zero impurity to a completely pure node."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Applying a strictly monotonic transformation to an individual continuous feature changes its numerical scale and reported split thresholds, but ordinarily preserves the candidate partitions of the training observations and therefore need not change the fitted tree’s structure or predictions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A strictly monotonic transform changes reported thresholds but preserves observation ordering → same candidate partitions → same fitted tree (barring ties/precision exceptions).", "whyPool": "CART evaluates candidate splits according to the partition they create, such as X௝ ≤s versus X௝ >s. A strictly increasing transformation preserves the ordering of the observations. A strictly decreasing transformation reverses the ordering but still permits the corresponding complementary partitions. The numerical threshold changes, but the same groups of training observations can ordinarily be separated. For example, if X₁ > 0, a split based on X₁ ≤shas a corresponding split on ln(X₁)at ln(s). Consequently, strictly monotonic transformations ordinarily preserve the available partitions and fitted predictions. This invariance may fail when a transformation is not strictly monotonic, changes or creates ties, is undefined for some observations, or interacts with finite numerical precision or implementation-specific behavior. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 8.1)"}, {"L": "E", "text": "For Gini impurity or cross-entropy, the weighted average impurity of the two child nodes created by any candidate binary split cannot exceed the impurity of the parent node, although the reduction may equal zero.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "By concavity, weighted child impurity ≤ parent impurity, so impurity reduction ≥ 0 (can equal 0).", "whyPool": "Let a parent node contain class-probability vector p, and suppose a candidate split produces child nodes with weights ŵand ŵand probability vectors p̂and p̂. Then p=ŵp̂ +ŵp̂. Because both Gini impurity and cross-entropy are concave functions of the class probabilities, I(p) ≥ŵI(p̂)+ŵI(p̂). Therefore, the weighted child impurity cannot exceed the parent impurity. The impurity reduction, ΔI =I(p)−[ŵI(p̂)+ŵI(p̂)], 173 Rev:081020261230 is nonnegative. Equality is possible—for example, when both children have the same class proportions as the parent—so a candidate partition does not necessarily provide a strictly positive improvement. In practice, CART chooses the admissible split with the greatest reduction and may decline to split when the best improvement is zero or fails a minimum-improvement or stopping requirement. G2: Splitting Criteria Intuition"}], "reference": "Gini/entropy/classification-error impurity & concavity — ISLR §8.1; ESL §9.2.3.", "pdfArtifacts": "௞௠", "n": 188}, {"id": "ML-D6G2Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A classification tree is considering two root splits for predicting whether a stock will outperform. Split A creates child nodes that are each strongly dominated by one class. Split B creates two child nodes with nearly equal mixtures of outperformers and underperformers. The split criterion rewards reductions in class impurity.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Split A is likely to produce a larger impurity reduction than Split B.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Separating classes produces purer children and a larger impurity decrease than a near-even split.", "whyPool": "Separating the classes produces purer children and usually a larger impurity decrease."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A pure child node contains observations from only one target class.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A pure child contains observations of only one class.", "whyPool": "A node is pure when all of its training observations have the same class label."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The feature with the largest numerical scale must be selected for the split.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Splits are compared by criterion value, not by raw feature scale.", "whyPool": "Candidate splits are compared by their criterion values, not by raw feature scale."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The target labels are used when evaluating candidate splits during supervised tree training.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Supervised splitting uses the known target labels to measure separation quality.", "whyPool": "Supervised splitting measures how well feature thresholds separate known targets."}, {"L": "E", "text": "An impurity reduction at the root guarantees minimum possible test error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A locally useful training split does not guarantee minimum test error / global optimality.", "whyPool": "A locally useful training split does not guarantee globally optimal structure or test performance."}], "reference": "Impurity-reduction split selection — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn \"Mathematical formulation\" of tree splitting.", "n": 189}, {"id": "ML-D6G2Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A regression tree predicts daily returns. At one node, it compares candidate thresholds on momentum and volatility. For each candidate, it evaluates how much the split reduces squared prediction error within the resulting child nodes.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A desirable split tends to group observations with similar target values into the same child.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A good split groups similar-target observations together, reducing residual variance/squared error.", "whyPool": "Similar targets within children reduce residual variation and squared error."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Candidate thresholds can be evaluated using the target values of observations at that node. 174 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Candidate thresholds are evaluated using the target values of the node's observations.", "whyPool": "Regression tree criteria compare how candidate partitions organize the training targets."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The chosen split must make both child nodes contain the same number of observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Balanced child sizes are not required unless separately constrained.", "whyPool": "Balanced child sizes are not generally required unless separately constrained."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A large reduction in training error at the current node can motivate selecting that split.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Greedy growth selects the candidate with the best local error reduction.", "whyPool": "Greedy tree growth selects the candidate with the best local criterion improvement."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The method proves that no different sequence of future splits could produce a better full tree.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A greedy local choice does not prove global optimality of the completed tree.", "whyPool": "A greedy local choice does not establish global optimality of the completed tree."}], "reference": "Regression-tree variance-reduction splitting / greedy growth — ISLR §8.1.", "n": 190}, {"id": "ML-D6G2Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "At the root of a trading decision tree, a split on volatility yields the largest immediate error reduction, so the algorithm chooses it. An analyst later discovers that a momentum split with a smaller immediate improvement would have enabled more effective splits at lower levels.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This outcome is consistent with greedy tree construction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Choosing the best available split now is exactly greedy construction.", "whyPool": "Greedy construction selects the best available split at the current step."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Standard greedy training normally revisits every earlier split after seeing the completed tree and guarantees the globally best arrangement.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Standard growth does not revisit earlier splits or exhaustively optimize all complete trees.", "whyPool": "Ordinary tree growth does not exhaustively optimize all possible complete trees."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The locally best split can lead to a suboptimal overall tree.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A locally best split can yield a suboptimal overall tree.", "whyPool": "A local optimum need not be part of the global optimum."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The existence of a better multi-step structure means the original volatility split had no immediate predictive value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The volatility split may still have had the largest immediate reduction — just not the best long-run structure.", "whyPool": "It may still have provided the largest immediate reduction, just not the best long-run structure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Validation can help assess whether the resulting tree generalizes despite the greedy construction process.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Out-of-sample validation assesses whether the greedily-built tree generalizes.", "whyPool": "Out-of-sample validation evaluates the practical consequences of the learned structure. 175 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Greedy recursive binary splitting & suboptimality — ISLR §8.1; ESL §9.2.", "n": 191}, {"id": "ML-D6G2Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Machine Learning Researcher at a quantitative hedge fund developing a decision tree model to classify stocks as potential outperformers or underperformers. The Portfolio Manager notices that the tree keeps selecting splits that reduce prediction errors in the current dataset and asks whether these decisions represent a globally optimized investment strategy. You are asked to explain how decision trees choose splits and what limitations arise from their splitting approach.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Decision trees typically choose splits using a greedy approach that selects the best available split at each step.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Trees use a greedy algorithm selecting the best split at each stage without checking all future combinations.", "whyPool": "Decision trees use a greedy algorithm that selects the best split at the current stage without considering all possible future split combinations."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A split that improves the current node’s prediction quality is always guaranteed to produce the globally optimal final decision tree.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A locally optimal split does not guarantee a globally optimal tree.", "whyPool": "A locally optimal split does not guarantee a globally optimal tree because future splits and interactions among decisions are not fully considered during the greedy construction process."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Decision tree splitting criteria aim to create child nodes that are more homogeneous or have lower prediction error than the original node.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Splitting criteria aim to make children more homogeneous / lower error than the parent.", "whyPool": "Splitting criteria are designed to create groups where observations are more similar, reducing measures such as classification impurity or regression error within each resulting node."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Decision trees evaluate every possible combination of future splits before selecting the first split at the root node.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees do not enumerate all future split combinations (computationally infeasible) — they build recursively/greedily.", "whyPool": "Decision trees do not search all possible combinations of splits because that would be computationally expensive. Instead, they build trees recursively using greedy split selection."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The quality of a split can be evaluated by how much it reduces impurity or prediction error in the resulting groups.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Split quality is judged by impurity/error reduction in the resulting groups.", "whyPool": "A good split is one that improves the quality of the resulting nodes by reducing impurity for classification or reducing error for regression."}], "reference": "Greedy split selection — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn tree \"Mathematical formulation.\"", "n": 192}, {"id": "ML-D6G2Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Researcher at a hedge fund responsible for validating a decision tree that predicts whether corporate bonds should be included in a portfolio. The research team reports that the tree selected a credit-quality split because it produced the “cleanest” separation between profitable and unprofitable investments. The Portfolio Manager asks whether the tree has discovered the true drivers of returns or whether the split-selection process has limitations that should be considered before deployment. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate. 176 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A decision tree split is selected based on how well it improves the chosen objective at the current node, not necessarily because the feature is the most important factor in the entire dataset.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a split is chosen for immediate objective improvement at the node, not because the feature is the dataset's most important factor.", "whyPool": "Decision trees choose splits based on immediate improvement at the current node, such as reducing impurity or error. A top-level split is useful for prediction but does not necessarily represent the true underlying economic cause."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A decision tree examines every possible sequence of future splits before selecting the best split at the current node.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: trees do **not** examine every possible future split sequence; they use greedy recursion (exhaustive search is infeasible).", "whyPool": "Decision trees do not evaluate every possible future sequence of splits. They use a greedy recursive approach because exhaustive optimization over all possible trees is computationally infeasible."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A feature chosen near the top of a decision tree is always the strongest economic driver of the target variable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a near-root feature is highly predictive for the model but not guaranteed the strongest economic driver (correlation/interactions/greedy placement).", "whyPool": "A feature appearing near the root may be highly predictive for the model, but it is not guaranteed to be the most important economic driver. Correlation, interactions, and the greedy construction process can affect feature placement."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A decision tree may select a split that provides strong immediate improvement but prevents the discovery of a better alternative tree structure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a strong immediate split can foreclose a better overall structure (greedy limitation).", "whyPool": "Since each split is selected using local information, a decision tree can make an early choice that limits later possibilities and prevents a potentially better overall structure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The splitting process in decision trees is an example of global optimization because each decision accounts for all possible future outcomes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: tree construction is greedy/local, not global optimization accounting for all future outcomes.", "whyPool": "Decision tree construction is not global optimization. It is a greedy algorithm that makes locally optimal decisions at each step without considering every possible future outcome."}], "reference": "Greedy vs. global optimization; feature importance caveats — ISLR §8.1; ESL §9.2.", "n": 193}, {"id": "ML-D6G2Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A junior quantitative analyst is learning how decision trees partition data to predict daily stock market movements (\"Up\" vs. \"Down\"). The analyst wants to understand how the CART (Classification and Regression Trees) algorithm decides where to place its splits when building a model from scratch. The supervisor explains that the algorithm uses a top-down, \"greedy\" approach to evaluate features and numerical split points, using metrics like Gini Impurity for classification or Mean Squared Error (MSE) for regression. Which of the following statements correctly describes how decision tree splitting criteria and optimization work? For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "At every node, the algorithm checks all available features and possible split values to choose the single split that provides the greatest immediate reduction in impurity or error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a local exhaustive search scans every feature and threshold to pick the max immediate impurity/error reduction.", "whyPool": "Decision trees perform a local exhaustive search at the current node, scanning every feature and every split threshold to pick the split that maximizes immediate error or impurity reduction."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The term \"greedy\" means that the algorithm looks ahead 3 to 4 steps into the future to ensure that a split made today leads to the best overall tree structure at the end.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: \"greedy\" means best choice now with **no** lookahead — it does not look 3–4 steps ahead.", "whyPool": "\"Greedy\" in machine learning means the algorithm makes the best choice at the current step without looking ahead to future steps or considering the long-term global outcome."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Decision trees evaluate splits globally, meaning that changing a split point in the left branch of a tree will automatically recalculate and adjust all split points in the right branch. 177 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: splits are local to their branch; changing the left branch does not auto-recalculate the right branch.", "whyPool": "Splits in a decision tree are local to their respective branches. Once a dataset is split into a left child and a right child, decisions made within the left child operate independently of the data points in the right child. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 8.1)"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Gini Impurity reaches its lowest value of 0.0 0.0 when a node contains a perfectly pure mix of 50% \"Up\" days and 50% \"Down\" days.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: Gini's **minimum 0.0** occurs at a **pure** node; a 50/50 mix is maximum impurity (Gini=0.5), not \"perfectly pure.\"", "whyPool": "A 50/50 mix represents maximum uncertainty and maximum impurity (Gini Impurity = 0.5). The minimum Gini Impurity (0.0) occurs when a node is 100% pure (contains only one class)."}, {"L": "E", "text": "When building a decision tree classifier, a node that contains only instances of a single class (100% \"Up\" days) has a Gini Impurity of zero.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a single-class (100%) node is pure → Gini = 0.", "whyPool": "Gini Impurity measures class heterogeneity. When all samples in a node belong to the exact same class, the node is completely pure and its impurity is 0.0. G3: Model Complexity and Overfitting"}], "reference": "Greedy local search & Gini extremes — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn tree criteria.", "n": 194}, {"id": "ML-D6G3Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A decision tree is allowed to grow until every training observation occupies its own leaf. It achieves zero training error, but its test error is substantially higher and its predictions change sharply across nearby feature values.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The tree shows evidence of overfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Very low training error with much worse test error is the classic overfitting signature.", "whyPool": "Very low training error paired with much worse test performance is a classic overfitting pattern."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Pure or singleton training leaves guarantee accurate predictions on unseen data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Pure/singleton training leaves do not guarantee accurate unseen predictions.", "whyPool": "Purity on training data does not guarantee that learned distinctions generalize. 178 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "The highly detailed tree may be fitting noise or idiosyncrasies in the training sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Unrestricted growth can encode sample-specific noise rather than stable structure.", "whyPool": "Unrestricted growth can encode sample-specific fluctuations rather than stable structure."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Restricting tree growth could improve generalization even if training error increases.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Restricting growth can improve generalization even if training error rises.", "whyPool": "A simpler tree may trade some training fit for lower variance and better unseen performance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Zero training error is sufficient evidence that the model has low variance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Zero training error often coexists with **high** variance, not low.", "whyPool": "Highly flexible trees often have high variance despite their low training error."}], "reference": "Overfitting / capacity & bias–variance — ISLR §8.1 (tree pruning); ESL ch.7.", "n": 195}, {"id": "ML-D6G3Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A shallow tree predicts whether a portfolio will exceed its benchmark. Both its training and validation accuracy are poor, and inspection shows that it cannot represent an interaction in which momentum matters only during low-volatility periods.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The tree may be underfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Poor performance even on training data indicates possible underfitting (insufficient capacity).", "whyPool": "Poor performance even on training data can indicate insufficient model capacity."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Adding enough permitted depth could allow the tree to represent the conditional interaction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "More permitted depth can express the sequential volatility→momentum conditional interaction.", "whyPool": "Additional levels can express sequential conditions involving volatility and momentum."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Poor training performance necessarily proves that the dataset contains no predictive structure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Poor training performance can reflect an over-constrained learner, not absence of predictive structure.", "whyPool": "The learner may simply be too constrained to capture available structure."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A shallow tree generally has lower capacity than a deep tree using the same features.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A shallow tree has lower capacity than a deeper tree on the same features.", "whyPool": "Fewer allowed splits limit the number and complexity of representable regions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Making the tree even shallower is guaranteed to correct the problem.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Making it even shallower reduces capacity further and worsens this underfitting.", "whyPool": "Further reducing capacity would usually worsen this form of underfitting."}], "reference": "Underfitting / capacity & interactions — ISLR §8.1; ESL ch.7 (bias–variance).", "n": 196}, {"id": "ML-D6G3Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A team evaluates decision trees with maximum depths from 1 through 30. Training error falls throughout the range. Validation error falls until depth 7 and then rises steadily, while the depth-30 tree has many leaves containing only one or two observations. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The falling training error alone does not identify the depth that best generalizes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: falling training error alone doesn't identify the best-generalizing depth.", "whyPool": "Greater complexity often improves in-sample fit without improving unseen performance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The validation pattern suggests that trees deeper than about 7 increasingly overfit.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: rising validation error past depth 7 indicates increasing overfitting.", "whyPool": "Rising validation error after depth 7 indicates deteriorating generalization."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Small leaves can make predictions depend heavily on a few training observations. 179 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: tiny leaves make predictions depend heavily on a few observations.", "whyPool": "Estimates based on very few cases are sensitive to their individual target values."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Depth 30 must be selected because it has the lowest training error.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: depth should be chosen on validation performance, not lowest training error.", "whyPool": "Hyperparameters should reflect validation performance, not training error alone."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The final test set should remain separate from the depth-selection process.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the final test set must stay separate from depth selection for an unbiased estimate.", "whyPool": "Reserving the test set preserves an unbiased final evaluation."}], "reference": "Validation-based complexity selection; small-leaf variance — ISLR §8.1 & ch.5 (CV); ESL ch.7.", "n": 197}, {"id": "ML-D6G3Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative analyst builds an automated regime-classification tree to predict equity market volatility regimes (\"High Volatility\" vs. \"Low Volatility\") using intraday order-flow features. The analyst leaves all growth constraints disabled (max_depth = None, min_samples_split = 2, min_samples_leaf = 1). The resulting tree grows to a depth of 28 levels until every leaf node is completely pure (100% single-class instances). When evaluated, the model achieves 100% accuracy (0.0% training error) on historical data, but its out-of-sample prediction performance on live market data degrades sharply. The risk team conducts a diagnostic review of model complexity, structural depth, and variance behavior in unrestricted decision trees. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates how unrestricted tree growth drives overfitting, structural complexity, and model instability?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Allowing a decision tree to grow without depth or leaf constraints guarantees low out-of-sample generalization error because deep trees capture all fine-grained, high-order feature interactions present in the data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Deep unrestricted trees fit training noise → severe overfitting and poor generalization; they do **not** guarantee low OOS error.", "whyPool": "Deep, unrestricted decision trees fit the training noise rather than the underlying signal, leading to severe overfitting and poor out-of-sample generalization."}, {"L": "B", "text": "An unrestricted decision tree will continue splitting nodes until all leaves are pure or contain fewer samples than min_samples_split, which effectively memorizes noisy training instances and creates fragmented, overfitted decision boundaries.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Without constraints, trees split until leaves are pure / below min_samples_split, memorizing noisy instances → fragmented boundaries.", "whyPool": "Without constraints, trees expand recursively until terminal leaves achieve maximum purity (100%). In noisy financial environments, this forces the model to create isolated spatial pockets around noisy outlier points, resulting in highly overfitted decision boundaries."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Deep, unrestricted decision trees exhibit high bias and low variance because the vast number of terminal nodes averages out estimation errors across the feature space.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Deep unrestricted trees have **low bias, high variance** — not high bias/low variance.", "whyPool": "Unrestricted decision trees have low bias (they fit training data almost perfectly) but high variance (small perturbations in the training data cause drastic shifts in tree structure and predictions)."}, {"L": "D", "text": "In noisy financial datasets, deep decision trees fit small, random market fluctuations near the bottom leaves, causing the model to interpret transient noise as true structural signals.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "In low signal-to-noise financial data, deep leaves isolate tiny subsets and treat noise as signal.", "whyPool": "Financial time-series data contains a low signal-to-noise ratio. Deep nodes isolate tiny subsets of historical data, treating random noise ticks as predictive rules and eroding test performance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A fully grown tree with K leaf nodes partitions the feature space into K distinct, non-overlapping hyper-rectangular regions, where each region assigns predictions based on a tiny handful of historical samples.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A K-leaf tree partitions the space into K non-overlapping axis-aligned hyper-rectangles, each predicting from few samples.", "whyPool": "Every split in a decision tree creates orthogonal cuts. An unconstrained tree with K leaves constructs K high- dimensional hyper-rectangles, predicting target values from tiny, localized clusters of data points."}], "reference": "Unrestricted growth → high variance; hyper-rectangular partitions — ISLR §8.1; ESL §9.2.", "n": 198}, {"id": "ML-D6G3Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative portfolio manager fits a Decision Tree Regressor to predict daily excess stock returns using 15 macroeconomic and technical features. The training sample contains 500 daily observations. The tree is grown without restrictions on maximum depth, minimum leaf size, or minimum impurity reduction. It continues splitting until nearly every terminal node contains only one training observation. The resulting performance differs sharply across datasets: •  Training set: MSEtrain= 0.000and Rtrain₂ = 1.000 •  Test set: MSE is substantially worse than that of a baseline model that always predicts the historical mean return, yതtrain The risk committee examines the tree’s capacity, geometric partitions, bias-variance behavior, and sensitivity to sample- specific noise. Which of the following statements correctly describe how unrestricted growth affects the complexity and prediction behavior of this regression tree?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Allowing the tree to grow deeper generally increases its flexibility and reduces its training bias, but it can also increase variance by making the fitted structure more sensitive to the particular observations and noise in the training sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Deeper trees reduce training bias but raise variance (more sensitive to the particular sample/noise).", "whyPool": "Tree depth is one measure of model flexibility. A shallow tree divides the feature space into relatively few regions and imposes a coarse approximation on the response function. A deeper tree creates more regions and can adapt more closely to the training observations. This increased flexibility generally reduces training bias and training error. However, it also makes the fitted model more dependent on the exact composition of the training sample. A small change in the observations can alter selected features or thresholds and propagate through downstream branches. Deep, minimally constrained trees therefore tend to have low training bias but high variance. It would be too strong to claim that bias must become exactly zero or variance must literally “explode” whenever another level is added. The practical point is that unrestricted growth commonly shifts the model toward lower bias and higher variance, potentially worsening test performance. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 8.1)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Repeated axis-aligned splits divide the feature space into progressively smaller hyperrectangular regions. When terminal nodes contain individual observations, the tree can effectively memorize the training responses.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Axis-aligned splits shrink regions; single-observation leaves let the tree memorize exact responses.", "whyPool": "A standard CART split has the form X௝ ≤s versus X௝ >s. Each split divides an existing region along one feature axis. Repeating this process partitions the feature space into axis- aligned hyperrectangular regions. For a regression tree, the prediction within a terminal region R௠is typically the mean response of the training observations assigned to that region: fመ(x) =yത̂೘. If R௠contains only one training observation i, then fመ(x̂) =ŷ. Thus, when distinct training observations can be isolated into single-observation leaves, the tree can reproduce their responses exactly. This explains the perfect training fit without implying that the fitted partitions capture stable market structure."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The tree’s zero training error demonstrates that daily excess returns are generated by a deterministic nonlinear process that can be recovered exactly from the 15 features.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Zero training error shows only interpolation capacity, not that returns are a deterministic noise-free function of the 15 features.", "whyPool": "Zero training error shows that the tree has enough capacity to interpolate or memorize the observed training sample. It does not establish that the underlying return-generating process is deterministic, noise-free, or completely represented by the available features. An unconstrained tree may fit both genuine relationships and observation-specific noise. The poor test performance— especially relative to the historical-mean baseline—is evidence that the learned partitions do not generalize well to unseen observations."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Increasing the training-sample size automatically limits an unconstrained tree’s maximum depth to log₂(p), where pis the number of features, thereby preventing overfitting without pruning or other stopping rules.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "p features impose **no** log2(p) depth cap — a tree can split repeatedly on the same feature; depth relates to N/arrangement (log2N balanced up to N−1 unbalanced).", "whyPool": "The number of features pdoes not impose a log₂(p)limit on tree depth. A tree may split repeatedly on the same feature using different thresholds, so its depth is not capped by the number of available features. Depth is more directly related to the number and arrangement of the training observations and the selected splits. A balanced binary tree with approximately one observation per leaf may have depth on the order of log₂(N), whereas a highly unbalanced tree may have depth approaching N− 1. 182 Rev:081020261230 Adding more observations can therefore permit a larger and more complex unconstrained tree. Preventing excessive growth requires explicit controls such as maximum depth, minimum leaf size, minimum impurity reduction, cost- complexity pruning, or validation-based model selection."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Splits made deep in the tree ordinarily distinguish relatively small subsets of the training sample and are therefore more likely than early splits to capture localized, sample-specific variation rather than broad patterns that generalize reliably.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Deep splits act on small subsets → more likely to capture localized, sample-specific variation that fails to generalize.", "whyPool": "Splits near the root act on large portions of the training sample and typically identify relatively broad distinctions. As the tree grows, each node contains fewer observations, and later splits distinguish increasingly localized subsets of the feature space. Because these deep splits are estimated from small samples, they are more susceptible to noise, outliers, and accidental patterns in the training data. They may improve the training fit while failing to represent relationships that recur out of sample. This is a general tendency rather than a rule that every deep split is spurious. Nevertheless, single-observation or very small leaves are particularly likely to reflect sample-specific variation, which helps explain the large gap between the tree’s training and test performance."}], "reference": "Interpolation vs. true process; depth not bounded by p; deep-split variance — ISLR §8.1; ESL ch.7.", "pdfArtifacts": "ത", "n": 199}, {"id": "ML-D6G3Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative research team is developing a machine learning pipeline to classify equity market regimes into three liquidity states (\"High\" , \"Moderate\" , \"Low\") using intraday order-flow and volatility indicators. During model exploration, the team compares two structural configurations of a Decision Tree Classifier trained on 2,500 historical intraday snapshots: Model A (Unconstrained Deep Tree): Allowed to grow fully until all terminal leaves are 100% pure (max_depth = None, min_samples_leaf = 1). * Model B (Constrained Shallow Tree): Restricted to a moderate depth (max_depth = 4, min_samples_leaf = 50). The lead researcher presents a diagnostic comparison of the two models to explain how unrestricted tree growth impacts model capacity, leaf variance, and out-of-sample generalization. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the relationship between tree depth, leaf sample size, and overfitting behavior?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Model A achieves zero training classification error because its unconstrained depth allows it to create isolated spatial partitions around every training sample, effectively memorizing sample-specific noise.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Model A's unconstrained depth isolates partitions around each training sample → 0 training error, memorizing noise.", "whyPool": "An unconstrained tree expands recursively until every terminal leaf is pure or contains fewer samples than min_samples_split. In noisy financial datasets, this creates fine-grained, localized decision regions that fit sample- specific noise, yielding 0% training error but poor generalization."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Model B exhibits lower variance than Model A because forcing terminal leaf nodes to contain at least 50 samples ensures that leaf predictions are calculated by averaging across larger subsets of data, smoothing out noisy fluctuations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "min_samples_leaf=50 averages each leaf over ≥50 points → variance reduction, smoother boundaries.", "whyPool": "Requiring min_samples_leaf = 50 acts as a direct variance-reduction mechanism. Instead of basing a leaf's prediction on 1 or 2 isolated data points, the prediction is backed by a statistically meaningful sample size (N≥50), making the model far less sensitive to individual noise ticks."}, {"L": "C", "text": "As a decision tree grows deeper, the expected bias of its predictions increases while the variance decreases, making deep trees structurally prone to underfitting.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Deep trees show **low bias, high variance** (prone to overfitting), not high bias/low variance/underfitting.", "whyPool": "Deep trees exhibit the exact opposite behavior: low bias (high capacity to fit training relationships) and high variance (extreme sensitivity to small changes in training data)."}, {"L": "D", "text": "In Model A, a slight change in a single training observation near a high-depth split can alter the entire subtree beneath it, demonstrating the high variance inherent in deep, unconstrained trees.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A slight change near a high-depth split can alter the whole subtree beneath it — high variance of deep trees.", "whyPool": "Deep decision trees are notoriously high-variance models. Small perturbations in the training dataset can alter split thresholds or feature choices, causing downstream branches to diverge and changing final predictions for unseen test cases."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Model B is guaranteed to achieve lower training error than Model A because setting max_depth = 4 forces the CART algorithm to find more globally optimal splits near the root of the tree.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Model B is **not** guaranteed lower training error (Model A hits 0); constraints reduce capacity, and CART stays greedy (no more \"global\" root splits).", "whyPool": "Model B is not guaranteed to achieve lower training error. Its depth and leaf-size constraints reduce the set of partitions it can represent, while Model A is explicitly allowed to grow until its terminal leaves are pure. Therefore, in this scenario, Training Error஺ = 0 and Training Error஻ ≥Training Error஺. Restricting the depth also does not cause CART to find more globally optimal root splits. CART remains a greedy algorithm: at each node, it selects the locally best available split without jointly optimizing the entire final tree. Model B may nevertheless achieve lower out-of-sample error because its constraints reduce variance and limit its ability to fit sample-specific noise. G4: Hyperparameters as Control Mechanisms"}], "reference": "Depth/leaf-size vs. bias–variance; greedy still greedy — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn tree hyperparameters.", "n": 200}, {"id": "ML-D6G4Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A regression tree for forecasting returns is changed from unlimited depth to a maximum depth of 4. No other setting changes. The restricted tree has fewer leaves, slightly higher training error, and lower validation error. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Maximum depth directly limits the length of root-to-leaf decision paths.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: max_depth caps the length of root-to-leaf decision paths (split levels).", "whyPool": "The setting caps how many successive split levels the tree may use."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The higher training error is compatible with reduced model capacity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: higher training error is consistent with reduced capacity.", "whyPool": "A constrained tree cannot fit all patterns available to the unrestricted tree."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The lower validation error suggests that the depth restriction improved generalization in this experiment.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: lower validation error suggests the depth cap improved generalization here.", "whyPool": "Performance on held-out data improved after the capacity constraint was imposed."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A maximum depth of 4 guarantees the best result for every financial dataset.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: no single depth is best for every dataset — appropriate complexity is data/task/validation dependent.", "whyPool": "Appropriate complexity depends on the data, task, and validation evidence."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Reducing maximum depth can act as a form of regularization.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: limiting depth reduces flexibility and acts as regularization.", "whyPool": "Limiting depth reduces flexibility and can curb overfitting."}], "reference": "max_depth as pre-pruning/regularization — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn `max_depth`.", "n": 201}, {"id": "ML-D6G4Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A classification tree currently permits leaves with one training observation. The team raises the minimum leaf size to 50 observations. Rare but genuine market regimes contain only 20 examples, while several isolated mislabeled records also exist. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The larger minimum leaf size can prevent singleton leaves caused by isolated records.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a larger minimum leaf size prevents singleton leaves from isolated records.", "whyPool": "Leaves smaller than the new threshold are no longer permitted."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The change may reduce variance by making leaf predictions depend on more observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: aggregating more observations makes leaf estimates less sample-sensitive → lower variance.", "whyPool": "Aggregating more cases generally makes individual leaf estimates less sample-sensitive."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The rare 20-observation regimes may be merged with broader patterns and become harder to detect.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: genuine 20-obs regimes may be merged away and become harder to detect.", "whyPool": "The constraint may suppress real patterns supported by fewer than 50 observations."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Raising minimum leaf size can only reduce bias and can never cause underfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: stronger constraints can **increase bias and cause underfitting**, not only reduce bias.", "whyPool": "Stronger constraints can increase bias and cause underfitting."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Selecting the setting involves a tradeoff between robustness and preservation of local structure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the setting trades off robustness against preservation of local structure.", "whyPool": "The hyperparameter controls both noise sensitivity and the resolution of learned patterns."}], "reference": "min_samples_leaf variance/bias tradeoff — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn `min_samples_leaf`.", "n": 202}, {"id": "ML-D6G4Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher jointly tunes maximum depth and minimum leaf size. One candidate uses depth 20 with leaves as small as 2; another uses depth 6 with leaves of at least 100. The first fits training data closely, while the second produces smoother, broader partitions. 185 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Maximum depth and minimum leaf size both influence effective tree complexity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double-negative: the inner claim (both max_depth and min leaf size influence effective complexity) is accurate, so \"the following claim is false\" is itself false.", "whyPool": "Both settings restrict how detailed the learned partition can become."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The depth-20, leaf-size-2 candidate is generally capable of representing finer partitions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Greater permitted depth and smaller leaves allow finer, more localized partitions.", "whyPool": "Greater permitted depth and smaller leaves allow more localized rules."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The depth-6, leaf-size-100 candidate is guaranteed to have lower test error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The simpler candidate may generalize better *or* underfit — validation is required, not guaranteed lower test error.", "whyPool": "Simpler structure may generalize better or may underfit; validation is required."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Evaluating combinations on validation data is more appropriate than choosing solely from their training errors.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Validation data estimates the generalization tradeoff among candidate settings.", "whyPool": "Validation estimates the generalization tradeoff among candidate settings."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Hyperparameter tuning on the final test set would compromise the test set's role as an unbiased final check.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Tuning on the final test set leaks test information into model selection, compromising its role.", "whyPool": "Repeatedly choosing settings from test results leaks test information into model selection."}], "reference": "Depth & leaf-size as joint complexity controls; validation vs. test — ISLR §8.1 & ch.5; scikit-learn model selection.", "n": 203}, {"id": "ML-D6G4Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A quantitative analyst is tuning a Decision Tree Classifier designed to predict short-term equity directional momentum using 12 technical and macroeconomic features. The model is trained on 10,000 hourly historical bar updates. To prevent the model from memorizing noise while maintaining sufficient capacity to capture genuine non-linear market regimes, the analyst systematically evaluates the structural effects of key tree-growth hyperparameters: max_depth, min_samples_split, min_samples_leaf, and max_leaf_nodes. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates how these hyperparameter controls govern decision tree capacity, bias-variance trade-offs, and structural growth? For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Increasing min_samples_leaf forces the algorithm to halt node partitioning whenever a candidate split would produce a child node with fewer than the specified threshold of training instances, acting as a variance-reduction mechanism.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: increasing min_samples_leaf blocks splits producing children below the threshold → variance reduction.", "whyPool": "min_samples_leaf sets a hard lower bound on the number of samples required to form a terminal node. By preventing the tree from creating leaves backed by only 1 or 2 isolated, potentially noisy data points, it effectively controls model variance and smoothes decision boundaries."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Decreasing max_depth reduces the structural capacity of the tree, which lowers model variance and increases systematic bias by forcing the model to rely on broader, coarser spatial partitions of the feature space.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: decreasing max_depth lowers capacity/variance and raises bias via coarser partitions.", "whyPool": "max_depth limits the maximum number of sequential splits from root to leaf. A shallower tree simplifies the model's spatial decision boundaries, reducing sensitivity to sample noise (lower variance) at the cost of less flexibility to fit complex interactions (higher bias)."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Setting min_samples_split = 100 guarantees that every resulting terminal leaf node in the final decision tree will contain at least 100 training observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: min_samples_split governs eligibility to split (e.g., 100→99/1 children); it does **not** guarantee every leaf has ≥100 obs — only min_samples_leaf does.", "whyPool": "min_samples_split governs whether an internal node is eligible to be split further, whereas min_samples_leaf governs the minimum size of the resulting child nodes. A split on a node with 100 samples could produce child nodes with 99 and 1 samples, yielding a leaf far smaller than 100 unless min_samples_leaf is also set."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Constraining max_leaf_nodes limits the total number of terminal regions in the feature space, allowing the tree to grow asymmetrically by prioritizing the splits that offer the highest global reduction in impurity regardless of uniform depth limits.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: max_leaf_nodes grows best-first by impurity reduction → asymmetric tree under a fixed leaf budget.", "whyPool": "While max_depth imposes a uniform horizontal depth cap across all branches, max_leaf_nodes grows the tree in a best-first (impurity-reduction) manner. It selectively expands whichever branch offers the highest immediate gain, resulting in an asymmetrical tree optimized for maximum overall error reduction under a fixed region budget."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Unlike L₁and L₂regularization, which add penalty terms to a model’s optimization objective, controls such as max_depth and min_samples_leaf constrain which tree structures CART is permitted to construct without changing the impurity criterion used to evaluate admissible splits. 186 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: max_depth/min_samples_leaf are structural pre-pruning constraints that keep the same impurity criterion, unlike L1/L2 penalty terms.", "whyPool": "Parameters such as max_depth and min_samples_leaf are structural constraints or pre-pruning rules. They reduce model flexibility by limiting the depth of the tree or prohibiting leaves containing too few observations. However, CART still evaluates permitted splits using the same underlying impurity criterion, such as Gini impurity, cross-entropy, or squared error. This differs from L₁and L₂regularization, which modify an optimization objective by adding a coefficient penalty: Objective=Loss+λΩ(β). Thus, the statement is accurate and is marked False under the question’s reversed scoring convention. Cost-complexity pruning is a related but distinct tree-regularization method that explicitly evaluates a penalized objective involving both fit and tree size."}], "reference": "Structural constraints vs. penalty regularization — ISLR §8.1 (pruning); scikit-learn tree hyperparameters.", "n": 204}, {"id": "ML-D6G4Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Machine Learning Engineer at a quantitative hedge fund improving a decision tree used to select stocks for a market-neutral portfolio. The Research Director notices that one version of the tree has grown hundreds of branches and perfectly classifies historical training data, but the model performs inconsistently on new market periods. The Model Risk Officer asks your team how tree complexity controls can improve reliability without eliminating useful predictive patterns.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Increasing the maximum depth of a decision tree generally allows the model to capture more complex relationships in the training data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Deeper trees add splits and capture more detailed patterns/interactions (but can fit noise).", "whyPool": "Deeper trees contain more splits and can represent more detailed patterns and interactions among variables. However, excessive depth can allow the model to fit noise in the training data."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Limiting tree depth can reduce overfitting by preventing the model from creating overly specific rules based on small subsets of observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Limiting depth prevents overly specific rules from small subsets → less overfitting.", "whyPool": "Restricting tree depth prevents the model from creating highly specific rules that apply only to small groups of training observations, reducing the risk of overfitting. ("}, {"L": "C", "text": "Increasing the minimum number of observations required in a leaf usually makes a decision tree more flexible and increases variance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Increasing minimum leaf size makes the tree **less** flexible and lowers variance, not more flexible.", "whyPool": "Increasing the minimum leaf size generally makes a tree less complex by requiring each terminal node to contain more observations. This often reduces variance and can improve generalization."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A decision tree with unlimited depth will always generalize better because it can learn more detailed patterns from the data. 187 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Unlimited depth can memorize training data and generalize poorly — more complexity ≠ better generalization.", "whyPool": "An unrestricted tree can memorize training examples and achieve very low training error while performing poorly on unseen data. Greater complexity does not guarantee better generalization."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Hyperparameters such as maximum depth and minimum leaf size control the tradeoff between model complexity and generalization.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "max_depth/min leaf size/pruning control the complexity–generalization tradeoff.", "whyPool": "Hyperparameters such as maximum depth, minimum leaf size, and pruning choices determine how complex the tree can become and help balance fitting the data against generalizing to new observations.)"}], "reference": "Complexity controls & generalization — ISLR §8.1 (pruning); scikit-learn tree tuning.", "n": 205}, {"id": "ML-D6G4Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A risk management quantitative analyst is auditing an automated trade-execution model that utilizes a Decision Tree Regressor to estimate market impact (in basis points) for large institutional orders. The training set consists of 50,000 historical order execution records across 20 microstructure features. During a hyperparameter grid search, the team compares four distinct tuning configurations: Config 1: max_depth = 3, min_samples_split = 2, min_samples_leaf = 1 * Config 2: max_depth = None, min_samples_split = 500, min_samples_leaf = 250 * Config 3: max_depth = None, min_samples_split = 10, min_samples_leaf = 1 * Config 4: max_leaf_nodes = 16 The team evaluates how these specific hyperparameter settings alter the tree's geometric structure, computational efficiency, and bias-variance profile. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the architectural and statistical behavior driven by these decision tree hyperparameter configurations? For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Config 1 limits every root-to-leaf path to at most three splits and therefore permits no more than 2₃ = 8terminal leaves. It does not require the tree to be complete or symmetric; some branches may stop before reaching the maximum depth.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: max_depth=3 caps paths at 3 splits → ≤23=8 leaves; it is an upper bound, so branches may stop early / be asymmetric.", "whyPool": "The statement is accurate. Setting max_depth = 3 limits the longest root-to-leaf path to three splits, so a binary tree can contain at most 2₃ = 8 terminal leaves. However, max_depth establishes only an upper bound: CART may stop a branch earlier when no admissible split improves the objective or another stopping condition applies. The resulting tree can therefore be asymmetric and contain fewer than eight leaves. This depth restriction reduces the model’s structural capacity, which generally lowers variance and improves interpretability while potentially increasing bias when market impact depends on complex interactions or highly localized regimes."}, {"L": "B", "text": "In Config 2, setting min_samples_leaf = 250 ensures that every leaf node prediction is an average computed over at least 250 historical orders, which significantly stabilizes the leaf-node variance compared to unconstrained trees.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: min_samples_leaf=250 averages each leaf over ≥250 orders → stabilizes leaf variance.", "whyPool": "min_samples_leaf = 250 acts as a direct lower bound on leaf sample size. Bounding the minimum sample size prevents the tree from forming isolated leaves around single outlier trades, smoothing out noise and lowering model variance."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Config 3 effectively prevents severe overfitting because setting min_samples_split = 10 forces every leaf node to contain at least 10 training instances. 188 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: min_samples_split=10 governs split eligibility (can leave a child of 1); it does **not** force ≥10 per leaf — that needs min_samples_leaf.", "whyPool": "min_samples_split = 10 dictates the minimum number of samples required to attempt a split on an internal node. A split on an internal node with 10 samples could split into child nodes of sizes 9 and 1, leaving a leaf with only 1 instance. min_samples_leaf is required to guarantee minimum leaf size."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Config 4 uses best-first node expansion (expanding nodes that offer the maximum reduction in impurity) to build an asymmetric tree capped at 16 leaves, offering a more flexible allocation of model capacity than a strict max_depth uniform restriction.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: max_leaf_nodes uses best-first impurity-reduction growth → asymmetric, flexible capacity allocation.", "whyPool": "max_leaf_nodes grows trees greedily based on relative impurity reduction rather than depth level. This allows deep splits along highly informative feature branches while keeping uninformative branches shallow, optimizing leaf budget allocation. ("}, {"L": "E", "text": "Increasing min_samples_leaf systematically increases training error (higher bias) while lowering out-of-sample prediction variance on noisy, unseen order-flow data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: larger min_samples_leaf raises training error (bias) while lowering OOS variance.", "whyPool": "Constraining tree capacity by requiring larger sample sizes per leaf prevents the model from fitting fine-grained sample noise, which increases training error (bias) but stabilizes out-of-sample performance (variance). G5: Stability and Sensitivity"}], "reference": "Hyperparameter semantics (depth, leaf, split, leaf_nodes) — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn tree parameters.", "withdrawn": true, "n": 206}, {"id": "ML-D6G5Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two decision trees are trained with identical settings on nearly identical datasets. The second dataset differs only because five observations were removed. The trees choose different root features and produce noticeably different predictions for some market states.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This behavior illustrates the instability of individual decision trees.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Large structural change from a small sample change is model instability.", "whyPool": "Substantial structural change from a small sample change is model instability."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Small data changes can alter an early split and thereby change many downstream nodes. 189 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "An altered upper-level split changes the data reaching later splits (cascade).", "whyPool": "An altered upper-level split changes the data available to later splits."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Identical hyperparameters guarantee identical trees even when the training samples differ.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Identical hyperparameters don't guarantee identical trees — structure depends on the particular sample.", "whyPool": "The learned structure also depends on the particular training observations."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Different tree structures can still make the same prediction for some observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Different structures can still coincide on predictions in some regions.", "whyPool": "Distinct paths or partitions may nevertheless assign equal outputs in some regions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Stability concerns are irrelevant if both trees achieve low training error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Low training error doesn't establish reliable predictions across samples / unseen data.", "whyPool": "Low training error does not show that predictions are reliable across samples or on unseen data."}], "reference": "Tree instability / high variance — ISLR §8.2 (bagging motivation); ESL §9.2.", "n": 207}, {"id": "ML-D6G5Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "At one node, splits on momentum and volatility yield almost identical impurity reductions. Adding one newly observed day causes the volatility split to edge ahead, replacing momentum at that node and changing several lower branches.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Near-ties among candidate splits can make tree structure sensitive to small data changes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Near-ties make structure sensitive: a small perturbation can reverse near-equal criterion values.", "whyPool": "A small perturbation can reverse the order of nearly equal criterion values."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Replacing one split can change which observations reach descendant nodes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Replacing one split changes which observations reach descendant nodes.", "whyPool": "Descendant nodes are trained on the cases routed to them by their ancestors."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The new structure proves that volatility is permanently and universally more important than momentum.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The choice is sample/criterion dependent, especially in a near-tie — not proof volatility is universally more important.", "whyPool": "The choice is sample- and criterion-dependent, especially in a near-tie."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Predictions far from the affected regions may remain unchanged.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Predictions far from affected regions can remain unchanged.", "whyPool": "Some observations can continue to reach leaves with the same predictions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A deterministic training algorithm must return the same tree for all possible training samples.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Determinism fixes output for a *given* dataset, not across different datasets.", "whyPool": "Determinism fixes the result for a given input dataset, not across different datasets. 190 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Near-tie sensitivity / instability — ISLR §8.2; ESL §9.2.", "n": 208}, {"id": "ML-D6G5Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio team retrains a single decision tree each month. Feature importance rankings and trade signals fluctuate considerably even though aggregate validation accuracy remains similar. The team is deciding how to assess reliability before deployment.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Similar aggregate accuracy does not guarantee that individual predictions are stable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Similar aggregate accuracy doesn't guarantee stable individual predictions.", "whyPool": "Models can have similar average performance while disagreeing on particular cases."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Comparing outputs across resampled or rolling training sets can reveal sensitivity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing outputs across resampled/rolling training sets reveals sensitivity.", "whyPool": "Repeated training under plausible data perturbations provides evidence about variability."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A stable average score proves that the same features and paths are used every month.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Equal summary scores can come from substantially different structures/decisions.", "whyPool": "Equal summary scores can arise from substantially different structures and decisions."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Operational risk can arise when small sample changes cause large changes in positions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Unstable signals create turnover, shifting exposures, unreliable behavior — operational risk.", "whyPool": "Unstable signals can create turnover, changing exposures, and unreliable behavior."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The team should ignore instability because prediction consistency never matters in trading.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Prediction consistency matters for risk, costs, trust, and deployment controls.", "whyPool": "Consistency can matter for risk, transaction costs, trust, and deployment controls."}], "reference": "Stability assessment / resampling — ISLR §8.2 (bagging); ESL ch.7–8.", "n": 209}, {"id": "ML-D6G5Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Research Lead at a hedge fund reviewing a decision tree used to classify trading opportunities. Two analysts independently retrain the model using nearly identical historical datasets, differing only by a handful of observations from recent market activity. Surprisingly, the resulting trees have noticeably different structures and occasionally make different predictions. The Chief Risk Officer asks whether this behavior is expected and what it implies about the reliability of decision trees.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Small changes in the training data can produce different decision tree structures because tree construction is sensitive to the available observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Small data changes can alter the optimal-looking split → different structure (trees are unstable).", "whyPool": "Decision trees are known to be unstable learners. Small changes in the training data can alter which split appears optimal, resulting in a different tree structure."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If two decision trees are trained on nearly identical datasets, they are guaranteed to produce identical sequences of splits.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Nearly identical datasets are **not** guaranteed to produce identical split sequences.", "whyPool": "Even minor differences in the training data can change impurity calculations and split choices, leading to different trees. Identical structures are not guaranteed."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The instability of a single decision tree is one motivation for ensemble methods such as bagging and random forests.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Instability of single trees is a primary motivation for bagging and random forests.", "whyPool": "One of the primary motivations for bagging and random forests is to reduce the high variance and instability of individual decision trees by averaging predictions across many trees."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A decision tree that changes structure after minor modifications to the training data necessarily indicates that the learning algorithm is incorrect.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Structural change is a known property of greedy tree construction, not evidence the algorithm is wrong.", "whyPool": "Structural changes do not imply that the algorithm is flawed. They are a known characteristic of greedy tree construction and reflect the model's sensitivity to the training data."}, {"L": "E", "text": "High sensitivity to small changes in the training data is associated with higher model variance. 191 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "High sensitivity to small training changes = high variance; trees are the classic example.", "whyPool": "Models whose predictions change substantially due to small changes in the training data are considered high- variance learners. Decision trees are a classic example."}], "reference": "Instability → bagging/random forests — ISLR §8.2; ESL §8.7, §15.", "n": 210}, {"id": "ML-D6G5Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative analyst builds a Decision Tree Classifier to predict daily equity-market regimes—“Bullish” or “Bearish”— using 10 macroeconomic and technical indicators. The model is trained on 2,000 historical trading sessions. To evaluate the model’s reliability, the analyst performs a sensitivity test. Five randomly selected trading days—only 0.25% of the training sample—are removed, and the tree is refitted using the same hyperparameters: •  max_depth = None •  min_samples_split = 2 After refitting, the root split changes from X₁, the 14-Day RSI, to X₃, 10-Day Volatility. Because all subsequent nodes are constructed conditionally on the root partition, the change produces a substantially different tree, including different downstream splits, branches, and predicted market regimes for hundreds of historical dates. The risk committee evaluates the causes, structural mechanics, and practical consequences of this instability. Which of the following statements correctly describe structural instability and data sensitivity in Decision Trees?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Decision trees are generally high-variance estimators because their greedy, hierarchical construction makes every downstream split conditional on the splits selected above it. A change to an early split can therefore alter much of the remaining tree.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Trees are high-variance: greedy hierarchical construction makes every downstream split conditional on those above, so an early change alters much of the tree.", "whyPool": "Decision trees are generally high-variance estimators because their greedy, hierarchical construction makes every downstream split conditional on the splits selected above it. A change to an early split can therefore alter much of the remaining tree."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Applying a strictly monotonic nonlinear transformation—such as a logarithm to a positive-valued feature—ordinarily reduces structural instability because the transformation changes the relative ordering of observations and creates more reliable candidate partitions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A strictly monotonic transform preserves ordering/candidate partitions, so it does **not** stabilize the tree against added/removed observations (claim of \"reduces instability by changing ordering\" is wrong).", "whyPool": "Applying a strictly monotonic nonlinear transformation—such as a logarithm to a positive-valued feature— ordinarily reduces structural instability because the transformation changes the relative ordering of observations and creates more reliable candidate partitions."}, {"L": "C", "text": "In a trading application, structural instability can produce changing feature-importance rankings and inconsistent trading signals when the model is retrained, potentially increasing turnover, transaction costs, and unintended risk exposures.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Structural instability produces changing feature-importance rankings and inconsistent signals on retrain → turnover, transaction costs, unintended exposures.", "whyPool": "In a trading application, structural instability can produce changing feature-importance rankings and inconsistent trading signals when the model is retrained, potentially increasing turnover, transaction costs, and unintended risk exposures."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The variance and sample sensitivity of individual decision trees provide an important motivation for bagging and Random Forests, which aggregate predictions from multiple trees to produce a more stable estimator.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Variance/sample-sensitivity of single trees is a core motivation for bagging and random forests (aggregate many trees → more stable).", "whyPool": "The variance and sample sensitivity of individual decision trees provide an important motivation for bagging and Random Forests, which aggregate predictions from multiple trees to produce a more stable estimator."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Increasing the permitted tree depth generally increases structural instability because deeper trees can form increasingly localized partitions that are sensitive to small changes in the training sample. 192 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Increasing permitted depth generally increases instability: deeper trees form more localized partitions sensitive to small sample changes.", "whyPool": "Increasing the permitted tree depth generally increases structural instability because deeper trees can form increasingly localized partitions that are sensitive to small changes in the training sample."}], "flag": "source-key letters were mislabeled — corrected & verified vs PDF", "reference": "High variance / instability & monotonic-transform invariance — ISLR §8.1–8.2; ESL §9.2.", "withdrawn": true, "n": 211}, {"id": "ML-D6G5Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative portfolio manager is reviewing an automated trading algorithm that uses a single Decision Tree Classifier to assign daily market regimes. During quarterly rebalancing, the model is retrained after appending the latest 5 days of market data to the existing 3-year historical dataset. The manager notices that adding these 5 new observations caused the root node feature split to change completely, triggering a total re-organization of all downstream splits and drastically altering predicted portfolio allocations across historical backtest dates. The quantitative research team investigates the root causes of structural sensitivity and data instability in decision trees. Which of the following statements correctly describes the mechanics and stability properties of decision trees under data perturbations?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Single decision trees are structurally stable estimators because their top-down, greedy split selection relies on global optimization, ensuring that adding new data points only causes localized adjustments at the deepest leaf nodes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees use **local greedy** splitting, not global optimization; a small change near the root can pick a different split and cascade — not just localized leaf tweaks.", "whyPool": "Decision trees rely on local, greedy splitting rather than global optimization. A slight change in data near the root node can select a completely different feature or split threshold, which cascades down and radically alters the entire downstream tree topology."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Applying a strictly monotonic transformation to a continuous feature—such as taking ln(X)for positive Xor applying z- score standardization—changes the feature’s numerical scale and corresponding split-threshold values but ordinarily preserves the candidate partitions of the training observations. Therefore, it does not inherently stabilize the tree against changes in the training sample.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A strictly monotonic transform (ln, z-score) preserves ordering/partitions, so it does **not** inherently stabilize the tree against sample changes.", "whyPool": "Standard CART evaluates splits according to the partition of observations produced by a threshold: X௝ ≤s versus X௝ >s. A strictly increasing transformation preserves the ordering of the observations. Thus, a split at X௝ ≤shas an equivalent transformed threshold that places the same observations in each child node. For example: X௝ ≤s ⟺ ln₅X௝₉≤ ln(s), when X௝ > 0. Z-score standardization similarly preserves ordering when it uses a positive standard deviation. The reported numerical thresholds change, but the available training-sample partitions—and ordinarily the fitted tree’s structure and predictions—remain unchanged. Consequently, these transformations do not make the tree less sensitive to adding or removing observations. Exceptions may arise from transformations that are not strictly monotonic, are undefined for some values, create ties, or interact with numerical precision or implementation details."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Increasing the maximum tree depth (max_depth) reduces structural variance because deeper trees provide a finer spatial grid that buffers the root node against perturbations in the training dataset.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Deeper trees increase capacity/variance and instability — they do **not** buffer the root against perturbations.", "whyPool": "Deepening a tree increases model capacity and sensitivity to sample noise, which increases (rather than decreases) structural instability and overall model variance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The structural sensitivity of individual decision trees to minor data changes is a primary driver of model variance, providing the core mathematical motivation for ensemble methods like Bagging and Random Forests that average over diverse bootstrapped trees.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Single-tree structural sensitivity drives model variance → core motivation for bagging/random forests over bootstrapped trees.", "whyPool": "Individual decision trees suffer from high variance and structural instability. Ensemble methods directly exploit this behavior by aggregating predictions across many decorrelated trees grown on bootstrapped data samples, dramatically stabilizing predictions and lowering variance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Decision trees exhibit low sensitivity to noisy outliers because continuous feature splits are determined by calculating the overall variance across all features simultaneously rather than evaluating individual feature thresholds. 193 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees evaluate splits feature-by-feature; skewed/outlier ticks near thresholds can displace split points → trees are sensitive to noisy data.", "whyPool": "Decision trees evaluate candidate splits feature by feature. Highly skewed noise or extreme outlier ticks near split thresholds can easily displace split points, making single decision trees notoriously sensitive to noisy training data. G6: Strengths of Decision Trees"}], "reference": "Local greedy sensitivity; monotonic invariance; ensemble motivation — ISLR §8.1–8.2; ESL §9.2, §15.", "withdrawn": true, "n": 212}, {"id": "ML-D6G6Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A trading rule depends on an interaction: positive momentum predicts gains only when volatility is below 2%; when volatility is higher, the relationship reverses. A decision tree first splits on volatility and then uses different momentum thresholds in the two branches.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The tree can represent different momentum relationships in different volatility regions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Separate branches apply different momentum rules to low- vs. high-volatility regions.", "whyPool": "Separate branches can apply different decision rules to low- and high-volatility observations."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Sequential splits can model nonlinear interactions without adding an explicit momentum-times-volatility feature.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Hierarchical splits model nonlinear interactions without an explicit momentum×volatility feature.", "whyPool": "Hierarchical partitioning naturally represents conditional feature effects."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A decision tree is limited to fitting one global linear coefficient for momentum.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees are not limited to one global linear coefficient for momentum.", "whyPool": "Trees do not require one global linear relationship."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The described structure can be expressed as a set of conditional rules. 194 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Each root-to-leaf path defines a conditional if-and-then rule.", "whyPool": "Each root-to-leaf path defines an if-and-then rule."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The ability to represent the interaction guarantees that it will generalize from a small noisy sample.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Representational capacity does not eliminate estimation error / overfitting risk on small noisy samples.", "whyPool": "Representational capacity does not eliminate estimation error or overfitting risk."}], "reference": "Interaction modeling via hierarchical splits — ISLR §8.1; ESL §9.2.", "n": 213}, {"id": "ML-D6G6Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A dataset contains return as a decimal, volume in shares, and volatility in percent. An analyst multiplies volume by 1,000 and asks whether an ordinary decision tree must standardize the features before fitting. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Tree splits compare values within one feature at a time, so differing feature units do not directly distort a cross-feature distance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: splits compare values within one feature, so mixed units don't distort a cross-feature distance.", "whyPool": "Trees choose per-feature thresholds rather than calculating distances across mixed units."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Multiplying volume by a positive constant changes its numerical thresholds but need not change the ordering of volume observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a positive rescaling changes thresholds but preserves volume ordering → equivalent partitions.", "whyPool": "A positive rescaling preserves order, allowing equivalent partitions with rescaled threshold values."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Standardization is mandatory for a decision tree to function correctly.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: standardization is **not** mandatory for a tree to function.", "whyPool": "Ordinary tree learning generally does not require feature standardization."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Rescaling a feature can matter for algorithms based on Euclidean distance even when it does not materially change the corresponding tree partitions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: distance-based methods (Euclidean) are sensitive to scale even when tree partitions are unchanged.", "whyPool": "Distance-based methods are sensitive to relative numeric scale in a way trees usually are not."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Freedom from required scaling means irrelevant features can never harm a tree.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: not needing scaling doesn't mean irrelevant features are harmless — they can drive spurious splits/overfitting.", "whyPool": "Irrelevant features can still encourage spurious splits and overfitting. 195 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Scale invariance of trees vs. distance methods — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn \"Tips on practical use.\"", "n": 214}, {"id": "ML-D6G6Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A compliance analyst receives a small tree that rejects a proposed trade whenever liquidity is below a threshold and leverage is above another threshold. The analyst can trace a rejected trade through four nodes to its leaf. A much deeper alternative has slightly better validation accuracy.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The small tree provides a human-readable path explaining this individual result.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The small tree gives a human-readable path explaining this individual rejection.", "whyPool": "The analyst can identify the exact sequence of conditions leading to rejection."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A tree's conditional structure can support rule-based communication with stakeholders.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Root-to-leaf paths can be presented as explicit conditional rules to stakeholders.", "whyPool": "Root-to-leaf paths can be presented as explicit conditional rules."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Every decision tree remains equally interpretable regardless of its size or depth.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Large/deep trees can become hard to understand — interpretability is not constant across size.", "whyPool": "Large, deep trees can become difficult to understand as a whole."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Interpretability can be a relevant model-selection consideration alongside predictive accuracy.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Deployment can balance accuracy against transparency/governance.", "whyPool": "Deployment choices may balance performance with transparency and governance needs."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A readable decision path proves that the learned rule is economically valid and fair.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A readable path permits inspection but does not prove economic validity, fairness, or causality.", "whyPool": "Transparency permits inspection but does not establish validity, fairness, or causality."}], "reference": "Interpretability tradeoffs — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn tree advantages/limits.", "n": 215}, {"id": "ML-D6G6Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A quantitative research team is presenting a machine-learning model-selection framework to an institutional risk committee. The team proposes replacing a high-dimensional logistic regression model—which required extensive feature engineering, nonlinear spline transformations, logarithmic scaling, and missing-data imputation—with a Decision Tree Classifier for modeling credit-default risk among corporate borrowers. The committee requests an evaluation of the inherent strengths and limitations of Decision Trees, with particular attention to feature scaling, nonlinear modeling, data preparation, computational behavior, and the interpretability of individual predictions. Which of the following statements correctly evaluate the architectural, computational, and operational properties of Decision Trees? For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Applying a strictly monotonic transformation to an individual continuous feature—such as a logarithmic transformation over a positive domain or z-score standardization—changes the numerical split thresholds but ordinarily preserves the candidate partitions of the training observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a strictly monotonic transform changes thresholds but preserves candidate partitions.", "whyPool": "The statement is accurate. A standard CART split partitions observations according to a threshold: X௝ ≤s versus X௝ >s. A strictly increasing transformation preserves the ordering of the observed feature values. Therefore, the original and transformed features ordinarily offer corresponding thresholds that produce the same partitions. For example, when X௝ > 0, X௝ ≤s ⟺ ln₅X௝₉≤ ln(s). Similarly, z-score standardization changes the feature’s scale but preserves its ordering. The reported threshold values change, but the candidate partitions—and ordinarily the fitted structure and predictions—remain the same. This invariance is not absolute. Exceptions can arise when a transformation is not strictly monotonic, is undefined for some values, creates or changes ties, or interacts with numerical precision or implementation-specific behavior. Nevertheless, Decision Trees generally do not require feature standardization in the way that distance-based methods such as KNN do."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Through hierarchical, conditional splitting, Decision Trees can represent nonlinear relationships and feature interactions without requiring the analyst to specify polynomial terms, splines, or explicit interaction variables in advance. 196 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: hierarchical conditional splits represent nonlinearity/interactions without manual polynomial/spline/interaction terms.", "whyPool": "The statement is accurate. Decision Trees create hierarchical conditional rules. A branch may, for example, split on X₂only after an earlier split establishes that X₁ >s₁. This allows the effect of one feature to depend on the value of another, thereby representing feature interactions without manually constructed interaction terms. Repeated splits also create piecewise-constant decision regions capable of representing nonlinear threshold effects. Trees therefore do not require polynomial features or spline bases to move beyond the global linear decision boundary of an ordinary logistic regression model. However, standard trees use axis-aligned splits. Smooth or diagonal relationships may require many rectangular regions to approximate effectively."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Standard Decision Tree implementations universally accept unordered categorical variables and missing feature values without preprocessing because native categorical splitting and surrogate splits are inherent requirements of the CART algorithm.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: native categorical handling and surrogate splits for missing values are **implementation-dependent**, not universal CART requirements.", "whyPool": "The statement is incorrect because these capabilities are implementation-dependent rather than universal properties of Decision Trees or CART. Some tree algorithms and software packages support categorical predictors directly, while others require categories to be converted into numerical representations, such as one-hot-encoded indicator variables. Similarly, some implementations use surrogate splits or other mechanisms to route observations with missing values, but many require missing-data imputation or provide only limited native missing-value support. Accordingly, an analyst must evaluate the capabilities of the particular tree implementation being used. Decision Trees should not automatically be assumed to eliminate all categorical-data encoding or missing-data preparation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A Decision Tree partitions the feature space into localized, axis-aligned regions, allowing an individual prediction to be explained by tracing a sequence of “if-then” conditions from the root node to a terminal leaf.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: axis-aligned partitions give traceable root-to-leaf if-then explanations for a prediction.", "whyPool": "The statement is accurate. Each binary split divides an existing region using a condition involving one feature, such as 197 Rev:081020261230 X௝ ≤s. Repeated splits partition the feature space into axis-aligned, hyperrectangular regions. An individual borrower’s prediction can be explained by following the borrower’s path from the root to a leaf—for example: Leverage> 0.65 and Interest Coverage≤ 2.1 ⟹ Elevated Default Risk. This rule-based path can be intuitive for analysts, risk managers, and regulators. However, interpretability generally declines as the tree becomes deeper and more complex; a small tree is considerably easier to understand than a large, unconstrained one."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because CART greedily chooses the best available split at each node, the resulting fully grown tree is globally optimal and does not require cross-validation, pruning, or other complexity controls.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: greedy best-split does **not** yield a globally optimal tree, and CV/pruning/complexity controls are still needed.", "whyPool": "The statement is incorrect. CART uses greedy, top-down recursive splitting. At each node, it selects the feature and threshold that produce the greatest immediate improvement in the chosen criterion. It does not jointly search all possible tree structures to guarantee a globally optimal final tree. An unconstrained tree can also create small, highly localized leaves that fit noise in the training data. This typically produces low training error but high variance and poor out-of-sample performance. Validation or cross-validation is therefore commonly used to select controls such as max_depth, min_samples_leaf, max_leaf_nodes, or the cost-complexity pruning parameter α. Greedy splitting neither guarantees global optimality nor eliminates the need for model selection and complexity control."}], "reference": "Tree strengths & implementation caveats — ISLR §8.1; scikit-learn \"Decision Trees\" (advantages/disadvantages).", "n": 216}, {"id": "ML-D6G6Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative portfolio manager is explaining to a compliance auditor why a Decision Tree Regressor was selected over a Multi-Layer Perceptron (Neural Network) to predict stock return volatility. The compliance auditor requires that all models deployed in production be fully auditable, resistant to arbitrary scale transformations, and capable of handling raw financial features without complex pre-processing pipelines. The manager highlights several core architectural advantages of decision trees that satisfy these regulatory and operational requirements. Which of the following statements correctly describes an architectural strength or operational advantage of Decision Trees?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Decision trees require minimal data pre-processing because they are immune to monotonic feature scaling, meaning that converting a raw dollar volume feature into logarithmic scale or applying min-max normalization has zero effect on the resulting tree splits.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Trees need little scaling: splits depend on ordering, and monotonic transforms (log, min-max) preserve candidate partitions (barring ties/precision exceptions).", "whyPool": "Decision Trees generally require little feature scaling because standard CART splits depend on the ordering of observations rather than the units in which a feature is measured. A strictly monotonic transformation preserves the candidate partitions of the training data. For example, when dollar volume is positive, X௝ ≤s ⟺ ln₅X௝₉≤ ln(s). Similarly, min-max normalization preserves the ordering of feature values. These transformations change the numerical values of the reported split thresholds, but ordinarily do not change which observations fall on each side of a split or, consequently, the fitted tree’s structure and predictions. This invariance is not absolute: exceptions can arise if a transformation is not strictly monotonic, is undefined for some values, creates or changes ties, or interacts with finite numerical precision or implementation-specific behavior."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Decision trees eliminate the risk of overfitting on noisy financial datasets because their top-down recursive partitioning algorithm automatically caps tree depth when model variance exceeds bias.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Unconstrained trees are prone to overfitting; they do **not** auto-cap depth when variance exceeds bias.", "whyPool": "Unconstrained decision trees are notoriously prone to severe overfitting. They continue splitting nodes until leaves are pure or contain fewer samples than min_samples_split, memorizing noise unless explicit stopping rules or post-pruning are enforced."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Decision trees scale seamlessly to high-dimensional datasets because their top-down recursive partitioning algorithm guarantees finding the globally optimal combination of splits across all features in linear time.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "CART is greedy/local; the globally optimal tree is NP-complete — no linear-time global guarantee.", "whyPool": "CART is a top-down greedy algorithm that selects splits locally at each step. Finding the globally optimal decision tree is an NP-complete combinatorial optimization problem. Greedy heuristics run efficiently, but they do not guarantee global optimality."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Decision trees generate linear decision boundaries across continuous features, making them mathematically equivalent to Multiple Linear Regression when fitted without depth constraints.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees build non-linear axis-aligned step partitions, not smooth linear hyperplanes; not equivalent to linear regression.", "whyPool": "Decision trees construct non-linear, step-wise spatial partitions made of orthogonal, axis-aligned hyper- rectangles—not smooth continuous hyperplanes like linear regression models."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Decision trees perform implicit feature selection, capturing conditional non-linear interactions natively and providing transparent \"if-then\" decision pathways from root to leaf node.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Trees do implicit feature selection, capture conditional nonlinear interactions, and give transparent if-then paths.", "whyPool": "Decision trees select only the most informative features at each split (implicit feature selection), capture non- linear step-function interactions natively through conditional branching, and produce fully interpretable rule paths."}], "reference": "Tree strengths (scale invariance, interpretability) & greedy limits — ISLR §8.1; ESL §9.2.", "n": 217}, {"id": "ML-D6G6Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative risk team is designing a credit-scoring model to evaluate corporate bond default risks. The team compares a Decision Tree Classifier against a standard Logistic Regression model. The lead analyst highlights several practical advantages of decision trees that simplify data preparation and enhance model transparency for regulatory reporting. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the functional and structural strengths of Decision Trees?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Decision trees are robust to outliers in feature space because split decisions depend on the rank ordering of observations rather than the absolute magnitude of feature values.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Splits depend on rank ordering, so trees are relatively robust to feature-space outliers (not fully immune, but less magnitude-driven).", "whyPool": "Decision-tree splits depend primarily on the ordering of observed feature values and the partitions produced by candidate thresholds, rather than on the numerical distance between observations. Consequently, changing an extreme feature value while preserving its relative position will often leave the available partitions unchanged. This makes trees less directly influenced by feature magnitude than methods based on distances, means, or linear coefficients. However, “robust” does not mean completely unaffected. An outlier may still become isolated by a split, alter the selected partition, or produce an unreliable prediction in a small terminal leaf. Response-variable outliers can also strongly affect regression-tree predictions because each leaf prediction is ordinarily the mean response within that leaf."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Decision trees estimate a continuous slope coefficient for each predictor, allowing the marginal effect of a feature to be interpreted in the same manner as a coefficient in linear or logistic regression.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees give piecewise-constant predictions, not continuous slope coefficients like linear/logistic regression.", "whyPool": "Decision trees do not estimate continuous marginal slopes or global predictor coefficients. They divide the feature space using threshold rules and assign a constant prediction within each terminal region. The resulting prediction function is therefore piecewise constant rather than continuously linear."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Decision trees can model complex non-linear boundaries and high-order interactions without requiring the analyst to manually specify interaction terms or polynomial features.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Hierarchical splitting models nonlinear boundaries and interactions without manual interaction/polynomial terms.", "whyPool": "Through repeated hierarchical splitting, a Decision Tree can create nonlinear, piecewise-constant decision boundaries without requiring polynomial features or spline transformations. It can also represent interactions because the relevance of a later split depends on the conditions established by earlier splits. For example, the tree may split on X₂only within the branch where X₁ >s₁. This allows the effect of X₂to differ across regions defined by X₁, capturing an interaction without requiring the analyst to construct an explicit X₁X₂term. Because standard trees use axis-aligned splits, however, smooth or diagonal relationships may require many terminal regions to approximate effectively."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Unlike distance-based algorithms like K-Nearest Neighbors (K-NN) or Support Vector Machines (SVMs), decision trees do not require feature standardization or feature scaling prior to training. 199 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Unlike distance-based KNN/SVM, trees don't require standardization/scaling.", "whyPool": "Scale transformations do not change the rank order of data points along any single feature axis. As a result, standardizing or scaling features has zero impact on split threshold selection or tree performance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Unconstrained decision trees guarantee finding the globally optimal feature space partitioning that minimizes global classification error across all possible tree depth structures.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Unconstrained trees do **not** guarantee the globally optimal partition — CART is greedy; global optimum is NP-complete.", "whyPool": "CART uses a top-down, greedy optimization algorithm that evaluates splits locally at each step. Finding a globally optimal tree structure is NP-complete, so greedy heuristics are used instead. G7: Weaknesses and Failure Modes"}], "reference": "Tree strengths vs. distance/linear methods; greedy limits — ISLR §8.1; ESL §9.2.", "n": 218}, {"id": "ML-D6G7Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A tree predicts next-day returns from 40 indicators using no depth or leaf-size constraints. Several leaves contain a single unusual market day, training error is nearly zero, and out-of-sample results are poor.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The unrestricted tree may have memorized noise in the training set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "High capacity can capture accidental sample patterns rather than durable relationships.", "whyPool": "High capacity can capture accidental sample patterns rather than durable relationships."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Singleton leaves can produce predictions that depend on one potentially unrepresentative observation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Singleton leaves estimate output from one possibly unrepresentative observation.", "whyPool": "Such leaves estimate their output from minimal evidence."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Near-zero training error rules out overfitting.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Near-zero training error does not rule out overfitting — it often accompanies it.", "whyPool": "Extremely low training error can coexist with severe overfitting."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Complexity constraints or pruning could be evaluated as possible remedies. 200 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Complexity constraints/pruning are reasonable remedies to evaluate.", "whyPool": "Reducing tree complexity may improve the bias–variance tradeoff."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Poor out-of-sample performance is irrelevant as long as the tree is interpretable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Interpretability does not compensate for failure to generalize.", "whyPool": "Interpretability does not compensate for failure to generalize."}], "reference": "Overfitting via tiny leaves; pruning — ISLR §8.1; ESL ch.7.", "n": 219}, {"id": "ML-D6G7Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A decision tree uses only axis-aligned splits. The true class boundary in a two-feature space is approximately diagonal, so the fitted tree uses many small rectangular regions to approximate it.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A single axis-aligned split tests one feature against a threshold.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A single axis-aligned split tests one feature against a threshold.", "whyPool": "Standard splits partition the space using a threshold on one coordinate."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Approximating a diagonal boundary may require multiple tree splits.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Approximating a diagonal boundary needs multiple splits (a staircase).", "whyPool": "Several rectangular partitions may be needed to trace a diagonal pattern."}, {"L": "C", "text": "More splits can increase complexity and create overfitting risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "More splits raise complexity and overfitting risk.", "whyPool": "Additional partitions provide flexibility that may also fit noise."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A decision tree can represent only linear decision boundaries.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Collections of splits form nonlinear piecewise-constant regions — trees are not limited to linear boundaries.", "whyPool": "Collections of splits can form nonlinear, piecewise-constant decision regions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because the tree can approximate the pattern, the number of observations needed is irrelevant.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Sufficient data are still needed to estimate the more detailed structure reliably.", "whyPool": "Sufficient data are still needed to estimate the more detailed structure reliably."}], "reference": "Axis-aligned staircase approximation — ISLR §8.1; ESL §9.2.", "n": 220}, {"id": "ML-D6G7Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A tree trained during a long bull market uses a sharp threshold: momentum above 1.00% leads to a large positive forecast, while momentum at or below 1.00% leads to a negative forecast. Two otherwise similar observations have momentum values of 0.99% and 1.01%. 201 Rev:081020261230 The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The two observations can receive very different predictions despite having nearly identical momentum values.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: nearly identical momenta on opposite sides of the threshold can reach different leaves → very different predictions.", "whyPool": "They fall on opposite sides of the threshold and may reach different leaves."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Piecewise-constant leaves can create abrupt changes at split boundaries.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: piecewise-constant leaves create abrupt jumps at split boundaries.", "whyPool": "Tree predictions can jump discontinuously where the routing rule changes."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The learned threshold necessarily reflects a permanent causal law of the market.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: a predictive threshold from historical data does not establish a permanent causal law.", "whyPool": "A predictive threshold learned from historical data does not establish causality or permanence."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Performance can deteriorate if the relationship learned in the bull-market sample does not persist in a new regime.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: regime/distribution change can make the historical partition unreliable.", "whyPool": "Distribution or regime change can make the historical partition unreliable."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Testing on later, unseen periods can help reveal this generalization failure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: time-ordered out-of-sample testing can reveal this generalization failure.", "whyPool": "Time-ordered out-of-sample evaluation tests performance under later conditions."}], "reference": "Piecewise-constant discontinuity; regime shift ≠ causality — ISLR §8.1; ESL §9.2.", "n": 221}, {"id": "ML-D6G7Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quantitative portfolio manager deploys an unconstrained Decision Tree Classifier to generate daily buy/sell execution signals for a momentum-based equity strategy. The model uses 25 technical and market microstructure indicators derived from high-frequency intraday trading data. During live paper trading over a three-month period, the strategy experiences severe drawdown and significant turnover costs. The risk management team conducts a diagnostic post-mortem to analyze the model's structural weaknesses, failure modes, and poor out-of-sample generalization. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the inherent weaknesses, failure modes, and generalization limitations of Decision Trees?", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "It is incorrect to conclude that decision trees struggle to approximate smooth, linear decision boundaries efficiently because creating a diagonal boundary requires a dense, complex \"staircase\" sequence of orthogonal, axis-aligned splits.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double-negative: the inner conclusion (trees struggle with smooth/diagonal boundaries, needing a staircase of axis-aligned splits) is accurate, so calling it \"incorrect\" is false.", "whyPool": "The conclusion described in the statement is correct, so claiming that it is incorrect is false. Standard Decision Trees construct boundaries through axis-aligned splits of the form X௝ ≤s. Consequently, a smooth diagonal or linear boundary generally must be approximated by a sequence of rectangular, stair- step regions. Achieving a close approximation may require many splits and terminal leaves, making a single tree relatively inefficient for representing a relationship that a linear model could express with one boundary. (Ref: ISLR Ch. 8.1)"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Unconstrained decision trees exhibit high susceptibility to sample noise because recursive node splitting isolates increasingly small subsets of data, causing deeper leaves to fit random sample fluctuations rather than true underlying market signals.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Recursive splitting isolates small subsets, so deep leaves fit random fluctuations rather than true signals.", "whyPool": "As an unconstrained tree grows, each recursive split operates on a progressively smaller subset of the training observations. Predictions in deep leaves may therefore be determined by only a few observations. In noisy financial data, these small-sample splits can capture outliers, microstructure noise, or accidental patterns that are specific to the training period rather than stable market relationships. This typically reduces training error while increasing model variance and degrading out-of-sample performance. The risk is especially pronounced when min_samples_leaf is small and no depth or pruning constraint is imposed."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Decision trees excel at extrapolating trend-based forecasts far outside the range of feature values observed in the training data because leaf nodes apply continuous linear regressions to predict unseen values.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees **cannot** extrapolate; leaves give constant step values, not continuous linear regressions on unseen ranges.", "whyPool": "Decision trees cannot extrapolate beyond the range of training data. Terminal leaf predictions are constant step values (majority class vote or sample mean); any test input exceeding the historical range simply falls into the outermost bounding box and receives a static prediction."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because CART uses a greedy, top-down search heuristic, an unconstrained decision tree can fail to discover optimal multi-level feature combinations if the initial top-level split does not offer a strong immediate reduction in impurity. 202 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Greedy top-down search can miss good multi-level combinations if the initial split's immediate impurity reduction is weak.", "whyPool": "CART evaluates splits one node at a time without looking ahead. If a valuable multi-feature interaction requires an initial split that yields little to no immediate impurity reduction, the greedy algorithm will bypass it, missing the superior global structure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Decision trees are highly prone to data-driven structural instability, where a tiny perturbation in the training dataset can alter the root node split and radically change the entire downstream tree layout and prediction outputs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A tiny perturbation can flip the root split and cascade a full topology change (high variance).", "whyPool": "Because downstream splits are strictly conditional on parent decisions, small shifts in training data near the root can flip the chosen split variable, causing a cascading redesign of the entire tree topology (high variance)."}], "reference": "Diagonal-boundary inefficiency, no extrapolation, greedy limits, instability — ISLR §8.1; ESL §9.2.", "n": 222}, {"id": "ML-D6G7Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A risk management team is reviewing a quantitative trading strategy that utilizes a single Decision Tree Regressor to predict daily asset return volatility based on 15 macroeconomic and technical inputs. During stress testing and out-of-sample live performance tracking, the model demonstrates several classic failure modes, including severe performance degradation during regime shifts and extreme turnover triggered by minor data updates. The team conducts an architectural audit to pinpoint the specific mathematical and structural limitations of decision trees that led to these failures. Which of the following statements correctly describes an inherent weakness or failure mode of Decision Trees? For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate. 203 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Decision trees struggle to efficiently model simple diagonal decision boundaries because they are restricted to axis- aligned splits, forcing the algorithm to construct a complex, high-depth \"staircase\" approximation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: axis-aligned splits force a high-depth staircase to model a diagonal boundary.", "whyPool": "Standard Decision Trees use axis-aligned splits of the form X௝ ≤s. A smooth diagonal boundary, such as X₁+X₂ > 0, cannot generally be represented by a single standard split. Instead, the tree must approximate it using multiple rectangular regions that form a stair-step boundary. A closer approximation typically requires additional splits, leaves, and depth, making a standard tree relatively inefficient at representing simple diagonal relationships compared with a model that can directly learn an oblique or linear boundary."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Decision trees excel at extrapolating return forecasts beyond historical feature ranges because leaf nodes fit continuous linear regression lines to out-of-sample data points.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: trees **cannot** extrapolate beyond training ranges; leaves give bounded constants, not continuous regression lines.", "whyPool": "Decision trees cannot extrapolate outside the bounds of their training data. Terminal leaf nodes predict constant values (the sample mean or majority class of that leaf). Any test instance with feature values far beyond historical training limits simply lands in an outer hyper-rectangle and receives a static, bounded prediction."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The CART algorithm guarantees finding the globally optimal tree structure by performing a multi-level combinatorial search over all possible subtree split combinations simultaneously during initial training.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: CART is greedy/local and does **not** perform a global combinatorial search; global optimum is NP-complete.", "whyPool": "CART is a top-down greedy algorithm. It evaluates candidate splits locally at each step without looking ahead to future splits down the tree. Finding a globally optimal decision tree is an NP-complete problem; greedy heuristics do not guarantee global optimality."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Single decision trees demonstrate strong structural robustness to small training set perturbations because early splits near the root node are insulated from noise in deep leaf nodes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: single trees are **not** robust — a small perturbation near the root can restructure the whole tree (high variance).", "whyPool": "Decision trees are notoriously sensitive to small changes in training data (high variance). A slight perturbation near the root node can alter the initial feature split, cascading downstream to create a completely different tree topology and set of predictions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Unconstrained decision trees suffer from extreme high variance on noisy financial time series because deep recursive partitioning isolates tiny sample sizes, causing the model to treat random noise ticks as predictive signals.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: unconstrained deep trees isolate tiny samples in noisy data → high variance, fitting noise as signal.", "whyPool": "In low signal-to-noise environments like financial markets, growing deep trees creates leaves containing very few samples (N→1). This forces the model to fit random sample fluctuations rather than underlying structural relationships, yielding 0% training error but poor generalization."}], "reference": "No extrapolation, greedy NP-completeness, instability, high variance — ISLR §8.1; ESL §9.2.", "n": 223}, {"id": "ML-D6G7Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Decision Trees", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Quantitative Researcher at an asset management firm evaluating a decision tree that predicts whether stocks will outperform the S&P 500 over the next quarter. A junior analyst argues that because decision trees can model nonlinear relationships and interactions, they should always outperform simpler models such as linear regression. 204 Rev:081020261230 Before approving the model for production, the Chief Investment Officer asks you to evaluate the limitations of relying on a single decision tree.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A single decision tree can produce different models when trained on slightly different datasets because of its sensitivity to the training data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Single trees are high-variance: small data changes alter selected splits → different structures/predictions.", "whyPool": "Decision trees are high-variance models. Small changes in the training data can change the selected splits and lead to different tree structures and predictions."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Since decision trees naturally model nonlinear relationships, they are guaranteed to outperform linear models on unseen data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "No model is guaranteed to outperform others on unseen data (no free lunch); it depends on data/sample size/bias–variance.", "whyPool": "Although decision trees can model nonlinear relationships, no model is guaranteed to outperform others on unseen data. Performance depends on the underlying data, sample size, and the bias–variance tradeoff."}, {"L": "C", "text": "An unconstrained decision tree may fit noise in the training data, reducing its ability to generalize to future market conditions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Deep unconstrained trees can memorize noise, hurting generalization.", "whyPool": "Deep, unconstrained trees can memorize idiosyncratic patterns or noise in the training data, leading to poor generalization."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Increasing the complexity of a decision tree always improves both training performance and test performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "More complexity generally improves training but can worsen test performance (overfitting).", "whyPool": "Increasing complexity generally improves training performance but can worsen test performance due to overfitting. More complexity does not always improve generalization."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Even when a decision tree captures complex patterns, controlling its complexity may improve out-of-sample performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Controlling complexity (hyperparameters/pruning) often improves out-of-sample performance even if training accuracy drops.", "whyPool": "Limiting tree complexity through hyperparameters or pruning often produces a model that generalizes better, even if training accuracy decreases. 205 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Single-tree variance, no-free-lunch, complexity control — ISLR §8.1–8.2; ESL ch.7.", "n": 224}, {"id": "ML-D7G1Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio manager has five independently trained regression trees that estimate next-day returns. Each tree is moderately accurate but its predictions vary substantially when the training sample changes; the manager asks whether averaging their forecasts could make position sizing more stable without changing the trading universe.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Averaging can reduce prediction variance when the trees do not make perfectly correlated errors.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Imperfectly correlated errors partially cancel in an average, so aggregation reduces prediction variance — the core variance-reduction argument for bagging/averaging.", "whyPool": "Imperfectly correlated errors can partially cancel in an average."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The ensemble is guaranteed to outperform its best member on every future observation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "An ensemble improves *expected* performance but carries no observation-by-observation guarantee to beat its best member on every future point.", "whyPool": "An ensemble may improve expected performance, but it has no observation-by-observation guarantee."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If each tree has the same systematic directional bias, averaging alone may preserve much of that bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A shared systematic directional bias does not disappear under averaging; only the uncorrelated (variance) component cancels.", "whyPool": "Shared systematic error does not disappear merely because predictions are averaged."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Aggregation can be useful even when no individual tree is consistently the best model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Combining unstable learners can yield a more reliable aggregate than repeatedly selecting one learner, even when no single tree is consistently best.", "whyPool": "Combining unstable learners can produce a more reliable aggregate than repeatedly choosing one learner."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Averaging five predictions is equivalent to selecting the prediction from the deepest tree.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Averaging uses all five outputs; it is not equivalent to selecting the single deepest tree's prediction.", "whyPool": "Averaging uses all five outputs rather than selecting one tree by depth. 207 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Bagging / averaging for variance reduction — ISLR ch.8 (§8.2 bagging); concept \"error cancellation requires imperfect correlation.\"", "n": 225}, {"id": "ML-D7G1Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A trading desk combines three classifiers to decide whether to enter a trade. The models vote “buy, ” “buy, ” and “do not buy, ” and the head trader wants a simple rule that is less dependent on any single model while preserving low decision latency. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A majority-vote ensemble would issue a “buy” decision in this case.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: two of three votes support \"buy,\" so a majority-vote ensemble issues \"buy.\"", "whyPool": "Two of the three votes support “buy. ”"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Majority voting ensures the ensemble is correct whenever at least one member is correct.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: majority voting does NOT ensure correctness whenever one member is right — a correct minority can be overruled by two incorrect members.", "whyPool": "A correct minority model can be overruled by two incorrect models."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Combining models can reduce dependence on an idiosyncratic error made by one learner.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: other members can outvote an isolated idiosyncratic error, reducing dependence on one learner.", "whyPool": "The other members can outvote an isolated mistake."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If all three classifiers always produce identical predictions, voting offers little error-cancellation benefit.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: perfectly aligned (identical) predictions provide no diversity, so voting offers little error-cancellation benefit.", "whyPool": "Perfectly aligned predictions provide no diversity for voting to exploit."}, {"L": "E", "text": "An ensemble necessarily requires more input features than any of its members.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: an ensemble does not require more features than its members — multiple learners may share the same feature set.", "whyPool": "Multiple learners may use the same feature set."}], "reference": "Majority voting and diversity — ISLR ch.8; scikit-learn \"Ensemble methods: Voting Classifier.\"", "n": 226}, {"id": "ML-D7G1Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk committee compares a single highly tuned tree with an ensemble of many modest trees for forecasting drawdown risk. The chief risk officer asks whether “many weak models must always become one strong model, ” but also values stability across retraining dates.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "An ensemble can improve stability when its members capture useful signal and make partly different errors.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Members with useful signal and partly different errors yield a more stable aggregate — the legitimate version of the \"wisdom of crowds\" claim.", "whyPool": "Useful but imperfectly correlated learners can yield a more stable aggregate."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Adding models that perform worse than random and make the same errors must improve the ensemble.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Adding worse-than-random members that make the *same* errors can damage the ensemble; correlated poor learners do not \"must improve\" it.", "whyPool": "Consistently poor, correlated members can damage rather than improve the result."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The quality of the aggregation rule affects the final ensemble prediction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The aggregation rule (mean, median, weighted vote) affects the final prediction, so it is part of ensemble design.", "whyPool": "Mean, median, weighted voting, and other rules can yield different predictions."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A single model can outperform an ensemble if the added learners contribute noise or systematic error.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A single model can beat an ensemble when added learners contribute noise or shared systematic error — more models ≠ better generalization.", "whyPool": "More models do not guarantee better generalization."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The phrase “wisdom of the crowd” removes the need for out-of-sample evaluation. 208 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "\"Wisdom of the crowd\" does not remove the need for out-of-sample evaluation; ensemble performance must still be tested on unseen data.", "whyPool": "Ensemble performance must still be tested on unseen data."}], "reference": "Limits of aggregation & aggregation rules — ISLR ch.8; ESL ch.16 (ensemble learning).", "n": 227}, {"id": "ML-D7G1Q4", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are the Head of Quantitative Research at a multi-strategy hedge fund evaluating whether to replace a well-performing decision tree with an ensemble model. The Portfolio Manager argues that if one decision tree is already reasonably accurate, training multiple trees seems like unnecessary computational overhead. Meanwhile, the Chief Risk Officer believes combining multiple models may improve the reliability of predictions during volatile market conditions. You are asked to explain the motivation for using ensemble methods.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The primary motivation for ensemble methods is that combining multiple models can often produce more accurate and stable predictions than relying on a single model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The primary motivation is that combining models often yields more accurate and stable predictions than a single learner (robustness).", "whyPool": "Ensemble methods combine predictions from multiple models to improve predictive performance and increase robustness compared with relying on a single learner."}, {"L": "B", "text": "An ensemble can improve predictive performance even when some individual models make errors, provided those errors are not perfectly correlated.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Ensembles help even when individual models err, provided the errors are not perfectly correlated so they can partly cancel.", "whyPool": "Ensembles are most effective when individual learners make different errors. Aggregating their predictions can reduce overall prediction error and improve stability."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If one decision tree consistently outperforms every other tree on the training data, an ensemble built from multiple trees is guaranteed to perform worse on unseen data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A tree that is best on *training* data is not guaranteed to generalize best; ensembles often outperform it out-of-sample by reducing variance — no \"guaranteed worse\" result.", "whyPool": "A single tree performing best on the training data does not imply it will generalize best. Ensembles often outperform individual trees on unseen data by reducing variance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Ensemble methods are motivated by the idea that different models may capture different aspects of the underlying data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Different models (or differently trained versions) capture complementary patterns; combining these perspectives is a key motivation.", "whyPool": "Different models or differently trained versions of the same model can capture complementary patterns. Combining these perspectives is a key motivation behind ensemble learning. 209 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "The main purpose of an ensemble is to reduce the amount of training data required for machine learning models.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Ensembles aim to improve performance/robustness, not to reduce required training data — they typically add computation on the same dataset.", "whyPool": "Ensemble methods are intended to improve predictive performance and robustness, not to reduce the amount of training data required. In practice, ensembles often require additional computation while using the same training dataset."}], "reference": "Motivation for ensembles — ISLR ch.8 (§8.2); concept \"accuracy + robustness vs. single learner.\"", "n": 228}, {"id": "ML-D7G1Q5", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are a Senior Quantitative Researcher presenting two competing models to your firm's Investment Committee. One team recommends deploying a single decision tree because it is easy to explain to clients, while another team proposes an ensemble of many decision trees. The committee asks whether combining multiple models simply increases computational complexity or whether it provides a meaningful statistical advantage.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "If individual models make different prediction errors, combining their predictions can improve overall performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "When individual models make different errors, aggregating predictions reduces overall error and improves generalization.", "whyPool": "Ensembles are most effective when individual learners make different errors. Aggregating these predictions can reduce overall prediction error and improve generalization."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The primary motivation for ensembles is to make individual base learners more interpretable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The motivation is improved performance/robustness, not interpretability — ensembles are usually *less* interpretable than a single model.", "whyPool": "The primary motivation for ensemble methods is improved predictive performance and robustness, not increased interpretability. In fact, ensembles are often less interpretable than individual models."}, {"L": "C", "text": "An ensemble of identical models that always make exactly the same prediction errors is unlikely to provide much improvement over a single model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Identical models that make exactly the same errors provide little improvement; diversity is a key ingredient.", "whyPool": "If every model makes identical errors, averaging their predictions provides little or no benefit. Diversity among learners is a key ingredient for successful ensembles."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Combining multiple models can improve the stability of predictions across different samples of data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Averaging across models yields more stable predictions, less sensitive to a single training sample's peculiarities.", "whyPool": "By averaging across multiple models, ensembles often produce more stable predictions that are less sensitive to the peculiarities of a single training sample."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Ensembles are guaranteed to outperform every individual model regardless of how the base learners are constructed.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "No guarantee an ensemble beats every individual model; it depends on base-learner quality, diversity, and method suitability.", "whyPool": "There is no guarantee that an ensemble will outperform every individual model. Performance depends on factors such as the quality and diversity of the base learners and the suitability of the ensemble method for the problem."}], "reference": "Statistical rationale for ensembles — ISLR ch.8; scikit-learn \"Ensemble methods\" overview.", "n": 229}, {"id": "ML-D7G1Q6", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A quantitative research team is transitioning from using a single Decision Tree Classifier to a Bagged Decision Tree Ensemble (using 300 unconstrained trees) to predict directional price movements for liquid futures contracts. During an internal peer review, a junior analyst asks why combining hundreds of decision trees trained on bootstrap samples improves trading signal stability compared to relying on a single decision tree. 210 Rev:081020261230 The team lead explains the core conceptual mechanics of bootstrap sampling, tree correlation, and out-of-bag validation. Which of the following statements correctly evaluates the intuitive mechanics and practical behavior of Bagging? For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Bagging improves predictive stability primarily by taking deep, complex trees—which are prone to overfitting individually—and averaging their predictions together to smooth out random noise.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: deep trees have low bias but high variance; averaging hundreds of them cancels sample-specific noise and lowers variance.", "whyPool": "Single deep decision trees have low bias but high variance (they are very sensitive to noise in training data). Averaging predictions across hundreds of deep trees cancels out sample-specific noise, significantly lowering overall variance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Each individual tree in a bagged ensemble is built using a bootstrap sample, which is created by randomly drawing observations from the training dataset with replacement.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: each tree uses a bootstrap sample — N observations drawn with replacement from the original N.", "whyPool": "Bootstrapping is the process of generating new datasets of size N by sampling randomly with replacement from the original N observations."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because each bootstrap sample leaves out roughly one-third of the original training data, these \"out-of-bag\" observations can be used to test the ensemble's accuracy without needing a separate validation set.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: each bootstrap sample leaves ~37% out-of-bag, and those rows give a built-in cross-validation check without a separate holdout.", "whyPool": "On average, each bootstrap sample contains about 63% of the original data, leaving the remaining ∼37% \"out- of-bag\" (OOB). Evaluating each observation using only the trees that didn't see it during training provides a free, built-in cross-validation check."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Bagging works best when base decision trees are pruned down to very shallow depths (max_depth = 1 or 2) because shallow trees have low variance, making them easier to average.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: bagging wants deep, low-bias trees; pruning to max_depth 1–2 keeps high bias that averaging cannot remove — shallow trees are *not* the right base learner.", "whyPool": "Bagging relies on using deep, unconstrained trees with high flexibility (low bias). If you average shallow trees (high bias), averaging reduces variance slightly but leaves the high systematic error unchanged."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Even though each tree in a bagged ensemble is trained on a different bootstrap sample, the trees still tend to look similar if a few dominant features are consistently chosen near the top of every tree.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a dominant feature becomes the root split in nearly every tree, keeping trees correlated and limiting bagging's variance reduction.", "whyPool": "Because all bootstrap samples are drawn from the exact same pool of historical data, a strongly predictive feature will likely be selected as the root split in almost every tree. This keeps the trees correlated with one another, which limits how much bagging alone can reduce variance. G2: Bagging and Bootstrap Sampling"}], "reference": "Bagging, OOB error, tree correlation — ISLR ch.8 (§8.2.1 bagging, OOB error); ESL §15 (random forests / OOB).", "n": 230}, {"id": "ML-D7G2Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An asset manager builds 100 decision trees from a training set of 10,000 trading days. For each tree, the research lead draws 10,000 rows with replacement and then averages the trees’ return forecasts; a junior analyst asks why some days appear more than once while others are absent. 211 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Sampling with replacement allows the same training row to appear multiple times in one bootstrap sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Sampling with replacement returns each drawn row to the pool, so the same row can appear multiple times in one sample.", "whyPool": "Each draw returns the selected row to the sampling pool."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A bootstrap sample of 10,000 draws must contain all 10,000 distinct original rows.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A 10,000-draw bootstrap need not contain all 10,000 distinct rows — repeats imply omissions (~37% omitted on average).", "whyPool": "Repeated selections mean some original rows will generally be omitted."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Different bootstrap samples can produce different fitted trees even when the tree algorithm is otherwise deterministic.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Different sampled observations change chosen splits and structure, so a deterministic algorithm still yields different fitted trees.", "whyPool": "Changes in the sampled observations can change chosen splits and tree structure."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Averaging these trees is intended primarily to reduce the variance of an unstable base learner.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Averaging is aimed primarily at reducing the variance of an unstable base learner such as a decision tree.", "whyPool": "Bagging commonly stabilizes high-variance learners such as decision trees."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Bagging requires the targets to be randomly permuted before each tree is trained.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Bagging does not permute targets; features and their correct targets must stay paired.", "whyPool": "Features and their correct targets must remain paired."}], "reference": "Bootstrap resampling — ISLR ch.8 (§8.2.1); ch.5 (§5.2 the bootstrap).", "n": 231}, {"id": "ML-D7G2Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A quant team bags several deep regression trees, but all trees still respond similarly during volatile markets. The portfolio manager says the trees were trained on different bootstrap samples, so their errors must be independent and should cancel completely. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Different bootstrap samples do not guarantee independent prediction errors.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: the choice asserts \"the claim [different bootstrap samples do not guarantee independent errors] is false.\" That inner claim is actually accurate (resampling promotes variation but not statistical independence), so asserting it is false is incorrect.", "whyPool": "Resampling promotes variation but does not establish statistical independence."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Shared market features and overlapping observations can keep the trees’ predictions correlated.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: shared market features and overlapping observations keep predictions correlated.", "whyPool": "The learners still draw from the same underlying dataset and may discover similar relationships. 212 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "If base-learner errors are strongly positively correlated, averaging provides less variance reduction than if they are weakly correlated.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: strongly positively correlated errors reinforce rather than cancel, giving less variance reduction than weakly correlated errors.", "whyPool": "Correlated errors reinforce one another instead of canceling effectively."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Increasing the number of highly correlated trees guarantees that ensemble variance reaches zero.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: adding more *highly correlated* trees does not drive ensemble variance to zero — a shared correlated component remains even in a large ensemble.", "whyPool": "A shared correlated component can remain even in a very large ensemble."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Measuring correlation among out-of-sample residuals can help assess whether the ensemble has useful error diversity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: measuring correlation among out-of-sample residuals directly assesses error diversity.", "whyPool": "Residual correlation is direct evidence about diversity in model errors."}], "reference": "Correlation limits on bagging variance reduction — ESL §15.2 (random forests, variance of an average of correlated variables); ISLR ch.8.", "n": 232}, {"id": "ML-D7G2Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A compliance-sensitive trading system uses bagged classifiers to flag suspicious orders. The team proposes training each model on a disjoint one-tenth of the data to “make the models independent, ” while the compliance officer worries that rare manipulation examples may disappear from several training subsets.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Standard bagging ordinarily uses bootstrap samples drawn from the full training set rather than mutually exclusive partitions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Standard bagging draws with-replacement bootstrap samples from the *full* training set, not mutually exclusive partitions.", "whyPool": "Each bagged learner normally receives a with-replacement sample from the full dataset."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Disjoint subsets can give each learner less training information than full-sized bootstrap samples.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A one-tenth disjoint partition exposes each learner to far fewer observations than a full-size bootstrap sample.", "whyPool": "A one-tenth partition exposes a learner to fewer observations."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Bootstrap sampling guarantees every rare positive example appears in every learner’s sample.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Bootstrap sampling does not guarantee every rare positive appears in every learner's sample — any observation may be absent.", "whyPool": "Any particular observation may be absent from a bootstrap sample."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Stratified or otherwise controlled sampling may be considered when rare-class representation is operationally important.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stratified or otherwise controlled sampling can address rare-class representation when handled consistently and evaluated properly.", "whyPool": "Sampling design can address class-representation constraints, provided it is handled consistently and evaluated properly."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Bagging changes the observed target label of a duplicated row each time that row is selected.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A duplicated row retains its original features and target; bagging never changes the observed label.", "whyPool": "A duplicated row retains its original features and target. G3: Random Forests"}], "reference": "Bagging vs. data partitioning; class imbalance — ISLR ch.8 (§8.2.1); scikit-learn \"Ensemble methods: Bagging,\" \"Imbalanced learning\" (stratified sampling).", "n": 233}, {"id": "ML-D7G3Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A research team finds that its bagged trees repeatedly choose recent momentum as the root split because momentum is the strongest feature. The director suggests a random forest that considers only a random subset of features at each split so the ensemble is not dominated by nearly identical trees. 213 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Random feature selection can cause some trees to use alternatives when momentum is unavailable at a candidate split.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Restricting candidates at a split can force some trees to use alternative predictive features when momentum is unavailable.", "whyPool": "Restricting candidates may allow other predictive features to determine a split."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Feature randomness is intended to increase diversity among trees.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Feature randomness is intended to increase diversity, decorrelating trees beyond bootstrap sampling alone.", "whyPool": "It decorrelates trees beyond what bootstrap sampling alone may achieve."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Every tree in a random forest must use a completely disjoint set of features.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Trees need not use disjoint feature sets; random subsets are re-drawn per split and features recur across trees.", "whyPool": "Random subsets are typically drawn anew at splits, and features may recur across trees."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Reducing correlation among useful trees can improve the variance-reduction benefit of aggregation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Reducing correlation among useful trees improves the variance-reduction benefit of aggregation.", "whyPool": "Less-correlated errors cancel more effectively."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A random forest ordinarily combines its trees without using their predictions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A random forest aggregates its trees' predictions by averaging or voting — it does not combine them without using predictions.", "whyPool": "Tree predictions are aggregated by averaging or voting."}], "reference": "Random forests / feature subsampling — ISLR ch.8 (§8.2.2); ESL §15.", "n": 234}, {"id": "ML-D7G3Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A hedge fund compares one deep tree with a 300-tree random forest for predicting weekly return direction. The forest improves validation accuracy and retraining stability, but the portfolio manager asks whether the improvement proves that every tree in the forest is individually better than the original tree. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: The forest can outperform the single tree even if many member trees are individually weaker.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: the choice asserts \"the claim [the forest can outperform even if many members are individually weaker] is false.\" That inner claim is accurate (collective error cancellation can outweigh weaker members), so asserting it false is incorrect.", "whyPool": "Collective error cancellation can outweigh weaker individual performance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Bootstrap and feature randomness can make the member trees differ from one another.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: bootstrap and feature randomness both alter the data/splits seen, making member trees differ.", "whyPool": "Both mechanisms alter the data or candidate splits seen by the trees."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The forest’s aggregate can be more stable than any particular deep tree.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: aggregation reduces sensitivity to any one tree's unstable structure, so the aggregate can be more stable than any single deep tree.", "whyPool": "Aggregation reduces sensitivity to a single tree’s unstable structure. 214 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Better validation accuracy proves the forest will outperform on every future market regime.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: good validation accuracy does not prove outperformance in *every* future regime — validation is evidence, not a guarantee.", "whyPool": "Validation evidence is not a guarantee across all future regimes."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The comparison should use the same time-aware evaluation design for both models.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a consistent, time-aware evaluation design for both models is needed for a meaningful comparison.", "whyPool": "A consistent evaluation design is needed for a meaningful comparison."}], "reference": "Random forest generalization vs. single tree; time-aware validation — ISLR ch.8; ESL §15.3.", "n": 235}, {"id": "ML-D7G3Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A low-latency desk is tuning a random forest. Considering fewer features at each split slightly lowers individual-tree accuracy but reduces correlation among trees; considering all features makes the method resemble ordinary bagged trees and lowers training time only marginally.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: Lower individual-tree accuracy can be acceptable if the resulting diversity improves the aggregate.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: the choice asserts \"the claim [lower individual-tree accuracy can be acceptable if diversity improves the aggregate] is false.\" The inner claim is accurate, so calling it false is inaccurate → False. Ensemble quality depends on both member strength and error correlation.", "whyPool": "Ensemble quality depends on both member strength and error correlation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Considering all available features at every split removes the random-feature component of the forest.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Considering all available features at every split removes the random-feature component, leaving only bootstrap variation (i.e., bagging).", "whyPool": "The method then relies on bootstrap variation but not feature subsampling at splits."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The number of candidate features per split is a tradeoff between tree strength and diversity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The number of candidate features per split is a genuine tradeoff between tree strength and diversity.", "whyPool": "Too many candidates can correlate trees; too few can weaken them excessively."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Selecting fewer candidate features guarantees lower ensemble test error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Fewer candidate features does not guarantee lower test error — the best setting is data-dependent and must be validated.", "whyPool": "The best setting depends on the data and must be validated."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Inference cost and latency can increase as the number of trees grows.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Inference cost and latency grow with the number of trees (more split traversals and aggregation at prediction time).", "whyPool": "More trees generally require more split traversals and aggregation work at prediction time. G4: Diversity Among Learners"}], "reference": "mtry tradeoff and inference cost — ISLR ch.8 (§8.2.2); ESL §15.3; scikit-learn \"RandomForest max_features.\"", "n": 236}, {"id": "ML-D7G4Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A model committee evaluates two five-model ensembles. Ensemble A contains five versions of the same tree that make almost identical mistakes; Ensemble B contains useful models whose errors occur on different observations. The committee chair asks which ensemble is more likely to benefit from averaging.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Ensemble B has more opportunity for errors to cancel.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Ensemble B's different error patterns give more opportunity for errors to cancel.", "whyPool": "Different error patterns provide error-cancellation opportunities."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Ensemble A effectively gains five independent sources of information merely because it contains five files.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Five nearly identical files do not confer five independent information sources; duplicates add little.", "whyPool": "Duplicate or nearly identical models add little independent information."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Diversity is useful only when the diverse learners retain some predictive skill.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Diversity helps only when the diverse learners retain predictive skill; otherwise it just injects noise.", "whyPool": "Diversity without useful signal can simply inject noise."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Deliberately adding random but uninformative predictions must improve Ensemble B. 215 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Deliberately adding random, uninformative predictions can degrade, not improve, Ensemble B.", "whyPool": "Uninformative diversity can degrade the combined forecast."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Comparing out-of-sample prediction or residual correlations can help distinguish the ensembles.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing out-of-sample prediction/residual correlations helps distinguish which ensemble has genuine diversity.", "whyPool": "Correlations reveal whether learners behave differently where it matters."}], "reference": "Diversity and error correlation — ESL ch.16; concept \"duplicate models add no information.\"", "n": 237}, {"id": "ML-D7G4Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A global asset manager combines a linear model, KNN, and a decision tree to forecast returns. The CIO likes their architectural differences, but the validation report shows that all three make large errors on the same crisis dates because none was trained on comparable regimes. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Different algorithm names do not guarantee diverse prediction errors.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: different algorithm names do not guarantee diverse errors — distinct methods may learn similar signals or fail on the same cases.", "whyPool": "Distinct methods may still learn similar signals or fail on the same cases."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Shared blind spots can limit the ensemble’s protection during crisis periods.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: shared blind spots (a common regime gap) create correlated errors and limit crisis protection.", "whyPool": "A common regime gap creates correlated errors."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Averaging must eliminate an error that all three models make in the same direction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: averaging does NOT eliminate an error all three make in the same direction — a shared directional error survives aggregation.", "whyPool": "A shared directional error survives aggregation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Diversity should be assessed using model behavior, not architecture labels alone.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: diversity should be judged by out-of-sample behavior/errors, not architecture labels alone.", "whyPool": "Out-of-sample predictions and errors provide more relevant evidence of diversity."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Adding a model that performs differently on crisis observations could improve ensemble robustness if it also generalizes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a model that behaves differently on crisis dates *and* generalizes can improve robustness by addressing the common weakness.", "whyPool": "Complementary, validated behavior can address an existing common weakness. 216 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Behavioral vs. nominal diversity; correlated errors — ESL ch.16; concept \"shared blind spots survive averaging.\"", "n": 238}, {"id": "ML-D7G4Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio team wants to assign ensemble weights. One model has the best standalone Sharpe ratio but is highly correlated with two existing members; another has a lower standalone Sharpe ratio but makes useful forecasts on dates when the existing members fail. The portfolio manager must balance individual quality against complementarity.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Standalone performance alone is insufficient to determine a model’s ensemble contribution.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Standalone performance alone is insufficient — marginal value depends on interaction with existing members.", "whyPool": "Marginal value depends on interaction with existing members as well as individual skill."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The lower-Sharpe model could add more incremental value because of its complementary errors.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The lower-Sharpe model can add more incremental value through complementary (uncorrelated) errors.", "whyPool": "A complementary model may improve the combined forecast despite weaker standalone results."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Giving the highest-performing model all the weight always maximizes out-of-sample ensemble performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Concentrating all weight on the top model discards diversification and can overfit model selection — not an out-of-sample maximizer.", "whyPool": "Concentrating weight can discard diversification and may overfit model selection."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Ensemble weights should be chosen using only in-sample fit because correlations are stable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Choosing weights on in-sample fit alone is unsafe; in-sample correlations may be optimistic or unstable.", "whyPool": "In-sample relationships may be optimistic or unstable."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Validation can compare whether weighting schemes improve the aggregate rather than merely its components.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Validation should compare whether weighting schemes improve the *aggregate*, the object actually deployed.", "whyPool": "The final combined model is the object that should be evaluated out of sample. G5: Boosting Intuition"}], "reference": "Ensemble weighting, diversification vs. concentration — Grinold & Kahn (combining signals / information ratio); ESL ch.16.", "n": 239}, {"id": "ML-D7G5Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quant team builds a sequence of shallow regression trees to forecast returns. After the first tree, the next tree is trained to address the remaining prediction errors, and the research lead explains that the final forecast adds the trees’ contributions using a learning rate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Later trees are trained with information about errors left by earlier trees.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Boosting trains later trees on information about earlier trees' errors, focusing on what the current ensemble missed.", "whyPool": "Boosting focuses successive learners on what the current ensemble has not captured."}, {"L": "B", "text": "This sequential dependence differs from standard bagging, where base learners can be trained independently.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "This sequential dependence differs from bagging, where base learners can be trained independently.", "whyPool": "Bagged learners do not ordinarily depend on prior learners’ errors."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The first tree must perfectly fit the targets before the second tree is useful.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The first tree need not perfectly fit the targets — remaining error is exactly what later learners reduce.", "whyPool": "Remaining error is precisely what later learners are intended to reduce."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A smaller learning rate can make each new tree’s correction more conservative.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A smaller learning rate (shrinkage) makes each new tree's correction more conservative.", "whyPool": "Shrinkage scales each added contribution."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The final boosted prediction necessarily equals an unweighted majority vote.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Regression boosting forms an additive, weighted prediction, not an unweighted majority vote.", "whyPool": "Regression boosting commonly forms an additive, weighted prediction."}], "reference": "Boosting / gradient boosting, shrinkage — ISLR ch.8 (§8.2.3 boosting); ESL ch.10.", "n": 240}, {"id": "ML-D7G5Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A fraud-detection desk uses a boosted classifier. After an early model misses several rare manipulative orders, subsequent learners place greater emphasis on difficult or misclassified cases; the compliance director asks whether this means those cases are simply duplicated at prediction time. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Boosting can shift training attention toward examples the current ensemble handles poorly.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: boosting can shift training attention toward examples the current ensemble handles poorly (some methods reweight, others fit residuals).", "whyPool": "Some boosting methods reweight hard cases, while others fit residual-like errors."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Emphasizing hard examples is a training mechanism, not a requirement to duplicate live observations at inference.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: emphasizing hard examples is a *training* mechanism; live predictions use the learned ensemble and need not alter incoming data.", "whyPool": "Live predictions use the learned ensemble; the training emphasis need not alter incoming data."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Later learners are designed to be exact independent copies of the first learner.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: later learners are not exact independent copies of the first — they respond to the current ensemble's weaknesses.", "whyPool": "Later learners respond to the current ensemble’s weaknesses."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A mislabeled training example may receive increasing attention and become harmful.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a mislabeled example can look persistently hard, attract increasing attention, and become harmful.", "whyPool": "Noise or bad labels can look persistently difficult and attract excessive focus."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Boosting and bagging use the same sequential error-correction logic.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: boosting and bagging do not share the same logic — standard bagging trains learners independently on resampled data.", "whyPool": "Standard bagging trains learners independently on resampled data."}], "reference": "Boosting reweighting vs. residual fitting; noise sensitivity — ESL ch.10 (AdaBoost / gradient boosting); ISLR ch.8.", "n": 241}, {"id": "ML-D7G5Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio manager compares a bagged-tree model and a boosted-tree model under a fixed compute budget. The bagged trees train in parallel and mainly stabilize unstable estimates; the boosted trees train sequentially and progressively refine the current prediction.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Bagging is naturally easier to parallelize across base learners than sequential boosting. 218 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Bagging parallelizes naturally because base learners are fit separately; boosting stages depend on the current model.", "whyPool": "Bagged learners can be fit separately, whereas each boosting stage depends on the current model."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Boosting can reduce systematic error by building corrections to the current ensemble.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Boosting reduces systematic error by building corrections to the current ensemble (bias reduction).", "whyPool": "Sequential corrections can fit patterns a single weak learner misses."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Bagging requires each new tree to fit the residuals of all earlier trees.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Fitting residuals of earlier trees characterizes boosting, not bagging — bagged trees do not depend on prior trees.", "whyPool": "Residual-focused sequential fitting characterizes boosting, not ordinary bagging."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Boosting may use shallow trees as weak learners whose combined output is expressive.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Boosting can use shallow weak learners whose combined additive output is expressive and nonlinear.", "whyPool": "Many limited learners can form a strong nonlinear additive model."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The two methods are guaranteed to produce identical forecasts if they use the same number of trees.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The methods differ in sampling, fitting, and aggregation, so equal tree counts do not force identical forecasts.", "whyPool": "Their sampling, fitting, and aggregation procedures differ. G6: Bias vs. Variance in Ensembles"}], "reference": "Bagging vs. boosting (parallel vs. sequential, variance vs. bias) — ISLR ch.8 (§8.2); ESL ch.10 & §15.", "n": 242}, {"id": "ML-D7G6Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A research director repeatedly retrains a deep decision tree on slightly different samples and observes large changes in forecasts. A bagged ensemble of such trees produces much more consistent predictions, although both models retain a tendency to underestimate returns after major policy announcements. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The single deep tree exhibits high variance across training samples.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: large prediction changes from small sample changes indicate high variance.", "whyPool": "Large prediction changes from small sample changes indicate variance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Bagging can reduce the sample sensitivity of the aggregate.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: bagging reduces the aggregate's sample sensitivity by smoothing learner-specific fluctuations.", "whyPool": "Averaging smooths learner-specific fluctuations."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The persistent underestimation suggests a bias that averaging similar trees may not remove.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the persistent underestimation is a systematic bias that averaging similar trees may not remove.", "whyPool": "A common systematic error can remain after averaging."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Reduced variance guarantees that the ensemble is unbiased.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: reduced variance does NOT guarantee an unbiased ensemble — bias and variance are distinct; improving one need not zero the other.", "whyPool": "Variance and bias are distinct; improving one does not ensure the other is zero. 219 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Stability across retraining samples and accuracy relative to true targets describe different properties.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: stability across retraining and accuracy vs. true targets are different properties (a consistently wrong model can be stable).", "whyPool": "A consistently wrong model can be stable, while an accurate-on-average model can be variable."}], "reference": "Bias-variance decomposition in ensembles — ISLR §2.2 & ch.8; ESL ch.7.", "n": 243}, {"id": "ML-D7G6Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A desk averages 200 very shallow trees, each constrained so heavily that it predicts almost the same value for every market state. The head of research expects the large ensemble to recover the nonlinear structure that no member learned.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Averaging can reduce variance but cannot create missing signal when all learners make nearly the same underfit prediction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Averaging reduces variance but cannot create signal absent from every underfit member.", "whyPool": "Aggregation cannot recover structure absent from every prediction."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Strongly underfit base learners can give the ensemble high bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Strongly underfit base learners give the ensemble high bias.", "whyPool": "Excessive constraints can prevent the learners from capturing the relationship."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Merely increasing the number of identical shallow trees must eliminate the bias.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Merely adding more identical shallow trees preserves, not eliminates, the shared bias.", "whyPool": "Repeating the same biased prediction preserves that bias."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Boosting weak learners may reduce bias by adding sequential corrections, subject to tuning and data quality.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Boosting weak learners can reduce bias via sequential corrections (subject to tuning and data quality), expanding effective capacity.", "whyPool": "Additive corrections can expand the model’s effective capacity."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Out-of-sample evaluation is unnecessary once an ensemble contains 200 members.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Out-of-sample evaluation is still required; ensemble size does not replace generalization testing.", "whyPool": "Ensemble size does not replace generalization testing."}], "reference": "High-bias ensembles; boosting for bias reduction — ISLR ch.8; ESL ch.10 & §7.", "n": 244}, {"id": "ML-D7G6Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A model-governance team compares three interventions: bagging unstable deep trees, boosting shallow trees that underfit, and averaging many copies of one deterministic linear model trained on identical data. The reviewer asks which changes address variance, which may address bias, and where benefits are limited.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Bagging the unstable trees is plausibly aimed at variance reduction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Bagging unstable deep trees is plausibly aimed at variance reduction (resampling + aggregation stabilizes them).", "whyPool": "Resampling and aggregation can stabilize high-variance trees."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Boosting shallow learners can reduce bias by building a more expressive combined model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Boosting shallow learners can reduce bias by building a more expressive combined model.", "whyPool": "Sequential additions can capture structure beyond one weak learner."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Averaging identical predictions from the deterministic linear model changes the prediction.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Averaging identical predictions from a deterministic model does not change the prediction — the average of identical values is that value.", "whyPool": "The average of identical values is that same value."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If every ensemble member shares the same omitted-variable error, aggregation may leave it intact.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If every member shares the same omitted-variable error, aggregation may leave that common systematic error intact.", "whyPool": "Common systematic errors do not cancel."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Bias and variance effects should be inferred solely from training error. 220 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Bias/variance behavior cannot be inferred from training error alone; generalization needs out-of-sample evidence.", "whyPool": "Generalization behavior requires validation or other out-of-sample evidence. G7: Failure Modes of Ensembles"}], "reference": "Ensemble interventions vs. bias-variance — ISLR §2.2 & ch.8; ESL ch.7, ch.10.", "n": 245}, {"id": "ML-D7G7Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A hedge fund reports spectacular validation results from an ensemble that uses a “revised earnings” feature. An audit finds that the feature was populated with values released after each predicted trading date, and the CIO argues that averaging many models should protect the result from this problem.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The ensemble’s reported performance may be driven by look-ahead leakage.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Future-released values give models information unavailable at trade time, so reported performance may be leakage-driven.", "whyPool": "Future-released values give the models information unavailable in real trading."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Averaging many leaked models makes the information valid at the historical prediction time.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Averaging many leaked models cannot make future-released information valid at the historical prediction time.", "whyPool": "Aggregation cannot repair invalid temporal availability."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If all members exploit the leaked feature, their agreement can create false confidence rather than independent confirmation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If all members exploit the same leaked feature, their agreement is correlated optimism — false confidence, not independent confirmation.", "whyPool": "Shared leakage produces correlated, systematically optimistic predictions."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The feature should be reconstructed using only values available as of each prediction timestamp.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The feature must be reconstructed using only point-in-time values available as of each prediction timestamp.", "whyPool": "Point-in-time data are necessary for a realistic evaluation."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Strong ensemble performance eliminates the need to audit data provenance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Unexpectedly strong results make data-provenance/leakage auditing *more* important, not unnecessary.", "whyPool": "Unexpectedly strong results make leakage review especially important."}], "reference": "Look-ahead / point-in-time leakage in backtests — López de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (leakage, purged CV); concept \"aggregation cannot repair invalid data.\"", "n": 246}, {"id": "ML-D7G7Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A boosted trading model keeps adding trees until training error is almost zero. Validation performance peaks after 80 trees and then deteriorates, especially around noisy news events; the portfolio manager wants to deploy the 600-tree endpoint because it fits history best. 221 Rev:081020261230 The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The divergence between falling training error and worsening validation performance is evidence of overfitting.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: falling training error alongside worsening validation is classic overfitting — added fit is not transferring to unseen data.", "whyPool": "The added fit is not transferring to unseen data."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Later boosting stages may focus excessively on noise or outliers.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: later boosting stages may focus excessively on persistent noisy/outlier cases.", "whyPool": "Persistently hard noisy cases can attract later corrections."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Selecting the number of boosting stages using time-aware validation could improve generalization.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: choosing the number of stages via time-aware validation improves generalization without relying on final training fit.", "whyPool": "Validation can identify a useful stopping region without relying on final training fit."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Zero training error guarantees the smallest future trading loss.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: zero training error does NOT guarantee the smallest future loss — perfect historical fit can reflect memorization.", "whyPool": "Perfect historical fit can reflect memorization."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Regularization choices such as learning rate, tree depth, or early stopping can affect the failure mode.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: learning rate, tree depth, and early stopping regularize the size/aggressiveness of sequential corrections.", "whyPool": "These controls limit the size or aggressiveness of sequential corrections."}], "reference": "Boosting overfitting, early stopping, regularization — ESL ch.10 (§10.12 regularization, shrinkage); ISLR ch.8 (boosting tuning).", "n": 247}, {"id": "ML-D7G7Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "07", "domainName": "Ensemble Methods", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk ensemble contains 50 trees trained with nearly identical samples and the same dominant features. During a sudden liquidity regime change, all 50 underestimate transaction costs, while the risk officer had assumed the large model count guaranteed diversification.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The following claim is false: A large number of members does not guarantee meaningful model diversity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: the choice asserts \"the claim [a large number of members does not guarantee meaningful diversity] is false.\" The inner claim is accurate (effective diversity is about behavior/errors, not count), so calling it false is inaccurate → False.", "whyPool": "Effective diversity concerns behavior and errors, not just member count."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Highly correlated errors can cause the full ensemble to fail together.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Highly correlated errors survive aggregation, so the full ensemble can fail together.", "whyPool": "Common errors survive aggregation."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Averaging 50 underestimates must produce an unbiased cost estimate.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Averaging 50 systematically low estimates stays systematically low — it does not produce an unbiased cost estimate.", "whyPool": "An average of systematically low estimates can remain systematically low."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Regime shift can expose a weakness shared by every learner because their training experience was similar.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A regime shift exposes a weakness shared by every learner because their training experience (data and features) was similar.", "whyPool": "Shared data and features can lead to the same extrapolation failure. 222 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Stress testing by regime can reveal vulnerabilities that average validation metrics may hide.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Regime/segment stress testing reveals concentrated failures that average validation metrics can hide.", "whyPool": "Segment-level evaluation can uncover concentrated failures in adverse conditions. 223 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Correlated-learner failure modes; regime stress testing — ESL §15.2 (correlation limits); concept \"member count ≠ diversification,\" López de Prado (backtest stress by regime).", "n": 248}, {"id": "ML-D8G1Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A research team divides ten years of market data into a training period, a validation period, and a final test period. It fits candidate models on the training period, uses the validation period to select hyperparameters, and plans to report performance from the test period.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The training set is used to estimate model parameters from observed examples.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Training data are used to estimate model parameters / learned structure from observed examples — the definition of the fitting step.", "whyPool": "Training data are used to fit parameters or learned structure."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The validation set can support model and hyperparameter selection without directly fitting the final test observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The validation set supports model and hyperparameter selection *before* the final test, giving feedback for pre-test decisions without fitting the test observations.", "whyPool": "Validation data provide feedback for choices made before the final test."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The team should repeatedly inspect test results while revising features because the test data are not part of training.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Repeatedly inspecting test results while revising features turns the test set into part of the development loop — even though the rows aren't fed to the fit method, the decisions adapt to it, destroying its out-of-sample value.", "whyPool": "Repeatedly adapting to test results turns the test set into part of the development process."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The test set is most informative as a final evaluation when earlier decisions have not been adapted to its results.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The test set is most informative as a *final* evaluation precisely when earlier decisions were not adapted to its results — an untouched set is a genuine out-of-sample check.", "whyPool": "An untouched test set better represents a final out-of-sample check."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Having a validation set guarantees that the selected model will perform equally well in all future markets.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A validation set cannot guarantee equal future performance in all markets — regime shifts and sampling uncertainty break any such guarantee.", "whyPool": "Market shifts and sampling uncertainty prevent such a guarantee."}], "reference": "Train/validation/test roles & the validation set — ISLR §5.1 (the validation-set approach); scikit-learn \"Cross-validation: evaluating estimator performance\" (train/validation/test split rationale).", "n": 249}, {"id": "ML-D8G1Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A student trains five decision-tree variants, chooses the depth with the lowest validation error, retrains that chosen configuration on the combined training and validation periods, and then evaluates it once on a later test period. 225 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Using validation performance to choose tree depth is an appropriate role for the validation set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Using validation performance to choose tree depth is the standard, intended role of the validation set (hyperparameter selection).", "whyPool": "Hyperparameter selection is a standard use of validation data."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Retraining the chosen configuration on training plus validation data can be reasonable if the test set remains untouched.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "After selection, refitting the chosen configuration on train+validation data is reasonable *provided the test set stays untouched* — it uses more pre-test data for the final fit.", "whyPool": "After selection, more pre-test data may be used to fit the final model."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The test score should be used to choose a different depth whenever it is disappointing.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Choosing a different depth whenever the test score disappoints contaminates the final evaluation by adapting the model to the test set.", "whyPool": "Changing depth in response to test performance contaminates the final evaluation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A later test period can better respect temporal order than a random mixture of earlier and later observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Evaluating on a *later* period respects temporal order and avoids training on observations from the test period's future (a form of look-ahead leakage a random mix would introduce).", "whyPool": "Evaluating on a later period avoids training on observations from the test period’s future."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Model selection on validation data means the validation score is completely free of selection optimism.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Selecting among many candidates on validation data introduces **selection optimism** — the winning validation score is optimistically low, not free of it.", "whyPool": "Trying many alternatives can produce an optimistically low validation error."}], "reference": "Validation for model selection, selection optimism, temporal splits — ISLR §5.1–5.2; concept \"look-ahead bias / walk-forward evaluation.\"", "n": 250}, {"id": "ML-D8G1Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A small trading project has only three years of observations. To preserve data, the analyst skips validation, selects features and model settings by repeatedly checking the test score, and then presents the best observed test result as an unbiased estimate of future performance. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Repeated test-set feedback can influence the analyst’s choices even if the test rows are never passed to the model’s fit method.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: human decisions driven by test feedback create *indirect* adaptation to the test set, even when the test rows are never passed to the fit method.", "whyPool": "Human decisions based on test feedback create indirect adaptation to the test set. 226 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "The reported best test result may be optimistic because the analyst selected it after many attempts.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: selecting the best of many trials exploits chance variation in that particular set, so the reported best result is optimistic.", "whyPool": "Selecting the best of repeated trials exploits chance variation in that particular set."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A separate validation procedure could reduce the amount of model-selection pressure placed on the final test set.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a separate validation procedure can absorb model-selection pressure and reserve the test set for a clean final evaluation.", "whyPool": "Validation data can guide development while reserving the test set for final evaluation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A small dataset makes test-set reuse harmless because fewer observations are available.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: scarce data does **not** make test reuse harmless — fewer observations make contamination worse and estimates less stable, not safer.", "whyPool": "Scarce data do not eliminate contamination and may make estimates even less stable."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The labels of the test observations must be available to calculate prediction error, but they need not be used to fit or tune the model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: test labels are required to compute prediction error (scoring) but should remain outside fitting and tuning.", "whyPool": "Test labels are needed for scoring but should remain outside fitting and tuning. G2: Error Metrics – MAE, MSE, RMSE"}], "reference": "Data leakage / test-set hygiene, optimism of the best-of-many — ISLR §5.1; concept \"multiple-comparisons / selection bias in evaluation.\"", "n": 251}, {"id": "ML-D8G2Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A client reviewing your model asks why RMSE is used instead of MAE. The risk team is concerned about large prediction errors during volatile periods.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "MSE penalizes large errors more than MAE.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "MSE's squared term amplifies large errors, so MSE penalizes big misses more than MAE's linear penalty does.", "whyPool": "The squared term amplifies large errors."}, {"L": "B", "text": "RMSE is less sensitive to outliers than MAE.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "RMSE is **more** (not less) sensitive to outliers/large errors than MAE — it inherits MSE's squaring.", "whyPool": "RMSE is more sensitive to large errors and outliers."}, {"L": "C", "text": "MAE treats all errors equally.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "MAE applies a linear penalty to absolute error magnitude, treating all errors proportionally (equally per unit).", "whyPool": "MAE applies a linear penalty to absolute error magnitude."}, {"L": "D", "text": "RMSE has the same units as the target variable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Taking the square root of MSE restores the target variable's original units, so RMSE shares the target's units.", "whyPool": "Taking the square root restores the target variable’s units."}, {"L": "E", "text": "RMSE highlights large errors more than MAE.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Squaring before averaging places greater emphasis on large deviations, so RMSE highlights large errors more than MAE — matching the risk team's concern.", "whyPool": "Squaring before averaging places greater emphasis on large deviations."}], "reference": "MAE vs. MSE vs. RMSE definitions and outlier sensitivity — scikit-learn \"Metrics and scoring\" (mean_absolute_error, mean_squared_error, RMSE); ISLR §2.2.1 (MSE).", "n": 252}, {"id": "ML-D8G2Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "Two return-forecasting models are evaluated on the same days. Most errors are small, but Model A makes one exceptionally large error during a market shock. The team compares MAE, MSE, and RMSE. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The exceptionally large error will generally have more influence on MSE than on MAE.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: quadratic loss gives the very large miss disproportionate weight, so it influences MSE more than MAE.", "whyPool": "Quadratic loss gives disproportionate weight to a very large miss."}, {"L": "B", "text": "RMSE and MAE can rank the two models differently because they weight error magnitudes differently. 227 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: RMSE and MAE weight error magnitudes differently, so a model with small typical errors but one big miss can look better under MAE and worse under RMSE — the rankings can flip.", "whyPool": "A model with typical small errors but one large miss may look better under MAE and worse under RMSE."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Squaring errors in MSE prevents positive and negative errors from canceling.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: squaring makes errors nonnegative regardless of sign, so positive and negative errors cannot cancel.", "whyPool": "Squared errors are nonnegative regardless of error direction."}, {"L": "D", "text": "RMSE must always be numerically smaller than MAE on the same predictions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: RMSE is generally **at least as large as** MAE for the same errors (RMSE ≥ MAE), so it need not — and generally will not — be numerically smaller.", "whyPool": "RMSE is generally at least as large as MAE for the same set of errors."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Removing the shock day solely because it hurts Model A would change the evaluation question and could bias the comparison.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: removing the shock day solely because it hurts Model A changes the evaluation question and biases the comparison.", "whyPool": "Selective removal based on one model’s performance compromises a fair assessment."}], "reference": "RMSE ≥ MAE inequality, squared vs. absolute loss, evaluation integrity — scikit-learn \"Metrics and scoring\"; ISLR §2.2.1; Jensen's-inequality relationship between mean-absolute and root-mean-square.", "n": 253}, {"id": "ML-D8G2Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A model predicts daily dollar profit for a strategy. Its MAE is $400, its MSE is 490,000 dollars squared, and its RMSE is $700. A manager wants to interpret these values and decide which loss function reflects operational concerns.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The MAE indicates an average absolute prediction error of $400.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "MAE averages the absolute dollar errors, so $400 is the average absolute prediction error.", "whyPool": "MAE averages the absolute dollar errors."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The MSE is expressed in dollars, just like the target.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "MSE is in **squared** units (dollars2), not dollars — 490,000 dollars2 is not directly comparable to the target.", "whyPool": "MSE has squared units, here dollars squared."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The RMSE is expressed in dollars and is therefore easier to compare directly with the target scale than MSE.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "RMSE returns the error to the target's original units ($700 in dollars), so it is easier to compare to the target scale than MSE.", "whyPool": "RMSE returns the error measure to the original target units."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The fact that RMSE exceeds MAE is consistent with variation in the sizes of the errors.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "RMSE exceeding MAE ($700 > $400) reflects dispersion in error sizes — greater spread in absolute errors widens the RMSE–MAE gap.", "whyPool": "Larger dispersion in absolute errors tends to widen the gap between RMSE and MAE."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Choosing MAE rather than RMSE is purely mathematical and need not reflect how costly large misses are.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Choosing MAE vs. RMSE is not purely mathematical — it should reflect whether large misses are disproportionately costly (linear vs. squared penalty).", "whyPool": "Metric choice should reflect whether error costs grow linearly or place extra weight on large misses. 228 Rev:081020261230 G3: Baselines and Benchmarking"}], "reference": "Units of MSE/RMSE and the RMSE–MAE gap as a dispersion signal — scikit-learn \"Metrics and scoring\"; ISLR §2.2.1.", "n": 254}, {"id": "ML-D8G3Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A regression model predicts next-day returns with an RMSE of 0.012. The team celebrates the low number, but it has not compared the model with a predictor that always outputs the training-set mean return.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The RMSE value alone does not show whether the model improves on a simple baseline.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "An RMSE value alone lacks context; it needs the target scale and a relevant comparator to show whether the model beats a simple baseline.", "whyPool": "The magnitude needs context from the target scale and a relevant comparator."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A mean-prediction baseline provides a reference for judging whether the model captures useful predictive structure under squared-error evaluation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Predicting the training mean is the standard naive benchmark for squared-error regression — it reveals whether the model captures useful predictive structure.", "whyPool": "Predicting the training mean is a simple benchmark for squared-error regression."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the model’s test RMSE is worse than the baseline’s on the same observations, the model has not added value according to that metric.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If the model's test RMSE is worse than the baseline's on the same observations, the model added no value under that metric (higher RMSE = worse).", "whyPool": "Higher RMSE indicates worse performance under that evaluation criterion."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A baseline must use a more complex algorithm than the candidate model to be informative.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A baseline need not be more complex — baselines are deliberately simple; a trivial constant predictor is exactly the point.", "whyPool": "Baselines are often deliberately simple."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The baseline’s mean should be calculated using the test targets before predictions are made.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Computing the baseline mean from the **test** targets leaks information from the evaluation set; the mean must come from training data.", "whyPool": "Using test targets to construct predictions leaks information from the evaluation set."}], "reference": "Naive/mean baseline for regression, leakage of test statistics — scikit-learn \"DummyRegressor\" (strategy='mean') and \"Metrics and scoring\"; concept \"skill relative to a baseline.\"", "n": 255}, {"id": "ML-D8G3Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A classifier predicts whether tomorrow’s market return will be positive. Because 60% of test days are positive, an always- up rule achieves 60% accuracy, while the new model achieves 58%.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The always-up rule is a relevant naive accuracy baseline for this imbalanced outcome.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Always predicting the majority class is the standard naive accuracy baseline for an imbalanced outcome.", "whyPool": "Always predicting the majority class establishes a simple reference accuracy."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The model’s 58% accuracy beats the always-up baseline because it uses features.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "58% is **below** the 60% baseline — using features does not make it a win on this metric.", "whyPool": "Fifty-eight percent is below the baseline’s 60% on this metric."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Comparing against the baseline reveals information that the model’s accuracy alone does not.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The baseline comparison exposes that apparent accuracy may not exceed a trivial rule — information the raw 58% hides.", "whyPool": "The comparison shows that apparent predictive accuracy may not exceed a trivial rule."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The model might still be useful under a different objective, but that claim would require an appropriate metric and evidence.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The model *might* be useful under a different objective (e.g., asymmetric payoffs), but that requires an appropriate metric and evidence, not assumption.", "whyPool": "For example, asymmetric payoffs could matter, but they must be evaluated rather than assumed."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A sophisticated model is automatically preferable to a naive rule even when it performs worse on the stated objective.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A sophisticated model is not automatically preferable when it underperforms on the stated objective — complexity alone does not establish value.", "whyPool": "Complexity alone does not establish value."}], "reference": "Accuracy baselines & class imbalance — scikit-learn \"DummyClassifier\" (strategy='most_frequent'), \"Metrics and scoring\" (accuracy caveats on imbalanced data); ISLR ch.4 (confusion matrices).", "n": 256}, {"id": "ML-D8G3Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A trading team compares a learned strategy with buy-and-hold. The learned strategy has a higher cumulative return in the test period but also takes substantially more risk and incurs frequent transaction costs that were omitted from the simulation. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Buy-and-hold can serve as one useful benchmark for a trading strategy.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: buy-and-hold is a simple, investable alternative and a useful benchmark for a trading strategy.", "whyPool": "It represents a simple investable alternative for comparison."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Higher raw return alone proves the learned strategy is superior under every reasonable objective.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: higher raw return does **not** prove superiority under every objective — risk, costs, drawdowns, and the investor's goal can reverse the conclusion.", "whyPool": "Risk, costs, drawdowns, and the investor’s objective can change the conclusion."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A cash or no-trade benchmark may provide additional context beyond buy-and-hold.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a cash / no-trade benchmark adds context beyond buy-and-hold, helping reveal what kind of value the strategy adds.", "whyPool": "Multiple simple alternatives can reveal what kind of value the strategy adds."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Omitting transaction costs can overstate the apparent value added by a high-turnover strategy.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: omitting transaction costs overstates value added, and frequent (high-turnover) trading magnifies the effect of those unmodeled costs.", "whyPool": "Frequent trading magnifies the effect of unmodeled costs."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Benchmarks should be evaluated over the same market period as the learned strategy when making a direct comparison.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: benchmarks should span the same market period as the strategy so the comparison controls for market conditions.", "whyPool": "A common period controls for differences in market conditions. G4: Fair Model Comparison"}], "reference": "Trading benchmarks (buy-and-hold, cash), transaction-cost realism — Grinold & Kahn \"Active Portfolio Management\" (benchmark-relative evaluation); concept \"backtest cost modeling / turnover.\"", "n": 257}, {"id": "ML-D8G4Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two analysts compare a linear model and a decision tree. The linear model is tested on a calm recent year, while the tree is tested on an earlier crisis year. Both report RMSE, and the team declares the model with the lower value the winner. 230 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Using different test periods confounds model quality with differences in market conditions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Different test periods confound model quality with market difficulty — one period may be intrinsically harder to predict.", "whyPool": "One period may be intrinsically harder to predict than the other."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The models should generally be scored on the same held-out observations for a direct comparison.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Models should be scored on the same held-out observations so both face a consistent evaluation challenge.", "whyPool": "Common observations provide a consistent evaluation challenge."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Using RMSE for both models is sufficient to make the comparison fair despite different test data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A shared metric (RMSE for both) does not correct for unequal test conditions — same metric, different data is still unfair.", "whyPool": "A common metric does not correct for unequal test conditions."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A paired comparison on the same observations can reveal which model errs on particular cases.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A paired comparison on identical observations enables case-by-case analysis of which model errs where.", "whyPool": "Matching observations supports case-by-case analysis of error differences."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The crisis-period model must be better if it was trained with more rows.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A larger training set does not by itself establish better test performance — training size alone proves nothing.", "whyPool": "Training-set size alone does not establish test performance."}], "reference": "Common held-out data for fair comparison — scikit-learn \"Cross-validation\" (same folds across estimators); ISLR §5 (comparing models on identical resamples).", "n": 258}, {"id": "ML-D8G4Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A team compares KNN with a regression tree. It standardizes all features using means and standard deviations computed from the full dataset before splitting, then trains both models and reports test error. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Computing scaling statistics from the full dataset exposes the comparison to information from the test set.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: computing scaling statistics from the full dataset uses the distribution of held-out observations, exposing the comparison to test information.", "whyPool": "The preprocessing step uses the distribution of held-out observations."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Scaling parameters should be estimated from the training data and then applied unchanged to validation and test data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: scaling parameters should be estimated on training data and applied unchanged to validation/test, keeping test information out of the fitted transform.", "whyPool": "This keeps test information out of the fitted transformation."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because trees are usually insensitive to monotonic feature scaling, leakage in the shared preprocessing is acceptable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: test leakage is a methodological flaw even if one learner (trees) is largely insensitive to monotonic scaling — it is not acceptable.", "whyPool": "Test leakage remains a methodological flaw even if one learner is less sensitive to scaling."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Applying a consistent, leakage-free preprocessing protocol supports a fairer comparison.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a consistent, leakage-free preprocessing protocol appropriate to each learner supports a fairer comparison.", "whyPool": "Models should be compared under a valid protocol appropriate to each learner. 231 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "KNN may be affected more directly by feature scaling than a decision tree because KNN relies on distances.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: KNN is affected more directly by feature scaling because it relies on distances, where scale changes each feature's relative contribution.", "whyPool": "Scale affects the relative contribution of features to KNN distance."}], "reference": "Preprocessing inside the CV/train fold to prevent leakage — scikit-learn \"Pipeline\" and \"Common pitfalls: data leakage\"; ISLR §5 (fit transforms on training data only); KNN distance scaling (ISLR §2.2.3).", "n": 259}, {"id": "ML-D8G4Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher reports MAE for Model A and RMSE for Model B, then selects whichever displayed number is smaller. Model A predicts in percentage points, while Model B’s output was converted to decimal returns.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Comparing the displayed MAE and RMSE values directly mixes different loss definitions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "MAE and RMSE weight the error distribution differently, so comparing the two displayed numbers mixes different loss definitions.", "whyPool": "MAE and RMSE weight the error distribution differently."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Different output units make the raw numerical values incomparable without conversion.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Different output units make raw values incomparable without conversion — one percentage point equals 0.01 in decimal returns.", "whyPool": "One percentage point equals 0.01 in decimal-return units."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Both models should be evaluated with the same chosen metric and units on the same observations for a direct ranking.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Both models should use the same chosen metric and units on the same observations, which isolates model performance from evaluation choices.", "whyPool": "Consistency isolates model performance from evaluation choices."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A smaller displayed number always means better predictions regardless of metric and scale.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A smaller number does not always mean better predictions — metric definition and scale determine what the number means.", "whyPool": "Metric definition and units determine what a number means."}, {"L": "E", "text": "It can be reasonable to report multiple metrics, provided each metric is calculated consistently for both models and interpreted separately.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Reporting multiple metrics is fine *if* each is computed consistently for both models and interpreted separately — different measures illuminate different tradeoffs.", "whyPool": "Multiple consistently calculated measures can illuminate different tradeoffs. G5: Metric Selection and Business Alignment"}], "reference": "Metric/units consistency for fair ranking — scikit-learn \"Metrics and scoring\" (choose one scorer per comparison); unit-conversion / scale-invariance considerations.", "n": 260}, {"id": "ML-D8G5Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A model has excellent next-day price RMSE, but a strategy that trades on its forecasts loses money after bid-ask spreads, commissions, and market impact. The research lead argues that prediction accuracy is all that matters. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Low prediction RMSE does not guarantee profitable trading after costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: execution costs and decision rules sit between predictions and profit, so low RMSE does not guarantee profitable trading.", "whyPool": "Execution costs and decision rules intervene between predictions and profit."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A trading-oriented evaluation may need to include returns, risk, turnover, and transaction costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: a trading-oriented evaluation may need returns, risk, turnover, and transaction costs to connect to the deployed objective.", "whyPool": "These measures connect evaluation to the deployed objective and constraints."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Improving RMSE by correcting small forecasts that never change a trade can have little effect on strategy performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: improving RMSE on small forecasts that never flip a trade has little effect — a statistical gain need not alter any economically relevant decision.", "whyPool": "A statistical improvement need not alter any economically relevant decision."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Statistical prediction metrics should be discarded entirely whenever the final application is trading. 232 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: statistical prediction metrics should **not** be discarded entirely for trading — they remain diagnostically useful even when insufficient.", "whyPool": "Prediction metrics remain diagnostically useful even when they are not sufficient."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The evaluation criterion should reflect how model outputs are converted into positions and outcomes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the evaluation criterion should reflect how model outputs convert into positions and outcomes (the full decision pipeline).", "whyPool": "Business value depends on the full decision pipeline."}], "reference": "Statistical metric vs. economic objective, net-of-cost evaluation — Grinold & Kahn \"Active Portfolio Management\"; concept \"signal vs. execution / implementation shortfall.\"", "n": 261}, {"id": "ML-D8G5Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk system predicts daily portfolio losses. The firm considers a $100 error on an ordinary day manageable but views a $10,000 underestimate during a stressed day as especially dangerous.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "An error measure that places extra weight on large misses may better reflect the stated concern than MAE alone.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A measure that places extra weight on large misses (e.g., squared error) better reflects the firm's disproportionate concern for severe misses than MAE alone.", "whyPool": "The firm assigns disproportionate importance to severe misses."}, {"L": "B", "text": "MSE or RMSE can emphasize large absolute errors through squaring.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "MSE/RMSE emphasize large absolute errors through squaring, magnifying the contribution of the big misses.", "whyPool": "Squaring magnifies the contribution of large errors."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A symmetric metric treats overestimates and underestimates of equal magnitude the same, even if the firm considers underestimation more harmful.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A symmetric metric treats equal-magnitude over- and under-estimates the same, even though the firm considers underestimation more harmful — MAE/MSE/RMSE ignore error direction.", "whyPool": "MAE, MSE, and RMSE do not distinguish error direction by themselves."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The lowest average error automatically identifies the safest model under all tail-risk objectives.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The lowest average error does not automatically identify the safest model under tail-risk objectives — averages can conceal dangerous behavior in critical cases.", "whyPool": "Average performance can conceal dangerous behavior in critical cases."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The team may need an asymmetric or tail-focused evaluation in addition to standard aggregate error metrics.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The team may need an asymmetric or tail-focused evaluation in addition to standard aggregate metrics to capture unequal, tail-specific costs.", "whyPool": "Additional measures can capture the firm’s unequal and tail-specific costs."}], "reference": "Asymmetric loss & tail-risk metrics — concept \"expected shortfall / quantile (pinball) loss / cost-sensitive evaluation\"; scikit-learn \"Metrics and scoring\" (custom/asymmetric scorers).", "n": 262}, {"id": "ML-D8G5Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A model predicts the correct direction of tomorrow’s return on 55% of days. Most correct calls occur on tiny moves, while it is often wrong on large moves. Another model has 52% directional accuracy but captures several large profitable moves. 233 Rev:081020261230 The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Directional accuracy weights each day equally regardless of the size of the market move.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: directional accuracy counts a correct tiny move and a correct large move each as one correct classification, weighting days equally regardless of move size.", "whyPool": "A correct tiny move and a correct large move each count as one correct classification."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The 55% model must produce the greater trading profit because it is correct more often.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: being correct more often does **not** guarantee greater trading profit — frequency of correct direction does not determine payoff magnitude.", "whyPool": "Frequency of correct direction does not determine the payoff magnitude."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Return magnitude, position sizing, and trading costs can make the 52% model more economically valuable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: return magnitude, position sizing, and trading costs can make the 52% model more economically valuable.", "whyPool": "Trading outcomes depend on more than the percentage of correct signs."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Evaluating only directional accuracy may be misaligned with a profit-oriented objective.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: evaluating only directional accuracy may be misaligned with a profit objective because it ignores the consequences of being right or wrong.", "whyPool": "The metric may ignore the consequences of being right or wrong."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Choosing an economically aligned metric removes the need to evaluate the strategy out of sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: choosing an economically aligned metric does **not** remove the need for disciplined out-of-sample evaluation — any metric still requires OOS testing.", "whyPool": "Any chosen metric still requires disciplined out-of-sample evaluation. G6: Variability and Stability of Results"}], "reference": "Directional accuracy vs. profit-weighted / magnitude-aware evaluation, and the necessity of OOS testing — concept \"hit-rate vs. P&L\"; ISLR §5 (out-of-sample validation).", "n": 263}, {"id": "ML-D8G6Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A decision tree achieves test RMSE values of 0.010, 0.011, 0.029, 0.013, and 0.026 across five reasonable time-based splits. The analyst wants to report only the lowest value because it demonstrates the model’s potential. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The range of results suggests that performance is sensitive to the evaluation period.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: the substantial spread across plausible windows shows performance is sensitive to the evaluation period.", "whyPool": "The errors vary substantially across plausible evaluation windows."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Reporting only 0.010 would conceal important information about variability.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: reporting only 0.010 conceals variability — that single value is not representative of all trials.", "whyPool": "The selected result is not representative of all reported trials."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Summarizing both central performance and dispersion across splits gives a fuller picture than reporting the best run alone.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: summarizing both central performance and dispersion gives a fuller, more honest picture than the best run alone.", "whyPool": "Typical performance and uncertainty are both relevant to reliability."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Variation across splits proves that every implementation of the model is incorrect.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Wrong: variation across splits does **not** prove every implementation is incorrect — it can arise from sampling and regime differences without any defect.", "whyPool": "Variation can arise from sampling and regime differences without an implementation defect."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Differences in market regimes could contribute to the observed instability.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct: differing market regimes (changing relationships/volatility over time) can contribute to the observed instability.", "whyPool": "Market relationships and volatility can change over time."}], "reference": "Cross-validation variability, report mean ± spread not the best fold — ISLR §5.1 (variance of the validation estimate); scikit-learn \"Cross-validation\" (cv score mean and std).", "n": 264}, {"id": "ML-D8G6Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A bagged learner is retrained ten times using different random seeds. Its validation scores are nearly identical, while a single deep tree’s scores vary widely. Their average validation errors are similar.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The bagged learner appears more stable with respect to the tested source of randomness.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The bagged learner is more stable with respect to the tested randomness — its scores barely change when the seed changes.", "whyPool": "Its scores change little when the seed changes."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Similar average error means the two models have identical reliability.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Similar average error does not mean identical reliability — models with the same mean can have very different dispersion.", "whyPool": "Models with the same mean can have very different dispersion."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Random seeds should be recorded when they affect resampling or model construction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Random seeds that affect resampling or model construction should be recorded to improve reproducibility and clarify experimental conditions.", "whyPool": "Recording seeds improves reproducibility and clarifies experimental conditions."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Repeated runs can help distinguish a typical result from a lucky initialization or sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Repeated runs distinguish a typical result from a lucky initialization or sample — a single outcome cannot reveal that sensitivity.", "whyPool": "Multiple runs reveal sensitivity that a single outcome cannot."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Low variation across seeds guarantees stability under future changes in market regime.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Low variation across seeds does not guarantee stability under future regime change — seed stability tests randomness, not distributional shift.", "whyPool": "Seed stability does not test robustness to distributional change."}], "reference": "Bagging variance reduction, seed/reproducibility, repeated evaluation — ISLR §8.2 (bagging reduces variance); scikit-learn \"Ensemble methods\" and reproducibility (random_state).", "n": 265}, {"id": "ML-D8G6Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Model Evaluation & Metrics", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3, 4], "weekMax": 4, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two models have the same mean out-of-sample MAE across six sequential test windows. Model A’s MAE is similar in every window, while Model B is excellent in four windows and very poor in two volatile windows. The system will be used where large performance swings are costly.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Equal mean MAE does not imply equal stability across windows.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Equal mean MAE does not imply equal stability — the same average can arise from very different distributions of performance.", "whyPool": "The same average can arise from different distributions of performance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Model A may be preferable if consistency is an important operational objective.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Model A may be preferable because its lower variability aligns with the stated cost of large swings.", "whyPool": "Lower variability aligns with the stated cost of large swings."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Examining window-level results can reveal failure patterns hidden by the overall average.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Examining window-level results exposes failure patterns (Model B's two bad windows) that the overall average conceals.", "whyPool": "Aggregation can conceal regime-specific weakness."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Model B’s strong performance in four windows guarantees that its two poor windows are irrelevant. 235 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Strong performance in four windows does **not** make the two poor windows irrelevant — poor behavior in volatile periods can be operationally critical.", "whyPool": "Poor performance during volatile periods may be operationally important."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The choice between the models may reasonably depend on both average error and variability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The choice may reasonably depend on both average error and variability, reflecting typical accuracy and stability requirements.", "whyPool": "Evaluation should reflect typical accuracy and stability requirements. 236 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Mean vs. dispersion of performance, regime-specific weakness — ISLR §5.1 (variance of estimates); concept \"consistency / drawdown-aware model selection.\"", "n": 266}, {"id": "ML-D9G1Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Your team presents a model with near-zero training error but much worse performance on new data. The PM asks whether this is acceptable.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This indicates overfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Near-zero training error with poor performance on unseen data is the textbook overfitting signature — the model fit sample-specific noise rather than the underlying signal.", "whyPool": "Near-zero training error combined with poor performance on new data is the classic overfitting pattern."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The model likely has high variance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Strong sensitivity to the particular training sample is the defining trait of high variance, which is what overfitting expresses.", "whyPool": "Strong sensitivity to the particular training sample is characteristic of high variance."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Increasing training data may help.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "More representative training data makes sample-specific noise harder to fit and typically shrinks the generalization gap (variance-reduction).", "whyPool": "More representative training data can make it harder for the model to fit sample-specific noise."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Reducing model complexity may help.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Reducing model capacity/complexity limits the model's ability to memorize noise, directly attacking overfitting.", "whyPool": "Reducing capacity can limit the model’s ability to fit noise."}, {"L": "E", "text": "This indicates underfitting.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Underfitting produces poor performance even on the *training* data; here training error is near zero, so this is not underfitting.", "whyPool": "Underfitting normally produces poor performance even on the training data."}], "reference": "Overfitting & the bias-variance tradeoff — ISLR §2.2 (assessing model accuracy, U-shaped test error); ESL ch.7 (model assessment).", "n": 267}, {"id": "ML-D9G1Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A student fits a shallow decision tree to a nonlinear relationship between market indicators and next-day returns. Both training and test errors are high and similar, and increasing the training sample without changing the tree produces little improvement. 238 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The similar, high training and test errors are consistent with underfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Similarly high training and test errors are the hallmark of underfitting — the model is too simple to fit even the data it has seen.", "whyPool": "Underfit models often perform poorly on both seen and unseen observations."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The tree may have insufficient capacity to represent the relevant relationship.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A shallow tree has limited capacity and may be unable to express the relevant nonlinear pattern (high bias).", "whyPool": "A tree that is too shallow may be unable to express an important nonlinear pattern."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Making the tree shallower would necessarily correct the problem.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Making the tree *shallower* reduces capacity further and would generally worsen underfitting, not correct it.", "whyPool": "Further reducing capacity would generally worsen an underfitting problem."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A modest increase in model complexity could reduce bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Adding modest capacity (more depth / richer features) can capture missed structure and reduce bias.", "whyPool": "Additional capacity may capture structure that the current model misses."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A small train–test gap by itself proves that the model generalizes well.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A small train–test gap alone only shows consistency; two similarly poor scores do not demonstrate useful generalization.", "whyPool": "Two similarly poor scores indicate consistency, not useful predictive performance."}], "reference": "Underfitting / high bias, capacity of trees — ISLR §2.2 (bias-variance); ISLR ch.8 (tree depth as complexity).", "n": 268}, {"id": "ML-D9G1Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Three models are evaluated on the same return-prediction task. Model A has high training and high validation error. Model B has very low training error but high validation error. Model C has moderate, similar training and validation error and outperforms both on validation data.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Model A shows a pattern consistent with underfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "High error even on training data means Model A is not capturing enough structure — underfitting / high bias.", "whyPool": "High error even on training data suggests that Model A is not capturing enough structure."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Model B shows a pattern consistent with overfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Excellent training fit with poor validation performance is the standard overfitting (high-variance) symptom.", "whyPool": "Excellent training fit with poor validation performance is a standard overfitting symptom."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Model C currently provides the strongest evidence of generalization among the three.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Model C has the best validation performance with no large train–validation gap, giving the strongest evidence of generalization.", "whyPool": "Model C has the best validation performance without a large gap from training performance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Model B should be preferred because the lowest training error always identifies the best model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Lowest training error rewards memorization and does not establish performance on new data; it never \"always\" identifies the best model.", "whyPool": "Training error alone rewards memorization and does not establish performance on new data."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The train–validation gap is useful only for classification models, not regression models.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The train–validation gap is informative for both regression and classification, not classification only.", "whyPool": "Training and validation behavior is informative for both regression and classification. 239 Rev:081020261230 G2: Bias–Variance Tradeoff"}], "reference": "Diagnosing over/underfitting from train vs. validation curves — ESL ch.7 (generalization gap); scikit-learn \"Underfitting vs. overfitting.\"", "n": 269}, {"id": "ML-D9G2Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "An analyst gradually increases the maximum depth of a decision tree. Training error falls at every step, while validation error first falls and then rises after the tree becomes deep. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The shallowest trees may have relatively high bias.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Very shallow trees systematically miss useful structure — relatively high bias. The statement is correct.", "whyPool": "Very limited trees may systematically miss useful structure."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Very deep trees may have relatively high variance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Very deep trees become sensitive to noise and small training-set changes — relatively high variance. Correct.", "whyPool": "Deep trees can become sensitive to noise and small changes in the training set."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The depth with the lowest validation error may be a better choice than the depth with the lowest training error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Validation error better reflects the goal of performing on unseen data, so the lowest-validation depth is a better choice than lowest-training depth. Correct.", "whyPool": "Validation performance better reflects the goal of performing well on unseen data."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Increasing depth must reduce both bias and variance simultaneously.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: greater capacity typically *reduces bias while increasing variance* — it does not reduce both simultaneously.", "whyPool": "Greater capacity often reduces bias while increasing variance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The rise in validation error suggests that additional capacity has begun to harm generalization.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Worsening validation despite improving training fit is evidence of overfitting. Correct.", "whyPool": "Worsening validation performance despite improving training fit is evidence of overfitting."}], "reference": "Bias-variance tradeoff, U-shaped validation curve — ISLR §2.2.2; scikit-learn \"Validation curves.\"", "n": 270}, {"id": "ML-D9G2Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A team compares a straight-line model with a flexible nonlinear learner across many independently sampled training sets. The linear model makes similar predictions each time but repeatedly misses a curved relationship. The nonlinear learner fits the curve on average but changes substantially across samples. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The linear model’s systematic miss is evidence of relatively high bias.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "The linear model's repeated, systematic miss of the same structure is high bias. Correct.", "whyPool": "Repeatedly missing the same structural pattern indicates systematic error."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The nonlinear learner’s sensitivity across samples is evidence of relatively high variance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "The nonlinear learner's large swings across plausible samples are high variance. Correct.", "whyPool": "Large prediction changes across plausible training samples indicate variance."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A model can have low variance yet still make consistently inaccurate predictions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A low-variance model can still be consistently inaccurate when too restrictive (stable but biased). Correct.", "whyPool": "Stable predictions can be consistently wrong when the model is too restrictive."}, {"L": "D", "text": "High variance means that a model must have high training error on every sample. 240 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: high variance does NOT require high training error — a high-variance model can fit each training set extremely well yet generalize poorly.", "whyPool": "A high-variance model can fit each training set extremely well while generalizing poorly."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Model selection should consider expected out-of-sample error rather than minimizing either bias or variance in isolation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Selection should balance both error sources via expected out-of-sample error, not minimize bias or variance alone. Correct.", "whyPool": "Useful selection balances sources of error according to performance on new observations."}], "reference": "Bias-variance decomposition — ISLR §2.2.2; ESL ch.7 (bias, variance, and their tradeoff).", "n": 271}, {"id": "ML-D9G2Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A KNN regressor is tested with several values of k. With k = 1, predictions follow individual training observations closely. With a very large k, predictions are nearly constant across the feature space.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The k = 1 model will generally have lower training error than the very-large-k model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With k=1 a point can be its own nearest neighbor, giving extremely low (often near-zero) training error versus a heavily smoothed large-k model.", "whyPool": "A point can be its own nearest neighbor, producing extremely close training fits."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Increasing k usually smooths the prediction function.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Larger k averages over a broader neighborhood, smoothing the prediction function.", "whyPool": "Each prediction averages over a broader neighborhood as k grows."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A very large k can increase bias by averaging away local structure.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Very large k over-smooths and averages away local structure, increasing bias.", "whyPool": "Excessive smoothing may miss real local relationships."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A very small k can increase variance by making predictions sensitive to individual observations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Very small k makes predictions hinge on a few neighbors, so they change sharply across samples — high variance.", "whyPool": "Predictions based on very few neighbors can change sharply with the sample."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Increasing k always increases model capacity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Larger k *reduces* effective flexibility/capacity of KNN; it does not always increase capacity.", "whyPool": "Larger k generally reduces the effective flexibility or capacity of KNN. 241 Rev:081020261230 G3: Model Capacity vs. Data Size"}], "reference": "KNN flexibility vs. k, bias-variance — ISLR §2.2.3 (KNN) and §3.5; scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors.\"", "n": 272}, {"id": "ML-D9G3Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A deep decision tree overfits a training set containing only 300 trading days. The team can collect several years of comparable observations from the same data-generating process.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Additional representative data may reduce the tree’s tendency to fit peculiarities of the original small sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A larger representative sample reduces sensitivity to accidental patterns in the small dataset (variance reduction).", "whyPool": "A larger representative sample can reduce sensitivity to accidental patterns in a small dataset."}, {"L": "B", "text": "More observations can make estimates of recurring patterns more reliable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "More observations make estimates of recurring patterns more reliable by separating signal from noise.", "whyPool": "Repeated evidence helps distinguish persistent structure from noise."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Adding duplicated copies of the same 300 rows provides the same benefit as collecting genuinely new observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Duplicated copies of the same 300 rows add no independent information, so they do not give the benefit of genuinely new data.", "whyPool": "Duplicates add no independent information about the underlying relationship."}, {"L": "D", "text": "More data guarantees that an arbitrarily complex model cannot overfit.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "More data does not *guarantee* an arbitrarily complex model won't overfit — excess capacity, noise, leakage, or poor sampling can still cause it.", "whyPool": "Excessive capacity, noise, leakage, or poor sampling can still produce overfitting."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The usefulness of additional data depends partly on whether it represents the deployment setting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The usefulness of extra data depends on whether it represents the deployment setting; unrepresentative data may not help.", "whyPool": "Large but unrepresentative datasets may not improve performance where the model is used."}], "reference": "Data size vs. variance, representativeness — ISLR §2.2 (more data reduces variance); ESL ch.7; concept \"learning curves.\"", "n": 273}, {"id": "ML-D9G3Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A linear model underfits a strongly curved relationship. The analyst adds one million more observations from the same relationship but retains exactly the same linear feature representation and model form. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "More data alone may not remove error caused by an inability to represent the curve.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "More data alone cannot remove error caused by inability to represent the curve — it doesn't change the model's expressible functions. Correct.", "whyPool": "Additional examples do not change the functions the model class can express."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The model’s limitation is primarily one of capacity rather than sample size.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "The limitation is capacity (hypothesis-class expressiveness), not sample size. Correct.", "whyPool": "The specified linear form cannot capture the relevant curvature."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A richer feature representation or a more expressive model may be needed.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A richer feature representation or more expressive model may be needed to reduce this systematic error. Correct.", "whyPool": "Greater representational capacity may reduce this systematic error."}, {"L": "D", "text": "With enough data, every model class can represent every possible target function.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: data quantity does not let *every* model class represent *every* target function — the hypothesis class still restricts what can be expressed.", "whyPool": "Data quantity does not eliminate restrictions imposed by the hypothesis class."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The larger sample can still improve the precision with which the best linear approximation is estimated.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "More data can still estimate the best linear approximation's parameters more precisely even though approximation bias remains. Correct.", "whyPool": "More data can estimate the model’s parameters more reliably even if approximation bias remains."}], "reference": "Hypothesis-class / approximation error (bias) vs. estimation error — ESL ch.7 (bias-variance, model capacity); ISLR §2.2.", "n": 274}, {"id": "ML-D9G3Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two teams train the same flexible learner. Team A uses 500 clean, relevant observations. Team B uses 50,000 observations, but most come from an old market regime with different relationships from the intended deployment period.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Team B’s larger row count does not automatically make its model more generalizable to the new regime.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A larger row count does not automatically generalize to the new regime — quantity cannot fix wrong-distribution data.", "whyPool": "Quantity cannot fully compensate for observations drawn from the wrong distribution."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Relevant sampling can matter as much as raw dataset size.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Relevant sampling can matter as much as raw size; training data should reflect the relationships the model will face.", "whyPool": "The training examples should reflect the relationships the model will encounter."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Historical regime mismatch can create poor deployment performance even when training error is low.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Regime mismatch (non-stationarity) can cause poor deployment performance even with low training error.", "whyPool": "A model may learn old-regime patterns that no longer apply."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Team A must outperform Team B because smaller datasets always contain less bias.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Smaller datasets do not \"always\" contain less bias; there is no rule guaranteeing Team A's superiority.", "whyPool": "Neither sample size alone nor a general rule guarantees Team A’s superiority."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Evaluating both models on representative out-of-sample data would help assess which dataset supports the intended use.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Evaluating both on representative out-of-sample data reveals which dataset supports the intended use.", "whyPool": "A representative evaluation provides evidence about performance under the target conditions. G4: Cross-Validation and Generalization Estimation"}], "reference": "Distribution shift / non-stationarity, representativeness — ESL ch.7; concept \"regime shift / covariate shift.\"", "n": 275}, {"id": "ML-D9G4Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher performs five-fold cross-validation on a dataset. In each round, the model is trained on four folds and evaluated on the remaining fold, with each fold serving as the evaluation fold once.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Each observation is used for evaluation in one of the five rounds. 243 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Every fold takes exactly one turn as the held-out evaluation subset, so each observation is evaluated once.", "whyPool": "Every fold takes one turn as the held-out evaluation subset."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Each fitted model is trained on all five folds, including its evaluation fold.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The evaluation fold is excluded from training in its round — a model is not trained on its own evaluation fold.", "whyPool": "The evaluation fold is excluded from training in that round."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Averaging the fold scores can provide a less split-specific estimate than relying on one arbitrary holdout.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Averaging over multiple partitions gives a less split-specific estimate than a single arbitrary holdout.", "whyPool": "Multiple partitions reveal how performance changes with the split."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Cross-validation produces five evaluations of one unchanged fitted model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A separate model is fit for each training-fold combination; CV is not five evaluations of one fixed model.", "whyPool": "A separate model is fitted for each training-fold combination."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Model preprocessing that learns from data should be fitted within each training portion rather than once on the full dataset.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Data-learning preprocessing must be fit within each training portion; fitting it globally leaks evaluation-fold information.", "whyPool": "Fitting preprocessing globally can leak information from evaluation folds into training."}], "reference": "k-fold cross-validation — ISLR §5.1 (cross-validation); scikit-learn \"Cross-validation\" + \"data leakage in preprocessing.\"", "n": 276}, {"id": "ML-D9G4Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A student uses cross-validation to select tree depth from 20 alternatives. The student reports the best mean cross- validation score as the final, unbiased estimate of performance and does not retain a separate test set. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Cross-validation can support the selection of tree depth.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing validation performance across depths is a valid use of CV. Correct.", "whyPool": "Comparing validation performance across depths is a valid use of cross-validation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Choosing the best of many alternatives can make the selected cross-validation score optimistic.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Choosing the best of many alternatives makes the selected CV score optimistic (selection bias). Correct.", "whyPool": "Selection can exploit chance advantages in the folds used for tuning."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A separate untouched test set can provide a stronger final check after model selection.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A separate untouched test set gives a stronger final check by not reusing evidence for selection and assessment. Correct.", "whyPool": "An untouched test set avoids reusing the same evidence for selection and final assessment."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Cross-validation guarantees that the selected depth will generalize equally well to all future periods.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: CV cannot *guarantee* the selected depth generalizes equally well to all future periods — sampling uncertainty and market change forbid such a guarantee.", "whyPool": "Sampling uncertainty and market change prevent such a guarantee."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The folds should be constructed in a way that respects the structure of the data and prediction task.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Folds should respect the data's structure (e.g., time order); ordinary random folds may be inappropriate. Correct.", "whyPool": "Time order or other dependencies may make ordinary random folds inappropriate. 244 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Selection bias / optimism of the tuned score, held-out test set — ISLR §5.1 & §6 (model selection); ESL ch.7 (the \"training-set reuse\" fallacy).", "n": 277}, {"id": "ML-D9G4Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A forecasting team randomly assigns daily observations from ten years of market data to cross-validation folds. As a result, models are sometimes trained on later dates and evaluated on earlier dates, even though deployment will predict the future from the past.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Random folds can create an evaluation that does not match the temporal direction of deployment.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Random folds let future information influence models evaluated on the past — mismatching deployment's temporal direction.", "whyPool": "The random split can allow future information to influence models evaluated on the past."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Time-aware folds that train on earlier observations and validate on later observations may be more appropriate.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Time-aware (forward/rolling) folds that train on earlier and validate on later data better mirror forecasting use.", "whyPool": "Forward or rolling splits better mirror forecasting use."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Using future observations for training can produce misleading estimates when relationships change over time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Using future observations for training can mislead when relationships change over time (non-stationarity).", "whyPool": "Regime changes can make access to later patterns an unrealistic advantage."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Cross-validation is invalid for every time-series problem.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "CV is not invalid for every time-series problem — it can be adapted (blocked/rolling) to preserve time order.", "whyPool": "Cross-validation can be adapted to preserve time order."}, {"L": "E", "text": "More folds automatically correct any violation of temporal order.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Increasing the fold count does not repair an inappropriate splitting design; the leakage is structural.", "whyPool": "Increasing the fold count does not repair an inappropriate splitting design. G5: Detecting Overfitting in Practice"}], "reference": "Time-series cross-validation / look-ahead leakage — scikit-learn \"TimeSeriesSplit\"; concept \"walk-forward validation.\"", "n": 278}, {"id": "ML-D9G5Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A model reports 99.9% directional accuracy on unseen market data, far above every prior experiment. The analyst discovers that features were normalized using statistics calculated from the full dataset and that one feature was constructed using the next day’s closing price. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The extraordinary result should prompt checks for leakage and evaluation errors.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "An implausibly strong result should prompt checks for leakage and evaluation errors. Correct.", "whyPool": "Results that are implausibly strong warrant careful examination of data and procedure."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Using the next day’s closing price to predict next-day direction gives the model information unavailable at decision time.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Using the next day's close to predict next-day direction feeds the model information unavailable at decision time (target leakage). Correct.", "whyPool": "The feature directly incorporates future information about the target period."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Full-dataset normalization can contaminate the evaluation because test-period information influences preprocessing.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Full-dataset normalization contaminates evaluation because test-period info influences preprocessing. Correct.", "whyPool": "Even unsupervised preprocessing should be learned without access to held-out observations."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A very high score proves the model has discovered a durable market inefficiency. 245 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: a very high score does NOT prove a durable market inefficiency — leakage is the more immediate explanation and the result needs validation.", "whyPool": "Leakage provides a more immediate explanation and the result requires validation."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Rebuilding the pipeline using only information available at each training and prediction point is necessary for a credible assessment.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Rebuilding the pipeline to use only information available at each point is necessary for a credible assessment. Correct.", "whyPool": "The experiment must reproduce the information boundaries of actual use."}], "reference": "Data leakage (target and preprocessing) — scikit-learn \"Common pitfalls: data leakage\"; concept \"look-ahead bias.\"", "n": 279}, {"id": "ML-D9G5Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A single decision tree is retrained on five slightly different samples. Its training error remains near zero, but its chosen splits and out-of-sample returns change dramatically from run to run.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The changing structure and performance are signs of model instability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Small data changes producing large changes in the learned model and its results is model instability.", "whyPool": "Small data changes are producing large changes in the learned model and its results."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Near-zero training error rules out overfitting.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Near-zero training error does not rule out overfitting — extremely low training error often accompanies it.", "whyPool": "Extremely low training error can accompany severe overfitting."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Sensitivity to small sample changes is consistent with high variance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Sensitivity to small sample changes is a defining symptom of high variance.", "whyPool": "Sample sensitivity is a defining symptom of variance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Examining results across repeated samples reveals information that one run would hide.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Examining results across repeated samples exposes variability that a single run would hide.", "whyPool": "Repeated trials expose the range and reliability of performance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The most profitable run should be reported alone because it shows the model’s true expected performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reporting only the most profitable run conceals variability and creates a misleadingly optimistic impression.", "whyPool": "Selecting the best run conceals variability and creates an optimistic impression."}], "reference": "Model stability / variance under resampling — ESL ch.7 & ch.8 (tree instability, motivation for bagging); concept \"bootstrap stability.\"", "n": 280}, {"id": "ML-D9G5Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An analyst tries 500 combinations of indicators, lookback periods, and model settings on one validation period. One combination earns an exceptional Sharpe ratio, but its training score is ordinary and its performance collapses on a later test period. 246 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Trying many alternatives increases the chance of finding a configuration that benefits from random validation noise.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Trying many alternatives raises the chance of finding a config that benefits from random validation noise (multiple-comparisons effect).", "whyPool": "A broad search can identify a chance winner even when no durable advantage exists."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The later test failure is consistent with selection overfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The later test failure is consistent with selection overfitting — the config adapted to validation-period peculiarities.", "whyPool": "The selected configuration may have adapted to peculiarities of the validation period."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The best validation result among 500 trials is necessarily an unbiased estimate of future performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The best of 500 trials is not an unbiased estimate; taking the maximum observed score introduces selection optimism.", "whyPool": "Choosing the maximum observed score introduces selection optimism."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Recording the full search process and evaluating the selected model on untouched data would improve the credibility of the conclusion.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Recording the full search and evaluating the pick on untouched data improves credibility by testing for a durable edge.", "whyPool": "Transparency and fresh evaluation data help distinguish stable performance from data mining."}, {"L": "E", "text": "An ordinary training score guarantees that the validation success was genuine.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "An ordinary training score does not guarantee the validation success was genuine; search-driven results still need fresh validation.", "whyPool": "Training behavior does not validate an exceptional result found through repeated search. G6: Controlling Model Complexity"}], "reference": "Multiple-comparisons / backtest overfitting — concept \"data snooping, deflated Sharpe ratio\"; ISLR §5–6 (selection with a held-out set).", "n": 281}, {"id": "ML-D9G6Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A deep decision tree fits nearly every fluctuation in the training data but performs poorly on validation data. The team considers reducing maximum depth and increasing the minimum number of observations required in each leaf. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Reducing maximum depth limits the number of successive splits the tree can make.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Reducing max depth limits the number of successive splits — fewer levels to partition the data. Correct.", "whyPool": "A shallower tree has fewer levels available to partition the data."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Increasing the minimum leaf size can prevent the tree from creating leaves for very small groups of observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A larger minimum leaf size prevents leaves for very small groups, discouraging over-specific partitions. Correct.", "whyPool": "Larger leaves discourage highly specific partitions. 247 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Both changes can reduce the tree’s effective complexity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Both changes reduce the tree's effective complexity. Correct.", "whyPool": "Each constraint limits the tree’s ability to fit fine-grained sample variation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "These changes are guaranteed to improve test performance for every dataset.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: these changes are NOT guaranteed to improve test performance on every dataset — excessive restriction can cause underfitting, and results are data-dependent.", "whyPool": "Excessive restriction can cause underfitting, and results depend on the data."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Complexity controls should be selected using appropriate validation evidence rather than test-set feedback.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Complexity controls should be chosen using validation evidence, keeping the final test set independent. Correct.", "whyPool": "The final test set should remain independent of hyperparameter selection."}], "reference": "Tree pruning / complexity control hyperparameters — ISLR ch.8 (pruning, min node size); scikit-learn \"Decision Trees: controlling complexity.\"", "n": 282}, {"id": "ML-D9G6Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A team uses regularized linear regression with a penalty on large coefficient values. It gradually increases the penalty strength while monitoring training and validation error.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The penalty discourages the model from relying on extremely large coefficients.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The penalty makes large coefficient magnitudes costly, discouraging reliance on them (shrinkage).", "whyPool": "Large coefficient magnitudes make the optimization objective more costly."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Stronger regularization typically increases the freedom of the model to fit the training data.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A stronger penalty *constrains* the fitted solution rather than increasing its freedom to fit training data.", "whyPool": "A stronger penalty constrains the fitted solution rather than increasing its freedom."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Moderate regularization may improve generalization when the unregularized model has high variance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Moderate regularization trades a little bias for lower variance, improving generalization when the unregularized model overfits.", "whyPool": "Accepting some additional bias can reduce variance and improve performance on new data."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Excessively strong regularization can create underfitting.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "An excessively strong penalty over-shrinks and suppresses useful relationships, causing underfitting.", "whyPool": "An overly restrictive penalty can suppress useful relationships."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Regularization eliminates the need for validation because any positive penalty prevents overfitting.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A positive penalty does not eliminate the need for validation — the right strength is data-dependent and must be tuned.", "whyPool": "The appropriate penalty is data dependent and still requires disciplined selection and evaluation."}], "reference": "Regularization / shrinkage (ridge, lasso) — ISLR §6.2 (shrinkage methods); ESL ch.3 (ridge regression) & ch.7.", "n": 283}, {"id": "ML-D9G6Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Overfitting, Underfitting & Generalization", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "During iterative training, a learner’s training error keeps decreasing. Validation error decreases through iteration 40, then rises steadily while training continues to improve. The team considers early stopping.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The divergence after iteration 40 suggests that later training is fitting details that do not generalize.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Divergence after iteration 40 (validation up, training down) indicates later iterations are fitting non-generalizing detail — overfitting.", "whyPool": "Continued training helps the training set while harming validation performance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Stopping near the best validation iteration can act as a form of complexity control.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stopping near the best validation iteration limits how fully the learner adapts to the sample — a form of complexity control.", "whyPool": "Limiting the number of iterations restricts how fully the learner adapts to the training sample."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Selecting the stopping point by repeatedly examining the final test set is appropriate.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The stopping point should be chosen on a validation set, not by repeatedly examining the final test set (which would contaminate it).", "whyPool": "A validation set, not the final test set, should guide the stopping decision."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Training until training error is minimized must produce the best out-of-sample model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Minimizing training error need not give the best out-of-sample model; the lowest training error can occur after generalization worsens.", "whyPool": "The lowest training error may occur well after generalization begins to worsen."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If training is stopped too early, the model may underfit. 248 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stopping too early leaves useful structure unlearned, causing underfitting.", "whyPool": "Insufficient training can leave useful structure unlearned. 249 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Early stopping as regularization — ESL ch.7 (model selection); scikit-learn \"Early stopping\" / validation-curve docs.", "n": 284}, {"id": "ML-D10G1Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A systematic equity team finds that next-day returns change approximately in proportion to a small set of standardized factors. The PM wants a dependable baseline that can be retrained frequently and asks whether the team should begin with a deep tree instead of a linear model simply because the tree is more flexible.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A linear model is a reasonable starting point when the relevant relationship is approximately linear.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "When the observed relationship is approximately linear, a linear model's structure matches it and gives a transparent, low-variance, easily retrained baseline — a sound starting point.", "whyPool": "The model structure matches the observed proportional relationship and provides a useful baseline."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A more flexible learner must have lower out-of-sample error because it can represent more functions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Greater flexibility does not guarantee lower out-of-sample error; the extra capacity can fit noise and generalize worse. Ability to represent more functions ≠ better generalization.", "whyPool": "Additional flexibility can fit noise and does not guarantee better generalization."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A simple model may outperform a complex model when the sample is limited or noisy.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Under a limited or noisy sample, a simpler learner's lower variance can make it more reliable than a complex model that overfits the scarce data.", "whyPool": "Lower variance can make a simpler learner more reliable under limited information."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing candidates on representative validation data is preferable to choosing solely from their theoretical flexibility.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing candidates on representative validation data tests how the accuracy–flexibility tradeoff actually behaves on unseen observations — better than choosing by theoretical flexibility.", "whyPool": "Validation evidence tests how the tradeoff behaves on unseen observations."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Standardizing the factors automatically makes every relationship linear. 251 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Standardizing features rescales units; it does not change the underlying functional form, so it cannot make a nonlinear relationship linear.", "whyPool": "Scaling changes feature units, not the underlying functional form."}], "reference": "Flexibility–interpretability and bias–variance — ISLR §2.1.3, §2.2 (assessing model accuracy); concept \"no-free-lunch / simpler-model-under-limited-data.\"", "n": 285}, {"id": "ML-D10G1Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A commodity desk observes that returns rise only when momentum and inventory conditions jointly cross certain thresholds. The head researcher asks whether a straight-line model or a decision tree is better aligned with this structure, while requiring the recommendation to be supported out of sample. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A decision tree can represent threshold-based interactions without the analyst explicitly specifying each interaction.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A decision tree's successive splits can encode conditional threshold relationships without the analyst specifying each interaction explicitly — accurate, so not a flaw.", "whyPool": "Successive splits can encode conditional threshold relationships."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A basic linear model with only additive raw features naturally reproduces every possible threshold interaction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: a purely additive linear model with raw features does **not** automatically reproduce arbitrary threshold interactions; the word \"every\" overreaches.", "whyPool": "An additive linear form does not automatically capture arbitrary thresholds or interactions."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Feature engineering could allow a linear model to represent some nonlinear or interaction effects.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Feature engineering (transforms, interaction terms) can let a linear model represent some nonlinear/interaction effects — accurate.", "whyPool": "Transformed and interaction features can expand what a linear model represents."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The apparent threshold structure proves that the deepest possible tree is the best choice.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: an apparent threshold structure does not prove the *deepest possible* tree is best; unrestricted depth overfits sample-specific thresholds.", "whyPool": "Unrestricted depth can overfit sample-specific thresholds."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Time-aware validation can help determine whether the threshold behavior persists beyond the training period.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Time-aware validation on later periods tests whether the threshold behavior persists beyond training — accurate.", "whyPool": "Later validation periods test whether the observed structure generalizes through time. 252 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Trees and interactions — ISLR ch.8 (tree-based methods); feature engineering for linear models — ESL ch.5 (basis expansions); time-series/out-of-sample validation.", "n": 286}, {"id": "ML-D10G1Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G1", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A cross-sectional strategy has thousands of highly correlated indicators but only two years of monthly observations. A client asks the team to use the most complex available ensemble because “more inputs require a more powerful model, ” while the risk officer is concerned about unstable estimates. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A large feature count does not by itself justify selecting the most complex learner.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A large feature count alone does not justify the most complex learner; model choice depends on usable information and constraints — accurate.", "whyPool": "Model choice depends on usable information and constraints, not input count alone."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Limited observations relative to model flexibility can increase overfitting risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Few observations relative to model flexibility genuinely raises overfitting risk (a flexible learner finds accidental patterns) — accurate.", "whyPool": "A flexible learner can discover many accidental patterns in a small sample."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A constrained model or reduced feature set may be a defensible candidate.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A constrained model or reduced feature set is a defensible candidate; cutting effective complexity can improve stability and generalization — accurate.", "whyPool": "Reducing effective complexity may improve stability and generalization."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Correlated indicators guarantee that an ensemble will produce independent errors among its members.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: correlated indicators do **not** guarantee independent errors among ensemble members — shared correlated inputs tend to make members err similarly, defeating the diversification an ensemble relies on.", "whyPool": "Shared correlated inputs can cause learners to make similar errors."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Model selection should consider sample size, redundancy, stability, and the intended prediction task together.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Selection should weigh sample size, redundancy, stability, and the prediction task together — these jointly determine suitability, so this is accurate.", "whyPool": "These characteristics jointly determine whether a model is suitable. G2: Accuracy vs. Interpretability Tradeoffs"}], "reference": "Overfitting with p ≫ n and correlated predictors, ensemble error correlation — ISLR ch.6 (regularization/selection), ch.8 (bagging/random forests, decorrelation).", "n": 287}, {"id": "ML-D10G2Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A bank uses a model to approve leverage limits for trading clients. An ensemble improves validation accuracy slightly over a linear model, but the compliance officer must explain adverse decisions and asks whether the accuracy gain should settle the choice.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The ensemble’s higher validation accuracy is relevant but need not be the only selection criterion.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Higher validation accuracy is relevant but need not be the sole criterion; deployment decisions also weigh compliance and operational objectives.", "whyPool": "Deployment decisions can include compliance, operational, and predictive objectives."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A linear model may be preferred if its explanations satisfy an important governance requirement at an acceptable performance cost.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A linear model may be preferred if its explanations satisfy a material governance requirement at an acceptable performance cost — a justified small accuracy sacrifice.", "whyPool": "A small accuracy sacrifice may be justified by a material explanation requirement."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Interpretability matters only when two models have exactly equal predictive performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Interpretability is not relevant only when models tie on performance; it can be a binding constraint even when predictive performance differs.", "whyPool": "Interpretability can be a binding constraint even when performance differs."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Documenting the performance–interpretability tradeoff can support a defensible model decision. 253 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Documenting the performance–interpretability tradeoff makes the rationale and the accepted compromise reviewable and defensible.", "whyPool": "Explicit evidence makes the rationale and accepted compromise reviewable."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Calling a model interpretable guarantees that its inputs are appropriate and free of leakage.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Calling a model interpretable does not guarantee its inputs are appropriate or leakage-free; transparency does not validate the data or experimental design.", "whyPool": "Transparency does not validate the data or experimental design."}], "reference": "Interpretability vs. flexibility and governance constraints — ISLR §2.1.3; scikit-learn \"Model interpretation\"; concept \"adverse-action explainability.\"", "n": 288}, {"id": "ML-D10G2Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A hedge fund develops an internal signal used only to rank liquid stocks, and no individual client decision depends directly on the output. The PM favors a less transparent bagged-tree model because it produces a meaningful and stable out-of-sample improvement, while researchers still need to monitor it. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The absence of a client-facing explanation requirement may reduce, but not eliminate, the value of interpretability.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Absence of a client-facing explanation need may *reduce but not eliminate* interpretability's value — research, debugging, risk, and oversight still benefit — accurate.", "whyPool": "Research, debugging, risk, and oversight still benefit from understanding behavior."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A less interpretable model can be a reasonable choice when its benefit is material and adequate controls exist.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A less interpretable model can be reasonable when its benefit is material and adequate controls exist; the acceptable balance depends on objectives and controls — accurate.", "whyPool": "The acceptable balance depends on objectives, constraints, and controls."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Internal use means the team need not understand inputs, failure modes, or regime sensitivity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: internal use does **not** mean the team can skip understanding inputs, failure modes, or regime sensitivity — internal models still create financial and operational risk.", "whyPool": "Internal models can still create financial and operational risk."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Monitoring out-of-sample performance remains important regardless of model transparency.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Monitoring out-of-sample performance matters regardless of transparency; live reliability cannot be inferred from transparency alone — accurate.", "whyPool": "Live reliability cannot be inferred from transparency alone."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Interpretability and accuracy are always perfectly opposed, so improving one must reduce the other.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: interpretability and accuracy are **not** always perfectly opposed — some models improve one dimension without necessarily harming the other.", "whyPool": "Some models can improve one dimension without necessarily harming the other. 254 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Interpretability tradeoffs and monitoring — ISLR §2.1.3; ESL ch.8/ch.15 (bagging/random forests); concept \"model risk management for internal models.\"", "n": 289}, {"id": "ML-D10G2Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G2", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A pension fund compares a shallow decision tree with a much deeper tree for a tactical allocation model. The deeper tree has a tiny validation advantage, but its recommendations change through many narrow rules that the investment committee cannot reliably review.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The shallow tree’s decision path may be easier for the committee to inspect.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The shallow tree's fewer splits produce a more compact decision path that the committee can inspect more easily.", "whyPool": "Fewer splits generally produce a more compact explanation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The deeper tree should automatically be selected because any validation improvement dominates governance concerns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A validation improvement does not automatically dominate governance concerns; a negligible gain may not justify the added complexity and oversight cost.", "whyPool": "A negligible gain may not justify added complexity and oversight cost."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The small performance difference should be evaluated for statistical and practical significance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The small performance difference should be checked for statistical and practical significance — sampling variation can make a tiny observed edge meaningless.", "whyPool": "Sampling variation can make a tiny observed difference meaningless."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Stability across multiple time splits could inform whether the deeper tree’s advantage is credible.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stability across multiple time splits helps judge whether the deep tree's advantage is durable rather than a lucky single split.", "whyPool": "Repeated evidence helps distinguish a durable gain from a lucky split."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Choosing the shallow tree necessarily means abandoning all nonlinear relationships.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Choosing the shallow tree does not abandon all nonlinearity; even a shallow tree represents thresholds and limited interactions.", "whyPool": "Even a shallow tree can represent thresholds and limited interactions. G3: Training Time vs. Inference Time"}], "reference": "Tree depth, pruning, and interpretability; significance of small performance gaps — ISLR ch.8; ESL ch.7 (model assessment/selection).", "n": 290}, {"id": "ML-D10G3Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "You are selecting a model for a real-time trading system. The Head of Trading prioritizes speed, while a data scientist advocates for a complex ensemble.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Inference time is more important than training time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "For a real-time system, fast inference is the operative constraint, so inference time outweighs training time here.", "whyPool": "Real-time systems require fast inference."}, {"L": "B", "text": "KNN is often unsuitable due to latency.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "KNN is often unsuitable for real-time use because it defers heavy neighbor-search computation to inference (high latency).", "whyPool": "KNN is slow at inference."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Linear models are often preferred for speed.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Linear models are computationally efficient at inference (a dot product with the coefficient vector), so they are often preferred for speed.", "whyPool": "Linear models are efficient."}, {"L": "D", "text": "More complex models are always better.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "More complex models are not always better; tradeoffs among accuracy, latency, and robustness exist.", "whyPool": "Tradeoffs exist."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Model choice depends on system constraints.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The appropriate choice depends on system constraints (latency budget, throughput, memory) — context determines the decision.", "whyPool": "Context determines choice."}], "reference": "Train vs. inference cost, lazy vs. eager learners — ISLR §2.2.3 (KNN); scikit-learn \"Computational performance\"; concept \"latency-constrained deployment.\"", "n": 291}, {"id": "ML-D10G3Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "An asset manager retrains a weekly allocation model overnight and produces one set of portfolio weights before Monday’s open. The research director rejects a candidate because it takes 40 minutes to train, even though prediction takes milliseconds and the overnight window is four hours. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The training time fits within the stated retraining window.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Forty minutes of training fits well inside the four-hour retraining window — accurate.", "whyPool": "Forty minutes is below the available four-hour budget."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Millisecond inference may be sufficient for a model that predicts only once per week.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Millisecond inference is more than sufficient for a model that predicts only once per week — accurate.", "whyPool": "This use case has no stated need for high-frequency predictions."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Training time and inference time should be evaluated against the workflow in which each cost occurs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Training and inference costs should each be judged against the workflow where they occur; different schedules value them differently — accurate.", "whyPool": "Different operational schedules place different values on the two costs."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A model that trains more slowly must also make predictions more slowly.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: a slower-training model does **not** necessarily predict more slowly — training and inference have different computational patterns across learners (e.g., KNN trains fast, infers slowly).", "whyPool": "Training and prediction have different computational patterns across learners."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Rejecting the model solely because another model trains in one minute ignores whether the difference affects the deployment objective.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Rejecting the model solely because another trains in one minute ignores whether the difference affects the deployment objective — accurate.", "whyPool": "A cost difference matters only insofar as it affects relevant constraints or resources."}], "reference": "Decoupling of train vs. inference cost — ISLR §2.2.3; scikit-learn \"Computational performance\"; concept \"cost matters only against the binding constraint.\"", "n": 292}, {"id": "ML-D10G3Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G3", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A research platform serves thousands of interactive what-if requests against a fixed model each day but retrains only at month-end. An analyst proposes KNN because fitting mostly stores the data, while the platform owner is worried about response time and memory growth.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "KNN’s light fitting procedure shifts substantial computation to prediction time. 256 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "KNN's near-trivial fitting shifts substantial computation to prediction time (distance/neighbor search happens per query).", "whyPool": "Neighbor search occurs when a new query is evaluated."}, {"L": "B", "text": "KNN inference can become more expensive as the stored training set grows.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "KNN inference cost grows with the stored training set — more observations mean more distance computations absent special indexing.", "whyPool": "More stored observations generally mean more distance computations absent special indexing."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A fitted linear model can often answer each request without scanning the full training set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A fitted linear model answers each request from its learned coefficient vector, without scanning all training rows.", "whyPool": "Prediction typically uses the learned coefficient vector rather than all training rows."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because monthly retraining is infrequent, inference cost is irrelevant.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Infrequent retraining does not make inference cost irrelevant; with thousands of daily requests, inference can be the dominant operational cost.", "whyPool": "Frequent serving can make inference the dominant operational cost."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Expected request volume and memory constraints should be included in the model comparison.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Expected request volume and memory constraints belong in the comparison — a deployable choice must meet throughput and resource requirements.", "whyPool": "A deployable choice must meet throughput and resource requirements. G4: Sensitivity to Noise and Data Quality"}], "reference": "Lazy-learner inference scaling and memory — ISLR §2.2.3; scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors\" (query complexity), \"Computational performance.\"", "n": 293}, {"id": "ML-D10G4Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A return-prediction dataset contains a few extreme target values caused by bad price adjustments. The PM notes that a linear regression fit changes sharply when these rows are included and asks whether the resulting model should be trusted.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Extreme target errors can strongly influence a least-squares fit because large residuals are squared.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Extreme target errors strongly influence a least-squares fit because squared loss gives large residuals disproportionate weight.", "whyPool": "Squared loss gives unusually large residuals disproportionate weight."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Investigating and correcting confirmed data errors is preferable to selecting a model solely to accommodate them.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Investigating and correcting confirmed data errors at the source is preferable to picking a model just to accommodate corrupted rows.", "whyPool": "Data quality problems should be addressed at their source when possible."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Removing every economically extreme observation is automatically valid because outliers are always mistakes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Removing every economically extreme observation is not automatically valid — genuine market extremes may be informative and should not be discarded reflexively.", "whyPool": "Genuine market extremes may be informative and should not be discarded automatically."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing results with and without suspect rows can reveal sensitivity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing fits with and without suspect rows is a sensitivity analysis that reveals whether a few rows drive the conclusion.", "whyPool": "Sensitivity analysis shows whether a small set of rows drives the conclusion."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A good validation score guarantees that the underlying records are correctly adjusted.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A good validation score cannot certify record-level data integrity; aggregate performance does not confirm the records are correctly adjusted.", "whyPool": "Aggregate performance cannot certify record-level data integrity. 257 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Sensitivity of squared-error loss to outliers, robust regression, data quality — ISLR ch.3 (linear regression, outliers/leverage); ESL ch.10 (robust loss).", "n": 294}, {"id": "ML-D10G4Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A KNN strategy uses volatility, volume, and momentum features measured on very different scales. The head quant finds that small measurement noise in the high-magnitude volume feature changes which observations are selected as neighbors.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Distance-based KNN can be sensitive to feature scale.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Distance-based KNN is sensitive to feature scale — distance calculations reflect numerical magnitude unless features are treated appropriately.", "whyPool": "Distance calculations reflect numerical magnitudes unless features are treated appropriately."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Appropriate scaling can prevent one feature from dominating distance merely because of its units.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Appropriate scaling puts features on comparable scales so no feature dominates distance merely because of its units.", "whyPool": "Comparable scales make distance contributions less dependent on units."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Scaling guarantees that noisy features become useful predictors.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Scaling changes magnitude, not information content; it does not guarantee a noisy feature becomes a useful predictor.", "whyPool": "Scaling changes magnitude, not information content or measurement validity."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Increasing k may reduce sensitivity to a single mislabeled or noisy neighbor, although excessive smoothing can add bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Increasing k averages more neighbors and can dampen a single mislabeled/noisy neighbor, though excessive smoothing adds bias (erases local structure).", "whyPool": "Averaging more neighbors can dampen isolated noise but may erase local structure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Feature quality is irrelevant to KNN because it has no learned coefficients.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Feature quality is not irrelevant to KNN; neighbor selection depends directly on the quality of the feature representation, even without learned coefficients.", "whyPool": "Neighbor selection depends directly on the quality of the feature representation."}], "reference": "KNN scale sensitivity, choice of k, bias–variance — ISLR §2.2.3, §3.5; scikit-learn \"Preprocessing\" (StandardScaler) and \"Nearest Neighbors.\"", "n": 295}, {"id": "ML-D10G4Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G4", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A single deep decision tree produces very different trading rules after a few training observations are corrected. The risk officer proposes a bagged ensemble and better data controls, but a researcher claims bagging makes clean data unnecessary. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Deep trees can be unstable because small data changes may alter early splits and downstream branches.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Deep trees can be unstable: small data changes may alter early splits and cascade through downstream branches — accurate.", "whyPool": "A changed split can restructure much of a tree."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Bagging can reduce variance by averaging predictions from multiple resampled trees.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Bagging reduces variance by averaging predictions from multiple resampled trees — accurate.", "whyPool": "Aggregation can smooth sample-specific variation among members."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Bagging guarantees immunity to systematic errors present throughout the dataset.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: bagging does **not** guarantee immunity to systematic errors present throughout the data — shared bias or corrupted inputs affect every member.", "whyPool": "Shared bias or corrupted inputs can affect every learner."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Correcting upstream data defects can improve both single models and ensembles.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Correcting upstream data defects improves both single models and ensembles — accurate.", "whyPool": "Better inputs address a cause that aggregation alone cannot remove."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Robustness should be assessed using plausible perturbations rather than assumed from the algorithm’s name. 258 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Robustness should be assessed with plausible perturbation/stress tests, not assumed from the algorithm's name — accurate.", "whyPool": "Empirical stress tests are needed to establish stability for the actual task. G5: Data Size vs. Model Complexity"}], "reference": "Tree instability, bagging variance reduction and its limits vs. systematic error — ISLR ch.8 (bagging, random forests); ESL ch.8/ch.15; concept \"empirical robustness testing.\"", "n": 296}, {"id": "ML-D10G5Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A new crypto strategy has only 180 daily observations and 60 candidate indicators. The PM asks for a fully grown decision tree because it fits the training period perfectly, while the analyst warns that the sample cannot support so many adaptive choices. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Perfect training fit is weak evidence of generalization in this small-sample setting.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Perfect training fit is weak evidence of generalization in a small sample — a flexible model can memorize it — accurate.", "whyPool": "A flexible model can memorize a small dataset."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A fully grown tree can partition the limited observations into highly specific leaves.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A fully grown tree can partition the limited data into highly specific leaves that describe individual rows, not recurring structure — accurate.", "whyPool": "Fine partitions may describe individual observations rather than recurring structure."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Restricting depth or minimum leaf size may reduce overfitting risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Restricting depth or minimum leaf size limits effective capacity and can reduce overfitting risk — accurate.", "whyPool": "These controls limit effective model capacity."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Adding more candidate indicators always reduces the amount of data required.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: adding more candidate indicators does **not** reduce data needs — more features enlarge the search space and *increase* the data required.", "whyPool": "More features can enlarge the search space and increase data requirements."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Later out-of-sample observations are needed to evaluate whether the learned rules persist.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Later out-of-sample observations are needed to test whether the learned rules persist beyond the construction sample — accurate.", "whyPool": "Generalization must be tested beyond the observations used to construct the rules. 259 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Overfitting, tree complexity control, curse of dimensionality — ISLR ch.8 (pruning, min-leaf), §2.2 (overfitting); ESL ch.7.", "n": 297}, {"id": "ML-D10G5Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "An equity desk expands from 5,000 to 5 million clean training examples and wants to reconsider models previously rejected for high variance. The engineering lead also notes that KNN would have to retain the enlarged dataset for prediction. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "More representative observations can make fitting a flexible model more reliable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "More representative observations can make fitting a flexible model more reliable by separating stable patterns from sampling noise — accurate.", "whyPool": "More examples can help distinguish stable patterns from sampling noise."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A larger dataset eliminates the need to control model complexity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: a larger dataset does **not** eliminate the need to control complexity — flexible models can still overfit, and data can carry noise or mismatch.", "whyPool": "Flexible models can still overfit, and data can contain noise or mismatch."}, {"L": "C", "text": "KNN storage and naive inference costs can grow with the training set.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "KNN storage and naive inference costs grow with the training set (it stores observations and compares queries against them) — accurate.", "whyPool": "KNN generally stores observations and compares queries against them."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Scaling constraints may change which model is practical even when predictive accuracy improves.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Scaling constraints (memory, training, serving) may change which model is practical even when predictive accuracy improves — accurate.", "whyPool": "Memory, training, and serving costs are part of practical selection."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The value of the additional rows depends on their relevance to the deployment distribution.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "The value of extra rows depends on their relevance to the deployment distribution; stale/irrelevant data may not help — accurate.", "whyPool": "Irrelevant or stale data may not improve current performance."}], "reference": "Sample size vs. variance, KNN scaling, distribution relevance — ISLR §2.2.3, ch.8; scikit-learn \"Computational performance\"; concept \"data relevance vs. raw count.\"", "n": 298}, {"id": "ML-D10G5Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G5", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A global macro team pools decades of daily data to train a complex ensemble. The CIO argues that the raw row count is large enough, but many observations come from market structures that no longer exist and only a small fraction represents the current regime.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Raw dataset size can overstate the amount of information relevant to current deployment.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Raw dataset size can overstate the information relevant to current deployment — effective sample relevance matters beyond total count.", "whyPool": "Effective sample relevance matters in addition to total count."}, {"L": "B", "text": "More historical rows guarantee lower out-of-sample error in the current regime.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "More historical rows do not guarantee lower out-of-sample error now; distribution shift can make additional history misleading.", "whyPool": "Distribution shift can make additional history misleading."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A complex model may learn obsolete relationships if regime differences are not addressed.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A complex model may learn obsolete relationships if regime differences are not addressed — its flexibility lets it exploit patterns that no longer operate.", "whyPool": "Flexibility allows the learner to exploit patterns that may no longer operate."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Time-based evaluation can help reveal whether performance transfers to more recent periods.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Time-based evaluation on recent periods better approximates the intended use and reveals whether performance transfers.", "whyPool": "Later evaluation periods better approximate the intended use."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A simpler model may be competitive if it relies on fewer, more stable relationships.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A simpler model relying on fewer, more stable relationships may be competitive and more robust under regime change.", "whyPool": "Reduced dependence on fragile structure can improve robustness. G6: Real-Time Constraints in Trading Systems"}], "reference": "Distribution shift/non-stationarity, effective sample size, time-aware evaluation — ISLR §2.2; concept \"regime shift and stationarity.\"", "n": 299}, {"id": "ML-D10G6Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A market-making system must update quotes within 200 microseconds after receiving market data. A candidate ensemble improves offline error by 0.2% but exceeds the end-to-end latency budget during peak load, and the Head of Trading asks whether the gain justifies deployment. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A model that violates the latency budget may be unusable despite better offline error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A model that violates the latency budget may be unusable despite better offline error — predictions arriving too late cannot support the decision — accurate.", "whyPool": "Predictions that arrive too late cannot support the required decision."}, {"L": "B", "text": "End-to-end testing under peak load is more relevant than timing one prediction in isolation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "End-to-end testing under peak load is more relevant than timing one prediction in isolation (production latency includes contention and tails) — accurate.", "whyPool": "Production latency includes contention, tails, and surrounding system work."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The ensemble should be deployed because predictive accuracy always dominates execution speed.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: predictive accuracy does **not** always dominate execution speed — late decisions can erase or reverse the value of a small accuracy gain.", "whyPool": "Late decisions can erase or reverse the value of a small accuracy gain."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A faster model with slightly worse prediction error could produce better realized trading outcomes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A faster model with slightly worse error could produce better realized trading outcomes because timely execution can matter more — accurate.", "whyPool": "Timely execution can matter more than a modest offline improvement."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Feature computation and system overhead should be included in the latency assessment.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Feature computation and system overhead must be included in the latency assessment — inference is only one component — accurate.", "whyPool": "Model inference is only one component of the full response time."}], "reference": "Latency-constrained deployment, tail latency, end-to-end measurement — scikit-learn \"Computational performance\"; concept \"deployment constraints / realized vs. offline performance.\"", "n": 300}, {"id": "ML-D10G6Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A daily rebalancing strategy generates orders once after the close and has a 30-minute computation window. A junior engineer insists that only models suitable for high-frequency trading should be considered because all trading systems are real time. 261 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The daily strategy has a materially different latency requirement from a market-making system.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The daily strategy has a materially different latency requirement from a market-making system — its decisions occur on a much slower schedule.", "whyPool": "Its decisions occur on a much slower schedule."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A slower model can be acceptable if it completes reliably within the decision window.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A slower model is acceptable if it completes reliably within the decision window; meeting the actual deadline matters more than minimizing latency for its own sake.", "whyPool": "Meeting the actual deadline is more important than minimizing latency without purpose."}, {"L": "C", "text": "High-frequency suitability is a universal requirement for any model used in finance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "High-frequency suitability is not a universal requirement; model constraints depend on the strategy's holding period and operating design.", "whyPool": "Model constraints depend on the strategy’s holding period and operating design."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The team may reasonably prioritize robustness or interpretability over microsecond inference in this workflow.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With a generous time budget, the team may reasonably prioritize robustness or interpretability over microsecond inference.", "whyPool": "The generous time budget permits other objectives to carry more weight."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Operational deadlines still matter even when the strategy is not high frequency.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Operational deadlines still matter even for non-HFT strategies — missing the rebalance window could disrupt execution.", "whyPool": "Missing the rebalance window could still disrupt execution."}], "reference": "Strategy-specific latency requirements, batch vs. real-time — concept \"match latency budget to holding period\"; scikit-learn \"Computational performance.\"", "n": 301}, {"id": "ML-D10G6Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G6", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A low-latency desk considers moving from a linear model to KNN as its historical dataset grows each day. The proposed system must handle bursts of simultaneous predictions, and the engineering manager asks the research team to account for tail latency rather than average latency alone.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Naive KNN prediction can slow as the stored dataset grows.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Naive KNN prediction slows as the stored dataset grows — each query may require distances to an increasing number of observations.", "whyPool": "Each query may require distances to an increasing number of observations."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Burst traffic can make high-percentile latency more important than a low average.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Burst traffic makes high-percentile (tail) latency more important than a low average, which can hide rare deadline-violating delays.", "whyPool": "Averages can hide rare delays that violate trading deadlines."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The absence of a costly fitting phase makes KNN automatically suitable for low-latency serving.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The absence of a costly fitting phase does not make KNN automatically suitable for low-latency serving — it defers much of its computation to inference.", "whyPool": "KNN defers much of its computational burden to inference."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Limiting the reference set could improve speed but may change predictive behavior.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Limiting the reference set could improve speed but may exclude relevant neighbors and change predictive behavior.", "whyPool": "Fewer comparisons reduce work but can exclude relevant neighbors."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The model comparison should include throughput, memory, and latency under representative load.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The comparison should include throughput, memory, and latency under representative load — these determine whether the model fits the system constraints.", "whyPool": "These measures determine whether the model can operate within the system constraints. 262 Rev:081020261230 G7: Robustness and Stability in Production"}], "reference": "KNN inference scaling, tail latency under load — ISLR §2.2.3; scikit-learn \"Nearest Neighbors,\" \"Computational performance.\"", "n": 302}, {"id": "ML-D10G7Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A live forecasting model performed well during calm markets but its errors rise sharply when volatility increases. The risk committee asks whether strong average backtest performance is enough to keep the model active without changes. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Average performance can conceal failure in economically important regimes.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Average performance can conceal failure in economically important regimes — many calm observations hide poor stressed-period behavior — accurate.", "whyPool": "Poor stressed-period behavior may be hidden by many calm observations."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Evaluating results by volatility regime can reveal conditional weaknesses.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Evaluating by volatility regime reveals conditional weaknesses (whether performance depends on market state) — accurate.", "whyPool": "Segmented evaluation tests whether performance depends on market state."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Historical success guarantees that the relationship will remain stable in production.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: historical success does **not** guarantee the relationship stays stable in production — markets and data-generating relationships change.", "whyPool": "Markets and data-generating relationships can change."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Monitoring can support thresholds for review, fallback, or retraining when behavior deteriorates.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Monitoring can support thresholds for review, fallback, or retraining when behavior deteriorates, making degradation actionable — accurate.", "whyPool": "Defined responses make degradation actionable rather than merely observable."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A simpler fallback model may be valuable even if it is not the top performer in normal conditions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A simpler fallback model can be valuable during failures even if it is not the top performer in normal conditions — accurate.", "whyPool": "Operational resilience may favor a dependable alternative during failures."}], "reference": "Regime-segmented evaluation, robustness vs. average performance, monitoring/fallback — ISLR §2.2; concept \"non-stationarity, production monitoring, graceful degradation.\"", "n": 303}, {"id": "ML-D10G7Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A deep tree and a bagged-tree ensemble have similar average validation error. Across nearby time windows and small input perturbations, the single tree’s predictions vary much more, while the ensemble remains relatively consistent; the production owner values predictable exposures.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The ensemble’s lower sensitivity may be relevant even when average error is similar.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The ensemble's lower sensitivity is relevant even at similar average error — prediction variability affects turnover, exposure, and operational confidence.", "whyPool": "Prediction variability can affect turnover, exposure, and operational confidence."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Bagging can reduce variance by averaging unstable component predictions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Bagging reduces variance by averaging unstable component predictions, dampening sample-specific tree behavior.", "whyPool": "Aggregation reduces the influence of sample-specific tree behavior."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Similar average error proves that the two models create identical trading risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Similar average error does not prove identical trading risk; models with equal mean error can differ in tails, turnover, and sensitivity.", "whyPool": "Models with equal average error can differ in tails, turnover, and sensitivity."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Small-perturbation tests can provide evidence about local model stability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Small-perturbation tests provide evidence about local stability — whether modest input changes cause disproportionate output changes.", "whyPool": "Such tests measure whether modest changes cause disproportionate output changes."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Stability considerations may justify selecting the ensemble for production.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stability considerations can justify choosing the ensemble; production suitability includes reliability beyond mean accuracy.", "whyPool": "Production suitability includes reliability beyond mean accuracy."}], "reference": "Bagging variance reduction, stability vs. average accuracy — ISLR ch.8; ESL ch.15; concept \"prediction stability / turnover in production.\"", "n": 304}, {"id": "ML-D10G7Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G7", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A factor model is retrained monthly, and the selected features and coefficients change dramatically each time even though headline validation scores remain similar. The model-risk lead asks whether this instability should be investigated before clients rely on the resulting allocations.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Similar validation scores do not rule out unstable model behavior.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Similar validation scores do not rule out unstable behavior — different models can score alike while producing materially different outputs.", "whyPool": "Different models can achieve similar scores while producing materially different outputs."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Large month-to-month changes may indicate correlated features, limited data, or regime sensitivity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Large month-to-month changes may indicate correlated features, limited data, or regime sensitivity — each can shift fitted relationships across samples.", "whyPool": "Each condition can cause fitted relationships to shift across samples."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Comparing portfolio turnover and exposure changes can reveal practical consequences of instability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing portfolio turnover and exposure changes reveals the practical consequences of instability (costs and risk).", "whyPool": "Allocation changes translate statistical instability into costs and risk."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Retraining more often necessarily makes the model more stable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Retraining more often does not necessarily increase stability; more frequent fitting can amplify sensitivity to short-term sample changes.", "whyPool": "More frequent fitting may amplify sensitivity to short-term sample changes."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Stability analysis should consider both predictions and downstream decisions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stability analysis should consider both predictions and the downstream decisions the system makes with them.", "whyPool": "Production impact depends on what the system does with the predictions. G8: Comparative Model Reasoning"}], "reference": "Coefficient instability under collinearity/limited data, decision-level impact — ISLR ch.6 (collinearity, selection stability); concept \"model-risk stability analysis.\"", "n": 305}, {"id": "ML-D10G8Q1", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G8", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A PM must choose among linear regression, KNN, and a decision tree for a medium-sized return-prediction dataset. The relationship may contain thresholds, inference must be fast, and the investment committee wants a model it can inspect. 264 Rev:081020261230 The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Linear regression offers fast inference and transparent coefficients but may miss unengineered threshold effects.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Linear regression offers fast inference and transparent coefficients but may miss unengineered threshold effects — an accurate match to its strengths/limits.", "whyPool": "Its strengths and limitations align with the stated tradeoff."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A decision tree can represent thresholds and provide inspectable rules, subject to complexity limits.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A decision tree represents thresholds and gives inspectable rules, subject to complexity limits (pruning/depth) — accurate.", "whyPool": "Splits capture thresholds, while pruning or depth limits can aid review and generalization."}, {"L": "C", "text": "KNN naturally provides a compact global equation explaining all predictions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: KNN does **not** provide a compact global equation — its predictions depend on local stored examples, not one fitted equation.", "whyPool": "KNN predictions depend on local stored examples rather than one compact fitted equation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "No learner is universally best across the stated objectives.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "No learner is universally best across the stated objectives; each offers a different balance of structure, speed, and interpretability — accurate (no free lunch).", "whyPool": "The models offer different balances of structure, speed, and interpretability."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Representative validation and operational tests should inform the final choice.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Representative validation plus operational tests should inform the final choice — both predictive and deployment evidence are needed — accurate.", "whyPool": "Both predictive and deployment evidence are needed."}], "reference": "Comparative model properties (linear/tree/KNN), no-free-lunch — ISLR §2.1.3, §2.2.3, ch.8; scikit-learn \"Choosing the right estimator.\"", "n": 306}, {"id": "ML-D10G8Q2", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G8", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "inverted", "scenario": "A research team compares three learners on a nonlinear signal. KNN performs best on a small validation set, a tree is close behind, and a linear model is slightly worse but far more stable across time splits. The CIO asks the team to name the winner immediately. The team asks you to identify the flawed claims. For this question, mark each statement True if it is incorrect and False if it is accurate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The single validation ranking may be uncertain because the validation set is small.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "A single validation ranking may be uncertain because a small evaluation set is subject to sampling variation — accurate.", "whyPool": "Sampling variation can materially affect results from a small evaluation set. 265 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Stability across time splits is relevant evidence for a trading model.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Stability across time splits is relevant evidence for a trading model, since market relationships and samples vary through time — accurate.", "whyPool": "Market relationships and samples vary through time."}, {"L": "C", "text": "KNN must be selected because the lowest observed error always identifies the true best model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": false, "why": "Flawed: KNN need **not** be selected — the lowest observed error does not always identify the true best model; the lead may be noise and ignores other constraints.", "whyPool": "An observed lead may reflect noise and ignores other constraints."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The linear model’s stability may justify selection if the small accuracy difference is unreliable or operationally unimportant.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "The linear model's stability may justify selection if the small accuracy difference is unreliable or operationally unimportant — accurate.", "whyPool": "Reliability can outweigh a marginal, uncertain improvement."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The team should compare the models using consistent splits and metrics.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": true, "why": "Models should be compared using consistent splits and metrics; consistency is necessary for a fair comparison — accurate.", "whyPool": "Consistency is necessary for a fair comparison."}], "reference": "Validation-set variance, model comparison under small samples, stability — ISLR ch.5 (cross-validation, resampling); ESL ch.7.", "n": 307}, {"id": "ML-D10G8Q3", "part": "ML", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Model Selection & Practical Tradeoffs", "group": "G8", "weeks": [4, 5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A trading desk summarizes its options as follows: a linear model is fast and stable but underfits; a deep tree fits nonlinearities but is unstable; and KNN is accurate offline but too slow with the full dataset. The research director asks for a recommendation that improves the system rather than declaring one algorithm inherently superior.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Feature engineering may improve the linear model while retaining fast inference.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Feature engineering can improve the linear model (capturing more structure) while retaining its fast serving pattern.", "whyPool": "Appropriate transformations can capture additional structure without changing the basic serving pattern."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Restricting tree depth or using a bagged-tree ensemble may address some variance concerns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Restricting tree depth or using a bagged-tree ensemble can address variance/instability concerns, though each introduces its own tradeoffs.", "whyPool": "Complexity controls or averaging can reduce sensitivity, though they introduce their own tradeoffs."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Reducing or restructuring KNN’s reference data may improve latency but requires re-evaluating accuracy.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Reducing or restructuring KNN's reference data can improve latency but alters neighbor quality, so accuracy must be re-validated.", "whyPool": "Computational savings can alter neighbor quality and must be validated."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because each model has a weakness, none can be deployed under any circumstances.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Each model having a weakness does not mean none can be deployed; practical selection manages tradeoffs rather than requiring perfection.", "whyPool": "Practical model selection manages tradeoffs rather than requiring perfection."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The best choice is the one that achieves an acceptable balance under the desk’s measured constraints.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The best choice achieves an acceptable balance under the desk's measured predictive, operational, and governance constraints.", "whyPool": "Suitability is conditional on predictive, operational, and governance requirements. 266 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Practical tradeoff management, feature engineering, complexity control, no-free-lunch — ISLR §2.1.3, ch.6, ch.8; concept \"constraint-driven model selection.\"", "n": 308}, {"id": "QF-D1G1Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio manager buys a stock at $50. At the end of the first month, it closes at $55; at the end of the second month, it closes at $52.25. Ignoring dividends and trading costs, the manager asks a junior analyst to explain the two monthly returns and the return over the full holding period.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The first-month return is 10%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "HPR = (55 − 50)/50 = 5/50 = **10%**. Return is measured against the beginning value of that period.", "whyPool": "The first-month return is ($55 - $50) / $50 = 10%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The second-month return is -5%, measured relative to the $55 value at the beginning of that month.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "(52.25 − 55)/55 = −2.75/55 = **−5%**. The base is the value *entering* month 2 ($55), not the original $50.", "whyPool": "The second-month return is ($52.25 - $55) / $55 = -5%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The two-month cumulative return is 5% because the monthly returns sum to 5%. 273 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Returns compound, not add: (1.10)(0.95) − 1 = 1.045 − 1 = **4.5%**. Summing (10% − 5% = 5%) ignores the interaction term and overstates the true link.", "whyPool": "Linked returns compound: (1.10)(0.95) - 1 = 4.5%, not 5%."}, {"L": "D", "text": "One dollar invested at the beginning would not be worth $1.045 at the end.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Double-negative embedded claim: $1 actually *does* grow to $1 × 1.10 × 0.95 = **$1.045**. The negated statement reverses the correct conclusion, so it is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A cumulative return of 4.5% turns $1.00 into $1.045."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The $2.25 increase in price represents the same percentage return regardless of the stock's initial purchase price.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Percentage return = dollar change ÷ *starting price*, so it depends on the base. $2.25 on $55 (≈4.1%) differs from $2.25 on any other base.", "whyPool": "Percentage return depends on the price used as the starting value."}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — Time Value of Money / holding-period return; Investopedia, \"Holding Period Return.\"", "n": 309}, {"id": "QF-D1G1Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An investment committee reviews a fund that returned 8% in the first quarter and -6% in the second quarter. The chair wants a single first-half performance number and asks whether simply reporting 2% would be accurate. Assume there were no external cash flows or fees.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The first-half cumulative return is found by multiplying 1.08 by 0.94 and subtracting 1.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Consecutive periodic returns link multiplicatively via growth factors; this is the exact cumulative-return formula.", "whyPool": "Consecutive periodic returns are linked multiplicatively."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The fund's first-half cumulative return is 1.52%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "(1.08)(0.94) − 1 = 1.0152 − 1 = **1.52%**.", "whyPool": "(1.08)(0.94) - 1 = 1.52%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Adding the quarterly returns gives the exact cumulative return whenever the periods are consecutive.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "8% + (−6%) = 2% ≠ 1.52%. Addition drops the cross-product (0.08 × −0.06); it is only an approximation.", "whyPool": "Adding returns ignores compounding and is generally only an approximation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the fund began with $10 million, it ended the half-year with $10.152 million.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$10M × 1.0152 = **$10.152M**, consistent with the 1.52% compounded return.", "whyPool": "$10 million × 1.0152 = $10.152 million."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The second-quarter dollar loss is not computed using the portfolio value after the first-quarter gain.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: the −6% *does* apply to the post-Q1 balance ($10.8M). The statement reverses the correct conclusion, so it is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The -6% return applies to the $10.8 million value entering the second quarter. 274 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Bodie-Kane-Marcus, *Investments* — multi-period returns; Investopedia, \"Cumulative Return.\"", "n": 310}, {"id": "QF-D1G1Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G1", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A family office compares a stock that rose from $80 to $88 with another that rose from $20 to $24. A client calls the first stock the better performer because its price increased by more dollars. You are asked to evaluate that claim, ignoring dividends and costs.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that the first stock earned a 10% return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Twisted claim: the first stock *did* earn (88−80)/80 = **10%**, so accepting it is correct — \"would be wrong to accept\" is therefore False.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. ($88 - $80) / $80 = 10%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The second stock earned a 20% return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "(24 − 20)/20 = 4/20 = **20%**.", "whyPool": "($24 - $20) / $20 = 20%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The first stock had the larger dollar price change per share.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "+$8 (Stock 1) vs +$4 (Stock 2). Dollar change and percentage return are different metrics.", "whyPool": "The first rose $8 per share, while the second rose $4 per share."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The first stock outperformed the second on a percentage-return basis.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "10% < 20%; the smaller, lower-priced stock had the higher percentage return.", "whyPool": "The second stock's 20% return exceeds the first stock's 10% return."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If equal dollar amounts had been invested in the two stocks, the second investment would not have produced the larger dollar profit.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: with equal starting capital, the higher percentage return (20%) *does* produce the larger dollar profit. Reverses the correct conclusion, so False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. With equal starting capital, the higher percentage return produces the larger dollar profit. G2: Compounding and Geometric Growth"}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — return measurement basics; Investopedia, \"Rate of Return.\"", "n": 311}, {"id": "QF-D1G2Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A pension consultant reports that a private investment grew from $100 million to $121 million over two years. The pension board wants to compare the investment with alternatives quoted using annual growth rates and asks for the investment's CAGR. Assume there were no interim cash flows.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The investment's total two-year return is 21%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "121/100 − 1 = **21%** cumulative.", "whyPool": "$121 million / $100 million - 1 = 21%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Its CAGR is not 10% per year.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: CAGR = (121/100)^(1/2) − 1 = √1.21 − 1 = **10%**. So it *is* 10%; the negation reverses the correct conclusion → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. ($121 / $100)^(1/2) - 1 = 10%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A 10.5% annual simple average is the realized annual compound growth rate.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "21%/2 = 10.5% ignores compounding. The true compound rate is 10% (since (1.10)2 = 1.21, not (1.105)2).", "whyPool": "Dividing the 21% total return by two ignores compounding and does not produce CAGR."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Compounding a 10% annual return for two years produces a 21% cumulative return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "(1.10)2 − 1 = 1.21 − 1 = **21%** — confirms 10% is the correct CAGR.", "whyPool": "(1.10)^2 - 1 = 21%."}, {"L": "E", "text": "CAGR describes the constant annual rate that would connect the beginning and ending values. 275 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "By definition CAGR is the single geometric rate that maps V_begin to V_end over n periods.", "whyPool": "CAGR is the constant compounded rate consistent with the two endpoint values."}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — geometric mean / CAGR; Investopedia, \"Compound Annual Growth Rate (CAGR).\"", "n": 312}, {"id": "QF-D1G2Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A retirement-plan sponsor is comparing two long-term savings options. One is expected to earn 8% annually before a 1% annual fee, while the other is expected to earn 7.25% annually with no fee. The sponsor asks why a seemingly small annual fee matters over a 30-year horizon; assume each quoted net or gross rate compounds annually and ignore taxes.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The first option's assumed net annual growth rate is not 7% under the simplified fee assumption.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: 8% gross − 1% fee = **7%** net under the stated simplification. The negation reverses this, so False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Under the stated simplification, 8% gross less a 1% annual fee gives 7% net."}, {"L": "B", "text": "At a 7% net rate, $1 grows to approximately $7.61 over 30 years.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "(1.07)^30 ≈ **7.61**.", "whyPool": "(1.07)^30 is approximately 7.61."}, {"L": "C", "text": "At a 7.25% rate, $1 grows to more than it does at a 7% rate over the same horizon.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Same start and horizon, higher positive compound rate ⇒ greater terminal wealth ((1.0725)^30 > (1.07)^30).", "whyPool": "With the same starting wealth and horizon, the higher positive compound rate produces greater terminal wealth."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The 0.25 percentage-point difference can be evaluated by multiplying 0.25% by 30 to obtain the exact terminal-wealth difference.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Terminal wealth is exponential in the rate; you cannot linearize the gap by multiplying the rate difference by years.", "whyPool": "The terminal-wealth difference depends on compounding, not simple multiplication of the rate gap by time."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Repeated compounding cannot make a small annual return difference economically meaningful over a long horizon.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: it *can* — each year's difference changes the base later returns compound on, so gaps widen over time. Reverses the correct conclusion → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Each year's difference affects the base on which later returns compound."}], "reference": "Bodie-Kane-Marcus, *Investments* — costs and compounding; Investopedia, \"Compound Interest.\"", "n": 313}, {"id": "QF-D1G2Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G2", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A wealth manager uses the Rule of 72 to give a client a quick sense of long-horizon growth. The client is considering an account expected to earn 6% annually and wants to know how long it might take to double, while acknowledging that the return is not guaranteed. 276 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The Rule of 72 estimates a doubling time of about 12 years at 6% annual growth.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "72 / 6 = **12 years** (approximate).", "whyPool": "72 / 6 gives an approximate doubling time of 12 years."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The estimate is obtained by dividing 72 by the annual percentage return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "That is the standard Rule-of-72 formula.", "whyPool": "That calculation is the standard Rule of 72 approximation."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The Rule of 72 guarantees the account will double in the estimated period.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "It is an approximation on an *assumed* rate — no guarantee of realized performance.", "whyPool": "It is an approximation based on an assumed rate, not a performance guarantee."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The exact doubling time at a constant 6% annual compound rate is not slightly less than 12 years.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: exact time = ln(2)/ln(1.06) ≈ **11.9 years**, which *is* slightly less than 12. The negation reverses this → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. ln(2) / ln(1.06) is approximately 11.9 years."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the net return fell to 4%, the Rule of 72 would imply a longer doubling time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "72 / 4 = **18 years** > 12 — lower rate means longer doubling time.", "whyPool": "72 / 4 is about 18 years, which is longer than 12 years. G3: Arithmetic vs. Geometric Average Returns"}], "reference": "Investopedia, \"Rule of 72\"; CFA curriculum — compounding intuition.", "n": 314}, {"id": "QF-D1G3Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A hedge-fund analyst reports annual returns of +30% and -20% and tells the investment committee that the fund's average return was 5% per year. The committee asks whether that figure describes the fund's realized compound growth. Assume no cash flows or fees.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The arithmetic average return is 5%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "(30% + (−20%))/2 = **5%**.", "whyPool": "(30% - 20%) / 2 = 5%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The two-year cumulative return is not 4%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: (1.30)(0.80) − 1 = 1.04 − 1 = **4%**. So it *is* 4%; the negation reverses this → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. (1.30)(0.80) - 1 = 4%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The geometric average return is not approximately 1.98% per year.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: geometric mean = √(1.04) − 1 ≈ **1.98%**. So it *is* ≈1.98%; negation reverses → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The geometric average is sqrt(1.04) - 1, approximately 1.98%."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Investing $100 at the beginning would produce $110.25 after two years.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Terminal value = $100 × 1.30 × 0.80 = **$104**, not $110.25 (which would be (1.05)2).", "whyPool": "The terminal value is $100 × 1.30 × 0.80 = $104. 277 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "The arithmetic average overstates the annualized compound growth achieved over these two years.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "5% arithmetic > 1.98% geometric — the classic volatility-drag gap.", "whyPool": "The 5% arithmetic mean exceeds the approximately 1.98% geometric mean."}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — arithmetic vs geometric mean; Investopedia, \"Geometric Mean.\"", "n": 315}, {"id": "QF-D1G3Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quant researcher is using historical annual returns for two different tasks: estimating a representative one-year expected return and describing the constant annual rate actually earned across the full sample. The research lead asks the analyst to choose the averaging method appropriate to each task.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The arithmetic mean is generally more relevant for estimating the average outcome of a single future period from a historical sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The arithmetic mean is the unbiased estimator of a single-period expected return from a sample.", "whyPool": "The arithmetic mean summarizes the average one-period observation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The geometric mean is appropriate for describing the constant compound rate linking beginning wealth to ending wealth over multiple periods.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The geometric mean reproduces realized multi-period terminal wealth.", "whyPool": "The geometric mean reflects realized multi-period compounding."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The geometric mean can be calculated by adding periodic returns and dividing by the number of periods.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "That is the *arithmetic* mean. Geometric mean multiplies growth factors and takes the n-th root: (Π(1+ri))^(1/n) − 1.", "whyPool": "The geometric mean is based on multiplying growth factors and taking the appropriate root."}, {"L": "D", "text": "When periodic returns vary and compound wealth remains positive, the geometric mean cannot exceed the arithmetic mean.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Return dispersion forces geometric ≤ arithmetic (Jensen's inequality / volatility drag); equal only with zero dispersion.", "whyPool": "Return variability creates a gap in which the geometric mean is less than or equal to the arithmetic mean."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The arithmetic and geometric means are always identical when the sample contains both positive and negative returns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "They are equal only when *every* periodic return is identical — mixed signs alone do not make them equal.", "whyPool": "They are equal only when the periodic returns are identical, not merely because signs differ."}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — mean return measures; Bodie-Kane-Marcus, *Investments* — average returns.", "n": 316}, {"id": "QF-D1G3Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G3", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An allocator compares two hypothetical strategies over two years. Strategy A earns +10% in each year; Strategy B earns +40% in the first year and -20% in the second. Both have an arithmetic average return of 10%, and the allocator wants to know whether they create the same terminal wealth from a $100 investment.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that strategy A ends with $121.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Twisted claim: A *does* end at $100 × 1.10 × 1.10 = **$121**, so accepting it is correct → \"wrong to accept\" is False.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. $100 × 1.10 × 1.10 = $121."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that strategy B ends with $112.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Twisted claim: B *does* end at $100 × 1.40 × 0.80 = **$112**, so accepting it is correct → \"wrong to accept\" is False.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. $100 × 1.40 × 0.80 = $112."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Both strategies have the same geometric average because their arithmetic averages are equal.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A's geometric = 10%; B's = √(1.12) − 1 ≈ **5.83%**. Equal arithmetic means do not imply equal geometric means.", "whyPool": "Strategy A's geometric average is 10%, while Strategy B's is sqrt(1.12) - 1, approximately 5.83%."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Strategy B's greater return variability creates volatility drag relative to its arithmetic average. 278 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "B's dispersion pulls compound growth (5.83%) below its 10% arithmetic mean — textbook volatility drag.", "whyPool": "The dispersion in Strategy B's returns lowers its compound growth relative to its 10% arithmetic mean."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Strategy A has a 10% geometric average return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Identical 10% returns each year ⇒ geometric mean = **10%** (no dispersion, no drag).", "whyPool": "Identical 10% returns in both years produce a 10% geometric mean. G4: Annualization and Time-Scale Conversion"}], "reference": "Investopedia, \"Volatility Drag\"; CFA curriculum — geometric vs arithmetic returns.", "n": 317}, {"id": "QF-D1G4Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An asset manager reports a strategy that earned 1% in each of the past 12 months. A client asks for its annual return, while the risk team separately reports monthly volatility of 3%. Assume monthly returns compound and, for volatility scaling, monthly returns are independent with stable variance.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The compounded annual return is approximately 12.68%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "(1.01)^12 − 1 ≈ **12.68%**.", "whyPool": "(1.01)^12 - 1 is approximately 12.68%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Multiplying 1% by 12 gives the exact compounded annual return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "12% is a simple approximation; it omits intra-year compounding (the true value is 12.68%).", "whyPool": "12% is a simple approximation that omits monthly compounding."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The annualized volatility is approximately 10.39%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "3% × √12 ≈ 3% × 3.464 ≈ **10.39%** under i.i.d. scaling.", "whyPool": "3% × sqrt(12) is approximately 10.39%."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Monthly volatility is annualized by multiplying by 12.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Volatility scales with **√time**, not time: ×√12, not ×12.", "whyPool": "Under the stated assumptions, volatility scales with the square root of time."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Return and volatility use different common annualization rules in this scenario.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Returns compound (^12) while volatility scales by √12 — two distinct rules.", "whyPool": "Returns compound across periods, while volatility is scaled by sqrt(12) under the given assumptions. 279 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — annualizing returns and volatility; Investopedia, \"Annualized Volatility.\"", "n": 318}, {"id": "QF-D1G4Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A trading desk observes a mean daily strategy return of 0.04% and daily volatility of 1.2%. The desk uses 252 trading days per year and asks for annualized figures to compare with another strategy. Assume daily returns are independent and identically distributed for this simplified comparison.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Multiplying the mean daily return by 252 gives an arithmetic annualized-return approximation of 10.08%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "0.04% × 252 = **10.08%** (arithmetic/simple annualization).", "whyPool": "0.04% × 252 = 10.08%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Compounding 0.04% for 252 days gives an effective annual return of approximately 10.60%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "(1.0004)^252 − 1 ≈ **10.60%** (compounded > simple).", "whyPool": "(1.0004)^252 - 1 is approximately 10.60%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The annualized volatility is not approximately 19.05%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: 1.2% × √252 ≈ 1.2% × 15.87 ≈ **19.05%**. So it *is* ≈19.05%; negation reverses → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 1.2% × sqrt(252) is approximately 19.05%."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Annualized volatility should be calculated as 1.2% × 252.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Volatility scales with √252, not 252 — the ×252 version massively overstates it.", "whyPool": "Volatility scales with sqrt(252), not 252, under the stated assumptions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that the assumed number of trading days affects both reported annualized figures.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Twisted claim: both the return (×252) and volatility (×√252) *do* use the day count, so the underlying claim is correct → \"wrong to accept\" is False.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. Both calculations explicitly use the selected number of periods per year."}], "reference": "Bodie-Kane-Marcus, *Investments* — annualization conventions; Investopedia, \"Trading Days / Annualizing.\"", "n": 319}, {"id": "QF-D1G4Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G4", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk committee receives a report comparing Strategy X's 1.5% monthly return with Strategy Y's 14% annual return. The report declares X superior because 1.5 is greater than 14 after converting both percentages to decimals incorrectly. The committee asks you to put the returns on a consistent basis; assume X earns 1.5% every month.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The two quoted returns should not be converted to the same time horizon before comparison.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: valid comparison *requires* consistent units/horizons. The negation reverses this → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Valid comparison requires consistent units and horizons."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Strategy X's effective annual return is approximately 19.56%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "(1.015)^12 − 1 ≈ **19.56%**.", "whyPool": "(1.015)^12 - 1 is approximately 19.56%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Multiplying 1.5% by 12 gives an exact effective annual return of 18%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "18% omits monthly compounding; it is a simple approximation, not the exact effective rate (19.56%).", "whyPool": "18% omits monthly compounding and is only a simple annualized approximation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Under the stated constant-return assumption, Strategy X has the higher annual return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "19.56% (X) > 14% (Y) once both are annualized.", "whyPool": "Approximately 19.56% exceeds Strategy Y's 14% annual return."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Comparing a monthly figure directly with an annual figure can lead to an invalid conclusion even when both are expressed as percentages.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Percentage notation alone does not equalize measurement periods — horizon must match.", "whyPool": "Percentage notation alone does not make the measurement periods comparable. G5: Portfolio Return Aggregation"}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — time-scale consistency; Investopedia, \"Effective Annual Rate.\"", "n": 320}, {"id": "QF-D1G5Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A balanced fund begins the month with 60% invested in equities and 40% in bonds. During the month, equities return 10% and bonds return -5%. The portfolio manager wants the fund's return before fees and assumes there was no rebalancing or external cash flow during the month.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Equities contribute 6 percentage points to the portfolio return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "0.60 × 10% = **6.0pp** contribution.", "whyPool": "60% × 10% = a 6-percentage-point contribution."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Bonds contribute -2 percentage points to the portfolio return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "0.40 × (−5%) = **−2.0pp** contribution.", "whyPool": "40% × -5% = a -2-percentage-point contribution."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The portfolio return is 5%, the simple average of the two asset returns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Must weight by allocation: 0.60×10% + 0.40×(−5%) = 4%. Equal-weight average (2.5%... or the 5% midpoint) does not apply.", "whyPool": "Asset returns must be weighted by portfolio allocations; the equal-weight average is not applicable."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The portfolio return is not 4%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: 6% + (−2%) = **4%**. So it *is* 4%; the negation reverses this → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 6% - 2% = 4%."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The equity return and its contribution to portfolio return are the same because equities had a positive return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Return (10%) ≠ contribution (6pp); contribution = weight × return, so the 60% weight makes them differ regardless of sign.", "whyPool": "The asset earned 10%, but its portfolio contribution was 6 percentage points due to its 60% weight."}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — portfolio return aggregation; Investopedia, \"Weighted Average Return.\"", "n": 321}, {"id": "QF-D1G5Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A long-short equity fund begins the month 130% long Stock A and 30% short Stock B, leaving net exposure of 100% and gross exposure of 160%. During the month, Stock A rises 6% and Stock B rises 2%. Ignore financing costs, fees, and rebalancing. 281 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Stock A contributes 7.8 percentage points to portfolio return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "1.30 × 6% = **7.8pp** (leveraged long weight).", "whyPool": "1.30 × 6% = 7.8 percentage points."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The short position contributes -0.6 percentage points because Stock B increased in value.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "−0.30 × 2% = **−0.6pp**; a short loses when the shorted asset rises.", "whyPool": "The -0.30 weight multiplied by a +2% asset return contributes -0.6 percentage points."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The portfolio return is not 7.2%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: 7.8% + (−0.6%) = **7.2%**. So it *is* 7.2%; the negation reverses → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 7.8% - 0.6% = 7.2%."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The fund's 100% net exposure means leverage has no effect on its return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Gross exposure is 160%; the leveraged long and the short both affect return and risk regardless of net exposure.", "whyPool": "Gross exposure is 160%, and the leveraged long and short positions both affect return and risk."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If Stock B had instead fallen 2%, the short position would have contributed positively.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "−0.30 × (−2%) = **+0.6pp**; negative weight × negative return = positive contribution.", "whyPool": "A negative position weight multiplied by a negative asset return produces a positive contribution."}], "reference": "Bodie-Kane-Marcus, *Investments* — long/short weights and leverage; Investopedia, \"130-30 Strategy.\"", "n": 322}, {"id": "QF-D1G5Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G5", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A tactical portfolio invests 120% of capital in a market ETF and finances the extra 20% by borrowing at 1% for the period. The ETF returns 10% during that period. The chief investment officer asks for the portfolio return before any other fees and wants the effect of leverage made explicit.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The ETF position contributes 12 percentage points to portfolio return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "1.20 × 10% = **12pp** (levered long).", "whyPool": "A 120% weight multiplied by a 10% return contributes 12 percentage points."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The borrowing cost reduces portfolio return by 0.2 percentage points.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "0.20 borrowed × 1% = **0.2%** of portfolio capital, a drag.", "whyPool": "Borrowing 20% of capital at 1% costs 0.2% of portfolio capital."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The resulting portfolio return is not 11.8%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: 12% − 0.2% = **11.8%**. So it *is* 11.8%; negation reverses → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 12% - 0.2% = 11.8%."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because the portfolio's net invested weight is 100%, its return must equal the ETF's 10% return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Borrowing creates 120% levered exposure; both the enlarged position and the financing cost move the return away from 10%.", "whyPool": "Borrowing creates leveraged exposure, so both the 120% asset position and financing cost matter."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the ETF instead lost 10%, leverage would not magnify the loss before financing costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: a 120% position contributes −12% vs −10% unlevered — leverage *does* magnify the loss. Reverses the correct conclusion → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A 120% ETF position would contribute -12%, compared with - 10% without leverage, before borrowing cost. 282 Rev:081020261230 G6: Gross, Net, and Benchmark-Relative Performance"}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — leverage and return; Investopedia, \"Leverage / Margin.\"", "n": 323}, {"id": "QF-D1G6Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A systematic fund earns a 12% gross return for the year. Transaction costs equal 2% of beginning capital and the management fee equals 1% of beginning capital; for this question, subtract both directly from gross return. The fund's appropriate benchmark earns 8%, and the client asks whether the strategy added value after implementation costs and fees.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The fund's net return is 9%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "12% − 2% − 1% = **9%** under the stated simplification.", "whyPool": "12% - 2% - 1% = 9% under the stated simplification."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Its gross excess return over the benchmark is not 4 percentage points.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: 12% − 8% = **4pp**. So it *is* 4pp; negation reverses → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 12% - 8% = 4 percentage points."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Its net excess return over the benchmark is 1 percentage point.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "9% − 8% = **1pp**.", "whyPool": "9% - 8% = 1 percentage point."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because its gross return exceeded the benchmark, its net return must also exceed the benchmark regardless of costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Large enough costs/fees can erase or reverse gross outperformance — here they cut the 4pp gross edge to 1pp.", "whyPool": "Sufficiently large costs and fees can eliminate or reverse gross outperformance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Reporting only the 12% gross return would overstate the return retained by the investor under these assumptions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The investor keeps 9% net, not 12% — gross overstates what is actually retained.", "whyPool": "The investor retains 9%, not the 12% pre-cost result."}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — gross vs net and excess return; Investopedia, \"Excess Return / Net Return.\"", "n": 324}, {"id": "QF-D1G6Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A hedge fund earns 15% before fees on $100 million of beginning capital. Its agreement charges a 2% management fee on beginning capital and a 20% incentive fee on profits remaining after the management fee; ignore high-water marks and other expenses. An investor asks the analyst to calculate the simplified net result.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The management fee is not $2 million.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: 2% × $100M = **$2M**. So it *is* $2M; negation reverses → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 2% of $100 million is $2 million."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Profit remaining after the management fee is $13 million.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Gross profit $15M − $2M mgmt fee = **$13M**.", "whyPool": "The $15 million gross profit is reduced to $13 million after the management fee."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The incentive fee is $3 million because it is always 20% of gross profit.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Per the agreement, incentive fee = 20% of *post-management-fee* profit = 20% × $13M = **$2.6M**, not 20% of $15M.", "whyPool": "Under the stated agreement, the incentive fee is 20% of $13 million, or $2.6 million."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The investor's net profit is not $10.4 million.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: $15M − $2M − $2.6M = **$10.4M**. So it *is* $10.4M; negation reverses → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. $15 million - $2 million - $2.6 million = $10.4 million."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The investor's net return is 10.4% under the stated assumptions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$10.4M / $100M beginning capital = **10.4%**.", "whyPool": "$10.4 million divided by $100 million of beginning capital is 10.4%."}], "reference": "Bodie-Kane-Marcus, *Investments* — hedge fund fee structures; Investopedia, \"Two and Twenty / Incentive Fee.\"", "n": 325}, {"id": "QF-D1G6Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G6", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A technology-focused long-only fund loses 6% during a market selloff, while its agreed technology-sector benchmark loses 10%. A client says the manager failed because the fund had a negative absolute return; the manager says the fund succeeded because it beat the benchmark. The oversight committee asks you to distinguish the two claims.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The fund's absolute return is -6%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Absolute performance is the fund's own return: **−6%**.", "whyPool": "Absolute performance is the fund's own -6% return."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The fund's benchmark-relative return is not +4 percentage points.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: −6% − (−10%) = **+4pp**. So it *is* +4pp; negation reverses → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. -6% - (-10%) = +4 percentage points."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The fund can have a negative absolute return and a positive relative return at the same time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Losing less than the benchmark (−6% vs −10%) yields positive *relative* performance despite the absolute loss.", "whyPool": "Losing less than the benchmark creates positive relative performance despite an absolute loss."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Outperforming the benchmark means every investor objective was necessarily achieved.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Investors may also hold capital-preservation, risk, liability, or absolute-return objectives that a relative win doesn't satisfy.", "whyPool": "An investor may also have capital-preservation, risk, liability, or absolute-return objectives."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Replacing the technology benchmark with cash could materially change the assessment of relative performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Excess return depends on the chosen benchmark; vs cash (≈0%), −6% would look like underperformance — benchmark suitability matters.", "whyPool": "Excess return depends on the selected benchmark, so benchmark suitability matters. G7: Path Dependence and Return Sequencing"}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — benchmark selection and relative performance; Investopedia, \"Absolute vs Relative Return.\"", "n": 326}, {"id": "QF-D1G7Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G7", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio manager reports that a strategy gained 50% in the first year and lost 50% in the second year. She tells the investment committee that the average annual return was 0%, so the portfolio finished where it started. You are asked to assess that conclusion. 284 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The arithmetic average of the two annual returns is not 0%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: (50% + (−50%))/2 = **0%**. So it *is* 0%; the negation reverses this → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The arithmetic mean of +50% and -50% is 0%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The portfolio finishes with the same value it started with.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "$100 × 1.50 × 0.50 = **$75** — a 25% loss, not breakeven.", "whyPool": "A portfolio starting at $100 rises to $150 and then falls to $75."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A 50% loss requires a 100% gain to recover fully.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "To grow $75 back to $150 requires 150/75 − 1 = **100%** — recovery gain exceeds the loss %.", "whyPool": "A 100% gain is required to grow $75 back to $150."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The geometric average return over the two years is not negative.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: terminal wealth ($75) < start ($100), so the compound (geometric) rate *is* negative. Negation reverses → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Terminal wealth is below starting wealth, so the compounded average is negative."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The combination of gains and losses can produce terminal wealth below the starting value even when the arithmetic average return is 0%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Returns compound multiplicatively; equal % up/down moves don't cancel (volatility drag).", "whyPool": "Returns compound multiplicatively, and equal percentage gains and losses do not offset."}], "reference": "Investopedia, \"Volatility Drag / Recovery Math\"; CFA curriculum — geometric returns.", "n": 327}, {"id": "QF-D1G7Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G7", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two retirees each begin with $1 million and withdraw $100,000 at the end of each year. Portfolio A earns +20% in Year 1 and -20% in Year 2; Portfolio B earns -20% in Year 1 and +20% in Year 2. Their adviser claims the order cannot matter because both portfolios experience the same two returns. Ignore taxes and fees.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Before withdrawals, the same two returns would not produce the same terminal wealth regardless of their order.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: without cash flows, multiplication commutes — (1.20)(0.80) = (0.80)(1.20), so order *does not* matter and terminal wealth *is* the same. Negation reverses → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Without cash flows, multiplication is commutative: (1.20)(0.80) produces the same result in either order."}, {"L": "B", "text": "After the Year 1 return and withdrawal, Portfolio A has $1.1 million.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$1M × 1.20 − $100k = $1.2M − $0.1M = **$1.1M**.", "whyPool": "$1 million × 1.20 - $100,000 = $1.1 million."}, {"L": "C", "text": "After both returns and both withdrawals, Portfolio A has $800,000.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "$1.1M × 0.80 − $100k = $880k − $100k = **$780,000**, not $800k.", "whyPool": "$1.1 million × 0.80 - $100,000 = $780,000, not $800,000. 285 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "After both returns and both withdrawals, Portfolio B has $740,000.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$1M × 0.80 − $100k = $700k; then $700k × 1.20 − $100k = $840k − $100k = **$740,000**.", "whyPool": "$1 million × 0.80 - $100,000 = $700,000, then $700,000 × 1.20 - $100,000 = $740,000."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The withdrawal requirement creates sequence-of-returns risk even though the two portfolios have the same arithmetic and geometric average returns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Withdrawals change the capital exposed to later returns, so ordering ($780k vs $740k) matters — sequence risk.", "whyPool": "Withdrawals change the capital exposed to later returns, making their order economically important."}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — sequence-of-returns / cash-flow-weighted returns; Investopedia, \"Sequence Risk.\"", "n": 328}, {"id": "QF-D1G7Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "01", "domainName": "Investment Returns & Compounding", "group": "G7", "weeks": [1, 2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk committee reviews a strategy that falls from a previous peak value of $100 million to $70 million and later recovers to $91 million. The portfolio manager says the subsequent 30% gain completely erased the earlier 30% drawdown. The committee asks you to verify the recovery mathematics.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The decline from $100 million to $70 million is not a 30% drawdown.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double negative: (70 − 100)/100 = **−30%**. So it *is* a 30% drawdown; negation reverses → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. ($70 million - $100 million) / $100 million = -30%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A 30% gain on $70 million raises the portfolio to $91 million.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$70M × 1.30 = **$91M**.", "whyPool": "$70 million × 1.30 = $91 million."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that returning from $70 million to the prior $100 million peak requires approximately a 42.86% gain.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Twisted claim: 100/70 − 1 ≈ **42.86%** is correct, so accepting it is right → \"wrong to accept\" is False.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. $100 million / $70 million - 1 is approximately 42.86%."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A loss of x% is always recovered by a gain of x%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The recovery gain applies to a smaller post-loss base, so it must exceed x% (30% loss needs ≈42.86% gain).", "whyPool": "After a loss, the recovery gain is applied to a smaller base and must therefore be larger."}, {"L": "E", "text": "At $91 million, the portfolio remains 9% below its prior peak.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "(91 − 100)/100 = **−9%**; the 30% gain did not restore the peak.", "whyPool": "($91 million - $100 million) / $100 million = -9%. 286 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Investopedia, \"Drawdown / Recovery\"; CFA curriculum — return asymmetry.", "n": 329}, {"id": "QF-D2G1Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G1", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk committee compares two market-neutral strategies that each earned an average monthly return of 1%. Strategy A returned 1% in every observed month, while Strategy B alternated between 5% and -3%; the chief risk officer asks whether their identical average returns imply identical risk. 288 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Strategy A has zero historical return variance over the stated sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Every A observation equals its mean, so each deviation is 0 → variance = mean of squared deviations = 0. A perfectly constant series has zero historical variance.", "whyPool": "Every observed return for Strategy A equals its mean, so each deviation from the mean is zero."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Strategy B has greater historical return dispersion than Strategy A.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "B's returns swing far from the 1% mean (+4 and −4 deviations) while A's do not, so B has strictly greater historical dispersion.", "whyPool": "Strategy B's returns repeatedly deviate from the 1% mean, whereas Strategy A's do not."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The two strategies must have the same standard deviation because their arithmetic average returns are equal.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Equal *arithmetic means* say nothing about spread; two series with the same mean can have any variance. Equal average ≠ equal σ.", "whyPool": "Equal means do not imply equal dispersion or standard deviation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Variance is expressed in squared-return units, while standard deviation is expressed in the same units as return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Variance is in squared-return units; σ = √variance restores the original return units — the reason σ is the more interpretable risk figure.", "whyPool": "Standard deviation is the square root of variance and returns to the original return units."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Based only on these observations, Strategy B provided a smoother realized return path than Strategy A.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A's constant path is the *smoother* one; B's alternating ±returns form the rougher, more volatile path. The statement is backwards.", "whyPool": "Strategy A's constant returns form the smoother observed path."}], "reference": "Variance/standard deviation as dispersion — Investopedia \"Standard Deviation\" & \"Volatility\"; CFA curriculum (measures of dispersion).", "n": 330}, {"id": "QF-D2G1Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G1", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An asset allocator is choosing between Fund X, with a 12% expected annual return and 10% annual volatility, and Fund Y , with a 14% expected annual return and 24% annual volatility. The allocator's client wants growth but is concerned about year-to-year uncertainty and asks how to interpret the volatility estimates.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Fund Y has the higher expected raw return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "14% > 12%: Fund Y does have the higher expected *raw* return (before adjusting for risk).", "whyPool": "Fund Y's expected return is 14%, compared with 12% for Fund X."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Fund Y's return outcomes are estimated to be more dispersed around its expected return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A 24% σ vs. 10% σ means Y's outcomes are estimated to spread far more widely around its mean — greater dispersion.", "whyPool": "Its 24% standard deviation indicates greater estimated dispersion than Fund X's 10%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A 24% volatility estimate means Fund Y will lose exactly 24% in one out of every two years.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "σ is a dispersion measure, not a fixed loss size or a \"one-in-two-years\" frequency. Volatility does not promise a specific loss.", "whyPool": "Volatility is a dispersion measure, not a fixed loss amount or frequency guarantee."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Fund X's lower volatility guarantees it will never experience a large loss.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Lower σ reduces expected dispersion but does not eliminate tail events, estimation error, or the possibility of a large loss.", "whyPool": "Lower volatility does not eliminate tail events, estimation error, or the possibility of loss."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Choosing Fund Y solely because of its 14% expected return would not ignore a material difference in estimated risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Choosing Y \"solely for its 14% return\" *would* ignore materially higher risk (24% vs. 10% σ). The statement's double negative reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The higher expected return is accompanied by substantially higher estimated volatility. 289 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Volatility interpretation, risk-return tradeoff — Investopedia \"Volatility\"; CFA (return-risk tradeoff).", "n": 331}, {"id": "QF-D2G1Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G1", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quant team reviews a strategy whose first 23 monthly returns were clustered tightly around 0.5%, but whose twenty- fourth return was -18%. The portfolio manager wants to omit the final month as \"unrepresentative\" before presenting the strategy's risk statistics to an investment committee.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Including the -18% month will not generally increase the estimated variance relative to a sample containing only the tightly clustered months.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A return far from the rest creates a large squared deviation and generally *raises* measured variance. Saying it \"will not increase\" variance reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A return far from the rest of the sample creates a large deviation and generally raises measured dispersion."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Squaring deviations in the variance calculation makes a large deviation influential.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Variance squares deviations, so a large deviation is weighted disproportionately — one outlier can dominate the statistic.", "whyPool": "Squaring gives disproportionate weight to observations far from the mean."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Removing the observation solely because it worsens the reported risk could create a misleading comparison.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Removing a point only because it worsens reported risk understates the risk investors actually bore → a misleading comparison.", "whyPool": "Selective exclusion can understate the risk investors actually faced."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The extreme observation can affect standard deviation but cannot affect the sample's mean return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Every included observation affects the *mean* as well as dispersion. The −18% shifts the average return too.", "whyPool": "Every included observation affects the arithmetic mean as well as dispersion statistics."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A risk review should investigate whether the observation is erroneous or reflects a genuine strategy exposure before deciding how to treat it.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A data error and a genuine tail exposure require different treatment, so a risk review should establish the cause before deciding how to handle the point.", "whyPool": "Data errors and genuine tail outcomes require different treatment, so the cause matters. G2: Downside Risk and Drawdown"}], "reference": "Effect of outliers on variance; data-integrity vs. genuine tail — Investopedia \"Variance,\" \"Outlier\"; CFA (sample statistics).", "n": 332}, {"id": "QF-D2G2Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G2", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund rises from a net asset value of $100 to $125, declines to $90, and later recovers to $110. The fund manager highlights the $10 gain from inception, but a prospective investor asks the risk team to describe the loss experienced from the fund's high-water mark.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The decline from $125 to $90 is not a 28% drawdown.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "($90 − $125)/$125 = **−28%**, so the decline *is* a 28% drawdown. The negation makes the statement false.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. ($90 - $125) / $125 = -28%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The maximum drawdown over the stated path is not measured from the $125 peak to the $90 trough.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The max drawdown *is* measured from the $125 peak to the $90 trough (largest peak-to-subsequent-trough decline). The \"not\" reverses it.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. That is the largest stated peak-to-subsequent-trough decline."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because the ending value of $110 exceeds the initial $100, the fund experienced no drawdown.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Drawdown is measured from a prior peak, not from inception-to-ending. Ending above $100 does not mean \"no drawdown.\"", "whyPool": "Drawdown is measured relative to a prior peak, not only from inception to the ending value."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Recovering from $90 to the prior $125 peak requires a gain of approximately 38.9%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "To climb from $90 back to $125 needs $125/$90 − 1 ≈ **38.9%** — the recovery-asymmetry math.", "whyPool": "$125 / $90 - 1 is approximately 38.9%."}, {"L": "E", "text": "At the ending value of $110, the fund remains 12% below its prior peak. 290 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At $110, ($110 − $125)/$125 = **−12%**, so the fund is still 12% below its prior peak.", "whyPool": "($110 - $125) / $125 = -12%."}], "reference": "Drawdown / maximum drawdown definition, recovery asymmetry — Investopedia \"Maximum Drawdown,\" \"Drawdown.\"", "n": 333}, {"id": "QF-D2G2Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G2", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A pension plan compares Strategy A, which experiences frequent small fluctuations but never falls more than 6% from a prior peak, with Strategy B, which appears stable for years and then loses 30% before recovering. The investment committee has a 15% drawdown tolerance and asks whether ordinary volatility alone captures its concern.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Strategy B violates the committee's stated drawdown tolerance during the observed loss.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A 30% drawdown exceeds the stated 15% tolerance, so B violates it during the loss.", "whyPool": "A 30% drawdown exceeds the stated 15% tolerance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A strategy can show low volatility for much of a sample yet still experience a severe drawdown.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A rare large loss can follow a long calm stretch — low volatility for most of a sample does not preclude a deep drawdown.", "whyPool": "A rare large loss may follow a long period of apparent stability."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Maximum drawdown and return standard deviation measure exactly the same feature of a return path.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "σ measures dispersion around the mean; drawdown measures a path-dependent peak-to-trough loss. They are *not* the same feature.", "whyPool": "Standard deviation measures dispersion, while drawdown measures a path-dependent peak-to-trough loss."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Strategy A's frequent fluctuations necessarily make its maximum drawdown larger than Strategy B's.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The scenario fixes A's MDD at 6% and B's at 30%; frequent small fluctuations do not make A's max drawdown larger.", "whyPool": "The scenario states maximum drawdowns of 6% and 30%, respectively."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Drawdown is relevant to the pension because a sufficiently deep loss may trigger governance, liquidity, or behavioral constraints even if the strategy later recovers.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A deep enough loss can trigger governance, liquidity, or behavioral constraints even if the strategy later recovers — path and depth matter operationally.", "whyPool": "The path and depth of loss can matter operationally and behaviorally, not only the terminal return."}], "reference": "Volatility vs. drawdown distinction — Investopedia \"Maximum Drawdown\"; CFA (path-dependent risk / downside risk).", "n": 334}, {"id": "QF-D2G2Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G2", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A family office owns two strategies with the same annualized volatility and the same ending wealth. Strategy X suffered a 25% midyear drawdown and then recovered, while Strategy Y remained within 8% of its running peak; the family office may need to withdraw capital during the year. 291 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The equal ending wealth proves the investor faced identical path-dependent risk in both strategies.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Identical endpoints can conceal very different interim losses and liquidity consequences — equal ending wealth does not prove equal path-dependent risk.", "whyPool": "Identical endpoints can conceal materially different interim losses and liquidity consequences."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Strategy X had the larger maximum drawdown.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "X's 25% max drawdown exceeds Y's 8% bound, so X had the larger MDD.", "whyPool": "Its 25% maximum drawdown exceeds Strategy Y's stated 8% bound."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that the possibility of an interim withdrawal makes the timing and depth of losses economically relevant.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "*Embedded-claim double negative.* The inner claim (interim withdrawals make loss timing/depth relevant) is correct and should be accepted; a withdrawal during a deep drawdown forces loss realization and leaves less capital to recover — so \"a reviewer would be wrong to accept\" it is false.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. A withdrawal during a deep drawdown may force realization of losses and leave less capital for recovery."}, {"L": "D", "text": "After a 25% loss from a peak, a 25% gain from the trough is sufficient to regain that peak.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Recovery asymmetry: a 25% loss needs 1/0.75 − 1 ≈ **33.3%** gain to recover, not 25%.", "whyPool": "A 25% loss requires a gain of 1 / 0.75 - 1, or approximately 33.3%, to recover."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Reporting both volatility and drawdown can reveal risk differences hidden by either ending wealth or volatility alone.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "σ and drawdown describe different aspects of risk; reporting both reveals differences hidden by ending wealth or volatility alone.", "whyPool": "The measures describe different aspects of risk and are complementary here. G3: Sharpe Ratio"}], "reference": "Path dependence, recovery asymmetry, complementary risk measures — Investopedia \"Maximum Drawdown,\" \"Drawdown\"; CFA (liquidity risk).", "n": 335}, {"id": "QF-D2G3Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G3", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A client is comparing two funds. Fund A returned 12% with 20% volatility, while Fund B returned 9% with 8% volatility. The risk-free rate was 2%. The client initially prefers Fund A because its raw return was higher.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Fund A has the higher excess return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Excess return = return − rf. A: 12 − 2 = 10%; B: 9 − 2 = 7%. A has the higher excess return.", "whyPool": "Fund A's excess return is 10%, compared with 7% for Fund B."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Fund B has the higher Sharpe ratio.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Sharpe = excess/σ. A: 10/20 = **0.50**; B: 7/8 = **0.875**. B has the higher Sharpe despite lower raw return.", "whyPool": "Fund A's Sharpe ratio is 0.50, while Fund B's is 0.875."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Fund A must be the better investment because it earned the higher return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Raw return ignores risk. A earned more but per unit of volatility B is more efficient, so \"must be better\" is false.", "whyPool": "Raw return alone does not account for risk."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The risk-free rate should not be subtracted from return before computing the Sharpe ratio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The risk-free rate *must* be subtracted — Sharpe uses *excess* return. Saying it should not be reverses the definition.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The Sharpe ratio uses excess return over the risk-free rate. 292 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "A higher Sharpe ratio indicates more excess return per unit of volatility.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A higher Sharpe means more excess return per unit of total volatility — the core interpretation.", "whyPool": "That is the core interpretation of the Sharpe ratio."}], "reference": "Sharpe ratio definition — Investopedia \"Sharpe Ratio\"; CFA (risk-adjusted performance).", "n": 336}, {"id": "QF-D2G3Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G3", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A trading desk estimates a strategy's average daily excess return at 0.04% and its daily return standard deviation at 0.80%. The head of trading asks for an annualized Sharpe ratio using 252 trading days and assumes the daily observations are independent and identically distributed for this calculation.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The strategy's daily Sharpe ratio is 0.05.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Daily Sharpe = 0.04%/0.80% = **0.05**.", "whyPool": "0.04% / 0.80% = 0.05."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Under the stated assumptions, the annualized Sharpe ratio is not approximately 0.79.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Annualized = 0.05 × √252 ≈ **0.79**, so it *is* ~0.79; the \"not\" makes the statement false.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 0.05 × sqrt(252) is approximately 0.79."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Annualizing the Sharpe ratio requires multiplying the daily ratio by 252.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Under IID returns the ratio scales by **√252**, not ×252 — mean scales with T while σ scales with √T, so the ratio scales with √T.", "whyPool": "Under the stated assumptions, the ratio scales by sqrt(252), not 252."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The square-root-of-time adjustment relies on assumptions that may fail when returns are autocorrelated or volatility changes over time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The √time adjustment assumes IID returns; autocorrelation or time-varying volatility breaks it.", "whyPool": "Dependence and time-varying volatility undermine the simple square-root-of-time rule."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the desk annualizes the mean excess return, it must not also place volatility on a consistent annual scale before forming the ratio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "If the mean is annualized, σ must also be put on the annual scale — numerator and denominator must use compatible time units. Saying it \"must not\" reverses the rule.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The numerator and denominator must use compatible time units."}], "reference": "Sharpe annualization / √time rule — Investopedia \"Sharpe Ratio\" (annualizing); CFA (return/volatility scaling).", "n": 337}, {"id": "QF-D2G3Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G3", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An investment committee compares a liquid equity strategy with a short-volatility strategy. The short-volatility strategy reports a high historical Sharpe ratio because it earned small, steady gains before one rare, severe loss; the chief risk officer warns that the return distribution is highly skewed and fat-tailed.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A high historical Sharpe ratio by itself proves that the short-volatility strategy has little tail risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A high historical Sharpe summarizes past excess-return-to-σ; it does not prove low tail risk, especially in a benign window.", "whyPool": "Sharpe summarizes historical excess return relative to volatility and does not prove tail safety."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Standard deviation may incompletely describe the risk of a non-normal return distribution.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "σ (the Sharpe denominator) does not capture skewness, fat tails, or discontinuous losses — it incompletely describes non-normal risk.", "whyPool": "Skewness, fat tails, and discontinuous losses are not fully captured by standard deviation."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Rare losses can be underrepresented when the estimation window contains few or no tail events.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If the estimation window contains few/no tail events, rare losses are underrepresented and the strategy looks unusually stable.", "whyPool": "A benign sample can make a tail-sensitive strategy appear unusually stable."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Two strategies with the same Sharpe ratio must have the same drawdown profile and loss distribution. 293 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The same Sharpe can arise from very different return paths and loss distributions, so equal Sharpe ≠ equal drawdown profile.", "whyPool": "The same ratio can arise from very different return paths and distributions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The committee should supplement Sharpe with measures or scenario analyses that address downside and tail behavior.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Drawdown, downside metrics, stress tests, and tail analysis expose risks Sharpe misses — the committee should supplement it.", "whyPool": "Drawdown, downside metrics, stress tests, and tail analysis can expose risks Sharpe misses. G4: Sortino Ratio and Downside-Focused Metrics"}], "reference": "Sharpe limitations under non-normality — Investopedia \"Sharpe Ratio\" (limitations); CFA (skewness/kurtosis, tail risk).", "n": 338}, {"id": "QF-D2G4Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G4", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A wealth manager compares two funds with the same average return above a 3% minimum acceptable return. Fund A's variability comes mainly from unexpectedly strong gains, while Fund B frequently falls below the 3% target; the client says she is concerned about shortfalls, not positive surprises.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The Sortino ratio is not aligned more directly than the Sharpe ratio with the client's stated concern about downside shortfalls.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Sortino relates return above a target to *downside* deviation, so it *is* more aligned than Sharpe with a downside concern. The \"not\" reverses this.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Sortino relates return above a target to downside deviation below that target."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The Sharpe ratio treats upside and downside deviations as contributors to total volatility.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Sharpe uses total σ, which counts upside and downside deviations alike — it does not distinguish favorable from unfavorable moves.", "whyPool": "Total standard deviation does not distinguish favorable from unfavorable deviations."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Positive returns far above the target necessarily increase downside deviation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Downside deviation counts only observations *below* the target; large positive returns do not increase it.", "whyPool": "Downside deviation focuses on observations below the selected target, not large positive outcomes."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Fund A could have a better Sortino ratio than Fund B even if their total standard deviations are similar.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With similar total σ, A (upside-driven variability) can have a better Sortino than B (frequent shortfalls) — downside behavior differs.", "whyPool": "Similar total volatility can mask materially different downside behavior."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A Sortino ratio can be interpreted without specifying or understanding the return threshold used in its calculation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The chosen target drives both shortfall identification and the ratio's value, so it must be specified to interpret Sortino. Saying it can be ignored is false.", "whyPool": "The chosen target affects both shortfall identification and the resulting ratio. 294 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Sortino ratio / downside deviation vs. Sharpe — Investopedia \"Sortino Ratio,\" \"Downside Deviation.\"", "n": 339}, {"id": "QF-D2G4Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G4", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A retirement fund evaluates Strategy X, which has an 8% annual return and 4% downside deviation, and Strategy Y , which has a 10% annual return and 8% downside deviation. Using a 2% annual minimum acceptable return, the investment officer asks which strategy has provided more return above the target per unit of downside risk.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Strategy X's Sortino ratio is 1.5.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Sortino = (return − MAR)/downside deviation = (8 − 2)/4 = **1.5**.", "whyPool": "(8% - 2%) / 4% = 1.5."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Strategy Y's Sortino ratio is 1.0.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Y: (10 − 2)/8 = **1.0**.", "whyPool": "(10% - 2%) / 8% = 1.0."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Strategy Y has the higher Sortino ratio because it has the higher raw return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Y's higher raw return is more than offset by its larger downside deviation, so higher raw return does not make its Sortino higher.", "whyPool": "Its greater downside deviation more than offsets its higher raw return in the ratio."}, {"L": "D", "text": "On this metric and under the stated inputs, Strategy X has better downside-adjusted performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "X's Sortino 1.5 > Y's 1.0, so X has better downside-adjusted performance under these inputs.", "whyPool": "Strategy X's Sortino ratio of 1.5 exceeds Strategy Y's 1.0."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Changing the minimum acceptable return could not change both ratios and potentially affect the comparison.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Changing the MAR *would* change both numerators and which outcomes count as shortfalls, so it can affect the comparison. Saying it \"could not\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The target changes the excess-return numerator and which outcomes count as shortfalls."}], "reference": "Sortino calculation and MAR sensitivity — Investopedia \"Sortino Ratio,\" \"Downside Deviation.\"", "n": 340}, {"id": "QF-D2G4Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G4", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A hedge fund presents a very high Sortino ratio based on only 18 months of returns, during which no month fell below its chosen 0% target. The due-diligence team knows the strategy sells disaster insurance and can incur rare losses, so it questions whether the ratio is reliable.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The absence of observed negative months proves that the strategy has no downside risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "No observed negative months does not prove no downside risk — an unobserved tail event can still be plausible and central to the strategy.", "whyPool": "An unobserved tail event can still be economically plausible and central to the strategy."}, {"L": "B", "text": "With little or no observed downside deviation, the estimated Sortino ratio may be extremely high or not meaningfully defined.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With little/no downside deviation the denominator is near zero → the ratio is extremely large or undefined (division by ~0).", "whyPool": "A near-zero denominator makes the ratio unstable, and a zero denominator prevents an ordinary finite calculation."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A short sample cannot fail to capture the rare events that generate the strategy's principal risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A short sample *can* fail to capture rare events; rare-loss strategies look exceptionally safe in benign windows. The \"cannot fail\" phrasing reverses this.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Rare-loss strategies may look exceptionally safe during benign windows."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Sortino always resolves the non-normal-return limitations associated with the Sharpe ratio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Downside focus helps with one Sharpe limitation but does not eliminate sampling, tail, or estimation problems.", "whyPool": "Downside focus helps with one limitation but does not eliminate sampling, tail, or estimation problems."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Stress testing and a longer, relevant history would help assess the downside exposure not represented in the sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stress testing and a longer, relevant history help assess downside exposure absent from the observed record.", "whyPool": "Additional evidence is needed to evaluate losses absent from the observed record. G5: Information Ratio and Active Risk"}], "reference": "Sortino instability with near-zero denominator, short-sample tail risk — Investopedia \"Sortino Ratio\" (limitations); CFA (sampling/estimation risk).", "n": 341}, {"id": "QF-D2G5Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G5", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An active equity manager returned 11% while its benchmark returned 8%, and the standard deviation of monthly benchmark-relative returns, annualized, was 6%. The pension board asks whether the manager used active risk efficiently rather than merely benefiting from a rising market.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The manager's active return was 3%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Active return = portfolio − benchmark = 11 − 8 = **3%**.", "whyPool": "11% - 8% = 3% active return."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The stated tracking error is not 6%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Tracking error is the volatility of benchmark-relative returns, given as 6% annualized — so it *is* 6%. The \"not\" reverses it.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Tracking error is the volatility of benchmark-relative returns, given here as 6% annualized."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The manager's information ratio is 0.50 under the stated annual figures.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "IR = active return / tracking error = 3/6 = **0.50**.", "whyPool": "3% / 6% = 0.50."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The benchmark's 8% return should be treated as the risk-free rate when computing the manager's Sharpe ratio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A benchmark and a risk-free asset serve different roles; benchmark return drives active-return analysis, not the Sharpe risk-free rate.", "whyPool": "A benchmark and a risk-free asset serve different roles; benchmark return is used for active-return analysis."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A positive information ratio here indicates that realized return exceeded the benchmark per unit of tracking error.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A positive IR means realized return exceeded the benchmark per unit of tracking error (positive numerator over active-risk denominator).", "whyPool": "The numerator is positive active return and the denominator is active risk."}], "reference": "Information ratio & tracking error — Grinold & Kahn \"Active Portfolio Management\" (Information Ratio); Investopedia \"Information Ratio,\" \"Tracking Error.\"", "n": 342}, {"id": "QF-D2G5Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G5", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A foundation compares Manager A, who beat the benchmark by 2% with 2.5% tracking error, and Manager B, who beat it by 4% with 8% tracking error. The foundation wants consistent benchmark-relative performance and initially favors Manager B because of the larger excess return. 296 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Manager A's information ratio is 0.80.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "IR = 2%/2.5% = **0.80** for Manager A.", "whyPool": "2% / 2.5% = 0.80."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Manager B's information ratio is not 0.50.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "B's IR = 4%/8% = **0.50**, so \"not 0.50\" is false.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 4% / 8% = 0.50."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Manager B used active risk more efficiently because its active return was twice as large.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "B earned more active return but used proportionally far more active risk, so it did not use risk more efficiently.", "whyPool": "Manager B earned more active return but used proportionally much more active risk."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Manager A ranks higher by information ratio under the stated figures.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A's IR 0.80 > B's 0.50, so A ranks higher by information ratio.", "whyPool": "0.80 exceeds 0.50."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The information-ratio comparison alone determines which manager is appropriate regardless of the foundation's constraints or confidence in the estimates.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "IR alone does not settle the choice — mandate constraints, estimate reliability, fees, capacity, and risk tolerance also matter.", "whyPool": "Mandate constraints, estimation reliability, fees, capacity, and risk tolerance may also affect selection."}], "reference": "IR comparison and selection caveats — Grinold & Kahn \"Active Portfolio Management\"; Investopedia \"Information Ratio.\"", "n": 343}, {"id": "QF-D2G5Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G5", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A global portfolio earned 10% with 12% total volatility, while its policy benchmark earned 9%; the portfolio's tracking error was 2%. The chief investment officer asks analysts to distinguish an information-ratio evaluation from a Sharpe-ratio evaluation and notes that the risk-free rate was 3%.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The portfolio's information ratio is 0.50.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Active return = 10 − 9 = 1%; IR = 1/2 = **0.50**.", "whyPool": "Active return is 1%, and 1% / 2% = 0.50."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The portfolio's Sharpe ratio is approximately 0.58.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Sharpe = (10 − 3)/12 = 7/12 ≈ **0.58**.", "whyPool": "(10% - 3%) / 12% is approximately 0.58."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Information ratio uses total portfolio volatility as its denominator.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "IR uses **tracking error** (volatility of active returns), not total portfolio volatility.", "whyPool": "Information ratio uses tracking error, the volatility of active returns."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Sharpe ratio evaluates return above the risk-free rate, whereas information ratio evaluates return above a benchmark.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Sharpe measures return above the risk-free rate; IR measures return above a benchmark — different reference returns and risk denominators.", "whyPool": "The ratios use different reference returns and different risk denominators. 297 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "A portfolio cannot have a modest Sharpe ratio but a strong information ratio if it tracks a risky benchmark closely and adds consistent value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A portfolio *can* have a modest Sharpe yet a strong IR if it tracks a risky benchmark closely and adds consistent value — the properties are distinct, so \"cannot\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Benchmark-relative consistency and total risk-adjusted performance are distinct properties. G6: Risk-Return Tradeoffs"}], "reference": "IR vs. Sharpe (reference return & denominator) — Grinold & Kahn \"Active Portfolio Management\"; Investopedia \"Information Ratio,\" \"Sharpe Ratio.\"", "n": 344}, {"id": "QF-D2G6Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G6", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A university endowment compares Strategy A, expected to return 9% with 7% volatility, and Strategy B, expected to return 13% with 18% volatility. The endowment needs at least 8% expected return but strongly values spending stability; the investment committee asks whether Strategy B's higher return automatically makes it preferable.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Both strategies exceed the stated 8% expected-return requirement.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "9% and 13% both exceed the 8% requirement.", "whyPool": "Their expected returns are 9% and 13%, both above 8%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Strategy B offers 4 percentage points more expected return and 11 percentage points more estimated volatility.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Differences are 13 − 9 = 4 percentage points of return and 18 − 7 = 11 percentage points of volatility.", "whyPool": "The differences are 13% - 9% = 4% in return and 18% - 7% = 11% in volatility."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Strategy B is necessarily preferable because rational investors always choose the highest expected return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Rational investors weigh risk, constraints, funding needs, and preferences — not just the highest expected return, so B is not \"necessarily preferable.\"", "whyPool": "Investors also consider risk, constraints, funding needs, and preferences."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that the endowment's concern about spending stability makes the difference in volatility relevant to its selection.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "*Embedded-claim double negative.* The inner claim (spending-stability concern makes the volatility difference relevant) is correct and should be accepted; a more volatile path may disrupt planned spending — so \"would be wrong to accept\" it is false.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. A more volatile asset path may disrupt planned spending or governance tolerance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A risk-adjusted comparison cannot inform the decision but does not eliminate the need to consider the investor's objectives and constraints.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A risk-adjusted comparison *can* inform the decision (it just doesn't dictate a universal choice). Saying it \"cannot inform\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Metrics summarize tradeoffs but do not specify a universal choice for every investor."}], "reference": "Risk-return tradeoff, investor objectives/constraints — CFA (portfolio objectives & constraints); Investopedia \"Risk-Return Tradeoff.\"", "n": 345}, {"id": "QF-D2G6Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G6", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A leveraged trading strategy earned 18% with 30% volatility, while an unlevered strategy earned 10% with 10% volatility; the risk-free rate was 2%. A portfolio manager argues that the leveraged strategy is superior because it earned nearly twice the raw return.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The leveraged strategy's Sharpe ratio is not approximately 0.53.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Leveraged Sharpe = (18 − 2)/30 ≈ **0.53**, so \"not ~0.53\" is false.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. (18% - 2%) / 30% is approximately 0.53."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The unlevered strategy's Sharpe ratio is 0.80. 298 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Unlevered Sharpe = (10 − 2)/10 = **0.80**.", "whyPool": "(10% - 2%) / 10% = 0.80."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The leveraged strategy has both the higher raw return and the higher Sharpe ratio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The leveraged strategy has the higher raw return but the *lower* Sharpe (0.53 < 0.80), so \"both higher\" is false.", "whyPool": "It has the higher return but the lower Sharpe ratio."}, {"L": "D", "text": "On a Sharpe-ratio basis, the unlevered strategy used total risk more efficiently over the stated period.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "On a Sharpe basis, the unlevered strategy (0.80 > 0.53) used total risk more efficiently over the period.", "whyPool": "Its Sharpe ratio of 0.80 exceeds approximately 0.53."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The comparison does not by itself address leverage constraints, tail losses, or implementation costs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Return and σ alone omit leverage constraints, tail losses, and implementation costs — the comparison does not address these.", "whyPool": "Those considerations are not fully described by the provided return and volatility figures."}], "reference": "Leverage and risk-adjusted return — Investopedia \"Sharpe Ratio,\" \"Leverage\"; CFA (leverage effects on risk).", "n": 346}, {"id": "QF-D2G6Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G6", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A retiree and a well-capitalized proprietary trading firm are considering the same volatile strategy. The retiree depends on near-term withdrawals and cannot tolerate a 20% drawdown, while the trading firm has long-dated capital and an explicit 35% drawdown limit; an adviser asks whether one universal ranking should govern both decisions.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The same strategy cannot be acceptable to one investor and unsuitable for another because their constraints differ.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The same strategy *can* be acceptable to one investor and unsuitable for another — suitability depends on objectives, horizon, liquidity, and loss-bearing capacity. The claim reverses this.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Suitability depends on objectives, horizon, liquidity, and loss- bearing capacity."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The retiree's withdrawal needs make downside timing and drawdown particularly relevant.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The retiree's withdrawal needs make loss timing and drawdown particularly relevant (losses near withdrawals impair spending and recovery capital).", "whyPool": "Losses near withdrawals can impair capital available for both spending and recovery."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the strategy has a positive expected return, the retiree's drawdown limit becomes irrelevant.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A positive expected return does not prevent an unacceptable interim loss, so the retiree's drawdown limit stays relevant.", "whyPool": "Positive expected return does not prevent an unacceptable interim loss."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A single risk-adjusted metric completely captures liquidity needs, horizon, and behavioral tolerance for both investors.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Liquidity needs, horizon, and behavioral tolerance require information beyond one summary statistic.", "whyPool": "These constraints require information beyond one summary statistic."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Strategy selection should connect measured risk and expected return to the decision maker's capacity and willingness to bear loss.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Strategy selection should tie measured risk and expected return to each decision maker's capacity and willingness to bear loss — evaluation is investor-specific.", "whyPool": "Risk-return evaluation is investor-specific rather than a context-free ranking exercise. 299 Rev:081020261230 G7: Limitations of Risk Metrics"}], "reference": "Investor-specific suitability, drawdown tolerance — CFA (IPS, risk tolerance/ability); Investopedia \"Risk Tolerance,\" \"Maximum Drawdown.\"", "n": 347}, {"id": "QF-D2G7Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G7", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A venture fund and a loss-prone trading strategy report the same annual return standard deviation. The venture fund's deviations were mostly unexpectedly large gains, while the trading strategy's deviations were mostly losses; a limited partner asks whether standard deviation alone makes their risk equally attractive.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Standard deviation treats equally sized deviations above and below the mean symmetrically.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "σ squares deviations regardless of sign, so equal-sized moves above and below the mean count symmetrically.", "whyPool": "Squared deviations enter the calculation regardless of their direction."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Equal standard deviations imply that the two return distributions have identical skewness and tail behavior.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Equal σ does not imply identical skewness or tail behavior — distributions can share a σ yet differ greatly in shape.", "whyPool": "Distributions can share a standard deviation while differing greatly in shape and tails."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A downside-focused measure could distinguish features that total standard deviation obscures in this scenario.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A downside-focused measure (downside deviation, loss-based analysis) can distinguish the loss-heavy strategy from the gain-heavy one that σ obscures.", "whyPool": "Downside deviation or loss-based analysis can focus on unfavorable outcomes."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Large positive surprises can raise measured volatility even though investors may welcome them.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Large positive surprises raise measured σ even though investors welcome them — σ counts favorable dispersion as \"risk.\"", "whyPool": "Upside observations far above the mean increase total measured dispersion."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because standard deviation is imperfect, it contains no useful information about return dispersion.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "σ is imperfect but still informative about dispersion; \"no useful information\" overstates the limitation.", "whyPool": "It remains informative about dispersion even though it does not capture every dimension of risk."}], "reference": "σ symmetry limitation, downside measures — Investopedia \"Standard Deviation,\" \"Downside Deviation\"; CFA (skewness/kurtosis).", "n": 348}, {"id": "QF-D2G7Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G7", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A manager markets a strategy using a Sharpe ratio calculated only over the most recent three calm years, although the strategy performed poorly during an earlier crisis. A due-diligence analyst worries that the chosen window and the manager's presentation choices make the comparison unreliable.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A risk-adjusted metric can change materially when the sample period changes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Estimated means, volatility, and ratios depend on the observations included, so a risk-adjusted metric can change materially with the sample period.", "whyPool": "Estimated means, volatility, and ratios depend on the observations included."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Excluding a relevant stress period cannot make historical risk appear lower and performance appear more favorable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Excluding a relevant stress period *can* make risk look lower and performance more favorable — the \"cannot\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Removing adverse but relevant outcomes can understate the strategy's historical vulnerability."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The most recent three-year window is necessarily the only valid sample because newer data always dominate older evidence.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Relevance depends on the strategy and regime; recency alone does not make the three-year window uniquely valid.", "whyPool": "Relevance depends on the strategy and regime; recency alone does not make one window uniquely valid."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Applying the same disclosed methodology and economically comparable period across strategies improves comparability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Applying the same disclosed methodology over an economically comparable period across strategies improves comparability and reduces presentation bias.", "whyPool": "Consistent methods reduce one important source of presentation bias."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A precisely calculated ratio cannot be misleading if the underlying inputs were selectively chosen. 300 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Arithmetic precision does not cure biased sample selection or bad assumptions — a precisely computed ratio can still mislead.", "whyPool": "Arithmetic precision does not cure biased sample selection or inappropriate assumptions."}], "reference": "Sample-period sensitivity, metric gaming — Investopedia \"Sharpe Ratio\" (limitations), \"Backtesting bias\"; CFA (survivorship/selection bias).", "n": 349}, {"id": "QF-D2G7Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "02", "domainName": "Risk & Risk-Adjusted Performance", "group": "G7", "weeks": [2], "weekMax": 2, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A machine-learning strategy has an excellent backtested Sharpe ratio and low maximum drawdown after extensive hyperparameter tuning. Before allocating capital, the risk committee notes that the estimates are based on one historical sample, returns are fat-tailed, and many model variants were tried.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Extensive selection among model variants can inflate the reported performance of the chosen backtest.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Choosing the best of many trials capitalizes on noise → selection bias inflates the chosen backtest's reported performance.", "whyPool": "Choosing the best result from many trials can capitalize on noise and create selection bias."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Historical estimates are guaranteed to remain stable when the strategy begins live trading.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Historical estimates are *not* guaranteed to persist live — regime change, implementation, estimation error, and overfitting can alter results.", "whyPool": "Market regimes, implementation, estimation error, and overfitting can alter live results."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Fat-tailed returns make extreme outcomes more plausible than a normal-distribution interpretation may suggest.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Fat tails assign more probability to extreme outcomes than a normal model implies, making extremes more plausible.", "whyPool": "Fat tails assign more probability to extreme outcomes than a comparable normal distribution."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Low backtested maximum drawdown establishes a hard upper bound on future drawdown.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Backtested max drawdown is an observed sample statistic, not a guaranteed future upper bound.", "whyPool": "Historical maximum drawdown is an observed sample statistic, not a guaranteed future limit."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Out-of-sample testing, sensitivity analysis, and stress testing can provide evidence beyond the headline historical metrics.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Out-of-sample testing, sensitivity analysis, and stress testing provide evidence beyond headline historical metrics and probe robustness.", "whyPool": "These checks probe robustness and risks that a single optimized backtest can conceal. 301 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Backtest overfitting, fat tails, MDD as sample statistic — Investopedia \"Backtesting,\" \"Fat Tail,\" \"Maximum Drawdown\"; CFA (data-mining/overfitting bias).", "n": 350}, {"id": "QF-D3G1Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G1", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A family office holds two strategies with similar expected returns and volatilities. Their historical return correlation is - 0.60, and the investment committee asks whether this means one strategy reliably gains whenever the other loses.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The negative correlation indicates that the strategies have tended to move in opposite directions, but not in every period.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Negative correlation summarizes a *tendency* toward opposite co-movement over the sample, not an exceptionless period-by-period rule — some periods can still move together.", "whyPool": "Correlation summarizes a tendency toward opposite co-movement, not an exceptionless period-by-period rule."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A correlation of -0.60 implies that 60% of one strategy's losses were exactly offset by gains in the other. 303 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Correlation is a standardized measure of *linear* co-movement in [-1, +1]; it is not an \"offset percentage.\" ρ = -0.60 does not mean 60% of one strategy's losses were exactly offset.", "whyPool": "Correlation is a standardized measure of linear co-movement, not an offset percentage."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the correlation moved from -0.60 to +0.60 while other inputs stayed fixed, the volatility of a long-only combination would generally increase.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Moving ρ from -0.60 to +0.60 (weights and vols fixed) raises the covariance term `2·w_A·w_B·ρ·σ_Aσ_B`; with positive weights that increases portfolio variance and volatility.", "whyPool": "A more positive covariance term increases portfolio variance when weights and volatilities are positive."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Correlation alone establishes that movements in one strategy cause movements in the other.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A statistical association does not establish causation — correlation alone cannot show that one strategy's moves *cause* the other's.", "whyPool": "A statistical association does not by itself establish causation."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Correlation is not bounded between -1 and +1.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Embedded-claim / reverses the conclusion:** standardized correlation is bounded to [-1, +1]; saying it \"is not bounded\" reverses the correct fact, so the statement is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Standardized correlation cannot fall below -1 or exceed +1."}], "reference": "Correlation definition and bounds — Investopedia \"Correlation Coefficient\"; Bodie-Kane-Marcus *Investments*, risk & return chapter (correlation vs. causation).", "n": 351}, {"id": "QF-D3G1Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G1", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A pension fund compares Asset A with two possible diversifiers. Asset B has a 0.05 historical correlation with A, while Asset C has a 0.70 correlation with A; the chief investment officer proposes calling B “independent” and C “the same trade. ”", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Asset B's correlation near zero indicates little historical linear co-movement with Asset A.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A correlation near zero indicates little *linear* co-movement with A in the observed sample.", "whyPool": "A value near zero indicates little linear association in the observed sample."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A 0.05 correlation proves that A and B are statistically and economically independent.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Near-zero linear correlation does not prove statistical or economic independence — nonlinear dependence or other relationships can remain.", "whyPool": "Zero or near-zero linear correlation does not rule out nonlinear dependence or other relationships."}, {"L": "C", "text": "All else equal, B would ordinarily offer more diversification relative to A than C would.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Lower correlation generally reduces the covariance contribution to combined variance, so B ordinarily offers more diversification relative to A than C does.", "whyPool": "Lower correlation generally reduces the covariance contribution to the combined portfolio's variance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A 0.70 correlation means A and C always have returns with the same sign.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A 0.70 correlation is a strong-but-imperfect linear tendency; it still allows periods where A and C returns have different signs.", "whyPool": "A strong positive correlation allows periods in which returns have different signs."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The correlations should be interpreted with the sample period and estimation uncertainty in mind.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correlations are estimates that vary with the sample period and regime, so they should be read with estimation uncertainty in mind.", "whyPool": "Correlation is an estimate that can vary with the data window and market regime. 304 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Correlation vs. covariance and independence — Investopedia \"Covariance vs. Correlation\"; CFA curriculum, portfolio risk & return (correlation inputs, estimation uncertainty).", "n": 352}, {"id": "QF-D3G1Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G1", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A multi-asset fund holds equal weights in two assets, each with 20% annual volatility. A risk analyst reports that their estimated correlation rose from 0.10 to 0.80, although expected returns and individual volatilities were unchanged; the risk committee must decide whether to reduce exposure.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The rise in correlation increases the covariance between the assets under the stated assumptions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "`Cov = ρ·σ_Aσ_B`; with σ's fixed, raising ρ from 0.10 to 0.80 raises covariance directly.", "whyPool": "Covariance equals correlation times the two standard deviations, which are unchanged here."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The portfolio's expected return must fall because the correlation rose.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Portfolio *expected return* is the weighted average of the two expected returns and is unaffected by correlation; only the variance calculation changes.", "whyPool": "Correlation affects the portfolio variance calculation, not the weighted expected return under these assumptions."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that the portfolio's volatility increases when the correlation rises from 0.10 to 0.80, all else equal.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Embedded-claim / reverses the conclusion:** the underlying claim (higher ρ raises volatility, all else equal) is correct and should be accepted, so saying a reviewer \"would be wrong to accept\" it is False. Larger covariance raises portfolio variance for positive weights.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. For positive weights, the larger covariance term raises portfolio variance and volatility."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The assets' unchanged stand-alone volatilities are sufficient to conclude that portfolio risk is unchanged.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Unchanged stand-alone volatilities are not sufficient — portfolio risk also depends on covariance among holdings, which just rose.", "whyPool": "Portfolio risk also depends on covariance among holdings."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The change matters because portfolio risk depends on how holdings move together, not only on their individual risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Portfolio risk depends on how holdings move together, not only on individual risk; co-movement determines how much fluctuations offset or reinforce.", "whyPool": "Co-movement determines how much individual fluctuations offset or reinforce one another. G2: Covariance and Portfolio Risk"}], "reference": "Two-asset portfolio variance formula — Bodie-Kane-Marcus *Investments* (optimal risky portfolios); Markowitz portfolio theory (variance depends on covariance).", "n": 353}, {"id": "QF-D3G2Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G2", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An asset manager considers equal weights in Funds X and Y . X has 10% annual volatility, Y has 20%, and their correlation is 0.50; a junior analyst claims their covariance is 0.50 because covariance and correlation are interchangeable.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Using returns in decimal form, the covariance is not 0.50 × 0.10 × 0.20 = 0.010.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Embedded-claim / reverses the conclusion:** in decimals, `Cov = 0.50 × 0.10 × 0.20 = 0.010`. The choice says covariance is *not* 0.010, which reverses the correct arithmetic, so it is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The covariance is correlation multiplied by both standard deviations."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Correlation is unitless, whereas covariance depends on the units used for returns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correlation is standardized and unitless; covariance retains the product of the return units — they are not the same object.", "whyPool": "Standardization removes units from correlation, while covariance retains product units."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The correlation can be recovered by dividing covariance by the product of the two standard deviations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "`ρ = Cov / (σ_A·σ_B)` recovers correlation when both standard deviations are nonzero — the defining relationship.", "whyPool": "This is the defining relationship when both standard deviations are nonzero."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If both volatilities doubled while correlation stayed at 0.50, covariance would only double.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Doubling both volatilities multiplies their *product* by 4, so covariance (ρ fixed) is multiplied by 4, not merely doubled.", "whyPool": "Doubling both standard deviations multiplies their product, and therefore covariance, by four."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A positive covariance indicates that the funds have tended to move in the same direction relative to their means. 305 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Positive covariance reflects a tendency for deviations from each mean to share the same sign (co-movement in the same direction).", "whyPool": "Positive covariance reflects a tendency for deviations from each mean to share the same sign."}], "reference": "Covariance–correlation identity — Investopedia \"Covariance vs. Correlation\"; CFA curriculum, statistics/portfolio math.", "n": 354}, {"id": "QF-D3G2Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G2", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio contains 60% in Asset A and 40% in Asset B. Their annual variances are 0.0225 and 0.0100, and their covariance is 0.0030; the portfolio manager asks the quant team to explain the portfolio-variance calculation before approving the allocation.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The two-asset variance is (0.602 × 0.0225) + (0.402 × 0.0100) + (2 × 0.60 × 0.40 × 0.0030).", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correct expression: `(0.602×0.0225) + (0.402×0.0100) + (2×0.60×0.40×0.0030)` — weighted variances plus the cross-covariance term.", "whyPool": "The expression includes both weighted variance terms and the cross-covariance term."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Under the stated inputs, the portfolio variance equals 0.01114.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The terms are 0.00810 + 0.00160 + 0.00144 = **0.01114**, matching the stated value.", "whyPool": "The terms are 0.00810, 0.00160, and 0.00144, which sum to 0.01114."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Omitting the covariance term would correctly measure portfolio variance because each asset's variance is already included.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Omitting the covariance term ignores whether the assets reinforce or offset each other — it does not correctly measure portfolio variance.", "whyPool": "Omitting covariance ignores whether the assets' returns reinforce or offset each other."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If covariance were lower while all other inputs stayed fixed, portfolio variance would not be lower.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Embedded-claim / reverses the conclusion:** with positive weights a *lower* covariance reduces the cross term and thus lowers portfolio variance; \"would not be lower\" reverses this, so False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. With positive weights, a lower covariance reduces the cross term directly."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Portfolio volatility is not the square root of portfolio variance, not the weighted average of the two variances.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion (awkwardly worded):** volatility *is* the square root of portfolio variance and is *not* the weighted average of the variances. The choice's phrasing denies the correct relationship, so it is False. **Note:** the double-\"not\" wording is confusing; anchor on the verdict — variances combine via the covariance formula, then the square root gives volatility.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Variances combine through the covariance formula, after which the square root gives volatility. 306 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Portfolio variance & volatility — Bodie-Kane-Marcus *Investments* (portfolio math); Markowitz mean-variance framework.", "n": 355}, {"id": "QF-D3G2Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G2", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk committee compares two 50/50 portfolios. Both use assets with 15% annual volatility, but Portfolio P's pair has covariance 0.0180 while Portfolio Q's pair has covariance -0.0045; expected returns are identical, and the committee wants the lower-risk allocation.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Portfolio P has the larger covariance contribution to portfolio variance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "P's positive covariance produces a larger positive cross term than Q's negative covariance, so P contributes more to portfolio variance.", "whyPool": "Its positive covariance produces a larger cross term than Q's negative covariance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Portfolio Q can have lower volatility even though its component assets have the same stand-alone volatility as those in P .", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Offsetting (negative) co-movement can lower combined risk even though Q's component assets have the same stand-alone volatility as P's.", "whyPool": "Offsetting co-movement can lower combined risk without changing the component volatilities."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Negative covariance means that each asset in Q has negative stand-alone variance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A negative *covariance* describes joint movement; individual variances cannot be negative, so it says nothing about negative stand-alone variance.", "whyPool": "Individual variances cannot be negative; the negative value describes joint movement."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Evaluating only the assets' individual volatilities would miss the principal difference between P and Q.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The portfolios differ in co-movement, which is essential to total variance — evaluating only individual volatilities would miss the principal difference.", "whyPool": "The portfolios differ in co-movement, which is essential to total portfolio variance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because expected returns are identical, covariance cannot affect the allocation decision.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "With equal expected return, covariance is precisely what distinguishes the two; the lower-risk (lower-covariance) portfolio may be preferred, so covariance does affect the decision.", "whyPool": "With equal expected return, the lower-risk portfolio may be preferable precisely because covariance differs. G3: Specific vs. Systematic Risk"}], "reference": "Covariance's contribution to portfolio variance — Markowitz portfolio theory; Investopedia \"systematic vs. idiosyncratic risk\" (co-movement).", "n": 356}, {"id": "QF-D3G3Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G3", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A long-only equity fund holds nearly all its capital in one pharmaceutical company awaiting a regulatory decision. The portfolio manager argues that the broad market is calm, so the fund's risk is low; the chief risk officer asks the team to separate company-specific from market-wide exposure.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "An adverse decision affecting only the company is not primarily a specific-risk event.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Embedded-claim / reverses the conclusion:** a ruling tied to one issuer *is* primarily a specific-risk event; the choice denies this, so False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The regulatory outcome is tied to one issuer rather than the market as a whole."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Spreading the position across many unrelated companies could not reduce the portfolio's exposure to that single decision.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion:** spreading the position across many unrelated companies *can* reduce exposure to that single idiosyncratic decision; \"could not reduce\" is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Diversification can reduce the impact of idiosyncratic outcomes from one company."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A broad recession that depresses most equities is systematic risk from the fund's perspective.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A broad recession that depresses most equities is systematic (market-wide) risk that cannot be diversified away within equities.", "whyPool": "Economy-wide shocks tend to affect many securities and cannot be diversified away within equities."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Holding more companies guarantees elimination of all equity-market risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Holding more companies dilutes specific risk but broad market (systematic) exposure remains — it is not \"guaranteed elimination of all equity-market risk.\"", "whyPool": "Broad market exposure remains even in a well-diversified equity portfolio."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Low recent market volatility does not remove the concentrated company's event risk. 307 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Low recent market volatility does not remove the concentrated company's event risk; issuer-specific catalysts can move independently of the aggregate market.", "whyPool": "Company-specific catalysts can produce large moves independently of aggregate market conditions."}], "reference": "Systematic vs. idiosyncratic risk — Investopedia \"Systematic vs. Unsystematic Risk\"; Bodie-Kane-Marcus *Investments* (diversification and firm-specific risk).", "n": 357}, {"id": "QF-D3G3Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G3", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An index manager expands a portfolio from 20 to 500 stocks while maintaining roughly the same market and sector exposures. A client expects the expansion to eliminate both stock-selection risk and losses during market crashes.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Increasing the number of reasonably diversified holdings can reduce firm-specific risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "More reasonably diversified holdings give idiosyncratic gains/losses more opportunity to offset, reducing firm-specific risk.", "whyPool": "Idiosyncratic gains and losses have more opportunity to offset across many issuers."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The expansion necessarily reduces the portfolio's systematic market exposure to zero.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Retaining similar market/sector exposure preserves systematic risk; the expansion does not drive systematic exposure to zero.", "whyPool": "Retaining similar market exposure preserves systematic risk."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A market-wide interest-rate shock can still affect many or all 500 holdings.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A market-wide interest-rate shock is systematic and can still affect many or all 500 holdings.", "whyPool": "Common macroeconomic shocks can affect a broad cross-section of securities."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The incremental reduction in specific risk is likely to diminish as the portfolio becomes broadly diversified.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Diminishing marginal benefit: early additions remove more concentration, later additions add smaller reductions in specific risk.", "whyPool": "Early additions remove more concentration, while later additions often provide smaller marginal benefits."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A 500-stock portfolio cannot experience a negative return caused by an economy-wide shock.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Diversification does not immunize against economy-wide shocks; a 500-stock portfolio can still post a negative return in a market decline.", "whyPool": "Diversification does not immunize a portfolio against common market declines."}], "reference": "Diversification limits / diminishing benefit — Bodie-Kane-Marcus *Investments* (portfolio size vs. risk); Investopedia \"diversification.\"", "n": 358}, {"id": "QF-D3G3Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G3", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A market-neutral fund holds dozens of long and short positions but discovers that many depend on cheap financing and liquid credit markets. The head of risk asks whether the large position count proves that the portfolio is well protected from systemic stress. 308 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A large number of positions does not guarantee diversification if they share the same underlying risk driver.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Many positions do not guarantee diversification if they share the same underlying risk driver — they can be economically redundant despite different tickers.", "whyPool": "Positions can be economically redundant despite having different names or tickers."}, {"L": "B", "text": "An isolated accounting scandal at one small long position is not more diversifiable than a market-wide funding shock.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion:** an issuer-specific scandal *is* more diversifiable than a market-wide funding shock; the choice claims it is \"not more diversifiable,\" so False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. An issuer-specific event can be diluted, whereas a funding shock may affect the full portfolio."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Equal dollar exposure across positions necessarily eliminates systematic risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Equal dollar exposure (dollar neutrality) does not ensure neutrality to market, sector, liquidity, or other systematic factors.", "whyPool": "Dollar neutrality does not ensure neutrality to market, sector, liquidity, or other systematic factors."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Common exposure to financing conditions cannot cause positions that appeared distinct to lose together.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion:** a shared dependency on financing *can* cause seemingly distinct positions to lose together under stress; \"cannot cause\" is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A shared dependency can create correlated losses under stress."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Risk analysis should examine factor and scenario exposures in addition to counting securities.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Risk analysis should examine factor and scenario exposures, not just count securities — economic drivers reveal hidden concentrations.", "whyPool": "Economic drivers reveal concentrations that a simple holdings count can conceal. G4: Diversification Benefits"}], "reference": "Factor/shared-driver risk, dollar-neutral ≠ factor-neutral — CFA curriculum (factor risk); Investopedia \"systematic risk.\"", "n": 359}, {"id": "QF-D3G4Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G4", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A balanced fund considers combining two assets, each expected to return 8% annually with 12% volatility. Their correlation is zero, and the investment committee asks whether combining two risky assets can genuinely reduce risk without lowering expected return.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A 50/50 portfolio retains an expected return of 8% under the stated assumptions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Expected return is the weighted average: 0.5(8%) + 0.5(8%) = 8% under the stated assumptions.", "whyPool": "Expected portfolio return is the weighted average, here 0.5(8%) + 0.5(8%)."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The 50/50 portfolio's variance is 0.52(0.122) + 0.52(0.122) because the covariance term is zero.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With ρ = 0 the covariance term is zero, so `σ_p2 = 0.52(0.122) + 0.52(0.122)`.", "whyPool": "Zero correlation implies zero covariance when the volatilities are finite."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The resulting portfolio volatility is not approximately 8.49%, below either asset's 12% volatility.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Embedded-claim / reverses the conclusion:** variance = 0.0072, whose square root ≈ 0.0849 (8.49%), which is below 12%. The choice says volatility is \"not approximately 8.49%,\" reversing the correct result → False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The variance is 0.0072, whose square root is about 0.0849. 309 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Zero correlation means neither asset can ever lose in the same period as the other.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Zero *linear* correlation does not prohibit simultaneous losses in the same period.", "whyPool": "Zero linear correlation does not prohibit simultaneous losses."}, {"L": "E", "text": "This example demonstrates that risk reduction need not require a proportional reduction in expected return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Expected return stays 8% while volatility falls to ~8.49% — risk reduction need not require a proportional cut in expected return.", "whyPool": "Expected return remains 8% while volatility falls through imperfect co-movement."}], "reference": "Diversification with imperfect correlation — Markowitz portfolio theory; Bodie-Kane-Marcus *Investments* (two-asset portfolios).", "n": 360}, {"id": "QF-D3G4Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G4", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An endowment holds a growth asset and is considering an allocation to a defensive strategy with a -0.40 correlation to it. The strategy has a positive expected return but lower stand-alone return than the growth asset; trustees worry that any allocation to it must make the portfolio strictly worse.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The defensive strategy's negative correlation can reduce total portfolio volatility.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Negative correlation creates a negative covariance contribution to portfolio variance, reducing total volatility.", "whyPool": "Opposing co-movement can create a negative covariance contribution to portfolio variance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A lower stand-alone expected return automatically makes the defensive strategy undesirable in every portfolio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A lower stand-alone expected return does not automatically make the strategy undesirable — portfolio value depends on both return and interaction with existing holdings.", "whyPool": "Portfolio value depends on both expected return and interaction with existing holdings."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The portfolio may achieve a more attractive risk-return tradeoff even if its expected return falls modestly.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A sufficient reduction in risk can improve the risk-return tradeoff even if expected return falls modestly.", "whyPool": "A sufficient reduction in risk may compensate for a smaller reduction in expected return."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Negative historical correlation guarantees protection in every future drawdown.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Historical negative correlation does not guarantee protection in every future drawdown — correlations are estimates that shift across regimes.", "whyPool": "Correlations are estimates and can change across regimes."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The allocation decision should not consider joint portfolio outcomes rather than ranking assets solely by stand-alone return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion (awkward wording):** diversification IS a portfolio-level concept, so the decision *should* consider joint outcomes rather than rank by stand-alone return. The choice's \"should not consider joint outcomes\" reverses this → False. **Note:** the double-negative phrasing is confusing; the key's point is that joint/portfolio outcomes must be considered.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Diversification is inherently a portfolio-level concept."}], "reference": "Negative correlation as a hedge; portfolio-level evaluation — Markowitz theory; Investopedia \"hedge\" and \"diversification.\"", "n": 361}, {"id": "QF-D3G4Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G4", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A commodity producer invests its pension assets heavily in commodity-linked securities because management expects strong returns. An adviser proposes adding assets that tend to perform well when commodity prices fall, but management objects that these holdings may lag during commodity booms.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Assets with offsetting commodity exposure may hedge risk tied to the sponsor and current portfolio.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The sponsor and pension already share commodity sensitivity, so assets with offsetting commodity exposure can reduce aggregate risk.", "whyPool": "The pension and sponsor already share commodity sensitivity, so opposing exposure can reduce aggregate risk."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A hedge must have the highest expected stand-alone return to improve the portfolio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A hedge need not have the highest stand-alone expected return — it can add value through covariance reduction even with a lower return.", "whyPool": "A hedge can add value through covariance reduction even with a lower stand-alone return."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Some underperformance by the hedge during commodity booms can be the economic cost of protection.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Underperformance during commodity booms is the economic cost of protection — giving up some upside where the original exposure does well.", "whyPool": "Protection often gives up some upside in states where the original exposure performs well."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the proposed assets are perfectly negatively correlated and appropriately sized, they can theoretically eliminate the paired return variability. 310 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With ρ = -1 and appropriate sizing, weights can be chosen so the volatile components exactly offset, theoretically eliminating the paired variability.", "whyPool": "With correlation of -1, weights can be chosen so the volatile components exactly offset."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Adding another commodity producer with nearly identical exposure would ordinarily provide the same hedge as an offsetting asset.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Adding another commodity producer with nearly identical exposure would *reinforce*, not hedge, commodity risk — it does not provide the same offset.", "whyPool": "A similarly exposed producer is likely to reinforce, rather than hedge, commodity risk. G5: Concentration and Position Sizing"}], "reference": "Hedging via negative correlation; ρ = -1 risk elimination — Markowitz theory; Bodie-Kane-Marcus *Investments* (perfect hedge case).", "n": 362}, {"id": "QF-D3G5Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G5", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A hedge fund manager wants to place 35% of fund capital in a single high-conviction stock. The investment thesis is strong, but the fund's mandate limits severe drawdowns; the risk committee is evaluating whether conviction justifies the proposed size.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "If the stock loses 50% while all other holdings are unchanged, it reduces fund value by approximately 17.5% before secondary effects.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A 35% weight × a 50% loss ≈ 17.5% fund loss before secondary effects — direct arithmetic of position sizing.", "whyPool": "A 35% weight multiplied by a 50% loss produces a 17.5% portfolio loss."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A strong expected-return estimate eliminates the need to consider position size.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A strong expected-return estimate is uncertain; sizing determines the consequence of being wrong, so it cannot be ignored.", "whyPool": "Expected returns are uncertain, and sizing determines the consequence of being wrong."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The concentrated weight increases the portfolio's sensitivity to estimation error in the stock thesis.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A larger weight amplifies the portfolio impact of forecast errors or omitted risks — concentration raises sensitivity to estimation error.", "whyPool": "Errors in forecasts or omitted risks have a larger portfolio effect at a larger weight."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A position limit cannot constrain the damage from an issuer-specific event.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion:** a position limit *does* cap direct exposure to a single issuer/thesis; \"cannot constrain the damage\" is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Limits cap direct exposure to a single issuer or thesis."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Diversifying some capital away from the stock necessarily means the manager has no conviction in the thesis.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Diversifying some capital away balances conviction with uncertainty and portfolio risk — it does not imply the manager has no conviction.", "whyPool": "Position sizing balances conviction with uncertainty and portfolio-level risk. 311 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Position sizing & concentration risk — CFA curriculum (risk management, position limits); Investopedia \"concentration risk.\"", "n": 363}, {"id": "QF-D3G5Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G5", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio holds ten stocks at 10% each, but eight are regional banks with similar loan books and funding sources. The portfolio manager cites the ten equal positions as evidence that no concentration risk exists; the chief risk officer requests a broader assessment.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Equal security weights eliminate single-name concentration but may leave substantial sector concentration.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Equal weights remove single-name concentration but can leave large *sector* concentration — weight dispersion doesn't remove shared industry exposure.", "whyPool": "Weight dispersion across names does not remove shared industry exposure."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The eight bank positions may behave like a much smaller number of independent bets.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The eight bank positions share common drivers, so they may behave like a much smaller number of independent bets.", "whyPool": "Common economic drivers can make nominally separate holdings redundant."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A regional credit shock could not cause correlated losses across most of the portfolio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion:** similar loan/funding exposures create vulnerability to the same shock, so a regional credit shock *could* cause correlated losses; \"could not\" is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Similar loan and funding exposures create vulnerability to the same shock."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because no individual stock exceeds 10%, additional exposure limits would serve no risk-management purpose.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Aggregate sector and factor concentrations remain despite the 10% single-name cap, so additional limits do serve a purpose.", "whyPool": "Aggregate sector and factor concentrations remain despite the single-name cap."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Position controls can include sector, factor, and scenario limits in addition to single-name limits.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Position controls can add sector, factor, and scenario limits to catch risks that single-name limits miss.", "whyPool": "Multiple limit dimensions address risks that security-level limits miss."}], "reference": "Sector/factor concentration beyond single-name limits — CFA curriculum (risk controls); Investopedia \"diversification limits.\"", "n": 364}, {"id": "QF-D3G5Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G5", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A systematic fund sizes positions in inverse proportion to each asset's volatility, but it ignores correlations. One low- volatility asset receives a very large weight and has become strongly correlated with several other holdings; the portfolio manager asks whether inverse-volatility sizing alone is sufficient.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A low stand-alone volatility estimate does not guarantee a small contribution to portfolio risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Risk contribution depends on weight *and* covariance with the full portfolio, so a low stand-alone volatility does not guarantee a small contribution.", "whyPool": "Risk contribution depends on weight and covariance with the full portfolio. 312 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "The large weight can make the asset important to total risk even if its own volatility is relatively low.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A sufficiently large exposure can dominate total risk despite modest stand-alone volatility.", "whyPool": "A sufficiently large exposure can dominate despite modest stand-alone volatility."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Increased correlation with other holdings cannot raise the asset's marginal contribution to portfolio variance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion:** greater positive covariance means the position reinforces more of the portfolio's movement, *raising* its marginal contribution to variance; \"cannot raise\" is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Greater positive covariance means the position reinforces more of the portfolio's movement."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Inverse-volatility sizing automatically produces an optimally diversified portfolio for any covariance matrix.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Ignoring correlations can leave major factor/covariance concentrations, so inverse-vol sizing does not automatically produce an optimal portfolio for any covariance matrix.", "whyPool": "Ignoring correlations can leave major factor or covariance concentrations."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Portfolio-level risk controls should not consider weights, volatilities, and correlations together.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion:** portfolio-risk and marginal-risk calculations require weights, volatilities, and correlations *together*; \"should not consider [them] together\" is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. All three inputs enter portfolio-risk and marginal-risk calculations. G6: Diminishing Marginal Diversification"}], "reference": "Marginal contribution to risk (weight × covariance); risk parity vs. correlation — CFA curriculum (portfolio risk); Markowitz covariance matrix.", "n": 365}, {"id": "QF-D3G6Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G6", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A wealth manager begins with a single-stock portfolio and successively adds equal-sized positions from unrelated industries. A client sees risk fall sharply at first but only slightly after dozens of additions and asks whether the process has stopped working.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The first few independent additions often remove more firm-specific concentration than later additions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The first few independent additions remove the most firm-specific concentration; later ones remove less.", "whyPool": "Moving away from one or a few names initially removes substantial idiosyncratic exposure."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A diminishing marginal benefit means the later holdings increase risk in every case.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Diminishing marginal benefit means later holdings help by *progressively smaller* amounts, not that they increase risk in every case.", "whyPool": "Later additions may still reduce risk, just by progressively smaller amounts."}, {"L": "C", "text": "As specific risk is diversified away, systematic risk becomes a larger share of the remaining portfolio risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "As specific risk is diversified away, systematic (market) risk becomes a larger share of what remains.", "whyPool": "Common market exposure remains after much of the issuer-specific risk has been diluted."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Adding infinitely many stocks from the same market guarantees portfolio volatility will converge to zero.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Adding infinitely many same-market stocks cannot drive volatility to zero — systematic risk cannot be eliminated by adding names.", "whyPool": "Market-wide systematic risk cannot be eliminated merely by adding more stocks."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The observed pattern is consistent with diversification benefits approaching a floor set by common risks.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The pattern matches diversification benefits approaching a floor set by common (systematic) risks.", "whyPool": "Diminishing benefits arise as the diversifiable component shrinks."}], "reference": "Diminishing marginal diversification, systematic-risk floor — Bodie-Kane-Marcus *Investments* (portfolio size vs. risk curve); Investopedia \"diversification.\"", "n": 366}, {"id": "QF-D3G6Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G6", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A multi-manager fund is considering a new equity strategy. It has attractive stand-alone statistics but a 0.95 correlation with an existing strategy and uses nearly the same signals and securities; the allocator wants to count it as an additional source of diversification.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The new strategy is likely to provide limited marginal diversification because its exposures are largely redundant. 313 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Near-identical signals and 0.95 correlation imply little new risk-offsetting behavior — limited marginal diversification.", "whyPool": "Near-identical signals and high correlation imply little new risk-offsetting behavior."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Attractive stand-alone performance is sufficient to establish that the combined portfolio improves.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Attractive stand-alone performance is not sufficient; the effect on the existing portfolio depends on covariance.", "whyPool": "The effect on the existing portfolio depends on covariance as well as stand-alone results."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A different manager name does not make the underlying return stream economically independent.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Diversification follows economic exposures, not labels — a different manager name does not make the return stream independent.", "whyPool": "Diversification follows economic exposures, not labels or organizational separation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Replacing the candidate with a lower-correlated strategy could not provide more diversification, even if that alternative has similar stand-alone volatility.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion:** a lower-correlated alternative with similar stand-alone vol generally supplies *more* risk reduction; \"could not provide more diversification\" is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Lower co-movement generally supplies more portfolio-risk reduction, all else equal."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Adding the candidate necessarily lowers portfolio volatility because the number of strategies increases.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Adding a redundant strategy does not necessarily lower volatility; it can leave risk unchanged or, depending on funding/weights, raise it.", "whyPool": "A redundant strategy can leave risk unchanged or, depending on funding and weights, increase it."}], "reference": "Redundancy and marginal diversification — Markowitz theory (covariance drives benefit); Investopedia \"diworsification.\"", "n": 367}, {"id": "QF-D3G6Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G6", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An investment committee can add either Fund M or Fund N to a mature 40-holding portfolio. M closely resembles current holdings, while N introduces a genuinely different return driver but has modestly higher stand-alone volatility; the committee is focused on total portfolio risk.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Fund N may offer the larger marginal diversification benefit despite its higher stand-alone volatility.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Fund N's low covariance with current holdings can outweigh its somewhat higher stand-alone volatility, giving a larger marginal diversification benefit.", "whyPool": "Low covariance with current holdings can outweigh somewhat higher individual volatility."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The number of holdings alone is enough to determine which candidate improves diversification more.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The number of holdings alone is not enough — economic independence and covariance matter more than a raw count.", "whyPool": "Economic independence and covariance matter more than a raw count."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Fund M's similarity to existing holdings can make its incremental risk reduction small.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Fund M's similarity to existing holdings means redundant exposure and small incremental risk reduction.", "whyPool": "Redundant exposure adds little offset to risks already present."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Marginal diversification should be evaluated relative to the existing portfolio, not in isolation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Marginal diversification must be evaluated relative to the existing portfolio; the same asset can diversify one portfolio well and another poorly.", "whyPool": "The same asset may diversify one portfolio well and another poorly."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Choosing the candidate with lower stand-alone volatility always minimizes the resulting portfolio volatility.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Choosing the lower-stand-alone-vol candidate does not always minimize portfolio volatility — a low-vol look-alike can reinforce existing risk while a higher-vol diversifier reduces total risk.", "whyPool": "A low-volatility asset can reinforce existing risks, while a higher-volatility diversifier can reduce total risk. 314 Rev:081020261230 G7: Correlation Instability and Crisis Behavior"}], "reference": "Marginal diversification relative to portfolio; covariance over stand-alone vol — Markowitz theory; Bodie-Kane-Marcus *Investments*.", "n": 368}, {"id": "QF-D3G7Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G7", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk model estimates correlations using five calm years and predicts a maximum portfolio loss of 8%. During a liquidity crisis, several risky assets begin falling together; the chief risk officer asks why realized diversification is weaker than the model projected.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Correlations cannot rise during stress as assets respond to common deleveraging and liquidity shocks.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion:** correlations *can* rise in stress as assets respond to common deleveraging and liquidity shocks; \"cannot rise\" is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Shared funding pressure and simultaneous selling can synchronize returns."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Correlations estimated in calm periods are guaranteed to remain valid during crises.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Dependence structures can change materially across regimes, so calm-period correlations are not guaranteed valid in crises.", "whyPool": "Dependence structures can change materially across market regimes."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Higher positive correlations reduce the amount of offsetting movement among long positions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Higher positive correlations reduce offsetting movement among long positions, weakening expected diversification.", "whyPool": "More synchronized losses weaken the protection expected from diversification."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The model cannot understate crisis risk if it assumes a stable covariance matrix.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion:** a model assuming a stable covariance matrix *can* understate crisis risk because a calm-period matrix may not represent stressed co-movement; \"cannot understate\" is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A calm-period matrix may not represent stressed co- movement."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Stress tests using adverse correlation scenarios can complement estimates based only on historical averages.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stress tests using adverse correlation scenarios complement historical averages by probing relationships absent from calm history.", "whyPool": "Scenario analysis tests the portfolio against plausible relationships absent from average history."}], "reference": "Correlation instability / crisis co-movement — CFA curriculum (risk management, stress testing); Investopedia \"correlation breakdown.\"", "n": 369}, {"id": "QF-D3G7Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G7", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund chose three asset classes because their ten-year full-sample correlations were low. A researcher finds that each pair had near-zero correlation in normal months but correlations above 0.75 during the worst market months; the portfolio manager must reassess the fund's drawdown protection.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The full-sample averages can conceal dependence that is strongest precisely when protection is needed.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Full-sample averages can conceal dependence that is strongest exactly when protection is needed (tail/stress states).", "whyPool": "Averaging normal and stressed periods can mask state-dependent co-movement."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Low unconditional correlation ensures low correlation conditional on severe market losses.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Low *unconditional* correlation does not ensure low correlation *conditional* on severe market losses — conditional dependence can differ sharply.", "whyPool": "Conditional dependence in bad states may differ sharply from the full-sample statistic."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The stress-period evidence is relevant even if those months form a small portion of the sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stress-period evidence is relevant even if those months are a small share of the sample, since rare stress dominates drawdowns.", "whyPool": "Rare stress periods can dominate drawdowns and investor outcomes."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Diversification designed for drawdown control should examine tail and regime behavior, not only average correlation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Drawdown-focused diversification should examine tail and regime behavior, not only average correlation.", "whyPool": "Protection must be evaluated in the states where portfolio losses are most consequential."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because the assets belong to different labels or classes, their crisis correlations cannot be economically meaningful. 315 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Different asset labels/classes do not prevent common macro, liquidity, or funding exposures, so crisis correlations can be economically meaningful.", "whyPool": "Different asset labels do not prevent common macroeconomic, liquidity, or funding exposures."}], "reference": "Conditional/tail correlation, regime dependence — CFA curriculum (tail risk, scenario analysis); Investopedia \"correlation vs. covariance,\" \"correlation breakdown.\"", "n": 370}, {"id": "QF-D3G7Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "03", "domainName": "Diversification & Correlation", "group": "G7", "weeks": [2, 3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A machine-learning team forecasts next month's covariance matrix from a rolling historical window and uses it to construct a highly leveraged “diversified” portfolio. The risk committee is concerned that small estimation errors or a sudden regime shift could invalidate the allocation.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Leverage can magnify losses when realized correlations are higher than the forecast.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Leverage scales losses when realized correlations exceed the forecast — it magnifies the consequences of diversification failing under stronger co-movement.", "whyPool": "Leverage scales the consequences of diversification failing under stronger co-movement."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A precisely estimated historical matrix eliminates uncertainty about future relationships.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Even a precisely estimated historical matrix cannot eliminate uncertainty about the future — a structural or regime change can invalidate it.", "whyPool": "Even an accurate estimate of the past may fail after a structural or regime change."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Portfolio weights optimized from estimated correlations cannot be sensitive to estimation error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Reverses the conclusion:** optimization *can* amplify small input differences into large, concentrated weights, so weights are sensitive to estimation error; \"cannot be sensitive\" is False.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Optimization can amplify small input differences into large and concentrated weights."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Combining rolling estimates with shrinkage, exposure limits, or stress scenarios may improve robustness.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Combining rolling estimates with shrinkage, exposure limits, or stress scenarios reduces dependence on one noisy forecast and improves robustness.", "whyPool": "Robust controls reduce dependence on one noisy forecast."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Correlation instability means diversification has no value and should never be attempted.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Correlation instability does not make diversification worthless — it remains valuable but its estimates and limitations must be managed.", "whyPool": "Diversification remains valuable, but its estimates and limitations must be managed. 316 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Estimation error in covariance optimization, shrinkage — Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage; CFA curriculum (estimation risk, robust portfolio construction); Markowitz theory (input sensitivity).", "n": 371}, {"id": "QF-D4G1Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A university endowment allocates 40% to equities, 35% to bonds, 15% to real estate, and 10% to cash. Its policy requires the portfolio to be fully invested and prohibits both borrowing and short selling.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The stated weights sum to 100%, so the portfolio is fully invested.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "0.40 + 0.35 + 0.15 + 0.10 = 1.00, so `Σw_i = 1` — the fully-invested (budget) constraint is satisfied.", "whyPool": "The four weights add to 1.00, satisfying the fully invested requirement."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Under a long-only rule, every asset weight must be greater than or equal to zero. 318 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A long-only rule is exactly the constraint `w_i ≥ 0` for every asset; no negative (short) positions are allowed.", "whyPool": "Long-only constraints rule out negative positions."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A weight of -10% in bonds would comply with the policy as long as the other weights were adjusted to keep the sum at 100%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A −10% bond weight is a **short position**; shorting is prohibited, so it violates the policy no matter how the other weights are rebalanced to keep the sum at 100%.", "whyPool": "A negative bond weight is a short position and violates the stated policy."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the weights summed to 90%, the remaining 10% could be interpreted as unallocated cash only if cash were not already represented as an explicit asset weight.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Whether a 10% residual \"is cash\" depends on how the model defines its investable universe and budget constraint — if cash is already an explicit asset, an unallocated residual is a different thing. Definitional, so the statement can hold.", "whyPool": "Whether a residual represents cash depends on how the model defines its investable assets and budget constraint."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The sum-of-weights requirement alone prevents the optimizer from choosing negative weights.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The sum-of-weights (budget) constraint alone does not forbid negative weights: weights can sum to 1 while some are negative and others exceed 1. Only explicit sign/bound constraints prevent that.", "whyPool": "Weights can sum to one while some are negative and others exceed one unless separate constraints prohibit that behavior."}], "reference": "Portfolio weights & budget constraint; long-only constraints — Markowitz mean-variance framework; Investopedia \"Portfolio Weight,\" \"Long/Short constraints.\"", "n": 372}, {"id": "QF-D4G1Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A hedge fund proposes weights of 120% in Stock A, 30% in Stock B, and -50% in Stock C. The mandate permits short selling and requires net exposure of 100%, but the risk committee is reviewing the allocation's leverage.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The weights sum to 100%, so the portfolio satisfies the stated net-exposure requirement.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Net exposure = signed sum = 1.20 + 0.30 − 0.50 = 1.00, meeting the 100% net-exposure requirement.", "whyPool": "The net weights are 1.20 + 0.30 - 0.50 = 1.00."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The -50% weight represents a short position in Stock C.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A −50% weight is a **short position** in Stock C (negative weight = short exposure).", "whyPool": "A negative asset weight represents a short exposure."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because the weights sum to 100%, the portfolio has no leverage.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "100% *net* exposure does not imply no leverage — offsetting long (+120/+30) and short (−50) positions create leverage; gross exposure exceeds 100%.", "whyPool": "Net exposure can equal 100% even when offsetting long and short positions create leverage."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The portfolio's gross exposure is not 200%, calculated as the sum of the absolute weights.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Gross exposure = `Σ|w_i|` = |1.20| + |0.30| + |−0.50| = 2.00 = 200%. The claim \"gross is *not* 200%\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Gross exposure is |1.20| + |0.30| + |-0.50| = 2.00, or 200%."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Imposing a long-only constraint would not make this exact allocation infeasible.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** A long-only constraint *would* make this allocation infeasible because the −50% weight violates `w ≥ 0`. \"Would not make it infeasible\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The negative weight violates a long-only requirement. 319 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Net vs. gross exposure, leverage from long/short — Investopedia \"Gross Exposure,\" \"Net Exposure\"; Grinold & Kahn on long-short construction.", "n": 373}, {"id": "QF-D4G1Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G1", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A retirement plan optimizes among five funds subject to a 5% minimum and a 35% maximum weight in each fund. It must remain fully invested, and the investment committee is checking whether several proposed allocations are feasible.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Assigning 35% to each of three funds would not violate the fully invested constraint before allocating anything to the other two funds.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Three funds at 35% already sum to 105% > 100%, leaving no feasible positive allocation for the other two required holdings — so it *does* violate the fully-invested budget. \"Would not violate\" reverses the conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Three 35% positions already total 105%, leaving no feasible positive allocation to the other required holdings."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Assigning exactly 5% to a fund satisfies that fund's minimum-weight constraint.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A weight at exactly its bound (5%) satisfies a `w ≥ 5%` constraint; bounds are met at their endpoints unless stated as strict inequalities.", "whyPool": "A bound is satisfied at its endpoint unless the constraint is explicitly strict."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A proposed 40% weight in one fund is feasible because the other four weights could be reduced enough to preserve a 100% total.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A 40% weight exceeds the 35% maximum for that fund; the per-asset upper bound is violated regardless of how the other four weights are set.", "whyPool": "A 40% position exceeds the stated 35% maximum regardless of the other weights."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The five 5% minimums require at least 25% of the portfolio to be allocated before the optimizer distributes the remaining 75%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Five mandatory 5% minimums consume 5 × 5% = 25% of capital up front, leaving 75% for the optimizer to distribute.", "whyPool": "Five required allocations of 5% account for 25% of total capital."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Position bounds can change which portfolio the optimizer selects even when expected returns and the covariance matrix are unchanged.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Bounds reshape the **feasible set**, so they can change which portfolio is optimal even when expected returns and the covariance matrix are unchanged.", "whyPool": "Constraints change the feasible set and can therefore change the optimum. G2: Minimum-Variance Portfolios"}], "reference": "Position (box) constraints and feasible set — Markowitz optimization with bounds; Investopedia \"Portfolio Constraints.\"", "n": 374}, {"id": "QF-D4G2Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A risk-averse foundation asks for the global minimum-variance portfolio among its approved assets. One trustee assumes this must be the portfolio holding only the asset with the lowest individual volatility.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The global minimum-variance portfolio is the feasible portfolio with the lowest portfolio variance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The GMV portfolio is by definition the feasible portfolio minimizing `σ_p2 = wTΣw` over the entire feasible set.", "whyPool": "The global minimum-variance portfolio minimizes variance over the entire feasible set."}, {"L": "B", "text": "It cannot combine multiple assets if their imperfect co-movement reduces risk below that of the least-volatile asset alone.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** It *can* combine assets: with imperfect (or negative) co-movement, diversification drives portfolio volatility below that of the least-volatile component alone. \"Cannot combine\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Diversification can make a combination less volatile than any component considered independently."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Identifying it requires covariance information, not merely a ranking of individual asset volatilities.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "GMV depends on the full covariance structure (`σ_ij = ρ_ij σ_i σ_j`), so you need covariance information, not just a ranking of stand-alone volatilities.", "whyPool": "Portfolio variance depends on both stand-alone variances and cross-asset covariances."}, {"L": "D", "text": "It is defined as the feasible portfolio with the highest expected return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "GMV minimizes variance; the highest-expected-return portfolio is a different objective (top of the frontier), not GMV.", "whyPool": "Minimum variance and maximum expected return are different objectives."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Its expected return cannot be lower than that of another feasible portfolio. 320 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Minimizing risk does not require maximizing return, so GMV's expected return *can* be lower than other feasible portfolios'. \"Cannot be lower\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Minimizing risk does not require maximizing expected return."}], "reference": "GMV portfolio, role of covariance/diversification — Markowitz (1952); Investopedia \"Minimum Variance Portfolio,\" \"Efficient Frontier.\"", "n": 375}, {"id": "QF-D4G2Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An optimizer considers two assets with equal 20% annual volatility and a correlation of -0.50. The manager compares investing entirely in either asset with a 50/50 allocation.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The variance of either single-asset portfolio is 0.0400.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Single-asset variance = `σ2 = 0.202 = 0.0400`.", "whyPool": "Variance is 0.202 = 0.0400."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The covariance between the two assets is -0.0200.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Covariance = `ρσ1σ2 = −0.50 × 0.20 × 0.20 = −0.0200`.", "whyPool": "Covariance is -0.50 × 0.20 × 0.20 = -0.0200."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The 50/50 portfolio variance is not 0.0100.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** 50/50 variance = `0.25(0.0400) + 0.25(0.0400) + 2(0.5)(0.5)(−0.0200) = 0.0100`. \"Is not 0.0100\" reverses the correct calculation.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Portfolio variance is 0.25(0.0400) + 0.25(0.0400) + 2(0.50)(0.50)(-0.0200) = 0.0100."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The 50/50 portfolio volatility is 10%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Volatility = `√0.0100 = 0.10 = 10%` — diversification cut it from 20% (single asset) to 10%.", "whyPool": "The square root of 0.0100 is 0.10, or 10%."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the correlation rose to +1 while all other inputs stayed fixed, the 50/50 portfolio would retain the same 10% volatility.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "At `ρ = +1`, equal-volatility assets give no risk reduction: `σ_p = 20%`, not 10%. Correlation drives the diversification benefit.", "whyPool": "At perfect positive correlation, equal-volatility assets provide no volatility reduction and the portfolio volatility would be 20%."}], "reference": "Two-asset portfolio variance, correlation & diversification — Markowitz mean-variance; CFA curriculum \"Portfolio Risk and Return.\"", "n": 376}, {"id": "QF-D4G2Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G2", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio manager minimizes variance subject to a required expected return of 8%. After removing that return requirement, the optimizer chooses a portfolio with a 6% expected return and lower volatility. 321 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The original portfolio is a minimum-variance portfolio conditional on the 8% target, assuming the optimization was solved correctly.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With the 8% target imposed, the solution is the minimum-variance portfolio *conditional on* that return constraint (a point on the frontier at 8%).", "whyPool": "It minimizes variance among portfolios satisfying the specified return requirement."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The new portfolio may be the global minimum-variance portfolio because no target return above 6% is imposed.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With no target-return floor, the variance objective can select the overall lowest-risk feasible portfolio — plausibly the GMV (here 6%).", "whyPool": "Without a target-return restriction, the variance objective can select the lowest-risk feasible portfolio overall."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Removing a constraint cannot enlarge the feasible set.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Removing a constraint *enlarges* the feasible set: all previously feasible portfolios remain, plus lower-return ones become eligible. \"Cannot enlarge\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Every formerly feasible solution remains available, and additional lower-return portfolios may become eligible."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The optimizer's decision proves that 6% is the maximum achievable expected return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The objective was risk minimization, so the return it happens to select (6%) does not establish the maximum achievable return.", "whyPool": "The objective is risk minimization, so its selected return does not establish the maximum feasible return."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A portfolio with a higher expected return cannot also have higher variance than the global minimum-variance portfolio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Moving up from GMV trades more variance for more return, so a higher-return portfolio *can* have higher variance than GMV. \"Cannot also have higher variance\" reverses the conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Moving away from the global minimum-variance point can trade greater risk for greater expected return. G3: Maximum-Sharpe Portfolios"}], "reference": "Constrained minimum-variance vs. GMV, feasible-set expansion — Markowitz frontier; Investopedia \"Efficient Frontier.\"", "n": 377}, {"id": "QF-D4G3Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "With a 2% risk-free rate, an optimizer compares Portfolio A, with a 10% expected return and 16% volatility, against Portfolio B, with an 8% expected return and 8% volatility.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Portfolio A's expected excess return is 8%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Expected excess return = `10% − 2% = 8%`.", "whyPool": "Expected excess return is 10% - 2% = 8%. 322 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Portfolio A's Sharpe ratio is not 0.50.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** A's Sharpe = `(0.10 − 0.02)/0.16 = 0.08/0.16 = 0.50`. \"Is not 0.50\" reverses the correct value.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Portfolio A's Sharpe ratio is 0.08 / 0.16 = 0.50."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Portfolio B's Sharpe ratio is 0.75.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "B's Sharpe = `(0.08 − 0.02)/0.08 = 0.06/0.08 = 0.75`.", "whyPool": "Portfolio B's Sharpe ratio is (0.08 - 0.02) / 0.08 = 0.75."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Portfolio A has the higher expected return and therefore must be the maximum-Sharpe choice.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Maximizing Sharpe weighs excess return *and* volatility; A's higher raw return doesn't make it max-Sharpe (its Sharpe 0.50 < B's 0.75).", "whyPool": "Maximizing Sharpe considers both excess return and volatility, not expected return alone."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Based only on these two candidates and the stated inputs, Portfolio B has the higher expected excess return per unit of volatility.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "B's Sharpe (0.75) exceeds A's (0.50), so B has the higher excess return per unit of volatility under the stated inputs.", "whyPool": "Portfolio B's Sharpe ratio exceeds Portfolio A's under the stated assumptions."}], "reference": "Sharpe ratio definition & ranking — Sharpe (1966); Investopedia \"Sharpe Ratio\"; CFA curriculum.", "n": 378}, {"id": "QF-D4G3Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A mean-variance analyst identifies a tangency portfolio of risky assets and explains its role to an investment committee that can also lend or borrow at the modeled risk-free rate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The tangency portfolio is the risky portfolio with the maximum Sharpe ratio under the model's inputs and feasible set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The tangency portfolio is the risky portfolio with the maximum Sharpe ratio given the model's inputs and feasible set.", "whyPool": "It maximizes modeled excess return per unit of volatility among feasible risky portfolios."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Graphically, the capital allocation line through the risk-free asset is tangent to the risky-asset efficient frontier at that portfolio.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Graphically, the CAL from the risk-free asset is **tangent** to the risky efficient frontier at the tangency portfolio (the steepest line = highest Sharpe).", "whyPool": "The line with the greatest slope from the risk-free intercept touches the frontier at the tangency portfolio."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Every investor must hold only the tangency portfolio and no risk-free asset, regardless of risk tolerance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Investors do *not* all hold only the tangency portfolio; risk tolerance sets the mix between the tangency portfolio and risk-free lending/borrowing.", "whyPool": "Risk tolerance affects the mix between the tangency portfolio and risk-free borrowing or lending."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Investors cannot combine the tangency portfolio with the risk-free asset to select different overall risk levels under the standard assumptions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Investors *can* combine the tangency portfolio with the risk-free asset to choose different risk levels — that mix moves them along the CAL. \"Cannot combine\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Changing that mix moves the investor along the capital allocation line."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The tangency portfolio is necessarily the same as the global minimum-variance risky portfolio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The tangency (max-Sharpe) portfolio and the GMV (min-variance) portfolio are different objectives and generally different portfolios.", "whyPool": "The minimum-variance and maximum-Sharpe objectives generally select different portfolios."}], "reference": "Tangency portfolio, Capital Allocation Line, two-fund separation — Investopedia \"Tangency Portfolio,\" \"Capital Allocation Line\"; CFA curriculum \"Portfolio Risk and Return.\"", "n": 379}, {"id": "QF-D4G3Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G3", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two risky portfolios are under consideration. Portfolio X has a 9% expected return and 10% volatility; Portfolio Y has a 13% expected return and 20% volatility. The committee evaluates how the assumed risk-free rate affects their Sharpe ratios.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "At a 1% risk-free rate, Portfolio X's Sharpe ratio is not 0.80.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** At `R_f = 1%`, X's Sharpe = `(0.09 − 0.01)/0.10 = 0.80`. \"Is not 0.80\" reverses the correct value.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Portfolio X's Sharpe ratio is (0.09 - 0.01) / 0.10 = 0.80."}, {"L": "B", "text": "At a 1% risk-free rate, Portfolio Y's Sharpe ratio is 0.60.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At `R_f = 1%`, Y's Sharpe = `(0.13 − 0.01)/0.20 = 0.12/0.20 = 0.60`.", "whyPool": "Portfolio Y's Sharpe ratio is (0.13 - 0.01) / 0.20 = 0.60."}, {"L": "C", "text": "At a 5% risk-free rate, the two portfolios have equal Sharpe ratios of 0.40. 323 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At `R_f = 5%`: X = `(0.09 − 0.05)/0.10 = 0.40`; Y = `(0.13 − 0.05)/0.20 = 0.40` — equal Sharpe ratios.", "whyPool": "At 5%, X has (0.09 - 0.05) / 0.10 = 0.40 and Y has (0.13 - 0.05) / 0.20 = 0.40."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Changing the risk-free rate can change the Sharpe-ratio comparison when candidate portfolios have different volatilities.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Subtracting the same `R_f` divides by different volatilities, so changing `R_f` can change the Sharpe *ranking* when candidates differ in volatility (X leads at 1%, tie at 5%).", "whyPool": "The same risk-free-rate subtraction has a different ratio effect when volatilities differ."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Raising the risk-free rate increases both portfolios' expected excess returns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Raising `R_f` with risky returns fixed *lowers* excess return (`E[R] − R_f`) for both — it does not increase it.", "whyPool": "A higher risk-free rate lowers expected excess returns when risky expected returns stay fixed. G4: Efficient Frontier"}], "reference": "Sharpe ratio sensitivity to the risk-free rate — Sharpe (1966); Investopedia \"Sharpe Ratio,\" \"Risk-Free Rate.\"", "n": 380}, {"id": "QF-D4G4Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An investment committee compares three feasible portfolios. Portfolio A offers 7% expected return with 9% volatility, Portfolio B offers 7% with 12% volatility, and Portfolio C offers 9% with 12% volatility.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Portfolio B is not dominated by Portfolio A because A offers the same expected return with less risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** A *does* dominate B — same 7% return at lower volatility (9% < 12%). \"B is not dominated by A\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A provides an equal return at lower volatility."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Portfolio B is not also dominated by Portfolio C because C offers a higher expected return with the same risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** C *also* dominates B — higher return (9% > 7%) at equal 12% volatility. \"Not also dominated by C\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. C provides a higher return at equal volatility."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Portfolio B belongs on the efficient frontier because it lies between A and C numerically.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Lying \"numerically between\" A and C does not make B efficient; B is dominated by both, so it is off the frontier.", "whyPool": "Numerical location alone does not overcome B's domination by other feasible choices."}, {"L": "D", "text": "An efficient portfolio cannot be improved by increasing expected return without increasing risk or by decreasing risk without decreasing expected return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "This is the definition of mean-variance efficiency: an efficient portfolio can't raise return without raising risk, nor cut risk without cutting return.", "whyPool": "This expresses the risk-return tradeoff that defines mean-variance efficiency."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Based only on these three choices, both A and C can be efficient.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Neither A nor C dominates the other — C's higher return comes with higher risk — so both can be efficient.", "whyPool": "Neither A nor C dominates the other because C's higher return comes with higher risk. 324 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Dominated vs. efficient portfolios, efficient frontier — Markowitz (1952); Investopedia \"Efficient Frontier.\"", "n": 381}, {"id": "QF-D4G4Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An optimizer traces the minimum achievable volatility for target expected returns of 5%, 7%, 9%, and 11%. The global minimum-variance portfolio has an expected return of 6%.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "For each target return, the optimizer seeks the lowest-risk feasible portfolio meeting the specified target.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "For each target, the optimizer solves for the lowest-variance feasible portfolio meeting that return — this constructs the frontier point-by-point.", "whyPool": "The frontier can be constructed by minimizing variance at successive expected-return targets."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The upper branch beginning at the global minimum-variance portfolio forms the mean-variance efficient frontier.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The **upper** branch, starting at the GMV point, is the mean-variance efficient frontier (portfolios above GMV give the efficient risk-return tradeoffs).", "whyPool": "Portfolios above the global minimum-variance point provide the efficient risk-return tradeoffs."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A minimum-variance portfolio targeting 5% return must be efficient even though the global minimum-variance portfolio offers 6% at no greater risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A portfolio targeting 5% is *below* the GMV's 6% return; GMV offers a higher return at no greater risk, so the 5% portfolio is **dominated** and inefficient.", "whyPool": "A portfolio below the global minimum-variance portfolio's return is dominated when the global minimum- variance portfolio has at least as little risk and a higher return."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Moving upward along the efficient frontier generally involves accepting more risk for a higher expected return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Moving up the efficient frontier means accepting more volatility for higher expected return.", "whyPool": "Higher frontier returns ordinarily require higher volatility."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Every feasible portfolio lies on the efficient frontier.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Most feasible portfolios are interior or dominated (inefficient); only the frontier portfolios are efficient.", "whyPool": "Many feasible portfolios are interior or dominated and therefore inefficient."}], "reference": "Frontier construction, GMV as the frontier's turning point — Markowitz mean-variance; Investopedia \"Efficient Frontier.\"", "n": 382}, {"id": "QF-D4G4Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G4", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A new low-correlation asset becomes investable. The portfolio team reruns its optimization using the same expected- return targets and otherwise unchanged inputs.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Expanding the investable set cannot make the mathematical minimum variance at a given target return worse, provided old portfolios remain feasible.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Expanding the investable set cannot worsen the minimum variance at a given target: the optimizer can still choose any previously feasible portfolio, so the minimized objective can only stay equal or improve.", "whyPool": "The optimizer can still choose any previously feasible portfolio, so the minimum objective value cannot increase."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The efficient frontier cannot shift outward by offering lower risk for a given expected return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Useful diversification *can* shift the frontier outward (lower risk for a given return). \"Cannot shift outward\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Useful diversification can improve attainable risk-return combinations."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the optimizer gives the new asset zero weight at every target, the original frontier cannot remain unchanged.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** If the new asset gets zero weight everywhere, the selected portfolios and frontier are unchanged. \"Cannot remain unchanged\" reverses the conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. An unused asset does not alter the selected portfolios or their frontier."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Adding an asset guarantees that every efficient portfolio will hold it with a positive weight.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Adding an asset does not force positive weight in every efficient portfolio — it may be unattractive or redundant at some or all frontier points.", "whyPool": "The asset may be unattractive or redundant at some or all frontier points."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A shift in the frontier reflects changes in feasible risk-return combinations, not a guarantee about realized future performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A frontier shift reflects changes in *feasible* (model-implied) risk-return combinations; it is not a guarantee about realized future performance.", "whyPool": "The frontier is conditional on model inputs, which may differ from realized outcomes. G5: Optimization Inputs and Estimation Error"}], "reference": "Diversification & frontier improvement, model-conditional frontier — Markowitz; Investopedia \"Efficient Frontier,\" \"Diversification.\"", "n": 383}, {"id": "QF-D4G5Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quant team runs a mean-variance optimizer using historical expected returns and covariances. The output assigns 78% of the portfolio to one small-cap stock and small short positions to several others. The chief investment officer asks whether the optimizer has uncovered a uniquely attractive opportunity.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Small changes in expected-return estimates can produce large changes in optimized weights.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "MV optimization is highly sensitive to inputs — small changes in expected-return estimates can produce large weight shifts (**error maximization**).", "whyPool": "Mean-variance optimization can be highly sensitive to estimated inputs."}, {"L": "B", "text": "An extreme allocation can reflect estimation error rather than genuine economic attractiveness.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "An extreme allocation can reflect **estimation error** (noisy forecasts) rather than genuine economic attractiveness.", "whyPool": "Extreme weights may be artifacts of noisy forecasts."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The optimizer’s output is guaranteed to remain optimal out of sample.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "In-sample optimality does not guarantee the weights stay optimal out of sample.", "whyPool": "In-sample optimality does not guarantee future optimality."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Adding realistic position limits cannot produce a more stable portfolio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Realistic position limits *can* produce a more stable, implementable portfolio. \"Cannot produce a more stable portfolio\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Constraints can reduce instability and improve implementability."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Historical optimization can overfit the sample used to estimate inputs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Historical optimization can **overfit** the sample used to estimate inputs, exploiting sample-specific noise.", "whyPool": "The optimizer may exploit sample-specific noise."}], "reference": "Estimation error, error maximization, constraints for stability — Michaud (1989) \"The Markowitz optimization enigma\"; Investopedia \"Estimation Error.\"", "n": 384}, {"id": "QF-D4G5Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two analysts estimate expected returns for the same four assets using different historical windows. Their covariance estimates are similar, but their optimizers produce sharply different maximum-Sharpe allocations. 326 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Expected returns are inputs rather than facts known with certainty.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Expected returns are **estimates** subject to forecast error, not certainties.", "whyPool": "Future expected returns must be estimated and are subject to forecast error."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Small differences in expected-return estimates can be amplified into large differences in optimized weights.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Small differences in expected returns can be **amplified** into large differences in optimized weights.", "whyPool": "Optimization may respond aggressively to small estimated advantages among assets."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Similar covariance matrices guarantee similar maximum-Sharpe portfolios.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Similar covariance matrices do not guarantee similar max-Sharpe portfolios — different expected-return vectors can materially change the solution.", "whyPool": "Different expected-return vectors can materially change a mean-variance solution even when covariances are similar."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing allocations across estimation windows cannot reveal sensitivity to the chosen sample.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Comparing allocations across windows *does* reveal sensitivity to the chosen sample; large swings are evidence of instability. \"Cannot reveal sensitivity\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Large weight changes across windows are evidence that the result is unstable."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Using more decimal places in the optimizer's output eliminates the uncertainty in its estimated inputs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reporting more decimal places does not reduce the uncertainty in the estimated inputs — precision ≠ accuracy.", "whyPool": "Numerical precision in reported weights does not improve the economic accuracy of the inputs."}], "reference": "Input sensitivity of MV weights — Michaud (1989); Investopedia \"Estimation Error\"; CFA curriculum on inputs to optimization.", "n": 385}, {"id": "QF-D4G5Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G5", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio optimizer receives an estimated covariance matrix that understates the relationship between two technology stocks because the sample covers an unusually calm period. It assigns large weights to both stocks and reports substantial diversification.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Understated covariance can cause the optimizer to overstate the diversification benefit of holding both stocks.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Understated covariance makes the holdings look more independent than they are, so the optimizer **overstates** the diversification benefit.", "whyPool": "The model treats the holdings as more independent than the inputs justify."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If their realized covariance is higher than estimated, realized portfolio volatility may exceed the forecast.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If realized covariance is higher than estimated, the larger cross-covariance term raises realized portfolio variance above forecast (`σ_p2` rises with `w_i w_j σ_ij`).", "whyPool": "A larger realized cross-covariance term raises portfolio variance, all else equal."}, {"L": "C", "text": "An optimization algorithm can correct inaccurate inputs merely by solving the mathematical problem exactly.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Solving the specified model exactly cannot make an incorrect covariance assumption accurate — computation ≠ correct inputs.", "whyPool": "Exact computation solves the specified model but cannot make incorrect assumptions accurate."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Shrinkage, longer or alternative samples, and stress scenarios are possible ways to evaluate robustness.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "**Shrinkage**, longer/alternative samples, and stress scenarios are legitimate ways to test or reduce reliance on one noisy covariance estimate.", "whyPool": "These approaches test or reduce reliance on one noisy covariance estimate. 327 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "The phrase “garbage in, garbage out” applies because a correct optimizer cannot still produce a poor allocation from poor estimates.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** \"Garbage in, garbage out\" applies precisely because a mathematically correct optimizer *can* still produce a poor allocation from poor estimates. The statement's \"cannot still produce a poor allocation\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Mathematical correctness does not protect the result from input error. G6: Constraints and Practical Portfolio Design"}], "reference": "Covariance estimation error, shrinkage, garbage-in-garbage-out — Ledoit & Wolf shrinkage; Investopedia \"Covariance,\" \"Estimation Error.\"", "n": 386}, {"id": "QF-D4G6Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An unconstrained optimizer recommends investing 160% in equities, shorting 60% in bonds, and rebalancing most of the portfolio each month. A pension plan cannot short, permits no leverage, and wants to limit trading costs.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The unconstrained allocation is not infeasible under the pension plan's long-only policy.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** The −60% bond position is a short, which violates the long-only policy, so the allocation *is* infeasible. \"Is not infeasible\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The negative bond position violates the prohibition on short selling."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A gross-exposure or leverage constraint can prevent the 160% long position financed by the bond short.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A gross-exposure/leverage constraint limits total long + short exposure and can prevent the 160% long financed by the bond short.", "whyPool": "Such a constraint limits the total amount of long and short exposure the optimizer can employ."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A turnover constraint cannot limit how far new weights move from current weights.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** A turnover constraint *does* limit how far new weights move from current weights. \"Cannot limit\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Turnover measures trading needed to transition between existing and proposed allocations."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Adding these constraints must improve the portfolio's modeled maximum Sharpe ratio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Adding constraints cannot *improve* the modeled maximum Sharpe — restricting the feasible set can only hold or lower the in-sample objective (though it aids real-world robustness).", "whyPool": "Restricting the feasible set cannot improve the maximum value of the same mathematical objective, though it may improve real-world robustness."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A constrained portfolio may sacrifice some in-sample objective value in exchange for implementability.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A constrained portfolio may give up some in-sample objective value in exchange for being implementable/holdable by the institution.", "whyPool": "Practical controls can trade an idealized model optimum for a portfolio the institution can hold and trade."}], "reference": "Long-only/leverage/turnover constraints, feasible-set restriction — Grinold & Kahn \"Active Portfolio Management\"; Investopedia \"Portfolio Constraints.\"", "n": 387}, {"id": "QF-D4G6Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A diversified fund limits any sector to 30%. Its unconstrained maximum-Sharpe solution places 55% in technology because estimated technology returns are slightly higher than those of other sectors.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The sector limit makes the unconstrained solution infeasible.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A 55% technology allocation exceeds the 30% cap, so the unconstrained solution is infeasible under the constraint.", "whyPool": "A 55% sector allocation exceeds the 30% cap."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Under the constraint, at least 25 percentage points must not be reassigned from technology to other sectors.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Cutting technology from 55% to ≤ 30% requires reallocating **at least 25 percentage points** away from tech. The phrasing \"at least 25 pp must *not* be reassigned\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Reducing technology from 55% to no more than 30% requires reallocating at least 25 percentage points."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The constrained optimum must have a higher modeled Sharpe ratio than the unconstrained optimum.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The constrained optimum cannot have a *higher* modeled Sharpe than the unconstrained one — the unconstrained feasible set contains all constrained portfolios, so it attains at least as high an objective.", "whyPool": "The unconstrained feasible set includes all constrained portfolios and can attain at least as high a modeled objective value."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The limit can reduce exposure to estimation error concentrated in one sector forecast. 328 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A cap limits the damage if the optimizer exaggerated technology's attractiveness — it reduces exposure to estimation error concentrated in one sector forecast.", "whyPool": "A cap limits the damage if the optimizer exaggerated technology's attractiveness."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the technology weight lands exactly at 30%, the sector constraint may be binding.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If tech lands exactly at 30%, the sector constraint is **binding** (optimum sits on the boundary; relaxing it could change the solution).", "whyPool": "A constraint is binding when the optimum lies at its imposed boundary and relaxing it could affect the solution."}], "reference": "Sector limits, binding constraints, constrained vs. unconstrained objective — Investopedia \"Binding Constraint,\" \"Portfolio Constraints\"; Grinold & Kahn.", "n": 388}, {"id": "QF-D4G6Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G6", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund currently holds 50% in Asset A and 50% in Asset B. Its optimizer proposes 20% in A and 80% in B, but the mandate limits one-way turnover, defined here as one-half the sum of absolute weight changes, to 10% per rebalance.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The proposed changes are -30 percentage points for A and +30 percentage points for B.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A falls 50% → 20% (−30 pp); B rises 50% → 80% (+30 pp).", "whyPool": "A falls from 50% to 20%, while B rises from 50% to 80%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The proposed one-way turnover is 30%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "One-way turnover = `1⁄2(|−30%| + |+30%|) = 1⁄2(60%) = 30%`.", "whyPool": "One-half of (|−30%| + |+30%|) equals 30%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The proposed trade satisfies the 10% turnover limit.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The required 30% turnover exceeds the 10% cap, so the proposed trade violates the limit.", "whyPool": "The required 30% turnover exceeds the 10% limit."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A feasible move in the proposed direction could not be 40% in A and 60% in B.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Moving 10 pp from A to B (→ 40% A / 60% B) gives one-way turnover of exactly 10%, which *is* feasible. \"Could not be 40%/60%\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Moving ten percentage points from A to B produces one-way turnover of 10%."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The turnover limit cannot cause the implemented portfolio to adjust toward the unconstrained target over several rebalances rather than immediately.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** A binding turnover cap *can* cause the portfolio to adjust toward the target gradually over several rebalances rather than immediately. \"Cannot cause ... over several rebalances\" reverses the conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A binding trading limit can spread movement toward the target across time. 329 Rev:081020261230 G7: In-Sample Optimization and Out-of-Sample Performance"}], "reference": "Turnover constraints and phased rebalancing — Grinold & Kahn \"Active Portfolio Management\"; Investopedia \"Portfolio Turnover.\"", "n": 389}, {"id": "QF-D4G7Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G7", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher uses ten years of monthly returns to estimate inputs, choose portfolio weights, and report performance over those same ten years. The optimized portfolio substantially outperforms a simple equal-weight benchmark in that sample.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The reported comparison is not in sample because the same observations influenced both the weights and the evaluation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** The comparison *is* in-sample — the same observations drove both weight selection and performance measurement. \"Is not in sample\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The data served both model selection and performance measurement."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Selecting the best weights for the historical sample can capitalize on noise that will not repeat.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Choosing the best weights for the historical sample can capitalize on **noise** that won't repeat (overfitting).", "whyPool": "Optimization can fit sample-specific return and covariance patterns."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The in-sample outperformance guarantees superior future performance if the optimizer solved the problem correctly.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "In-sample outperformance does not guarantee superior future performance even if the optimizer solved the problem correctly — estimated relationships may not persist.", "whyPool": "Solving the historical problem exactly does not make the estimated relationships stable in the future."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Evaluating the fixed weights on later, previously unused data would provide an out-of-sample test.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Evaluating the fixed weights on later, previously unused data provides a genuine **out-of-sample** test.", "whyPool": "Unused subsequent observations can test how the selected portfolio generalizes."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Repeatedly revising the strategy after inspecting the test period can contaminate its status as an independent test.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Repeatedly revising the strategy after inspecting the test period contaminates its independence — the test data becomes part of development.", "whyPool": "Using test results to redesign the strategy turns that information into part of the development process."}], "reference": "In-sample vs. out-of-sample, overfitting portfolio weights — Investopedia \"Backtesting,\" \"Overfitting\"; ISLR ch.5 (validation) by analogy.", "n": 390}, {"id": "QF-D4G7Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G7", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A team computes the ex post optimal portfolio for every calendar year using that year's complete returns. It then presents the sequence of annual results as a strategy an investor could have followed in real time.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Each year's ex post weights use information that was not unavailable at the start of that year.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Stacked double negative.** \"Uses information that *was not unavailable*\" = \"was available.\" Full-year returns reveal outcomes an investor could **not** know at the year's start, so the info was *not* available — the statement is false.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Full-year returns reveal outcomes that an investor could not know at the year's beginning."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The reported performance is subject to look-ahead bias if the weights are treated as contemporaneously investable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Applying hindsight-selected (ex post) weights as if known earlier creates **look-ahead bias**.", "whyPool": "Applying hindsight-selected weights as though they were known earlier creates look-ahead bias."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A real-time implementation would not need to estimate weights using only information available before each investment decision.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** A realistic simulation *must* estimate weights using only information available before each decision. \"Would not need to\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A realistic simulation must respect the information set at each decision time."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because every annual optimization is mathematically valid, the sequence is automatically a valid backtest.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Each annual optimization being mathematically valid does not make the sequence a valid backtest — correct math doesn't cure improper timing of the information used.", "whyPool": "Correct mathematics does not cure improper timing of the information used."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The portfolio that was optimal after one year ended may not be optimal during the next year. 330 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Expected returns, volatilities, and correlations change across periods, so the portfolio optimal after one year need not stay optimal during the next.", "whyPool": "Expected returns, volatilities, and correlations can change across periods."}], "reference": "Look-ahead bias, ex post vs. ex ante optimization — Investopedia \"Look-Ahead Bias,\" \"Backtesting\"; Grinold & Kahn on realistic implementation.", "n": 391}, {"id": "QF-D4G7Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "04", "domainName": "Portfolio Optimization", "group": "G7", "weeks": [3], "weekMax": 3, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An asset manager estimates optimal weights using rolling five-year windows. The recommended allocation changes from 70% Asset A in one window to 15% in the next, while an equal-weight portfolio remains comparatively stable.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Large weight changes across adjacent samples suggest optimization sensitivity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A large allocation reversal from modest sample changes indicates an **unstable** estimated optimum (high sensitivity to inputs).", "whyPool": "A large allocation reversal from modest sample changes indicates an unstable estimated optimum."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Weight instability cannot generate turnover and trading costs in an implemented strategy.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "**Double negative.** Weight instability *does* generate turnover and trading costs — following shifting targets requires trades that erode realized performance. \"Cannot generate turnover\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Following shifting targets requires trades that can erode realized performance."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Stability alone proves that the equal-weight portfolio will earn a higher future return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Stability alone does not prove the equal-weight portfolio earns higher future returns (though it may reduce estimation/trading risk).", "whyPool": "Stable weights do not guarantee superior returns, though they may reduce estimation and trading risk."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing rolling-window and genuinely out-of-sample results can help assess robustness.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing rolling-window results with genuinely out-of-sample results helps assess whether the historical optima persist (robustness).", "whyPool": "These comparisons show whether historical optima persist beyond their estimation samples."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Regularization, shrinkage, or allocation bounds may reduce extreme changes in optimized weights.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Regularization, **shrinkage**, or allocation bounds can reduce the optimizer's extreme response to noisy inputs.", "whyPool": "Robustness techniques and constraints can reduce the optimizer's response to noisy inputs. 331 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Weight instability, robustness techniques (shrinkage/regularization/bounds) — Michaud (1989); Ledoit & Wolf; Investopedia \"Estimation Error.\"", "n": 392}, {"id": "QF-D5G1Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G1", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A stock is quoted at a best bid of $49.90 and a best ask of $50.10. An investor is considering an immediate round trip, ignoring commissions and price changes.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The quoted spread is $0.20 per share.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Spread = ask − bid = $50.10 − $49.90 = $0.20.", "whyPool": "The spread is $50.10 - $49.90 = $0.20."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The quoted midpoint is not $50.00.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Midpoint = ($49.90 + $50.10)/2 = $50.00, so \"not $50.00\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The midpoint is ($49.90 + $50.10) / 2 = $50.00."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A market buy would not normally execute against the ask side of the book.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "An aggressive buyer takes resting sell liquidity at the ask; a market buy lifts the ask.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. An aggressive buyer trades with resting sell liquidity at the ask."}, {"L": "D", "text": "An immediate market buy followed by an immediate market sell would gain $0.20 per share.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Buying at $50.10 and selling at $49.90 *loses* $0.20/share — the round trip pays the spread.", "whyPool": "Buying at $50.10 and selling at $49.90 produces a $0.20 loss per share."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The spread represents one component of the cost of demanding immediate liquidity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Crossing the spread is a cost of immediacy paid to liquidity providers.", "whyPool": "Crossing the spread is a cost paid for immediate execution."}], "reference": "Investopedia — Bid-Ask Spread (cost of immediacy). Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — ch. on bid/ask quotes and the cost of liquidity.", "n": 393}, {"id": "QF-D5G1Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G1", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Stock A is quoted at $20.00 bid and $20.04 ask, while Stock B is quoted at $200.00 bid and $200.20 ask. A trader compares their spreads.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Stock A's dollar spread is $0.04.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$20.04 − $20.00 = $0.04.", "whyPool": "$20.04 - $20.00 = $0.04. 334 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Stock B's dollar spread is larger than Stock A's.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$0.20 (B) > $0.04 (A) in dollar terms.", "whyPool": "Stock B's $0.20 spread exceeds Stock A's $0.04 spread."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Relative to the midpoint, Stock A's spread is approximately 0.20%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$0.04 / $20.02 ≈ 0.20%.", "whyPool": "$0.04 / $20.02 is approximately 0.20%."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Relative to the midpoint, Stock B's spread is not approximately 0.10%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. $0.20 / $200.10 ≈ 0.10%, so the \"not\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. $0.20 / $200.10 is approximately 0.10%."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The larger dollar spread proves that Stock B is less liquid by every meaningful measure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Liquidity also depends on relative spread, depth, volume, resilience, and trade size — dollar spread alone doesn't prove it.", "whyPool": "Liquidity also depends on relative spread, depth, volume, resilience, and trade size."}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — market microstructure / liquidity measures (relative spread, depth). Investopedia — Bid-Ask Spread and liquidity.", "n": 394}, {"id": "QF-D5G1Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G1", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "During a quiet period, a stock's quote changes from $75.00 × $75.02 to $74.95 × $75.07, with similar displayed depth.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that the midpoint remains $75.01 after the quote change.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded-claim trap. Both quotes have midpoint $75.01 ((75.00+75.02)/2 and (74.95+75.07)/2), so the underlying claim is correct and should be accepted.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. Both quotes have a midpoint of $75.01."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The spread widens from $0.02 to $0.12.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "New spread = $75.07 − $74.95 = $0.12; old was $0.02.", "whyPool": "The new spread is $75.07 - $74.95 = $0.12."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The wider spread generally makes immediate trading more expensive.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A liquidity demander must cross a larger spread.", "whyPool": "A liquidity demander must cross a larger spread."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The unchanged midpoint proves that the stock's liquidity is unchanged.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Spread width is itself a liquidity dimension; an unchanged midpoint doesn't prove unchanged liquidity.", "whyPool": "Spread width is itself an important dimension of liquidity."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A passive buyer posting at the bid cannot avoid crossing the spread but assumes fill risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: a bid-side limit order *avoids* crossing the spread (controls price) but may never execute (fill risk).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A bid-side limit order controls price but may never execute. G2: Market Orders and Limit Orders"}], "reference": "Investopedia — Bid-Ask Spread; Limit Order / fill risk. Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — liquidity dimensions.", "n": 395}, {"id": "QF-D5G2Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G2", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A trading desk must buy 200,000 shares of a moderately liquid stock before the close. The head trader wants guaranteed completion, while the portfolio manager wants to avoid paying substantially above the current ask.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A market order provides more certainty of execution than a limit order.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Market orders prioritize execution over price.", "whyPool": "Market orders prioritize execution over price."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A limit order guarantees execution at the specified price.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A limit order may never execute; it guarantees price bound, not a fill.", "whyPool": "A limit order may not execute."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A large market order may walk through multiple levels of the order book.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "It consumes liquidity at progressively worse prices when top-of-book size is insufficient.", "whyPool": "The order may consume liquidity at progressively worse prices."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Splitting the order can reduce immediate market impact but introduces timing risk. 335 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Slower execution lowers instantaneous footprint but exposes the order to adverse price moves.", "whyPool": "Slower execution can reduce impact but creates the risk of adverse price movement."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The quoted ask necessarily equals the average execution price for the full 200,000-share order.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Available depth at the best ask is limited; the fill VWAP typically exceeds the quoted ask.", "whyPool": "Available depth may be insufficient at the best ask."}], "reference": "Investopedia — Market Order vs. Limit Order. Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — order types and execution certainty.", "n": 396}, {"id": "QF-D5G2Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G2", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A stock is quoted at $30.00 bid and $30.05 ask. A trader submits a limit order to buy 1,000 shares at $30.02.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The order should immediately execute against the displayed $30.05 ask.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The $30.02 buy limit is below the $30.05 ask, so it is not marketable and won't execute immediately.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The buy limit is below the current ask."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The order cannot become resting bid-side liquidity at $30.02.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Subject to venue rules it can rest as a bid at its limit price.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Subject to venue rules, it can rest as a bid at its limit price."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The trader is guaranteed to receive 1,000 shares if the market later trades at $30.02.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Queue priority and available quantity can prevent a fill even if trades print at that price.", "whyPool": "Queue priority and available quantity can prevent a fill even if trades occur at that price."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If filled, the order cannot execute above its $30.02 limit price.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A buy limit sets the maximum acceptable execution price.", "whyPool": "A buy limit sets the maximum acceptable execution price."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Raising the buy limit to $30.05 could make the order immediately marketable against displayed ask liquidity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A buy priced at the ask can trade with resting sellers.", "whyPool": "A buy order priced at the ask can trade with resting sellers."}], "reference": "Investopedia — Limit Order (marketable limit, price protection). Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — limit order book mechanics and priority.", "n": 397}, {"id": "QF-D5G2Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G2", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Minutes before an earnings announcement, a manager wants to sell a position but is choosing between a market order and a limit order.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A market sell emphasizes prompt execution but leaves the realized price uncertain. 336 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Market orders prioritize execution, not a guaranteed price.", "whyPool": "A market order prioritizes execution, not a guaranteed price."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A sell limit establishes the minimum acceptable execution price.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A sell limit prevents execution below its limit.", "whyPool": "A sell limit prevents execution below its limit."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A sell limit placed above the current bid cannot remain unfilled if prices fall.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: the market can move away before anyone trades with the order, leaving it unfilled.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The market can move away before anyone trades with the order."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Greater urgency generally strengthens the case for accepting price uncertainty to obtain execution.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Urgent traders often accept more price risk to secure completion.", "whyPool": "Urgent traders often accept greater price risk for completion."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A market order eliminates both fill risk and the risk of an unfavorable execution price.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Market orders reduce non-execution risk but can still receive poor prices.", "whyPool": "Market orders reduce non-execution risk but can receive poor prices. G3: Order Book and Market Depth"}], "reference": "Investopedia — Market vs. Limit Orders; slippage under urgency. Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — execution vs. price risk trade-off.", "n": 398}, {"id": "QF-D5G3Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G3", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "The ask side of an order book shows 500 shares at $10.01, 1,000 at $10.03, and 2,000 at $10.05. A trader submits a market order to buy 2,000 shares, and no orders arrive or cancel during execution.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The order buys 500 shares at $10.01.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The best ask level is consumed first.", "whyPool": "The best ask is consumed first."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The order buys 1,000 shares at $10.03.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The next 1,000 consume the second ask level.", "whyPool": "The next 1,000 shares consume the second ask level."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that the remaining 500 shares execute at $10.05.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded-claim trap. After 1,500 shares, 500 remain and fill at $10.05, so the underlying claim is correct and should be accepted.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. After 1,500 shares, 500 remain for the $10.05 level."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The volume-weighted average execution price is not $10.03.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. VWAP = (500×10.01 + 1,000×10.03 + 500×10.05)/2,000 = $10.03, so \"not $10.03\" reverses the conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. (500×10.01 + 1,000×10.03 + 500×10.05) / 2,000 = $10.03."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The entire order executes at the original best ask because that was the displayed quote when it was submitted.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Only 500 shares were displayed at the original best ask; the rest fills deeper in the book.", "whyPool": "Only 500 shares were displayed at the original best ask. 337 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Investopedia — Order Book / market depth; slippage from walking the book. Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — limit order book and VWAP of a marketable order.", "n": 399}, {"id": "QF-D5G3Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G3", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two stocks both show a one-cent spread. Stock A displays 100 shares at each best quote, while Stock B displays 20,000 shares at each best quote.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The identical spreads imply identical displayed depth.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Spread and displayed quantity measure different aspects of liquidity.", "whyPool": "Spread and displayed quantity measure different aspects of liquidity."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A 10,000-share market order is more likely to walk the book in Stock A.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Only 100 shares sit at Stock A's best price, so the order exhausts it and moves to worse levels.", "whyPool": "Only a small quantity is available at Stock A's best price."}, {"L": "C", "text": "For a very small order, both stocks may initially offer the same quoted price concession.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "An order within the displayed size crosses the same one-cent spread in either name.", "whyPool": "An order within the displayed size may cross the same one-cent spread."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Market depth is a relevant liquidity measure even when quoted spreads match.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Depth determines how much can trade near the best quote.", "whyPool": "Depth helps determine how much can trade near the best quote."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Displayed depth is guaranteed to remain available until every incoming order has traded against it.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Orders may be canceled or changed before execution; displayed depth is not guaranteed.", "whyPool": "Orders may be canceled or changed before execution."}], "reference": "Investopedia — Market Depth / Order Book; liquidity beyond the spread. Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — depth and displayed vs. available liquidity.", "n": 400}, {"id": "QF-D5G3Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G3", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An order book shows much greater displayed buy quantity than sell quantity near the midpoint. An analyst treats this imbalance as a short-term bullish signal.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The imbalance describes an asymmetry in displayed depth.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "More displayed quantity appears on one side of the book.", "whyPool": "More displayed quantity appears on one side of the book."}, {"L": "B", "text": "It cannot contain information about near-term supply and demand.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: depth imbalance can sometimes provide short-horizon information.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Depth imbalance can sometimes provide short-horizon information. 338 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "It guarantees that the next price movement will be upward.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Order-book signals are probabilistic and can fail or be manipulated.", "whyPool": "Order-book signals are probabilistic and can fail or be manipulated."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Cancellations or hidden liquidity can weaken the signal.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The displayed book is incomplete and can change rapidly.", "whyPool": "The displayed book is incomplete and can change rapidly."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Using the signal without accounting for execution costs cannot overstate its trading value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: a predictive signal need not remain profitable after trading costs, so ignoring costs *can* overstate its value.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A predictive signal need not remain profitable after trading costs. G4: Liquidity, Slippage, and Market Impact"}], "reference": "Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — order-book information and imbalance. Investopedia — Order Book / liquidity; net-of-cost signal value.", "n": 401}, {"id": "QF-D5G4Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G4", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "When a buy decision is made, a stock's best ask is $40.00. The desk buys 10,000 shares at an average price of $40.12.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Relative to the quoted ask, the execution slippage is $0.12 per share.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$40.12 − $40.00 = $0.12 per share.", "whyPool": "$40.12 - $40.00 = $0.12 per share."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The total price difference relative to buying all shares at $40.00 is $1,200.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$0.12 × 10,000 = $1,200.", "whyPool": "$0.12 × 10,000 = $1,200."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The average execution was better for the buyer than the quoted ask.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A buyer prefers a *lower* price; $40.12 > $40.00 is worse.", "whyPool": "A buyer prefers a lower, not higher, execution price."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Limited depth and market impact could both contribute to the difference.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Walking the book and the order's own price impact can both worsen the fill.", "whyPool": "Walking the book and the order's effect on prices can both worsen execution."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The quotation alone is sufficient to identify exactly how much of the difference was temporary market impact.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Separating temporary vs. permanent impact requires additional data and a benchmark methodology.", "whyPool": "Separating impact components requires additional data and a benchmark methodology."}], "reference": "Investopedia — Slippage; Market Impact. Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — realized vs. quoted price and impact decomposition.", "n": 402}, {"id": "QF-D5G4Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G4", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund trades the same dollar amount in a deep large-cap stock and a thin small-cap stock. The small-cap order is large relative to normal volume.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The small-cap trade will generally face greater liquidity risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Thin markets absorb large relative orders less easily.", "whyPool": "Thin markets generally absorb large relative orders less easily."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Equal dollar orders necessarily produce equal percentage market impact.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Impact depends on depth, volume, volatility, and size relative to liquidity — not dollars alone.", "whyPool": "Impact depends on depth, volume, volatility, and trade size relative to liquidity."}, {"L": "C", "text": "An order's size relative to available liquidity matters for execution quality.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Capacity is not determined by dollars alone.", "whyPool": "Capacity is not determined by dollars alone."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A profitable paper strategy cannot become infeasible if its required trades exceed market capacity. 339 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: liquidity can constrain whether theoretical signals are implementable at scale.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Liquidity can constrain whether theoretical signals can be implemented at scale."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Reducing the order's participation rate cannot lower immediate impact while increasing exposure to price movement during execution.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: slower trading exchanges impact risk for timing risk (it *does* lower immediate impact).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Slower trading exchanges impact risk for timing risk."}], "reference": "Investopedia — Market Impact / Liquidity; strategy capacity. Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — order size vs. available liquidity.", "n": 403}, {"id": "QF-D5G4Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G4", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "After a large buy order, a stock rises from $25.00 to $25.30. Ten minutes after the order ends, it falls to $25.08 and remains near that level for the rest of the day.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The initial increase could not include temporary and permanent impact.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: the immediate response can contain both transient pressure and lasting information effects.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The immediate response can contain both transient pressure and lasting information effects."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The reversal from $25.30 to $25.08 is consistent with some temporary impact decaying.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Price reversion after completion is consistent with transient pressure dissipating.", "whyPool": "Price reversion after completion is consistent with temporary pressure dissipating."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The remaining $0.08 increase proves that the order permanently changed fundamental value by exactly $0.08.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Other trades, information, and benchmark choices prevent that exact causal conclusion.", "whyPool": "Other trades, information, and benchmark choices prevent that exact causal conclusion."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Market-wide news during the interval could confound the impact estimate.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Concurrent market moves can affect the observed price path.", "whyPool": "Concurrent market moves can affect the observed price path."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Temporary impact matters to execution cost even if the price later reverses.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The trader may have paid the temporarily elevated prices during execution.", "whyPool": "The trader may have paid the temporarily elevated prices. 340 Rev:081020261230 G5: Short Selling Mechanics"}], "reference": "Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — temporary vs. permanent price impact. Investopedia — Market Impact; slippage attribution.", "n": 404}, {"id": "QF-D5G5Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G5", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A trader borrows and sells short 1,000 shares at $50, then later buys them back at $42, ignoring all fees and dividends.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The opening transaction is not a sell-to-open.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: selling borrowed shares *is* a sell-to-open that establishes the short.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Selling borrowed shares establishes the short position."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The closing transaction is a buy-to-close.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Repurchasing shares closes the short position.", "whyPool": "Repurchasing shares closes it."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The gross trading profit is $8,000.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "($50 − $42) × 1,000 = $8,000.", "whyPool": "($50 - $42) × 1,000 = $8,000."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The short position profits because the stock price increased.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A short profits when the price *declines*.", "whyPool": "The short profits because the price declined."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The shares purchased to close cannot be returned to the lender.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: the repurchased shares satisfy the obligation to return the borrowed shares.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The repurchased shares satisfy the obligation to return borrowed shares."}], "reference": "Investopedia — Short Selling (sell-to-open / buy-to-close). CFA curriculum — short-position mechanics.", "n": 405}, {"id": "QF-D5G5Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G5", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An investor shorts 500 shares at $20. During the holding period, the company pays a $0.40 dividend, and the borrow fee is $150. The investor covers at $18.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The gross price profit before carrying costs is $1,000.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "($20 − $18) × 500 = $1,000.", "whyPool": "($20 - $18) × 500 = $1,000. 341 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "The short seller generally owes a payment equivalent to the $200 dividend on the borrowed shares.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The lender is economically entitled to the dividend; $0.40 × 500 = $200 owed by the short.", "whyPool": "The share lender is economically entitled to the dividend, costing the short seller $200."}, {"L": "C", "text": "After the dividend payment and borrow fee, the net profit is not $650, ignoring other costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. $1,000 − $200 − $150 = $650, so \"not $650\" reverses the conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. $1,000 - $200 - $150 = $650."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Borrow fees increase the net return of a short position.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Borrow fees are a *cost* that reduces net return.", "whyPool": "Borrow fees are a cost that reduces net return."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A high-conviction price forecast can still be unprofitable after short-position carrying costs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Dividends, borrow charges, and other costs can consume a forecasted gain.", "whyPool": "Dividends, borrow charges, and other costs can consume a forecasted gain."}], "reference": "Investopedia — Short Selling costs (borrow fee, dividend obligation). CFA curriculum — carrying costs of short positions.", "n": 406}, {"id": "QF-D5G5Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G5", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A crowded short position rises sharply after unexpected good news. Lenders also begin recalling shares and borrow availability falls.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A short seller's maximum gain is bounded because a stock price cannot fall below zero.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The greatest price decline is from the sale price to zero, capping the gain.", "whyPool": "The greatest price decline is from the sale price to zero."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A short seller's theoretical loss is not unlimited because the stock price has no fixed upper bound.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: because price has no upper bound, repurchase cost can keep rising — loss *is* theoretically unlimited.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Repurchase cost can keep rising as the share price increases."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Forced buying by short sellers cannot intensify a short squeeze.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: buy-to-close demand adds upward pressure and can intensify a squeeze.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Buy-to-close demand can add upward pressure."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Recall risk can force a trader to close even when the trader still believes the stock is overvalued.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Continued borrowing is not always under the short seller's control.", "whyPool": "Continued borrowing is not always under the short seller's control."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Posting collateral eliminates price risk and borrow risk for the short seller.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Collateral supports the position but does not remove its economic or operational risks.", "whyPool": "Collateral supports the position but does not remove its economic or operational risks. G6: Transaction Costs and Implementation Shortfall"}], "reference": "Investopedia — Short Squeeze; unlimited loss; recall risk. CFA curriculum — short-selling risks.", "n": 407}, {"id": "QF-D5G6Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G6", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A manager decides to buy 5,000 shares when the stock is $100.00. Because approval is delayed, execution occurs at an average price of $100.40, with a $100 commission.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The decision-value benchmark is not $500,000.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. 5,000 × $100.00 = $500,000, so \"not $500,000\" reverses the conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 5,000 × $100.00 = $500,000."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The execution-price difference costs $2,000 relative to the decision price.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "5,000 × ($100.40 − $100.00) = $2,000.", "whyPool": "5,000 × ($100.40 - $100.00) = $2,000."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Including the commission, the implementation shortfall is $2,100 under this simplified benchmark. 342 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$2,000 price cost + $100 commission = $2,100.", "whyPool": "Adding the $100 commission gives $2,100."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The $0.40 adverse move may be described as a delay or execution-related cost, depending on the timing decomposition used.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Attribution depends on benchmark timestamps and the execution process.", "whyPool": "The attribution depends on benchmark timestamps and the execution process."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because the position may later be profitable, the implementation shortfall at execution must be zero.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Later investment performance does not erase the cost of implementing the decision.", "whyPool": "Later investment performance does not erase the cost of implementing the decision."}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — Implementation Shortfall (decision price benchmark, delay/execution decomposition). Investopedia — Implementation Shortfall.", "n": 408}, {"id": "QF-D5G6Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G6", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A strategy earns a 12% gross annual return. Its annual commissions are 0.5% of capital, spread costs are 1.0%, and estimated market impact is 1.5%, with no other costs.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The stated trading costs total 3.0% of capital.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "0.5% + 1.0% + 1.5% = 3.0%.", "whyPool": "0.5% + 1.0% + 1.5% = 3.0%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The simplified net return is not 9.0%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. 12.0% − 3.0% = 9.0% under the additive treatment, so \"not 9.0%\" reverses the conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 12.0% - 3.0% = 9.0% under the simplified additive treatment."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Reporting only the 12% gross return overstates the investor's realized performance under these assumptions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Investors keep returns *after* implementation costs, not before them.", "whyPool": "Investors retain returns after implementation costs, not before them."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Eliminating commissions would also eliminate spread and market-impact costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Spread and impact can remain even with zero explicit commission.", "whyPool": "Spreads and impact can remain even with zero explicit commission."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A high-turnover strategy cannot be more sensitive to per-trade costs than a low-turnover strategy.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: more frequent trading creates more opportunities to incur costs.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. More frequent trading creates more opportunities to incur costs. 343 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Investopedia — Transaction Costs; gross vs. net return. CFA curriculum — cost drag and turnover.", "n": 409}, {"id": "QF-D5G6Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G6", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio manager orders the sale of 10,000 shares at a $50.00 decision price. The desk sells 6,000 shares at $49.80 and 4,000 at $49.60, paying $200 in commissions.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The average execution price is $49.72.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "(6,000×49.80 + 4,000×49.60)/10,000 = $49.72.", "whyPool": "(6,000×49.80 + 4,000×49.60) / 10,000 = $49.72."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The price shortfall relative to the decision price is $0.28 per share.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "For a seller, $50.00 − $49.72 = $0.28 per share is adverse.", "whyPool": "For a seller, $50.00 - $49.72 = $0.28 per share is adverse."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The price component of implementation shortfall is $2,800.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$0.28 × 10,000 = $2,800.", "whyPool": "$0.28 × 10,000 = $2,800."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Including commissions, total simplified implementation shortfall is not $3,000.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. $2,800 + $200 = $3,000, so \"not $3,000\" reverses the conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. $2,800 + $200 = $3,000."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because this is a sale, receiving less than the decision price improves execution quality.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A seller benefits from a *higher* execution price; less than decision price is worse.", "whyPool": "A seller benefits from a higher execution price. G7: Execution Strategy and Order Splitting"}], "reference": "CFA curriculum — Implementation Shortfall (sell-side sign convention). Investopedia — VWAP; Implementation Shortfall.", "n": 410}, {"id": "QF-D5G7Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G7", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund must buy 1 million shares in a stock whose normal daily volume is 2 million shares. It considers executing immediately or splitting the order across the day.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The order equals 50% of normal daily volume and is not therefore large relative to typical activity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: 1M / 2M = 50% *is* large relative to typical activity.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 1 million / 2 million = 50%. 344 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Immediate execution cannot reduce timing risk but increase market impact.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: speed reduces exposure time but demands liquidity aggressively (raising impact).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Speed reduces exposure time but demands liquidity aggressively."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Slower execution may reduce participation at any moment but leaves more time for the price to move adversely.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Lower urgency reduces footprint while increasing timing exposure.", "whyPool": "Lower urgency can reduce footprint while increasing timing exposure."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Splitting the order guarantees a lower total execution cost.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Adverse price moves or information leakage can make slower execution more expensive.", "whyPool": "Adverse price moves or information leakage can make slower execution more expensive."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Strategy capacity should be evaluated because the desired trade is large relative to available market volume.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The ability to trade the required size is an implementation constraint.", "whyPool": "The ability to trade the required size is an implementation constraint."}], "reference": "Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — order splitting, timing risk, information leakage. Investopedia — Market Impact / strategy capacity.", "n": 411}, {"id": "QF-D5G7Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G7", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A trading algorithm releases equal-sized child orders every five minutes regardless of actual market volume. A second algorithm varies participation with observed volume.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Both approaches split a parent order into smaller child orders.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Each stages execution rather than submitting the full order at once.", "whyPool": "Each approach stages execution rather than submitting the full order at once."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The fixed schedule necessarily completes at the market's volume-weighted average price.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A schedule does not guarantee any particular benchmark price.", "whyPool": "A schedule does not guarantee any particular benchmark price."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A volume-sensitive schedule may trade more when market activity is greater.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Participation-based execution scales trading with market activity.", "whyPool": "Participation-based execution scales trading with market activity."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Predictable child orders cannot create information-leakage risk if other traders infer the parent order.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: detectable patterns *can* reveal sustained buying or selling interest.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Detectable patterns can reveal sustained buying or selling interest."}, {"L": "E", "text": "No execution algorithm can simultaneously eliminate market impact, timing risk, and non-completion risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Execution involves trade-offs among competing risks.", "whyPool": "Execution involves trade-offs among competing risks."}], "reference": "Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — execution algorithms (TWAP/VWAP/POV) and information leakage. Investopedia — VWAP; algorithmic execution.", "n": 412}, {"id": "QF-D5G7Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "05", "domainName": "Market Microstructure & Trade Execution", "group": "G7", "weeks": [5], "weekMax": 5, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A desk initially plans to buy gradually over six hours. After unexpected news, its signal is expected to decay within one hour, so the manager increases execution speed.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Faster execution can be rational when the opportunity is short-lived.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Delayed completion may miss a transient opportunity.", "whyPool": "Delayed completion may miss a transient opportunity."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Increasing speed generally reduces exposure to signal-decay and adverse-movement risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "More of the order is completed before the signal or price can change.", "whyPool": "More of the order is completed before the signal or price can change."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that increasing speed can require crossing the spread or consuming deeper liquidity more aggressively. 345 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded-claim trap. Urgency *can* increase spread costs and market impact, so the underlying claim is correct and should be accepted.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. Urgency can increase both spread costs and market impact."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The change in urgency has no relevance to the appropriate execution strategy.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Urgency is central to balancing completion, timing, and price risks.", "whyPool": "Urgency is central to balancing completion, timing, and price risks."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that the best schedule depends on expected alpha decay as well as transaction costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded-claim trap. Optimal execution balances the value of acting quickly against implementation costs, so the underlying claim is correct and should be accepted.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. Optimal execution balances the value of acting quickly against implementation costs. 346 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Harris, *Trading and Exchanges* — urgency, alpha decay, and the impact–timing trade-off. CFA curriculum — implementation shortfall and optimal execution scheduling.", "n": 413}, {"id": "QF-D6G1Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G1", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A research analyst estimates that a stock is intrinsically worth $70 per share, while it currently trades at $55. The portfolio manager asks whether the price difference is sufficient evidence to buy immediately.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The $55 market price is directly observable from current transactions or quotations.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Market price ($55) is an observed trading/quotation value, unlike a model-derived estimate — it is directly observable.", "whyPool": "Market price is an observed trading value, unlike a model-derived estimate."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The $70 intrinsic value is an estimate that depends on the analyst's assumptions and model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Intrinsic value ($70) depends on the analyst's inputs — expected cash flows, growth, and discount rate — so it is an estimate, not an observed fact.", "whyPool": "Intrinsic value depends on inputs such as expected cash flows, growth, and discount rates."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the analyst's estimate is correct, the stock appears undervalued by $15 per share.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If the $70 estimate is correct, the stock is undervalued by $70 − $55 = $15 per share.", "whyPool": "The estimated value exceeds the market price by $70 - $55 = $15."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The difference between estimated value and market price guarantees a profitable trade.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The estimate may be wrong, and even a correct value can fail to converge within the investor's horizon — a gap does not *guarantee* profit.", "whyPool": "The estimate may be wrong, and even correct value can fail to converge within the investor's horizon."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Another informed analyst could not reasonably estimate a different intrinsic value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: reasonable analysts *can* estimate a different intrinsic value using different forecasts, risk assessments, and methods.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Reasonable analysts may use different forecasts, risk assessments, and valuation methods."}], "reference": "Intrinsic value vs. market price, uncertainty of estimates — Damodaran, *Investment Valuation* (intro chapters on the nature of valuation); Investopedia \"Intrinsic Value.\"", "n": 414}, {"id": "QF-D6G1Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G1", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two analysts evaluate the same software company. Analyst A estimates a value of $40 using conservative growth and a 12% discount rate. Analyst B estimates $65 using stronger growth and a 9% discount rate. The shares trade at $50, and the investment committee asks which analyst has identified the \"true\" value.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The same market price can appear overvalued under one defensible model and undervalued under another.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$50 exceeds A's $40 estimate (overvalued to A) but is below B's $65 estimate (undervalued to B) — the same price reads both ways under defensible models.", "whyPool": "$50 exceeds Analyst A's estimate but is below Analyst B's estimate."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Analyst B's lower discount rate tends to increase estimated present value, all else equal.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A lower discount rate discounts future cash flows less heavily, raising estimated present value, all else equal.", "whyPool": "A lower discount rate reduces the amount by which future cash flows are discounted."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Analyst A's more conservative growth assumption tends to increase estimated value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Lower assumed growth reduces projected cash flows and thus lowers estimated value — it does not increase it.", "whyPool": "Lower assumed growth generally reduces projected cash flows and estimated value."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The disagreement cannot arise from uncertainty rather than an arithmetic error by either analyst.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: disagreement *can* arise from uncertain forecasts and judgment, not just arithmetic error.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Valuation requires uncertain forecasts and judgments on which informed analysts may disagree."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The $50 market price proves that both intrinsic-value estimates are incorrect.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Market price records where trading occurs; it does not reveal a uniquely correct intrinsic value, so it cannot prove both estimates wrong.", "whyPool": "Market price records where trading occurs; it does not reveal a uniquely correct intrinsic value."}], "reference": "Discount-rate/growth inputs drive value; analyst disagreement — CFA equity valuation curriculum (DCF inputs); Investopedia \"Discounted Cash Flow.\"", "n": 415}, {"id": "QF-D6G1Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G1", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A merger rumor pushes a company's shares from $30 to $44. A fundamental analyst's stand-alone valuation remains $34, while a completed acquisition could plausibly be worth $48. The fund must decide whether to trade before the rumor is confirmed.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The new $44 price can reflect the market's probability-weighted assessment of multiple outcomes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The $44 price can reflect a probability-weighted blend of deal ($48) and no-deal ($34) outcomes, not a single certain value.", "whyPool": "Prices can incorporate possible deal and no-deal outcomes rather than a single certain outcome."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Comparing $44 only with the $34 stand-alone estimate is sufficient to prove that the stock is mispriced.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Comparing $44 only to the $34 stand-alone estimate ignores the credible acquisition scenario and its probability — insufficient to prove mispricing.", "whyPool": "The acquisition scenario and its probability are relevant to value while the rumor remains credible."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the acquisition fails, the price could fall even though the $44 price was reasonable given information available earlier.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A reasonable ex ante price can still produce a loss when the uncertain adverse outcome (deal fails) occurs.", "whyPool": "A reasonable ex ante price can lead to a loss when an uncertain adverse outcome occurs."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A valuation-based trade should consider both payoff magnitude and the probability of each scenario.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Expected value depends on both scenario payoff magnitudes and their probabilities — a valuation trade must weigh both.", "whyPool": "Expected value depends on both scenario payoffs and their probabilities."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Observing the price increase reveals with certainty that informed investors value the completed acquisition at exactly $44.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The price increase does not uniquely identify investors' scenario valuations or probabilities with certainty.", "whyPool": "The price does not uniquely identify investors' scenario valuations or probability estimates. G2: Book Value and Asset-Based Valuation"}], "reference": "Probability-weighted (expected-value) valuation, merger arbitrage intuition — CFA curriculum (scenario/expected-value analysis); Investopedia \"Expected Value,\" \"Risk Arbitrage.\"", "n": 416}, {"id": "QF-D6G2Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G2", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A manufacturer reports $500 million in assets, $320 million in liabilities, and 30 million shares outstanding. Its shares trade at $9.00. The portfolio manager asks for a quick book-value assessment.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Reported shareholder equity is not $180 million. 350 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: equity = assets − liabilities = $500M − $320M = $180M, so equity *is* $180M.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Shareholder equity is assets minus liabilities: $500 million - $320 million = $180 million."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Reported book value per share is not $6.00.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: $180M ÷ 30M shares = $6.00, so book value per share *is* $6.00.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. $180 million divided by 30 million shares equals $6.00 per share."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The stock's price-to-book ratio is 1.5.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "P/B = $9.00 ÷ $6.00 = 1.5.", "whyPool": "Price-to-book is $9.00 / $6.00 = 1.5."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Trading above book value proves that the stock is overvalued.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Market value may rationally exceed book value due to earning power, growth, or unrecorded intangibles — trading above book does not prove overvaluation.", "whyPool": "Market value may rationally exceed book value because of earning power, growth, or unrecorded intangible assets."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A later $60 million asset write-down, with liabilities unchanged, would reduce book value per share to $4.00.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A $60M write-down cuts equity to $120M → $120M ÷ 30M = $4.00 per share.", "whyPool": "Equity would fall to $120 million, or $4.00 for each of 30 million shares."}], "reference": "Book value per share, price-to-book — Investopedia \"Book Value Per Share,\" \"Price-to-Book (P/B) Ratio\"; CFA financial-statement/asset-based valuation.", "n": 417}, {"id": "QF-D6G2Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G2", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A profitable consulting firm has few physical assets, substantial employee expertise, and a strong client network developed internally. Its shares trade at four times reported book value. A client concludes that the company must be severely overpriced.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Internally developed human capital and client relationships may be incompletely reflected on the balance sheet.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Internally developed human capital and client relationships are often not recognized as balance-sheet assets, so book value understates economic value.", "whyPool": "Many valuable internally developed intangible resources are not recognized as balance-sheet assets."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Book value is not based primarily on accounting measurements rather than a direct estimate of future earning power.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: book value *is* based on accounting measurement of recorded assets less liabilities, not a direct estimate of future earning power.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Accounting book value measures recorded assets less liabilities, not the present value of all future profits."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A high price-to-book ratio is conclusive evidence of mispricing.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A high P/B must be interpreted alongside profitability, asset composition, growth, and accounting choices — it is not conclusive evidence of mispricing.", "whyPool": "The ratio must be interpreted in light of profitability, asset composition, growth, and accounting choices. 351 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing price-to-book ratios is generally more informative when firms have similar business models and accounting practices.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "P/B comparisons are more informative when firms share similar business models and accounting conventions.", "whyPool": "Comparability improves when the economic activities and measurement conventions are similar."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If important productive assets are absent from reported book value, price-to-book can look high without the stock necessarily being overvalued.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A small accounting denominator (few recorded assets) can produce a high P/B even when market value is economically reasonable.", "whyPool": "A small accounting denominator can produce a high ratio even when market value is economically reasonable."}], "reference": "Limits of accounting book value, intangibles, comparability of multiples — Damodaran on relative valuation/multiples; Investopedia \"Price-to-Book Ratio,\" \"Intangible Asset.\"", "n": 418}, {"id": "QF-D6G2Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G2", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A distressed retailer reports inventory with a book value of $100 million and total book equity of $40 million. In a forced liquidation, the inventory may sell for only $55 million, and closing costs are expected to be material. A distressed-debt team evaluates the equity using reported book value.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Reported book value cannot overstate the amount available to shareholders in liquidation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: reported book value *can* overstate liquidation proceeds — distressed assets realize less than carrying value and liquidation itself has costs.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Distressed assets may realize less than their accounting carrying values, and liquidation itself creates costs."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Replacing the inventory's $100 million carrying amount with a $55 million recovery estimate reduces asset value by $45 million.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Marking inventory from $100M to $55M reduces asset value by $45M.", "whyPool": "The assumed shortfall is $100 million - $55 million = $45 million."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Before considering closing costs, that adjustment alone would not eliminate the reported $40 million of equity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: a $45M markdown *exceeds* the $40M equity cushion, so it does eliminate reported equity (before closing costs).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A $45 million reduction exceeds the reported $40 million equity cushion."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Creditors generally have claims ahead of common shareholders in liquidation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Creditors have claims senior to common shareholders in liquidation.", "whyPool": "Common equity is a residual claim after creditor obligations are satisfied."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because book equity is positive, common shareholders are guaranteed a positive liquidation recovery.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Positive book equity does not guarantee positive recovery — markdowns, senior claims, and liquidation costs can leave nothing for common shareholders.", "whyPool": "Asset markdowns, senior claims, and liquidation costs can leave nothing for common shareholders. G3: Time Value of Money"}], "reference": "Liquidation/distress value, priority of claims, book value limits — CFA (asset-based valuation, distressed); Investopedia \"Liquidation Value,\" \"Absolute Priority Rule.\"", "n": 419}, {"id": "QF-D6G3Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G3", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A pension fund can receive $100 million today or a guaranteed $121 million two years from now. Its opportunity cost is 10% annually, compounded annually. The investment committee asks whether the two choices are economically equivalent.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The present value of $121 million received in two years is $100 million at a 10% discount rate.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "PV of $121M in two years at 10% = $121M ÷ 1.102 = $100M.", "whyPool": "$121 million / 1.102 = $100 million."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The future value of $100 million invested for two years at 10% is $121 million. 352 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "FV of $100M for two years at 10% = $100M × 1.102 = $121M.", "whyPool": "$100 million × 1.102 = $121 million."}, {"L": "C", "text": "At the stated discount rate, the fund should strictly prefer the delayed payment because $121 million is numerically larger.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "At a 10% opportunity cost the two are equivalent; a larger nominal amount later is not strictly preferable once discounted.", "whyPool": "The two payments are equivalent at a 10% opportunity cost under the stated assumptions."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the appropriate discount rate were 12%, the present value of the delayed payment would not be less than $100 million.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: at 12%, PV = $121M ÷ 1.122 ≈ $96.46M, which *is* less than $100M.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Discounting by a higher rate produces $121 million / 1.122, which is about $96.46 million."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The discount rate reflects the return available on a comparable alternative and the relevant risk of the cash flow.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The discount rate reflects the return on a comparable alternative and the risk of the cash flow.", "whyPool": "Opportunity cost and risk are central to selecting an appropriate discount rate."}], "reference": "Present value / future value, discount-rate selection — Investopedia \"Time Value of Money,\" \"Present Value\"; CFA quantitative methods (TVM).", "n": 420}, {"id": "QF-D6G3Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G3", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An asset manager compares two risk-equivalent contracts. Contract A pays $50,000 at the end of year 1, and Contract B pays $50,000 at the end of year 4. The appropriate annual discount rate is positive.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Contract A has the higher present value.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The same payment discounted for fewer periods (year 1) has the higher present value.", "whyPool": "The same payment is discounted for fewer periods when received at the end of year 1."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The two contracts have equal present value because their nominal payments are equal.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Equal nominal payments have equal PV only at a zero rate; with a nonzero rate, timing changes PV.", "whyPool": "Timing affects present value whenever the discount rate is nonzero."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Increasing the discount rate widens the present-value disadvantage of the later payment.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The longer-dated payment is more sensitive to the discount rate, so a higher rate widens A's PV advantage.", "whyPool": "The longer-dated payment is more sensitive to a change in the discount rate."}, {"L": "D", "text": "At a zero discount rate, the two payments would not have equal present value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: at a zero discount rate both PVs equal their $50,000 nominal amounts (they *are* equal).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. With no discounting, both present values equal their $50,000 nominal amounts."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that receiving cash earlier creates flexibility to reinvest it before year 4.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The embedded claim (earlier cash allows reinvestment before year 4) is correct and should be accepted, so it is wrong to reject it.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. Earlier receipt allows the investor to earn a return during the intervening years. 353 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Discounting and timing sensitivity — Investopedia \"Time Value of Money,\" \"Present Value\"; CFA quantitative methods (TVM).", "n": 421}, {"id": "QF-D6G3Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G3", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A university endowment sets aside $100,000 today for a commitment due in five years. It expects the funds to earn 6% annually, compounded annually, and forecasts that the commitment will be $130,000.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The expected future value of the set-aside is approximately $133,823.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "FV = $100,000 × 1.065 ≈ $133,823.", "whyPool": "$100,000 × 1.065 is approximately $133,823."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Under the forecast, the set-aside is expected to cover the $130,000 commitment.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$133,823 exceeds the $130,000 commitment by ≈ $3,823, so the set-aside is expected to cover it.", "whyPool": "The expected future value exceeds the forecast commitment by about $3,823."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Simple interest of $6,000 per year is the correct calculation under annual compounding.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Annual compounding earns returns on prior returns, so growth exceeds simple interest of $6,000/year.", "whyPool": "Annual compounding earns returns on earlier returns, so growth is not limited to simple interest."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the realized return is lower than 6%, the ending value will be lower, all else equal.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A realized return below 6% produces a smaller compound future value, all else equal.", "whyPool": "A lower compound growth rate produces a smaller future value."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The present value of the $130,000 commitment at 6% is greater than $130,000.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "At a positive discount rate, the PV of $130,000 is *less* than $130,000, not greater.", "whyPool": "At a positive discount rate, its present value is less than its future nominal amount. G4: Discounted Cash Flow and Dividend Valuation"}], "reference": "Compound interest vs. simple interest, future/present value — Investopedia \"Compound Interest,\" \"Future Value\"; CFA quantitative methods (TVM).", "n": 422}, {"id": "QF-D6G4Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G4", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An analyst values a stable dividend-paying company using a dividend discount model. The chief financial officer argues that increasing the discount rate should increase the stock's estimated value because investors would be demanding a higher return.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A higher discount rate generally lowers the present value of future dividends.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A higher discount rate discounts future dividends more heavily, lowering present value (the CFO is wrong).", "whyPool": "Future cash flows are discounted more heavily."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A lower discount rate generally raises estimated intrinsic value.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A lower discount rate discounts future cash flows less, raising estimated intrinsic value.", "whyPool": "Lower discounting raises present value."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that the discount rate represents an opportunity cost or required return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The embedded claim (discount rate = opportunity cost / required return) is correct and should be accepted, so it is wrong to reject it.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. The discount rate reflects the return required for bearing risk or foregoing alternatives."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Holding dividends constant, intrinsic value is unaffected by the discount rate.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The discount rate is a key present-value input; holding dividends constant, value still changes with the rate.", "whyPool": "Discount rate is a key input in present-value calculations."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Small changes in discount-rate assumptions cannot materially affect valuation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: long-duration cash flows are *especially* sensitive to the discount rate, so small changes can materially move value.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Long-duration cash flows are especially sensitive to the discount rate."}], "reference": "Dividend discount model, discount-rate mechanics — Investopedia \"Dividend Discount Model,\" \"Discount Rate\"; CFA equity valuation (DDM).", "n": 423}, {"id": "QF-D6G4Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G4", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A utility is expected to pay a dividend of $3.00 one year from now, with dividends growing at 4% perpetually. An analyst uses a 9% required return and the constant-growth dividend discount model. The investment committee asks about the resulting valuation.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The model estimates value as $3.00 / (0.09 - 0.04) = $60.00 per share.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Gordon model: V = $3.00 ÷ (0.09 − 0.04) = $3.00 ÷ 0.05 = $60.00.", "whyPool": "The Gordon growth formula gives $3.00 / 0.05 = $60.00."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Raising the required return to 10%, while holding other assumptions fixed, would reduce estimated value to $50.00.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Raising r to 10% gives V = $3.00 ÷ (0.10 − 0.04) = $3.00 ÷ 0.06 = $50.00.", "whyPool": "At a 6% spread between return and growth, value is $3.00 / 0.06 = $50.00."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Raising perpetual growth to 5%, while holding the 9% required return fixed, would reduce estimated value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Raising g to 5% narrows the denominator (0.09 − 0.05 = 0.04), *raising* value to $75.00 — not reducing it.", "whyPool": "A higher growth rate narrows the denominator and raises the model value."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The model requires the perpetual growth rate to be below the required return for a finite positive value.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The model requires g < r; if g ≥ r the perpetuity formula yields no finite positive value.", "whyPool": "If growth equals or exceeds the discount rate, the standard perpetuity formula does not yield a finite positive value."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The $60.00 estimate is certain because the formula is mathematically precise.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The $60.00 output is highly dependent on uncertain dividend, growth, and required-return assumptions — not \"certain.\"", "whyPool": "The output is highly dependent on uncertain dividend, growth, and required-return assumptions."}], "reference": "Gordon growth model, g < r constraint — Investopedia \"Gordon Growth Model,\" \"Dividend Discount Model\"; CFA equity valuation (constant-growth DDM).", "n": 424}, {"id": "QF-D6G4Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G4", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A DCF model projects free cash flow of $10 million in year 1 and $12 million in year 2, plus a terminal value of $120 million at the end of year 2. The analyst discounts all cash flows at 10% annually.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The present value of year-1 cash flow is not approximately $9.09 million. 355 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: PV of year-1 FCF = $10M ÷ 1.10 ≈ $9.09M (it *is* ≈ $9.09M).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. $10 million / 1.10 is approximately $9.09 million."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The combined present value of year-2 cash flow and terminal value is not approximately $109.09 million.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: ($12M + $120M) ÷ 1.102 ≈ $109.09M (it *is* ≈ $109.09M).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. ($12 million + $120 million) / 1.102 is approximately $109.09 million."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The total enterprise value from these modeled cash flows is approximately $118.18 million.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Enterprise value ≈ $9.09M + $109.09M ≈ $118.18M.", "whyPool": "Adding $9.09 million and $109.09 million gives approximately $118.18 million."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The terminal value accounts for less than half of the total estimated present value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Terminal value alone contributes ≈ $99.17M ($120M ÷ 1.102) — well over half of the ≈ $118.18M total, not less than half.", "whyPool": "The terminal value alone contributes about $99.17 million in present value, well over half of the total."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because terminal value is a large portion of the estimate, its assumptions deserve careful sensitivity analysis.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A valuation dominated by terminal value is especially sensitive to long-run assumptions, warranting sensitivity analysis.", "whyPool": "A valuation dominated by terminal value is especially sensitive to long-run assumptions. G5: Growth-Based Valuation"}], "reference": "Multi-period DCF, terminal value, sensitivity — Damodaran on DCF/terminal value; Investopedia \"Discounted Cash Flow,\" \"Terminal Value\"; CFA equity valuation (FCFF/DCF).", "n": 425}, {"id": "QF-D6G5Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G5", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A company currently generates $5.0 million in annual free cash flow. A growth-oriented analyst projects 8% annual growth for the next three years, while the risk committee asks the team to verify the compounding logic.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Projected year-1 free cash flow is $5.40 million.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Year-1 FCF = $5.0M × 1.08 = $5.40M.", "whyPool": "$5.0 million × 1.08 = $5.40 million."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Projected year-3 free cash flow is approximately $6.30 million.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Year-3 FCF = $5.0M × 1.083 ≈ $6.30M.", "whyPool": "$5.0 million × 1.083 is approximately $6.30 million."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The three-year cumulative growth rate is exactly 24% because 8% × 3 = 24%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Compound three-year growth is ≈ 25.97% (1.083 − 1), not exactly 24% (8% × 3) — simple multiplication understates it.", "whyPool": "Compound growth is about 25.97%, not exactly 24%."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Compounding causes the year-3 forecast to depend on growth applied to earlier growth.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Each year's growth compounds on the prior year's enlarged base — growth applied to earlier growth.", "whyPool": "Each year's growth builds on the prior year's enlarged cash flow."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the starting cash-flow estimate is too high, each projected cash flow will not also be biased upward, all else equal.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: an overstated starting value scales the *entire* projected path, so each cash flow *is* biased upward.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The starting value scales the entire projected cash-flow path. 356 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Compound growth vs. simple growth, projection base sensitivity — Investopedia \"Compound Annual Growth Rate (CAGR),\" \"Compound Interest\"; CFA (growth forecasting).", "n": 426}, {"id": "QF-D6G5Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G5", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A mature company's next dividend is expected to be $2.00 per share. An analyst uses a 10% required return and initially assumes 4% perpetual growth, but a colleague proposes increasing perpetual growth to 5% based on one strong year.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "At 4% perpetual growth, the constant-growth model estimates value at approximately $33.33.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At 4% growth: V = $2.00 ÷ (0.10 − 0.04) ≈ $33.33.", "whyPool": "$2.00 / (0.10 - 0.04) is approximately $33.33."}, {"L": "B", "text": "At 5% perpetual growth, the same model estimates value at $40.00.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At 5% growth: V = $2.00 ÷ (0.10 − 0.05) = $40.00.", "whyPool": "$2.00 / (0.10 - 0.05) = $40.00."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A one-percentage-point change in perpetual growth increases estimated value by only 1%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The value rises ≈ 20% ($33.33 → $40.00), far more than 1% — sensitivity is nonlinear, not one-for-one.", "whyPool": "The modeled value rises about 20%, illustrating nonlinear sensitivity."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Long-run growth assumptions should not be economically sustainable, not extrapolated mechanically from a single strong year.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: long-run growth *should* be economically sustainable and *not* extrapolated mechanically from a single strong year.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A temporary result does not necessarily support an indefinitely sustainable growth rate."}, {"L": "E", "text": "As perpetual growth approaches the required return, the valuation becomes less sensitive to estimation error.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "As g approaches r the denominator shrinks, making valuation *more* sensitive and unstable — not less.", "whyPool": "The denominator becomes small, making valuation increasingly sensitive and unstable."}], "reference": "Gordon-model denominator sensitivity, sustainable growth — Investopedia \"Gordon Growth Model,\" \"Sustainable Growth Rate\"; CFA equity valuation (growth assumptions).", "n": 427}, {"id": "QF-D6G5Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G5", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio manager compares a rapidly expanding technology company trading at a high valuation multiple with a slow- growing industrial company trading at a low multiple. A client labels the first stock \"growth\" and the second \"value\" and assumes the labels determine future returns.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A growth stock's valuation generally depends heavily on expectations of future expansion.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A growth stock's valuation depends heavily on expectations of future expansion (much value sits in far-future cash flows).", "whyPool": "A large share of value may come from cash flows expected well into the future."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A value stock cannot still be overvalued if its apparent cheapness reflects deteriorating fundamentals inadequately captured by the analyst.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: a value stock *can* still be overvalued if a low multiple reflects deteriorating fundamentals (a value trap), not mispricing.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A low multiple can reflect genuine economic impairment rather than mispricing."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A growth company cannot be undervalued because it trades at a high multiple.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A high multiple alone does not determine price relative to intrinsic value — a growth stock *can* be undervalued.", "whyPool": "Price relative to a simple current metric does not by itself determine price relative to intrinsic value."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If expected growth fails to materialize, the growth stock's valuation may fall substantially.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If expected growth fails to materialize, both forecast cash flows and the multiple can fall, cutting the growth stock's valuation substantially.", "whyPool": "Disappointing growth can reduce both forecast cash flows and the multiple investors are willing to pay."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Growth and value characteristics are descriptive inputs to analysis, not guarantees of investment performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Growth/value are descriptive inputs, not performance guarantees — either can outperform depending on price, realized fundamentals, and expectations.", "whyPool": "Either type can outperform or underperform depending on price, realized fundamentals, and changing expectations. G6: Margin of Safety and Scenario Analysis"}], "reference": "Growth vs. value characteristics, value traps — Investopedia \"Growth Stock,\" \"Value Stock,\" \"Value Trap\"; CFA equity (style classification).", "n": 428}, {"id": "QF-D6G6Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G6", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A research team estimates bear, base, and bull values of $40, $60, and $90 for a stock, with probabilities of 25%, 50%, and 25%, respectively. The shares trade at $50. The portfolio manager asks whether the scenario analysis supports a purchase.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The probability-weighted estimated value is not $62.50.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: 0.25($40) + 0.50($60) + 0.25($90) = $62.50 (it *is* $62.50).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 0.25($40) + 0.50($60) + 0.25($90) = $62.50."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The market price is 20% below the probability-weighted estimate when the discount is measured relative to estimated value.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "($62.50 − $50) ÷ $62.50 = 20% below the probability-weighted estimate.", "whyPool": "($62.50 - $50) / $62.50 = 20%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that the bear case implies a 20% loss from the current market price.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The embedded claim (a fall from $50 to $40 is a 20% loss) is correct and should be accepted, so it is wrong to reject it.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. A decline from $50 to $40 is a 20% loss."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The probability-weighted value guarantees a positive realized return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "One outcome will actually occur, probabilities may be wrong, and price may not converge — the weighted value does not guarantee a positive return.", "whyPool": "One outcome will occur, probabilities may be wrong, and price may not converge to modeled value."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The scenario framework makes the consequences of differing outcomes more visible than a single point estimate alone.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A scenario framework exposes downside, central, and upside assumptions rather than hiding them in a single point estimate.", "whyPool": "Multiple scenarios expose downside, central, and upside assumptions rather than hiding them in one number."}], "reference": "Expected value, scenario analysis, valuation ranges — CFA (scenario/sensitivity analysis); Investopedia \"Expected Value,\" \"Scenario Analysis.\"", "n": 429}, {"id": "QF-D6G6Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G6", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An analyst's model produces an intrinsic value of $73.42 per share. The chief risk officer asks the analyst to present a reasonable range because revenue growth, margins, and the discount rate are uncertain. 358 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Reporting $73.42 without sensitivity analysis can imply more precision than the inputs justify.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Reporting $73.42 without sensitivity analysis implies more precision than uncertain inputs justify (false precision).", "whyPool": "Precise arithmetic does not remove uncertainty in forecasts and model choice."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A valuation range can show how plausible combinations of assumptions affect the conclusion.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A valuation range shows how plausible combinations of assumptions affect the conclusion.", "whyPool": "Ranges connect the valuation conclusion to alternative assumptions."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A bear case should use assumptions at least as optimistic as the bull case.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A bear case uses *less* favorable assumptions than the base and bull cases — not assumptions at least as optimistic as the bull case.", "whyPool": "A bear case normally represents less favorable assumptions than the base and bull cases."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If every plausible scenario produces a value below the market price, the analysis does not support an undervaluation thesis.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If every plausible scenario yields a value below market price, the analysis does not support an undervaluation thesis.", "whyPool": "Values below price across the stated range are inconsistent with a claim that price is below modeled value."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Scenario analysis eliminates model risk by considering more than one outcome.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Scenario analysis illuminates model risk but cannot *eliminate* it — omitted variables, structural errors, and poor probability judgments remain.", "whyPool": "Scenarios illuminate model risk but cannot eliminate omitted variables, structural errors, or poor probability judgments."}], "reference": "Valuation ranges, avoiding false precision, model risk — Damodaran on uncertainty in valuation; Investopedia \"Sensitivity Analysis,\" \"Scenario Analysis.\"", "n": 430}, {"id": "QF-D6G6Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G6", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A value fund requires a 25% margin of safety relative to its conservative intrinsic-value estimate. An analyst values a company at $80 under conservative assumptions, and its stock trades at $62. The investment committee asks whether it clears the rule.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A 25% discount to the $80 estimate corresponds to a maximum purchase price of $60.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A 25% discount to $80 = $80 × (1 − 0.25) = $60 maximum purchase price.", "whyPool": "$80 × (1 - 0.25) = $60."}, {"L": "B", "text": "At $62, the stock trades 22.5% below the $80 estimate.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "($80 − $62) ÷ $80 = 22.5% below the estimate.", "whyPool": "($80 - $62) / $80 = 22.5%. 359 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "The stock satisfies the fund's stated 25% margin-of-safety rule at $62.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The 22.5% discount is *smaller* than the required 25%, so the stock does not satisfy the rule at $62.", "whyPool": "The 22.5% discount is smaller than the required 25% discount."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the conservative estimate were revised down to $72, the permitted purchase price under the rule would not fall to $54.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: a revised $72 estimate gives a permitted price of $72 × 0.75 = $54 (it *does* fall to $54).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. $72 × 0.75 = $54."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that a margin of safety can reduce exposure to estimation error but cannot guarantee against loss.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The embedded claim (a margin of safety reduces exposure to estimation error but cannot guarantee against loss) is correct and should be accepted.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. The estimate can still be wrong, adverse events can occur, and convergence is uncertain. G7: Valuation-Based Long and Short Decisions"}], "reference": "Margin of safety — Graham, *The Intelligent Investor* (margin-of-safety principle); Investopedia \"Margin of Safety.\"", "n": 431}, {"id": "QF-D6G7Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G7", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund estimates that a stock worth $80 trades at $60. Its bear-case value is $45, and management may announce a strategic review within six months. The portfolio manager considers a long position.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Convergence from $60 to the $80 estimate would not produce a 33.3% price gain.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: ($80 − $60) ÷ $60 ≈ 33.3% (convergence *would* produce ≈ 33.3% gain).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. ($80 - $60) / $60 is approximately 33.3%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A decline from $60 to the $45 bear case would produce a 25% price loss.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "($60 − $45) ÷ $60 = 25% loss to the bear case.", "whyPool": "($60 - $45) / $60 = 25%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The possible strategic review could serve as a catalyst, but its occurrence and effect are uncertain.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A strategic review may act as a catalyst, but its occurrence and price effect are uncertain.", "whyPool": "A catalyst may prompt reassessment, but it is neither certain to occur nor certain to raise price."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Buying below estimated value removes timing risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Buying below estimated value does not remove timing risk — price can stay below value or fall further for a long time.", "whyPool": "Price can remain below estimated value or fall further for an extended period."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Position sizing should reflect downside scenarios as well as the estimated upside.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Position sizing should reflect the full payoff distribution (downside scenarios), not just the central-case upside.", "whyPool": "The payoff distribution, not only the central valuation estimate, should inform risk taken."}], "reference": "Catalyst/timing risk, position sizing from payoff distribution — CFA (portfolio construction, risk); Investopedia \"Catalyst,\" \"Margin of Safety,\" \"Risk Management.\"", "n": 432}, {"id": "QF-D6G7Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G7", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A short seller believes a stock trading at $120 is worth $90. The position has no near-term catalyst, borrow costs are high, and enthusiasm for the stock is increasing. The risk manager reviews the proposed trade.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "If the price falls from $120 to $90, the short earns $30 per share before borrowing and other costs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Selling at $120 and repurchasing at $90 yields a $30 gross gain per share before costs.", "whyPool": "Selling at $120 and repurchasing at $90 produces a $30 gross gain per share."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that that convergence represents a 25% gross return relative to the initial $120 short-sale proceeds.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The embedded claim ($30 ÷ $120 = 25% gross return before financing/borrow fees) is correct and should be accepted.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. $30 / $120 = 25%, before financing, borrow fees, and other costs."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that if the price instead rises to $150, the short loses $30 per share before costs. 360 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The embedded claim (repurchasing at $150 after selling at $120 = $30 loss per share before costs) is correct and should be accepted.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. Repurchasing at $150 after selling at $120 creates a $30 loss per share."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The maximum possible loss on an unhedged short is limited to the initial $120 share price.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A stock's price can rise without bound, so an uncovered short has theoretically *unlimited* loss — not capped at the $120 share price.", "whyPool": "A stock's price can theoretically rise without bound, so an uncovered short has theoretically unlimited loss."}, {"L": "E", "text": "High borrow costs and uncertain timing can make the trade unsuccessful even if the $90 valuation is ultimately correct.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "High borrow costs and uncertain timing can make the trade unsuccessful even if the $90 valuation is ultimately correct.", "whyPool": "Carrying costs and an adverse price path can overwhelm eventual convergence."}], "reference": "Short-selling mechanics, unlimited loss, carrying costs — Investopedia \"Short Selling,\" \"Short Squeeze,\" \"Cost of Carry\"; CFA (short positions, financing).", "n": 433}, {"id": "QF-D6G7Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "06", "domainName": "Valuation & Mispricing", "group": "G7", "weeks": [6], "weekMax": 6, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A hedge fund shorts an apparently overvalued company at $50 with an estimated value of $30. Over the next year, strong momentum drives the shares to $85, triggering the fund's risk limit and forcing it to cover. Two years later, the company disappoints and the price falls to $28. The investment committee evaluates the original thesis.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The later decline is evidence that the valuation concern may have had merit.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The eventual decline to $28 is consistent with (though not alone proof of) the original overvaluation thesis.", "whyPool": "The eventual outcome is consistent with, though it does not alone prove, the original overvaluation thesis."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The fund's realized trade was profitable because the eventual price fell below $30.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The fund exited at $85 and did not participate in the later decline, so the realized trade was a loss, not a profit.", "whyPool": "The fund exited at $85 and therefore did not participate in the later decline."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Covering at $85 after shorting at $50 creates a $35 loss per share before costs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Shorting at $50 and covering at $85 is a $35 loss per share before costs.", "whyPool": "The short sold at $50 and bought back at $85, losing $35 per share before costs."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A correct long-run valuation thesis is sufficient to ensure a successful trade regardless of path and horizon.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A correct long-run thesis is not sufficient — adverse interim moves can force liquidation or exhaust capital before convergence.", "whyPool": "Adverse interim moves can force liquidation or exhaust capital before convergence."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The case illustrates that financing, risk limits, catalysts, and timing are part of translating valuation into a trade.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Financing, risk limits, catalysts, and timing all matter in translating a valuation view into a successful trade; value and trade viability are distinct.", "whyPool": "Investment value and trade viability are related but distinct questions. 361 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Value vs. trade outcome, forced liquidation, \"markets can stay irrational longer than you can stay solvent\" — CFA (risk limits, drawdown); Investopedia \"Short Squeeze,\" \"Margin Call,\" \"Risk Management.\"", "n": 434}, {"id": "QF-D7G1Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G1", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An analyst models a stock's monthly return as stock return = 0.2% + 1.3 × market return + residual return. During one month, the market returns 2.0%, and the stock's residual return is -0.5%.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The market-related component of the stock's return is not 2.6%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. The market-related component is `β·r_market = 1.3 × 2.0% = 2.6%`, so \"not 2.6%\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The market-related component is 1.3 × 2.0% = 2.6%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The model-implied total stock return for the month is not 2.3%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Total = intercept + market component + residual = `0.2% + 2.6% − 0.5% = 2.3%`, so \"not 2.3%\" is false.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The components sum to 0.2% + 2.6% - 0.5% = 2.3%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The negative residual means the stock must have had a negative total return.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A negative residual does not force a negative total return — here the positive intercept and positive market component outweigh the −0.5% residual (total = +2.3%).", "whyPool": "A negative residual can coexist with a positive total return when the other components are positive."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The 0.2% intercept is the portion predicted when the market return is zero and the residual is zero.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "In `r = α + β·r_market + ε`, the intercept (0.2%) is exactly the predicted return when both the market return and the residual are zero.", "whyPool": "In this linear specification, the intercept is the predicted return when both the market return and residual are zero."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The residual represents the part of this month's return not explained by the intercept and modeled market exposure.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The residual is the asset-specific piece left unexplained after applying the fitted intercept and modeled market exposure (actual − predicted).", "whyPool": "The residual is the unexplained asset-specific portion remaining after applying the fitted model."}], "reference": "Single-factor market model / return decomposition — Bodie-Kane-Marcus *Investments*, \"The Index Model\" chapter; Investopedia \"Systematic vs. Unsystematic Risk.\"", "n": 435}, {"id": "QF-D7G1Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G1", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A manager regresses a fund's monthly excess returns on the excess returns of a broad equity index. The estimated intercept is 0.15% per month, beta is 0.8, and the residuals vary substantially from month to month.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The estimated beta is the slope relating the fund's excess return to the benchmark's excess return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Beta is the fitted regression slope relating the fund's excess return to the benchmark's excess return.", "whyPool": "Beta is the fitted regression slope on the benchmark's excess return."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A 1% increase in the benchmark's excess return is associated with an estimated 0.8% increase in the fund's excess return, before considering the residual.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A beta of 0.8 implies a ~0.8-for-1 expected response: a 1% benchmark excess return maps to ~0.8% fund excess return before the residual.", "whyPool": "A beta of 0.8 implies an estimated 0.8-for-1 response in excess returns."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The intercept proves that the manager will earn exactly 0.15% of alpha every future month.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "An in-sample intercept is an *estimate* of average alpha, not a guarantee of exactly 0.15% every future month.", "whyPool": "An in-sample intercept is an estimate, not a guaranteed future monthly return."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Large residual variation indicates that the benchmark does not explain all of the fund's return variation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Large residual variation means the benchmark leaves much of the fund's return variation unexplained (idiosyncratic component).", "whyPool": "Residuals capture variation left unexplained by the modeled benchmark exposure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Replacing the broad index with a materially different benchmark could not change the estimated alpha and beta.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Alpha and beta are measured *relative to the chosen benchmark/model*; a materially different benchmark can change both estimates. \"Could not change\" reverses this.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Alpha and beta are measured relative to the selected model and benchmark."}], "reference": "Index-model regression, alpha/beta estimation — Bodie-Kane-Marcus *Investments*, index model & Security Characteristic Line; Investopedia \"Jensen's Measure (Alpha).\"", "n": 436}, {"id": "QF-D7G1Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G1", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A stock has an estimated beta of 1.2 and a zero intercept in a simple market model. On a day when the market falls 4%, the stock falls only 2%.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The model predicts a market-related stock return of -4.8% for the day.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With a zero intercept, predicted market-related return = `1.2 × −4% = −4.8%`.", "whyPool": "With a zero intercept, the prediction is 1.2 × -4% = -4.8%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The stock's realized residual return is not +2.8% for the day.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Residual = actual − predicted = `−2.0% − (−4.8%) = +2.8%`, so \"not +2.8%\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The residual is actual minus predicted return: -2.0% - (-4.8%) = +2.8%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Because the residual is positive, the stock's total return must also be positive.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A positive residual does not force a positive total return — realized total was −2% despite outperforming the −4.8% prediction.", "whyPool": "The stock's realized total return was -2%, despite its positive residual."}, {"L": "D", "text": "One positive residual observation is sufficient to establish persistent positive alpha.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "One positive residual is not evidence of persistent alpha; that requires many observations and accounting for estimation uncertainty.", "whyPool": "Persistent alpha requires evidence across many observations and must account for estimation uncertainty."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The stock outperformed the return predicted by its market exposure even though it lost money in absolute terms.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Losing 2% beats the modeled −4.8%, so benchmark-adjusted (residual) performance was positive even though the stock lost money.", "whyPool": "Losing 2% is better than the modeled loss of 4.8%, so benchmark-adjusted performance was positive. G2: Beta and Market Sensitivity"}], "reference": "Residual return = actual − model-predicted; benchmark-relative performance — Grinold & Kahn *Active Portfolio Management* (residual return/risk); Bodie-Kane-Marcus index model.", "n": 437}, {"id": "QF-D7G2Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G2", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A cyclical stock has an estimated beta of 1.5. An investor interprets this estimate using a simple market model with no alpha and no residual return.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "If the market rises 4%, the modeled stock return is approximately 6%. 365 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Modeled response to a +4% market = `1.5 × 4% = 6%`.", "whyPool": "The modeled response is 1.5 × 4% = 6%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "If the market falls 4%, the modeled stock return is approximately -6%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The same linear exposure gives `1.5 × −4% = −6%` when the market falls 4%.", "whyPool": "The same linear exposure implies 1.5 × -4% = -6%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A beta of 1.5 guarantees the stock will move by exactly 1.5 times the market every day.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Beta is an *average* estimated relationship; realized returns also contain residual variation, so it cannot \"guarantee exactly 1.5× every day.\"", "whyPool": "Beta is an estimated average relationship; realized returns also contain residual variation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The beta indicates greater modeled market sensitivity than a stock with a beta of 0.7.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A larger beta (1.5 vs. 0.7) means greater modeled sensitivity to the benchmark.", "whyPool": "A larger positive beta represents greater sensitivity to movements in the modeled market benchmark."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The beta estimate by itself demonstrates superior stock-selection skill.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Beta measures market exposure, not stock-selection skill or abnormal (alpha) performance.", "whyPool": "Beta measures exposure, not skill or abnormal performance."}], "reference": "Beta as SCL slope / market sensitivity — Sharpe CAPM; Investopedia \"Beta.\"", "n": 438}, {"id": "QF-D7G2Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G2", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An asset has an estimated beta of -0.4 relative to the equity market. A portfolio manager considers adding it to a long- only equity portfolio.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The asset's modeled market-related return is -2% when the market returns +5%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Modeled market component = `−0.4 × 5% = −2%` when the market returns +5%.", "whyPool": "The modeled market component is -0.4 × 5% = -2%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The asset tends to move opposite the market component on average under the fitted model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A negative slope means the asset moves opposite the market component on average under the fitted model.", "whyPool": "A negative slope represents an inverse modeled relationship with the benchmark."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A negative beta guarantees a positive realized return whenever the market declines.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negative beta does not *guarantee* a positive realized return when the market declines — alpha, residuals, and estimation error can override it.", "whyPool": "Alpha, residual returns, and estimation error can prevent the realized return from being positive."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A negative-beta asset cannot provide hedging value in an otherwise positive-beta portfolio.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negative market exposure *can* offset a portfolio's positive market exposure — it provides hedging value, so \"cannot provide hedging value\" reverses the truth.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Negative market exposure can offset part of a portfolio's positive market exposure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Negative beta means that the asset has negative volatility.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Volatility is nonnegative; beta is negative because *covariance* with the market is negative, not because volatility is \"negative.\"", "whyPool": "Volatility is nonnegative; beta can be negative because covariance with the market is negative. 366 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Negative beta / negative covariance, hedging — Investopedia \"Negative Beta\"; Bodie-Kane-Marcus (covariance and beta).", "n": 439}, {"id": "QF-D7G2Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G2", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Using historical returns, an analyst estimates that a stock's covariance with the market is 0.018 and the market-return variance is 0.012 over the same horizon.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The estimated beta is 1.5.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "`β = Cov/Var = 0.018 / 0.012 = 1.5`.", "whyPool": "Beta is 0.018 / 0.012 = 1.5."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Beta is calculated as the asset-market covariance divided by the market variance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Beta is defined as asset-market covariance divided by market variance — the single-factor regression slope.", "whyPool": "This covariance-to-market-variance ratio is the regression slope for the single-factor market model."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If the covariance were zero while market variance remained positive, the estimated beta would not be zero.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Zero covariance ⇒ zero numerator ⇒ **β = 0**, so \"would not be zero\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Zero covariance produces a zero numerator and therefore a zero beta."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A zero beta would prove that the stock has no idiosyncratic risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A zero beta means no market sensitivity but does not eliminate idiosyncratic (asset-specific) risk.", "whyPool": "Zero market sensitivity does not eliminate asset-specific return variation."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Changing the return horizon or sample period can change the estimated beta.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Beta is an empirical estimate and can vary across frequencies, samples, and market regimes.", "whyPool": "Beta is an empirical estimate and may vary across frequencies, samples, and market regimes. G3: Alpha and Active Performance"}], "reference": "`β = Cov(i,m)/Var(m)` — Sharpe CAPM; Investopedia \"How to Calculate Beta.\"", "n": 440}, {"id": "QF-D7G3Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G3", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Over one year, a fund returns 14%, the market returns 10%, and the risk-free rate is 2%. The fund's estimated beta is 1.2. For this question, use the CAPM expected return risk-free rate + beta × (market return - risk-free rate).", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The market's excess return is not 8%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Market excess return = `10% − 2% = 8%`, so \"not 8%\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The market excess return is 10% - 2% = 8%. 367 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "The fund's CAPM-implied return is 11.6%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "CAPM-implied return = `2% + 1.2 × 8% = 11.6%`.", "whyPool": "The CAPM-implied return is 2% + 1.2 × 8% = 11.6%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The fund's realized alpha relative to this CAPM calculation is 2.4%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Realized alpha = `14% − 11.6% = 2.4%` (return above CAPM expectation).", "whyPool": "The realized return exceeds the CAPM-implied return by 14% - 11.6% = 2.4%."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Comparing the fund's 14% return only with the market's 10% return fully adjusts for the fund's market risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "With beta 1.2 the fund carries more market risk than the index; comparing 14% to 10% alone does **not** risk-adjust.", "whyPool": "The fund has beta above one, so a raw comparison does not fully adjust for its greater market exposure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Positive measured alpha for one year guarantees that the fund has persistent manager skill.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "One year of positive alpha may be luck, residual risk, or estimation error — it does not guarantee persistent skill.", "whyPool": "A single positive observation may reflect luck, residual risk, or estimation error rather than persistent skill."}], "reference": "CAPM / SML expected return and Jensen's alpha — Sharpe CAPM; Investopedia \"Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM)\" and \"Jensen's Measure.\"", "n": 441}, {"id": "QF-D7G3Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G3", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A strategy's estimated annual alpha is 1.0%, with a standard error of 0.8%. Implementing the strategy is expected to cost 1.3% per year. A researcher argues that the positive alpha estimate is enough to justify deployment.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The estimated alpha is positive before implementation costs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Reported gross alpha estimate is +1.0% (before costs).", "whyPool": "The reported gross alpha estimate is +1.0%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The alpha estimate is only 1.25 standard errors above zero.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Estimate-to-standard-error ratio = `1.0 / 0.8 = 1.25` — only 1.25 SEs above zero.", "whyPool": "The estimate-to-standard-error ratio is 1.0 / 0.8 = 1.25."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The estimate is necessarily statistically significant at conventional two-sided levels because it is positive.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A positive point estimate alone does not establish conventional two-sided statistical significance (1.25 SEs is short of ~1.96).", "whyPool": "A positive point estimate alone does not establish conventional statistical significance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Subtracting the estimated costs produces an expected net alpha of -0.3%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Expected net alpha = `1.0% − 1.3% = −0.3%` after costs.", "whyPool": "Expected net alpha is 1.0% - 1.3% = -0.3%."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Statistical significance and economic significance are not distinct considerations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Statistical and economic significance are *distinct*: an effect can be detectable yet uneconomic after costs, or large yet imprecise. \"Not distinct\" reverses this.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. An effect can be statistically detectable yet too small after costs, or economically large but estimated imprecisely."}], "reference": "Statistical vs. economic significance, net-of-cost alpha — Grinold & Kahn (information ratio, t-stat of alpha); CFA curriculum (hypothesis testing / performance evaluation).", "n": 442}, {"id": "QF-D7G3Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G3", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "During a strong bull market, the risk-free rate is 2% and the market returns 12%. A fund with beta 1.5 also returns 17%. Use the CAPM relationship for this simplified evaluation.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The market risk premium realized over the period is 10%.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Realized market risk premium = `12% − 2% = 10%`.", "whyPool": "The realized market excess return is 12% - 2% = 10%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The fund's CAPM-implied return is not 17%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. CAPM-implied return = `2% + 1.5 × 10% = 17%`, which equals the realized 17%, so \"not 17%\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The CAPM-implied return is 2% + 1.5 × 10% = 17%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The fund's estimated alpha for the period is not approximately zero.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Realized equals CAPM-implied, so alpha *is* ≈ 0; \"not approximately zero\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Realized and CAPM-implied returns are equal in this simplified calculation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The fund's 5 percentage-point outperformance of the market necessarily represents manager alpha.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The 5-point outperformance is fully explained by beta 1.5 under CAPM — it is not necessarily manager alpha.", "whyPool": "The higher return is explained by the fund's beta of 1.5 under the stated assumptions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "High raw returns can arise from high market exposure without positive alpha. 368 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "High raw returns can come from high systematic exposure amplifying a bull market, without any abnormal (alpha) performance.", "whyPool": "Greater systematic exposure can amplify bull-market returns without producing abnormal performance. G4: Correlation vs. Beta"}], "reference": "Beta-driven returns vs. alpha, SML — Sharpe CAPM; Investopedia \"Alpha\" and \"CAPM.\"", "n": 443}, {"id": "QF-D7G4Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G4", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A low-volatility stock has a 0.90 correlation with the market. The stock's volatility is 8%, while market volatility is 16%, measured over the same horizon. Use beta = correlation × stock volatility / market volatility.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The stock's estimated beta is 0.45.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "`β = 0.90 × 8% / 16% = 0.45`.", "whyPool": "Beta is 0.90 × 8% / 16% = 0.45."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The stock can have high correlation with the market while having beta below one.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "High correlation can pair with beta below one when the stock is less volatile than the market.", "whyPool": "Strong directional co-movement can occur with smaller-magnitude movements in the stock."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Correlation describes standardized co-movement, while beta also reflects relative volatility.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Correlation is standardized co-movement; beta additionally reflects the asset-to-market volatility ratio.", "whyPool": "Beta combines correlation with the ratio of asset volatility to market volatility."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the market rises 2%, the beta-based market component of the stock's return is approximately 1.8%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Beta-based market component = `0.45 × 2% = 0.9%`, not 1.8% — the choice ignores the low beta.", "whyPool": "The beta-based component is 0.45 × 2% = 0.9%."}, {"L": "E", "text": "High positive correlation necessarily implies beta above one.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "High positive correlation does not imply beta above one; a low volatility ratio can keep beta below one.", "whyPool": "Beta may remain below one when the asset is less volatile than the market."}], "reference": "`β = ρ·(σ_i/σ_m)`, correlation vs. beta — CFA curriculum (correlation, beta); Investopedia \"Beta\" and \"Correlation.\"", "n": 444}, {"id": "QF-D7G4Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G4", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A speculative asset has 30% volatility, the market has 15% volatility, and their return correlation is 0.20 over the same sample. 369 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The asset's estimated beta is 0.40.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "`β = 0.20 × 30% / 15% = 0.40`.", "whyPool": "Beta is 0.20 × 30% / 15% = 0.40."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The asset's high volatility requires it to have a beta above one.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "High total volatility does not force beta above one — low correlation drives beta down to 0.40 here.", "whyPool": "Low correlation can produce a beta below one despite high total volatility."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Much of the asset's volatility may be unrelated to contemporaneous market movement.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Low correlation means market co-movement explains only a limited share of the asset's movement; most is idiosyncratic.", "whyPool": "Low correlation indicates that market co-movement explains only a limited portion of the asset's movement."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Correlation and beta are interchangeable because both are unitless.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Correlation and beta are not interchangeable: correlation is standardized co-movement, beta is the magnitude of market sensitivity.", "whyPool": "Correlation measures standardized co-movement; beta measures the magnitude of market sensitivity."}, {"L": "E", "text": "An investor concerned with total risk should not consider more than beta alone.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "An investor concerned with *total* risk must look beyond beta — substantial idiosyncratic volatility remains even when beta is modest.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Idiosyncratic volatility can be substantial even when beta is modest."}], "reference": "Idiosyncratic vs. systematic volatility, correlation ≠ beta — Bodie-Kane-Marcus (total vs. systematic risk); Investopedia \"Unsystematic Risk.\"", "n": 445}, {"id": "QF-D7G4Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G4", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An asset has 12% volatility, the market has 8% volatility, and their correlation is -0.50. A manager is comparing correlation and beta as hedge diagnostics.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The asset's estimated beta is not -0.75.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. `β = −0.50 × 12% / 8% = −0.75`, so \"not −0.75\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Beta is -0.50 × 12% / 8% = -0.75."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The negative correlation indicates that the asset tends to move opposite the market over the sample.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Negative correlation means the asset moves opposite the market on average over the sample.", "whyPool": "Negative correlation represents inverse directional co-movement on average."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The beta's magnitude depends partly on the asset's volatility relative to market volatility.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Beta scales correlation by the asset-to-market volatility ratio, so its magnitude depends partly on relative volatility.", "whyPool": "Beta scales correlation by the asset-to-market volatility ratio."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A two-to-one leveraged position in the asset would have the same beta contribution as an unleveraged position of the same equity amount.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Ignoring financing, a 2× leveraged position roughly *doubles* the beta contribution relative to equity — it is not the same as an unleveraged position of equal equity.", "whyPool": "Ignoring financing details, doubling exposure approximately doubles the position's beta contribution relative to the investor's equity. 370 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Neither the sample correlation nor the sample beta guarantees the same relationship in a future crisis.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Both correlation and beta are historical estimates and can change in a future crisis (regime dependence).", "whyPool": "Both measures are estimated from historical data and can change across regimes. G5: Portfolio Beta and Exposure Aggregation"}], "reference": "Negative beta, leverage and beta contribution, regime instability — Grinold & Kahn (beta/exposure); Investopedia \"Beta\" and \"Leverage.\"", "n": 446}, {"id": "QF-D7G5Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G5", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fully invested long-only portfolio places 60% in Stock A with beta 1.4 and 40% in Stock B with beta 0.5.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Stock A contributes 0.84 to portfolio beta.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stock A's contribution = `0.60 × 1.4 = 0.84`.", "whyPool": "Stock A's contribution is 0.60 × 1.4 = 0.84."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Stock B contributes 0.20 to portfolio beta.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stock B's contribution = `0.40 × 0.5 = 0.20`.", "whyPool": "Stock B's contribution is 0.40 × 0.5 = 0.20."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The portfolio beta is not 1.04.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Portfolio beta = `0.84 + 0.20 = 1.04`, so \"not 1.04\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Portfolio beta is the sum of contributions: 0.84 + 0.20 = 1.04."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The portfolio's beta must not lie between 0.5 and 1.4 under the stated positive weights.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. A positive-weighted average must lie within the component-beta range [0.5, 1.4]; \"must not lie between\" reverses this.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A positive-weighted average lies within the range of the component betas."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A portfolio beta of 1.04 means the portfolio will earn exactly 4% more than the market each year.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Beta is market sensitivity, not a guaranteed return premium — a 1.04 beta does not mean \"4% more than the market each year.\"", "whyPool": "Beta is market sensitivity, not a guaranteed return premium over the market."}], "reference": "Portfolio beta = weighted average of component betas — Bodie-Kane-Marcus (portfolio beta); Investopedia \"Portfolio Beta.\"", "n": 447}, {"id": "QF-D7G5Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G5", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A leveraged portfolio has weights of +120% in a stock basket with beta 1.1, -40% in another basket with beta 0.8, and +20% in cash with beta zero. Assume the weights are measured relative to portfolio equity.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The long basket contributes 1.32 to portfolio beta.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Long-basket contribution = `1.20 × 1.1 = 1.32`.", "whyPool": "The contribution is 1.20 × 1.1 = 1.32."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The short basket contributes -0.32 to portfolio beta.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Short-basket contribution = `−0.40 × 0.8 = −0.32`.", "whyPool": "The short contribution is -0.40 × 0.8 = -0.32."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The portfolio beta is 1.00.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Cash adds 0, so portfolio beta = `1.32 − 0.32 = 1.00`.", "whyPool": "Cash contributes zero, so total beta is 1.32 - 0.32 = 1.00."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The portfolio's gross risky-asset exposure is not 160% of equity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Gross risky exposure = `120% + 40% = 160%` of equity, so \"not 160%\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Gross risky exposure is 120% + 40% = 160% of equity."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because the signed weights sum to 100%, the portfolio is unleveraged.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Signed weights (incl. cash) summing to 100% does not mean unleveraged — the 160% gross risky exposure reveals leverage.", "whyPool": "Gross exposure reveals leverage even though signed weights, including cash, sum to 100%."}], "reference": "Gross vs. net exposure, leverage, beta aggregation — Grinold & Kahn (portfolio exposures); Investopedia \"Gross Exposure.\"", "n": 448}, {"id": "QF-D7G5Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G5", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund with $50 million of equity is long $80 million of stocks with an aggregate beta of 1.25, short $30 million of low-beta stocks with beta 0.5, and short $20 million of a market ETF with beta 1.0.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The long position contributes +$100 million of beta-weighted exposure.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Long beta-weighted exposure = `$80M × 1.25 = $100M`.", "whyPool": "The long beta-weighted exposure is $80 million × 1.25 = $100 million."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The two short positions together contribute -$35 million of beta-weighted exposure.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Shorts contribute `−($30M × 0.5) − ($20M × 1.0) = −$15M − $20M = −$35M`.", "whyPool": "The shorts contribute -($30 million × 0.5) - ($20 million × 1.0) = -$35 million."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Net beta-weighted exposure is not +$65 million.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Net beta-weighted exposure = `$100M − $35M = $65M`, so \"not +$65M\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Net beta-weighted exposure is $100 million - $35 million = $65 million."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Relative to $50 million of fund equity, the portfolio beta is not 1.3.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Portfolio beta vs. equity = `$65M / $50M = 1.3`, so \"not 1.3\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Portfolio beta relative to equity is $65 million / $50 million = 1.3."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The portfolio is beta neutral because its short positions total $50 million.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Beta neutrality requires beta-weighted exposures to cancel; matching short *dollars* ($50M) leaves +$65M net beta exposure, so it is not beta neutral.", "whyPool": "Beta neutrality requires beta-weighted exposures to cancel, and this portfolio retains positive exposure. G6: Dollar Neutrality vs. Beta Neutrality"}], "reference": "Beta-weighted (beta-dollar) exposure, portfolio beta relative to equity — Grinold & Kahn; Bodie-Kane-Marcus (net beta exposure).", "n": 449}, {"id": "QF-D7G6Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G6", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A hedge fund is long $50 million of Stock A and short $50 million of a market ETF . Stock A has a beta of 1.4, while the ETF has a beta of 1.0. The portfolio manager calls the trade “market neutral” because the long and short dollar amounts are equal. 372 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The portfolio is dollar neutral.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Gross long and short dollar amounts are equal ($50M each) → dollar neutral.", "whyPool": "The gross long and short dollar amounts are equal."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The portfolio is beta neutral.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Beta-weighted exposures do not cancel: long `50 × 1.4 = 70` beta-dollars vs. short `50 × 1.0 = 50` → net +20, so it is not beta neutral.", "whyPool": "The beta-weighted exposures do not cancel."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The long side contributes more market exposure than the short side removes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The long side adds 70 beta-dollars while the short removes only 50 — long market exposure exceeds what the short removes.", "whyPool": "The long contributes 50 × 1.4 = 70 beta-dollars, while the short removes 50."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Equal long and short dollar amounts do not necessarily imply equal market risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Equal dollar amounts do not imply equal market risk when betas differ — risk depends on sensitivities, not just dollars.", "whyPool": "Risk depends on sensitivities, not just dollar amounts."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The short ETF position would not need to be larger in absolute dollars to offset Stock A’s higher beta.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A *larger* ETF short would be needed to offset Stock A's higher beta; \"would not need to be larger\" reverses this.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A larger ETF short would be needed for beta neutrality."}], "reference": "Dollar neutrality vs. beta/market neutrality — Investopedia \"Market-Neutral Strategy\"; Grinold & Kahn (beta-neutral construction).", "n": 450}, {"id": "QF-D7G6Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G6", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund is long $30 million of a stock with beta 1.5 and wants to hedge using a different stock with beta 0.75. Ignore alpha, residual returns, and trading costs.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The long position has +$45 million of beta-weighted exposure.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Long beta-weighted exposure = `$30M × 1.5 = $45M`.", "whyPool": "The long exposure is $30 million × 1.5 = $45 million of beta-weighted exposure."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A $30 million short in the hedge stock would make the trade beta neutral.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A $30M short at β 0.75 removes only `$30M × 0.75 = $22.5M` of beta exposure — not enough for beta neutrality.", "whyPool": "A $30 million short with beta 0.75 removes only $22.5 million of beta-weighted exposure."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A $60 million short in the hedge stock would not make the trade beta neutral.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. A $60M short removes `$60M × 0.75 = $45M`, exactly offsetting the long, so \"would not make the trade beta neutral\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A $60 million short removes $60 million × 0.75 = $45 million, exactly offsetting the long."}, {"L": "D", "text": "With the $60 million short, the trade would be beta neutral but not dollar neutral.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "With the $60M short the beta exposures cancel, but the book is net short $30M in dollars → beta neutral yet not dollar neutral.", "whyPool": "The beta exposures cancel, but the position is net short $30 million in dollar terms."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Beta neutrality necessarily requires equal long and short dollar amounts.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Different security betas require *unequal* dollar amounts for equal-and-opposite market exposure; beta neutrality does not require equal dollars.", "whyPool": "Different security betas can require unequal dollar amounts for equal and opposite market exposure. 373 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Beta-adjusted hedge ratio (`hedge$ = long$ · β_long/β_hedge`) — Bodie-Kane-Marcus (hedging with beta); Investopedia \"Beta Hedging.\"", "n": 451}, {"id": "QF-D7G6Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G6", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A portfolio is long $40 million of a defensive stock basket with beta 0.6 and short $40 million of a cyclical stock basket with beta 1.2.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The portfolio is not dollar neutral.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Both legs are $40M, so the book *is* dollar neutral; \"not dollar neutral\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Long and short dollar amounts are both $40 million."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The long basket contributes +$24 million of beta-weighted exposure.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Long contribution = `$40M × 0.6 = +$24M` beta-weighted exposure.", "whyPool": "The long contribution is $40 million × 0.6 = $24 million."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The short basket contributes -$48 million of beta-weighted exposure.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Short contribution = `−$40M × 1.2 = −$48M` beta-weighted exposure.", "whyPool": "The short contribution is -$40 million × 1.2 = -$48 million."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The portfolio has net negative beta-weighted exposure of $24 million.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Net beta-weighted exposure = `$24M − $48M = −$24M` (net negative, i.e., net short beta).", "whyPool": "Net beta-weighted exposure is $24 million - $48 million = -$24 million."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Increasing the long defensive basket to $80 million, while leaving the short unchanged, would make the portfolio both dollar neutral and beta neutral.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Raising the long to $80M would offset beta (`80×0.6 = 48 = 40×1.2`), but $80M long vs. $40M short breaks dollar neutrality — so it cannot be *both*.", "whyPool": "An $80 million long would offset beta exposure, but it would exceed the $40 million short and therefore break dollar neutrality. G7: Hedging and Residual Risk"}], "reference": "Dollar vs. beta neutrality tradeoff — Investopedia \"Market-Neutral Strategy\"; Grinold & Kahn (neutral construction).", "n": 452}, {"id": "QF-D7G7Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G7", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund owns $10 million of a stock with beta 1.3 and shorts a broad market ETF with beta 1.0 to hedge the stock's market exposure.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A $13 million ETF short produces equal and opposite beta-weighted exposure under the estimates.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stock contributes `$10M × 1.3 = $13M` beta-weighted exposure; a $13M short in a β-1 ETF offsets it.", "whyPool": "The stock contributes $10 million × 1.3 = $13 million, which a $13 million beta-one ETF short offsets."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The hedge eliminates all possible losses on the stock position.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The hedge targets modeled *market* exposure only — it does not eliminate idiosyncratic, model, liquidity, or other risks, so it cannot remove \"all possible losses.\"", "whyPool": "The hedge targets modeled market exposure, not idiosyncratic, model, liquidity, or other risks."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Company-specific news can still affect the hedged trade.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "An index hedge does not offset issuer-specific news, so company-specific developments can still move the hedged trade.", "whyPool": "An index hedge does not directly offset issuer-specific developments."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the stock earns a positive asset-specific return while the hedge is effective, the trade cannot retain that return before costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A well-matched market hedge is *designed* to remove systematic return while *preserving* asset-specific (alpha) return before costs; \"cannot retain that return\" reverses the purpose.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A well-matched market hedge is intended to remove systematic return while preserving asset-specific performance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Estimation error in the stock's beta cannot leave unintended market exposure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "If true future beta differs from the estimate, the hedge ratio is imperfect and leaves unintended market exposure — beta estimation error *can* leave exposure.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. If the true future beta differs from the estimate, the hedge ratio will be imperfect."}], "reference": "Index hedge, systematic vs. idiosyncratic risk, hedge-ratio (beta) error — Bodie-Kane-Marcus (hedging market risk); Investopedia \"Hedge\" and \"Basis Risk.\"", "n": 453}, {"id": "QF-D7G7Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G7", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A pair trade is long $5 million of Stock A with beta 1.4 and short Stock B with beta 0.7. The two companies operate in the same industry, but their historical relationship can change.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The long side contributes +$7 million of beta-weighted exposure.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Long beta-weighted exposure = `$5M × 1.4 = $7M`.", "whyPool": "The long beta-weighted exposure is $5 million × 1.4 = $7 million."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A $5 million short in Stock B would not make the trade dollar neutral.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Equal $5M long and $5M short *are* dollar neutral, so \"would not make the trade dollar neutral\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Equal $5 million long and short positions are dollar neutral."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A $5 million short in Stock B would also make the trade beta neutral.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A $5M short at β 0.7 removes only `$5M × 0.7 = $3.5M` of beta exposure — well short of the $7M long, so it is not beta neutral.", "whyPool": "The $5 million short removes only $3.5 million of beta-weighted exposure."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A $10 million short in Stock B would offset the long side's estimated market beta.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A $10M short at β 0.7 removes `$10M × 0.7 = $7M`, offsetting the long side's estimated market beta.", "whyPool": "A $10 million short at beta 0.7 removes $7 million of beta-weighted exposure."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Even a beta-neutral pair trade can lose if the companies' relative fundamentals or historical relationship changes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Even a beta-neutral pair trade can lose on relative-value, company-specific, or relationship-breakdown risk.", "whyPool": "Beta neutrality does not eliminate relative-value, company-specific, or relationship-breakdown risk."}], "reference": "Pair trading, dollar vs. beta neutrality, relative-value risk — Investopedia \"Pairs Trade\"; Grinold & Kahn (residual/relative-value risk).", "n": 454}, {"id": "QF-D7G7Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "07", "domainName": "CAPM, Alpha, Beta & Hedging", "group": "G7", "weeks": [6, 7], "weekMax": 7, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A $20 million equity portfolio has beta 0.8. A manager shorts $16 million of a beta-one index future equivalent to reduce market exposure. During the period, the market rises 6%, and the portfolio earns an additional 2% asset-specific return. Ignore financing and trading costs for the initial calculations.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The unhedged portfolio's modeled market-related gain is not $960,000. 375 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Modeled market-related gain = `$20M × 0.8 × 6% = $960,000`, so \"not $960,000\" reverses the correct conclusion.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The market-related gain is $20 million × 0.8 × 6% = $960,000."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The index hedge's modeled loss is not $960,000.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Negation trap. Short index exposure loses `$16M × 6% = $960,000`, so \"not $960,000\" reverses the correct conclusion — the hedge loss exactly offsets the market gain.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The short index exposure loses $16 million × 6% = $960,000."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The offsetting market components leave approximately $400,000 of asset-specific gain before costs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "After the two market components cancel, the 2% asset-specific return on $20M = `$400,000` remains before costs.", "whyPool": "The 2% asset-specific return on $20 million is $400,000 after the modeled market components cancel."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The hedge guarantees the portfolio's realized beta will remain exactly zero in every future period.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Beta and hedge relationships change and returns carry basis/residual risk — a hedge cannot guarantee realized beta \"exactly zero in every future period.\"", "whyPool": "Beta and hedge relationships can change, and realized returns contain basis and residual risk."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Trading costs, financing, imperfect index matching, and changing beta can reduce the effectiveness of the hedge.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Real hedges are imperfect: trading costs, financing, imperfect index matching, and changing beta all reduce effectiveness.", "whyPool": "Real hedges are imperfect and impose implementation and carrying costs. 376 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Beta hedge with index futures, basis risk, return drag — Bodie-Kane-Marcus (hedging with index futures); Investopedia \"Basis Risk\" and \"Beta Hedging.\"", "n": 455}, {"id": "QF-D8G1Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G1", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher finds that a trading rule based only on the previous five days of prices and volume earned positive returns in the historical sample. She concludes that weak-form market efficiency has been disproved. 378 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Weak-form efficiency concerns whether historical market data can be used to earn abnormal returns consistently.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Weak-form efficiency is precisely about whether *past* price/volume can be used to earn *consistent* abnormal returns; the choice states the hypothesis correctly.", "whyPool": "Weak-form efficiency states that information contained in past prices and volume is already reflected in current prices."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A profitable in-sample result alone is sufficient to disprove weak-form efficiency.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A single profitable in-sample result can be sample-specific, overfit, or uneconomic after costs; it does not by itself establish an *exploitable* inefficiency.", "whyPool": "The result may be sample-specific, overfit, or uneconomic after costs and therefore does not by itself establish exploitable inefficiency."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Data snooping could produce an apparently successful historical rule even when no durable predictability exists.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Data snooping — searching many candidate rules — will surface a chance winner in historical data even when no durable predictability exists.", "whyPool": "Searching many rules can identify a chance winner in historical data."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Transaction costs and out-of-sample performance are relevant when evaluating the claimed inefficiency.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Transaction costs and out-of-sample performance *are* relevant when judging a claimed inefficiency; a usable abnormal return must survive independent testing and realistic implementation costs.", "whyPool": "A usable abnormal return must survive independent testing and realistic implementation costs."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Weak-form efficiency implies that a stock's price can never rise on two consecutive days.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Weak-form efficiency says past data are already impounded; it does **not** imply prices can never rise on two consecutive days — random/weakly predictable returns still produce runs.", "whyPool": "Random or weakly predictable returns can still produce runs of gains or losses."}], "reference": "Fama (1970) EMH — weak form; ISLR ch.5 (out-of-sample validation) and data-snooping concept.", "n": 456}, {"id": "QF-D8G1Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G1", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A stock closes at $100. Under a simplified random-walk model, tomorrow's price equals today's price plus an unpredictable innovation with an expected value of zero.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The model's expected price for tomorrow is not $100.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: with a zero-mean innovation, the *conditional expected* next price equals the current price ($100), so \"expected price is not $100\" is wrong.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. With a zero-mean innovation, the conditional expected next price is the current price."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The model guarantees that tomorrow's realized price will be $100.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The innovation is uncertain, so the *realized* price need not equal its expectation; the model guarantees nothing about the realized value.", "whyPool": "The innovation is uncertain, so the realized price need not equal its expectation. 379 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "New information can cause tomorrow's realized price to differ substantially from $100.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Unexpected news is represented by a large positive or negative innovation, which can move the realized price substantially away from $100.", "whyPool": "Unexpected information can be represented by a large positive or negative innovation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A random-walk intuition means price changes are independent of all possible information, including news released tomorrow.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The random-walk claim is about *unpredictability from currently available information*, not independence from all future information; tomorrow's news can affect tomorrow's price.", "whyPool": "The model concerns predictability using currently available information; future news can affect future prices."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If price changes are unpredictable, a chart can still describe the past without necessarily forecasting abnormal future returns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A chart can describe the past without conferring profitable predictive power — descriptive patterns ≠ forecastable abnormal returns.", "whyPool": "Descriptive patterns do not automatically provide profitable predictive power."}], "reference": "Random-walk model of prices (Fama 1965); EMH weak form and conditional-expectation intuition.", "n": 457}, {"id": "QF-D8G1Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G1", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An analyst tests a momentum signal derived exclusively from past returns. Before costs, the signal earns 6% per year above the benchmark; realistic turnover costs are estimated at 7% per year.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The gross backtest suggests predictability based on historical price information.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The pre-cost result is associated with a signal built from past returns, i.e., apparent predictability from historical price data.", "whyPool": "The pre-cost result is associated with a signal constructed from past returns."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Under the stated cost estimate, the strategy earns approximately -1% per year relative to the benchmark after costs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Net abnormal return ≈ 6% − 7% = −1%/yr under the stated cost estimate — simple arithmetic.", "whyPool": "The approximate net abnormal return is 6% - 7% = -1% per year."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A negative net result provides investors with an economically exploitable violation of weak-form efficiency.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A strategy that loses money after realistic costs is **not** an economically exploitable violation of weak-form efficiency.", "whyPool": "An opportunity that loses money after realistic costs is not economically exploitable as described."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The finding could still be academically interesting even if the signal cannot be traded profitably after costs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Predictability and net profitability are related but distinct research questions; a signal can be academically interesting yet untradeable.", "whyPool": "Predictability and practical profitability are related but distinct research questions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Lowering turnover while preserving enough of the signal could not change the strategy's net economics.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: reducing turnover/implementation costs *could* let some of the gross edge survive, changing net economics.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Reducing implementation costs could allow some of the gross edge to survive. G2: Semi-Strong Market Efficiency"}], "reference": "EMH weak form and net-of-cost tradeability; transaction-cost/turnover analysis (Grinold & Kahn, implementation shortfall).", "n": 458}, {"id": "QF-D8G2Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G2", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A company releases unexpectedly strong earnings at 8:30 a.m. The stock price jumps before the market opens. A junior analyst claims that the semi-strong form of market efficiency requires the stock to keep rising for several days because the news was public.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Semi-strong efficiency implies public information should be incorporated rapidly into price.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Semi-strong efficiency is about *rapid* incorporation of public information into price.", "whyPool": "Semi-strong efficiency concerns rapid incorporation of public information."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A delayed multi-day drift is required by semi-strong efficiency.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A persistent, predictable multi-day drift is **not** required — it would in fact *challenge* the hypothesis.", "whyPool": "Persistent predictable drift would challenge the hypothesis."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Fundamental analysis based only on already-public information should consistently earn abnormal returns under semi-strong efficiency. 380 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: under semi-strong efficiency, fundamental analysis on already-public info should **not** consistently earn abnormal returns.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Public information should already be reflected in prices."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The immediate pre-market price jump is not consistent with semi-strong efficiency.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: an immediate pre-market jump is *consistent* with rapid adjustment, not inconsistent with it.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Rapid adjustment is consistent with the hypothesis."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Semi-strong efficiency does not claim that prices are always correct in an absolute sense.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Efficiency concerns information incorporation, not perfect (absolutely correct) valuation.", "whyPool": "Efficiency concerns information incorporation, not perfect valuation."}], "reference": "Fama EMH — semi-strong form; event-study \"speed of adjustment\" literature.", "n": 459}, {"id": "QF-D8G2Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G2", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A company publishes an annual report at noon. At 2:00 p.m., an analyst reads the report, values the company using only the disclosed figures, and plans to buy before other investors notice the information.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The annual report is public information once released.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A published annual report is publicly available information once released.", "whyPool": "A published annual report is publicly available information."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Semi-strong efficiency predicts that the market must wait until every investor reads the report before adjusting the price.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Semi-strong efficiency does **not** require every investor to read the report first — prices can adjust via the trades of *informed* participants.", "whyPool": "Prices may adjust through the trades of informed market participants without every investor processing the news."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Competition among analysts and algorithms can cause the price to respond before this particular analyst completes the valuation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Competing analysts and algorithms can analyze and trade on the disclosure before this one analyst finishes.", "whyPool": "Other participants can analyze and trade on the disclosure rapidly."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the analyst repeatedly earns abnormal risk-adjusted returns from newly released reports after costs, that evidence would challenge semi-strong efficiency.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Repeatedly earning abnormal *risk-adjusted, net-of-cost* returns from newly public disclosures would be evidence *against* semi-strong efficiency.", "whyPool": "Consistent abnormal performance based on public disclosures would be evidence against the hypothesis."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Semi-strong efficiency rules out returns earned from bearing systematic risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The hypothesis targets abnormal returns from *public information*, not compensation for bearing *systematic risk* (which is expected).", "whyPool": "The hypothesis addresses abnormal returns from public information, not compensation for systematic risk. 381 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Fama EMH — semi-strong form; competitive price discovery / informed-trading concept.", "n": 460}, {"id": "QF-D8G2Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G2", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "After a central bank makes an unexpected public rate announcement, an index falls 2% within one minute, rebounds 0.4% over the next ten minutes, and then trades in a narrow range.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The rapid initial movement is consistent with fast incorporation of public news.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The large immediate move is consistent with rapid processing of a public surprise.", "whyPool": "A large immediate response is consistent with rapid processing of the public surprise."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Semi-strong efficiency requires the first transaction after the announcement to establish the exact final price with no subsequent trading.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Efficiency does **not** require the first post-announcement trade to fix the exact final price with no further trading — it doesn't demand instantaneous perfection or eliminate price discovery.", "whyPool": "Efficiency does not require instantaneous perfection or eliminate price discovery."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A brief period of price discovery and disagreement can occur even in a relatively efficient market.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A brief window of price discovery/disagreement can occur even in a relatively efficient market as participants interpret news and liquidity adjusts.", "whyPool": "Participants may interpret the news differently while liquidity and order flow adjust."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The 0.4% rebound by itself proves that the announcement was ignored initially.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A 0.4% partial rebound does not *prove* the news was initially ignored — it can reflect price discovery, liquidity, or mild overreaction; one observation isn't proof.", "whyPool": "A partial reversal can arise from price discovery, liquidity effects, or an initial overreaction; one observation is not proof."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Testing efficiency would not require a systematic analysis of whether predictable abnormal returns remain after the announcement and after costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: testing efficiency **does** require systematic analysis of whether predictable abnormal returns persist after the announcement and after costs.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The relevant issue is whether a reliable, tradeable pattern persists beyond chance and costs. G3: Strong-Form Market Efficiency"}], "reference": "Semi-strong efficiency & price discovery; microstructure of announcement responses.", "n": 461}, {"id": "QF-D8G3Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G3", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A pharmaceutical executive privately learns that a major clinical trial failed before the result is announced. The executive believes strong-form market efficiency means the information must already be reflected in the company's stock price.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Strong-form efficiency claims that prices reflect both public and private information.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Strong-form efficiency claims prices reflect *both public and private* information.", "whyPool": "The strong form extends the information set to private as well as public information."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The executive's ability to profit from genuinely private information would contradict the strong form.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If the executive can profit from genuinely private information, that contradicts the strong-form claim (which denies such abnormal profits).", "whyPool": "Consistent abnormal profits based on nonpublic information are inconsistent with the strong-form claim."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Semi-strong efficiency necessarily predicts that the failed trial is already reflected before public disclosure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Semi-strong efficiency concerns *public* info only, so it does **not** require an undisclosed trial result to be in the price.", "whyPool": "Semi-strong efficiency concerns public information, so it does not require undisclosed results to be in the price."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Information can be economically valuable before it is legally permissible to trade on it.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Information can be economically valuable *before* it is legally permissible to trade on it — trading restrictions don't erase informational value.", "whyPool": "Trading restrictions do not eliminate the informational value of private news."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Insider-trading laws are proof that the strong form always holds in practice. 382 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Insider-trading laws restrict *behavior*; they do not demonstrate that prices already contain all private information (i.e., they don't prove the strong form).", "whyPool": "Regulation restricts behavior; it does not demonstrate that prices already contain all private information."}], "reference": "Fama EMH — strong form; insider-information and regulation distinction.", "n": 462}, {"id": "QF-D8G3Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G3", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A study finds that corporate insiders' legally reported purchases are followed, on average, by positive abnormal returns. The purchases become public only after the insiders trade and file the required reports.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The finding may indicate that insiders possessed information not fully reflected in price when they traded.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The finding may indicate insiders held information not fully reflected in price when they traded (private knowledge / superior context).", "whyPool": "Insiders may act on private knowledge or superior internal context before public disclosure."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Such a finding is not easier to reconcile with semi-strong efficiency than with strong-form efficiency at the time of the insider trades.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: the finding *is* easier to reconcile with semi-strong efficiency (which doesn't require private info in price) than with strong-form efficiency (which does) — so calling them equivalent is wrong.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Semi-strong efficiency does not require private information to be reflected, while strong-form efficiency does."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Once the filing becomes public, it enters the information set considered by semi-strong efficiency.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Once the required filing is released, it becomes public information within the semi-strong information set.", "whyPool": "A public filing is public information once it is released."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Strong-form efficiency predicts that insiders can systematically profit because they are better trained investors.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Strong-form efficiency *denies* consistent abnormal profits even to those with private information; it does not predict insiders profit for being \"better trained.\"", "whyPool": "Strong-form efficiency denies consistent abnormal profits even to investors with private information."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The result alone proves that every reported insider purchase is profitable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "An average relationship does not imply *every* individual insider purchase is profitable.", "whyPool": "An average relationship does not imply that every individual observation succeeds."}], "reference": "Fama EMH — strong vs. semi-strong; insider-trading event studies (e.g., Seyhun).", "n": 463}, {"id": "QF-D8G3Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G3", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A hedge fund pays for legal satellite imagery that estimates activity at retailers' parking lots. The images are commercially available to any customer willing to pay, but most investors do not subscribe. 383 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The classification of the imagery as public or private may depend on its availability, access, and applicable definition of the information set.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Alternative data can sit in a practical gray area between broadly disseminated and costly-to-access information; classification depends on availability, access, and the chosen information-set definition.", "whyPool": "Alternative data can occupy a practical gray area between broadly disseminated and costly-to-access information."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Semi-strong efficiency requires every investor to own the same data subscriptions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Semi-strong efficiency does **not** require every investor to own the same subscriptions — prices can aggregate information without universal ownership.", "whyPool": "Market prices can aggregate information without universal ownership of each source."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Prices can incorporate information through the trades of a subset of informed investors.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Prices can incorporate information through the trades of a *subset* of informed investors, transmitting it to the wider market.", "whyPool": "Informed trading can transmit information into prices for the wider market."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the data are legally available but costly to acquire and process, information-acquisition costs can limit perfect incorporation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If data are legal but costly to acquire/process, information-acquisition costs reduce incentives and slow complete incorporation.", "whyPool": "Acquisition and analysis costs can reduce incentives and slow complete incorporation."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Strong-form and semi-strong efficiency are identical because both include all private information.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Strong and semi-strong forms are **not** identical: the strong form includes private information; semi-strong is limited to public information.", "whyPool": "The strong form includes private information; the semi-strong form is limited to public information. G4: Arbitrage and Law-of-One-Price Reasoning"}], "reference": "Grossman–Stiglitz (costly information / limits to efficiency); Fama EMH form distinctions.", "n": 464}, {"id": "QF-D8G4Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G4", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "The same freely transferable stock trades at $50.00 in Market A and $50.40 in Market B. A trader can buy and sell simultaneously, with total transaction and transfer costs of $0.10 per share and no quantity limits.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Buying in Market A and selling in Market B produces a gross spread of $0.40 per share.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Gross spread = $50.40 − $50.00 = $0.40/share.", "whyPool": "The gross price difference is $50.40 - $50.00 = $0.40 per share."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The stated net profit is not $0.30 per share.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: after $0.10 total costs the net spread *is* $0.30/share, so \"net profit is not $0.30\" is wrong (double-negative trap).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. After $0.10 of total costs, the net spread is $0.30 per share. 384 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "The strategy requires the stock's fundamental value to rise.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The profit comes from the *contemporaneous* price discrepancy, not a directional forecast of fundamental value.", "whyPool": "The profit comes from the contemporaneous price discrepancy, not a directional forecast of fundamental value."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Simultaneous execution helps reduce the risk that one price changes before both sides are not completed.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: simultaneous execution *does* reduce the risk of one price moving before both legs complete (the choice's negated wording makes it false).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Locking in both legs limits execution-related price risk."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Repeated arbitrage trading would tend to raise the price in Market A and lower the price in Market B.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Repeated arbitrage — buying in A, selling in B — pushes A's price up and B's down toward convergence.", "whyPool": "Buying pressure in A and selling pressure in B push the two prices toward convergence."}], "reference": "Law of one price / cross-market arbitrage; Investopedia \"Arbitrage.\"", "withdrawn": true, "n": 465}, {"id": "QF-D8G4Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G4", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A convertible security and a replicating portfolio promise identical future cash flows under all relevant states. The convertible costs $102, while the replicating portfolio costs $100. Both can be traded without costs, financing constraints, or default risk.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The law of one price suggests the two positions should not have the same price under the stated assumptions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: identical state-contingent cash flows *should* command the same price under the stated assumptions (LOOP), so \"should not have the same price\" is wrong.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Identical state-contingent cash flows should command the same price in a frictionless market."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A trader could buy the $100 replicating portfolio and short the $102 convertible.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The arbitrage buys the cheaper equivalent ($100 portfolio) and shorts the dearer one ($102 convertible).", "whyPool": "The strategy buys the cheaper equivalent and sells the more expensive one."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The initial gross cash inflow from that trade is $2.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Initial cash flow = $102 short proceeds − $100 purchase = $2 inflow.", "whyPool": "Short-sale proceeds of $102 less the $100 purchase produce $2 initially."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because the future cash flows offset in every state, the described discrepancy represents a theoretical arbitrage.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Offsetting future obligations in every state leave the $2 initial difference as profit — a theoretical arbitrage under the assumptions.", "whyPool": "The offsetting future obligations leave the initial price difference as profit under the assumptions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the cash flows were only highly correlated rather than identical, the trade would remain riskless.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "If cash flows were only *highly correlated* rather than identical, residual (basis) risk remains — the trade would not be riskless.", "whyPool": "High correlation does not ensure identical payoffs, so residual risk would remain."}], "reference": "Law of one price / replication; riskless vs. risky arbitrage (CFA curriculum, no-arbitrage pricing).", "n": 466}, {"id": "QF-D8G4Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G4", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An exchange-traded fund holds a basket worth $100.00 per share but trades at $99.60. Creating and redeeming ETF shares costs $0.25 per share, and the basket value can change while the arbitrage is executed.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The observed discount is not $0.40 per share.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: the ETF trades $100.00 − $99.60 = $0.40 below the basket, so \"the discount is not $0.40\" is wrong.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The ETF trades $100.00 - $99.60 = $0.40 below the basket."}, {"L": "B", "text": "After the stated creation/redemption cost, the apparent spread is not $0.15 per share before other risks and costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: subtracting the $0.25 cost leaves $0.15/share before other frictions, so \"the spread is not $0.15\" is wrong.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Subtracting the $0.25 cost leaves $0.15 per share before other frictions."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The trade is necessarily riskless because the ETF and basket are closely related.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The trade is **not** necessarily riskless — timing, execution, financing, and tracking risks can prevent locking in the spread.", "whyPool": "Timing, execution, financing, and tracking risks can prevent the spread from being locked in."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Execution delay and movements in the basket can eliminate the apparent profit. 385 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A small gross opportunity can vanish before all legs complete due to execution delay and basket-value movement.", "whyPool": "A small gross opportunity can disappear before all legs are completed."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Authorized creation/redemption activity can help keep an ETF's market price near the value of its holdings.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Authorized creation/redemption lets equivalent ETF shares and baskets be exchanged, supporting price–NAV convergence.", "whyPool": "The mechanism permits economically equivalent ETF shares and baskets to be exchanged, supporting convergence. G5: Statistical Arbitrage and Mean Reversion"}], "reference": "ETF creation/redemption arbitrage mechanism; law of one price with frictions (Investopedia \"ETF arbitrage\").", "n": 467}, {"id": "QF-D8G5Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G5", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two historically related stocks have a fitted spread with a long-run mean of $0 and standard deviation of $2. The current spread is +$5. A pair-trading model interprets positive spreads as Stock A being expensive relative to Stock B.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The current spread has a z-score of +2.5 under the stated estimates.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "z = ($5 − $0) / $2 = +2.5.", "whyPool": "The z-score is ($5 - $0) / $2 = +2.5."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A mean-reversion trade would generally short the relatively expensive Stock A and buy Stock B.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Mean-reversion trade shorts the relatively expensive Stock A and buys Stock B, profiting if the spread declines.", "whyPool": "The position is designed to profit if A becomes cheaper relative to B and the spread declines."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The trade requires both stocks to fall in absolute price to succeed.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The *relative* spread can narrow with both stocks rising, both falling, or diverging — it doesn't require both to fall in absolute terms.", "whyPool": "The relative spread can narrow with both stocks rising, both falling, or moving in opposite directions."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A large positive z-score guarantees that the spread will converge.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A large positive z-score measures historical unusualness; it does **not** guarantee future convergence.", "whyPool": "A z-score measures historical unusualness, not certain future convergence."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A structural change in the companies could make the historical spread relationship unreliable.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Structural changes (mergers, business-model shifts, leverage changes) can break the historical spread relationship.", "whyPool": "Mergers, business-model changes, leverage, or other shifts can break the prior relationship. 386 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Statistical arbitrage / pairs trading, z-score entry; mean reversion (Investopedia \"Statistical arbitrage\").", "n": 468}, {"id": "QF-D8G5Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G5", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A pair trade buys $100,000 of Stock B and shorts $100,000 of Stock A. Over the holding period, A rises 8% and B rises 5%, excluding costs.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The long position in B earns $5,000.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Long B gain = $100,000 × 5% = $5,000.", "whyPool": "The long gain is $100,000 × 5% = $5,000."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The short position in A loses $8,000.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Short A loss = $100,000 × 8% = $8,000 (the shorted stock rose).", "whyPool": "The short loses $100,000 × 8% = $8,000."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The pair trade earns $3,000 before costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Net P&L = $5,000 − $8,000 = **−$3,000** before costs, so \"earns $3,000\" is wrong (it *loses* $3,000).", "whyPool": "Net P&L is $5,000 - $8,000 = -$3,000 before costs."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Equal long and short dollar amounts prevent losses if the spread moves against the trade.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Dollar neutrality limits net dollar exposure but does **not** eliminate relative-price risk.", "whyPool": "Dollar neutrality limits net dollar exposure but does not eliminate relative-price risk."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The result shows that a dollar-neutral trade cannot lose money even when both securities rise.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: a dollar-neutral trade **can** lose money when both rise — here the short rose more than the long, producing a loss despite balanced initial dollars.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The short rose more than the long, producing a loss despite balanced initial dollars."}], "reference": "Dollar-neutral / long-short P&L mechanics; relative-value risk (CFA long-short strategies).", "n": 469}, {"id": "QF-D8G5Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G5", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quant team chooses a mean-reversion threshold after testing z-score entry levels from 0.5 to 4.0 on the same 15-year data set. The 2.3 threshold performs best, so the team reports its Sharpe ratio without independent validation.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Selecting the best threshold on the evaluation sample can overfit historical noise.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Choosing the best threshold on the *same* evaluation sample overfits historical noise.", "whyPool": "The threshold was optimized on the same data used to report performance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The reported Sharpe ratio may overstate expected future performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Selection bias makes the winning in-sample Sharpe unusually favorable, likely overstating future performance.", "whyPool": "Selection bias makes the winning in-sample result unusually favorable. 387 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "An independent out-of-sample period would provide a stronger test of the selected rule.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "An independent out-of-sample period is a stronger test of whether the chosen rule generalizes.", "whyPool": "Untouched data better assess whether the chosen relationship and threshold generalize."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Calling the strategy “statistical arbitrage” means its trades are riskless.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Calling a strategy \"statistical arbitrage\" does **not** make it riskless — it typically carries model, convergence, market, and execution risk.", "whyPool": "Statistical arbitrage typically carries model, convergence, market, and execution risk."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Frequent re-estimation of the mean and variance always prevents relationships from breaking.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Frequent re-estimation of mean/variance may adapt to change but cannot *guarantee* a valid economic relationship persists.", "whyPool": "Re-estimation may adapt to change but cannot guarantee that a valid economic relationship remains. G6: Event-Driven and Relative-Value Strategies"}], "reference": "Data snooping / selection bias; out-of-sample validation (ISLR ch.5 cross-validation); statistical-arbitrage risk.", "n": 470}, {"id": "QF-D8G6Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G6", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A buyer agrees to acquire a target company for $50 per share in cash. The target trades at $47 after the announcement because investors believe the deal might fail or be delayed.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The announced-deal spread is not $3 per share.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: the deal spread *is* $50 − $47 = $3/share, so \"not $3\" is wrong (double-negative trap).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The difference between the $50 offer and $47 market price is $3."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Buying at $47 and receiving $50 at closing would not produce a $3 gain per share before costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: buying at $47 and receiving $50 at close *does* produce a $3 gain before costs, so the negated wording is false.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Successful closing at the stated price produces $50 - $47 = $3 before costs."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The $3 spread is a guaranteed arbitrage profit once the merger is announced.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Completion is uncertain, so the $3 is **not** a guaranteed profit — merger arbitrage is risky event-driven investing.", "whyPool": "Completion is uncertain, so merger arbitrage is generally risky event-driven investing."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Regulatory rejection or financing failure could cause the target price to fall and generate a loss.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Regulatory rejection or financing failure can remove the offer-price support and cause a loss.", "whyPool": "Deal failure can remove the support of the offer price and expose the trader to downside."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The annualized return depends partly on how long the deal takes to close.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Annualized return depends on time-to-close: the same $3 earned sooner yields a higher annualized return.", "whyPool": "Earning the same spread sooner produces a higher annualized return than earning it later."}], "reference": "Merger (risk) arbitrage — deal spread & completion risk; event-driven strategies (CFA / Investopedia \"Merger arbitrage\").", "n": 471}, {"id": "QF-D8G6Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G6", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An investor believes Airline A is undervalued relative to otherwise similar Airline B. She buys $1 million of A and shorts $1 million of B before an industry-wide fuel-price announcement.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The trade expresses a relative-value view rather than a purely directional view on the airline industry.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The trade expresses a *relative-value* view (A outperforms B), not a purely directional bet on airlines.", "whyPool": "The thesis is that A will outperform B, not necessarily that airlines will rise."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Equal dollar sizing removes every difference in the two airlines' sensitivities to fuel prices.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Equal dollar sizing does **not** remove differences in fuel-price sensitivity — the airlines may differ in hedges, routes, costs, leverage, and betas.", "whyPool": "The companies may have different hedges, routes, costs, leverage, and betas despite equal notional positions."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If both stocks fall but B falls more than A, the trade can earn a positive gross return. 388 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "If both fall but B falls more, the short gain can exceed the long loss → positive gross return.", "whyPool": "The gain on the B short can exceed the loss on the A long when B underperforms."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Company-specific news affecting only A can influence the trade's outcome.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Company-specific news affecting only A still influences the outcome — residual idiosyncratic risk remains in a relative-value position.", "whyPool": "Residual company-specific risk remains in a relative-value position."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Borrow fees and short-sale availability for B are irrelevant because the long and short notionals are equal.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Borrow fees and short availability for B are **not** irrelevant — they directly affect feasibility and net performance despite equal notionals.", "whyPool": "Borrow costs and availability directly affect feasibility and net performance."}], "reference": "Relative-value / pairs trading, residual idiosyncratic risk; short-sale cost considerations.", "n": 472}, {"id": "QF-D8G6Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G6", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A model predicts that a retailer will beat the market's earnings expectation. The trader buys the stock immediately before the announcement. The company reports earnings above the published analyst consensus, but below the higher result already implied by the stock price, and the stock falls.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Beating the published consensus does not guarantee a positive price reaction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Beating published consensus does not guarantee a positive reaction — returns depend on the surprise vs. what was already priced.", "whyPool": "Returns depend on the surprise relative to what investors had already priced."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Market prices may reflect expectations that differ from the stated analyst consensus.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Market prices can embed expectations that differ from stated analyst consensus (positioning, informal forecasts, whisper numbers).", "whyPool": "Informal forecasts, positioning, and other information can produce a higher embedded expectation."}, {"L": "C", "text": "An event-driven strategy must not forecast both the event outcome and how that outcome compares with the market's priced-in expectations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: an event-driven strategy *must* forecast **both** the outcome and how it compares to priced-in expectations — a correct number forecast can still lose if the market expected more.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A correct forecast of the reported number may still lose if the market expected more."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The stock's decline proves that the reported earnings were objectively poor.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The decline does not prove earnings were objectively poor — a report can be strong absolutely yet disappoint the market's implicit expectation.", "whyPool": "The report can be strong in absolute terms while disappointing the market's implicit expectation."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Volatility, spreads, and gaps around announcements cannot increase implementation risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: volatility, spreads, and gaps around announcements *do* increase implementation risk (rapid repricing, reduced execution certainty).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Event windows often involve rapid repricing and reduced execution certainty. 389 Rev:081020261230 G7: Limits to Arbitrage and Market Efficiency"}], "reference": "Post-earnings-announcement drift & \"beat vs. whisper\" expectations; event-driven execution risk.", "n": 473}, {"id": "QF-D8G7Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G7", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund manager believes an overpriced stock will eventually fall from $120 to its estimated value of $80. The manager shorts it, but the stock first rises to $170, causing margin calls and forcing the fund to close the position.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The manager can have a correct long-run valuation thesis and still lose money on the trade.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A correct long-run valuation thesis can still lose money because of the price *path* and financing constraints.", "whyPool": "A trade can fail because of its path and financing even if the terminal valuation view is eventually correct."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Timing uncertainty can prevent an investor from holding a position until convergence.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Timing/horizon uncertainty (capital and margin limits) can prevent holding until convergence — a core limit to arbitrage.", "whyPool": "Capital and margin constraints make the time to convergence economically important."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The interim rise represents noise-trader risk for the short seller.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The interim rise driven by other traders is noise-trader risk for the short seller.", "whyPool": "Other traders can push price farther from estimated value before correction."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because the estimated fundamental value is $80, the broker must allow the fund unlimited time to maintain the short.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Brokers set collateral/margin on *current exposure*, not the manager's $80 valuation estimate — they need not grant unlimited time.", "whyPool": "Brokers impose collateral and margin requirements based on current exposure, not the manager's valuation estimate."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Forced liquidation cannot allow mispricing to persist even when informed investors recognize it.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: forced liquidation *can* let mispricing persist — constrained informed capital cannot necessarily eliminate it immediately.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Constrained informed capital cannot necessarily eliminate an inefficiency immediately."}], "reference": "Shleifer & Vishny \"Limits of Arbitrage\" (noise-trader & horizon risk); Shleifer *Inefficient Markets*.", "n": 474}, {"id": "QF-D8G7Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G7", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Several funds hold the same leveraged convergence trade. When one fund receives investor redemptions, it unwinds the position, moving prices farther against the remaining funds and triggering additional margin calls.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "This is not an example of crowded-trade risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: this *is* crowded-trade risk — many investors are exposed to the same position and may exit together.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Many investors are exposed to the same trade and may try to exit together."}, {"L": "B", "text": "One fund's liquidation can adversely affect other funds holding similar positions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Price impact from the first liquidation transmits losses and constraints to other funds in the same trade.", "whyPool": "Price impact from the first liquidation can transmit losses and constraints to others."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Leverage cannot convert a temporary adverse move into forced selling.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: leverage *can* convert a temporary adverse move into forced selling — leveraged portfolios absorb less adverse mark-to-market.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Leveraged portfolios have less capacity to absorb adverse mark-to-market movements."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Crowding guarantees faster convergence because more informed capital is always stabilizing.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Crowding does **not** guarantee faster convergence — crowded exits can amplify divergence and *delay* convergence.", "whyPool": "Crowded exits can amplify divergence and delay convergence."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A strategy's risk can depend on other investors' positioning as well as on the underlying securities.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A strategy's risk depends on other investors' positioning (endogenous liquidity / feedback loops), not only the underlying securities.", "whyPool": "Common positioning creates endogenous liquidity and feedback-loop risk."}], "reference": "Shleifer & Vishny \"Limits of Arbitrage\"; crowded trades / LTCM-style deleveraging spirals.", "n": 475}, {"id": "QF-D8G7Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "08", "domainName": "Market Efficiency & Arbitrage", "group": "G7", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An investor identifies an apparently overpriced stock but learns that shares are difficult to borrow. The annual borrow fee is 18%, the lender may recall the shares, and the timing of any correction is unknown.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A high borrow fee reduces the expected net profit from the short position.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A high borrow fee is a direct carrying cost that reduces expected net profit on the short.", "whyPool": "Borrow expense is a direct carrying cost of maintaining the short."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A share recall can force the investor to close the trade before the expected correction.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A lender recall can force the investor to close before the expected correction — horizon/forced-closeout risk.", "whyPool": "The lender's recall right introduces horizon and forced-closeout risk."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Short-sale constraints cannot allow overpricing to persist longer than under frictionless arbitrage.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the conclusion: short-sale constraints *can* let overpricing persist longer than under frictionless arbitrage — pessimistic views aren't fully expressed when shorting is costly/infeasible.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Pessimistic views may not be fully expressed when shorting is costly or infeasible."}, {"L": "D", "text": "If the stock is 10% overvalued by the investor's estimate, an 18% annual borrow fee is economically irrelevant.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "An 18% annual borrow fee is **not** irrelevant against a 10% estimated overvaluation — carrying costs can exceed the expected convergence profit, especially with uncertain timing.", "whyPool": "Carrying costs can exceed the expected convergence profit, especially when timing is uncertain."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Difficulty exploiting a suspected inefficiency can coexist with relatively efficient market prices.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Difficulty exploiting a suspected inefficiency can coexist with relatively efficient prices — efficiency is shaped by trading cost/risk, not \"every discrepancy is free to remove.\"", "whyPool": "Efficiency is shaped by the cost and risk of trading; it need not mean every apparent discrepancy is free or immediately removable. 391 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Shleifer *Inefficient Markets* (short-sale constraints); Miller (1977) overpricing under short constraints; limits to arbitrage.", "n": 476}, {"id": "QF-D9G1Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G1", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher randomly assigns daily observations from 2010–2025 to training and test sets. The model uses lagged returns to predict the next day's return.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A random split cannot place observations from 2025 in training while observations from 2012 appear in testing.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: random assignment does *not* preserve chronological order, so it absolutely can put 2025 in training while 2012 is in testing — that is exactly the problem with random splits.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Random assignment does not preserve chronological order."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The split faithfully simulates training in 2012 using only information available by 2012.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A random split does not simulate training-as-of-2012; future observations can influence a model that is then evaluated on earlier dates (temporal leakage).", "whyPool": "Future observations can influence a model evaluated on earlier dates."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Dependence between nearby time-series observations can make random-split test results overly optimistic.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Autocorrelation and overlapping regimes make nearby (randomly separated) observations unusually similar, so random-split test scores are over-optimistic.", "whyPool": "Autocorrelation and overlapping regimes can make randomly separated observations unusually similar."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Training on an earlier period and testing on a later period better preserves the direction of time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Training on an earlier period and testing on a later one preserves the arrow of time and better mimics live deployment.", "whyPool": "A chronological split more closely represents future deployment."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Using only lagged features guarantees that the overall research design is free of look-ahead bias.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Using only lagged features does not guarantee a leak-free design — leakage can still enter via splitting, preprocessing, labels, or universe construction.", "whyPool": "Leakage can enter through splitting, preprocessing, labels, universe construction, or other pipeline steps."}], "reference": "Look-ahead bias & chronological splits — Investopedia \"Look-Ahead Bias\"; Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial Machine Learning*, ch. 7 (cross-validation in finance).", "n": 477}, {"id": "QF-D9G1Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G1", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "For each trading day, an analyst creates a feature equal to that day's closing price minus the average closing price over a centered 21-day window: the preceding 10 days, the current day, and the following 10 days. The signal is used to place a trade at that day's close.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The feature contains prices that were unavailable at the trade time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Ten *future* closing prices enter the feature that drives the current trade — those prices were unavailable at trade time.", "whyPool": "Ten future closing prices enter the feature used for the current decision. 394 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "The centered moving average introduces look-ahead bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The centered calculation lets future information affect a past decision, the definition of look-ahead bias.", "whyPool": "The calculation allows future information to affect a past trade."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Replacing the centered window with a trailing window cannot remove this particular use of future prices.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: replacing the centered window with a *trailing* window uses only information available by the decision time, so it *does* remove this use of future prices.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A trailing window can be computed from information available by the decision time."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because all prices eventually became historical, the original backtest is valid.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Later availability is irrelevant — prices that only became historical afterward were not knowable at the original trade time, so the backtest is not valid.", "whyPool": "Later availability does not make the information available at the historical trade time."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Even a trailing calculation must be shifted if the day's close was not known when the trade was executed.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Even a trailing calculation must be shifted/lagged if the day's close was not yet known when the simulated order was placed; feature timing must match execution timing.", "whyPool": "Feature timing must match the exact time at which the simulated order could have been placed."}], "reference": "Look-ahead bias in feature construction — Investopedia \"Look-Ahead Bias\"; Lopez de Prado, *AFML*, ch. 3 (labeling) & ch. 7.", "n": 478}, {"id": "QF-D9G1Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G1", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A model predicts five-day forward returns. Each label uses returns from day t+1 through day t+5. The researcher ends the training sample on June 30 and begins the test sample on July 1, without removing overlapping labels near the boundary.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A training label dated June 30 can contain returns realized during the July test period.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A June 30 label's five-day forward window extends into July, so a training label literally contains test-period returns.", "whyPool": "Its five-day forward window extends into July."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The calendar boundary alone guarantees complete separation of the economic information in training and testing.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The calendar boundary alone does not separate the economic information — label windows can cross the nominal boundary.", "whyPool": "Label windows can cross the nominal boundary."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Purging boundary observations whose label windows overlap the test period can reduce leakage.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Purging boundary observations whose label windows overlap the test period prevents test-period outcomes from leaking into training labels.", "whyPool": "Purging prevents test-period outcomes from entering training labels."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Longer forward-return horizons cannot require a wider separation around the train/test boundary.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: longer forward horizons create *longer* overlap regions, so they *do* require wider separation around the boundary.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Longer horizons create longer regions of potential overlap."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Shuffling the rows after labels are constructed would solve the overlap problem.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Shuffling rows only changes order; the shared future-return information in overlapping labels remains, so it does not solve the overlap.", "whyPool": "Shuffling changes row order but does not remove shared future-return information. G2: Survivorship and Selection Bias"}], "reference": "Purging/embargo for overlapping labels — Lopez de Prado, *AFML*, ch. 7 (purged k-fold CV); Investopedia \"Look-Ahead Bias.\"", "n": 479}, {"id": "QF-D9G2Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G2", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher backtests a strategy on the current members of a major stock index from 2005 through 2025. Firms that were removed from the index or went bankrupt are not included. The resulting strategy appears unusually strong.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The test may suffer from survivorship bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The sample contains only firms that survived into the present universe — the textbook setup for survivorship bias.", "whyPool": "The sample contains only firms that survived to the present universe."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Excluding failed firms can overstate historical performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Systematically omitting failed firms drops the poor outcomes, inflating measured historical performance.", "whyPool": "Poor outcomes are systematically omitted."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Using today’s index membership recreates the investment universe that was available in 2005. 395 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Today's membership was not known or available in 2005, so it does not recreate the historical opportunity set.", "whyPool": "Today’s membership was not known or available in 2005."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A point-in-time constituent database would improve the research design.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A point-in-time constituent database reconstructs who was actually in the index at each date, improving the design.", "whyPool": "Point-in-time membership better reconstructs the historical opportunity set."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Survivorship bias cannot affect both return estimates and apparent risk.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: omitting failures can distort *both* return estimates *and* apparent risk (understating losses and volatility).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Omitting failures can understate losses and volatility."}], "reference": "Survivorship bias — Investopedia \"Survivorship Bias\"; CFA curriculum (backtesting / data biases).", "n": 480}, {"id": "QF-D9G2Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G2", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An analyst downloads returns for hedge funds that are operating today and studies their performance back to each fund's inception. Funds that closed before the download date do not appear in the database.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The sample conditions on funds surviving until today.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Requiring current operation conditions the sample on survival to today.", "whyPool": "Current operation is a survival requirement for inclusion."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Poorly performing funds cannot be more likely to disappear from the observed sample.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: poorly performing funds *are* more likely to close and disappear, making the missingness systematic, not random.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Closure can be related to poor results, making the missingness systematic."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The surviving funds' average historical return may exceed the average an investor actually experienced across all available funds.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Omitting failed funds raises the measured average return and suppresses observed downside relative to what a real investor faced.", "whyPool": "Omitted failures can raise the measured average and suppress observed downside."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Adding more years of return history for the same surviving funds necessarily removes the bias.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Adding more history for the *same* surviving funds does not restore the missing (dead) funds, so it does not remove the bias.", "whyPool": "More observations from the same selected survivors do not restore missing funds."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A database containing dead funds and their final returns would not support a less biased analysis.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: a database that includes dead funds and their final returns *does* support a less biased analysis.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Dead-fund histories help reconstruct the opportunity set investors actually faced. 396 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Survivorship bias in fund data — Investopedia \"Survivorship Bias\"; CFA curriculum (hedge-fund performance databases).", "n": 481}, {"id": "QF-D9G2Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G2", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "After inspecting results in 20 countries over 30 years, a researcher reports a value strategy only for the three countries and one decade in which it performed best. The paper does not disclose how those markets and dates were chosen.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Choosing the sample after observing results creates selection bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Choosing which evidence to present *after* seeing outcomes is selection bias — the result influenced the sample.", "whyPool": "The outcome influenced which evidence was presented."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The reported performance may overstate how well the strategy would work in a market and period selected in advance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Cherry-picking winners from many candidates favors unusually strong realized results, overstating pre-specified performance.", "whyPool": "Selecting winners from many candidates favors unusually strong realized results."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A strong result within the chosen subsample proves the strategy is universal.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A favorable selected subsample does not establish general validity; it may be luck from a wide search.", "whyPool": "A favorable selected subsample does not establish general validity."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Reporting the complete search process would not help readers assess the evidence.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: disclosing the full search *does* help readers gauge how many opportunities produced the apparent winner.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Disclosure reveals the number of opportunities from which the apparent winner emerged."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Testing a pre-specified rule on untouched countries or later dates could provide useful validation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Testing the pre-specified rule on untouched countries or later dates checks whether the finding generalizes.", "whyPool": "Untouched data can test whether the finding generalizes beyond the selected sample. G3: Data Snooping and Multiple Testing"}], "reference": "Selection/data-snooping bias — Investopedia \"Data Snooping\"; Bailey & Lopez de Prado (backtest overfitting; multiple trials).", "n": 482}, {"id": "QF-D9G3Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G3", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A research team tests 1,000 signals that have no true predictive power. At a 5% significance threshold, it retains every signal with a statistically significant result.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Even when every null hypothesis is true, approximately 50 significant results may arise by chance on average.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Under the simplified assumption, 5% of 1,000 true nulls ≈ 50 false positives arise by chance.", "whyPool": "Five percent of 1,000 null tests is 50 expected false positives under the simplified assumptions. 397 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "B", "text": "Selecting the best result from 1,000 trials is equivalent to testing one strategy specified in advance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Selecting the best of 1,000 trials is *not* equivalent to testing one pre-specified strategy — the search vastly raises the chance of a lucky winner.", "whyPool": "The search across many candidates greatly increases the opportunity to find a chance winner."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Multiple-testing adjustments cannot reduce the rate of false discoveries.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: multiple-testing adjustments *do* reduce false discoveries by accounting for the number/structure of hypotheses tested.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Adjustments account for the number or structure of hypotheses examined."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The retained signals should be evaluated on data that did not participate in their selection.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Retained signals must be evaluated on data that did not participate in their selection (independent validation).", "whyPool": "Independent validation tests performance after the selection process."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A p-value below 0.05 for one selected signal proves that the signal has durable economic value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A single p<0.05 on a selected signal can be spurious; it does not prove persistence, magnitude, or profitability after costs.", "whyPool": "Significance can be spurious and does not establish persistence, magnitude, or profitability after costs."}], "reference": "Multiple testing / deflated Sharpe — Bailey & Lopez de Prado, \"The Probability of Backtest Overfitting\"; Investopedia \"Data Snooping.\"", "n": 483}, {"id": "QF-D9G3Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G3", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A developer checks a strategy against the same nominal holdout set, changes parameters after every disappointing result, and reruns the test until the Sharpe ratio looks attractive.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The holdout set is not gradually becoming part of the model-development process.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: the holdout *is* becoming part of model development, because its outcomes are guiding design choices.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Test outcomes are directly guiding design choices."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Repeated feedback from the holdout can cause overfitting to that dataset.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Repeated feedback lets the developer fit dataset-specific noise — overfitting to the holdout.", "whyPool": "The developer can learn dataset-specific noise through repeated experimentation."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Calling the data a test set preserves its independence regardless of how often it guides revisions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Labeling data a \"test set\" does not preserve independence; functional use (guiding revisions) determines it.", "whyPool": "Functional use, not the label assigned to the data, determines independence."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A final evaluation on an untouched dataset would provide a more credible estimate of generalization.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A final evaluation on a truly untouched dataset gives a more credible generalization estimate.", "whyPool": "Untouched observations have not supplied feedback used to tune the strategy."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Keeping a log of all attempted variants would make the extent of the search more transparent.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Logging all attempted variants makes the extent of the search (multiple testing / researcher degrees of freedom) transparent.", "whyPool": "The research record helps reveal multiple testing and researcher degrees of freedom."}], "reference": "Data-snooping / p-hacking — Investopedia \"Data Snooping\"; Bailey & Lopez de Prado (backtest overfitting).", "n": 484}, {"id": "QF-D9G3Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G3", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A strategy was selected because it had the highest Sharpe ratio among 200 variants tested on U.S. equities. The team next evaluates the fixed specification, without retuning it, on later U.S. data and on several international markets.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The original maximum Sharpe ratio is not subject to a winner's-curse effect.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: the max Sharpe of many noisy estimates *is* subject to a winner's curse — the best of many overstates true performance.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The best of many noisy estimates is likely to overstate its underlying performance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Freezing the specification before further testing reduces additional selection on the validation data.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Freezing the specification before further testing prevents validation outcomes from being turned into more tuning (avoids selection on validation data).", "whyPool": "A fixed rule prevents validation outcomes from immediately being converted into more tuning choices."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Failure in every new dataset would strengthen the claim that the original effect is robust.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Failure in every new dataset would *weaken*, not strengthen, the robustness claim — it suggests the original result did not generalize.", "whyPool": "Broad failure would suggest that the selected historical result did not generalize."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Success across later periods and different markets would not be more persuasive than the selected in-sample result alone. 398 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: success across later periods and different markets *is* more persuasive than the selected in-sample result alone (replication evidence).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Replication beyond the selection sample provides additional evidence of persistence."}, {"L": "E", "text": "International validation is informative only if differences in market structure and implementation are considered.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "International validation is informative only if market-structure, cost, instrument, and implementation differences are considered.", "whyPool": "Costs, instruments, rules, and data quality can differ across markets and affect comparability. G4: Point-in-Time Data and Revisions"}], "reference": "Winner's curse / out-of-sample replication — Bailey & Lopez de Prado (backtest overfitting); CFA curriculum (out-of-sample testing).", "n": 485}, {"id": "QF-D9G4Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G4", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A backtest run in 2026 uses a vendor's current historical earnings-per-share series. The series incorporates corporate restatements that were issued years after the original financial reports. The strategy assumes every revised value was known on the original quarter-end date.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The backtest can contain information that investors did not possess on the simulated decision date.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Treating later restatements as historically known injects information investors did not have on the decision date.", "whyPool": "Later restatements are being treated as if they were historically known."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Restated data can differ from the values originally reported.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Restated data can differ from the values originally reported.", "whyPool": "Restatements can change previously published accounting values."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Assigning a value to the quarter it describes is always equivalent to assigning it to the date investors could first observe it.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The economic reference period (the quarter described) and the public availability date are distinct, so assigning a value to its quarter ≠ assigning it to when it was first observable.", "whyPool": "The economic reference period and public availability date are distinct."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A point-in-time database should preserve original releases, revisions, and their availability dates.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A point-in-time database preserves original releases, revisions, and availability dates so the researcher can reconstruct what was knowable at each date.", "whyPool": "Those fields allow the researcher to reconstruct what was knowable at each date."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Using the cleanest currently available series necessarily produces the most realistic historical simulation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The cleanest current series may be accurate retrospectively but unrealistic for contemporaneous decisions, so it does not necessarily give the most realistic simulation.", "whyPool": "A corrected series may be accurate retrospectively but unrealistic for contemporaneous decisions. 399 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Point-in-time data & restatements — Investopedia \"Point-in-Time Data\"; CFA curriculum (data preparation / look-ahead bias).", "n": 486}, {"id": "QF-D9G4Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G4", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A strategy ranks companies using annual financial statements. Fiscal year ends on December 31, reports are released on March 15, and the backtest first trades on January 2 using the completed fiscal-year figures.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The January 2 trade uses figures before their stated release date.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The figures were not released until March 15, so a Jan 2 trade uses them before their stated release date.", "whyPool": "The figures were not released until March 15."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The two-and-a-half-month publication lag is not economically relevant to the backtest.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: the ~2.5-month publication lag *is* economically relevant — the strategy cannot use a disclosure while it remained unavailable.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The strategy cannot use a disclosure during the period in which it remained unavailable."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that delaying first use of the figures until they were publicly available would improve temporal validity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Embedded double-negative: the inner claim (delaying first use until public availability improves temporal validity) is *correct and should be accepted*, so saying a reviewer \"would be wrong to accept\" it is false.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. This aligns the simulated decision with information availability."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because the figures describe the year ending December 31, investors must have known them on December 31.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The measurement period (year ending Dec 31) does not determine the publication date, so investors did not know the figures on Dec 31.", "whyPool": "The measurement period does not determine the publication date."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A conservative additional processing delay may be appropriate if the strategy could not trade instantaneously at release.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A conservative extra processing/execution delay is appropriate because the strategy could not trade instantaneously at release.", "whyPool": "Realistic ingestion, analysis, and execution timing can matter even after release."}], "reference": "Publication lag / availability dates — Investopedia \"Look-Ahead Bias\" & \"Point-in-Time Data\"; CFA curriculum (data timing).", "n": 487}, {"id": "QF-D9G4Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G4", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A macroeconomic strategy uses the latest available historical GDP-growth series to trade on every original announcement date. Several past growth estimates were materially revised after those trades would have occurred.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The latest-vintage series may embed revisions unavailable on the original announcement dates.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The latest-vintage series may embed revisions that were unavailable on the original announcement dates.", "whyPool": "Today's history can differ from the sequence observed in real time."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A real-time vintage database cannot show the values actually published at each historical date.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: a real-time *vintage* database is precisely what preserves the values actually published at each historical date.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Vintage data preserve successive releases and revision dates."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Macro data revisions can change both a model's inputs and the apparent historical strength of its signal.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Macro revisions can change both the model's inputs and the apparent historical strength of the signal.", "whyPool": "Revised values may improve apparent measurement and relationships retrospectively."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Revision bias is impossible because GDP data are public.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Public data can still be revised after the historical decision point, so \"public\" does not make revision bias impossible.", "whyPool": "Public data can still be revised after the historical decision point."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Comparing results based on initial releases and later revised values can reveal sensitivity to data vintage.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing initial-release vs. later-revised results reveals whether the backtest depends on hindsight-cleaned data (vintage sensitivity).", "whyPool": "The comparison shows whether the backtest depends on hindsight-cleaned observations. G5: Corporate Actions and Price Adjustments"}], "reference": "Real-time/vintage macro data — Investopedia \"Point-in-Time Data\"; CFA curriculum (data revisions).", "n": 488}, {"id": "QF-D9G5Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G5", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A stock closes at $100 and undergoes a 2-for-1 split overnight. It opens near $50, with each pre-split share replaced by two post-split shares. A program using unadjusted prices records a -50% return.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The apparent price decline does not represent a 50% economic loss to the shareholder.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Two shares near $50 replace one near $100, so the apparent decline is not a 50% economic loss to the shareholder.", "whyPool": "Two shares near $50 replace one share near $100."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Ignoring the change in share count creates a false return signal.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Ignoring the change in share count mistakes a unit-of-account change for a real loss — a false return signal.", "whyPool": "The program mistakes a unit-of-account change for a loss."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A properly split-adjusted series should not remove this mechanical discontinuity.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: a properly split-adjusted series *should* remove this mechanical discontinuity by placing pre/post prices on a comparable basis.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Adjustment places pre- and post-split prices on a comparable basis."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The shareholder's position value is not approximately unchanged by the split alone.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: position value *is* approximately unchanged — shares double while per-share price roughly halves.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The number of shares doubles while the per-share price approximately halves."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The split necessarily doubles the company's total market capitalization.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Shares outstanding and price adjust inversely, so market cap is ~unchanged (absent other news), not doubled.", "whyPool": "Shares outstanding and price adjust inversely, leaving market value approximately unchanged absent other news."}], "reference": "Corporate-action (split) adjustment — Investopedia \"Adjusted Closing Price\" / stock splits; CFA curriculum (return calculation).", "n": 489}, {"id": "QF-D9G5Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G5", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A stock closes at $50, pays a $2 cash dividend, and closes the next day at $48 with no other price-moving event. One backtest uses price returns only; another includes the cash dividend.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The price-only return is not -4%. 401 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: the price-only return *is* −4% = ($48 − $50)/$50.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The price change is ($48 - $50)/$50 = -4%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The shareholder's total return is approximately 0% before taxes and costs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Total return ≈ 0%: ($48 + $2 − $50)/$50, since the $2 dividend offsets the $2 price decline (before taxes/costs).", "whyPool": "The $2 dividend offsets the $2 price decline: ($48 + $2 - $50)/$50 = 0%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Treating the ex-dividend price drop as a pure investment loss understates total return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Treating the ex-dividend price drop as a pure loss ignores the cash received, understating total return.", "whyPool": "It ignores cash received by the shareholder."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A dividend-adjusted total-return series is useful when evaluating an investor who receives and retains or reinvests distributions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A dividend-adjusted total-return series is appropriate when the investor receives and retains or reinvests distributions.", "whyPool": "Such a series accounts for distributions as part of investment performance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Dividend adjustments and split adjustments address exactly the same cash flow.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Dividend adjustments (a cash distribution) and split adjustments (a change in share units, no cash) address different actions.", "whyPool": "A dividend is a distribution; a split changes share units without distributing cash."}], "reference": "Total-return vs. price return, dividend adjustment — Investopedia \"Total Return\" / \"Adjusted Closing Price\"; CFA curriculum (return measurement).", "n": 490}, {"id": "QF-D9G5Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G5", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A distressed stock last trades at $4 and is then delisted. A dataset ends the return history at the last exchange price and assigns no subsequent delisting return. The investor actually receives only $0.50 per share in liquidation.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Ending the series at $4 omits a substantial investor loss.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Ending the series at $4 omits the substantial further loss down to the $0.50 recovery.", "whyPool": "The investor ultimately recovers far less than the last recorded exchange price."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The economic return from $4 to the $0.50 recovery is not -87.5%.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: the economic return *is* −87.5% = ($0.50 − $4.00)/$4.00.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. ($0.50 - $4.00)/$4.00 = -87.5%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Omitting adverse delisting outcomes can create survivorship-related upward bias.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Omitting adverse delisting outcomes creates survivorship-related upward bias, since disappearing securities often have poor terminal outcomes.", "whyPool": "Disappearing securities often have poor terminal outcomes that affect measured returns."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Because the security stopped exchange trading, its terminal value is irrelevant to portfolio performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The terminal value the investor realizes remains part of portfolio performance even after exchange trading stops.", "whyPool": "The value ultimately realized remains part of the investor's result."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Researchers should not investigate delisting codes, cash recoveries, and terminal returns rather than automatically treating the last quote as full value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth (double-negative): researchers *should* investigate delisting codes, cash recoveries, and terminal returns rather than treating the last quote as full value.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Correct treatment depends on the event and the value received after delisting. 402 Rev:081020261230 G6: Missing, Stale, and Irregular Data"}], "reference": "Delisting returns & survivorship — Investopedia \"Survivorship Bias\" / delisting; CFA curriculum (data handling).", "n": 491}, {"id": "QF-D9G6Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G6", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An illiquid bond last traded at $100 on Monday. It does not trade Tuesday through Thursday, so a dataset forward-fills $100 for all three days while comparable bonds decline. The bond trades Friday at $94.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The filled series reports zero daily returns for Tuesday through Thursday.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Repeating $100 mechanically produces zero recorded daily returns for Tue–Thu.", "whyPool": "Repeating the same value mechanically creates zero recorded changes."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The $100 observations prove the bond's tradable market value remained constant on those days.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Absence of a trade does not prove the tradable market value stayed constant — the filled price is not evidence of unchanged value.", "whyPool": "Absence of a trade does not prove absence of a change in economic value."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Forward filling cannot defer the observed price change until Friday.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: forward-filling *does* defer the accumulated price change until Friday, when a new transaction price arrives.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The accumulated adjustment appears when a new transaction price arrives."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The filled series can understate measured daily volatility and autocorrelation patterns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Artificial zero returns followed by a jump distort measured daily volatility and autocorrelation.", "whyPool": "Artificial zero returns followed by a jump distort the return process."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A stale quote can be different from a price at which the full position could actually have traded.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A stale quote can differ from a price at which the full position could actually trade (executable value depends on liquidity, spreads, depth).", "whyPool": "Executable value depends on current liquidity, spreads, depth, and market conditions."}], "reference": "Stale prices / forward-filling — Investopedia \"Stale Price\"; Lopez de Prado, *AFML* (data structuring / illiquidity).", "n": 492}, {"id": "QF-D9G6Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G6", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher compares daily returns of a U.S. ETF and a foreign index. On a U.S. holiday, the ETF does not trade but the foreign market does. The researcher fills the ETF price with its prior close and treats both same-calendar-date returns as synchronized.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The filled ETF return for the holiday is not mechanically zero.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: the unchanged filled price *does* create a mechanical zero return for the holiday.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The unchanged filled price creates a zero recorded return."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Different trading calendars cannot create nonsynchronous observations.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: different trading calendars *do* create non-synchronous observations (one market open, one closed).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. One market is open while the other is closed."}, {"L": "C", "text": "The apparent same-day relationship may be distorted because the two returns cover different information intervals.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The apparent same-day relationship is distorted because the two returns cover different information intervals (news incorporated at different times).", "whyPool": "News is incorporated at different times across the two recorded returns."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Forward filling guarantees an unbiased correlation estimate.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Forward filling does not guarantee an unbiased correlation — artificial zeros and timing mismatch bias it.", "whyPool": "Artificial zeros and timing mismatch can bias correlation."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Aligning common trading intervals or explicitly modeling lead-lag timing could improve the analysis.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Aligning common trading intervals or explicitly modeling lead-lag timing improves the analysis.", "whyPool": "The research design should account for calendar and market-hour differences."}], "reference": "Non-synchronous trading / calendar mismatch — Investopedia \"Stale Price\" / non-synchronous data; CFA curriculum (data alignment).", "n": 493}, {"id": "QF-D9G6Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G6", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Some firms omit a newly requested disclosure when their results are poor. A researcher replaces every missing value with the cross-sectional mean and then treats missingness as random.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The missingness may contain information about firm performance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Because poor performers are described as more likely to omit the value, the missingness itself carries information about firm performance.", "whyPool": "Firms with poor results are described as being more likely to omit the value."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Mean imputation can weaken or conceal the relationship between the disclosure and poor outcomes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Replacing systematically poor observations with an average attenuates or hides the relationship between the disclosure and poor outcomes.", "whyPool": "Replacing systematically poor observations with an average can attenuate the signal."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Imputation creates genuine observations of what the firms would have reported.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Imputed values are modeling choices, not genuine observations of what the firms would have reported.", "whyPool": "Imputed values are modeling choices, not observed disclosures."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Adding a missingness indicator may help test whether missing records have distinct outcomes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A missingness indicator can capture the information carried by the absence itself, letting you test whether missing records have distinct outcomes.", "whyPool": "The indicator can capture information carried by the absence itself."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Sensitivity analyses using alternative missing-data assumptions cannot improve research credibility.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: sensitivity analyses under alternative missing-data assumptions *do* improve credibility by showing how much conclusions depend on the imputation rule.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Robustness checks show how strongly conclusions depend on the imputation rule. G7: Nonstationarity and Regime Change"}], "reference": "Missing-not-at-random / imputation bias — CFA curriculum (data cleaning / missing data); Investopedia (data-cleaning bias).", "n": 494}, {"id": "QF-D9G7Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G7", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A model estimates that a stock's beta was 0.8 during a long low-volatility period. After a major acquisition, the company doubles its leverage and changes industries. The risk system continues using the old estimate indefinitely.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The historical beta need not remain valid after the company's economic exposure changes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Beta depends on business and financial exposures that can change, so the historical estimate need not stay valid.", "whyPool": "Beta depends on business and financial exposures that can change."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The acquisition and leverage change can represent a structural break. 404 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A major acquisition and leverage change can be a structural break — the return-generating process may differ before/after.", "whyPool": "The return-generating process may differ before and after the event."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A longer pre-acquisition sample guarantees a more accurate post-acquisition beta.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "More observations from the *old* regime make an estimate precise for a relationship that no longer applies — not more accurate for the new regime.", "whyPool": "More observations from the old regime can make an estimate precise for a relationship that no longer applies."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Rolling estimates and stability tests cannot help detect changing exposure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: rolling estimates and stability/break tests *can* help detect changing exposure.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Time-varying estimates and break tests can reveal instability."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Parameter uncertainty should not be considered when using historical relationships for future hedging.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: parameter uncertainty *should* be considered, since hedge performance is sensitive to error and drift in the estimated beta.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Hedge performance is sensitive to error and drift in the estimated beta."}], "reference": "Nonstationarity / structural breaks in beta — CFA curriculum (nonstationarity, rolling regressions); Investopedia \"Nonstationary.\"", "n": 495}, {"id": "QF-D9G7Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G7", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A short-volatility strategy performs steadily from 2012–2019, suffers a severe loss in 2020, recovers during later calm markets, and is proposed for deployment because its full-sample average return remains positive.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A positive full-sample average can conceal materially different regime-specific risks.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A positive full-sample average can conceal materially different regime-specific risks (aggregation masks tail losses).", "whyPool": "Aggregation can mask tail losses and shifts in the return distribution."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The 2020 loss may contain important evidence about the strategy's exposure to stressed conditions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The 2020 loss is important evidence about the strategy's exposure to stressed conditions (short-vol accumulates gains but is exposed to rare shocks).", "whyPool": "Short-volatility strategies can accumulate gains while remaining exposed to rare large shocks."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Removing 2020 solely because it is unfavorable necessarily improves the validity of the estimate.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Removing 2020 solely because it is unfavorable is selection bias — excluding an economically relevant regime does not improve validity.", "whyPool": "Excluding an adverse but economically relevant regime can create selection bias."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Stress testing and reporting performance by regime would provide information beyond the overall mean.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stress testing and per-regime reporting provide information beyond the overall mean (whether the edge depends on a particular environment).", "whyPool": "Conditional analysis reveals whether the strategy depends on a particular environment."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Recovery in calm markets proves that future crisis losses cannot recur.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Recovery in calm markets does not prove future crisis losses cannot recur — the underlying exposure remains.", "whyPool": "Later recovery does not eliminate the underlying exposure or guarantee future conditions. 405 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Regime-conditional / stress analysis — CFA curriculum (scenario & stress testing); Lopez de Prado, *AFML* (strategy risk under regimes).", "n": 496}, {"id": "QF-D9G7Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "09", "domainName": "Financial Data & Research Bias", "group": "G7", "weeks": [8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A mean-reversion relationship was stable when transaction costs were high and few firms traded it. After market structure changes and widespread adoption of similar algorithms, deviations become smaller and reverse faster than the strategy can execute.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Changes in market structure and competitor behavior cannot cause strategy decay.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the truth: changes in market structure and competitor behavior *can* cause strategy decay by altering available opportunities.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The trading environment and actions of other participants affect available opportunities."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A relationship that existed historically must remain stationary after it becomes widely exploited.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A historically existing relationship need *not* remain stationary once widely exploited — crowding and institutional change can remove it.", "whyPool": "Exploitation and institutional changes can alter or remove historical patterns."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Smaller, faster deviations can eliminate net profitability even if some mean reversion remains.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Smaller, faster deviations can eliminate net profitability after costs even if some mean reversion remains (the gross edge is too small/brief to capture).", "whyPool": "The remaining gross opportunity may be too small or too brief to capture after costs."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Monitoring live feature distributions, execution quality, and performance can help identify drift.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Monitoring live feature distributions, execution quality, and performance helps detect drift between training and deployment.", "whyPool": "These diagnostics can reveal differences between training conditions and deployment."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Retraining a model automatically guarantees adaptation to every new regime.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Retraining does not *guarantee* adaptation — it cannot ensure useful predictive structure exists or that the new sample represents future regimes.", "whyPool": "Retraining cannot guarantee that useful predictive structure exists or that the new sample represents future regimes. 406 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Strategy decay / nonstationarity & crowding — Lopez de Prado, *AFML* (backtesting, strategy decay); CFA curriculum (nonstationarity).", "n": 497}, {"id": "QF-D10G1Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G1", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A quant team finds that stocks with unusually high social-media mentions outperform over the next day. The portfolio manager asks whether the pattern justifies a strategy, but requires an economic explanation and evidence that the data would have been available before trading.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The observed correlation alone proves that social-media activity causes the subsequent returns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Observed correlation does not prove causation — it can arise from confounding variables, reverse causality, data leakage (look-ahead), or pure chance.", "whyPool": "Correlation can arise from confounding, reverse causality, data leakage, or chance."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A defensible hypothesis should explain why mentions might predict returns and when the relationship should weaken or reverse.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A useful investment hypothesis identifies a plausible mechanism (why mentions might move prices) and the conditions under which the effect should weaken or reverse.", "whyPool": "A useful investment hypothesis identifies a plausible mechanism and conditions governing the effect."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Testing whether the effect survives controls for news, liquidity, and recent returns can help distinguish the proposed mechanism from alternatives.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Testing whether the effect survives controls for news, liquidity, and recent returns checks whether a known alternative influence better explains the relationship.", "whyPool": "Relevant controls test whether another known influence better explains the relationship."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The team should define the signal and trading rule before using an untouched sample for validation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Freezing the signal definition and trading rule *before* validating on an untouched sample reduces selection/tuning bias on the evaluation data.", "whyPool": "Freezing the design before validation reduces selection and tuning on the evaluation data."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A statistically significant relationship necessarily remains profitable after transaction costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Statistical significance establishes neither sufficient economic magnitude nor net-of-cost profitability; a real, significant edge can still be uneconomic after costs.", "whyPool": "Statistical evidence does not establish sufficient economic magnitude or net profitability."}], "reference": "Correlation vs. causation and multiple-testing/leakage in strategy research — Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (backtesting pitfalls); Investopedia \"Backtesting\" and \"Data mining bias.\"", "n": 498}, {"id": "QF-D10G1Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G1", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "An asset manager believes that slow-moving institutional demand creates medium-term price continuation. The research director asks the team to translate the idea into a measurable signal without merely choosing whichever lookback performed best historically.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A past-return measure over a stated lookback could not operationalize the direction of the proposed continuation effect.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: a defined past-return feature over a stated lookback *is* one valid operationalization of the direction of a continuation effect.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. A defined past-return feature is one measurable representation of price continuation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The hypothesis predicts that stronger recent performance should always create a more negative position.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Continuation implies stronger recent performance maps toward a *more positive* forecast/position, not a more negative one.", "whyPool": "Continuation implies that stronger recent performance generally maps toward a more positive, not more negative, forecast."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Trading volume or fund-flow measures could be tested as evidence related to the proposed demand mechanism. 409 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Volume or fund-flow measures can provide corroborating evidence that persistent institutional demand is actually present (supports the mechanism).", "whyPool": "These variables may provide evidence about whether persistent demand is actually present."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Selecting the lookback solely because it maximizes full-sample performance increases overfitting risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Choosing the lookback solely because it maximizes full-sample performance capitalizes on sample-specific noise — classic overfitting.", "whyPool": "Choosing the best result after searching the whole history capitalizes on sample-specific noise."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Pre-specifying expected behavior in liquid versus illiquid securities cannot make the hypothesis more falsifiable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: pre-specifying expected liquid-vs-illiquid behavior creates conditional predictions that make the hypothesis *more* falsifiable.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Conditional predictions create evidence that can support or contradict the proposed mechanism."}], "reference": "Overfitting from parameter search and falsifiable, mechanism-driven signals — Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (selection bias under multiple testing); Investopedia \"Overfitting.\"", "n": 499}, {"id": "QF-D10G1Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G1", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher reports that firms with ticker symbols beginning with letters A–M outperformed N–Z firms in one historical sample. The chief investment officer will fund further work only if the team can explain the effect and demonstrate that it is not a chance partition.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The alphabetical pattern is automatically an investment hypothesis because it separates firms into two groups.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A partition that merely splits firms into two groups is not an investment hypothesis; a measurable correlation is not by itself a defensible explanation of expected returns.", "whyPool": "A measurable correlation is not by itself a defensible explanation of expected returns."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Searching many arbitrary partitions can produce an apparently strong result by chance.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Searching many arbitrary partitions produces apparently strong results by chance — the multiple-testing / data-snooping problem.", "whyPool": "Multiple testing creates many opportunities to discover spurious relationships."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Stability across different periods would be more persuasive than performance in the discovery sample alone.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Stability across different time periods (temporal replication) is more persuasive than performance confined to the discovery sample.", "whyPool": "Temporal replication helps assess whether the finding generalizes beyond its selected sample."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A plausible channel linking the feature to expected returns would strengthen the case for continued research.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A plausible economic channel supports conditional predictions and helps distinguish genuine signal from coincidence.", "whyPool": "A mechanism supports conditional predictions and helps distinguish signal from coincidence."}, {"L": "E", "text": "If the pattern has no economic rationale, increasing leverage is an appropriate substitute for validation.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Leverage magnifies exposure; it creates no evidence that a signal without economic rationale is genuine — it is not a substitute for validation.", "whyPool": "Leverage magnifies exposure; it does not create evidence that the signal is genuine. 410 Rev:081020261230 G2: Forecasts, Positions, and Trades"}], "reference": "Data-snooping / multiple-testing bias and the deflated Sharpe ratio — Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (deflated Sharpe, backtest overfitting); Investopedia \"Data mining bias.\"", "n": 500}, {"id": "QF-D10G2Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G2", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A model predicts next-day returns of +0.04%, +0.01%, and -0.03% for three stocks. The trader estimates a 0.02% round- trip cost for each and asks whether every nonzero forecast should become a trade.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The sign of a forecast can help determine position direction, but it does not alone determine whether to trade.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A forecast's sign helps set direction, but the decision to trade also depends on magnitude, confidence, costs, risk, and portfolio constraints.", "whyPool": "Action also depends on magnitude, confidence, costs, risk, and portfolio constraints."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Before considering risk, the +0.01% forecast is not smaller than its estimated round-trip cost.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: the +0.01% expected gross edge *is* smaller than the 0.02% round-trip cost, so it does not clear costs.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The expected gross edge is 0.01%, compared with a 0.02% cost estimate."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A no-trade threshold cannot prevent forecasts too small to cover expected costs from generating positions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: a no-trade threshold *can* prevent sub-cost forecasts from generating positions by reserving trades for sufficient expected net value.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Thresholding can reserve trades for signals with sufficient expected net value."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Forecast magnitude and uncertainty can both be inputs to position sizing.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Both forecast magnitude and its uncertainty are valid inputs to position sizing — larger, more reliable forecasts can justify more exposure.", "whyPool": "Larger and more reliable forecasts may justify different exposure than weak or uncertain ones."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Because the first forecast is positive, a long position is guaranteed to earn a profit.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A forecast is uncertain; realized returns can differ from predictions, so a positive forecast does not guarantee a profit.", "whyPool": "A forecast is uncertain and realized returns can differ from its predicted value."}], "reference": "Net-edge thresholds and cost-aware sizing — Grinold & Kahn, *Active Portfolio Management* (signal-to-trade, forecasting); Investopedia \"Transaction costs.\"", "n": 501}, {"id": "QF-D10G2Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G2", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A classifier correctly predicts whether the market will close up or down on 60% of days. The portfolio manager notes that its correct calls tend to occur on tiny moves while several incorrect calls coincide with large market declines.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Directional accuracy alone is sufficient to establish trading profitability.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Directional accuracy treats small and large outcomes alike and ignores costs and sizing, so it is not sufficient to establish profitability.", "whyPool": "Accuracy treats small and large outcomes alike and ignores costs and sizing."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The magnitude of returns on correct and incorrect days affects strategy P&L.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Trading P&L depends on the signed size of each realized outcome, not merely the count of correct calls.", "whyPool": "Trading P&L depends on the signed size of each realized outcome, not only the number correct."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A 60% hit rate can coexist with a negative average trading return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A 60% hit rate can coexist with a negative average return when a few large losses outweigh many small gains.", "whyPool": "A few large losses can outweigh many small gains."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Position sizing that ignores forecast confidence and downside exposure cannot worsen the large-error problem.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: sizing that ignores confidence and downside exposure *can* worsen the large-error problem by over-allocating to uncertain, high-consequence calls.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Equal or aggressive sizing can assign too much capital to uncertain, high-consequence predictions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Evaluating a return-weighted objective can provide information that classification accuracy omits.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A return-weighted objective reflects economic magnitude more directly than hit rate, capturing information classification accuracy omits.", "whyPool": "Return-based evaluation reflects economic magnitudes more directly than hit rate."}], "reference": "Return-weighted vs. accuracy objectives — Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (labeling, meta-labeling, sample weights); Investopedia \"Win rate vs. risk/reward.\"", "n": 502}, {"id": "QF-D10G2Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G2", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A market-neutral fund converts stock-return forecasts into positions proportional to predicted return. The risk manager observes that the model repeatedly assigns its largest positions to volatile, thinly traded stocks and requires a safer construction rule.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Raw forecast-proportional sizing can concentrate risk in securities with large forecast magnitudes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Raw forecast-proportional sizing mechanically gives large weights to large-magnitude forecasts, concentrating risk unless additional controls intervene.", "whyPool": "Large predictions mechanically receive large weights unless additional controls intervene."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Scaling forecasts by volatility cannot help make position risk more comparable across securities.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: scaling forecasts by volatility *does* help — it lowers the allocation to a high-risk security for a given forecast, equalizing risk across names.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Volatility scaling reduces the allocation to a high-risk security for a given forecast."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Liquidity and position limits can constrain trades even when a forecast is strong.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Liquidity and position limits can constrain trades even when a forecast is strong (tradability/concentration override the raw signal).", "whyPool": "Tradability and concentration constraints can override an unconstrained signal mapping."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Dollar neutrality guarantees neutrality to every market, sector, and factor exposure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Dollar neutrality (equal long/short dollars) does not guarantee neutrality to beta, sector, style, or other factor exposures.", "whyPool": "Equal long and short dollars can retain beta, sector, style, and other systematic exposures."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Converting forecasts into portfolio weights is not a separate design problem from producing the forecasts.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: converting forecasts into portfolio weights *is* a distinct design problem — construction must turn predictions into feasible, risk-controlled actions.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Portfolio construction must translate predictive information into feasible, risk-controlled actions. G3: Backtesting and Benchmark Selection"}], "reference": "Volatility scaling, risk-based position construction, and residual factor exposure — Grinold & Kahn, *Active Portfolio Management* (portfolio construction, risk models); Investopedia \"Market-neutral strategy.\"", "n": 503}, {"id": "QF-D10G3Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G3", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A long-only U.S. equity model earned 12% annually in a period when its investable index earned 14%. The investment committee asks the researcher to compare the strategy with meaningful alternatives before claiming success. 412 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A zero-return cash benchmark alone is sufficient to determine whether the equity selection model added value.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A zero-return cash benchmark is insufficient — beating cash may just reward taking equity-market risk during a rising market.", "whyPool": "Beating cash may merely reward taking equity-market risk during a rising market."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The investable index is relevant because it represents a passive exposure available to the strategy.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "The investable index is a plausible opportunity-cost benchmark: it is the passive exposure actually available to a long-only U.S. equity mandate.", "whyPool": "It is a plausible opportunity-cost benchmark for a long-only U.S. equity mandate."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Under the stated returns, the strategy underperformed the index by 2 percentage points annually before further risk adjustment.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Arithmetic: 12% − 14% = −2 percentage points per year, so the strategy underperformed the index before further risk adjustment.", "whyPool": "12% - 14% = -2 percentage points per year."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Differences in beta, sector exposure, risk, costs, and constraints cannot matter when attributing the performance gap.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: differences in beta, sector, risk, costs, and constraints *do* matter — raw return alone does not explain *why* the strategy differed from its benchmark.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Raw return alone does not identify why the strategy differed from its benchmark."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Comparing with a simple signal or naive allocation can reveal whether model complexity adds incremental value.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing with a simple/naive alternative tests whether the added model complexity delivers incremental value worth its cost.", "whyPool": "A simple alternative tests whether sophistication improves performance enough to justify it."}], "reference": "Benchmark/opportunity-cost selection and performance attribution — CFA curriculum (performance evaluation, benchmark quality); Grinold & Kahn, *Active Portfolio Management* (benchmarks, residual return).", "n": 504}, {"id": "QF-D10G3Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G3", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A researcher tunes a strategy on 2010–2022 data and reports performance on the same years. The head of research asks for evidence that the result could survive deployment beginning in 2023.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The reported 2010–2022 result is not in-sample because it helped determine the final strategy.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: the 2010–2022 result *is* in-sample precisely because that data helped determine the final strategy — data used for specification/tuning is part of the development sample.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Data used for specification or tuning are part of the model- development sample."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Testing the frozen strategy on later, untouched data provides a more credible out-of-sample assessment.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Testing the frozen strategy on later, untouched data better approximates prospective use of the fixed rule (a credible out-of-sample assessment).", "whyPool": "Untouched later data better approximate prospective use of the fixed rule."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Strong in-sample performance guarantees comparable performance after 2022.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Strong in-sample performance guarantees nothing after 2022 — the strategy may be overfit or the return-generating process may change.", "whyPool": "The strategy may be overfit or the return-generating process may change. 413 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "D", "text": "Walk-forward testing cannot repeatedly train on past data and evaluate on subsequent periods while preserving temporal order.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: walk-forward *can* repeatedly train on past data and evaluate on subsequent periods while preserving temporal order — each evaluation window follows the fitting information.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Each evaluation window occurs after the information used to fit its model."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Retuning after observing every out-of-sample result eventually makes those observations part of development.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Retuning after observing each out-of-sample result eventually folds those observations into development, eroding the holdout's independence.", "whyPool": "Repeated feedback erodes the independence of the nominal holdout set."}], "reference": "In-sample/out-of-sample discipline and walk-forward analysis — Investopedia \"In-sample vs. out-of-sample,\" \"Walk-forward optimization\"; Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (cross-validation in finance, purging/embargo).", "n": 505}, {"id": "QF-D10G3Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G3", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A sector-rotation strategy beats the broad market, but the performance report shows that it maintained a persistent overweight to technology during a technology-led rally. The client asks how much return came from the timing model rather than static exposure.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Broad-market outperformance alone proves that the rotation decisions added alpha.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Broad-market outperformance alone does not prove rotation added alpha — a persistent sector tilt can explain the result without successful timing.", "whyPool": "A persistent sector tilt can explain the result without successful rotation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A sector-matched or exposure-matched benchmark can improve performance attribution.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A sector- or exposure-matched benchmark creates a more informative counterfactual for attribution.", "whyPool": "Matching relevant exposures creates a more informative counterfactual."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Comparing the strategy with a fixed portfolio holding its average sector weights can help isolate timing value.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing against a fixed portfolio holding the strategy's *average* sector weights isolates the value of dynamic timing from the average allocation.", "whyPool": "The comparison separates dynamic changes from the return of the average allocation."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Factor regression may reveal that known systematic exposures explain part of the excess return.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Factor regression can reveal that known systematic exposures explain part of the excess return (compensation for risk, not unique skill).", "whyPool": "Estimated factor loadings can identify compensation for systematic risks rather than unique skill."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Attribution is unnecessary if the backtest's total return is positive.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A positive total return does not make attribution unnecessary — investors need to know which exposures produced returns and whether they match the intended process.", "whyPool": "Investors need to know what exposures produced returns and whether they match the intended process. G4: Turnover, Costs, and Net Performance"}], "reference": "Return attribution, factor regression, and exposure-matched benchmarks — Grinold & Kahn, *Active Portfolio Management* (attribution, factor models); CFA curriculum (performance attribution).", "n": 506}, {"id": "QF-D10G4Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G4", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A predictive model has a small but statistically significant edge and trades every few minutes. In backtesting, the strategy earns 8% annually before costs. The head of trading estimates that spreads, commissions, and market impact would total roughly 10% annually at the proposed turnover.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The strategy is not profitable on a gross basis.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: the strategy *is* profitable on a gross basis — 8% before costs is positive.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Gross return is positive before costs."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The strategy is profitable on a net basis under the cost estimate.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "It is not profitable on a net basis: estimated 10% costs exceed the 8% gross return.", "whyPool": "Estimated costs exceed gross return."}, {"L": "C", "text": "High turnover cannot cause a statistically useful signal to become economically unviable.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: high turnover *can* make a statistically useful signal economically unviable — small edges get consumed by implementation costs.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Small edges can be consumed by implementation costs."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Transaction costs should be incorporated before deciding whether to deploy the strategy. 414 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Deployment decisions must be based on net, not gross, performance.", "whyPool": "Deployment decisions should be based on net, not gross, performance."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Reducing turnover could improve net performance even if gross predictive accuracy falls slightly.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Reducing turnover can improve net economics even if gross predictive accuracy falls slightly.", "whyPool": "Lower trading intensity may improve net economics."}], "reference": "Turnover, transaction-cost realism, and gross-vs-net alpha — Investopedia \"Transaction costs,\" \"Turnover ratio\"; Grinold & Kahn, *Active Portfolio Management* (cost of trading, net alpha).", "n": 507}, {"id": "QF-D10G4Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G4", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A daily strategy manages $10 million and turns over 40% of portfolio value each day, where turnover denotes the dollar value traded divided by portfolio value. The execution team estimates an average one-way trading cost of 0.10% of dollars traded and asks for a first-order cost estimate.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The strategy trades approximately $4 million per day under the stated turnover definition.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "40% × $10M = $4M traded per day under the stated turnover definition.", "whyPool": "40% × $10 million = $4 million traded."}, {"L": "B", "text": "The estimated daily trading cost is approximately $4,000.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "0.10% × $4M = $4,000 estimated daily trading cost.", "whyPool": "0.10% × $4 million = $4,000."}, {"L": "C", "text": "If gross expected profit is $3,000 per day, the strategy remains profitable after the estimated trading cost.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "$3,000 gross profit − $4,000 cost = a $1,000 estimated daily net loss, so it is not profitable after costs.", "whyPool": "$3,000 - $4,000 = a $1,000 estimated daily net loss."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Reducing unnecessary rebalancing can improve net returns without changing the underlying forecast model.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A turnover-aware position rule (less unnecessary rebalancing) can cut implementation drag separately from the forecast model.", "whyPool": "A turnover-aware position rule can reduce implementation drag separately from prediction."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The 0.10% estimate must remain constant regardless of order size, liquidity, or market conditions.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "The 0.10% rate need not stay constant — spreads and market impact vary with order size, liquidity, volatility, and execution conditions.", "whyPool": "Spreads and market impact can vary with size, liquidity, volatility, and execution conditions."}], "reference": "Turnover-to-cost arithmetic and variable market impact — Investopedia \"Transaction costs,\" \"Market impact\"; Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (transaction-cost modeling).", "n": 508}, {"id": "QF-D10G4Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G4", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Two versions of a signal have identical 9% gross annual returns. Version A costs an estimated 2% annually and Version B costs 6%; the portfolio manager prefers B because it reacts faster to every forecast change. 415 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Version A has an estimated 7% net return before other fees.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "9% gross − 2% cost = 7% net for Version A (before other fees).", "whyPool": "9% gross minus 2% costs equals 7% net."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Version B has an estimated 3% net return before other fees.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "9% gross − 6% cost = 3% net for Version B (before other fees).", "whyPool": "9% gross minus 6% costs equals 3% net."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Faster reaction necessarily makes Version B economically superior.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Faster reaction does not make B superior — its higher implementation cost leaves it with lower estimated net performance.", "whyPool": "The higher implementation cost leaves B with lower estimated net performance."}, {"L": "D", "text": "A no-trade band could not reduce turnover by leaving small target-weight changes unexecuted.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: a no-trade band *can* reduce turnover by leaving small target-weight changes unexecuted, avoiding costs that exceed their benefit.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Ignoring immaterial adjustments can avoid costs that exceed their expected benefit."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that cost estimates should include market impact as well as explicit commissions when those costs are material.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Double-negative embedded claim: the inner claim — that cost estimates should include market impact as well as explicit commissions when material — is correct and should be accepted, so \"a reviewer would be wrong to accept it\" is false. Impact/spread can dominate explicit fees.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. Impact and spread costs can dominate explicit fees for some strategies. G5: Capacity, Liquidity, and Scalability"}], "reference": "Net-return arithmetic, no-trade bands, and full-cost accounting (impact + spread + commissions) — Grinold & Kahn, *Active Portfolio Management* (rebalancing, cost control); Investopedia \"Transaction costs,\" \"Bid-ask spread.\"", "n": 509}, {"id": "QF-D10G5Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G5", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A strategy earned strong returns while managing $5 million in small-cap stocks. A prospective investor wants to allocate $500 million, but the head trader warns that many desired positions represent a substantial fraction of average daily volume.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Historical percentage returns at $5 million necessarily remain unchanged at $500 million.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Percentage returns at $5M need not survive at $500M — scaling can worsen execution and prevent obtaining historical positions at observed prices.", "whyPool": "Scaling can worsen execution and prevent the fund from obtaining the historical positions at observed prices."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Larger orders cannot consume available depth and create greater market impact.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: larger orders *can* consume available depth and create greater market impact, walking the order book.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Larger demand often walks the order book or moves other participants' quotes. 416 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "C", "text": "Participation-rate or average-daily-volume limits can constrain feasible position changes.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Participation-rate or average-daily-volume limits can constrain feasible position changes to a manageable share of liquidity.", "whyPool": "Such limits restrict trading to a manageable portion of market liquidity."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Spreading trades over time may reduce immediate impact but can introduce delay and price risk.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Spreading trades over time may cut immediate impact but introduces delay and price risk (prices can move before completion).", "whyPool": "Slower execution trades impact against the risk that prices move before completion."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Capacity analysis should consider how the strategy's own trading changes execution prices.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Capacity analysis must account for how the strategy's own trading moves execution prices — a scalable backtest cannot assume unlimited fills at unaffected historical prices.", "whyPool": "A scalable backtest cannot assume unlimited fills at unaffected historical prices."}], "reference": "Capacity, market depth, and impact from own trading — Investopedia \"Market impact,\" \"Liquidity\"; Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (strategy capacity).", "n": 510}, {"id": "QF-D10G5Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G5", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A relative-value strategy has an expected gross edge of 0.50% per trade. Estimated round-trip impact is 0.10% at $1 million, 0.30% at $5 million, and 0.70% at $10 million; the portfolio manager asks whether more capital always produces proportionally more profit.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "At $1 million, the estimated net edge is not 0.40% before other costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: at $1M the net edge *is* 0.40% (0.50% − 0.10%), so \"not 0.40%\" is false.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. 0.50% - 0.10% = 0.40%."}, {"L": "B", "text": "At $5 million, the estimated net edge is 0.20% before other costs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "At $5M, net edge = 0.50% − 0.30% = 0.20% before other costs.", "whyPool": "0.50% - 0.30% = 0.20%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "At $10 million, the estimated net edge remains positive.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "At $10M, net edge = 0.50% − 0.70% = −0.20% before other costs — negative, not positive.", "whyPool": "0.50% - 0.70% = -0.20% before other costs."}, {"L": "D", "text": "The relationship illustrates that percentage return can decline as strategy size increases.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Rising impact consumes an increasing share of the fixed gross opportunity, so percentage return can decline as size grows.", "whyPool": "Rising impact consumes an increasing share of the fixed gross opportunity."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Maximizing assets under management and maximizing net investment performance are always the same objective.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Maximizing AUM and maximizing net performance are not the same objective — added capital can reduce returns/profits once capacity constraints bind.", "whyPool": "Additional capital can reduce returns or profits once capacity constraints bind."}], "reference": "Return degradation with size and the capacity/AUM tradeoff — Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (capacity); Investopedia \"Market impact,\" \"Diseconomies of scale in funds.\"", "n": 511}, {"id": "QF-D10G5Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G5", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "Several funds begin trading similar signals in the same securities. The chief risk officer observes faster price adjustment, more crowded entries, and sharp losses when many funds attempt to exit simultaneously.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Crowding can reduce the amount or duration of the original opportunity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Crowding/competition can pull information into prices sooner, reducing the size or duration of the original opportunity.", "whyPool": "Competition can cause information to enter prices sooner."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Independent firms using similar data and models can still create correlated positions.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Independent firms using similar data and models can create correlated positions without any explicit coordination.", "whyPool": "Similar processes can lead to the same trades even without explicit coordination."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Exit liquidity cannot be worse precisely when crowded strategies seek to unwind.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: exit liquidity *can* be worse precisely when crowded strategies unwind — simultaneous selling overwhelms buyers and amplifies impact.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Simultaneous selling can overwhelm available buyers and amplify impact."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Diversifying fund names guarantees diversification of underlying strategy exposure.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Diversifying fund *names* does not guarantee diversified *exposure* — different managers can hold substantially overlapping positions and risks.", "whyPool": "Different managers can hold substantially overlapping positions and risks."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Capacity estimates based only on calm, uncrowded periods may be optimistic. 417 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Capacity estimates from calm, uncrowded periods may be optimistic; stress and competitor activity can materially reduce executable capacity.", "whyPool": "Stress conditions and competitor activity can materially reduce executable capacity. G6: Leverage, Exposure, and Risk Controls"}], "reference": "Crowding, correlated unwinds, and stress-conditional capacity — Investopedia \"Crowded trade,\" \"Liquidity risk\"; CFA curriculum (liquidity and market structure risk).", "n": 512}, {"id": "QF-D10G6Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G6", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A fund has $10 million of equity and uses borrowed funds to hold $30 million of a risky portfolio, creating 3-to-1 gross exposure relative to equity. The risk committee asks how leverage changes outcomes before financing costs.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A 2% gain on the risky portfolio produces approximately a 6% gain on fund equity before costs.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "2% × $30M = $0.6M gain = 6% of $10M equity before costs.", "whyPool": "2% × $30 million = $0.6 million, or 6% of $10 million equity."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A reviewer would be wrong to accept the claim that a 2% loss on the risky portfolio produces approximately a 6% loss on fund equity before costs.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Double-negative embedded claim: the inner claim — a 2% loss produces ~6% loss on equity ($0.6M is 6% of equity) — is correct and should be accepted, so \"a reviewer would be wrong to accept it\" is false.", "whyPool": "The underlying claim is correct and should be accepted. A $0.6 million loss is likewise 6% of fund equity."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Leverage magnifies gains but leaves losses unchanged.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Leverage magnifies both favorable *and* adverse portfolio returns — losses are not left unchanged.", "whyPool": "Leverage magnifies both favorable and adverse portfolio returns."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Adverse moves can trigger margin calls or forced deleveraging even if the long-run thesis remains valid.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Adverse moves can trigger margin calls or forced deleveraging even if the long-run thesis remains valid, because lenders/brokers set collateral needs by current conditions.", "whyPool": "Lenders and brokers impose collateral requirements based on current conditions."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Financing cost and availability cannot affect the realized benefit of leverage.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: financing cost and availability *do* affect the realized benefit of leverage — borrowing is neither free nor guaranteed to remain available.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Borrowing is neither free nor guaranteed to remain available."}], "reference": "Leverage math, margin/funding risk, and symmetric magnification — Investopedia \"Leverage,\" \"Margin call\"; CFA curriculum (leverage and financing risk).", "n": 513}, {"id": "QF-D10G6Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G6", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A market-neutral portfolio holds $120 million long and $120 million short against $40 million of investor equity. The chief risk officer is concerned that the team reports only zero net dollar exposure. 418 Rev:081020261230", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The portfolio's net dollar exposure is zero.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "$120M long − $120M short = $0 net dollar exposure.", "whyPool": "$120 million long minus $120 million short equals zero net dollars."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Its gross exposure is $240 million, or 600% of investor equity.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Gross exposure = $120M + $120M = $240M; $240M / $40M = 6, i.e., 600% of investor equity.", "whyPool": "$120 million + $120 million = $240 million; $240 million/$40 million = 6, or 600%."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Zero net dollar exposure means the portfolio cannot suffer a large loss.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Zero net dollars does not preclude large losses — high gross exposure, imperfect hedges, and correlated adverse moves can create substantial losses.", "whyPool": "High gross exposure, imperfect hedges, and correlated adverse moves can create substantial losses."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Long and short positions cannot both lose if their relative prices move adversely.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: longs and shorts *can* both lose — longs can fall while shorts rise, harming both sides of the relative trade.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. The longs can fall while the shorts rise, harming both sides of the relative trade."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Sector, beta, factor, liquidity, and concentration limits can provide information not captured by net dollars alone.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Sector, beta, factor, liquidity, and concentration limits capture economically important risks that net dollar exposure alone does not measure.", "whyPool": "Net dollar exposure does not measure every economically important risk."}], "reference": "Gross vs. net exposure and residual risk in long/short books — Investopedia \"Gross exposure,\" \"Net exposure\"; Grinold & Kahn, *Active Portfolio Management* (long/short risk).", "n": 514}, {"id": "QF-D10G6Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G6", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A trading desk proposes an automatic 5% stop-loss on every position. The portfolio manager expects it to prevent large losses, while the quant warns that volatile assets can cross the threshold during ordinary price noise and that gaps can bypass the intended exit price.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A stop-loss guarantees execution at exactly 5% below entry.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A stop-loss does not guarantee execution at exactly 5% below entry — a gap or lack of liquidity can cause a fill well beyond the trigger.", "whyPool": "A gap or lack of liquidity can cause execution well beyond the trigger level."}, {"L": "B", "text": "A fixed percentage threshold can affect high- and low-volatility assets differently.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A fixed percentage threshold affects assets differently: a 5% move can be routine for one asset and exceptional for another.", "whyPool": "A 5% move may be routine for one asset and exceptional for another."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Frequent stop-outs cannot crystallize losses shortly before a valid signal recovers.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: frequent stop-outs *can* crystallize losses just before a valid signal recovers (whipsaw when prices reverse after the threshold).", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Mechanical exits can create whipsaw when prices reverse after reaching the threshold."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Position sizing and portfolio-level exposure limits can complement, rather than necessarily replace, stop-loss rules.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Position sizing and portfolio-level exposure limits complement — rather than necessarily replace — stop-loss rules, addressing different dimensions of risk.", "whyPool": "Different controls address different dimensions of loss and concentration risk. 419 Rev:081020261230"}, {"L": "E", "text": "Risk controls should not be evaluated for behavior during gaps, illiquidity, and correlated stress events.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: risk controls *should* be evaluated for behavior during gaps, illiquidity, and correlated stress — controls designed for normal conditions may fail there.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Controls designed from normal trading conditions may fail or behave unexpectedly in stress. G7: Robustness, Degradation, and Live Deployment"}], "reference": "Stop-loss mechanics (gaps, slippage, whipsaw) and layered risk controls — Investopedia \"Stop-loss order,\" \"Slippage\"; CFA curriculum (risk management, stress testing).", "n": 515}, {"id": "QF-D10G7Q1", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G7", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A model performed well in a low-volatility training period but loses money after deployment during a high-volatility regime. The research lead asks whether the model should be retrained immediately or whether the failure reflects a deeper change.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "A regime shift cannot change feature distributions and the relationship between features and returns.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: a regime shift *can* change both feature distributions and the conditional feature→return relationship.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Both the inputs and the conditional return process can change across regimes."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Retraining on the most recent observations always restores profitable performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Retraining on recent observations does not always restore profitability — recent data may be limited/noisy, and the predictive relationship may have disappeared.", "whyPool": "Recent data may be limited or noisy, and the predictive relationship may have disappeared."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Comparing live inputs and outcomes with training distributions can help diagnose drift.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Comparing live inputs and outcomes against training distributions (distribution/residual monitoring) helps diagnose drift.", "whyPool": "Distribution and residual monitoring can identify ways deployment differs from training."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Performance by volatility regime can reveal dependence hidden by an overall backtest average.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Breaking down performance by volatility regime can reveal dependence hidden by an overall backtest average.", "whyPool": "Aggregate results can conceal that success was concentrated in one environment."}, {"L": "E", "text": "The team should consider recalibration, redesign, or retirement if the original economic relationship no longer holds.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Recalibration, redesign, or retirement should be considered when the original economic relationship no longer holds — retraining is not the only response.", "whyPool": "Retraining is not the only response when the underlying mechanism has degraded."}], "reference": "Regime dependence, model drift, and retrain/retire decisions — Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (structural breaks, drift); Investopedia \"Regime change,\" \"Model risk.\"", "n": 516}, {"id": "QF-D10G7Q2", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G7", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A strategy's backtest assumes fills at the closing price, but live orders arrive after the close signal is computed and execute at worse prices. The trading desk also reports rejected orders and occasional missing data, none of which appeared in research.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "The discrepancy cannot arise from implementation assumptions even if the predictive model behaves as coded.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: the discrepancy *can* arise from implementation assumptions even when the predictive model behaves as coded — timing/execution/system failures separate live from theoretical results.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Timing, execution, and system failures can separate live results from theoretical model output."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Backtest success alone validates the reliability of the production data and order pipeline.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Backtest success alone does not validate the production data and order pipeline — a historical simulation doesn't test every live data, software, broker, and execution dependency.", "whyPool": "A historical simulation does not test every live data, software, broker, and execution dependency."}, {"L": "C", "text": "Paper trading or staged deployment can expose operational problems before full capital is committed.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Paper trading or staged deployment exposes operational problems before full capital is committed (controlled rollout with limited financial risk).", "whyPool": "Controlled rollout provides evidence about the complete implementation with limited financial risk."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Monitoring signal timestamps, order status, fills, slippage, and data quality cannot help locate the performance gap. 420 Rev:081020261230", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: monitoring timestamps, order status, fills, slippage, and data quality *does* help locate the performance gap by linking forecasts to submitted trades and realized outcomes.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. These records connect research forecasts to submitted trades and realized outcomes."}, {"L": "E", "text": "Production evaluation should compare realized execution with prices that were actually attainable after the decision time.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Valid production evaluation compares realized execution against prices *actually attainable after the decision time* — respecting when the signal became available and an order could reach the market.", "whyPool": "A valid comparison must respect when the signal became available and an order could reach the market."}], "reference": "Implementation shortfall, execution realism, and staged deployment — Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (backtesting realism, false discoveries); Investopedia \"Implementation shortfall,\" \"Slippage,\" \"Paper trading.\"", "n": 517}, {"id": "QF-D10G7Q3", "part": "QF", "domain": "10", "domainName": "Quantitative Strategy Design & Evaluation", "group": "G7", "weeks": [7, 8], "weekMax": 8, "polarity": "normal", "scenario": "A deployed signal's six-month rolling information coefficient falls from 0.06 to near zero, turnover rises, and net returns become negative. The investment committee asks for a monitoring rule that avoids both ignoring decay and retiring a strategy after one unlucky week.", "choices": [{"L": "A", "text": "Several related diagnostics provide stronger evidence than a single short-term P&L observation.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "Several related diagnostics (signal quality, costs, exposures, outcomes) together provide stronger evidence than a single short-term P&L observation.", "whyPool": "Signal quality, costs, exposures, and outcomes together help distinguish noise from structural degradation."}, {"L": "B", "text": "Predefined review thresholds cannot reduce purely emotional reactions to recent performance.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "Reverses the correct conclusion: predefined review thresholds *can* reduce emotional reactions — governance set in advance makes escalation/review more consistent.", "whyPool": "The statement reverses the correct conclusion. Governance established in advance makes escalation and review more consistent."}, {"L": "C", "text": "A declining information coefficient alongside higher costs can explain deterioration in net returns.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A declining information coefficient alongside higher costs explains deteriorating net returns: weaker predictive value cuts gross opportunity while higher turnover adds drag.", "whyPool": "Weaker predictive value reduces gross opportunity while higher turnover increases implementation drag."}, {"L": "D", "text": "Continuing indefinitely is justified because the original backtest was statistically significant.", "verdict": "False", "accurate": false, "why": "A statistically significant original backtest does not justify continuing indefinitely — historical evidence does not guarantee the relationship persists after deployment.", "whyPool": "Historical evidence does not guarantee that a relationship persists after deployment."}, {"L": "E", "text": "A retirement decision should consider statistical uncertainty, implementation quality, current market conditions, and whether the economic thesis remains credible.", "verdict": "True", "accurate": true, "why": "A retirement decision should weigh statistical uncertainty, implementation quality, current market conditions, and whether the economic thesis still holds — no single metric distinguishes temporary underperformance from permanent decay.", "whyPool": "No single metric conclusively distinguishes temporary underperformance from permanent decay. 421 Rev:081020261230"}], "reference": "Signal decay monitoring (information coefficient), governance thresholds, and retirement criteria — Grinold & Kahn, *Active Portfolio Management* (information coefficient, fundamental law of active management); Lopez de Prado, *Advances in Financial ML* (deflated Sharpe, strategy decay).", "n": 518}], "overridden": ["ML-D6G5Q5"], "suspects": [], "withdrawn": ["ML-D1G2Q8", "ML-D2G4Q5", "ML-D3G1Q4", "ML-D3G3Q6", "ML-D5G5Q4", "ML-D6G4Q6", "ML-D6G5Q5", "ML-D6G5Q6", "QF-D8G4Q1"]};